INSERT INTO `news_articles` VALUES (1,20,'Snapdragon 8 Gen 4 Became the 8 Elite: How to Read Qualcomm Chip Names When You Shop','snapdragon-8-gen-4-debut-qualcomm-summit-2024','<p>If you remember waiting for a \"Snapdragon 8 Gen 4\", you won\'t find it on any spec sheet. Qualcomm launched that chip in October 2024 under a new name, the <strong>Snapdragon 8 Elite</strong>. It was the first phone chip to use Qualcomm\'s own Oryon CPU cores, the same family that powers Snapdragon X laptops, and it was a big jump in speed over the 8 Gen 3.</p>\n<p>The new name stuck, and the numbering started again. Since then we\'ve had the <strong>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5</strong> and, most recently, the 2nm <strong>8 Elite Gen 6</strong>, which arrived first in the <a href=\"/news/xiaomi-18-pro-snapdragon-8-elite-gen-6-launch\">Xiaomi 18 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Why this matters in a shop</h2>\n<p>Older and newer flagships sit side by side in Nepal, and the names make them easy to mix up. Two examples from our catalogue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">Galaxy S25 Ultra</a>: Snapdragon 8 Elite, Rs 1,84,999.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a>: Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy, Rs 2,24,999.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Gen 5 is faster, but the original 8 Elite is still far more power than most people will use. If a seller offers last year\'s flagship at a good discount, the chip alone is not a reason to say no. Battery health, the warranty and how many years of updates are left matter more.</p>\n<p>A quick rule: \"8 Elite\" with no generation number is the 2024 chip, and a \"Gen 5\" or \"Gen 6\" label means a newer one. \"For Galaxy\" versions are slightly higher-clocked editions that Samsung uses.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Why Qualcomm changed the name</h2>\n<p>For three years the pattern was easy. Snapdragon 8 Gen 1, Gen 2, Gen 3. Everyone expected Gen 4. Qualcomm chose \"Elite\" because the processor inside was no longer built around cores licensed from Arm. Qualcomm had bought a company called Nuvia, whose engineers had designed chips at Apple, and the new Oryon cores came from that team. The company wanted the break to be visible on the box.</p>\n<p>The trouble is that a name which means something to engineers confuses everybody else. A customer who knows that Gen 3 is older than Gen 5 has no way of guessing where \"Elite\" belongs. Then the 8 Gen 5 arrived beside the 8 Elite Gen 5, two different chips with nearly the same name, and shop staff began to mix them up.</p>\n<h2>The complete order, from oldest to newest</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8 Gen 2</strong> (late 2022). Found in used flagships. Still quick.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8 Gen 3</strong> (late 2023). Galaxy S24 Ultra, Xiaomi 14, OnePlus 12.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8s Gen 3</strong> (2024). A cheaper relative, in the OnePlus Nord 5 and others.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8 Elite</strong> (late 2024). Galaxy S25 series, OnePlus 13, POCO F8 Pro, Honor 600 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8s Gen 4</strong> (2025). POCO F7, OnePlus Nord 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5</strong> (late 2025). OnePlus 15R. Below the Elite Gen 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5</strong> (late 2025). Galaxy S26 Ultra, OnePlus 15, Xiaomi 17 Ultra.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 6</strong> (2026). Xiaomi 18 Pro and the next wave.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The number after \"Gen\" counts the generation for the whole family, which is why the Elite jumped straight to Gen 5. There never was an Elite Gen 2, 3 or 4.</p>\n<h2>What the small letters and words mean</h2>\n<p><strong>\"s\"</strong>, as in 8s Gen 4, marks a lower-cost version with slower cores and graphics. It is still far above any 7-series chip.</p>\n<p><strong>\"For Galaxy\"</strong> is a small speed increase reserved for Samsung. You will not feel it.</p>\n<p><strong>\"Extreme\", \"Leading Version\" and similar</strong> are higher-clocked editions sold to gaming brands. They run faster and hotter, and need cooling.</p>\n<p><strong>\"7s\", \"6s\" and \"4s\"</strong> follow the same logic in lower families, with one trap: a 7s chip can be slower than a plain 6 of a newer year.</p>\n<h2>Does a flagship chip matter to you?</h2>\n<p>Consider what a phone does most of the day. Messaging, scrolling, video, calls, maps, banking. A mid-range chip from 2023 handles those without effort. The flagship chip earns its price in four situations: demanding games for long periods, 4K and 8K video work, camera processing on large sensors, and keeping a phone smooth in its fifth year. If none describes you, a 7-series or 8s chip is the sensible place to stop, and the money is better spent on battery, storage or a telephoto lens.</p>\n<h2>Same chip, different phone</h2>\n<p>Two phones with the same Snapdragon can behave very differently. Cooling decides how long the chip can stay fast. A thin phone with a small vapour chamber may lose a third of its speed after fifteen minutes of a heavy game. Memory type matters as well. LPDDR5X and UFS 4.0 storage let the chip breathe, and older parts hold it back. Software tuning adds another layer. So when a seller says \"same processor as the S26 Ultra\", treat it as the start of the conversation.</p>\n<h2>And Samsung\'s Exynos, MediaTek and Google?</h2>\n<p>MediaTek\'s Dimensity 9500 and 9500s stand beside the Elite Gen 5 and appear in the vivo X300 Pro, OPPO Find X9 Pro and POCO X8 Pro Max. The Dimensity 8500 and 8350 are rivals of the 8s class. Samsung uses its Exynos 2600 in some S26 models and the Exynos 2500 in the S26 FE. Google\'s Tensor G5 concentrates on photo and AI work and is slower in games. All of them are strong enough that brand alone should not decide your purchase.</p>\n<h2>How to check a chip in the shop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Look in Settings, About phone, for the processor name.</li>\n<li>Install CPU-Z or DevCheck and read the model number. SM8750 is the 8 Elite, SM8850 is the 8 Elite Gen 5, SM8650 is the 8 Gen 3.</li>\n<li>Compare with the specification on the maker\'s site for that exact variant and region.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The last point is important with Samsung, as different markets received different processors in the same model. An official Nepali unit and an imported one may not match.</p>\n<h2>Buying an older flagship today</h2>\n<p>A phone with an 8 Gen 3 or 8 Elite is often the best value in the country. It is fast enough for years, its camera system was built for the top of the market, and its price has fallen. The questions to ask are different from those for a new phone. How many years of updates remain? What is the battery health? Is it a Nepali unit with MDMS registration? Has the screen been replaced? Our <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-s24-review-snapdragon-8-gen-3-flagship-value\">Galaxy S24 review</a> looks at one such phone in detail.</p>\n<h2>What to expect next</h2>\n<p>The Elite Gen 6 is built on a 2nm process, which should mean better efficiency more than higher speed. The first phones are already in China and Europe, led by the <a href=\"/news/xiaomi-18-pro-snapdragon-8-elite-gen-6-launch\">Xiaomi 18 Pro</a>. Samsung\'s S27 series is expected to follow. For buyers in Nepal, the arrival of a new generation is mostly good news because it pulls down the price of the last one.</p>\n<h2>A short glossary for the counter</h2>\n<p><strong>Core:</strong> a worker inside the processor. Big cores do heavy work, small cores save power. <strong>GHz:</strong> how quickly a core works, useful only when comparing similar designs. <strong>nm:</strong> the manufacturing generation. Smaller is usually cooler. <strong>NPU:</strong> the part for AI tasks. <strong>Throttling:</strong> slowing down to avoid heat. <strong>AnTuTu:</strong> a benchmark. A higher number is faster on paper, and differences under 20 per cent are hard to feel.</p>\n<h2>Our advice</h2>\n<p>Do not chase the newest name. Decide your budget, list the phones in it, and use the chip as one line among six: screen, battery, camera, storage, support and speed. If two phones tie on everything else, take the newer chip. That is the only time it should have the casting vote.</p>','technology','news/news-snapdragon.jpg',1,2743,'Qualcomm renamed the Snapdragon 8 Gen 4 to Snapdragon 8 Elite, then added Gen 5 and Gen 6. What the names mean when you compare phones in Nepal.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(2,20,'Apple Intelligence in Nepal: Which iPhones Get It and How to Switch It On','apple-intelligence-nepal-iphone-users-guide','<p>Apple Intelligence is Apple\'s name for the AI features built into iOS, such as rewriting text, summarising notifications and removing people from photos. A lot of Nepali iPhone owners ask us whether it works here. In short, it does on the right phone, but you have to turn it on yourself.</p>\n<h2>Which iPhones support it</h2>\n<p>It needs an <strong>iPhone 15 Pro or 15 Pro Max</strong>, or <strong>any iPhone 16 or 17 model</strong>, including the cheaper 16e and 17e. Older phones, including the regular iPhone 15, don\'t get it, because the feature needs more memory than they have.</p>\n<p>If you\'re buying new, the cheapest current model with Apple Intelligence is the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a> at Rs 1,32,499, followed by the <a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-16\">iPhone 16</a> (Rs 1,46,599) and <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> (Rs 1,64,999).</p>\n<h2>How to turn it on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update to the latest iOS.</li>\n<li>Set your iPhone language and Siri language to a supported language. English works.</li>\n<li>Go to <em>Settings › Apple Intelligence &amp; Siri</em> and switch it on. The phone downloads the models, which can take a while on mobile data, so use Wi-Fi.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What you\'ll actually use</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Writing Tools</strong>: select text in almost any app to proofread it, change the tone or shorten it.</li>\n<li><strong>Clean Up</strong> in Photos removes a stranger from the background of a shot.</li>\n<li><strong>Notification summaries</strong> condense busy group chats. They\'re handy, but they occasionally get the meaning wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>ChatGPT in Siri</strong> is optional, and the phone asks before it sends anything to ChatGPT.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Apple keeps adding languages and features, so if something isn\'t showing, check for an iOS update first.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Why some iPhones are left out</h2>\n<p>Apple Intelligence keeps language models on the phone so that your messages do not have to leave it. Those models sit in memory while they work, and 8GB is the practical minimum. The iPhone 15 and 15 Plus have 6GB, and the older ones have less. It is a hard limit and no update will change it. The 15 Pro, with 8GB, is the oldest phone on the list. Newer models with 12GB, such as the 17 Pro and 18 Pro families, have more room and are likely to receive the heavier features first.</p>\n<h2>Storage matters too</h2>\n<p>The models need several gigabytes of free space, and the amount grows as Apple adds languages. If your phone is full, the switch will not turn on, or the download will stop without explanation. Check Settings, General, iPhone Storage. Remove old offline maps, WhatsApp media and unused apps, and aim for at least 10GB free.</p>\n<h2>Setting it up in Nepal, step by step</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update iOS in Settings, General, Software Update.</li>\n<li>In Settings, General, Language and Region, choose English as the phone language. English (India), English (UK) and English (US) are fine. You can keep Nepali as a second language for keyboards and apps.</li>\n<li>In Apple Intelligence and Siri, set Siri\'s language to match.</li>\n<li>Turn on Apple Intelligence and connect to Wi-Fi and a charger. The first download can take from twenty minutes to a few hours.</li>\n<li>Open Notes, write two sentences, select them and look for Writing Tools. If it appears, you are ready.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your region can stay Nepal. The requirement concerns language and device, and there have been restrictions for Apple accounts in some markets. If the option does not appear at all, check the model, the iOS version and the language in that order.</p>\n<h2>Does it work in Nepali?</h2>\n<p>Not at the moment, as far as Apple\'s list of languages goes. You can write Nepali as usual, but Writing Tools, summaries and Siri\'s new abilities work in the supported languages. Romanised Nepali confuses the summaries, sometimes amusingly. Turn off summaries for chats where family writes in a mix of Nepali and English, as the results can be wrong in ways that matter.</p>\n<h2>The features, ranked by usefulness</h2>\n<p><strong>Writing Tools.</strong> The best of them. If English is your second language, the Proofread button is a patient teacher. It shows each change and explains why. Professional changes the tone of a hurried email. It works in nearly any text box, including browsers and Gmail.</p>\n<p><strong>Clean Up.</strong> Removes a stranger, a wire or a bin from a photo. It is excellent with simple backgrounds and poor with patterns such as temple carvings.</p>\n<p><strong>Call recording and transcripts.</strong> Both sides hear a notice. Useful for students and journalists in English.</p>\n<p><strong>Mail summaries and priority.</strong> Helpful if you get many newsletters.</p>\n<p><strong>Visual intelligence.</strong> Point the camera at a plant, a poster or a dish and ask. It sends a picture to a service, so it needs data.</p>\n<p><strong>Image Playground and Genmoji.</strong> Fun for a week.</p>\n<p><strong>Live translation.</strong> Handy with supported languages. Guides in Thamel will find it useful with Spanish, French, German, Japanese, Korean and Chinese-speaking guests, depending on what Apple supports at the time.</p>\n<h2>What leaves your phone</h2>\n<p>Small tasks stay on the device. Larger ones can go to Apple\'s Private Cloud Compute, where Apple says data is not stored and is not accessible to the company. Requests to ChatGPT are separate. The phone asks each time, and you can use it without an account. If you work with confidential documents, leave the ChatGPT extension off. You can see a report of requests in Settings, Privacy and Security, Apple Intelligence Report.</p>\n<h2>Battery and heat</h2>\n<p>For a day or two after enabling it, the phone indexes photos and messages. It may feel warm and drain faster. This settles. Notification summaries and Clean Up use little power. Long sessions of image generation use a lot. If battery life is poor after a week, turn off features one by one and keep the ones you use.</p>\n<h2>Should you buy a new iPhone for it?</h2>\n<p>In our opinion, no. Buy an iPhone for its camera, battery, size and years of updates. Apple Intelligence is a pleasant extra and it will matter more as it improves. If you are choosing between an iPhone 15 and a 16 or 17e at similar prices, take the one that supports it, since it will also receive longer updates. The <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a> is the cheapest current model with it, and the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> is the better phone. Read <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-17-review-nepal\">our iPhone 17 review</a>.</p>\n<h2>For used iPhone buyers</h2>\n<p>A used 15 Pro, with its titanium frame and telephoto camera, is popular in Nepal. It supports Apple Intelligence and will for some years. Check battery health, that all parts are genuine in Settings, General, About, that Activation Lock is off, and that the IMEI is registered. The regular iPhone 15 is cheaper for a reason, and one reason is this.</p>\n<h2>Compared with Galaxy AI and Gemini</h2>\n<p>Android phones offer more AI tools, earlier, and some of them are better: Circle to Search, call translation and generative photo editing are widely used. Apple\'s approach is slower and more private. Samsung allows on-device processing in its settings. Nobody\'s assistant understands Nepali as well as you would like, though Google\'s is closest. If AI tools in Nepali matter to you, try Gemini\'s voice mode on either platform.</p>\n<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The menu is missing.</strong> Wrong model, old iOS or unsupported language.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Downloading\" for hours.</strong> Connect to power and Wi-Fi, and free some storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing Tools do not show.</strong> Select more text, or use the Apple Intelligence icon above the keyboard.</li>\n<li><strong>Summaries are wrong.</strong> Settings, Notifications, Summarise Notifications: turn off the offending app.</li>\n<li><strong>Siri sounds the same.</strong> Siri\'s larger changes come with later updates and depend on the model.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Parents and shared phones</h2>\n<p>Screen Time lets you restrict image creation, writing tools and the ChatGPT extension for a child\'s phone. Open Content and Privacy Restrictions, then Intelligence and Siri. It takes a minute, and it is worth doing before homework season.</p>\n<h2>In one paragraph</h2>\n<p>If your iPhone supports it, switch it on, keep Proofread and Clean Up, turn off summaries for Nepali chats, and leave the rest until you need it. If your iPhone does not support it, you are missing a few conveniences and nothing essential.</p>','mobile','news/news-apple-intelligence.jpg',1,1432,'Which iPhones support Apple Intelligence, how to turn it on in Nepal, and what the features actually do. Plus the cheapest current iPhone that has it.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(3,20,'Gaming Laptops Under Rs 1.5 Lakh in Nepal: What Is Actually Left in 2026','best-gaming-laptops-nepal-under-150000-2024','<p>A couple of years ago Rs 1.5 lakh bought a decent RTX 4060 laptop in Nepal. Prices have moved a lot since then, and that budget is now the very bottom of the gaming market. You can still play, but you need to know where the compromises are.</p>\n<h2>What we\'d consider</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-2023-intel-core-i5-13420h-rtx-3050-8gb-512gb-156-fhd-144hz\">HP Victus 15 (i5-13420H, RTX 3050 6GB)</a>: Rs 1,46,499 at ONIN Infosys. A quiet-looking laptop that runs esports titles well on its 144Hz screen.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-f16-fx607-core-5-210h-16gb-512gb-rtx-3050\">ASUS TUF F16 (Core 5 210H, RTX 3050)</a>: Rs 1,42,999. A bigger 16-inch screen and the sturdy TUF build.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai-ryzen-5-240-16gb-512gb-rtx-5050\">Acer Nitro V 16 AI (RTX 5050)</a>: listed at Rs 1,39,999. It\'s the only RTX 50-series laptop near this price, so check that it\'s actually in stock before you plan around it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The honest advice</h2>\n<p>The RTX 3050 handles Valorant, CS2, Dota 2 and Fortnite comfortably, but new AAA games will need low or medium settings. If you mostly play big single-player games, stretching to the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-15ahp9-2024-ryzen-7-7445hs\">LOQ 15AHP9</a> (Rs 1,64,499, a full 95W RTX 3050) or the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">LOQ 15 with an RTX 5050</a> (Rs 1,94,999) makes a real difference.</p>\n<p>Whatever you choose, ask the shop for the GPU wattage (TGP). The same chip name can mean very different performance. For the full range at every budget, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptop guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The three laptops side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a15-fa506ncg-2024\">Asus TUF Gaming A15 FA506NCG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,55,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 7445HS (4nm), 6 cores / 12 thread...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 48 Wh; 180W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-2025-13th-gen-i7-13620h-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb\">Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-51 2025 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,69,999</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1080), 165H...</td><td>Intel Core i7-13620H (Intel 7), 10 cores / 16...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 57 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>15.6&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare,...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB...</td><td>1x 16GB SO-DIMM DDR5-5600</td><td>60Wh</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table includes two machines above the limit, since the most useful question at this budget is what a little more money would change.</p>\n<h2>How did we get here?</h2>\n<p>In 2023 a student with Rs 1.4 lakh could choose among RTX 3060 and 4050 laptops. Three things pushed prices up. Memory and SSDs became dearer in 2026. Graphics chips were in short supply as makers favoured AI hardware. And the rupee did not help. So the same money now reaches one class lower. It is nobody\'s fault in the shop, and bargaining will not undo it.</p>\n<h2>What an RTX 3050 can do in 2026</h2>\n<p>There are two versions, with 4GB and 6GB of video memory. The 6GB version is meaningfully better, and it is the only one we would buy. With it, at 1080p:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Very smooth:</strong> Valorant, CS2, League of Legends, Dota 2, Rocket League, Minecraft without shaders.</li>\n<li><strong>Good:</strong> Fortnite in performance mode, Apex Legends, GTA V, Forza Horizon 5 at medium, Genshin Impact.</li>\n<li><strong>Playable with compromises:</strong> Cyberpunk 2077, Hogwarts Legacy and Red Dead Redemption 2 at low to medium with DLSS.</li>\n<li><strong>Not recommended:</strong> the newest releases that list 8GB of video memory as a minimum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If your friends play Valorant on weekday evenings, this is enough. If you dream of open-world games with ray tracing, it is not.</p>\n<h2>A laptop for class as well</h2>\n<p>Most people at this price are students, and the machine must also carry notes, slides and perhaps AutoCAD. Weight matters: 2.2 to 2.3kg plus the charger. Battery matters: two to four hours of light work. Screen colour matters if you design: ask for sRGB coverage. Look for a keyboard you can type on for an hour. The Victus is quiet-looking and fits a classroom. The TUF is sturdier.</p>\n<h2>Do not skip the memory question</h2>\n<p>Some listings under Rs 1.5 lakh quote a price for 8GB. Games today use 12GB or more together with Windows, and a gaming laptop with 8GB stutters. Plan for 16GB in two sticks. A spare stick costs several thousand rupees in 2026, more than it did a year ago, so compare the total and not the sticker.</p>\n<h2>Storage</h2>\n<p>A single large game can take 100 to 150GB. With 512GB you keep three or four. A second M.2 slot lets you add a drive without reinstalling Windows. Ask whether the slot exists, and what speed it supports.</p>\n<h2>Alternative one: a desktop</h2>\n<p>For Rs 1.4 lakh you can build a desktop with a processor such as a Ryzen 5 and an RTX 5060, with a Full HD monitor close behind. It will be around twice as fast as any laptop at this price, quieter, cooler, and upgradeable for years. If you study at home, or your college has labs, it is the wiser purchase. Try the <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a> with your budget.</p>\n<h2>Alternative two: a console and a simple laptop</h2>\n<p>A PlayStation 5 Digital Edition at about Rs 95,000 plays every big release at high quality, on your TV, for years. Pair it with a Rs 55,000 to 70,000 laptop for study. You lose mouse-and-keyboard games like Valorant and Dota, and gain GTA VI, which is not on PC. See <a href=\"/news/ps5-price-in-nepal-november-2026\">PS5 prices in Nepal</a>.</p>\n<h2>Alternative three: used</h2>\n<p>RTX 3060 laptops from 2021 and 2022 appear at Rs 85,000 to 1,10,000. A 6GB RTX 3060 at 100W or more is considerably faster than a new 3050. The risks are heat damage, tired batteries and no warranty. Test for twenty minutes with a game or a tool such as FurMark, watching for shutdowns. Check that the hinge is tight, the ports are firm and the keyboard is even. Pay for a technician\'s inspection if you are unsure. It is money well spent.</p>\n<h2>Alternative four: wait</h2>\n<p>Dashain, the new year and college sessions bring offers. A Victus with RTX 4050 falls close to Rs 1.5 lakh on some days. If you can wait a month and watch the <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a>, you may get a whole class higher for the same money.</p>\n<h2>Why the watts matter more at this price</h2>\n<p>Cheaper gaming laptops often limit power to keep the chassis cool. A 3050 at 95W can be a quarter faster than one at 60W. It is not printed on the sticker. Open Nvidia Control Panel, choose System Information, and read the maximum graphics power. If the seller does not allow this, buy elsewhere.</p>\n<h2>Heat in the Terai</h2>\n<p>In forty-degree rooms, entry-level cooling struggles. Use a stand, clean the vents every three months, and set a frame cap at 60 in story games. Undervolting the processor with ThrottleStop, or choosing the balanced mode, can lower temperatures by several degrees at no cost. Avoid beds and sofas, as blocked vents age the machine faster than anything else.</p>\n<h2>For engineering and design students</h2>\n<p>The same RTX 3050 accelerates SolidWorks, Blender\'s viewport and Premiere effects. Lumion and Twinmotion will open, and large scenes will be slow. For architecture in the third year, try to reach an 8GB RTX 5050 or 5060. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">LOQ 15</a> with its full-power 5050 and accurate screen is the first laptop we would call comfortable for that work. Our <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-loq-15-2025-review-nepal\">review</a> explains why.</p>\n<h2>Checklist for the day of purchase</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Graphics chip, video memory and watts.</li>\n<li>Memory: size and number of sticks.</li>\n<li>Screen: refresh rate and colour.</li>\n<li>Free M.2 and memory slots.</li>\n<li>Warranty: who, how long, where.</li>\n<li>Charger: original and wattage.</li>\n<li>Ten-minute stress test.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Our recommendation</h2>\n<p>With exactly Rs 1.5 lakh, choose the HP Victus 15 in the best graphics configuration you can find, and spend what remains on memory. With Rs 20,000 more, the <a href=\"/reviews/acer-nitro-lite-16-rtx-4050-review-nepal\">Nitro Lite 16</a> or an RTX 4050 Victus is worth the wait. And if the laptop will never leave your room, build a desktop and do not look back.</p>','laptop','news/news-gaming-laptops.jpg',1,6007,'Laptop prices rose in 2026, so under Rs 1.5 lakh the gaming options are thin. The few worth buying, and when to stretch your budget instead.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(4,20,'5G in Nepal: Where Things Stand, and Whether Your Next Phone Needs It','ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024','<p>NTC and Ncell have both run 5G trials, and 5G gets mentioned in every phone launch here. For most people in Nepal, though, the phone in their pocket still spends its whole day on 4G. That\'s worth keeping in mind when a salesperson leads with \"5G\" as the reason to spend more.</p>\n<h2>What to know before you buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A good 4G phone today beats a weak 5G phone.</strong> Signal quality, the battery and the chip matter more for how fast your phone feels on NTC and Ncell right now.</li>\n<li><strong>5G is cheap to get now.</strong> Phones like the <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> start around Rs 34,000, so you rarely need to pay much extra for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Keeping a phone four or five years?</strong> Then choose 5G if the price is close. It costs little and keeps the option open for when commercial service reaches your area.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy official units.</strong> Network support depends on the model sold for this region, and unregistered phones can be blocked. Here is <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">how to check a phone is official</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When NTC or Ncell announce commercial 5G for your city, we\'ll cover which phones and bands work. Until then, treat 5G as a nice extra rather than the main reason to buy.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Trials and service are different things</h2>\n<p>A trial means that an operator has built a small number of 5G sites with temporary permission, to test equipment and measure performance. Service means that ordinary customers with ordinary SIMs can use 5G at an advertised price over a useful area. News reports often blur the two. When you read that 5G \"has reached\" a city, ask whether you could walk into a shop and buy a plan for it. Until the answer is yes, 5G on your phone is a feature waiting for a road.</p>\n<h2>What stands in the way</h2>\n<p><strong>Spectrum.</strong> 5G needs wide blocks of frequency. The regulator has to decide which bands to offer, at what price and with what obligations. Operators have complained for years that Nepal\'s spectrum fees are heavy compared with what customers pay.</p>\n<p><strong>Money.</strong> Average revenue from a Nepali mobile customer is low and has been falling as calls move to apps. Each 5G site costs a lot and covers a smaller area than a 4G one. Operators must believe that people will pay more, or that it lowers their cost of carrying data.</p>\n<p><strong>Geography.</strong> Hills block high frequencies. Fibre to connect the towers is missing in many places. Power is better than it was, though backup is still needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Phones.</strong> A network without 5G phones earns nothing. That part is being solved, since handsets with 5G now start at about Rs 34,000.</p>\n<h2>What 5G would change for a user</h2>\n<p>In a crowded place such as a stadium, a bus park or New Road on a festival day, 4G sites run out of capacity. Pages hang though the signal bars are full. 5G has far more capacity, so the benefit would be felt first in crowds. Downloads would be faster, and video calls steadier. Fixed wireless internet at home, without digging for fibre, could become a real option in towns.</p>\n<p>What it would not change: coverage in your village, the price of data, battery life in weak signal, or the speed of a slow website.</p>\n<h2>4G is not finished</h2>\n<p>There is a lot of room to improve the present network. VoLTE gives clearer calls and frees older bands. Carrier aggregation combines bands for higher speed. More sites and more fibre would lift speeds in places where the network is slow at eight in the evening. If your 4G is poor, a 5G phone will not help. A phone with better antennas and support for more 4G bands might.</p>\n<h2>Bands to look for</h2>\n<p>When buying, check that the phone supports the 4G bands used here, commonly band 3 (1800MHz), band 20 (800MHz), and others depending on operator and area. For 5G, mid-band n78 around 3.5GHz is the most widely used in the world, and n41, n28 and n1 appear in many countries. Official Nepali and Indian units of major brands support a broad list. Phones made for the United States or Japan can miss some. The maker\'s specification page lists bands by model number. Compare yours.</p>\n<h2>Standalone and non-standalone</h2>\n<p>Early 5G networks lean on 4G for control and are called non-standalone. Newer ones stand alone and can offer lower delay and voice over 5G. Cheap 5G phones support both in most cases. You do not need to study this before buying. It is enough to know that a phone bought today should work with whatever Nepal\'s operators launch, provided that it has the right bands and a software update enables it.</p>\n<h2>Battery cost</h2>\n<p>A 5G modem searching for a network it cannot find wastes power. If your phone lasts poorly, set the preferred network to 4G/LTE in SIM settings. Newer phones do this automatically. It is one of the quickest fixes for a 5G phone in a 4G country.</p>\n<h2>Should you pay extra for 5G today?</h2>\n<p>Here is how we would decide.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Budget under Rs 30,000:</strong> no. Take the better screen, memory or battery.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 30,000 to 45,000:</strong> only if the rest of the phone is equal.</li>\n<li><strong>Above Rs 45,000:</strong> most phones have it. A 4G-only phone should be clearly better in other ways.</li>\n<li><strong>You keep phones five years:</strong> lean towards 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>You travel to India, the Gulf or Europe often:</strong> yes, you will use it there now.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our guide <a href=\"/guides/top-reasons-buy-5g-phone-nepal\">Should your next phone be 5G?</a> looks at individual models.</p>\n<h2>Travellers and migrant workers</h2>\n<p>If you are leaving for Qatar, the Emirates, Malaysia, Korea or Japan, 5G is everyday life there, and a phone bought in Nepal with wide band support will use it. Check the list for your destination. When you return, a phone bought abroad needs MDMS registration to keep working on Nepali SIMs. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">This guide</a> shows how.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and other changes</h2>\n<p>Operators here have introduced eSIM for supported phones, which helps travellers and people with two numbers. It is separate from 5G. If you buy an imported phone without a physical SIM tray, confirm with your operator before paying that your number can move to eSIM and what it costs to change phones.</p>\n<h2>Security and health questions</h2>\n<p>5G uses radio waves in ranges similar to those of 4G and Wi-Fi, and international bodies set exposure limits that networks must follow. There is no need to fear a 5G phone. Security depends more on your own habits: updates, a SIM PIN, and caution with calls that ask for OTPs.</p>\n<h2>Signs that service is near</h2>\n<p>Watch for four events: a spectrum decision by the regulator, licences with coverage obligations, operators publishing tariffs, and phone makers releasing updates that mention Nepali carriers. When those line up, commercial service is months away. Until then, announcements of trials are interesting but do not change what you should buy.</p>\n<h2>In the meantime</h2>\n<p>Use VoLTE, keep your phone updated, set the network mode to suit your area and use Wi-Fi calling where your operator supports it. Choose a phone that makes today pleasant. If it happens to be ready for 5G, that is a small gift to your future self.</p>\n<h2>Our promise on this topic</h2>\n<p>When commercial service is announced for a city, we will update this article with the plans, the bands and a list of phones that work. Until then we will not describe a trial as a launch. If you see a shop advertising a \"5G plan\" today, ask which operator sells it, under what name and where the coverage map is. The silence that follows will be informative.</p>','telecom','news/news-5g-rollout.jpg',1,6639,'NTC and Ncell have trialled 5G, but day-to-day you are still on 4G. What that means for the phone you buy now, and when paying extra for 5G makes sense.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(5,20,'On-Device AI on Phones: What Really Runs Offline, and What Still Needs the Internet','on-device-ai-revolution-npu-gemini-nano-apple-intelligence','<p>Every phone launch now leans on \"AI\", and the chip makers quote ever-bigger NPU numbers. It\'s fair to ask what that changes for someone in Kathmandu using a phone on a Rs 799 data pack.</p>\n<h2>What usually runs on the phone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Live transcription of recordings, and some call and voice translation.</li>\n<li>Photo edits like removing objects or improving low-light shots.</li>\n<li>Smart replies, notification summaries and text proofreading on newer iPhones and Galaxies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because these run locally, they work without signal and don\'t use your data. That\'s genuinely useful on a long bus ride or a trek.</p>\n<h2>What still goes online</h2>\n<p>Chatbots like Gemini and ChatGPT, image generation and longer \"ask me anything\" requests mostly run on the company\'s servers. They need data, and they\'re only as fast as your connection. Apple sends some heavier requests to its own Private Cloud Compute servers, and Samsung lets you choose to keep Galaxy AI processing on the device in settings.</p>\n<h2>Does it change what you should buy?</h2>\n<p>A little. AI features lean on memory, so for a phone you plan to keep four or five years, <strong>12GB of RAM</strong> is a sensible target on Android. On iPhone, Apple Intelligence needs an iPhone 15 Pro or newer. See <a href=\"/news/apple-intelligence-nepal-iphone-users-guide\">which iPhones support it</a>. Beyond that, buy for the camera, battery and screen you\'ll use every day.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>What an NPU is</h2>\n<p>A phone chip contains several kinds of worker. The CPU follows instructions one after another and is good at everything. The GPU does many small calculations at once, for games and graphics. The NPU does one kind of calculation, the multiplication of huge tables of numbers, extremely efficiently. AI models consist almost entirely of such tables. So an NPU can run a model with a fraction of the energy that a CPU would need.</p>\n<p>Makers quote its power in TOPS, trillions of operations per second. The figure is close to useless for buyers. It is measured in ideal conditions and with different precision from one brand to another, and most of the features you use need only a little of it. Memory is the real limit.</p>\n<h2>Why memory decides</h2>\n<p>A language model must sit in memory to answer quickly. A small one, with three billion parameters compressed cleverly, takes two to three gigabytes. On a phone with 8GB that is a third of the total, and the rest must hold Android, your apps and the camera. That explains why features appear on the 12GB version of a phone and not on the 8GB version, and why Apple drew its line at 8GB.</p>\n<h2>A test you can do today</h2>\n<p>Turn on airplane mode. Then try each AI feature on your phone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recorder transcription on a Pixel, Galaxy or iPhone: usually works.</li>\n<li>Live translation in the Samsung phone app with a downloaded language: works.</li>\n<li>Google Translate with a downloaded language: works, including Nepali text.</li>\n<li>Object eraser: basic versions work, generative fill usually does not.</li>\n<li>Gemini, ChatGPT, Copilot: do not work.</li>\n<li>Circle to Search: does not work.</li>\n<li>Keyboard suggestions and voice typing: work in downloaded languages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The list you get is the honest measure of on-device AI. It is shorter than the advertisement and more useful than people expect.</p>\n<h2>What this means in Nepal</h2>\n<p><strong>Data cost.</strong> Local features cost nothing. A chat with a cloud assistant uses little data, but image and video generation uses a lot.</p>\n<p><strong>Coverage.</strong> In the hills, where signal comes and goes, offline translation, transcription and maps are worth more than any chatbot. Download the languages and regions before you leave.</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy.</strong> A recording of a meeting that is transcribed on your phone stays with you. One that is uploaded may be stored elsewhere. For lawyers, doctors and journalists this is a practical difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Language.</strong> Nepali support is growing in translation and voice typing, and is thin in assistants and summaries. Try before buying a phone for it.</p>\n<h2>The features worth having</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Transcription with speaker labels.</strong> Students can record a lecture in English and search it later.</li>\n<li><strong>Photo clean-up.</strong> Removing a passer-by from a picture at Patan Durbar Square takes seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Night photography.</strong> This is AI too, though nobody calls it that any more.</li>\n<li><strong>Call screening and spam detection.</strong> Where supported.</li>\n<li><strong>Voice typing.</strong> Faster than thumbs, and much improved in Nepali.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility.</strong> Live captions, image descriptions and sound alerts help people with disabilities enormously.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The features to ignore</h2>\n<p>Wallpapers made by AI, stickers, assistants that summarise short messages, and \"AI cooling\" or \"AI battery\" labels, which usually mean ordinary software. A sticker on the box does not mean that the phone has a useful NPU.</p>\n<h2>Gemini Nano, Apple\'s models, and Samsung\'s mix</h2>\n<p>Google\'s Gemini Nano is a small model built into Android on certain phones, available to apps that ask for it. Apple\'s on-device model serves Writing Tools and summaries, with larger requests going to its private cloud. Samsung mixes its own models with Google\'s, and a switch in settings lets you keep processing on the device. MediaTek and Qualcomm supply the hardware for everyone else. As a buyer you do not choose a model. You choose a phone, and it comes with one.</p>\n<h2>How long will today\'s AI phone stay current?</h2>\n<p>Nobody knows. Models are getting smaller and cleverer, which helps older phones, while features grow, which hurts them. A phone with 12GB and an update promise of six or seven years is the safest guess. A phone with 8GB will keep its present features and may miss the next ones. A phone with 6GB or less should be bought for other reasons.</p>\n<h2>AI on laptops</h2>\n<p>Windows laptops with NPUs above 40 TOPS carry the Copilot+ name and run features such as improved search, live captions with translation and image tools locally. They are pleasant. Most AI software that professionals use still runs on a graphics card or in the cloud. If you plan to run language models yourself, video memory on an Nvidia card is what matters, and a 16GB RTX 5060 Ti is the entry level we would suggest. See <a href=\"/news/nvidia-rtx-50-series-blackwell-gpus-nepal-guide\">our RTX 50 guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Security concerns</h2>\n<p>AI makes scams better. Voices can be copied from a few seconds of audio, and messages no longer have spelling mistakes. Agree on a family code word for emergencies. Do not share OTPs. Treat urgent requests for money by voice or video with suspicion, and call back on a known number. No NPU will protect you from a good story.</p>\n<h2>Battery and heat</h2>\n<p>Local AI uses little energy for short tasks and a lot for long ones. A thirty-minute transcription is fine. Generating images for half an hour will warm the phone. If battery life dropped after an update that added AI, look in the battery settings for background indexing and wait a few days before judging.</p>\n<h2>Buying advice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 40,000:</strong> ignore AI. Buy screen, memory and battery.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 40,000 to 80,000:</strong> choose 8GB at least, 12GB if the price is close.</li>\n<li><strong>Above Rs 80,000:</strong> 12GB should be standard. Compare the features on the test above.</li>\n<li><strong>iPhone:</strong> 15 Pro or newer for Apple Intelligence.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p>On-device AI is a set of small conveniences that happen to be quick and private. It is not a reason to replace a good phone, and it is a reason to prefer more memory when you do replace it. Ask for features you will use next week. If the answer is none, keep your money.</p>','ai','gadgets/2026/iphone-18-pro.webp',1,4532,'Phone makers call everything \"AI\" now. Which features run on the phone itself, which go to the cloud, and how much RAM you actually need for them.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(6,20,'RTX 50-Series in Nepal: Graphics Card and Laptop Prices, and Which One to Buy','nvidia-rtx-50-series-blackwell-gpus-nepal-guide','<p>NVIDIA\'s RTX 50-series has been on sale since early 2025, and both desktop cards and laptops with these GPUs are now easy to find in Nepal. Here is what the main desktop cards cost in our catalogue, and what each one is for.</p>\n<h2>Desktop graphics cards</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/pny-geforce-rtx-5050-8gb-gddr6-dual-fan-graphics-card\">RTX 5050 8GB</a>: Rs 54,999. An entry card for 1080p esports and lighter games.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/pny-geforce-rtx-5060-8gb-oc-dual-fan\">RTX 5060 8GB</a>: from about Rs 75,000 to Rs 87,999, depending on the model. The sensible 1080p card for most builds.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/pny-geforce-rtx-5060-ti-16gb-overclocked-dual-fan-graphics-card\">RTX 5060 Ti 16GB</a>: Rs 1,03,999. Pay the extra over the 8GB Ti. The added memory keeps new games and AI tools happy for longer.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/pny-geforce-rtx-5070-12gb-oc-triple-fan\">RTX 5070 12GB</a>: Rs 1,64,999. The card for 1440p gaming.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/pny-geforce-rtx-5070-ti-16gb-gddr7-triple-fan-graphics-card\">RTX 5070 Ti 16GB</a>: from Rs 1,75,999. High settings at 1440p with room to spare, and a strong choice for Blender and video work.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of them support DLSS 4 with multi-frame generation, which lifts frame rates a lot in games that support it. Check your power supply before you buy. A typical RTX 5060 build is happy with a good 550W unit, while a 5070 Ti wants more.</p>\n<h2>Laptops are a different story</h2>\n<p>A laptop \"RTX 5050\" or \"RTX 5060\" can run anywhere from about 45W to over 100W depending on the model, and performance follows the wattage. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> runs its RTX 5050 at 100W, while some slimmer laptops cap the same chip at 45W. Always ask for the TGP. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptop guide</a> lists it where we know it.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Choose by monitor first</h2>\n<p>The graphics card and the monitor are one purchase. A card that is too strong for the screen wastes money, and a card that is too weak wastes the screen.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1080p at 60 to 144Hz:</strong> RTX 5050 for esports, RTX 5060 for everything.</li>\n<li><strong>1440p at 144 to 180Hz:</strong> RTX 5060 Ti 16GB with DLSS, RTX 5070 without much compromise.</li>\n<li><strong>1440p ultrawide or 4K at 60Hz:</strong> RTX 5070 Ti.</li>\n<li><strong>4K at 120Hz and above:</strong> RTX 5080 or higher.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you do not plan to change a 1080p monitor, the RTX 5060 is where gaming value ends. See <a href=\"/products?category=monitor\">monitors</a> before deciding.</p>\n<h2>The video memory argument</h2>\n<p>Games in 2026 use more video memory than they did. At 1080p with high textures, 8GB is usually enough, and in some titles it is the limit. When it is exceeded, the card does not slow down politely. Frame times jump and textures blur. The 16GB version of the RTX 5060 Ti avoids this for years, and it is the card we would choose for a system that must last. The 8GB version of the same card is hard to recommend at anything but a large discount.</p>\n<p>For AI work the argument is stronger. Language and image models must fit in video memory. Sixteen gigabytes opens many doors that eight keeps closed.</p>\n<h2>What DLSS 4 does</h2>\n<p>DLSS has three parts. Super resolution renders fewer pixels and rebuilds the picture, with little loss at Quality mode. Ray reconstruction cleans up ray tracing. Frame generation inserts new frames between real ones, and the 50-series can insert up to three. The last makes the counter rise dramatically, but the game still responds at the speed of the real frames. If your base rate is 60, multi-frame generation looks and feels great. If it is 25, it looks smooth and feels like mud. So treat the advertised numbers as a bonus on top of real performance.</p>\n<h2>Power supply and case</h2>\n<p>A cheap power supply is the most common fault in Nepali builds. Buy a known brand with an 80 Plus Bronze rating or better. Use 550W for an RTX 5050 or 5060, 650W for a 5060 Ti or 5070, 750 to 850W for a 5070 Ti or 5080. Check that the connector matches, and if your card uses the 16-pin plug, push it in fully until it clicks and leave a straight run of cable before the first bend.</p>\n<p>Measure the case too. Triple-fan cards can exceed 320mm. Two intake fans and one exhaust make a real difference in summer.</p>\n<h2>Processor pairing</h2>\n<p>You do not need an expensive processor. A Ryzen 5 7500F or 7600, a Ryzen 5 9600X or a Core i5 of the 13th or 14th generation suits everything to the RTX 5070. For competitive games at very high frame rates, an X3D Ryzen is worth the money. Older systems with PCIe 3.0 lose a little speed with the 5050 and 5060, which use eight lanes.</p>\n<h2>AMD and Intel alternatives</h2>\n<p>AMD\'s RX 9060 XT 16GB and RX 9070 series compete well in ordinary rendering, and often give more video memory for the price. Nvidia keeps an edge in ray tracing, DLSS support, streaming encoders and CUDA software for creators. Intel\'s Arc B580 with 12GB is good value where it can be found. Availability in Nepal is uneven, so compare real stock and not only names. Our <a href=\"/products?category=pc-component\">component listings</a> show what is on sale.</p>\n<h2>Used cards: worth it?</h2>\n<p>A used RTX 3060 12GB, 3070 or 4060 can be good value. Ask how it was used. Mining cards are not automatically bad, but fans and thermal pads may be worn. Test with a demanding game for half an hour. Watch the temperature, listen for rattling and look for coloured dots or lines. Ask for the bill, since some brands honour a warranty by serial number.</p>\n<h2>Laptops: the watt problem again</h2>\n<p>In laptops, the name describes the chip and the wattage describes the performance. An RTX 5060 laptop at 115W can match one called 5070 at 60W. Ask for TGP, and prefer thicker machines with big chargers if speed is the goal. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> and <a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Legion 5</a> are examples that do not hide it. Laptop cards also have less video memory: 8GB in the 5050, 5060 and 5070, 12GB in the 5070 Ti.</p>\n<h2>For creators and students</h2>\n<p><strong>Video editing:</strong> the 50-series encoders handle 4:2:2 footage from modern cameras, which older cards could not. A 5060 is enough for 1080p and light 4K.</p>\n<p><strong>Blender and rendering:</strong> OptiX is fast. More video memory allows larger scenes. Choose the 5060 Ti 16GB or 5070 Ti.</p>\n<p><strong>Architecture:</strong> Lumion and Enscape like 12GB or more.</p>\n<p><strong>Machine learning:</strong> 16GB minimum for comfort.</p>\n<h2>Electricity cost</h2>\n<p>A system with an RTX 5060 draws about 250 to 300W while gaming. Three hours a day is roughly 25 units a month. With a 5070 Ti it is 40 or more. An undervolt, which takes ten minutes with MSI Afterburner, reduces power by 15 to 20 per cent with almost no loss. It also lowers noise and heat.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Graphics cards from official distributors usually carry two or three years. Parallel imports may have a shop promise. Ask for the distributor\'s sticker and keep the box, as many require it for claims. Do not remove the cooler yourself within the warranty period. Cards are fragile in transport: if you send a PC to another city, take the card out and pack it separately.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Prices in 2026 were affected by memory shortages, and cards with more memory rose most. A sudden fall is unlikely before supply improves. If your present card is dead, buy what you need. If it works, watch the price history on product pages for a few weeks, and look for offers at Dashain and the new year.</p>\n<h2>Our picks in a line each</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tight budget:</strong> RTX 5050 8GB, for esports.</li>\n<li><strong>Most builds:</strong> RTX 5060 8GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep for five years, or AI study:</strong> RTX 5060 Ti 16GB.</li>\n<li><strong>1440p gaming:</strong> RTX 5070 12GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Serious creators:</strong> RTX 5070 Ti 16GB, which is priced close enough to the 5070 to be tempting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use the <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a> to see what the whole system would cost with each.</p>','gpu','gadgets/2026/pny-geforce-rtx-5070-ti-16gb-gddr7-triple-fan-graphics-card.webp',1,6344,'RTX 5050, 5060, 5060 Ti, 5070 and 5070 Ti desktop card prices in Nepal, what each is good for, and why laptop RTX 50 chips depend so much on wattage.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(7,20,'RAM and SSD Prices Rose in 2026: What to Buy Now and What Can Wait','electronic-price-hikes-nepal-ram-ssd-smartphones-analysis','<p>Memory has been the story of 2026. Huge demand for AI data-centre hardware squeezed the supply of RAM and flash storage, and the higher costs worked their way into the price of phones, laptops and upgrade parts here. We\'ve tracked the device price rises separately in <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">why gadgets got more expensive in Nepal this year</a>. This piece is about what to do with your own money.</p>\n<h2>Buy now</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The storage size you\'ll need.</strong> On phones and MacBooks, storage can\'t be upgraded later, and the jump between sizes hasn\'t got cheaper. If you know 128GB won\'t last, buy 256GB now.</li>\n<li><strong>16GB in a new laptop.</strong> Many laptops have soldered memory. An 8GB machine bought today will feel tight long before the rest of it wears out.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>It can wait</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A bigger external drive.</strong> Portable SSDs like the <a href=\"/products/lexar-sl300-portable-ssd\">Lexar SL300</a> go on offer from time to time. If your current drive isn\'t full, wait for a better price.</li>\n<li><strong>A RAM upgrade for a desktop that works fine.</strong> Unless you\'re running out of memory today, there\'s no rush.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every product page here shows the dated prices we\'ve recorded, so you can see whether something is near its usual price before you pay.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>How a data centre raises the price of your phone</h2>\n<p>There are only three large makers of memory chips in the world: Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron. Each factory can produce several kinds of memory, and the kind used beside AI accelerators, called HBM, earns far more per wafer than the kind used in phones and laptops. When buyers with very deep pockets ask for as much HBM as can be made, factories shift towards it. What remains for ordinary DDR5 and LPDDR5X is less, and its price rises. Flash storage, used in SSDs and phones, followed a similar road.</p>\n<p>New factories take two to three years to build. That is why the pressure has lasted, and why nobody sensible promises quick relief.</p>\n<h2>How it reaches Nepal</h2>\n<p>Importers here pay in dollars, add freight, customs duty and VAT, and then a margin. A rise of twenty dollars at the factory can be Rs 4,000 to 5,000 on the shelf. Shops with old stock keep the old price for a while, and that is why two shops on one street may differ widely. Distributors adjust price lists in jumps, and the jump often arrives when a new batch lands.</p>\n<h2>What rose most</h2>\n<p><strong>Laptops with 16GB or more.</strong> Memory is a larger share of their cost. Many makers responded by offering 8GB at the old price, which is a hidden increase.</p>\n<p><strong>Desktop RAM kits.</strong> DDR5 kits that were affordable in 2024 became one of the most expensive parts of a build. DDR4 has also risen as production ends.</p>\n<p><strong>SSDs.</strong> Large drives in particular.</p>\n<p><strong>Budget phones.</strong> Memory and storage make up a big part of a cheap phone. Some models reappeared with 64GB where 128GB used to be standard.</p>\n<p><strong>Graphics cards with more video memory.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Consoles.</strong> Sony and Nintendo both raised prices.</p>\n<h2>What barely moved</h2>\n<p>Earbuds, watches, power banks, keyboards, mice, most monitors and televisions without large memory. If your list includes both a laptop and a pair of headphones, the laptop is the one to plan around.</p>\n<h2>A decision table in words</h2>\n<p><strong>Your phone is broken.</strong> Buy now, and buy the memory you will need in three years.</p>\n<p><strong>Your phone is slow but works.</strong> Replace the battery, clear storage, reset it, and wait for an offer. That costs Rs 4,000 to 8,000 and buys a year.</p>\n<p><strong>Your laptop has a free memory slot.</strong> Add a stick when you find a good price. A used module from a reliable shop is fine, since memory rarely fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Your laptop has soldered memory and 8GB.</strong> Lighten the software, use web apps, and replace the laptop only when work demands it.</p>\n<p><strong>You want to build a PC.</strong> Buy memory and SSD early if you see a fair price, as these move most. Cases, power supplies and coolers can wait.</p>\n<p><strong>You need backup storage.</strong> Hard drives remain cheap per terabyte. For files you seldom open, they are the better choice.</p>\n<h2>Eight or sixteen? Doing the sum</h2>\n<p>Say the 16GB version of a laptop costs Rs 12,000 more. Over four years that is Rs 250 a month. Against it, put ten minutes a day lost in waiting, which is a full working week each year. For anyone who earns or studies on a computer, the larger memory is the cheaper choice. The same logic applies to 256GB against 128GB on a phone, where running out of space often forces an early replacement.</p>\n<h2>Do not panic-buy</h2>\n<p>Shortages produce bad purchases. People buy a laptop they dislike because \"prices will rise tomorrow\". Prices may rise a few per cent. A poor choice costs you for years. Fix your requirements, know the fair price from the <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a>, and act when a suitable product meets it.</p>\n<h2>Beware of tricks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Virtual RAM counted as real.</strong> \"16GB\" on a cheap phone may be 8GB plus storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Single-channel memory.</strong> One 16GB stick in place of two 8GB sticks. Ask.</li>\n<li><strong>eMMC sold as SSD.</strong> It is many times slower and soldered.</li>\n<li><strong>SSDs without a known brand.</strong> Some report a false capacity. Test with a tool such as H2testw before trusting them with files.</li>\n<li><strong>Refurbished drives sold as new.</strong> CrystalDiskInfo shows power-on hours.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Second-hand as a hedge</h2>\n<p>When new prices rise, used prices follow more slowly. Business laptops with 16GB, two-year-old flagships and desktop memory are attractive in such years. The usual checks apply. For phones, look at MDMS registration first.</p>\n<h2>Stretching what you own</h2>\n<p>On a computer: uninstall what you do not use, disable startup apps, keep 20 per cent of the drive free, clean the fan. On a phone: turn off automatic downloads in WhatsApp, use Google Photos or a computer for old videos, delete unused apps, and restart weekly. On both: replace the battery before the device. These steps feel dull. They are the cheapest upgrade in 2026.</p>\n<h2>For small businesses</h2>\n<p>If you must buy ten machines, ask suppliers for a quotation valid for thirty days and lock it. Consider refurbished business desktops with 16GB, which cost less than new 8GB laptops and are easier to repair. Standardise on one model so that parts can be shared. Keep one spare SSD and one memory module in the cupboard.</p>\n<h2>When will it end?</h2>\n<p>Nobody can say with confidence. Memory has always moved in cycles: shortage, high prices, new factories, surplus, low prices. The present cycle is unusual because demand from AI has been so strong. Announcements of new capacity point to easing over the next couple of years, not weeks. If you can wait until then, wait. If you cannot, buy carefully and keep it long.</p>\n<h2>A short list to remember</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Memory you cannot add later: buy now.</li>\n<li>Memory you can add later: wait for a price.</li>\n<li>External storage: only when full.</li>\n<li>Accessories: no rush.</li>\n<li>Any device that works: maintain it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What we would do with Rs 10,000 this month</h2>\n<p>A reader asked how to spend a small amount well in an expensive year. Our order would be: a new battery for the phone that dies at four o\'clock, a 500GB SSD for the old laptop that still has a hard disk, and a good surge protector for both. None of the three is exciting. Together they give two devices several more useful years, and they delay the day when you must buy new memory at today\'s prices.</p>','price-trends','gadgets/2026/pny-xlr8-gaming-epic-x-rgb-32gb-2x16gb-ddr5-6400mhz-desktop-ram.webp',1,8125,'Memory and storage got more expensive in 2026. Practical advice for Nepali buyers: which upgrades to make now and where to save.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(8,20,'Sci-Fi Tech You Can Actually Buy in Nepal Today','from-cyberpunk-to-reality-sci-fi-cinema-tech-exists-today','<p>It\'s easy to forget how much once-futuristic tech now just sits on shop shelves in New Road. A few examples you can actually buy here, and what they\'re like to live with.</p>\n<h2>A phone that unfolds into a tablet</h2>\n<p>Folding phones used to look like film props. Apple\'s first foldable, the <a href=\"/products/iphone-duo\">iPhone Duo</a>, now sells in Nepal from Rs 4,30,900, with a 7.6-inch screen inside a phone-sized body. It\'s expensive, but the idea is no longer an experiment.</p>\n<h2>A laptop with two screens</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-duo-gx651ax-2026\">ROG Zephyrus Duo</a> puts two 16-inch OLED touchscreens in one laptop, with a keyboard that comes off. It looks like a prop from a heist film and costs about the same as a motorbike, Rs 13,38,900. Video editors and traders do get real use out of the second screen, though.</p>\n<h2>A camera drone that fits in a bag</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/dji-flip-drone-with-rc-2\">DJI Flip</a> folds down small, records 4K and follows you on its own. Check the rules before you fly it: drone flights in Nepal need permission, and many areas around airports and heritage sites are off limits.</p>\n<h2>Translation in your pocket</h2>\n<p>Recent phones from Apple, Samsung and Google can translate a conversation as it happens, some of it without internet. It isn\'t perfect with Nepali yet, but for a tourist asking directions in Thamel, it already works.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The gap between film and footpath</h2>\n<p>Science fiction writers were usually right about the object and wrong about the life around it. They imagined video phones, and we got them, along with a cousin who calls at dinner. They imagined talking computers, and did not guess that we would use them mostly to set timers. Walking through the shops of New Road or Putalisadak with that in mind is good fun, and occasionally useful. Here is a longer tour of things that were props and are now products, what they cost in spirit and money, and whether they deserve a place in your home.</p>\n<h2>Communicators: the foldable phone</h2>\n<p>The flip of the original Star Trek communicator came back in phones that fold like a compact. They make a full-size phone fit a shirt pocket, and their outer screen lets you answer without opening. Book-style foldables do the opposite and give you a small tablet. After several generations the hinges are dependable, the crease is faint, and repairs remain expensive. A broken inner screen can cost as much as a good mid-range phone. If you are careful and curious, they are the most futuristic object you can own. If you drop things, they are a warning.</p>\n<h2>Tricorders: the watch on your wrist</h2>\n<p>Doctors in films wave a device and read the body. Our watches read heart rate, estimate oxygen and take a simple ECG in some models. They are nowhere near a hospital, yet they have alerted people to problems they did not know about. The useful part is the long record. A resting heart rate that climbs over a month says something. Our <a href=\"/guides/smartwatch-buying-guide-nepal-2024\">smartwatch guide</a> explains what to trust.</p>\n<h2>The universal translator</h2>\n<p>Conversation mode in Google Translate supports Nepali. Two people can speak in turn and read or hear the result. It is slow, it stumbles on dialect and mixed speech, and it works better in a quiet room. For a trekking guide with Japanese guests, or a nurse with a patient who speaks another language, it is already a tool. Download the language in advance so that it works without signal. Earbuds with translation add comfort and not accuracy.</p>\n<h2>The computer you talk to</h2>\n<p>HAL spoke calmly and refused to open doors. Today\'s assistants speak calmly and invent facts. They can explain a contract, draft an email, plan a trip to Rara and debug a program. They need an internet connection for anything clever, and they should not be trusted with medicine, law or money without checking. Voice in Nepali is improving quickly. Used as a patient assistant and not as an oracle, they are the nearest thing to the future we were promised.</p>\n<h2>Holograms, almost: VR and AR</h2>\n<p>Headsets place you in another room convincingly enough to make you step back from a ledge that is not there. They are sold in small numbers in Nepal, usually through importers, with little local support. For games and design studios they are remarkable. Glasses that overlay information on the world exist too, with short battery life and few apps. Most people will try one, smile, and go back to a screen.</p>\n<h2>Robots in the house</h2>\n<p>The robot vacuum is the only household robot that has succeeded. In a flat with smooth floors it works well. In a traditional house with thresholds, carpets and shoes at the door, it needs help. Mapping models avoid chairs and return to charge. Dust in our cities fills the bin daily. It will not replace anyone. It will keep the floor cleaner between proper cleanings.</p>\n<h2>Flying cameras</h2>\n<p>Drones made a director of anyone with a lakh and a steady hand. A small model such as the <a href=\"/products/dji-flip-drone-with-rc-2\">DJI Flip</a> follows you, avoids some obstacles and lands by itself. In Nepal, the rules matter more than the machine. Weight classes, permissions from the civil aviation authority and local administration, and bans near airports, borders, heritage sites and national parks all apply. Ask before you fly. Confiscation is a sad ending to an expensive purchase.</p>\n<h2>Doors that know your face</h2>\n<p>Fingerprint and face locks, common in spy films, are now on office doors and flats. A good one opens by finger, code, card and key, and warns when its batteries are low. Choose a model with a mechanical key as backup, and place it where monsoon rain will not reach. Check the thickness of the door before ordering.</p>\n<h2>Screens as thin as paper</h2>\n<p>OLED televisions are a few millimetres thick at the edge. Laptops with two screens exist, and rollable phones have been shown. E-ink readers are the quiet success: no glare, weeks of battery and no notifications. For a student with many PDFs, a ten-inch e-ink tablet is perhaps the most sensible futuristic device of all, though few shops stock it.</p>\n<h2>Power from the sun</h2>\n<p>Portable power stations and folding panels are ordinary now. They run a router, lights and a laptop through a cut, or a film projector in a village school. Compare them by watt-hours, and look for lithium iron phosphate cells, which last for thousands of cycles. For trekking lodges and field clinics, they are less a gadget than infrastructure.</p>\n<h2>Electric vehicles</h2>\n<p>Blade Runner\'s flying cars are late. Silent electric cars and scooters have arrived, and Nepal has adopted them faster than most countries thanks to hydropower and taxes. They need a home charger and a calm approach to range in the hills. It is the one science-fiction prediction that you can hear, or rather cannot hear, on the Ring Road.</p>\n<h2>What has not arrived</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A battery that lasts a month.</li>\n<li>A screen that works perfectly in full sun and full dark.</li>\n<li>An assistant that understands a Nepali family conversation.</li>\n<li>Teleportation, which Kalanki would welcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to buy the future sensibly</h2>\n<p>Three questions help with any novel gadget. Who will repair it here? What happens to it when the company\'s servers close? Would I still use it in March? If you can answer them, enjoy it. If not, wait for the second generation. The future tends to get cheaper, and it never sells out for long.</p>\n<h2>Our favourites, honestly</h2>\n<p>If we had to choose three pieces of the future for an ordinary home in Nepal, we would take a good phone with offline translation, a power station for the cuts that remain, and a pair of noise-cancelling headphones. None would look impressive in a film. All of them would improve an ordinary Tuesday, which is the better test.</p>','sci-fi',NULL,1,5894,'Folding phones, dual-screen laptops, palm-sized drones and live translation: film ideas that have quietly become things you can buy in Nepal.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(20,20,'iPhone 17 Pro Max: What the Leaks Got Right, What They Missed, and the Nepal Price','iphone-17-pro-max-leaks-2nm-a19-pro-nepal-pricing-radar','<p>For most of 2025, the iPhone 17 Pro Max rumours ran ahead of reality. Now that the phone is on sale in Nepal, we can check them against what Apple actually shipped.</p>\n<h2>What the leaks got wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The chip.</strong> Several reports promised a 2nm processor. The A19 Pro is built on a 3nm process. The 2nm jump came a year later with the A20 Pro in the <a href=\"/news/iphone-18-pro-pro-max-iphone-duo-nepal-price\">iPhone 18 Pro</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The price.</strong> Early Nepali estimates of about Rs 2.2 lakh were too optimistic. The 256GB model costs <strong>Rs 2,42,499</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What they got right, and what surprised us</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A bigger change in design than usual: an aluminium unibody with vapour-chamber cooling replaced the titanium frame.</li>\n<li>Three 48MP rear cameras, including a 4x periscope telephoto.</li>\n<li>12GB of RAM, the most Apple had put in an iPhone at the time.</li>\n<li>A larger battery, rated at 5,088mAh in our listing. Battery life is the improvement owners notice most.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Nepal prices</h2>\n<p>256GB Rs 2,42,499 · 512GB Rs 2,84,599 · 1TB Rs 3,27,099 · 2TB Rs 4,11,399. See the <a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro-max\">iPhone 17 Pro Max page</a> for full specs and price history. If you want the newest Apple flagship, the <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a> is now on sale too.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications as sold</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>iPhone 17 Pro Max</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.9 inches LTPO Super Retina XDR OLED, 120Hz, 1000 nits (typ), 1600 nits (HBM), 3000 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB LPDDR5</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB / 1TB / 2TB NVMe</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 48MP, f/1.8, 24 mm, OIS, PDAF, 1/1.28” CMOS sensor + Ultrawide: 48MP, f/2.2, 13 mm, PDAF, 120° FOV + Periscope: 48MP, f/2.8, 100 mm, 4x optical zoom, O...</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 5088 mAh, non-removable; Up to 50% in around 20 minutes; 25W Wireless Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>iOS 26, iOS 26</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / CDMA / HSPA / EVDO / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 7, Tri-band, Bluetooth 6.0, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>233 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>163.4 x 78 x 8.8 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Keeping score of the rumours</h2>\n<p>Leaks come from several places, and they do not deserve equal trust. Factory drawings and cases, made months ahead, are usually right about shape and buttons. Component orders are usually right about screens and cameras. Chip details come from analysts and are right more often than not, though they confuse one year with the next, as happened here. Prices are guesses until the last weeks, and prices for Nepal are guesses about guesses.</p>\n<p>So when you read about the next iPhone, give weight in that order: design first, cameras next, chip after that, and price last. The same method works for Samsung, as in our reports on the <a href=\"/news/galaxy-s27-ultra-leaks-launch-date-nepal-price\">Galaxy S27 Ultra</a>.</p>\n<h2>What the aluminium body changed</h2>\n<p>Moving from titanium to aluminium looked like a step down and turned out to be a practical one. Aluminium carries heat better, and together with the vapour chamber it lets the A19 Pro keep its speed in long games and video recording. The earlier titanium phones became warm and slowed in the sun. For anyone who films at weddings in May, this is a real improvement.</p>\n<p>The cost is that coloured aluminium marks more easily at sharp edges. A case that covers the camera plateau prevents most of it.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: three 48MP sensors</h2>\n<p>With all three cameras at 48MP, quality no longer drops when you change lens. The 4x telephoto gives a natural perspective for portraits, and a crop reaches 8x with good detail. Video remains Apple\'s strongest card: stable, well-exposed, with professional formats for those who need them. The front camera\'s larger sensor lets you take wide group selfies while holding the phone upright.</p>\n<h2>Battery: the improvement people feel</h2>\n<p>Owners talk about battery life more than any other change. It comfortably lasts a long day of maps, camera and hotspot. Charging is quicker with a suitable USB-C PD adapter, reaching about half in some twenty minutes by Apple\'s figures. Note that models sold in some countries without a SIM tray have a slightly larger battery. In Nepal, a model with a physical SIM is usually more convenient. Check which one you are being offered.</p>\n<h2>17 Pro Max or 18 Pro Max?</h2>\n<p>The newer phone costs about Rs 37,000 more at 256GB. It brings the 2nm A20 Pro, a 5x periscope and other refinements described in <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-18-pro-max-review-nepal\">our review</a>. If you own a 15 Pro or older, either is a big step. If you want to save, the 17 Pro Max is the sensible choice as long as it is sealed and official. At less than a Rs 25,000 difference, take the newer one, since it will be updated and resold for a year longer.</p>\n<h2>And the alternatives</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>iPhone 17 Pro Max</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5088 mAh, non-removable; Up to 50% in aroun...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-n5\">Oppo Find N5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,49,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 5600 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,900</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>Up to 36 hours video playback; 50W wired (about 50...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,24,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy (3nm)</td><td>5,000mAh; 60W wired, 25W wireless, 4.5W reverse wi...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-v5\">Honor Magic V5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,66,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 5820 mAh, non-removable; 65W Honor SuperChar...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> offers a pen, a longer zoom and a privacy screen for a little less. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> takes better zoom photos for less money. Neither matches the iPhone\'s video or resale. If you live with a Mac, an iPad and AirPods, the decision is made already.</p>\n<h2>How much storage?</h2>\n<p>A minute of 4K at 60 frames per second takes about 400MB, and ProRes takes many times more. For ordinary photos and video, 256GB lasts most people two or three years. For creators, 512GB is the sensible choice. The terabyte versions are for professionals who cannot carry an external drive, and even they can record straight to a USB-C SSD.</p>\n<h2>Buying in Nepal: official and otherwise</h2>\n<p>Apple sells through authorised resellers here. An official unit has a bill, a local service route and MDMS registration. Grey units from Dubai, Hong Kong, India or the United States are sold beside them, often Rs 10,000 to 20,000 cheaper. The differences to check are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SIM type.</strong> United States units use eSIM only. Hong Kong and mainland China units take two physical SIMs.</li>\n<li><strong>Warranty.</strong> Apple\'s one-year cover may depend on country. Ask what will happen if it fails.</li>\n<li><strong>MDMS.</strong> The IMEI must be registered or the phone may lose service.</li>\n<li><strong>Activation date.</strong> Enter the serial at Apple\'s coverage page.</li>\n<li><strong>Camera shutter.</strong> Units from Japan and Korea may have a permanent shutter sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our guide on <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">checking an official phone</a> has the steps.</p>\n<h2>Used 17 Pro Max</h2>\n<p>They have begun to appear. Look for battery health above 90 per cent, original parts in Settings, General, About, no Activation Lock and a matching IMEI. Test Face ID, all cameras, the telephoto\'s focus, the Camera Control button and charging. Deduct for a replaced screen. If the price is within Rs 30,000 of a new one, buy new.</p>\n<h2>Repairs and insurance</h2>\n<p>Screen and back glass repairs on a Pro Max cost a large share of a mid-range phone. AppleCare is not sold here in the way it is abroad. Some shops and insurers offer screen plans. Read the limits: number of claims, excess, and whether genuine parts are used. A good case and glass cost Rs 3,000 and remove most of the risk.</p>\n<h2>How long will it last?</h2>\n<p>Apple supports iPhones for six to seven years or more. With 12GB of memory, the 17 Pro Max is well placed for features to come. Budget for a battery in year three. On that plan, Rs 2,42,499 over six years is about Rs 3,400 a month, before counting what it sells for at the end. It is expensive, and less so than it looks.</p>\n<h2>What the episode taught us</h2>\n<p>Rumours are entertainment. They are useful for one decision, whether to wait a few weeks, and dangerous for another, whether to sell your phone early. When a price in Nepali rupees appears before launch, remember that nobody knows it. We mark our own estimates as estimates and replace them when an official price is announced. That is the only honest way to do it.</p>','mobile','gadgets/2026/apple-iphone-17-pro-max.webp',1,9456,'Before launch, leaks promised a 2nm chip and a Rs 2.2 lakh price. The iPhone 17 Pro Max shipped with a 3nm A19 Pro and costs Rs 2,42,499 in Nepal.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(21,20,'Galaxy S25 Ultra in Late 2026: Still Worth Buying Next to the S26 Ultra?','samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra-leak-roundup-snapdragon-8-elite-nepal','<p>The Galaxy S25 Ultra is a year old, and it\'s now <strong>Rs 40,000 cheaper</strong> than its replacement. In our catalogue it lists at <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">Rs 1,84,999</a>, against <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Rs 2,24,999</a> for the S26 Ultra. That gap makes it worth a serious look.</p>\n<h2>What you keep</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The Snapdragon 8 Elite, still one of the fastest chips in any phone.</li>\n<li>A 6.9-inch AMOLED screen, the titanium frame and the S Pen.</li>\n<li>The 200MP main camera and a 5x periscope.</li>\n<li>Seven years of Android and security updates. Even bought now, it will be supported for years.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What the S26 Ultra adds</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Faster charging, 60W against 45W.</li>\n<li>The Privacy Display, which hides your screen from people beside you, useful on a crowded micro bus.</li>\n<li>A brighter f/1.4 main camera, and the newer Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our take</h2>\n<p>If the S25 Ultra is an official unit with a full warranty, it\'s the better value. You lose a little charging speed and some new extras, not anything essential. Buy the S26 Ultra if you keep phones for many years and want the newest hardware. Our <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra-review-nepal\">S26 Ultra review</a> goes deeper, and the <a href=\"/news/galaxy-s27-ultra-leaks-launch-date-nepal-price\">S27 Ultra</a> is expected early next year.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The two phones compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S25 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,84,999</td><td>6.9 inches Dynamic LTPO AMOLED 2X, 120Hz, 260...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,24,999</td><td>6.9-inch QHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 3120×1440, 1...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 60W wired, 25W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>6.78-inch BOE Q10+ AMOLED, 2800×1260, 120Hz,...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>6.9-inch LTPO OLED, 2608×1200, 1–120Hz, 12-bi...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reve...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Rs 40,000 is a lot of phone</h2>\n<p>The difference between the S25 Ultra and the S26 Ultra buys a Galaxy A17 for your mother, or a pair of Galaxy Buds and a watch, or two years of mobile data. So the question is not whether the new phone is better, because it is. The question is whether you would notice Rs 40,000 worth of difference on a Wednesday. For most people, the honest answer is no.</p>\n<h2>Performance a year later</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8 Elite for Galaxy was the fastest chip in any Android phone at its launch. A year does not make it slow. Games run at the highest settings, DeX is fluid and the camera is instant. The newer Elite Gen 5 is ahead in benchmarks and a bit cooler. Samsung also enlarged the vapour chamber in the S26 Ultra. Only long gaming sessions make these differences visible.</p>\n<h2>Screen: the privacy question</h2>\n<p>Both have huge, bright, anti-reflective 6.9-inch panels that are among the best anywhere. The S26 Ultra\'s Privacy Display narrows the viewing angle on demand, so the person beside you on a microbus sees a dark screen. If you handle salaries, patient records or a private life in public, that is an actual reason to pay more. If not, a privacy protector on the S25 Ultra costs Rs 1,500 and does a coarser version of the job.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Both use a 200MP main sensor, a 50MP ultra wide, a 10MP 3x and a 50MP 5x. The S26 Ultra\'s main lens is brighter at f/1.4 and gathers more light at night. In daylight you will not tell the pictures apart. Indoors, moving children come out sharper on the newer phone more often. Video is excellent on both.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Both have 5,000mAh, and both last a full day. The S26 Ultra charges at 60W and the S25 Ultra at 45W, saving perhaps fifteen minutes on a full charge. Neither comes close to the 6,500 to 7,500mAh that Chinese flagships offer. If battery is your complaint with Samsung, neither of these solves it.</p>\n<h2>S Pen</h2>\n<p>The pen on both phones is for writing, sketching and precise editing. The Bluetooth functions of older pens, such as remote shutter, are gone. People who take notes on their phone love it, others forget it is there. Be honest about which you are.</p>\n<h2>Updates</h2>\n<p>Samsung promised seven years for both. The S25 Ultra will be covered into early 2032 and the S26 Ultra into 2033. If you keep phones four years, this changes nothing.</p>\n<h2>Resale value</h2>\n<p>The S25 Ultra has already taken its steepest loss. From here it falls more slowly than the S26 Ultra will in its first year. Someone who sells every two years loses less money with the older phone. Samsung\'s Ultra models hold value better than other Androids in Nepal, so both are reasonable.</p>\n<h2>What else does the money buy?</h2>\n<p>At around Rs 1,85,000 to 2,00,000, two camera flagships compete. The <a href=\"/reviews/vivo-x300-pro-review-nepal\">vivo X300 Pro</a> has the best zoom camera in this price range and a 6,510mAh battery. The <a href=\"/reviews/xiaomi-17-ultra-review-nepal\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> has a one-inch class main sensor with Leica tuning. For photography and battery, both beat the S25 Ultra. For software, service, DeX, the pen and resale, Samsung wins.</p>\n<h2>Buying an S25 Ultra safely in late 2026</h2>\n<p>Stock of older flagships comes from three sources: distributors clearing inventory, shops with display units, and importers. Look closely.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dial *#06# and check the IMEI in MDMS.</li>\n<li>Enter the IMEI on Samsung\'s warranty page or the Members app and read the start date. Samsung warranties begin at activation.</li>\n<li>Check the model code. The suffix tells the region. Ask for the Nepali one.</li>\n<li>A sealed phone that has sat for a year may have a slightly aged battery. Charge it fully and look at battery status in Members.</li>\n<li>Ask for the same one-year warranty as a new phone, in writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Display units</h2>\n<p>They are sold at tempting prices. Their screens have been on at full brightness for months, and OLED panels can show a ghost of the demo. Display a plain grey image and look carefully. Expect 25 per cent off at least, and a warranty.</p>\n<h2>Used S25 Ultra</h2>\n<p>Good used units cost Rs 1,20,000 to 1,50,000 depending on condition, and at that price the phone is superb value. Check the pen, all cameras at each zoom level, the fingerprint reader, the speakers and the back glass. Look for a pink or green line on the screen. Make sure Knox is intact: in settings the warranty bit should read 0x0, as a tripped one disables Secure Folder and Samsung Wallet features.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait for the S27 Ultra?</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s next launch is expected in the first months of 2027. When it arrives, the S26 Ultra should come down and the S25 Ultra will become scarce. If your phone works, waiting until then improves your position on either. Reports so far point to a larger battery, which would be the first real fix for Samsung\'s weak point. <a href=\"/news/galaxy-s27-ultra-leaks-launch-date-nepal-price\">Here is what is known</a>.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy the S25 Ultra</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People coming from an S21, S22 or Note who want the pen.</li>\n<li>Value-minded buyers who still want the best Samsung hardware.</li>\n<li>Professionals who use DeX and long zoom.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should buy the S26 Ultra</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who keep a phone for six or seven years.</li>\n<li>Those who need the privacy screen.</li>\n<li>Night photographers and heavy gamers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should buy neither</h2>\n<p>If you do not use the pen or zoom, a <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> at Rs 1,39,999 is smaller and cheaper. If battery is everything, look at OnePlus, vivo or OPPO.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,84,999 with a full official warranty, the S25 Ultra is the Samsung flagship we would recommend to a friend this winter. Under Rs 1,75,000 it is easy. Above Rs 1,95,000, the reason to choose it disappears.</p>','mobile','gadgets/2026/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra.webp',1,8726,'The Galaxy S25 Ultra costs Rs 1,84,999 in Nepal, Rs 40,000 less than the S26 Ultra. What you give up, what you keep, and who should buy which.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(22,20,'ROG Zephyrus G16 in Nepal: The 2025 and 2026 Models Compared','asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-2025-rtx-5080-benchmarks-leaked','<p>The Zephyrus G16 is ASUS\'s answer to people who want a gaming laptop that doesn\'t look or weigh like one. It has a 16-inch OLED screen in an aluminium body under 2 kg. Two generations are listed in Nepal right now.</p>\n<h2>Zephyrus G16 (2025)</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu605cm-2025\">GU605CM</a> configuration costs <strong>Rs 5,05,900</strong>. It pairs an Intel Core Ultra 7 255H with 16GB of RAM, a 1TB SSD and a 2.5K 240Hz OLED, in a body that weighs 1.85 kg. It has a 90Wh battery, so it lasts better away from the socket than most gaming laptops.</p>\n<h2>Zephyrus G16 (2026)</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu606aw-tb103x-2026-intel-core-ultra-9-386h-rtx-5080\">GU606AW</a> moves to a Core Ultra 9 386H and an <strong>RTX 5080</strong>, for <strong>Rs 7,49,999</strong>. That\'s the one to choose for 1440p gaming at high settings or serious 3D and video work on the move.</p>\n<h2>Which one?</h2>\n<p>Both are premium laptops, and you pay a lot for thinness. If you don\'t need to carry it every day, a thicker gaming laptop gets you the same GPU for much less. The <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-scar-16-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5080\">ROG Strix Scar 16 with RTX 5080</a> costs Rs 6,01,499 at ONIN Infosys. If you want the Zephyrus look but also need two screens, there\'s the <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-duo-gx651ax-2026\">Zephyrus Duo</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The versions on sale</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu605cr-core-ultra-9-285h-32gb-1tb-rtx-5070-ti\">ASUS ROG Zephyrus G16 GU605CR (Core Ultra 9 285H, 32GB/1TB, RTX 5070 Ti)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,44,100</td><td>ROG Nebula Display 16-inch 2.5K (2560 x 1600,...</td><td>Intel® Core™ Ultra 9 Processor 285H 2.9 GHz (...</td><td>32GB LPDDR5X 7467 on board(Actual memory spee...</td><td>90WHrs, 4S1P, 4-cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu605cm-2025\">Asus ROG Zephyrus G16 GU605CM 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,05,900</td><td>16 inches, OLED, 2.5K (2560 x 1600 pixels), 2...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 255H (TSMC N3B), 16 cores...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 90 Wh; 200W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu606aw-tb103x-2026-intel-core-ultra-9-386h-rtx-5080\">Asus ROG Zephyrus G16 GU606AW-TB103X 2026 (Intel Core Ultra 9 386H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,49,999</td><td>16 inches, OLED, 2.5K (2560 x 1600 pixels), 2...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 386H (Intel 18A), 16 cores...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 90 Wh; 250W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-scar-16-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5080\">ASUS ROG Strix Scar 16 G635LW (Core Ultra 9 275HX, RTX 5080)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,01,499</td><td>ROG Nebula HDR Display 16-inch 2.5K (2560 x 1...</td><td>Intel® Core™ Ultra 9 Processor 275HX 2.7 GHz...</td><td>32GB DDR5-5600 SO-DIMM, the memory speed of t...</td><td>90WHrs, 4S1P, 4-cell Li-ion</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Look at the table carefully, because it shows something odd. The GU605CR with a Core Ultra 9 285H, an RTX 5070 Ti and 32GB is listed below the GU605CM, which has a lesser processor and half the memory. Prices of imported premium laptops depend on when a batch arrived and who brought it. It pays to ask three sellers about the same model code, and to judge by the graphics chip and memory and not by the family name.</p>\n<h2>What thinness costs</h2>\n<p>A laptop is a box for moving heat. A thick box with two large fans and a liquid metal compound can let an RTX 5080 draw 150 to 175 watts. A 1.85kg aluminium body cannot. Asus limits the graphics power in a Zephyrus to keep the keyboard touchable and the noise bearable. In games that means the same chip gives perhaps 15 to 25 per cent fewer frames than in a Strix Scar. In short bursts, such as applying an effect in Premiere, the difference disappears.</p>\n<p>So the Zephyrus is not a slower Strix. It is a different idea: most of the speed, in something you would gladly carry to a client.</p>\n<h2>The screen is the reason</h2>\n<p>A 16-inch 2.5K OLED at 240Hz covers the cinema colour range, with a response time that LCDs cannot match. For colour grading, photo work and films it is one of the best laptop screens sold here. Static toolbars over years may leave faint marks, so use dark themes, hide the taskbar and let Asus\'s pixel refresh run. Glossy glass reflects windows, and that bothers some people more than burn-in ever will.</p>\n<h2>Battery: unusual for the class</h2>\n<p>With 90Wh and efficient modes, the Zephyrus can last a working afternoon of documents and browsing. Switch the screen to 60Hz, choose the integrated graphics in Armoury Crate and dim the panel. Gaming on battery is possible and short. USB-C charging at 100W is enough for office work, so the heavy brick can stay at home.</p>\n<h2>Who buys it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Video editors and motion designers</strong> who visit clients.</li>\n<li><strong>Architects</strong> who present models on site.</li>\n<li><strong>Developers and data scientists</strong> who want CUDA in a bag.</li>\n<li><strong>Gamers</strong> who refuse to carry 2.8kg.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Competitive players at a desk. Buy a Strix, Legion or a desktop.</li>\n<li>Students on a budget. An RTX 5060 laptop at half the price does the same coursework.</li>\n<li>People who need upgrades. The memory is soldered on most configurations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sixteen gigabytes in a five-lakh laptop?</h2>\n<p>The GU605CM\'s 16GB deserves a warning. Modern games together with a browser and a voice chat use that much, and creative software uses more. As the memory cannot be added later, a premium laptop with 16GB has a short professional life. Choose 32GB. If a seller offers the 16GB model at a discount, the discount has to be large.</p>\n<h2>RTX 5060, 5070, 5070 Ti or 5080?</h2>\n<p>In a thin chassis the step from a 5070 Ti to a 5080 is smaller than the price suggests, because both run near the same power limit. The jump from 8GB of video memory on the 5070 to 12GB on the 5070 Ti, however, is real for creators. Our choice in this body is the 5070 Ti. Take the 5080 if your software needs its 16GB.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives in the same price range</h2>\n<p><strong>MacBook Pro 14 or 16.</strong> Better battery, quieter, with excellent video engines, and no gaming or CUDA. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">MacBook Pro 14 M5</a> starts far lower.</p>\n<p><strong>Razer Blade.</strong> Similar idea, rarer here, and harder to service.</p>\n<p><strong>Lenovo Legion Pro and Asus Strix.</strong> More speed, more weight. The <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-scar-16-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5080\">Strix Scar 16 with an RTX 5080</a> costs about Rs 1.5 lakh less than the Zephyrus with the same chip.</p>\n<p><strong>A desktop plus a thin laptop.</strong> For five lakhs you can have a desktop with an RTX 5080 and a Yoga or MacBook Air. If you do not render while travelling, this is the better pair.</p>\n<h2>What to test before paying five to seven lakhs</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Run a game or Cinebench for twenty minutes. Note the sustained speed, noise and keyboard temperature.</li>\n<li>Read the graphics power in Nvidia Control Panel.</li>\n<li>Check the screen with grey, red, green and blue images.</li>\n<li>Generate a battery report. Full charge should be near 90Wh.</li>\n<li>Look at the serial number on Asus\'s warranty page.</li>\n<li>Try the speakers, webcam and Windows Hello.</li>\n<li>Check the charger\'s wattage and plug.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Warranty is not a footnote here</h2>\n<p>A motherboard in a thin laptop contains the processor, graphics chip and memory. If it fails outside warranty, the repair can cost half the price. Ask whether the laptop has Asus\'s own warranty valid in Nepal, for how long, and who handles it. Model codes with letters for other regions, such as those ending in a North American suffix, are imports. A shop warranty for a Rs 7,49,999 machine should be in writing, with a stamp, and the shop should have existed for some years. Consider insurance.</p>\n<h2>About leaked benchmarks</h2>\n<p>When this story began, numbers were circulating from engineering samples. They were roughly right about the chip and wrong about this laptop, because early samples ran at higher power than the retail model. It is a good lesson. A benchmark belongs to a chip at a given wattage in a given chassis. Read results for the exact model, from someone who states the mode and the room temperature, and ignore the rest.</p>\n<h2>Living with one in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Dust is the enemy of thin cooling. Have it opened and cleaned once a year by someone who has done it before. Use a surge protector, as the power supply is expensive. Keep it out of the sun in a car. In the hot months, raise the rear and use the balanced profile. Back up daily, since a machine this attractive is attractive to others too.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Zephyrus G16 remains the best-looking way to carry real graphics power. Buy it if you are paid for work that happens away from a desk, choose 32GB and a 5070 Ti, and settle the warranty first. For everyone else, thicker is cheaper, faster and easier to repair.</p>','laptop','gadgets/2026/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu605cr-core-ultra-9-285h-32gb-1tb-rtx-5070-ti.webp',1,7637,'The Zephyrus G16 is ASUS\'s thin gaming laptop. The 2025 model costs Rs 5,05,900 in Nepal and the 2026 RTX 5080 version Rs 7,49,999. Which makes sense?',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(23,20,'Snapdragon X Laptops in Nepal: Battery Life, App Compatibility and Prices','qualcomm-oryon-snapdragon-x-elite-laptops-nepal-battery-benchmarks','<p>Windows laptops with Qualcomm\'s Snapdragon X chips have one big selling point: they last a very long time on a charge, close to what MacBook owners are used to. They also have one big question mark, and that\'s app compatibility. Both are worth understanding before you buy.</p>\n<h2>What\'s on sale in Nepal</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-16-ai-2025-snapdragon-x-x1-26100-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga\">Acer Aspire 16 AI</a> (Snapdragon X): Rs 1,19,999. The cheapest way in.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-5441-2024-snapdragon-x-plus-x1p-42-100\">Dell Inspiron 14 5441</a> (X Plus): Rs 1,31,999.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-2024-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">ASUS Vivobook S15 OLED</a> (X Elite, 1TB): Rs 1,54,999 at ONIN Infosys, with a 3K 120Hz OLED screen.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9345-snapdragon-x-plus-16gb-256gb\">Dell XPS 13 9345</a> (X Plus): from Rs 1,69,999.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-ultra-g1q-b16xzua-2025-snapdragon-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">HP EliteBook Ultra G1q</a> (X Elite): about Rs 1,88,000, a business laptop.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What works and what doesn\'t</h2>\n<p>Office, Chrome, Edge, Zoom, Teams, Spotify, Photoshop and Lightroom all run natively and fast. Most other programs run through Windows\' built-in translation layer, called Prism, and you usually won\'t notice. The trouble spots are online games with anti-cheat software, some VPN clients, older printer and scanner drivers, and specialist engineering or accounting software.</p>\n<p>Our advice: if your work lives in a browser and Office, these laptops are excellent. If you depend on one particular program, check that it supports Windows on Arm before you pay. If you game, buy an Intel or AMD laptop instead.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Models and prices today</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-16-ai-2025-snapdragon-x-x1-26100-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga\">Acer Aspire 16 AI A16-11M-X0LW 2025 (Snapdragon X X1-26100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,19,999</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>Snapdragon X X1-26-100</td><td>–</td><td>Li-Ion 65 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-5441-2024-snapdragon-x-plus-x1p-42-100\">Dell Inspiron 14 5441 2024 (Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>14 inches, WVA LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100 (3nm),...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 54 Wh; 65W adapter, Supports ExpressCh...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-oled-x-plus-16gb-1tb-15-6-3k-oled\">Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA-MA004W (Snapdragon X Plus)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,42,800</td><td>15.6 inches, OLED, 3K (2880 x 1620 pixels), 1...</td><td>Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100, 8 cores / 8 thr...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 70 Wh; 70W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-2024-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">ASUS Vivobook S15 OLED S5507QA (Snapdragon X Elite, 16GB/1TB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,54,999</td><td>15.6-inch, 3K (2880 x 1620) OLED 16:9 aspect...</td><td>Snapdragon® X Elite X1E 78 100 Processor (12...</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X on board</td><td>3-cell Li-ion ,70WHrs, 3S1P</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9345-snapdragon-x-plus-16gb-256gb\">Dell XPS 13 9345 (Snapdragon X Plus, 16GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,69,999</td><td>13.4″ 2K (~2880×1800) IPS, up to 120 Hz, 500...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus (X1P‑42‑100, 8-cor...</td><td>16 GB LPDDR5X (8448 MT/s, onboard)</td><td>55 Wh integrated, all-day endurance</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-ultra-g1q-b16xzua-2025-snapdragon-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">HP EliteBook Ultra G1Q B16XZUA 2025 (Snapdragon X Elite X1E-78-100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,88,100</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, 2.2K (2240 x 1400 pixels)...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Elite X1E-78-100 (4nm),...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 59 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>X, X Plus and X Elite</h2>\n<p>Qualcomm sells one design in three sizes. <strong>Snapdragon X</strong> has eight cores at lower speeds and a small graphics unit, for laptops around Rs 1.2 lakh. <strong>X Plus</strong> comes with eight or ten cores. <strong>X Elite</strong> has twelve. All share the same neural processor, so AI features are identical. For office work you will not feel the difference between Plus and Elite. For compiling and photo exports you will. Nobody should choose an Elite for gaming.</p>\n<h2>How the battery life happens</h2>\n<p>Three things work together. The cores finish short tasks quickly and go back to sleep. Standby uses very little, so a laptop left closed overnight loses one or two per cent. And performance on battery is the same as on the charger, where many Intel and AMD laptops slow down when unplugged to save power. In everyday work with moderate brightness, twelve hours and more is realistic on the larger batteries. Video playback lasts longer, video calls far less.</p>\n<p>For a country where some neighbourhoods still lose power, and where a student may sit in a classroom with two sockets for sixty people, this is worth more than any benchmark.</p>\n<h2>Prism, the translator</h2>\n<p>Programs written for Intel and AMD processors are translated as they run. It works well for most. The first launch is slower. Speed drops by perhaps ten to thirty per cent. Programs that use special instructions used to fail, and later Windows updates have added support for many of them. Programs that install drivers remain the problem: a driver must be written for Arm. That is why VPN clients, anti-cheat systems, hardware keys, antivirus software and older printers are the usual failures.</p>\n<h2>A Nepali compatibility list to test</h2>\n<p>We suggest that you try these before paying, as they matter here and international lists ignore them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your bank\'s website and any token or signing tool.</li>\n<li>The digital signature certificate driver used for tax and company filings.</li>\n<li>Tally, Swastik, Busy or whichever accounting program your office runs.</li>\n<li>Preeti and other Nepali font typing tools, and Unicode keyboards.</li>\n<li>The exact printer and scanner in your office.</li>\n<li>Your college\'s exam browser, if it uses one.</li>\n<li>Zoom, Teams and Google Meet, which are fine.</li>\n<li>AutoCAD, ETABS, SAP2000, MATLAB: check the vendor\'s statement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ask the shop for a demonstration, or a three-day return in writing.</p>\n<h2>Development work</h2>\n<p>VS Code, Git, Node, Python, .NET, Java and Go have native versions. WSL runs Arm Linux, and Docker runs Arm images, which is fine until a project needs an image built only for x86. Android Studio and its emulator have been a weak point. iOS development is of course impossible. Web developers are happy here. Mobile developers should choose Intel, AMD or a Mac.</p>\n<h2>Games</h2>\n<p>Older and lighter games run through translation at low settings. Anything with kernel-level anti-cheat, such as Valorant, does not. If games are part of your week, this is the wrong laptop.</p>\n<h2>Compared with Intel\'s Lunar Lake</h2>\n<p>Intel answered Qualcomm with the Core Ultra 200V series, which comes close in battery life and runs everything. That is the greatest threat to Snapdragon laptops. In Nepal, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Acer Aspire 14 AI</a> with a Core Ultra 7 258V, 32GB and 1TB costs about Rs 1,39,999, and the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> about Rs 1,64,999. If a Snapdragon model is not clearly cheaper or does not have a clearly better screen, take the Intel.</p>\n<h2>Compared with a MacBook</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s chips are quicker in single tasks, and macOS has no translation problems left to speak of. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> is in the same price range with half the memory. The Air is much dearer. If you can live in macOS, a used M2 Air with 16GB is a fierce competitor. If you need Windows and long battery life, we are back to Qualcomm and Intel.</p>\n<h2>Which model makes sense?</h2>\n<p><strong>Acer Aspire 16 AI</strong> is the cheapest, with a large screen for study. <strong>Vivobook S15 X Plus</strong> is the one we like most, thanks to its 3K OLED and a terabyte of storage. <a href=\"/reviews/asus-vivobook-s15-snapdragon-review-nepal\">Our review</a> covers it. <strong>Dell Inspiron 14</strong> is sensible. <strong>XPS 13</strong> is beautiful, with only 256GB in its base version. <strong>EliteBook Ultra</strong> is for companies that have tested their software.</p>\n<h2>Copilot+ features</h2>\n<p>These laptops qualify for Windows features that run on the neural processor: live captions with translation, Studio effects for the camera, improved search, and Recall, which is optional and takes encrypted snapshots of your screen. Read what Recall does before turning it on. On a shared family computer, leave it off.</p>\n<h2>Repair and resale</h2>\n<p>Memory is soldered on all of them. SSDs can usually be replaced. Few technicians in Nepal have experience with Arm motherboards, so official warranty matters. Resale is uncertain, since buyers are cautious. Plan to keep the laptop for its whole life.</p>\n<h2>Who will be happy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Writers, teachers, managers, marketers, translators and researchers.</li>\n<li>Students of management, law, education and humanities.</li>\n<li>People who travel by bus and work on the way.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who will return it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Engineers and architects.</li>\n<li>Accountants with local software, unless tested.</li>\n<li>Gamers and mobile app developers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Setup tips</h2>\n<p>Choose Arm64 downloads when a site offers them. Use Edge, Chrome or Firefox in their native versions. Run Windows Update until nothing is left, as translation improves with each release. Prefer printers with driverless IPP support. Set the battery limit to 80 per cent if you work at a desk.</p>\n<h2>Our view</h2>\n<p>Snapdragon laptops changed what Windows users expect from a battery, and forced Intel to respond. As products they are very good for a limited group. Write your list of programs. If it fits on the safe side, buy for the screen and the price. If one item is doubtful, choose Lunar Lake and sleep well.</p>','technology','gadgets/2026/asus-vivobook-s15-2024-x-elite-x1e-78-100.webp',1,6214,'Windows laptops with Qualcomm Snapdragon X chips last a long time on battery. Prices in Nepal, which apps work, and who should avoid them.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(24,20,'Do You Need Wi-Fi 7 in Nepal Yet?','wifi-7-adoption-nepal-routers-speed-isp-support','<p>Wi-Fi 7 is showing up on spec sheets everywhere. Well over ninety laptops in our catalogue list it, including the <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air M5</a> and most new gaming laptops. That\'s no reason to rush out and replace your router.</p>\n<h2>Why your new laptop may not feel faster</h2>\n<p>Wi-Fi 7 only helps when <strong>both ends</strong> support it: the laptop or phone, and the router. Most homes in Nepal use the router their fibre ISP supplied, and those are usually Wi-Fi 5 or Wi-Fi 6. A Wi-Fi 7 laptop connects to them happily, at Wi-Fi 6 speeds.</p>\n<h2>When upgrading the router makes sense</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You pay for a fast plan, say 300Mbps or more, and don\'t get close to it over Wi-Fi.</li>\n<li>Many devices share the network: a family on video calls, a smart TV and a gaming PC at the same time.</li>\n<li>You move large files around the house, for example to a home NAS.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For most people a good Wi-Fi 6 router placed in the middle of the house, or a mesh system in a multi-storey home, fixes more problems than Wi-Fi 7 does. Slow Wi-Fi in a concrete building is usually about coverage, not the Wi-Fi standard.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>What Wi-Fi 7 adds</h2>\n<p>Three changes matter. Channels can be twice as wide, up to 320MHz, in the 6GHz band. Devices can use two bands at once, called multi-link operation, which reduces delay and dropouts. And data is packed more densely when the signal is strong. In a laboratory that produces astonishing numbers. In a home, the second change is the one people would feel: steadier video calls and games.</p>\n<p>There is a catch. The widest channels need the 6GHz band, and each country decides whether and how it may be used. Check what the regulator in Nepal permits before paying for a router whose main advantage depends on it. Without 6GHz, Wi-Fi 7 works on 2.4 and 5GHz and is only modestly better than Wi-Fi 6.</p>\n<h2>Your plan is the ceiling</h2>\n<p>If your fibre package is 100Mbps, every generation of Wi-Fi from the last ten years can carry it across a room. A faster standard will not make a download quicker. It may make it reach the bedroom better, and that depends on walls. Most Nepali households have packages between 80 and 300Mbps. Wi-Fi 6 carries those comfortably.</p>\n<h2>Concrete and brick: the real problem</h2>\n<p>Houses here are built of brick and reinforced concrete. Each wall takes away a large part of a 5GHz signal, and a floor slab takes more. 6GHz is worse still. So a router in the corner of the ground floor cannot serve a room on the third, whatever the number on its box. The cure is placement and more access points.</p>\n<h3>Placement rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>In the middle of the home, high on a shelf, in the open.</li>\n<li>Away from the television, microwave, fridge and aquarium.</li>\n<li>Not inside the metal box with the fibre terminal.</li>\n<li>Antennas at different angles if you have floors above and below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Try this first. Moving a router two metres can double the speed in the next room, free of charge.</p>\n<h2>Mesh, extender or cable?</h2>\n<p><strong>A cable</strong> from the router to a second access point is best, and costs little during construction or painting. If you are building a house, run Ethernet to every floor. You will thank yourself for thirty years.</p>\n<p><strong>Mesh</strong> systems with two or three units are the easiest cure for a finished house. Choose one with a dedicated wireless link, or connect units by cable if you can. A Wi-Fi 6 mesh is enough.</p>\n<p><strong>Extenders</strong> are cheap, and halve the speed. Use them for a doorbell or a distant light.</p>\n<p><strong>Powerline adapters</strong> are a gamble with older wiring.</p>\n<h2>The router from your ISP</h2>\n<p>It is usually basic, locked, and shared with the fibre terminal. You can often keep it as the terminal and connect your own router behind it. Ask the ISP to put theirs in bridge mode, or turn off its Wi-Fi and use yours as an access point. If two routers both hand out addresses, some things such as games and cameras will misbehave. Support staff know the setting as \"bridge\" or \"double NAT\".</p>\n<h2>How to measure properly</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Connect a laptop by cable to the router and run a speed test. This is your plan\'s real speed.</li>\n<li>Repeat on 5GHz Wi-Fi beside the router. It should be close.</li>\n<li>Repeat in each room. Write it down.</li>\n<li>Repeat at nine in the evening. If the cable result is also slow, the problem is the ISP and no router will help.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What to buy by situation</h2>\n<p><strong>Flat, 100Mbps, few devices.</strong> Keep the ISP router and move it.</p>\n<p><strong>Three-storey house, 150 to 300Mbps.</strong> Wi-Fi 6 mesh with two or three units.</p>\n<p><strong>Gaming.</strong> A cable to the PC or console. Nothing wireless is as steady.</p>\n<p><strong>Home office with video calls.</strong> Wi-Fi 6 router near the desk and a small UPS for router and terminal.</p>\n<p><strong>500Mbps or more, new devices, NAS.</strong> Here Wi-Fi 7 starts to make sense.</p>\n<p><strong>Small office.</strong> Proper access points on the ceiling with wired connections, from a business range.</p>\n<h2>A small UPS is the best network upgrade</h2>\n<p>Routers and fibre terminals use 10 to 20 watts together. A mini UPS for Rs 2,500 to 4,500 keeps them on for hours in a power cut. Fibre networks usually stay up. This one purchase improves life more than any new Wi-Fi standard.</p>\n<h2>Which devices have Wi-Fi 7?</h2>\n<p>Recent flagship phones, many laptops from 2025 onwards and the newest Macs. They work with older routers, so there is no hurry. When you replace a router in two or three years because it has failed, Wi-Fi 7 models will be the ordinary choice and cost less.</p>\n<h2>Wi-Fi 6E: a stop on the way</h2>\n<p>Wi-Fi 6E is Wi-Fi 6 with the 6GHz band. If you see it at a good price, the same question applies: is 6GHz permitted and useful in your home? For most homes, plain Wi-Fi 6 is the same thing for less.</p>\n<h2>Security settings worth five minutes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Change the router\'s admin password, not only the Wi-Fi password.</li>\n<li>Use WPA2/WPA3 and a long passphrase.</li>\n<li>Create a guest network for visitors and smart devices.</li>\n<li>Turn off WPS and remote management.</li>\n<li>Update the firmware twice a year.</li>\n<li>Check the list of connected devices and remove strangers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Common myths</h2>\n<p><strong>\"More antennas mean more speed.\"</strong> Not by themselves. <strong>\"5GHz is always better.\"</strong> Near the router, yes. Two rooms away, 2.4GHz may win. <strong>\"Wi-Fi 7 is dangerous.\"</strong> It uses low power, like every version before it. <strong>\"A booster app will help.\"</strong> No app can.</p>\n<h2>When we would upgrade to Wi-Fi 7</h2>\n<p>If we were buying a new router anyway, had a plan above 500Mbps, owned at least two Wi-Fi 7 devices, and could use 6GHz in the room where we work. If three of those are true, go ahead. If one is true, buy a good Wi-Fi 6 mesh and spend the difference on a UPS and an Ethernet cable.</p>\n<h2>Our advice</h2>\n<p>Move the router, measure the rooms, add a cable or a mesh unit, and protect the power. Do these in order and stop when the problem is gone. In most Nepali homes it will be gone before you reach the shop that sells Wi-Fi 7.</p>','technology',NULL,1,5485,'Lots of new laptops and phones have Wi-Fi 7, but you only benefit with a Wi-Fi 7 router. When it is worth upgrading your home network in Nepal.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(36,20,'GTA VI Is Out: A Record Launch, and How to Buy It in Nepal','gta-6-launch-record-sales-how-to-buy-nepal','<p>Grand Theft Auto VI finally launched on <strong>19 November 2026</strong> for PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series X|S, after being delayed twice from its original 2025 window. There is still no PC version.</p>\n\n<h2>A record-breaking first day</h2>\n<p>Variety reported that Take-Two counted <strong>$3.79 billion</strong> in sales within the first 24 hours. Sensor Tower\'s independent estimate was lower, at about 24.6 million copies and $2.36 billion on launch day including pre-orders, with roughly 18.4 million of those copies on PS5. The two figures measure different things, but by either count it\'s one of the biggest entertainment launches ever.</p>\n\n<h2>How to buy GTA VI in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Rockstar doesn\'t sell the game at a Nepal-specific price, so players here have two options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The PlayStation Store</strong>, paying $79.99 for the Standard Edition or $99.99 for the Ultimate Edition with a dollar card.</li>\n<li><strong>A local game shop.</strong> Expect about Rs 13,499 for the Standard Edition and Rs 16,999 for the Ultimate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Be careful with very cheap \"secondary account\" offers. You are sharing someone else\'s account, and access can disappear if they change the password.</p>\n\n<h2>Which console?</h2>\n<p>GTA VI runs on every PS5 model. See our <a href=\"/reviews/ps5-digital-slim-pro-review-nepal\">PS5 buying verdict</a>. The Digital Edition at Rs 95,000 is the cheapest way in, and the PS5 Pro gives the best image on a 4K TV. Full details are on our <a href=\"/products/gta-6\">GTA VI page</a>.</p>\n<h3>Sources</h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://variety.com/2026/gaming/news/gta-6-sales-billions-first-day-1236954198/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Variety – GTA 6 first-day sales</a></li><li><a href=\"https://hothardware.com/news/gta-6-launch-haul-dwarfs-gta-5s-day-one-record\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">HotHardware – Sensor Tower launch estimate</a></li></ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Choosing your console</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Storage</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-playstation-5\">Sony PlayStation 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;95,000</td><td>AMD Zen 2, 8 cores / 16 threads, up to 3.5GHz</td><td>16GB GDDR6, 256-bit, 448GB/s</td><td>825GB, 1TB or 2TB custom SSD + M.2 NVMe expan...</td><td>Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Ethernet, HDMI 2.1, USB-C</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table shows our current listing for the PlayStation 5 family, and the product page has every version. Rockstar\'s game runs on all of them, with differences in picture quality and frame rate that the console decides. The cheapest way to play is the Digital Edition. The catch is that you can only buy from the store, at store prices, for the life of the console.</p>\n<h2>Digital or disc? Do the sums</h2>\n<p>The disc model costs much more at present. In return you can buy used games, lend them and sell them. In Kathmandu, a new disc at Rs 9,000 to 13,000 can often be resold at 60 to 70 per cent after a month. If you buy six big games a year and sell them, the drive pays for itself within a couple of years. If you play one or two games for hundreds of hours, as many will with GTA VI, the Digital Edition is the thrifty choice. A detachable drive can be added to the Slim Digital and the Pro later, where it is available.</p>\n<h2>Which edition of the game?</h2>\n<p>The Standard Edition contains the full story. The Ultimate Edition adds in-game items, and its value depends on how you feel about cosmetic extras. Nothing in it is needed to finish the game. With special editions the sensible approach is to buy Standard, play ten hours, and upgrade if you still want to. Most stores sell the upgrade separately.</p>\n<h2>Paying from Nepal</h2>\n<p>There are four routes, from safest to riskiest.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Your own dollar card on the PlayStation or Xbox store.</strong> Nepali banks issue prepaid dollar cards within the yearly limit set by Nepal Rastra Bank. The game belongs to your account.</li>\n<li><strong>Gift cards.</strong> Buy a code for the region of your account from a reputable seller. Region must match. A United States code does not work on an Indian account.</li>\n<li><strong>A physical disc from a shop.</strong> Check the region printed on it for add-on content.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared accounts.</strong> Cheap for a reason. The owner can change the password, the store can close the account, and you lose everything including your progress. We advise against them.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Choose the account region once</h2>\n<p>Sony has no Nepal store. People choose the United States, India, the United Arab Emirates or others. Prices, sales and payment methods differ, and you cannot move your library later. If you have a card that works with one store and you are comfortable with its prices, choose that and stay. Changing regions is the commonest regret of new owners.</p>\n<h2>Storage and download</h2>\n<p>Modern open-world games are over 100GB, and updates keep arriving. The Digital Edition\'s 825GB holds about six large games. A second NVMe drive fits inside every PS5: choose a PCIe 4.0 model with a heatsink, rated above 5,500MB per second. On a 100Mbps connection, 120GB takes around three hours in good conditions. Set it to download overnight in rest mode, and put the console and router on a UPS. A power cut in the middle of a system update is a bad way to begin.</p>\n<h2>Your television matters as much</h2>\n<p>To see what the console can do, you want 4K, HDR with real brightness, a 120Hz panel with HDMI 2.1 and a game mode. A Full HD television works perfectly well and looks good from three metres. Turn on game mode whichever you have. The delay of ordinary picture modes makes driving feel drunk, and in this series that is meant to be optional. See <a href=\"/products?category=tv\">televisions</a> and <a href=\"/products?category=monitor\">monitors</a>.</p>\n<h2>Is the PS5 Pro worth it?</h2>\n<p>The Pro has stronger graphics, two terabytes and an upscaler that sharpens the picture on large 4K sets. If you own one, or plan to buy, a big 4K television, and if the gap to the Slim Disc is as small as it is now in Nepal, the Pro is the better purchase. On a Full HD television it is money thrown away. <a href=\"/reviews/ps5-digital-slim-pro-review-nepal\">Our buying verdict</a> compares all three.</p>\n<h2>Xbox in Nepal</h2>\n<p>The game is also on Series X and S. Both are less common here, with fewer used discs and fewer friends to play with. The Series S is cheap, all-digital and limited in storage. Buy one if you already have an Xbox library or Game Pass.</p>\n<h2>What about PC?</h2>\n<p>No PC version has been announced for launch. Rockstar\'s earlier titles reached PC a year or more after consoles. Anything offered today as GTA VI for PC is fake and probably malware. The same goes for APK files that claim to be a mobile version. Please tell your younger cousins.</p>\n<h2>Online play</h2>\n<p>Online modes need a PlayStation Plus subscription for most games, and a stable connection more than a fast one. Use an Ethernet cable. If voice chat fails or friends cannot join, the cause is usually double NAT from an ISP router. Ask for bridge mode.</p>\n<h2>Parents, please read this part</h2>\n<p>Grand Theft Auto games are rated for adults, with violence, sexual content and drug use. They are superb and not for children. PlayStation\'s family settings allow you to restrict games by age, set spending limits and control playing time. Set them up in ten minutes. If your child says that everyone at school plays it, that may be true, and it does not change the rating.</p>\n<h2>Scams to expect</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Activation keys\" for consoles at a quarter of the price.</li>\n<li>Social media pages taking advance payment for pre-orders after launch.</li>\n<li>Links offering free in-game money in exchange for your login.</li>\n<li>Used consoles that have been banned from the network. Sign in and test before paying.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Turn on two-step verification for your account today.</p>\n<h2>Should you buy a console for one game?</h2>\n<p>If you play for a hundred hours, a Rs 95,000 console plus a Rs 13,499 game costs about Rs 1,085 an hour, falling with each further game. If a friend owns one, try it first. The console also plays hundreds of older titles, many cheaper than a cinema ticket in sales. And its price has been rising and not falling, so waiting for a discount may not be rewarded this year.</p>\n<h2>Final word</h2>\n<p>Buy the console that suits your television, buy the Standard Edition from your own account, protect the power, and lock the profile if there are children at home. Then enjoy a game that took more than a decade to make.</p>','gaming','news/news-gta-6.jpg',1,3,'GTA VI launched on 19 November 2026 with record first-day sales. How Nepali players can buy it, what it costs and which console to get.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(37,20,'PS5 Prices in Nepal After Sony\'s 2026 Increases','ps5-price-in-nepal-november-2026','<p>Demand for PlayStation 5 consoles has jumped in Kathmandu with GTA VI\'s release, and prices are higher than they were a year ago. Sony raised PS5 prices in several regions in spring 2026, citing economic pressures and production costs. These are the prices as of 20 November 2026:</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Storage</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>PS5 Digital Edition</td><td>825GB</td><td>Rs 95,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PS5 Slim Disc Edition</td><td>1TB</td><td>Rs 1,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PS5 Pro</td><td>2TB</td><td>Rs 1,45,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DualSense controller</td><td>–</td><td>Rs 9,500</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The odd one out is the Slim Disc, which is listed only about Rs 5,000 below the far more powerful Pro. Shop prices move with stock, so it\'s worth checking a couple of sellers. If the gap is that small where you buy, the Pro is the better deal, unless you need a disc drive. Our <a href=\"/reviews/ps5-digital-slim-pro-review-nepal\">PS5 buying verdict</a> goes through the choice in detail.</p>\n<p>You can switch between models and see each price on the <a href=\"/products/sony-playstation-5\">PS5 product page</a>.</p>\n<h3>Sources</h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://blog.playstation.com/2026/03/27/20260327-ps/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">PlayStation Blog – 2026 price changes (Southeast Asia)</a></li></ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Why is the Digital Edition so much cheaper?</h2>\n<p>Two reasons. It has no disc drive, and its storage is smaller. It is also the version that importers have brought in larger numbers, so competition keeps its price honest. The Slim Disc is in short supply, and that has pushed it to within a few thousand rupees of the Pro. It is a strange situation that will not last, and it means the middle model is the worst buy of the three at the moment.</p>\n<h2>What each model includes</h2>\n<p><strong>Digital Edition (825GB).</strong> A console, one DualSense controller, cables and horizontal feet. All games from the store.</p>\n<p><strong>Slim Disc (1TB).</strong> The same with a 4K Blu-ray drive. Plays PS4 and PS5 discs and films.</p>\n<p><strong>Pro (2TB).</strong> Faster graphics, machine-learning upscaling, Wi-Fi 7 and more storage. No disc drive in the box. The same detachable drive can be added.</p>\n<p>None includes a vertical stand. The Slim and Pro can lie flat with the feet provided.</p>\n<h2>Real prices to expect in a shop</h2>\n<p>Listed prices are a guide. In practice you will meet three numbers: the cash price with no gifts, the bundle price with a second controller or a game, and the EMI price. Ask for all three. Work out the bundle by subtracting what the extras cost on their own. A DualSense is about Rs 9,500, and a popular used disc Rs 4,000 to 6,000. Some bundles are fair. Others hide a Rs 5,000 increase.</p>\n<h2>Official, imported and refurbished</h2>\n<p>Sony does not run a PlayStation operation in Nepal in the way it does for televisions, so many consoles are brought by importers. Warranty comes from the shop or an importer. Ask three things: how long, who repairs it, and whether the console is new, refurbished or a display unit. The model number on the box shows the region. Games are not region locked, though discs and downloadable content should match the account\'s region. Plugs and voltage are not an issue, as the power supply handles 100 to 240 volts.</p>\n<h2>Checking a console before paying</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Seal intact, with serial numbers matching on the box and console.</li>\n<li>Power on, set up a user and connect to Wi-Fi.</li>\n<li>Sign in to PlayStation Network. A banned console cannot.</li>\n<li>Play a disc, if it has a drive, and listen for grinding.</li>\n<li>Check that the controller sticks do not drift. Open a game menu and leave it alone.</li>\n<li>Test USB ports and HDMI. A loose HDMI port is the most common repair.</li>\n<li>Ask for the bill with the serial number.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Used PS5: a reasonable idea?</h2>\n<p>Original models from 2020 to 2022 are sold at Rs 55,000 to 75,000. They are bigger and as capable as the Slim. Check the fan noise after half an hour of a heavy game, and look at the vents for dust. Vertical use for years with the liquid metal inside has caused trouble in some opened units, so avoid consoles that have been repaired. Make sure that the previous owner has deactivated the console from the account and reset it.</p>\n<h2>The cost of owning one</h2>\n<p>The console is the beginning. Over two years, plan for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Four to six games a year, new, used or on sale: Rs 15,000 to 50,000.</li>\n<li>PlayStation Plus, if you play online: the price of one or two games a year.</li>\n<li>A second controller: Rs 9,500.</li>\n<li>A 1TB drive, later: Rs 15,000 or more at present prices.</li>\n<li>A headset: Rs 3,000 to 10,000.</li>\n<li>A surge protector or small UPS: Rs 2,000 to 8,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sales on the store are deep. Games from a year or two ago often cost a quarter of their launch price, so patience is the best discount.</p>\n<h2>Electricity</h2>\n<p>A PS5 draws about 180 to 220 watts in demanding games, less in lighter ones and films, and around a watt in rest mode. Three hours a day is close to 20 units a month. Your television adds more. Turn off rest-mode features you do not use.</p>\n<h2>Heat, dust and monsoon</h2>\n<p>Give it ten centimetres on every side. Do not put it in a closed cabinet. Clean the vents and the dust catchers with a vacuum every couple of months. In the monsoon, let it run for half an hour every few days, as moisture is kinder to warm electronics. Keep it off the carpet.</p>\n<h2>PS5 against a gaming PC</h2>\n<p>A PC that matches a PS5 costs Rs 1.3 lakh or more in Nepal today, with a monitor extra. It also does homework, editing and mods, and plays games such as Valorant and Dota. A console is cheaper, simpler and better for the living room. If there is already a decent laptop for study, the console is the better entertainment purchase. If not, a PC does two jobs. Compare with our <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a>.</p>\n<h2>PS5 against Nintendo Switch 2</h2>\n<p>They hardly compete. The Switch is portable, with Nintendo\'s own games, and suits families and commuters. The PS5 is for cinematic games on a big screen. Homes with small children often get more use from the Switch. Check the language of imported units before paying.</p>\n<h2>Is a PS6 coming?</h2>\n<p>Sony has spoken in general terms about its next hardware and has given no date or price. Nothing suggests that it would reach Nepal soon, and first-year consoles are always scarce and expensive here. Waiting for it makes little sense if you want to play now.</p>\n<h2>Will prices fall?</h2>\n<p>Historically consoles became cheaper with age. This generation has gone the other way because of component costs and exchange rates. GTA VI has raised demand further. There may be some relief after the festival season and the launch rush. We would not expect a dramatic fall. Watch the <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a>, and buy when a genuine seller offers the Digital Edition below Rs 90,000 or a Pro below Rs 1,40,000.</p>\n<h2>Which one should you buy?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most people:</strong> Digital Edition.</li>\n<li><strong>Collectors and traders of discs:</strong> Digital or Pro with the detachable drive, or a used disc model.</li>\n<li><strong>Large 4K television:</strong> Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Nobody, at today\'s price:</strong> Slim Disc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For a fuller comparison, read our <a href=\"/reviews/ps5-digital-slim-pro-review-nepal\">PS5 buying verdict</a>, and if GTA VI is the reason, see <a href=\"/news/gta-6-launch-record-sales-how-to-buy-nepal\">how to buy it in Nepal</a>.</p>\n<h2>A short word on gifts</h2>\n<p>If the console is a present, check three things quietly: whether the television has a free HDMI port, whether the home internet can download a 100GB game in a night, and whether the person already has an account in a particular store region. Set up the account and the updates the evening before, so the first hour is spent playing and not waiting for a progress bar.</p>','price-trends','news/news-ps5.jpg',1,0,'Current PS5 prices in Nepal: Digital Rs 95,000, Slim Disc Rs 1,39,999 and PS5 Pro Rs 1,45,000, after Sony raised prices in several regions in 2026.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(38,20,'iPhone 18 Pro, Pro Max and iPhone Duo: Official Prices in Nepal','iphone-18-pro-pro-max-iphone-duo-nepal-price','<p>Apple\'s autumn 2026 iPhones are all on sale in Nepal now. The iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max arrived on 29 September, and Apple\'s first foldable, the iPhone Duo, followed on 30 October. Here\'s what each costs.</p>\n\n<h2>iPhone 18 Pro</h2>\n<p>256GB Rs 2,59,900 · 512GB Rs 3,01,900 · 1TB Rs 3,86,900 · 2TB Rs 5,13,900. It has a 6.3-inch display, the A20 Pro chip and a 4x telephoto. <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">Full specs</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>iPhone 18 Pro Max</h2>\n<p>256GB Rs 2,79,900 · 512GB Rs 3,22,900 · 1TB Rs 4,07,900 · 2TB Rs 5,34,900. It moves up to a 6.9-inch screen and a 5x periscope. Read our <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-18-pro-max-review-nepal\">iPhone 18 Pro Max review</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>iPhone Duo</h2>\n<p>256GB Rs 4,30,900 · 512GB Rs 4,72,900 · 1TB Rs 5,57,900 · 2TB Rs 6,84,900. The foldable has a 7.6-inch inner screen, a 5.4-inch cover screen and a titanium hinge. It uses Touch ID on the side instead of Face ID. <a href=\"/products/iphone-duo\">Full specs</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Where\'s the regular iPhone 18?</h2>\n<p>Apple split its line-up this year. The standard <a href=\"/products/iphone-18\">iPhone 18</a> and the <a href=\"/products/iphone-18e\">iPhone 18e</a> are expected in spring 2027. Until then, the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> (Rs 1,64,999) is the newest non-Pro iPhone.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The family at a glance</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,900</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Up to 36 hours video playback; 50W wired (abo...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,79,900</td><td>6.9-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1320×2868, LT...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>50W wired, 25W MagSafe wireless, 4.5W reverse...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-duo\">iPhone Duo</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,30,900</td><td>7.6-inch inner LTPO OLED (1878×2670, 120Hz, n...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm) with vapour chamber</td><td>12GB</td><td>Dual-cell; up to 31 hours video (inner screen...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1206×2622, LT...</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>25W MagSafe, 15W Qi2; about 50% in 20 minutes...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,32,499</td><td>6.1-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1,200 nits</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>4,005mAh; 15W MagSafe/Qi2 wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What changed this year</h2>\n<p>For more than a decade Apple launched its phones together in September. In 2026 it split them. The expensive phones came in autumn, and the regular models are expected in spring. For buyers in Nepal this has two effects. Anyone who wants a new iPhone under Rs 2.5 lakh this winter is choosing from last year\'s line. And the usual price cut on older models, which comes when a successor arrives, has been smaller than people expected.</p>\n<h2>iPhone 18 Pro or 18 Pro Max?</h2>\n<p>The difference is Rs 20,000 at 256GB. For that the Pro Max gives you a 6.9-inch screen, a bigger battery, and a 5x periscope camera where the Pro has 4x. The Pro gives you something that cannot be bought: a phone that fits a hand. Hold both for five minutes, with a case. If you read and watch a lot, or film at a distance, choose the Max. If your phone lives in a trouser pocket, choose the Pro. The chip, memory and main camera are the same.</p>\n<h2>The iPhone Duo: who is it for?</h2>\n<p>A phone with a 7.6-inch inner display costs Rs 4,30,900 and up. It is a small tablet that becomes a phone. People who read documents, review spreadsheets, sketch or watch films while travelling will use the inner screen every day. Everyone else will open it for a week to show friends, and then use the outer screen. The first generation of any foldable carries risk: hinges, inner screens and repairs. With Touch ID in place of Face ID and a first-generation design, we would wait unless the price means little to you.</p>\n<p>If you like the idea and not the price, Samsung\'s foldables are several generations old by now. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold8\">Galaxy Z Fold8</a> costs about Rs 2,74,999, and the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold-7\">Fold 7</a> less.</p>\n<h2>Storage: where Apple earns its money</h2>\n<p>Going from 256GB to 512GB costs about Rs 42,000 to 43,000. A 512GB portable SSD costs a fraction of that, and an iPhone can record video directly to one. Cloud storage is another route. Our view is simple. If you shoot a lot of 4K video and do not want to manage files, pay for 512GB. Otherwise 256GB and a habit of moving old videos to a computer each month will do. A terabyte or two makes sense only for people who are paid for their footage.</p>\n<h2>Should you buy the iPhone 17 instead?</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,64,999 the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> has a 120Hz display, two 48MP cameras, a good battery and years of updates. It lacks the telephoto and some of the polish. For ninety per cent of buyers it is the right iPhone, and it leaves almost a lakh in your pocket. <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-17-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains its few weaknesses. The <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">17e</a> is cheaper still at Rs 1,32,499, with one rear camera.</p>\n<h2>Or the 17 Pro Max?</h2>\n<p>It remains available at about Rs 2,42,499 for 256GB. The gap to the 18 Pro Max is some Rs 37,000. We compare them in <a href=\"/news/iphone-17-pro-max-leaks-2nm-a19-pro-nepal-pricing-radar\">this article</a>. If you find it sealed and official at Rs 2,25,000 or less, it is the better value.</p>\n<h2>Official or grey?</h2>\n<p>At these prices a grey iPhone can be Rs 15,000 to 40,000 cheaper. Understand what you give up: local warranty, certainty about MDMS, and sometimes a SIM tray. United States units have eSIM only. Chinese and Hong Kong units take two physical SIMs. Japanese units may make a shutter sound that cannot be switched off. If you travel and know what you are doing, a grey phone may suit you. If you want to walk into a shop when something fails, pay for official.</p>\n<p>Check any unit at Apple\'s coverage page, verify the IMEI with the MDMS system, and insist on a bill with the serial number. Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">checking an official phone</a> has the steps.</p>\n<h2>EMI and exchange</h2>\n<p>On Rs 2,59,900, a twelve-month plan at zero interest is about Rs 21,658 a month. Banks may ask for a processing fee and a minimum salary. Longer plans with interest can add Rs 20,000 to 30,000 in total, so ask for the full amount you will have paid at the end. Exchange values for older iPhones are often generous in launch season and lower in winter. Compare with a private sale. The EMI estimate on our product pages shows the monthly figure.</p>\n<h2>Protecting a phone that costs more than a motorbike</h2>\n<p>Screen and back glass repairs are severe. A MagSafe case and a good tempered glass are the minimum. Some insurers and shops sell accidental damage plans. Ask about the excess, the number of claims and whether parts are genuine. Turn on Stolen Device Protection, Find My and a six-digit passcode. Do not leave Control Centre available on the lock screen, as thieves switch on airplane mode first.</p>\n<h2>What the 2nm chip does for you</h2>\n<p>The A20 Pro is the first iPhone chip on a 2nm process. In daily use you feel it mainly as battery life and coolness. Games hold their frame rate longer, video exports are quicker, and Apple Intelligence tasks have more memory to work with. Compared with an A19 Pro phone, the change is small. Compared with a 14 Pro or older, it is large.</p>\n<h2>Trade-in timing</h2>\n<p>If you own a 16 Pro or 17 Pro, its value will drop again when the spring models arrive and once more next September. If you intend to upgrade within the year, selling before spring is wiser. If you are content, keep it. Apple\'s phones age slowly, and a new battery does more for a 14 Pro than most people expect.</p>\n<h2>Waiting for the iPhone 18</h2>\n<p>The regular iPhone 18 and the 18e are expected around spring 2027, and our estimate for the former is about Rs 1,75,000. If the 17 feels like last year\'s phone to you, that is four or five months away. <a href=\"/news/iphone-18-iphone-18e-spring-2027-what-we-know\">Here is what is known</a>.</p>\n<h2>Our recommendations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best for most:</strong> iPhone 17, 256GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Best compact flagship:</strong> iPhone 18 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for video and battery:</strong> iPhone 18 Pro Max. <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-18-pro-max-review-nepal\">Read the review</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value flagship:</strong> iPhone 17 Pro Max at a discount.</li>\n<li><strong>For enthusiasts only:</strong> iPhone Duo.</li>\n</ul>','mobile','news/news-iphone-18-pro.jpg',1,1,'Official Nepal prices for the iPhone 18 Pro (from Rs 2,59,900), iPhone 18 Pro Max (from Rs 2,79,900) and the foldable iPhone Duo (from Rs 4,30,900).',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(39,20,'M6 MacBook Pro and OLED MacBook Pro: Expected Prices in Nepal','macbook-pro-m6-oled-macbook-pro-expected-price-nepal','<p>Apple announced two new MacBook Pro models at the end of October 2026, a day apart. Neither is officially on sale in Nepal yet, so the prices below are estimates converted from US prices, not official figures.</p>\n\n<h2>MacBook Pro 14 with M6</h2>\n<p>This model keeps the familiar design and mini-LED XDR screen, with a new M6 chip (12-core CPU and GPU), Wi-Fi 7 and battery life of up to 24 hours. It starts at $1,999 in the US with 16GB of memory and 1TB of storage. <strong>Expected Nepal price: about Rs 3,85,999</strong>, rising to Rs 4,81,999 with 2TB. <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m6\">See every configuration</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>MacBook Pro with ProLED XDR display</h2>\n<p>This is the bigger news: a tandem-OLED touchscreen, the first touchscreen on a Mac, plus a Dynamic Island, offered with M5 Pro and M5 Max chips in 14 and 16-inch sizes. US prices start at $2,899. <strong>Expected Nepal price: about Rs 5,35,999</strong> for the 14-inch M5 Pro, up to Rs 8,79,999 for the 16-inch M5 Max. <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-proled-xdr\">Specs and expected prices</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Should you wait?</h2>\n<p>If you need a Mac for work now, the <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">M5 MacBook Pro 14</a> is on sale here at Rs 3,76,500, and the M6 is an incremental step up. Most people don\'t need a Pro at all. The <a href=\"/reviews/macbook-air-m5-13-review-nepal\">MacBook Air M5</a> is still our top pick.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Current and coming models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">Apple MacBook Air 13 (M5, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>13.6-inch (diagonal) LED-backlit display with...</td><td>Apple M5 (10-core CPU, 8/10-core GPU)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X Unified Memory</td><td>Built-in 53.8‑watt‑hour lithium‑polymer batte...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">Apple MacBook Pro 14 (M5, 16GB/1TB, Space Black)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,64,999</td><td>14.2-inch 3024 × 1964 resolution, 254 PPI 1,0...</td><td>Total CPU Cores 10 cores consisting of 4 high...</td><td>16GB Unified Memory</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5-pro\">Apple MacBook Pro 14 (M5 Pro, 24GB/1TB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,63,499</td><td>Liquid Retina XDR display 14.2-inch (diagonal...</td><td>Apple M5 Pro (up to 18-core CPU, 20-core GPU)</td><td>24GB unified memory</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m6\">Apple MacBook Pro 14 (M6)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,85,999</td><td>14.2-inch Liquid Retina XDR (3024×1964), ProM...</td><td>Apple M6 (12-core CPU, 12-core GPU with Neura...</td><td>16GB, 24GB or 32GB unified memory</td><td>72.4Wh; up to 24 hours video streaming, 16 ho...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-proled-xdr\">Apple MacBook Pro with ProLED XDR Display</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,35,999</td><td>ProLED XDR tandem OLED touchscreen, ProMotion...</td><td>Apple M5 Pro (up to 18-core CPU, 20-core GPU)...</td><td>24GB to 128GB unified memory</td><td>72.6Wh (14-inch) / 100Wh (16-inch); up to 24...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The last two rows are estimates based on prices in the United States, with duty, VAT and the usual margin added. They are not official Nepali prices. Treat a difference of Rs 20,000 in either direction as normal.</p>\n<h2>How we estimate a Nepal price</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s dollar price excludes sales tax. To reach a shelf in Kathmandu a laptop passes through exchange, freight, customs duty, VAT at 13 per cent and the reseller\'s margin. Looking back at several years of MacBooks, the final number has tended to land between 1.25 and 1.4 times the plain converted price. With a starting price of $1,999 that gives a range around Rs 3.5 to 3.95 lakh, and the middle of it is our estimate. We replace it as soon as a reseller announces the real figure.</p>\n<h2>M5 to M6: what changes in work</h2>\n<p>The M6 adds cores to both processor and graphics. Expect exports, compiles and renders to finish perhaps 15 to 25 per cent sooner than on the M5, going by Apple\'s past steps. Single tasks improve less. Wi-Fi 7 and longer battery life round it off. If you own an M1 or M2, the sum of four or five such steps is large. If you own an M4 or M5, it is not worth the loss on resale.</p>\n<h2>An OLED Mac at last</h2>\n<p>Tandem OLED stacks two light-emitting layers. It reaches higher brightness, lasts longer, and gives perfect blacks with no halo around subtitles. For colourists and photographers it is the best screen Apple has put in a laptop. The touchscreen is the surprise, since Apple argued against the idea for fifteen years. macOS was designed for a pointer, so in the first versions touch will be handy for scrolling, scrubbing a timeline and signing, and little else.</p>\n<p>There are reasons to wait. First generations of new screens cost more to repair. Long-term wear with a fixed menu bar is unknown. And the price, starting at more than five lakhs here by our estimate, is for professionals who bill by the project.</p>\n<h2>Which Mac do you need? Four questions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Do you export video longer than ten minutes, several times a week?</strong> Pro. If not, Air.</li>\n<li><strong>Do you need more than 24GB of memory?</strong> M5 Pro class.</li>\n<li><strong>Do you work in bright rooms or with HDR?</strong> Pro for its XDR screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Is it for study, writing, spreadsheets, code for the web?</strong> Air or even the Neo.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Most people who buy a Pro could have bought an Air, with money left for a monitor. Our <a href=\"/reviews/macbook-air-m5-13-review-nepal\">MacBook Air M5 review</a> goes into it.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage for professionals</h2>\n<p>Unified memory cannot be added. For Final Cut, Logic, Xcode and Docker, 24GB is comfortable and 16GB is workable. For After Effects, 3D and local language models, look at 36GB or more, which means Pro or Max chips. Storage is different: a fast external SSD over Thunderbolt or USB4 is nearly as quick as the internal drive for a quarter of Apple\'s price. So spend on memory inside and storage outside.</p>\n<h2>Buy now or wait?</h2>\n<p><strong>Buy the M5 now</strong> if you need a machine this month, or find a discount of Rs 25,000 or more. The M5 is not slow and will receive macOS for many years.</p>\n<p><strong>Wait for the M6</strong> if your current Mac works, if you keep machines six years or more, or if Wi-Fi 7 and battery matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Wait for the OLED model</strong> if colour is your profession and you can let others discover the early faults.</p>\n<p><strong>Buy used</strong> if you are a student. An M1 Pro or M2 Pro 14-inch at Rs 1.6 to 2.2 lakh remains a wonderful computer with the same class of screen and speakers.</p>\n<h2>When will they reach Nepal?</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s authorised resellers usually receive new MacBooks four to ten weeks after the global launch. Importers bring a few earlier at a premium. With the launch in late October, January is a fair expectation for the M6 and a little later for the OLED machines, which are in shorter supply. Early imports carry the risk of a keyboard layout or warranty you did not want.</p>\n<h2>Warranty for Macs</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s limited warranty on Macs is generally international, and service here goes through authorised providers. Confirm by entering the serial number at Apple\'s coverage site. AppleCare+ bought in another country may or may not be honoured. Liquid damage is never covered by the standard warranty. A sleeve and a habit of keeping tea away are the cheapest insurance.</p>\n<h2>Windows alternatives</h2>\n<p>For the expected price of an M6 Pro you could buy a <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3406ga\">Zenbook 14 OLED</a> and a serious desktop, or a thin RTX laptop such as a Zephyrus. If your software uses CUDA, games matter or your clients use Windows-only tools, those are better. If you edit in Final Cut, value battery and quiet, or live in Apple\'s world, they are not.</p>\n<h2>Education and business pricing</h2>\n<p>Resellers in Nepal offer education prices from time to time, typically a few per cent, and more during admission season. Bring a college ID. Businesses with a PAN can claim VAT input in some cases, so ask your accountant. Company purchases should specify configuration, warranty terms and delivery date in the quotation, since exchange rates move.</p>\n<h2>Setting up a new Mac in Nepal</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update macOS before installing anything.</li>\n<li>Add the Nepali Devanagari keyboards and check old Preeti files with a converter.</li>\n<li>Turn on FileVault and Find My.</li>\n<li>Set Optimised Battery Charging.</li>\n<li>Start Time Machine on an external drive the same evening.</li>\n<li>Use a surge protector with the MagSafe charger.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Trading in the old one</h2>\n<p>Macs hold value. Back up, sign out of iCloud, turn off Find My, erase all content and settings, and keep the box. A clean M1 Air still sells quickly. Prices for M5 machines will soften when M6 stock lands, so if you plan to switch, sell a few weeks earlier.</p>\n<h2>In short</h2>\n<p>The M6 is a steady step. The OLED model is the beginning of a new shape for Macs. Neither makes an M5 a mistake, and for most readers the <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air M5</a> is still the one to buy.</p>','laptop','news/news-macbook-pro-m6.jpg',1,2,'Apple announced the M6 MacBook Pro 14 and a tandem-OLED MacBook Pro with touch. Expected Nepal prices start at Rs 3,85,999 and Rs 5,35,999.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(40,20,'Galaxy S27 Ultra: What the Leaks Say About Launch, Specs and Price','galaxy-s27-ultra-leaks-launch-date-nepal-price','<p>Samsung hasn\'t said anything officially about the Galaxy S27 series, but the leaks have settled into a fairly consistent picture. Here\'s what\'s being reported, and how much weight to give it.</p>\n\n<h2>Launch timing</h2>\n<p>Most reports expect a Galaxy Unpacked event in <strong>January or February 2027</strong>. One tipster says early January, while others expect late February. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">S26 Ultra</a> was unveiled on 25 February 2026. Four models are expected: the S27, S27+, a new S27 Pro and the S27 Ultra.</p>\n\n<h2>Reported upgrades</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Battery:</strong> about 5,700mAh, up from 5,000mAh. This is the most widely repeated leak, and the most welcome one.</li>\n<li><strong>Chip:</strong> a 2nm Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 6 for Galaxy.</li>\n<li><strong>Design:</strong> CAD renders show the three cameras in a single horizontal strip with a smaller bump.</li>\n<li><strong>S Pen:</strong> stays, despite earlier rumours that it would go.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Expected price in Nepal</h2>\n<p>US reports suggest $1,399–1,499, up from $1,299, and Indian outlets estimate about ₹1,49,990. The S26 Ultra launched here at Rs 2,24,999, so our estimate for the S27 Ultra is <strong>around Rs 2,40,000</strong>. We\'ll replace it with the official price as soon as Samsung Nepal announces one. <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra\">Follow the S27 Ultra page</a> for updates.</p>\n<h3>Sources</h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.digit.in/news/mobile-phones/samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra-leaks-price-in-india-launch-timeline-design-camera-and-other-details-we-know.html\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Digit – Galaxy S27 Ultra leaks and India pricing</a></li><li><a href=\"https://tomsguide.com/phones/samsung-phones/samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra-rumors-release-display-upgrades-and-everything-we-know-so-far\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Tom\'s Guide – Galaxy S27 Ultra rumours</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.androidheadlines.com/exclusive-samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra-official-cad-renders-leak\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Android Headlines – S27 Ultra CAD renders</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.phonearena.com/galaxy-s27-ultra-release-date-price-features-news\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">PhoneArena – Galaxy S27 Ultra release date and price expectations</a></li></ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>How much weight does each rumour deserve?</h2>\n<p>We sort leaks into three baskets.</p>\n<p><strong>Likely.</strong> Things that appear in several independent places, including component suppliers and certification papers: a larger battery, a Snapdragon from the Elite Gen 6 family, the S Pen staying, four models in the line.</p>\n<p><strong>Possible.</strong> Things seen in drawings but not yet in cases and parts: the horizontal camera strip, dimensions, colours.</p>\n<p><strong>Guesswork.</strong> Prices, exact dates, and any number in Nepali rupees, including ours. Ours is printed so that you can plan, and it is labelled.</p>\n<h2>Why the battery would matter most</h2>\n<p>Every Galaxy Ultra since 2020 has had 5,000mAh. In that time Chinese brands moved to silicon-carbon cells with 6,500mAh or more in bodies of the same size. Samsung held back, partly from caution after older battery troubles, partly because some shipping rules make single cells above 20Wh more complicated. A move to around 5,700mAh would not match rivals, but it would add two hours or so to a heavy day. For Nepali users who travel, use hotspots and play video on mobile data, it is the only change that would be felt by everybody.</p>\n<h2>The 2nm chip</h2>\n<p>Smaller transistors switch with less energy. The likely benefit is steadier performance in long games and video recording, and better battery life in mixed use. Peak speed will rise, and few people will notice. There is also the question of whether some regions will get an Exynos. For the Ultra, Samsung has used Snapdragon everywhere in recent years, and reports suggest that will continue. For the smaller S27 models it is open.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: a new layout</h2>\n<p>If the three lenses move into one strip, Samsung may have changed the sensors behind them as well. A rumoured 5x periscope at 50MP would match the current one, so improvements may come from processing, a brighter lens or a larger main sensor. Until real samples exist, nothing more can be said honestly.</p>\n<h2>What will it cost here?</h2>\n<p>Our estimate is around Rs 2,40,000 for the base version. It comes from the S26 Ultra\'s launch price of Rs 2,24,999, the reported increase of $100 to $200 in the United States, and the way Samsung Nepal has followed such changes before. There is a range. If memory prices ease, Rs 2,30,000 is possible. If Samsung raises base storage or the rupee weakens, Rs 2,55,000 would not shock us.</p>\n<p>Launch offers usually soften it. In recent years early buyers received double storage, a trade-in bonus, or a discount on buds and watches. If you plan to buy at launch, do it in the pre-order window with an exchange.</p>\n<h2>What happens to the S26 Ultra?</h2>\n<p>When the new model arrives, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">S26 Ultra</a> will come down, by Rs 15,000 to 30,000 in the first two months if history is a guide, and stock will thin after that. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">S25 Ultra</a> will probably vanish from official shelves. Both remain good phones with many years of support. Our <a href=\"/news/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra-leak-roundup-snapdragon-8-elite-nepal\">comparison of the two</a> is the place to start if you want to save.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait? A simple test</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Your phone is dead or failing:</strong> do not wait. Three or four months without a proper phone costs more than a price drop.</li>\n<li><strong>You own an S24 Ultra or S25 Ultra:</strong> wait, and perhaps skip. Nothing in the leaks demands an upgrade.</li>\n<li><strong>You own an S21, S22 or Note 20:</strong> waiting is worthwhile. Either you get the new battery or a cheaper S26 Ultra.</li>\n<li><strong>You want an S26 Ultra at the lowest price:</strong> February and March 2027 will be your season.</li>\n<li><strong>You were going to buy an S26 or S26+:</strong> the same logic, with smaller amounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The new Pro in the middle</h2>\n<p>A fourth model, the S27 Pro, is expected to bring flagship cameras in a smaller body without a pen. For people who found the Ultra too large and the Plus too plain, it may be the most interesting of all. <a href=\"/news/galaxy-s27-pro-s27-ultra-design-leak\">Our report on the design leak</a> has the details.</p>\n<h2>Competition by then</h2>\n<p>By the time the S27 Ultra reaches Kathmandu, it will face the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-18-pro\">Xiaomi 18 Pro</a> and its larger versions, vivo\'s next X series, OPPO\'s Find line and the iPhone 18 Pro Max. Chinese rivals will have bigger batteries and faster charging. Samsung\'s answer is software, service, resale, seven years of updates and the pen. Those have been enough so far.</p>\n<h2>Charging</h2>\n<p>The S26 Ultra went to 60W after years at 45W. Nothing reliable suggests another jump. With a larger battery, the same 60W would take slightly longer to fill. A PPS charger of 60W or more with a 5A cable is needed for the full speed, and Samsung does not include one. If you already bought one for the S26 Ultra, it will serve.</p>\n<h2>Storage and memory</h2>\n<p>Base storage has been 256GB. With memory expensive, we do not expect a generous increase, and some reports even mention fewer memory options. If you need 512GB, the launch period is the time, as Samsung often gives the larger size for the price of the smaller one.</p>\n<h2>Buying at launch in Nepal</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Register interest through Samsung\'s official channels or a known dealer.</li>\n<li>Read what the pre-order deposit covers and whether it can be refunded.</li>\n<li>Compare the exchange value of your old phone with a private sale.</li>\n<li>Ask which bank offers apply, and what the cap is.</li>\n<li>Confirm the delivery date in writing. Popular colours are often late.</li>\n<li>Check the IMEI in MDMS when it arrives, and register the warranty.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>How we will update this</h2>\n<p>As Samsung confirms details, the estimates on the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra\">product page</a> will be replaced with official figures and dates. If a leak proves wrong, we will say so here. For other phones on the way, see <a href=\"/upcoming-launches\">upcoming launches</a>.</p>\n<h2>Our honest expectation</h2>\n<p>A bigger battery, a cooler chip, a cleaner back and a higher price. It sounds modest. For the person who keeps an Ultra for five years, modest improvements every year add up to a phone that is much better than the one in their pocket. For everyone else, its greatest service will be to make last year\'s model cheaper.</p>','rumors',NULL,1,7,'Galaxy S27 Ultra leaks point to a January–February 2027 launch, a 5,700mAh battery and a 2nm Snapdragon. Our Nepal price estimate and what to believe.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(41,20,'iPhone 18 and iPhone 18e Expected in Spring 2027: What We Know','iphone-18-iphone-18e-spring-2027-what-we-know','<p>For the first time, Apple didn\'t launch its whole iPhone line-up together. The <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a>, Pro Max and the foldable iPhone Duo came in autumn 2026. The cheaper models are expected in spring 2027.</p>\n\n<h2>iPhone 18</h2>\n<p>Bloomberg\'s Mark Gurman has repeatedly reported a spring 2027 launch, and later supply-chain reports point to around March. The rumoured specs are a 2nm A20 chip (a small step below the Pro\'s A20 Pro), a 6.3-inch display, two 48MP rear cameras, a 24MP front camera and more RAM than the iPhone 17\'s 8GB.</p>\n<p><strong>Our Nepal estimate: about Rs 1,75,000.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> sells for Rs 1,64,999 at a US price of $799, and reports expect the iPhone 18 at $849–899. <a href=\"/products/iphone-18\">iPhone 18 page</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>iPhone 18e</h2>\n<p>The 18e is expected to stay close to the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a>, with the A20 chip, a single 48MP camera and Apple\'s C2 modem. One leak suggests another 60Hz screen. <strong>Our Nepal estimate: about Rs 1,35,000</strong> if Apple holds the $599 US price, or closer to Rs 1,50,000 if it moves to $649–699. <a href=\"/products/iphone-18e\">iPhone 18e page</a>.</p>\n\n<p>None of this is confirmed by Apple. Reports also note that Apple could still change its plans.</p>\n<h3>Sources</h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">MacRumors – iPhone 18 roundup</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.macworld.com/article/3188152/iphone-18e-18-2027-release-date-specs-price-cameras.html\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Macworld – iPhone 18 and 18e: release date, specs, price</a></li><li><a href=\"https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/27/apples-low-cost-iphone-18-details-revealed-by-leaker/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">9to5Mac – low-cost iPhone 18 details from leaker</a></li></ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Why Apple may have split the launch</h2>\n<p>Selling four or five phones in one month strains factories, advertising and shops. Spreading them over the year gives each a moment and evens out sales. It also lets Apple answer Samsung, whose S series arrives in the first quarter. For buyers the effect is simple: there will be two iPhone seasons, one for expensive models in autumn and one for ordinary models in spring.</p>\n<h2>What we can say with some confidence</h2>\n<p>Reports from several sources agree on the outline: a spring launch, an A20 chip on a 2nm process, and a design close to the iPhone 17. They agree less on memory and cameras. Nobody outside Apple knows the prices. We have listed estimates for Nepal and will replace them when real numbers come.</p>\n<h2>iPhone 17 today or iPhone 18 in spring?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1206×2622, LT...</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18\">Apple iPhone 18</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,75,000</td><td>6.3-inch (rumoured)</td><td>Apple A20, 2nm (reported)</td><td>More than the iPhone 17&#039;s 8GB (reported)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,32,499</td><td>6.1-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1,200 nits</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18e\">Apple iPhone 18e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,35,000</td><td>–</td><td>Apple A20 (reported)</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The iPhone 17 was the year Apple gave its regular model a 120Hz display, brighter screen and better front camera. It is a mature phone. The 18 is expected to add a newer chip, more memory and a better front camera. If you use a phone for messages, photos and video, you would be hard pressed to tell the two apart after a week. The stronger reasons to wait are length of support, about one more year, and more memory for Apple Intelligence.</p>\n<p>The reason not to wait is life. If your phone\'s battery dies at two in the afternoon, four or five months is a long time.</p>\n<h2>What will happen to the price of the 17?</h2>\n<p>When a successor arrives, Apple usually lowers the older model by about $100, or stops selling it. In Nepal, resellers clear stock with discounts of Rs 8,000 to 20,000. If Apple keeps the 17 in the line at a lower price, that is the moment to buy one. If it drops the 17 in favour of the 17e and 18, stock will vanish quickly. So in spring, be ready.</p>\n<h2>The e line: what it is for</h2>\n<p>The 16e and 17e proved that many people want an iPhone and do not care about the number of cameras. One good 48MP camera, strong battery life, the newest chip and a lower price. The cuts are the ultra wide lens, MagSafe strength on earlier models, screen brightness and refresh rate. If reports are right, the 18e will follow the same recipe. For parents, students and people moving from an iPhone 11 or 12, it will be the one to consider, provided the Nepal price stays near Rs 1,35,000.</p>\n<p>At Rs 1,50,000 it would be a bad purchase, as an iPhone 17 on discount would cost nearly the same. That is the risk with the e models here, where the gap between them and the regular phone tends to shrink after duties.</p>\n<h2>Is a 60Hz screen acceptable in 2027?</h2>\n<p>For someone who has only used 60Hz, yes. For someone who has used a 120Hz phone, scrolling feels slightly rough for a few days, and then you forget. On Android, phones at Rs 20,000 have 120Hz, so it looks mean at Rs 1.35 lakh. It saves battery, and that is a genuine benefit. Try both in a shop before deciding how much you care.</p>\n<h2>Compared with Android at the same price</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1.3 to 1.8 lakh you can buy a Galaxy S26, an OPPO Find X9 Pro, a Pixel 10 or an Honor 600 Pro. All have telephoto lenses, faster charging and, except the Samsung and Pixel, far larger batteries. iPhones reply with video, longevity, resale, iMessage and FaceTime with family abroad, and apps that work the same for years. Decide which list is yours. Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-150000-nepal\">phones under Rs 1,50,000</a> sets out the Android side.</p>\n<h2>eSIM and the SIM tray</h2>\n<p>Apple has been removing the SIM tray in more countries. If the models sold in our region become eSIM-only, buyers here will need an operator that supports eSIM for their number. Both major operators have introduced it, with conditions. Before ordering any iPhone from abroad, ask what SIM arrangement it has. A phone that cannot take your SIM is an expensive camera.</p>\n<h2>Who should wait</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Owners of an iPhone 13, 14 or 15 in good health.</li>\n<li>People who keep phones six years.</li>\n<li>Anyone hoping for a discount on the 17.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Owners of an iPhone 11 or older, as updates are ending.</li>\n<li>People with a damaged phone.</li>\n<li>Students who need one now. An <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a> is fine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A bridge: change the battery</h2>\n<p>If your current iPhone is only tired, a genuine battery at an authorised provider restores its life for Rs 10,000 to 15,000, less at independent shops with the usual caution. It buys a year of comfort while you wait. Check battery health in Settings. Below 80 per cent, replacement transforms the phone, since iOS stops slowing it to protect a weak cell.</p>\n<h2>Trading in</h2>\n<p>Resale of an iPhone 14, 15 or 16 will dip in spring. If you plan to upgrade, listing your phone in February gets you more than in April. Erase it properly, sign out of iCloud, and meet the buyer somewhere public.</p>\n<h2>What might still change</h2>\n<p>Apple can move dates, rename models and adjust features until weeks before launch. Supply of 2nm chips is limited, and memory is expensive, so a price rise is quite possible. Reports that mention lower specifications to hold the price should be taken seriously. Wait for the announcement before making a decision that cannot be undone.</p>\n<h2>How to prepare</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write down what bothers you about your current phone.</li>\n<li>Note the price of an iPhone 17 today at two official resellers.</li>\n<li>Check your battery health.</li>\n<li>Set a budget, including a case and a plan for storage.</li>\n<li>Look again when Apple sends its invitations, probably in late winter.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Our expectation</h2>\n<p>The iPhone 18 will be a better iPhone 17 and not a different phone. That is praise, as the 17 is very good. The 18e will be as attractive as its Nepali price allows. And the best purchase of spring 2027 may well be a discounted iPhone 17. Follow the <a href=\"/products/iphone-18\">iPhone 18</a> and <a href=\"/products/iphone-18e\">iPhone 18e</a> pages, where we will post confirmed details.</p>','rumors',NULL,1,8,'The standard iPhone 18 and iPhone 18e are expected in spring 2027 with the A20 chip. Rumoured specs and our expected Nepal prices.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(42,20,'Why Phones, Laptops and Consoles Got More Expensive in Nepal in 2026','why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026','<p>If a device you priced in January now costs more, you\'re not imagining it. 2026 was a year of price rises rather than falls, in Nepal and worldwide. Here are the changes we could date and source.</p>\n\n<h2>The increases we tracked</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a>:</strong> the 256GB model went up from Rs 2,12,999 to Rs 2,24,999, and the 512GB model up from Rs 2,42,999 to Rs 2,59,999.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a>:</strong> recorded at Rs 1,33,999 in June, now Rs 1,39,999.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">MacBook Pro 14 (M5)</a>:</strong> up on 1 July 2026 to Rs 3,76,500. The rise came with the global memory shortage.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a>:</strong> from Rs 1,79,999 to Rs 1,99,999 on 1 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/sony-playstation-5\">PlayStation 5</a>:</strong> Sony raised prices in several regions in spring 2026. The Slim Disc is now listed at Rs 1,39,999.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/nintendo-switch-2\">Nintendo Switch 2</a>:</strong> the US price went from $449.99 to $499 on 1 September 2026, after Japan\'s rise in May. Imports to Nepal follow those prices.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Why it\'s happening</h2>\n<p>The biggest single cause is memory. Demand for AI data-centre hardware has tightened the supply of RAM and flash storage, and manufacturers have passed those costs on. Apple raised Mac and iPad prices in June 2026, and the same pressure shows up in phones, where more RAM and storage now cost more. Exchange rates and import costs add to the effect in Nepal.</p>\n\n<h2>What this means for buyers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Don\'t count on quick drops.</strong> With memory still expensive, several devices went up in price this year rather than down.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy the storage you need up front.</strong> Upgrades cost more than ever. See the storage prices on any product page.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch the price history.</strong> Every product page on this site now shows the dated prices we\'ve recorded, and the chart fills in as prices change.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Sources</h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.digitaltrends.com/gaming/nintendo-just-announced-a-price-hike-for-the-switch-2-in-the-us/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Digital Trends – Switch 2 US price increase</a></li><li><a href=\"https://blog.playstation.com/2026/03/27/20260327-ps/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">PlayStation Blog – 2026 price changes (Southeast Asia)</a></li></ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>What a price in Nepal is made of</h2>\n<p>To understand why a phone costs what it does, open it up. Suppose a mid-range phone leaves the factory gate at 300 dollars. Freight and insurance add a little. Customs duty and any excise are charged on that value, then VAT at 13 per cent on the total. The distributor adds a margin to cover warranty, marketing and the cost of money. The dealer adds another. By the time it is on the counter, the rupee figure is far above a plain conversion.</p>\n<p>Two conclusions follow. A small rise at the factory is multiplied on the way. And a weaker rupee raises every one of those steps at once. In 2026 both happened.</p>\n<h2>Memory: the largest single cause</h2>\n<p>Memory and storage can be a fifth or more of the parts cost of a budget phone and a large share of a laptop. When they rose, makers had four choices: raise the price, reduce the memory, cut something else, or accept less profit. We have seen the first three. If a successor arrives with a smaller base storage, a missing charger or an older chip at the same price, that is also a price rise. It is just harder to see. Our article on <a href=\"/news/electronic-price-hikes-nepal-ram-ssd-smartphones-analysis\">RAM and SSD prices</a> explains the mechanism.</p>\n<h2>Who was hit hardest</h2>\n<p><strong>Buyers under Rs 40,000.</strong> Memory is a bigger share of cheap phones. Some models that started at Rs 20,000 in 2025 now begin at Rs 25,000 with the same specification.</p>\n<p><strong>Students buying laptops.</strong> The 16GB machines that used to be Rs 85,000 moved towards a lakh, and 8GB returned to the shelves.</p>\n<p><strong>PC builders.</strong> Memory, SSDs and graphics cards all rose together.</p>\n<p><strong>Console buyers.</strong> For the first time in memory, an ageing console costs more than it did at launch.</p>\n<h2>Who was hardly affected</h2>\n<p>People buying earbuds, watches, power banks, basic monitors and older televisions. Competition there is fierce and memory content is small. If you were postponing a pair of headphones because \"everything is expensive\", you need not.</p>\n<h2>Why prices do not fall quickly</h2>\n<p>Prices rise by email and fall by clearance. A distributor who imported a thousand phones at a high cost will not sell them below it unless forced. So even when parts become cheaper, shelves need months to turn over. Retailers also remember: once customers accept Rs 45,000 for a certain phone, nobody hurries back to Rs 37,000. The pressure comes from a rival with newer, cheaper stock, and that is why watching several brands helps.</p>\n<h2>How to buy well in an expensive year</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Buy less often.</strong> A new battery for Rs 4,000 to 8,000 gives a phone two more years.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy one step down, with more memory.</strong> An A-series phone with 8GB/256GB ages better than a fancier one with 6GB/128GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy last year\'s flagship.</strong> Its price falls while new mid-range prices rise. The two lines have crossed.</li>\n<li><strong>Use festivals, with a notebook.</strong> Record the price before. Our <a href=\"/blog/dashain-tihar-tech-deals-how-to-spot-a-real-discount\">festival deals article</a> shows how.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask for exchange and bank offers together.</strong> They sometimes combine.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare across brands.</strong> Increases were uneven. One brand\'s rise is another\'s opportunity.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>New, used and refurbished in 2026</h2>\n<p>Used prices rose too, since demand moved there, but by less. Business laptops and two-year-old flagships are the sweet spots. Refurbished goods depend wholly on who did the work. Insist on a warranty of at least three months, a battery health figure and, for phones, MDMS registration. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">How to check</a>.</p>\n<h2>Is a grey import worth the saving now?</h2>\n<p>When official prices rise, the gap to grey units widens and the temptation grows. Consider the whole cost. No local warranty, uncertain registration and weaker resale. On a Rs 40,000 phone the saving is rarely worth it. On a Rs 2 lakh phone it may be Rs 25,000, and some people take that risk knowingly. If you do, verify the IMEI before paying, and do not call it a bargain until the first year has passed.</p>\n<h2>EMI in a year of rising prices</h2>\n<p>Instalments make a high price feel small. Zero per cent plans are useful if the cash price is the same, because your money keeps working for you. If the plan carries a fee or interest, or removes a discount, you are paying more for a product that is already expensive. Ask for the total, and keep the monthly amount under a tenth of your income.</p>\n<h2>For shops and small businesses</h2>\n<p>If you buy equipment, ask suppliers to hold quotations for a month. Standardise on one laptop model. Consider desktops, which give more performance and can be upgraded. Stretch replacement cycles from three to four years with an SSD and a memory upgrade. And keep a spare machine, because emergencies are when people pay the most.</p>\n<h2>Do official announcements help?</h2>\n<p>Government decisions on customs and VAT, usually with the annual budget in Jestha and Asar, can move prices more than any factory. A change in duty on phones or computers shows up within weeks on new stock. If you read that a rate will change, ask your dealer whether current stock was cleared under the old one. It can work in your favour in either direction.</p>\n<h2>What we expect next</h2>\n<p>We are careful with predictions. New memory capacity is being built, and it takes time. Flagship prices in early 2027 look more likely to rise than fall, judging by reports on the coming Galaxy and iPhone models. Budget phones may stabilise if competition among Xiaomi, Realme, Infinix and Tecno continues. Consoles depend on Sony and Nintendo. Laptops with 16GB should remain around or above a lakh for some time.</p>\n<h2>How we record prices</h2>\n<p>Each product page shows dated prices and, where there is more than one, a history. When a price changes we add a new entry and do not overwrite the old one. Some prices come from official lists and others from what sellers ask, and shops vary. If you see a difference in your city, tell us through the <a href=\"/contact\">contact page</a>. The <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a> lists recent movements in both directions.</p>\n<h2>A calmer way to think about it</h2>\n<p>A gadget is expensive when it is replaced early and cheap when it lasts. Divide any price by the months you will use it. A Rs 45,000 phone over four years is under Rs 940 a month. A Rs 98,000 laptop over five years is about Rs 1,630. Seen that way, choosing well and looking after it will save you more than any discount 2026 has to offer.</p>','price-trends','news/news-memory-prices.jpg',1,5,'From the Galaxy S26 Ultra to the MacBook Pro and PS5, 2026 brought price increases in Nepal. The dated hikes, the reasons, and what to do.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(43,20,'Samsung and Ncell Team Up: EMI From Rs 1,221, Free Data and a New Trade-in System','samsung-ncell-partnership-galaxy-offers-nepal','<!--depth-->\n<p>Samsung Nepal and Ncell have joined hands on a set of offers meant to move people from feature phones to 4G, and to make Galaxy phones easier to pay for. Some of it is marketing. Some of it can save you real money if it matches what you were going to buy anyway. Here is each part, what it is worth, and the questions to ask before you sign.</p>\n<h2>The offers in brief</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>2G to 4G exchange.</strong> Bring an old feature phone and receive a bonus of Rs 5,000 on a selected smartphone. Monthly payments begin at about Rs 1,221.</li>\n<li><strong>Long EMI.</strong> Plans of 24 to 36 months through Hulas Fin Serve, with 20 per cent down, starting at around Rs 5,106 a month on costlier models.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra warranty and exchange bonus.</strong> Up to Rs 10,000 more for your old phone, plus an additional year of warranty, on selected Galaxy Z and S models.</li>\n<li><strong>Data packs.</strong> Up to 12 months of Ncell\'s Rs 799 pack on Z and S phones, and nine months of the Rs 449 pack on A and M phones.</li>\n<li><strong>App-based valuation.</strong> A tool called PhoneShield used at service centres and selected stores to check a phone before exchange.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Offers of this sort change by month and by store. Treat the figures as the shape of the programme and confirm the detail at the counter.</p>\n<h2>Is the free data worth what they say?</h2>\n<p>Twelve packs of Rs 799 come to Rs 9,588 on paper. Their worth to you depends on your habits. If you already buy that pack every month, it is real cash in your pocket. If you use Wi-Fi all day and buy a Rs 200 pack twice a month, the value is closer to Rs 400 a month. If your main SIM is from NTC, it is worth whatever you would pay for a second line, which may be nothing.</p>\n<p>There are conditions to look for. The packs are usually credited month by month, must be activated by a date, and may be tied to the number registered at purchase. Ask what happens if you miss a month.</p>\n<h2>Rs 5,000 for a feature phone</h2>\n<p>This is the most generous part for the people it targets. A keypad phone that works sells for a few hundred rupees. Getting Rs 5,000 for it against a 4G smartphone is a real subsidy, provided the smartphone\'s price has not been raised to pay for it. Check the normal price on our product pages first. A <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> has been about Rs 25,499. If the exchange brings it close to Rs 20,000, take it.</p>\n<p>Before handing in the old phone, copy its contacts to the SIM or write them down, remove the memory card and delete messages. It may go to recycling, and recycling does not mean that the data is destroyed.</p>\n<h2>Thirty-six months is a long time</h2>\n<p>Long plans make a flagship look affordable. Take the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> at Rs 1,39,999. With 20 per cent down you pay Rs 28,000 on the day and finance Rs 1,12,000. Over 36 months at an ordinary finance rate, the total interest can be Rs 20,000 or more, though the monthly amount looks gentle. Three questions decide whether the plan is good:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the total of all payments, including the fee?</li>\n<li>Is the cash price the same, or is there a discount that EMI buyers lose?</li>\n<li>What happens if a payment is late or the phone is stolen?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The last one matters in the third year. You may still be paying for a phone that is broken. For that reason we prefer plans of twelve months, or eighteen at most, and a phone whose price lets you manage that. The EMI estimate on our product pages shows the monthly figure for different periods.</p>\n<h2>An extra year of warranty</h2>\n<p>For a Galaxy Z this is the best item on the list. Foldables have more to go wrong, and a hinge or an inner screen outside warranty costs a large part of the phone. Read what the second year covers. Manufacturing faults are normal, accidents usually not. Ask whether the extension is registered in Samsung\'s system by IMEI and can be seen in the Members app. A stamp on paper is weaker.</p>\n<h2>How valuation apps work</h2>\n<p>PhoneShield and similar tools test the screen, touch, cameras, buttons, speakers, battery and connectivity, look at the model and storage, and offer a price. They bring some fairness, since the figure no longer depends on the mood of the person at the counter. They also find faults you have stopped noticing: a dead microphone, a swollen battery, a line on the screen.</p>\n<p>Before you go, clean the phone, charge it, remove the case and the cracked protector, and sign out of your accounts. Look up private sale prices for your model. A clean phone often fetches 15 to 25 per cent more in a private sale, at the cost of time and some risk. Exchange is worth it when the bonus closes that gap.</p>\n<h2>AI features on cheaper phones</h2>\n<p>Part of the campaign promotes Galaxy AI on models such as the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a>. Be clear about what that means. Budget phones get a short list, for example Circle to Search and some assistant features, most of which need the internet. Call translation, generative photo editing and note summaries belong to the S and Z series and some higher A models. If a seller mentions AI, ask to see the feature working on the phone in question.</p>\n<h2>Who benefits most</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Families moving a parent from a keypad phone.</strong> The exchange bonus is excellent.</li>\n<li><strong>Ncell users who already buy monthly packs.</strong> The data is close to its face value.</li>\n<li><strong>Foldable buyers.</strong> The warranty is worth having.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who benefits least</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who mainly use NTC.</li>\n<li>Cash buyers who can bargain for a discount in place of gifts.</li>\n<li>Anyone who would stretch to a costlier phone because the instalment looks small.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What about the 4G argument?</h2>\n<p>Operators want customers off 2G for good reasons. Old networks occupy valuable frequencies and serve few people well. VoLTE calls on 4G are clearer and connect faster. For users, the change brings wallets, video calls with children abroad and government services. It also brings scams. If you are setting up a first smartphone for an older relative, take an hour to turn on automatic updates and Play Protect, block unknown app installs, put a lock on the wallet app, and explain that nobody from a bank ever asks for an OTP.</p>\n<h2>Compare before you accept</h2>\n<p>A package of gifts can hide a higher price. Take the final amount you would pay, subtract only the things you would have bought anyway, and compare the result with the same phone at two other shops and with rivals. At Rs 45,000, for example, a <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> or <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> offers stronger hardware without any gifts. At Rs 1.4 lakh, so do several others. Samsung\'s advantages are support and service, and those do not depend on this campaign.</p>\n<h2>Questions to take to the counter</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Which of these offers apply to this exact model in this shop today?</li>\n<li>Can exchange, bank discount and data offers be used together?</li>\n<li>How and when is the data credited, and to which number?</li>\n<li>Is the phone an official Nepali unit, registered in MDMS?</li>\n<li>May I have the conditions in writing on the bill?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Our take</h2>\n<p>It is a useful programme for two groups: people leaving feature phones and people who were about to buy a premium Galaxy on an Ncell number. For others it is a pleasant extra at best. Choose the phone first, with our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">guide to phones by budget</a>, and then see which offers fall into your lap. Doing it the other way round is how people end up with a phone they did not want and a pack they do not use.</p>','telecom','gadgets/2026/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g.webp',1,0,'Samsung Nepal and Ncell have partnered on 2G-to-4G upgrades, longer EMI plans, free Ncell data packs and an app-based trade-in check. What Galaxy buyers get.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(44,20,'Huawei OSTA 1 Pro: A New Brand Starts With a Wide-Screen Phone and 7,000mAh Battery','huawei-osta-1-pro-new-brand-wide-screen-phone','<!--depth-->\n<p>Huawei has started a new line called OSTA, and its first phone looks like nothing else on the shelf. The OSTA 1 Pro is short and very wide. Where nearly every phone since 2017 has been tall and narrow, this one is closer to a passport. It has been announced in China, with no word about Nepal, but the idea is worth understanding, because shapes that succeed in China tend to spread.</p>\n<h2>What has been announced</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>OSTA 1 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Display</td><td>6.39-inch OLED, 2232 × 1320, 120Hz, 16:9.5 shape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Size</td><td>144.1 × 87 × 6.68mm, about 200g</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>200MP main, 50MP periscope telephoto</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery</td><td>7,000mAh, 66W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Protection</td><td>IP68 and IP69</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The closest relative is Huawei\'s own Pura X View, and the two share a lot. OSTA appears to be Huawei\'s way of selling the idea under a fresh name to a younger audience.</p>\n<h2>Why a wide phone?</h2>\n<p>Tall screens are good for scrolling. They are poor for nearly everything else. A 16:9 video on a tall phone leaves black bars at both ends. A PDF page must be zoomed. Two apps side by side are ribbons. A spreadsheet shows three columns.</p>\n<p>A wide screen gives a page, a map or a video more room without folding. At 87mm across, the OSTA shows an A5 page at a comfortable size. Reading news, marking a document, following a map while riding in a taxi and watching a film all improve. In some ways it offers half of what a foldable offers, without a hinge and at less weight.</p>\n<h2>And why not?</h2>\n<p>Hands. The average adult can wrap fingers around about 75mm. Above 80mm, one-handed typing becomes a stretch, and 87mm is wider than any ordinary phone sold in Nepal. You will use it with two hands, like a small tablet. Trouser pockets designed for narrow phones may object. Cases and protectors will be scarce. And apps made for tall screens may look stretched until developers adapt them.</p>\n<p>People with large hands, readers and those who keep a phone in a bag will not mind. Commuters holding a handle on a bus will.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging in a thin body</h2>\n<p>Seven thousand milliampere-hours in a 6.68mm body shows how far silicon-carbon cells have come. A conventional battery of that capacity would need a phone two millimetres thicker. Wired charging at 66W and wireless at 50W are both quick, though you need Huawei\'s own chargers to see those figures. For a screen of this size, a day and a half of ordinary use is a fair expectation.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Huawei has made some of the best phone cameras of the past decade. A 200MP main sensor with a 50MP periscope suggests that the OSTA is not a cheap experiment. We have no measurements and no pictures of our own to judge, so anything more would be guesswork.</p>\n<h2>The software question</h2>\n<p>Huawei phones sold in China run HarmonyOS, and those sold elsewhere run EMUI. Neither includes Google\'s services. In practice that means no Play Store, no built-in Google Maps, Gmail or YouTube apps, and banking or wallet apps that may refuse to start. There are workarounds, including Huawei\'s AppGallery, the Aurora Store and tools that imitate Google\'s services. Some people live with them happily. For a Nepali user who depends on eSewa, Khalti, a bank\'s app and Google Maps, it is a risk to check in advance, app by app.</p>\n<p>The newest Chinese version, HarmonyOS without Android inside, cannot run Android apps at all except through a compatibility layer. A phone imported from China with that system would be a poor choice here, whatever its hardware.</p>\n<h2>Will it come to Nepal?</h2>\n<p>Huawei has not announced it. The company sells watches, bands, earbuds and some other devices here, and its phones have been rare for years. If the OSTA line goes global, it would probably appear first in the Middle East and South-East Asia. Importers may bring a few units. If you see one, remember that it will have no local warranty, must be registered in MDMS, and may miss some of the network bands used by Ncell and NTC. Screens for such a shape will be impossible to find on New Road.</p>\n<h2>What to buy if you like the idea</h2>\n<p><strong>A book-style foldable.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold-7\">Galaxy Z Fold 7</a> gives you an ordinary phone outside and a wide screen inside, with Google services and service centres. It costs more than three times what a good phone costs.</p>\n<p><strong>A large ordinary phone.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> with its 6.9-inch screen is cheap and good for maps. It is still tall.</p>\n<p><strong>A small tablet.</strong> The 8.7-inch <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Galaxy Tab A11</a> at about Rs 22,000 reads pages and plays films better than any phone, beside whichever phone you already own. For most readers it is the practical answer.</p>\n<h2>Big batteries you can buy now</h2>\n<p>If the 7,000mAh battery is what caught your eye, the Nepali market is full of them. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> costs about Rs 40,000, the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a> about Rs 60,000, and the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> reaches 10,000mAh at about Rs 70,000. All have Google\'s services and local warranty.</p>\n<h2>A short history of odd shapes</h2>\n<p>Wide phones have been tried before. BlackBerry\'s square Passport of 2014 was loved by a few and bought by fewer. LG made phones with a 4:3 screen. Microsoft\'s Surface Duo folded two wide screens together. Each failed for a similar reason: apps were not designed for them, and people would not hold them in public for long. Two things are different today. Foldables have pushed developers to support many shapes, and video is what most people do on a phone. Perhaps the time has come. Perhaps it will join the museum.</p>\n<h2>What it would need to succeed here</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Google services, or dependable access to banking and wallet apps.</li>\n<li>A price below Rs 1 lakh.</li>\n<li>Screens and parts held by a distributor.</li>\n<li>A case with a grip.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first point is political and unlikely to change soon. That alone makes a large launch in Nepal improbable.</p>\n<h2>Huawei products that make sense in Nepal</h2>\n<p>It would be unfair to leave the impression that Huawei has nothing for you. Its watches are among the best fitness watches sold here, as our <a href=\"/reviews/huawei-watch-gt-6-review-nepal\">Watch GT 6 review</a> explains. The <a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Band 10</a> is an excellent cheap tracker, and the FreeBuds earbuds are good. They work with any Android phone and with iPhones, and do not depend on Google\'s services on Huawei\'s side.</p>\n<h2>Our view</h2>\n<p>We like that somebody is still experimenting. A phone that treats reading and video as its main work is an honest idea in 2026. As a purchase for Nepal, the OSTA 1 Pro is for collectors with a second phone in the other pocket. Enjoy it as news, and watch whether Xiaomi, OPPO or vivo copy the shape. If one of them does it with Google services and a service centre in Pokhara, we will be the first to try it with two hands.</p>\n<h2>How to imagine the size without one</h2>\n<p>Cut a piece of card 144 by 87 millimetres and carry it for a day. Put it in every pocket you use. Hold it as if you were typing a message while standing. Lay a page of a book under it and see how many lines fit. This costs nothing, and it answers the only question that matters about a wide phone. Most people discover within an hour whether the shape suits them.</p>','mobile',NULL,1,0,'Huawei has launched OSTA, a new sub-brand, with the OSTA 1 Pro: a 6.39-inch wide OLED, 200MP camera, 50MP periscope and 7,000mAh battery. Nepal launch not con',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(45,20,'Xiaomi 18 Pro Brings the First Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 6 and Dual 200MP Cameras','xiaomi-18-pro-snapdragon-8-elite-gen-6-launch','<!--depth-->\n<p>Xiaomi has launched the 18 Pro and the larger 18 Pro Max, starting in Europe. The 18 Pro is the first phone with Qualcomm\'s Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 6, the company\'s first flagship chip on a 2nm process. It is also something rarer in 2026: a top phone with a 6.4-inch screen and a 6,000mAh battery. Below is what has been announced, what it means in practice, and whether a buyer in Nepal should wait.</p>\n<h2>The announced specification</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Xiaomi 18 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Display</td><td>6.4-inch LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, up to 4,000 nits, hardware privacy mode</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chip</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 6 (2nm)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Leica-tuned, two 200MP sensors, 3.2x periscope with 10cm close focus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery</td><td>6,000mAh silicon-carbon, 90W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Body</td><td>8.46mm and 198g (Cosmic Blue 8.56mm and 203g)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>What 2nm does for you</h2>\n<p>A smaller process lets a chip do the same work with less energy, or more work with the same. Phone makers usually take some of each. In daily life the first is what you notice: cooler video calls, longer navigation, a battery that lasts an hour or two more. Peak speed rises as well, and it is wasted on messaging. Gamers get something in between, as the phone can hold a high frame rate for longer before heat forces it to slow down.</p>\n<p>Compared with the Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 in this year\'s flagships, the step should be pleasant and not dramatic. Compared with an 8 Gen 2 or older, it will be large. Our explainer on <a href=\"/news/snapdragon-8-gen-4-debut-qualcomm-summit-2024\">Qualcomm\'s chip names</a> puts them in order.</p>\n<h2>A small flagship with a big battery</h2>\n<p>Compact phones have always asked for a sacrifice. The Galaxy S26 has 4,300mAh, the iPhone 17 less than that on paper. At 6,000mAh, a phone of nearly the same size would last perhaps a third longer. For people who gave up small phones because they died at five in the afternoon, this is the interesting part of the announcement.</p>\n<p>The trick is silicon-carbon. It stores more energy in the same volume. Early concerns about ageing have eased as brands have shipped tens of millions of such cells, though we would still use the 80 per cent charge limit on nights at home.</p>\n<h2>Two 200MP cameras</h2>\n<p>Megapixels by themselves mean little. What a 200MP sensor behind a periscope gives is freedom to crop. At 3.2x the lens frames a portrait nicely. By taking the middle of the sensor, the phone can offer something like 6x with good detail, and more with processing. The ability to focus at 10cm turns the same lens into a macro camera, useful for flowers, food, documents and jewellery, and far better than the 2MP macro lenses on cheap phones.</p>\n<p>Leica\'s part is tuning: contrast, colour and vignetting that many photographers like. You can switch between an authentic and a vibrant look. People who want bright, flattering faces straight from the camera may prefer vivo or Samsung.</p>\n<h2>The privacy display</h2>\n<p>At a touch, the screen can narrow its viewing angles, so that the person beside you sees little. Samsung introduced a similar idea with the S26 Ultra. On a full microbus, in an office with an open plan or in a bank queue, it is more useful than it sounds. It may lower brightness a little when on. Being built into the panel, it can be applied only to certain apps or parts of the screen, and it can be turned off, which a stick-on film cannot.</p>\n<h2>Nepal price and timing</h2>\n<p>There is no official price. The catalogue entry on our <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-18-pro\">product page</a> carries an estimate of about Rs 1,89,999, and it will be replaced when Xiaomi Nepal announces one. The present flagship here, the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a>, costs Rs 1,99,999. Xiaomi has brought its flagships to Nepal some months after their global launch, in limited numbers and sometimes only one version. It has also skipped models, so nothing is certain.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait for it?</h2>\n<p><strong>Yes, if</strong> you want a compact flagship with a strong battery, your phone still works, and you can live with uncertainty for several months.</p>\n<p><strong>No, if</strong> you need a phone this season. Good compact choices exist. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">OnePlus 13s</a> at about Rs 1,20,000 has a 6.32-inch screen and 5,850mAh. The <a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a> at about Rs 1,30,000 has a 6.57-inch screen, 7,000mAh and a telephoto. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> has the best support.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi 17T and 17 Ultra in the meantime</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17t\">Xiaomi 17T</a> at about Rs 93,000 brings Leica tuning and strong performance for half the price of a flagship. The 17 Ultra is the camera specialist, with a one-inch class sensor, and <a href=\"/reviews/xiaomi-17-ultra-review-nepal\">our review</a> explains who it suits. When the 18 series arrives, both should fall. If you want the Ultra, patience will be rewarded.</p>\n<h2>Grey imports from Europe and China</h2>\n<p>Units will appear in Kathmandu before any official launch. European versions have Google services and many languages, and Chinese ones have HyperOS for China, with Google services that can be enabled, notifications that arrive late and no local warranty. Network bands differ. MDMS registration is needed in every case. For a phone near two lakhs, the saving does not cover the risk for most people. If you go ahead, check the model code on the box, test VoLTE with your SIM in the shop, and confirm that the IMEI is registered before paying.</p>\n<h2>HyperOS: the part that has not changed</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s software is fast, full of options and busy. Expect extra apps to remove, recommendations to switch off and permissions to review. Support for flagships has improved to several Android versions and around six years of patches. Samsung and Google still lead. If you keep a phone for four years, Xiaomi\'s promise is enough.</p>\n<h2>How it may compare with the Galaxy S27 and iPhone 18</h2>\n<p>The small Galaxy S27 is expected in the first months of 2027 with the same generation of chip in some regions. It will have Samsung\'s support, and reports suggest only a modest battery increase. The iPhone 18 is expected in spring. On hardware, the Xiaomi looks stronger than both. On resale, service and longevity, it will be weaker. That is the usual trade, only with a better battery than usual.</p>\n<h2>What we would like to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>How quickly it slows in a forty-minute game.</li>\n<li>Whether the privacy mode dims the screen outdoors.</li>\n<li>How the telephoto handles indoor light.</li>\n<li>Whether Nepal will get 12GB/256GB or only a costlier version.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We have not used the phone, and we will not pretend otherwise. When there is a local unit and a local price, this page and the product page will be updated.</p>\n<h2>In short</h2>\n<p>A compact body, a large battery, two high-resolution cameras and the newest chip make the 18 Pro one of the most appealing announcements of the year. For Nepal it is, for now, a reason to watch prices of the 17 series, and a hint about what every flagship will look like in 2027. If small phones are your thing, be glad that someone is still making them properly.</p>\n<h2>Pro or Pro Max?</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi launched both, and the larger one will tempt people who watch a lot of video. The smaller is the rarer thing. There are many excellent 6.9-inch flagships and very few at 6.4 inches with a large battery and two high-resolution cameras. If both reach Nepal, hold them one after the other with your usual grip. People who choose a compact phone are often surprised by how much more they use it while walking, on a bus or with a bag in the other hand.</p>','mobile','gadgets/2026/xiaomi-18-pro.webp',1,0,'Xiaomi 18 Pro is the first phone with Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 6 (2nm): 6.4-inch privacy display, dual 200MP cameras and a 6,000mAh battery. What we know for Ne',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-20 18:15:00','2026-11-20 18:15:00'),(46,20,'OPPO Reno 17 Launches With an 8,000mAh Battery and Three 50MP Cameras','oppo-reno-17-launched-8000mah-battery','<!--depth-->\n<p>OPPO has launched the Reno 17 in China. The Reno line has always been about portraits and slimness, and the new model keeps both while adding something nobody expected from it: an 8,000mAh battery in a phone with a 6.56-inch screen. Here is what has been announced, how it differs from the Renos sold in Nepal, and what to do if you were about to buy one.</p>\n<h2>Announced specification</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>OPPO Reno 17</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Display</td><td>6.56-inch 1.5K flat panel, 120Hz</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chip</td><td>Snapdragon 7 Gen 4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory and storage</td><td>Up to 12GB and 1TB</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rear cameras</td><td>Three 50MP cameras, including a 3.5x periscope; 4K at 60 frames</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery</td><td>8,000mAh, 80W wired charging</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Software and build</td><td>ColorOS 17, metal frame, glass back</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>There is also a special edition with a three-dimensional cat on the back, and an accessory called the OPPO Bubble, a small magnetic screen that clips behind the phone so you can see yourself while using the main cameras for a selfie. It is sold separately.</p>\n<h2>Why 8,000mAh in this size matters</h2>\n<p>Look at what is on sale. Phones with 8,000mAh or more, such as the Honor X9d or the Redmi Note 17 Pro, are large, with screens of 6.8 inches and weights above 210 grams. A 6.56-inch phone usually has 5,000 to 6,500mAh. Smaller screens also use less power. Put the two together and you have a phone that should last two days for many people and still fit a small hand.</p>\n<p>This is the direction of the whole industry. Silicon-carbon batteries are becoming denser every year, and it is reasonable to expect mid-range phones in 2027 to make today\'s 5,000mAh models look old.</p>\n<h2>Three real cameras</h2>\n<p>Many phones at this level have one good camera and two decorations. Here all three are 50MP, and one is a 3.5x periscope. For portraits that is the most useful lens on a phone. A face photographed from two metres with a longer lens has natural proportions, and the background falls away without artificial blur. The ultra wide at 50MP should hold detail in groups and landscapes, where 8MP lenses turn faces to mush.</p>\n<p>If OPPO brings its colour tuning from the Find series, the Reno 17 could be the portrait phone to beat below flagship prices. We say could because no one outside the launch has used it for long.</p>\n<h2>The chip: sensible</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 is a solid mid-range part. Everyday use is smooth, games run at medium to high, and efficiency is good. It does not compete with the 8s Gen 4 in a POCO F7 or the 8 Gen 5 in a OnePlus 15R. If Nepal\'s price lands near that of the Reno 16, at about Rs 1,10,000, the phone will be slower than rivals by a wide margin. Reno buyers rarely mind, and heavy gamers should know.</p>\n<h2>What is on sale in Nepal now</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16-5g\">OPPO Reno 16</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,09,999</td><td>6.32 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 18...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 6700 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPE...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16f-5g\">OPPO Reno 16F</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 14...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Energy (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPE...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-15-f\">OPPO Reno 15F</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;63,999</td><td>6.59 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 14...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,25,999</td><td>6.78 inches LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>Si-Ca 7500 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16-5g\">Reno 16</a> is the direct predecessor, and the <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16f-5g\">Reno 16F</a> is the cheaper branch with a 7,000mAh battery. The <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-15-f\">Reno 15 F</a> is older and cheaper still, and <a href=\"/reviews/oppo-reno-15-f-review-nepal\">our review</a> discusses its confusing specification. And for not much more than a Reno 16, the <a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">Find X9 Pro</a> offers a proper flagship chip and camera at about Rs 1,26,000. That is a strange overlap in OPPO\'s own range, and a good one for buyers.</p>\n<h2>When might it reach Nepal?</h2>\n<p>No date has been given. OPPO has usually brought the Reno series to Nepal a few months after China, following India. Models change on the way. The global Reno may have a smaller battery for shipping reasons, a different chip, or a missing camera. In past generations the Indian and Chinese versions have differed noticeably. So wait for the specification of the Nepali unit before getting attached to 8,000mAh.</p>\n<h2>Why batteries sometimes shrink on the way</h2>\n<p>Rules for transporting lithium cells become stricter when a single cell exceeds 20 watt-hours. At about 3.8 volts, that is a little over 5,200mAh. Makers get around it with two cells in one pack, which adds cost and takes space. For Europe and the United States in particular, brands often choose a smaller battery. South Asian models have more often kept the full size. Nepal usually follows the Indian version.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait?</h2>\n<p><strong>Wait if</strong> you like compact phones, take portraits and want long battery life, and your current phone can last until spring.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not wait if</strong> you need a phone now. For portraits, the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> already has a 3.5x periscope at Rs 69,999. For battery, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> has 8,300mAh at Rs 54,999. For a compact phone with a big battery and a strong camera, the <a href=\"/products/honor-600\">Honor 600</a> at about Rs 85,000 has 7,000mAh and 200MP. For OPPO\'s own best camera, the Find X9 Pro.</p>\n<h2>What it means for Reno 16 prices</h2>\n<p>When the successor lands, dealers will want to clear the Reno 16. OPPO shops are generous with exchange bonuses, accessories and cash discounts at such times. At Rs 1,10,000 the Reno 16 is hard to justify against the Find X9 Pro. At Rs 85,000 to 90,000 it becomes a nice portrait phone. If you like it, let them know that you know about the 17.</p>\n<h2>The Bubble and other ideas</h2>\n<p>A clip-on screen for selfies is a clever answer to an old problem: the best cameras are on the back, and you cannot see yourself. Vloggers might love it. It must be charged, carried and paid for, and a small mirror does part of the job for Rs 100. If it ever comes to Nepal, try it before buying.</p>\n<h2>ColorOS 17</h2>\n<p>OPPO\'s software is one of the smoothest on Android, and recent versions have good privacy tools and links with Windows and Mac. It includes bundled apps and a feed that you should switch off. Update promises on the Reno series are moderate, commonly a few Android versions with longer security patches. Ask for the figure for the model sold here.</p>\n<h2>Service and resale</h2>\n<p>OPPO has an established service network and reasonable parts prices. Resale is average. Renos lose value faster than Samsung\'s phones and more slowly than POCOs. A curved or unusual screen costs more to replace, and a flat panel, as on the Reno 17, is good news for repair bills and for cheap protectors.</p>\n<h2>Our view</h2>\n<p>The Reno 17 fixes the two complaints we have had about the line: ordinary batteries and weak secondary cameras. If the global model keeps them and the price stays near Rs 1 lakh, it will be the Reno to recommend. If the battery shrinks and the price grows, it will be another pretty phone. We will add it to the catalogue with confirmed details when OPPO Nepal announces it. Until then, see <a href=\"/guides/oppo-phone-price-in-nepal\">current OPPO prices in Nepal</a>.</p>','mobile',NULL,1,1,'OPPO Reno 17 launched in China with Snapdragon 7 Gen 4, triple 50MP cameras with a 3.5x periscope and an 8,000mAh battery. Nepal launch expected later.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-19 18:15:00','2026-11-19 18:15:00'),(47,20,'REDMAGIC 12 Pro Gaming Phone Is Coming to Nepal in December','redmagic-12-pro-coming-to-nepal-december','<!--depth-->\n<p>The REDMAGIC 12 Pro, a gaming phone from Nubia, has been launched globally and is expected in Nepal in December. It is not a normal phone with a sporty sticker. It has a fan inside, a screen without a camera hole and a chip pushed past the speeds other makers allow. For a certain kind of player it will be the most desirable object in the country. For everybody else it is a lesson in what such devices give up. Here is how to tell which you are.</p>\n<h2>What has been announced</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>REDMAGIC 12 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Chip</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Extreme Gen 6 (2nm), up to 5.0GHz, with RedCore R5 gaming chip</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cooling</td><td>Dual-loop liquid system and a 25,500rpm internal fan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Display</td><td>6.85-inch 1.5K AMOLED, 185Hz, under-display front camera</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery</td><td>8,400mAh, 80W wired and wireless charging</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory</td><td>Up to 16GB and 1TB</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Dual 50MP on the back</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The price in Nepal has not been announced. Gaming phones of this class have generally landed between Rs 1.1 and 1.6 lakh depending on memory, so plan around that and wait for the official number.</p>\n<h2>Why a fan inside a phone?</h2>\n<p>Every phone is fast for five minutes. After that, heat builds up and the chip slows to protect itself. In a long match you see it as dropping frames, dimming screen and warm fingers. A vapour chamber spreads heat, but it has nowhere to go except your hand. A fan moves it out of the body. The result is a frame rate in the fortieth minute close to that in the first. For competitive players that consistency is worth more than a higher peak.</p>\n<p>The costs are real. You can hear the fan in a quiet room. It takes space. Dust enters, and Kathmandu has plenty, so expect to clean the vents with a soft brush. Water resistance is limited in a phone with holes for air. Ask about the exact IP rating before taking it out in the rain.</p>\n<h2>185Hz: useful or not?</h2>\n<p>Few games reach that rate. Popular shooters on mobile are capped at 90, 120 or, in some cases, 144 frames. The larger benefit is touch sampling, how often the screen checks your finger, which makes aiming feel direct. If you play at 60 frames today, 120 will impress you. Above 144 it is for the specification sheet.</p>\n<h2>No hole in the screen</h2>\n<p>Hiding the front camera under the display leaves the picture unbroken. It is lovely in games and films. The camera itself suffers, since it looks through pixels. Selfies and video calls are softer than on any phone at half the price. If you stream with your face on screen, use another camera.</p>\n<h2>Who should consider it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who play two or more hours a day and compete in tournaments.</li>\n<li>Streamers who run a game, a recorder and chat at the same time.</li>\n<li>Emulation fans who need sustained speed.</li>\n<li>Anyone who finds the idea delightful and has another phone for photos.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Casual players. An hour of Free Fire does not need a fan.</li>\n<li>Photographers. Two 50MP cameras without a telephoto are average here.</li>\n<li>People who want long software support. Nubia\'s record is shorter than Samsung\'s, Google\'s or OnePlus\'s.</li>\n<li>Users in towns without service. Parts for a niche phone take weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sensible alternatives</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;98,499</td><td>6.83-inch AMOLED, 165Hz, Dolby Vision, HDR10+</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,400mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">Poco F8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,04,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HBM), 3...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">Poco X8 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;94,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 20...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500s (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 8500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">Realme GT 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>6.79-inch QHD+ AMOLED (1440×3136), 144Hz, 7,0...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,000mAh; 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 17...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">POCO F8 Pro</a> with a Snapdragon 8 Elite at about Rs 1,05,000 gives most of the speed with a better balance. The <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">POCO X8 Pro Max</a> has an 8,500mAh battery for long sessions. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a> is the all-rounder with a 165Hz screen. The <a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">Realme GT 8 Pro</a> adds a serious camera. And the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> at Rs 69,999 runs every game well. With any of them, a clip-on cooler for Rs 1,500 to 3,000 provides a good part of the REDMAGIC\'s advantage.</p>\n<h2>The same money on other things</h2>\n<p>For Rs 1.3 lakh you could also buy a PlayStation 5 Digital Edition with several games, or an entry gaming laptop with a used phone beside it. Mobile esports have their own appeal and community, and if those are your games, a phone is right. If you simply like games, think about the bigger screen first. See <a href=\"/news/ps5-price-in-nepal-november-2026\">PS5 prices</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptops</a>.</p>\n<h2>Which games will notice?</h2>\n<p><strong>PUBG Mobile and BGMI.</strong> At 120 frames, cooling matters. PUBG Mobile itself has had a complicated legal history in Nepal, so follow the rules in force.</p>\n<p><strong>Genshin Impact, Wuthering Waves, Zenless Zone Zero.</strong> Heavy titles where sustained speed shows clearly.</p>\n<p><strong>Call of Duty Mobile, Free Fire.</strong> Any good phone is enough.</p>\n<p><strong>Mobile Legends.</strong> Network quality matters far more than the phone.</p>\n<p>That last remark applies widely. With 80 milliseconds of delay and packet loss, no chip will save you. Use Wi-Fi on 5GHz near the router, or a good 4G cell away from crowds, and close downloads.</p>\n<h2>Buying an official unit</h2>\n<p>If a distributor launches it in December, choose that route. Imported REDMAGIC phones have been sold in Nepal for years without warranty, sometimes with Chinese software. Check that the phone has the global ROM, that the IMEI is in MDMS, that VoLTE works with your SIM, and who will replace the fan if it fails. A moving part is the first thing to wear out in any electronic device.</p>\n<h2>Living with it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use bypass charging in long sessions at home, so that the battery is not heated while you play.</li>\n<li>Clean the vents monthly.</li>\n<li>Set the fan to automatic, and off at night.</li>\n<li>Do not block the intake with a case that is not made for it.</li>\n<li>Turn off the RGB light if you want a little more battery and a lot more dignity in class.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>With 8,400mAh it should run four to five hours of demanding games, or two days of ordinary use. Fast charging at 80W fills it in under an hour with the correct charger. Wireless charging at the same figure requires a special stand, so count on the cable.</p>\n<h2>What we do not know yet</h2>\n<p>Local price, memory versions, warranty terms, the number of updates, and how it behaves in a hot Terai room. We have not used one. When an official price arrives we will add a product page with the full specification and the date.</p>\n<h2>Our advice</h2>\n<p>If you read the list of who should consider it and recognised yourself, wait for the official unit and go and hold it. If you were attracted by the numbers, buy a POCO or a OnePlus, add a clip-on cooler, and put the Rs 30,000 to 50,000 you save into a better router, a pair of low-latency earphones and something sweet for the family, who will hear less of the fan.</p>','mobile',NULL,1,0,'REDMAGIC 12 Pro launches in Nepal in December: Snapdragon 8 Elite Extreme Gen 6, 185Hz AMOLED, liquid cooling with a fan and an 8,400mAh battery.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-19 18:15:00','2026-11-19 18:15:00'),(48,20,'iPhone Air 2 Could Add a Second Camera and Stay Just 5.2mm Thin','iphone-air-2-rumours-dual-camera','<!--depth-->\n<p>The first iPhone Air impressed everyone who held it and puzzled many who considered buying it. It was astonishingly thin, and it had a single rear camera at a price close to that of a Pro. Rumours about its successor suggest that Apple heard the complaint. The iPhone Air 2 is said to gain a second camera while staying about 5.2mm thin. Nothing has been confirmed. Here is what is being reported, how far to believe it, and what it means if you are choosing an iPhone in Nepal this winter.</p>\n<h2>What the rumours say</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Two 48MP rear cameras, adding an ultra wide to the main camera.</li>\n<li>An A20 Pro chip with 12GB of memory.</li>\n<li>A 6.55-inch OLED at 120Hz, with a smaller Dynamic Island.</li>\n<li>The same 5.2mm thickness, with a titanium frame.</li>\n<li>A launch around spring 2027, beside the iPhone 18 and 18e.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dummy models used by case makers show two openings on the camera bar, and that makes the second camera the most credible item. Chips and dates are plausible. Screen details are uncertain.</p>\n<h2>How thin is 5.2mm?</h2>\n<p>Stack three one-rupee coins and you are near it. The current phone is about 5.6mm, the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> is close to 8mm, and the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> a little over 7mm. In the hand, the difference is obvious at once. In a pocket, you forget it. With a case, which most people in Nepal use from the first hour, a good part of the magic disappears. That is the Air\'s central problem: it is designed to be touched, and priced so that you dare not.</p>\n<h2>What thinness costs</h2>\n<p><strong>Battery.</strong> Less volume means less energy. The first Air manages a day for moderate users and struggles with heavy ones. An efficient 2nm chip would help. Physics still applies.</p>\n<p><strong>Cameras.</strong> A telephoto lens needs depth. There is no realistic way to put a periscope in such a body, so zoom will remain digital.</p>\n<p><strong>Heat.</strong> There is less metal to soak it up, so long games and 4K recording will slow it earlier than a Pro.</p>\n<p><strong>Sound.</strong> The first Air has a single speaker at the earpiece. Films in landscape sound one-sided.</p>\n<p><strong>Repairs.</strong> Thin phones are packed tightly, and glass on both sides is costly.</p>\n<h2>Why a second camera changes the story</h2>\n<p>An ultra wide lens is used more than people think. Family groups in a small room, temples, landscapes, close-ups with macro mode. Its absence was felt on every trip. With two 48MP cameras the Air would match the regular iPhone in most photography and come closer to justifying its price. It still would not replace a Pro for portraits and stages.</p>\n<h2>SIM: an important point for Nepal</h2>\n<p>The first iPhone Air has no physical SIM slot in any country. It uses eSIM only. If the Air 2 follows, you need an operator here that supports eSIM for your number, and you should understand how to move it to a new phone. Both major operators have introduced eSIM with their own procedures and fees. Ask before buying, and ask what happens if the phone breaks while you are travelling in a district without a service office. For people who swap SIM cards between phones, the Air is simply the wrong device.</p>\n<h2>What would it cost?</h2>\n<p>The present <a href=\"/products/iphone-air\">iPhone Air</a> is listed at Rs 1,90,699 here. Memory has become more expensive, and Apple\'s other models have been going up. It would be reasonable to expect something between Rs 1,95,000 and 2,15,000 for the base version. That is a guess, marked as such, and we have not put it in the catalogue as a price.</p>\n<h2>What else could you buy?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-air\">iPhone Air</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,90,699</td><td>6.5-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1260×2736, LT...</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>MagSafe wireless and wired charging; 4.5W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1206×2622, LT...</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>25W MagSafe, 15W Qi2; about 50% in 20 minutes...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,900</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Up to 36 hours video playback; 50W wired (abo...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Samsung Galaxy S26</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>6.3-inch FHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm) in most markets; Snapdragon...</td><td>12GB</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For about Rs 26,000 less than the Air, the iPhone 17 has two cameras, stereo speakers, a larger battery and the same 120Hz display. For about Rs 70,000 more, the 18 Pro has everything. The Air sits between them and is chosen by the heart. There is nothing wrong with that, as long as you know it.</p>\n<h2>Thin Android phones</h2>\n<p>Samsung tried the same idea with the Galaxy S25 Edge, and several Chinese brands have shown phones near 6mm with batteries of 5,500mAh or more, thanks to silicon-carbon cells. Sales of thin phones have been modest everywhere. If you like slimness and battery together, phones such as the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> at 7.5mm with 7,000mAh, or the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g\">Galaxy A57 5G</a> at 6.9mm, offer much of the feeling for far less.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait for the Air 2?</h2>\n<p><strong>Wait if</strong> you love the Air\'s design, were put off by the single camera, use eSIM already and can manage until spring.</p>\n<p><strong>Buy the current Air if</strong> you find it at a deep discount, say Rs 1,50,000 or less, and take mostly ordinary photos. When a successor arrives, prices of the first generation will fall further.</p>\n<p><strong>Forget about it if</strong> you need battery, zoom, loud stereo sound or a SIM slot.</p>\n<h2>Buying a used iPhone Air</h2>\n<p>A few are already on the second-hand market. Check battery health first, since a small battery that has lost a tenth of its capacity is felt at once. Look along the frame for bends with a straight edge. Test the eSIM by adding your own line. Confirm that Activation Lock is off and that parts are genuine in Settings, General, About. Look at the glass on both sides under a light.</p>\n<h2>Accessories</h2>\n<p>If you get one, resist thick cases. A thin aramid or silicone case with a raised edge around the camera bar keeps the point of the phone. MagSafe batteries designed for the Air add most of a charge and can be removed at home, and a small power bank does the same for less.</p>\n<h2>How reliable are these rumours?</h2>\n<p>At this stage before an iPhone launch, shapes are usually right, cameras mostly right, and chips right in the family but not in the details. Apple has also been known to cancel or delay models whose sales disappoint. There were reports of weak demand for the first Air. The second may come later than expected or not at all. Nobody should sell a working phone because of it.</p>\n<h2>Where it fits in Apple\'s plan</h2>\n<p>With the launch split in two, spring could bring the iPhone 18, the 18e and the Air 2, while autumn belongs to the Pro and the foldable. Some observers think that the Air was practice for the folding iPhone, which needs thin halves. That may be the truth of it: a product made to learn how to make another. Our article on the <a href=\"/news/iphone-18-iphone-18e-spring-2027-what-we-know\">iPhone 18 and 18e</a> covers the rest of the spring line.</p>\n<h2>Our view</h2>\n<p>A second camera would turn the Air from a beautiful compromise into a beautiful phone with ordinary limits. It would still be for people who value how a thing feels over what it does. In Nepal, with eSIM, service costs and the price, that is a small group. If you are in it, spring should be interesting. If you are not, the iPhone 17 is cheaper, louder and lasts longer, and nobody at dinner will measure it.</p>','rumors',NULL,1,4,'iPhone Air 2 rumours: dual 48MP cameras, A20 Pro with 12GB RAM, 6.55-inch 120Hz OLED and a 5.2mm body. What leaked dummies show and when it may launch.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(49,20,'Galaxy S27 Pro and S27 Ultra Leak: New Camera Layout, and the Pro Skips the S Pen','galaxy-s27-pro-s27-ultra-design-leak','<!--depth-->\n<p>Design sketches said to show two of Samsung\'s 2027 flagships have been shared by a well-known tipster. If they are accurate, Samsung will add a Pro to the Galaxy S family for the first time, and change the look of the cameras on the back. Samsung has said nothing. Here is what the sketches suggest, why a Pro would make sense, and how a buyer in Nepal might use the information.</p>\n<h2>What the sketches show</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A compact cluster of three lenses on both phones, replacing the separate rings Samsung has used since the S22 Ultra.</li>\n<li>An S27 Ultra with a 50MP periscope at 5x optical zoom and an S Pen.</li>\n<li>An S27 Pro with a 10MP telephoto at 3x and no pen.</li>\n<li>A family of four: S27, S27+, S27 Pro and S27 Ultra.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sketches are drawn from dimensions given to case makers or from files seen by someone in the supply chain. They are often right about where things are, and less so about what those things can do. A lens drawn in the right place tells you nothing about the sensor behind it.</p>\n<h2>Why add a Pro?</h2>\n<p>Look at the present range from a customer\'s point of view. The S26 is small and simple. The S26+ is the same phone, larger. The Ultra is enormous, with a pen that many owners take out twice. Between the Plus and the Ultra is a gap of about Rs 50,000 and one missing idea: the best camera and chip in a phone that fits a normal hand, without a pen.</p>\n<p>Apple has sold that idea for years as the iPhone Pro, and Chinese brands sell it as the Pro below the Ultra. Samsung\'s customers have had to choose between too little and too much. A Pro would answer them.</p>\n<h2>The 10MP telephoto is the doubt</h2>\n<p>If the sketch and the notes are right, the Pro would use a 10MP 3x camera similar to the one in recent S and S+ models. That would be a disappointment. Rivals at this price have 50MP or 200MP telephotos with larger sensors, which hold detail indoors and allow closer focusing. A Pro without a better telephoto than the base phone would be a Pro in name. We hope the detail is wrong.</p>\n<h2>The camera layout</h2>\n<p>Separate rings gave Samsung phones a clean, recognisable back. They also collected dust between the lenses and made the phone rock on a table. A single cluster or bar can be stiffer, easier to clean and steadier on a desk. Some will miss the old look. In a case, which is how most phones live in Nepal, nobody will notice after a week.</p>\n<h2>What the Pro might cost in Nepal</h2>\n<p>We do not have a reliable figure and will not print one as fact. Reasoning from the present ladder, with the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">S26</a> at Rs 1,39,999 and the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">S26 Ultra</a> at Rs 2,24,999, a Pro would sit somewhere around Rs 1.8 to 2 lakh. That is precisely where the <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> and <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> are sold. Samsung would be relying on software, service, resale and seven years of updates against larger sensors and batteries.</p>\n<h2>And the Ultra</h2>\n<p>For the Ultra, the more interesting reports concern the battery, said to grow to about 5,700mAh, and the 2nm chip. We keep those in a separate article: <a href=\"/news/galaxy-s27-ultra-leaks-launch-date-nepal-price\">what the leaks say about the Galaxy S27 Ultra</a>. The design sketches add that the pen stays and that the corners may be rounder.</p>\n<h2>What the pen is worth</h2>\n<p>The S Pen occupies a tunnel inside the Ultra that could hold battery. Samsung could give Ultra owners perhaps 500mAh by removing it. The company keeps it since a devoted group buys the phone for nothing else: site engineers who sign drawings, teachers who mark, designers, traders, and people who type Devanagari faster by handwriting. If you are not one of them, a Pro without a pen is a more honest phone for you.</p>\n<h2>How to decide this winter</h2>\n<p><strong>You want the biggest Samsung and the pen.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">S25 Ultra</a> at about Rs 1,84,999 is good value today. The S26 Ultra will fall after the launch. Waiting pays.</p>\n<p><strong>You want a smaller flagship.</strong> The S26 is very good and limited by its battery. If the Pro is real, it may be what you were missing. Three or four months of patience will tell.</p>\n<p><strong>You want the best camera under Rs 2 lakh.</strong> Do not wait for Samsung. Look at vivo, Xiaomi and OPPO now.</p>\n<p><strong>You need a phone this week.</strong> Buy one. Rumours do not make calls.</p>\n<h2>Reading a leak like a professional</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Who said it?</strong> Some tipsters have a long record. Even they are right perhaps two times in three.</li>\n<li><strong>What is it based on?</strong> Parts and certification beat drawings, and drawings beat \"I have heard\".</li>\n<li><strong>Does a second source agree?</strong> Independently, and not by quoting the first.</li>\n<li><strong>How close is the launch?</strong> Accuracy rises sharply in the last six weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Who benefits?</strong> Some leaks are advertising.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>By those measures, the new camera layout is likely, the existence of a Pro is probable, and the camera specification is a coin toss.</p>\n<h2>What a four-phone family means for shops</h2>\n<p>More models make stock harder to manage. In Nepal, distributors may bring only two or three of the four at first, probably the S27 and the Ultra. The Plus has been the least popular here for years, and a Pro could replace it on local shelves. If you want a particular model, ask a dealer in January and leave a small refundable deposit, with the terms in writing.</p>\n<h2>Trade-in strategy</h2>\n<p>Phone values drop in the two months after a launch. If you own an S23 Ultra or S24 Ultra and plan to move, get quotations in January and compare Samsung\'s exchange bonus during the pre-order period. That bonus is usually the best of the year. Clean the phone, replace a cracked protector, and keep the box and bill.</p>\n<h2>Other phones expected around then</h2>\n<p>The first half of 2027 should also bring the regular iPhone 18 and 18e, several Xiaomi 18 models for wider markets, and the next vivo and OPPO flagships. Competition at around Rs 1.5 to 2 lakh will be unusually strong. That is good for patience. See <a href=\"/upcoming-launches\">upcoming launches</a> for those with more certain dates.</p>\n<h2>What we would like Samsung to do</h2>\n<p>Give the Pro a telephoto with a larger sensor and a 5,500mAh battery, keep it under 190 grams, include a case in the box and price it under Rs 1.8 lakh in Nepal. It would be the easiest Samsung flagship to recommend in years. If it arrives with a 10MP telephoto and 4,700mAh at Rs 2 lakh, people will buy the Plus in their thousands, or the vivo.</p>\n<h2>Summary</h2>\n<p>A Pro is coming, probably. The back will change, probably. The pen stays in the Ultra. Prices are unknown. Use the information to time a purchase, and do not let a drawing decide it for you.</p>\n<h2>A quick guide for current owners</h2>\n<p><strong>S24 and S25 owners:</strong> nothing here asks you to upgrade. <strong>S22 and S23 owners:</strong> a Pro with a better battery could be a fine replacement, so wait for the announcement before spending on repairs beyond a battery. <strong>Note and S21 Ultra owners:</strong> updates are ending. If the pen matters, the discounted S25 Ultra is the calm choice today. <strong>A-series owners who dream of a flagship:</strong> a year-old S26 in the summer of 2027 will probably be the best value of them all.</p>','rumors',NULL,1,4,'Leaked sketches show the Galaxy S27 Pro and S27 Ultra with a new compact camera cluster. The Ultra gets a 50MP 5x periscope; the Pro a 10MP 3x and no S Pen.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-17 18:15:00','2026-11-17 18:15:00'),(50,20,'Redmi Note 18 5G Tipped as a Note 17 5G With a Bigger Battery','redmi-note-18-5g-bigger-battery-rumour','<!--depth-->\n<p>The Redmi Note is the phone that much of Nepal buys. So even a small rumour about the next one travels fast. The latest says that the Redmi Note 18 5G will be very close to the Note 17 5G, with a larger battery as its main change. If that is true, it answers a practical question for anyone with Rs 48,000 in hand: buy now or wait? We think the answer is clear, and here is why.</p>\n<h2>What is being said</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A battery of 8,100mAh in the global model, up from 7,700mAh. A Chinese version could reach 8,888mAh.</li>\n<li>Charging unchanged at 45W.</li>\n<li>The same Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 chip, 6.99-inch FHD+ AMOLED and 50MP main camera.</li>\n<li>Possible sale as a POCO in some markets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It comes from a single tipster and has not been supported by certification papers so far. Xiaomi often prepares several versions of a phone and changes plans by region, so treat the numbers as a direction and no more.</p>\n<h2>The phone it would replace</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi Note 17 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.99 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1800 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 512GB UFS 2.2</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8, 26 mm, PDAF, 1/2.76&quot; sensor | 16MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Si-C Li-Ion 7700 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, HyperOS 3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, WiFi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.1, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>225 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>169.7 x 79.1 x 8.3 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-5g\">Redmi Note 17 5G</a> at Rs 47,999 is a big, long-lasting phone with an ordinary processor. <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-5g-review-nepal\">Our review</a> says as much. Its battery already outlasts nearly everything at the price. An increase of 400mAh is about five per cent, or roughly an hour of screen time. You would not notice without a stopwatch.</p>\n<h2>Why a lightly updated model makes sense to Xiaomi</h2>\n<p>Memory has become expensive. A maker can respond by raising the price or by reusing parts. Keeping the same screen, chip and camera means no new tooling, lower cost and a price that stays near the old one. Adding battery gives the advertisements a number. It is not exciting, but it is better than removing storage, which some rivals have done. Our article on <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">rising prices in 2026</a> has the background.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait?</h2>\n<p>If the report is right, no. Three reasons.</p>\n<p><strong>The gain is tiny.</strong> Same speed, same screen, same camera.</p>\n<p><strong>The wait may be long.</strong> Redmi Notes arrive in Nepal weeks or months after their first launch elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>The price may be higher.</strong> New stock carries new component costs. The outgoing phone, on the other hand, will probably be discounted.</p>\n<p>So the best use of this news is as a bargaining tool. Dealers know when a successor is coming. Ask for the Note 17 5G at Rs 43,000 to 45,000 with a tempered glass and case, or wait for the first week after the new model is announced.</p>\n<h2>Is the Note 17 5G right for you at all?</h2>\n<p>Its strengths are battery, screen size and IP protection. Its weakness is speed. The Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 is fine for messaging, video, maps and office work, and slow for games. If you play, consider others at the same price.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Redmi Note 17 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-C Li-Ion 7700 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50-pro\">Tecno Spark 50 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>6000mAh Li-Po, non-removable; 60W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-30\">Infinix GT 30</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;46,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7400 (4nm)</td><td>45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Motorola Edge 50 Fusion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;46,499</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 68W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> at about Rs 44,000 is in some ways the better phone: a faster chip, a 108MP stabilised camera and a brighter screen, with a smaller battery. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a> is quick. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> has long support. Choose the Note 17 5G only if endurance comes first.</p>\n<h2>More battery today</h2>\n<p>If endurance is everything, you do not need to wait for anyone.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>6.79-inch AMOLED (2640×1200), 120Hz, 6,000 ni...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse c...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;57,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K AMOLED (2772×1280), 120Hz, 3,5...</td><td>Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,340mAh; 67W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,50...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>6.8-inch quad-curved 1.5K AMOLED, 144Hz, 6,50...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reve...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> has 8,300mAh and drop protection at Rs 54,999. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> has 10,000mAh with 100W charging at Rs 69,999. The second is a different class of phone altogether and probably the best battery phone sold in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>Understanding battery numbers</h2>\n<p>A milliampere-hour figure tells you the size of the tank. How long it lasts depends on the engine and the driver. A bright 6.99-inch screen uses more than a 6.5-inch one. An old chip on weak signal uses more than a new one on good signal. As a rough guide for ordinary use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>5,000mAh: a day.</li>\n<li>6,000 to 6,500mAh: a day and a half.</li>\n<li>7,000 to 8,000mAh: close to two days.</li>\n<li>10,000mAh: two to three days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Heavy users should halve those. Beyond 8,000mAh, charging speed begins to matter more than capacity, because filling such a battery at 18W takes half the night. At 45W, 8,100mAh would need around an hour and a half.</p>\n<h2>Weight</h2>\n<p>Large batteries are heavy, and the Note 17 5G is already a substantial phone. Hold one for ten minutes as if you were reading in bed. If your wrist complains, a 190 gram phone with 6,000mAh will make you happier than a 230 gram one with 8,000.</p>\n<h2>Silicon-carbon and ageing</h2>\n<p>The new cells pack more energy by adding silicon to the negative electrode. Silicon swells slightly as it charges, and makers manage that with the structure of the material. Most promise that the battery keeps around 80 per cent of its capacity after a thousand or more cycles. With a two-day battery, a thousand cycles is over five years. Keep it cool, avoid leaving it empty, and use the charge limit at night.</p>\n<h2>What a true upgrade would look like</h2>\n<p>We would like to see the next Redmi Note with a faster chip of the Snapdragon 6 class, UFS 3.1 storage, 8GB as the minimum and the Note 15\'s camera. Put those into the Note 17\'s body and nobody would care about 400mAh. If the leak is right, that phone is not coming this time, at least not in the basic model. The Pro versions may be different.</p>\n<h2>The POCO connection</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi sells many phones twice, with a different badge and a different price. If a POCO version appears, compare it carefully. Sometimes it costs Rs 2,000 to 3,000 less with the same hardware and a brighter back. Service is the same in practice, as POCO phones are handled by Xiaomi\'s centres.</p>\n<h2>Official units</h2>\n<p>Redmi Notes are among the most imported phones across the open border. An Indian unit may be a few thousand rupees cheaper, without Nepali warranty and with extra steps for MDMS. Check the IMEI and the sticker of the distributor. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">This guide</a> shows how.</p>\n<h2>If you own an older Redmi Note</h2>\n<p>From a Note 10, 11 or 12, any phone mentioned here will feel new. From a Note 13 Pro or 14 Pro, the basic Note 17 or 18 may be a step down in speed and camera. Replace the battery for Rs 2,500 to 4,000 and wait for a Pro model on sale.</p>\n<h2>Our verdict on the rumour</h2>\n<p>It is believable and unimportant. The Note 18 5G, if this is it, will be a fine phone for the same people who like the Note 17 5G, at a similar or slightly higher price. Buy the current one at a discount, or better, look at the Note 15 5G for speed or the Pro Max for battery. We will add the new model to the catalogue when it has an official specification, and not before.</p>','rumors',NULL,1,4,'Redmi Note 18 5G is tipped to be a rebranded Note 17 5G with an 8,100mAh global battery (8,888mAh in China), Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 and 45W charging.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-17 18:15:00','2026-11-17 18:15:00'),(51,20,'Honor\'s 13,500mAh Phone Battery Passes Certification','honor-13500mah-battery-phone-certified','<!--depth-->\n<p>A Honor battery with a typical capacity of 13,500mAh has passed safety certification in China. Certification papers do not name a phone, but they do mean that a cell of that size is close to production. Five years ago this was the capacity of a power bank. Here is what is known, how such a battery fits in a pocket, and whether it would change anything for someone living in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>What is known</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The battery is rated at 13,300mAh, with a typical 13,500mAh.</li>\n<li>It appears under model number HB6674118ELW-C in China\'s 3C system.</li>\n<li>It is expected in a 7-inch mid-range phone, possibly from the Power series.</li>\n<li>Honor\'s earlier large models used 10,080mAh, 11,000mAh and 12,070mAh cells, and the company is said to be trying 14,000 to 15,000mAh.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no name, date, price or mention of any market outside China. Everything beyond the certificate is inference.</p>\n<h2>How can a phone hold that much?</h2>\n<p>Ordinary lithium-ion batteries use graphite for the negative electrode. Silicon can hold roughly ten times more lithium by weight, but it swells and cracks. The solution in use today mixes silicon with carbon in a structure that gives it room to expand. Each year the share of silicon has gone up, and the energy in a given volume with it. A phone from 2021 with 5,000mAh and one from 2026 with 8,000mAh can be the same size.</p>\n<p>At 13,500mAh the phone will still be large. Expect a 7-inch screen and a weight of 230 grams or more. Honor has been clever about thickness, so a body under 9mm is possible.</p>\n<h2>How long would it last?</h2>\n<p>Nobody outside Honor has measured it. We can reason from phones on sale. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a>, with 10,001mAh, lasts about two days for heavy users and three for light ones. A third more capacity, with a similar screen and chip, points to three days for many people and four for those who mostly call and chat. Continuous video might approach thirty hours.</p>\n<h2>Who needs three days?</h2>\n<p><strong>Trekking guides and porters.</strong> Charging in tea houses costs Rs 300 to 1,000 a time at altitude, and cold drains batteries faster. A phone that crosses from Lukla to Gorak Shep on one or two charges pays for itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Drivers.</strong> Long-distance bus and truck drivers with maps and calls all day.</p>\n<p><strong>Field staff.</strong> Surveyors, health workers, journalists, vaccinators.</p>\n<p><strong>People in places with poor supply.</strong> Fewer now than ten years ago, though there are still many.</p>\n<p><strong>Migrant workers.</strong> Long shifts on sites where charging is not allowed.</p>\n<p>For someone with an office and a bedside table, 6,000mAh is enough. Charging is a habit, not a hardship.</p>\n<h2>The other side of a giant battery</h2>\n<p><strong>Weight.</strong> You will feel it in a shirt pocket and while reading in bed.</p>\n<p><strong>Charging time.</strong> At 80W, around an hour and a quarter. At 18W with an old charger, five or six hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Air travel.</strong> The limit for batteries in devices is 100 watt-hours without approval. At about 3.85 volts, 13,500mAh is some 52 watt-hours, so a phone like this is fine in the cabin. Spare power banks of 20,000mAh at 74 watt-hours are within the limit as well, and must be in hand luggage.</p>\n<p><strong>Repair.</strong> A large special cell costs more and may have to be ordered. Never fit an unknown replacement in a phone that stores this much energy.</p>\n<p><strong>Price.</strong> More battery in a mid-range phone usually means a plainer camera.</p>\n<h2>Phone or power bank?</h2>\n<p>A fair question. A 10,000mAh power bank costs Rs 1,500 to 3,000, and delivers about 6,000 to 6,500mAh to your phone after conversion losses. It weighs 200 grams, and you leave it at home on ordinary days. A phone with a huge battery is one object, charges faster and never needs a cable in the rain. If you need endurance once a month, buy the power bank. If you need it every day, buy the phone. See <a href=\"/products?category=accessory\">accessories</a> for current prices.</p>\n<h2>Reverse charging</h2>\n<p>Big-battery phones increasingly charge other devices by cable at 20W or so. With 13,500mAh, you could fill a friend\'s phone and still have two days left. For a family on a trip, one such phone serves as everyone\'s emergency supply.</p>\n<h2>Big batteries on sale in Nepal today</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>6.79-inch AMOLED (2640×1200), 120Hz, 6,000 ni...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse c...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;57,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K AMOLED (2772×1280), 120Hz, 3,5...</td><td>Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,340mAh; 67W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>6.8-inch quad-curved 1.5K AMOLED, 144Hz, 6,50...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reve...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,50...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">Poco X8 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;94,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 20...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500s (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 8500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> is the toughest, with 8,300mAh at Rs 54,999. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> balances 10,000mAh with 100W charging. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a> is the simplest choice for pure endurance. The <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">POCO X8 Pro Max</a> joins 8,500mAh to a fast chip. We have reviews of the <a href=\"/reviews/honor-x9d-review-nepal\">X9d</a>, the <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-pro-max-review-nepal\">Note 17 Pro Max</a> and the <a href=\"/reviews/realme-p4-power-review-nepal\">P4 Power</a>.</p>\n<h2>Would Honor bring it to Nepal?</h2>\n<p>Honor has been active here, with phones from Rs 16,000 to more than a lakh, and its strength has been battery and toughness. A phone with this battery would suit the market well. It may appear under another name and with a smaller cell, as happens with many Chinese models. The X9 series usually arrives within months of its global launch. A domestic Power model may never come. Importers will certainly try, and an imported unit means no local warranty and a check of MDMS.</p>\n<h2>Is it safe?</h2>\n<p>Certification exists to answer that. Cells are tested for short circuit, crushing, heat and overcharging. More energy does mean more consequences when something is abused. So the ordinary rules matter more: use a good charger, do not cover the phone while it charges at high speed, do not use it after the body swells or bends, and do not let an unknown shop fit a battery. A phone that becomes too hot to hold should be unplugged and left on a hard surface.</p>\n<h2>How it ages</h2>\n<p>Batteries wear by cycles and by heat. A phone charged twice a week instead of every day goes through a third as many cycles. After four years it may still hold most of its capacity, while a small-battery phone is on its second cell. Endurance, then, is also longevity, a quiet argument in favour of the giants.</p>\n<h2>What to watch for</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>A second certificate with charging speed.</li>\n<li>A product name in China\'s network access list.</li>\n<li>Honor\'s announcement, probably with a round number in its title.</li>\n<li>A global model with an IMEI registered for South Asia.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Our view</h2>\n<p>Every few months someone announces a larger battery, and each time the previous giant becomes the sensible choice at a lower price. That is the useful way to read this. If you need endurance now, a 10,000mAh phone at Rs 70,000 is already more than most people can finish in two days. If you can wait a year, 8,000mAh will be ordinary at Rs 40,000. Either way, the old worry about reaching the evening is coming to an end, and that is better news than any single phone.</p>','rumors',NULL,1,4,'A 13,300mAh rated (13,500mAh typical) Honor battery has passed China\'s 3C certification, likely for a 7-inch mid-range phone, possibly the Honor Power 3.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-17 18:15:00','2026-11-17 18:15:00'),(52,20,'Phone Price Updates in Nepal: November 2026','phone-price-updates-nepal-november-2026','<p>Phone prices in Nepal changed several times through 2026, mostly upward. These are the current prices, checked on 22–23 November 2026, for phones whose prices have moved recently or that people ask us about most.</p>\n\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Phone</th><th>Variant</th><th>Current price</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a-pro\">Nothing Phone (4a) Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,09,999 (after repeated hikes this year)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Motorola Edge 70 Fusion</a></td><td>12GB/256GB · 12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 79,999 · Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE 6</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB · 12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,29,999 · Rs 1,49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a></td><td>8GB/256GB · 12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999 · Rs 79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,24,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Why prices keep rising</h2>\n<p>Memory chips have become more expensive worldwide, and phone makers have passed that on. Our explainer covers it in detail: <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">why phones, laptops and consoles got more expensive in Nepal in 2026</a>.</p>\n<p>Every product page on this site shows the dated prices we\'ve recorded, so you can check whether a price has moved before you buy. Browse <a href=\"/products?category=mobile\">all phones</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The full comparison</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a-pro\">Nothing Phone 4a Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,09,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 16...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4 nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 5080 mAh, non-removable; 50W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Motorola Edge 70 Fusion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 5, 200 nits (peak)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 68W Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ), 18...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>7, 500mAh, non-removable; 80W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 8000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Hono...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,50...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,24,999</td><td>6.9-inch QHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 3120×1440, 1...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 60W wired, 25W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>6.7-inch Super AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass V...</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table is drawn from the live catalogue, so it will follow later changes. The paragraphs below were written with November prices in front of us.</p>\n<h2>How to read a price change</h2>\n<p>When a price moves, three different things may have happened. The maker has changed the official price. A distributor has passed on a new cost with the latest batch. Or a dealer is clearing stock. The first lasts, the second usually lasts, and the third ends when the shelf is empty. A rise is nearly always the first two. A fall is often the third, so if you see one and you want the phone, do not think for a week.</p>\n<h2>Phones that became expensive for what they are</h2>\n<p><strong>Galaxy A37 5G.</strong> Good, safe and now about Rs 75,000. At its original Rs 64,999 we recommended it easily. <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains who should still buy it.</p>\n<p><strong>Galaxy A57 5G.</strong> Close to Rs 97,000 for the base version puts it beside phones with flagship chips. Buy it for support and slimness, with an exchange offer.</p>\n<p><strong>Galaxy A17 5G.</strong> From the high thirties to Rs 44,999. Still a sensible phone for parents.</p>\n<p><strong>Nothing Phone (4a) Pro.</strong> Repeated increases have taken it to Rs 1,09,999, beyond the OnePlus 15R and near the POCO F8 Pro. The regular <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">4a</a> at Rs 69,999 is the one we would choose.</p>\n<p><strong>vivo X300 Pro.</strong> Up by Rs 20,000 since its launch. The camera remains superb. <a href=\"/reviews/vivo-x300-pro-review-nepal\">Read the review</a>.</p>\n<h2>Phones that held their value</h2>\n<p><strong>Redmi Note 17 Pro Max.</strong> Rs 69,999 for 10,000mAh and 100W charging is still remarkable.</p>\n<p><strong>POCO F7.</strong> The fastest phone under Rs 70,000 a year after its launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Honor X9d.</strong> At Rs 54,999 there is nothing tougher.</p>\n<p><strong>Redmi Note 15 5G.</strong> About Rs 44,000, and the best general-purpose phone below Rs 50,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Infinix Note 50x.</strong> Around Rs 22,000 for a chip that embarrasses phones costing twice as much.</p>\n<h2>New arrivals to judge carefully</h2>\n<p><strong>OnePlus Nord CE 6.</strong> At Rs 74,999 it costs more than the older Nord 5 with a faster chip. It wins on battery and on being new. Compare the two before choosing. <a href=\"/reviews/oneplus-nord-5-review-nepal\">Our Nord 5 review</a> covers the older one.</p>\n<p><strong>Motorola Edge 70 Fusion.</strong> Rs 79,999 for a pretty, light phone. Motorola has fewer service points, so ask about screens.</p>\n<p><strong>Honor 600 Pro.</strong> Rs 1,29,999 with a Snapdragon 8 Elite, a 200MP camera, a telephoto and 7,000mAh in a compact body. On hardware it is among the best in its class.</p>\n<h2>Where the gaps are strange</h2>\n<p>An attentive buyer looks for places where the price ladder has missing or crooked rungs. At present we see four.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The OPPO Find X9 Pro at about Rs 1,26,000 is cheaper than some mid-range phones with more memory.</li>\n<li>The POCO F8 Pro at Rs 1,04,999 costs less than the Nothing 4a Pro and is in another class.</li>\n<li>The Galaxy S25 Ultra at Rs 1,84,999 is only slightly above the top Galaxy S26 version.</li>\n<li>The Galaxy A37 in its 12GB version costs exactly what a base A57 costs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Such gaps appear when one brand\'s price list is updated and another\'s is not. They close within weeks.</p>\n<h2>What to do if your budget has not risen</h2>\n<p>Wages have not kept pace with phone prices this year. There are four ways to respond that do not involve debt.</p>\n<p><strong>Keep the old phone.</strong> A battery at Rs 2,500 to 5,000 and a clean reset bring most phones back.</p>\n<p><strong>Buy last year\'s model.</strong> A Realme P3 or Moto G96 gives up little.</p>\n<p><strong>Change brand.</strong> Infinix, POCO and Honor have absorbed more of the increase than Samsung and Nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Buy used, with care.</strong> Use our checks for <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">official phones</a> before paying.</p>\n<h2>If you must buy this month</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rs 25,000:</strong> Galaxy A07 or Infinix Note 50x.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 35,000:</strong> Infinix Note 50s or Redmi 17 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 45,000:</strong> Redmi Note 15 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 55,000:</strong> Honor X9d.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 70,000:</strong> POCO F7 or Redmi Note 17 Pro Max.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 1,00,000:</strong> OnePlus 15R.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 1,30,000:</strong> OPPO Find X9 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Above that:</strong> Galaxy S25 Ultra on discount, or the flagship you have been dreaming of.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a> explains each choice.</p>\n<h2>If you can wait until spring</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s S27 series, the regular iPhone 18 and several Chinese flagships are expected in the first half of 2027. New models push the present ones down, especially the S26 Ultra and iPhone 17. New mid-range phones, on the other hand, may come with higher prices or smaller storage, as memory remains dear. So waiting helps flagship buyers more than budget buyers.</p>\n<h2>Festival discounts are over. What next?</h2>\n<p>After Tihar, shops are quieter and more willing to talk. Stock brought for the season that did not sell is the best source of real discounts until the new year. Colours that nobody wanted, display units and bundles can be had for less. Ask directly: \"What have you got that you would like to clear?\" It is a better opening than \"What is your last price?\"</p>\n<h2>Accessories and the true total</h2>\n<p>Several makers have stopped including chargers. Add Rs 1,500 to 4,000 for a proper one, Rs 500 for glass, Rs 500 to 1,500 for a case. On a Rs 25,000 phone that is a tenth of the price. A bundle that includes them is worth more than a small cash discount.</p>\n<h2>How we record changes</h2>\n<p>Each product page keeps dated entries, and the <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a> lists the latest movements. Shops differ, cities differ, and an exchange or bank offer can change the final figure by thousands. We show the list price and the date on which it was checked. If what you are quoted is far from it, in either direction, ask why. An unusually low price has a reason, and you want to know it before you pay.</p>\n<h2>The month in one paragraph</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s A series and Nothing rose, big-battery Redmis and POCOs held, OPPO and Honor offered unexpected value at the top, and the cheapest phones quietly lost storage. Memory is still the cause, and nothing in November suggests that it will change by the new year. Buy what you need, buy enough memory, and let the rest wait.</p>','price-trends',NULL,1,0,'Latest phone prices in Nepal for November 2026, including Nothing Phone (4a) Pro, Motorola Edge 70 Fusion, OnePlus Nord CE 6 and Honor 600 Pro.',0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00');
