INSERT INTO `reviews` VALUES (1,1,20,'Samsung Galaxy S24 Review: A Small Flagship That Has Aged Well','samsung-galaxy-s24-review-snapdragon-8-gen-3-flagship-value','<!--depth-->\n<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We have not run our own lab test of the Galaxy S24. This review is based on Samsung\'s specifications, what is known about the phone after almost three years on sale, and how it compares with phones you can buy in Nepal today.</p>\n<p>The Galaxy S24 came out in January 2024 as the small, sensible member of Samsung\'s flagship family. It is no longer sold new through official channels in Nepal, so most people reading this in late 2026 are looking at a used or leftover unit and asking one question: is it still worth having, or should the money go to a new mid-range phone? The short answer is that it has aged well, with one clear weakness that you need to plan around.</p>\n<h2>Which S24 are you looking at?</h2>\n<p>Before anything else, find out which chip is inside. Samsung sold the S24 with its own Exynos 2400 in most of the world and with the Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 for Galaxy in the United States, South Korea and China. Units sold officially in Nepal belong to the Exynos group. Units brought in from America by relatives, or through grey-market shops, may have the Snapdragon.</p>\n<p>In daily use the gap is smaller than forum arguments suggest. Messaging, photos, maps, YouTube and banking apps feel the same on both. The Snapdragon holds its speed longer in heavy games and uses a little less power while doing it. If you play Genshin Impact or PUBG for an hour at a time, it is worth paying slightly more for that version. If you do not, buy whichever unit is in better condition. You can check in Settings under About phone, where the model number ends in B for the international version and U or U1 for the American one.</p>\n<h2>Size is the reason to buy it</h2>\n<p>Very few good phones are this small any more. The S24 has a 6.2-inch screen and weighs 167 grams, which means you can reach most of the display with one thumb and forget it is in a shirt pocket. Compare that with the 6.8 and 6.9-inch phones that fill shop counters on New Road. If you ride a scooter, hold your phone on crowded buses, or simply have smaller hands, this matters every single day in a way that a faster chip does not.</p>\n<p>The screen is also excellent. It is a Dynamic AMOLED panel that runs between 1Hz and 120Hz and reaches 2,600 nits at its peak, so maps stay readable in direct sun. Resolution is Full HD+, which at this size looks perfectly sharp.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy S24</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.2 inches Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 120Hz, 2600 nits</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Global: Samsung Exynos 2400, US, South Korea, China: Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 For Galaxy</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB, 256GB, 512GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main + 12MP ultra wide + 10MP 3x telephoto | 12MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 4000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging; Wireless PowerShare, Fast Wireless Charging 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 14, One UI 6.1</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / WCDMA / LTE / 5G, WiFi 6E (802.11 a/b/g/n/ac/ax), Tri-Band, WiFi Direct, Hotspot, Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>167 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>147.00 x 70.60 x 7.60 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Cameras: still better than most mid-rangers</h2>\n<p>The camera set is a 50MP main sensor, a 12MP ultra wide and a 10MP telephoto with 3x optical zoom. That telephoto is the quiet advantage over a new phone at a similar used price. Mid-range phones usually replace the zoom lens with a 2MP macro camera that nobody uses. On the S24 you can photograph a child on a school stage, a temple detail or a distant peak without the picture turning to mush, as long as there is reasonable light.</p>\n<p>Samsung\'s processing leans towards bright faces and vivid skies. Some people like that, others find it heavy. Night mode works well on the main camera and noticeably less well on the other two. Video goes up to 8K, but 4K at 60 frames per second is the setting worth using because files stay manageable and all the stabilisation works.</p>\n<p>The selfie camera is 12MP with autofocus, which helps with group shots taken at arm\'s length.</p>\n<h2>The battery is the catch</h2>\n<p>There is no polite way around it. The S24 has a 4,000mAh battery in a year when budget phones carry 6,000mAh and some mid-rangers reach 10,000mAh. Modern screens and a slim body make the most of it, but if you are out from morning college until evening tuition with mobile data on, expect to look for a charger before bed. On 5G the drain is faster still.</p>\n<p>Charging tops out at 25W by cable. It also supports wireless charging and can charge earbuds from its back, both rare in the price range where used units now sit. Samsung did not put a charger in the box, so check whether a seller is including one, and use a USB Power Delivery charger that supports PPS to get the full speed.</p>\n<p>There is a second issue with any phone from early 2024: the battery inside has been through a few hundred charge cycles. Lithium cells lose capacity with every cycle. A used S24 with eighty-five per cent of its original capacity behaves like a 3,400mAh phone. Include the price of a battery replacement at a Samsung service centre in your budget, and treat a unit that already has a new genuine battery as worth more.</p>\n<h2>Software support is its best long-term feature</h2>\n<p>Samsung promised seven years of Android upgrades and security patches for the S24 family, which takes it to 2031. Almost no mid-range phone on sale in Nepal today can match that. It launched with Android 14 and One UI 6.1 and has been updated since. For someone who keeps a phone until it breaks, that promise counts for more than the processor.</p>\n<p>The Galaxy AI tools that were the headline in 2024 still work: Circle to Search for identifying something on the screen, call and text translation, and generative photo edits. Language support varies, and Nepali is not covered by every feature, so do not buy the phone for translation without trying it first.</p>\n<h2>What to check on a used unit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MDMS registration.</strong> Dial *#06#, note the IMEI and check it on the NTA portal before you hand over money. An unregistered phone can lose network access.</li>\n<li><strong>Screen.</strong> Open a plain white and a plain grey image at low brightness. Look for green tint, faint keyboard outlines or dark patches along the edges.</li>\n<li><strong>Accounts.</strong> The previous owner must remove their Google and Samsung accounts in front of you, otherwise the phone locks after a reset.</li>\n<li><strong>Water seals.</strong> The S24 is rated IP68 from the factory. A phone that has been opened for repair no longer has that protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Knox.</strong> In the service menu or in Settings, confirm that Knox has not been tripped. If it has, banking features and Samsung Wallet may be blocked.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What else the money buys</h2>\n<p>A new phone such as the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g\">Galaxy A57 5G</a> gives you a fresh battery, a full warranty and a bigger screen, but loses the telephoto lens, the compact size and the wireless charging. A <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25\">Galaxy S25</a>, where you can find one, keeps the size and adds a faster chip. If you want the newest small Samsung, look at the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a>, and expect to pay properly for it.</p>\n<p>Our rule of thumb: an S24 in clean condition, with registration and a healthy battery, is a better buy than a new phone at the same price for anyone who cares about size, zoom and long updates. It is a worse buy for gamers and for people who are away from a socket all day.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy S24 was never the most exciting phone of its year, and that has helped it. It does nearly everything well, it fits in a hand, and it will still be receiving updates when many newer phones have been forgotten. Buy it for the size, the zoom lens and the support. Keep a power bank in your bag and you will get along fine.</p>',8.5,'Compact 167g body that fits one hand\n3x optical telephoto, rare at its used price\nSeven years of updates from 2024\nBright 2,600-nit adaptive 120Hz screen','4,000mAh battery is small by today\'s standards\n25W charging and no charger in the box\nChip differs by region, so check before buying used','A very good compact phone to buy used for its size, zoom lens and long updates. Check the battery health and keep a power bank handy.',1,0,0,1,0,0,0,'2026-05-18 11:33:58','2026-05-18 11:43:58'),(2,2,20,'iPhone 15 Pro Review: Still Worth Buying in Nepal in 2026?','iphone-15-pro-review-titanium-action-button-and-usb-c-finally','<!--depth-->\n<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We have not run our own lab test of the iPhone 15 Pro. This review uses Apple\'s specifications, what is known about the phone three years after launch, and current prices in Nepal.</p>\n<p>When the iPhone 15 Pro arrived in September 2023 it changed three things that iPhone owners touch every day: the metal around the edge, the switch on the side and the port at the bottom. Three years on, with the iPhone 18 Pro in shops, it has become a popular second-hand choice and still appears in some listings at around Rs 1,49,999. That price deserves a hard look, because newer phones now sit very close to it.</p>\n<h2>The changes that still matter</h2>\n<p>Apple replaced the steel frame of the 14 Pro with titanium. The result is a 187 gram phone that feels noticeably lighter than the one before it. The edges are gently rounded, so it is more comfortable to hold for a long call. The brushed finish hides small scratches, although the darker colours show marks from a coin or a key if you carry the phone without a case.</p>\n<p>The mute switch became the Action button. You can leave it as a silent toggle or make it open the camera, the torch, a voice memo or any shortcut you build. It sounds trivial until you set it to something you use ten times a day.</p>\n<p>The third change is the one everyone in the house will notice. USB-C means one cable for the phone, a laptop, an Android tablet and a power bank. On the Pro models the port also runs at USB 3 speeds of up to 10 gigabits per second, but only if you buy a suitable cable. The one in the box is limited to slower USB 2 transfers. For anyone who shoots long videos, that cable is a worthwhile purchase, because moving 100GB over the slow one takes the better part of an hour.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>iPhone 15 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.1-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 2556×1179, 120Hz ProMotion</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple A17 Pro</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>48MP + 12MP + 12MP | 12MP TrueDepth Selfie</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>3274mAh, 27W Fast Charging, MagSafe</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>iOS 17</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>5G, Wi-Fi 6E, Bluetooth 5.3, NFC, USB-C</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>187g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>146.6 × 70.6 × 8.25 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Performance has not become a problem</h2>\n<p>The A17 Pro was the first phone chip built on a 3nm process. Paired with 8GB of memory it still opens apps instantly, exports 4K video quickly and runs demanding games. It is also the oldest iPhone chip that supports Apple Intelligence, which matters if you want the writing tools, notification summaries and the newer Siri. The ordinary iPhone 15, despite looking similar, does not get those features because it has less memory and an older chip. If you are choosing between a used 15 and a used 15 Pro, that is the strongest argument for the Pro.</p>\n<p>Heat was the main complaint at launch. Early software let the phone get uncomfortably warm during setup and heavy use, and Apple changed the behaviour with an update within weeks. It remains a compact phone with no fan, so long gaming sessions in a Terai summer will still make it dim the screen and slow down.</p>\n<h2>Cameras, including one common mistake</h2>\n<p>The back has a 48MP main camera, a 12MP ultra wide that doubles as a macro lens, and a 12MP telephoto with 3x zoom. Please note the number. The 5x periscope-style lens belongs to the larger iPhone 15 Pro Max only. Listings and older articles, including an earlier version of this page, often mix the two up. If long zoom is what you want, the small Pro is the wrong phone.</p>\n<p>What it does have is consistency. Photos match each other as you move between lenses, skin tones stay natural, and the main camera saves 24MP images by default with good detail. Portraits can be created after the fact from normal photos of people and pets. Video is still the reason many wedding and event shooters in Nepal use iPhones as second cameras: 4K at 60 frames per second looks steady and clean, and ProRes with Log recording gives editors room to work. You can even record ProRes straight to an external SSD through the USB-C port.</p>\n<h2>Battery life is average, and age makes it worse</h2>\n<p>The cell holds 3,274mAh. When new, that meant a normal day for moderate use and an evening top-up for heavy use. A phone that has been used since 2023 will have lost some of that. On any used unit, open Settings, then Battery, then Battery Health, and read the maximum capacity figure and the cycle count. The 15 series was the first to show cycles clearly. Below eighty per cent, plan for a replacement, and remember that a cheap unofficial battery will show a warning message that puts off the next buyer too.</p>\n<p>Charging takes roughly half an hour to reach the halfway mark with a 20W or stronger USB-C charger. MagSafe wireless charging works at up to 15W, and a setting lets you stop at eighty per cent to slow the ageing of the battery.</p>\n<h2>Is Rs 1,49,999 fair in late 2026?</h2>\n<p>For a new, sealed unit with a warranty, it is difficult to recommend. At almost the same money you will find phones that are two years younger.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>iPhone 15 Pro</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>Apple A17 Pro</td><td>3274mAh, 27W Fast Charging, MagSafe</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">Realme GT 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>7,000mAh; 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-16\">iPhone 16</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,46,599</td><td>Apple A18 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 3561 mAh, non-removable; Wired, PD2.0, 50%...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip-7\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,54,999</td><td>Samsung Exynos 2500 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Po 4300 mAh, non-removable; 25W Wired; 25W Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Honor Sup...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> is the comparison that hurts. For a small step up it brings a 120Hz screen to the standard model, a newer chip, a longer period of software updates ahead of it and a battery that starts at full health. You give up the telephoto lens and the titanium. Most buyers should make that trade. On the Android side, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> offers a similar compact size with seven years of updates from 2026.</p>\n<p>A used 15 Pro is a different story. Prices in the second-hand market depend on storage, battery health and whether the box is present, so compare several sellers. If one is clearly cheaper than everything else, ask why before you ask how much.</p>\n<h2>Buying one safely in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Match the IMEI on the screen, the SIM tray area and the box. Check MDMS registration. Ask the owner to sign out of iCloud and turn off Find My while you watch, then start the phone from the Hello screen yourself. Test Face ID, all three lenses, both speakers, the microphones by recording a voice memo, and wireless charging. In Settings under General, About, look at Parts and Service History: it tells you whether the battery, screen or camera has been replaced, and whether the part was genuine. American units have no physical SIM tray and use eSIM only, so make sure your Nepali operator can set that up for you before you buy.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Good fit:</strong> someone who wants a small, light iPhone with Apple Intelligence and a zoom lens, and has found a clean used unit at a sensible price.</li>\n<li><strong>Good fit:</strong> video creators who need ProRes and fast USB-C without paying for the newest Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Poor fit:</strong> anyone buying new at the old price, when the iPhone 17 is within reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Poor fit:</strong> heavy users who are away from a charger all day. Look at a Pro Max or a large Android phone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The iPhone 15 Pro was a turning point and it remains a lovely object: light, quick and good at video. Its problem in 2026 is the price tag, not the phone. Used and healthy, it is one of the better compact iPhones to own. New at Rs 1,49,999, spend a little more on something current.</p>',9.0,'Light titanium body at 187g\nOldest iPhone that supports Apple Intelligence\nUSB-C with fast transfers on a suitable cable\nConsistent cameras and excellent video','3,274mAh battery, and used units will have aged\n3x zoom only; the 5x lens is on the Pro Max\nHard to justify new at Rs 1,49,999 next to the iPhone 17','Lovely to use and strong at video, but the price has not kept up. Buy a healthy used one, or spend a little more on an iPhone 17.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-05-18 11:33:58','2026-05-18 11:33:58'),(3,10,20,'Sony WF-1000XM5 Review: Still Worth It Next to the XM6?','sony-wf-1000xm5-review-still-the-best-noise-cancelling-earbuds','<!--depth-->\n<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We have not run our own sound test of the WF-1000XM5. This review draws on Sony\'s specifications, what is known about the earbuds after three years on sale, and current prices in Nepal.</p>\n<p>Sony\'s WF-1000XM5 set the standard for small noise-cancelling earbuds when they launched, and they are still on sale in Nepal at around Rs 50,000. The catch is that Sony has since released the <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">WF-1000XM6</a>, and Apple has the <a href=\"/products/airpods-pro-3\">AirPods Pro 3</a> at Rs 47,999. So the question is no longer whether the XM5 are good. They are. It is whether they make sense at this price today.</p>\n<h2>What changed from the older model</h2>\n<p>If you remember the XM4, you remember a chunky bud that some ears could not tolerate for long. The XM5 are about a quarter smaller. Each one weighs 5.9 grams, and the case is 39 grams, light enough for a jeans pocket. The shell is smooth and rounded, which looks elegant and makes them a little slippery when you lift them out of the case with cold fingers.</p>\n<p>Sony packs four sizes of foam-filled tips in the box, from extra small to large. This matters more than any feature in the app. Foam expands in the ear canal and blocks noise physically, before the electronics do any work. Spend ten minutes trying all four, and use the fit test in Sony\'s app. A poor seal ruins both the bass and the noise cancelling, and many unhappy reviews come down to the wrong tip.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling in Nepali conditions</h2>\n<p>Noise cancelling is strongest against steady, low sounds: the drone of a bus engine on the Prithvi Highway, a generator outside a shop, a ceiling fan, the cabin of a domestic flight. The XM5 use two processors and three microphones on each bud to deal with those, and they do it very well. Sharp, irregular sounds are harder for any earbud. Horns at Kalanki and a colleague\'s laugh will be softened, not erased.</p>\n<p>An ambient mode lets outside sound in when you need it, and you can set how much. There is also a feature that pauses music when you start speaking. It is clever, and it also triggers when you sing along or cough, so most people end up switching it off.</p>\n<p>One safety note: do not ride or drive with noise cancelling on. Cutting out traffic noise on a two-wheeler is dangerous, and the ambient mode is not a substitute for open ears.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Sony WF-1000XM5</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 16 hours (case); Quick charge: 3 minutes = 1 hour playback</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC, LDAC, LC3</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>Earbuds: 5.9 gm, Case: 39 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Case: 49.8 × 50.3 × 27.2 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Sound and the LDAC question</h2>\n<p>Out of the box the tuning is warm, with full bass and smooth treble. Vocals in Nepali folk and pop tracks sit comfortably in front. If you prefer more sparkle, the equaliser in the app is easy to use and the result is stored on the buds themselves.</p>\n<p>LDAC is Sony\'s high-quality Bluetooth codec, and it only works with Android phones that support it. With an iPhone, the connection uses AAC. That does not make the XM5 a bad choice for iPhone owners, but it removes one of the reasons to pay extra for them. LDAC also drains the battery faster and can stutter in places with heavy wireless interference, so leave it on the automatic setting and let the buds decide.</p>\n<p>Be realistic about your source. Songs streamed at a low-quality setting to save mobile data will not sound any better through a Rs 50,000 earbud. Set your music app to high quality on Wi-Fi and download albums before a trip.</p>\n<h2>Calls and everyday use</h2>\n<p>Call quality is good for earbuds of this size, thanks to a sensor that picks up the vibration of your voice through bone. In a quiet office or at home you will be heard clearly. In strong wind, or on a footpath next to traffic, the person at the other end will know you are outside.</p>\n<p>The XM5 can stay connected to two devices at once, for example a laptop for a meeting and a phone for calls. Touch controls can be changed in the app. They are rated IPX4, which means sweat and light rain are fine, while a swim or a downpour in Asar is not. The case itself has no water rating.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Sony quotes eight hours of music with noise cancelling on and another sixteen hours from the case, for twenty-four in total. Three minutes of charging gives about an hour of listening, which is useful when you find them empty just before leaving home. Charging is by USB-C or by any Qi wireless pad. An older version of this page said the case could not charge wirelessly; that was wrong.</p>\n<p>Like all earbuds, the tiny batteries wear out. After two to three years of daily use expect noticeably shorter life, and keep in mind that replacing the cells is rarely economical. If you are offered a used pair, ask when they were bought and listen for one side dying before the other.</p>\n<h2>How the price looks now</h2>\n<p>At Rs 50,000 these cost more than the AirPods Pro 3 and about Rs 15,000 less than Sony\'s newer WF-1000XM6. They also cost several times as much as good mid-range earbuds.</p>\n\n<p>If you use an iPhone, the AirPods Pro 3 connect more smoothly, switch between Apple devices and include features that Sony cannot offer on iOS. If you use Android and want the best Sony makes, compare the price of the XM6 on the same day, because shops discount the older model only when stock is moving. If you simply want quiet on a bus, look at earbuds between Rs 8,000 and Rs 20,000. Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a> lists models that give you most of the silence for a fraction of the money.</p>\n<h2>Avoiding fakes</h2>\n<p>Sony earbuds are copied often, and some copies even trigger the pairing pop-up. Buy from a seller who gives a bill and a warranty card. Pair the buds with the official Sony app before paying: a genuine pair is recognised by name, shows the firmware version and offers updates. Check the spelling on the box, the serial number on the case against the one on the packaging, and be suspicious of a price that is half of everyone else\'s.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy the XM5</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Buy them</strong> if you use Android, fly or commute often, and find them discounted well below Rs 50,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy them</strong> if the bulkier XM4 hurt your ears and you want Sony\'s sound in a smaller body.</li>\n<li><strong>Skip them</strong> if you use an iPhone and want simplicity.</li>\n<li><strong>Skip them</strong> if you need earbuds mainly for running. An open or hooked design will stay in place better.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The WF-1000XM5 are comfortable, quiet and still sound excellent, and nothing about their age makes them feel old in use. They are just no longer the automatic choice at full price. Wait for a discount, compare them with the XM6 and the AirPods Pro 3 on the same afternoon, and let the fit in your own ear make the final decision.</p>',9.2,'Strong noise cancelling for buses and flights\nSmall, light and comfortable, with four foam tip sizes\nLDAC on Android and stable two-device pairing\nWireless charging case','Rs 50,000 is more than the AirPods Pro 3\nLDAC does not work with iPhones\nOnly IPX4 and no water rating for the case','Quiet, comfortable and still excellent, but no longer the automatic choice at full price. Buy on a discount and compare with the XM6 the same day.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-05-18 11:33:58','2026-05-18 11:33:58'),(4,3,20,'Xiaomi 14 Review: A Small Phone With Big-Camera Character','xiaomi-14-review-leica-cameras-at-a-smarter-price','<!--depth-->\n<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We have not run our own lab test of the Xiaomi 14. This review is based on Xiaomi\'s specifications, what is known about the phone since its 2024 release, and the phones it competes with in Nepal now.</p>\n<p>The Xiaomi 14 was the phone that made people take Xiaomi seriously as a camera brand: a small flagship with three 50MP cameras tuned with Leica, a top Qualcomm chip and charging that embarrassed Samsung and Apple. It is discontinued now, so in Nepal you will meet it as a used phone or as an imported unit. Here is how it holds up, and the traps to avoid.</p>\n<h2>A small flagship without the usual compromises</h2>\n<p>Compact phones normally pay for their size with a weak battery. The Xiaomi 14 does not. Inside a body with a 6.36-inch screen and a weight of 193 grams there is a 4,610mAh cell, more than the Galaxy S24 and the iPhone 15 Pro managed in the same period. It charges at 90W with the right adapter, which fills an empty phone in a little over half an hour. Wireless charging runs at 50W on a compatible stand, and it can pass 10W to earbuds or another phone.</p>\n<p>The frame is flat, the edges of the screen are flat, and the camera block is a large square that makes the phone rock on a table. A case solves that. The panel is a 120Hz AMOLED that peaks at 3,000 nits, bright enough for midday in Pokhara, and it can drop its refresh rate very low to save power on still pages.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Xiaomi 14</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.36 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3000 nits (Peak)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X-8533</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256/512GB UFS 4.0</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main (Light Fusion 900) + 50MP ultra wide + 50MP 3.2x telephoto, Leica | 32MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Po 4610 mAh, non-removable; 90W TurboCharge; 50W Wireless Charging, 10W Reverse Wireless Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 14, HyperOS</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / UMTS / LTE / 5G, WiFi 7/6/6E (802.11 a/b/g/n/ac/ax), Triple-Band, 2x2 MIMO, WiFi Direct, Hotspot, Bluetooth 5.4, Dual-Bluetooth</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>193 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>152.8 x 71.5 x 8.20 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What the Leica name means in practice</h2>\n<p>There are two ways of seeing the cameras. Technically, you get a 50MP main camera with a large Light Fusion 900 sensor, a 50MP ultra wide and a 50MP telephoto with roughly 3.2x zoom. It is rare for all three to have the same resolution, and it means picture quality does not collapse when you change lenses.</p>\n<p>In practice, the important part is the choice of two looks. Leica Vibrant is closer to what most phones do: brighter shadows, stronger colour. Leica Authentic keeps contrast and lets shadows stay dark, so photos of a Bhaktapur courtyard or a foggy morning feel more like pictures from a camera than from a phone. If you are used to Samsung\'s processing it can look gloomy for a week, and then going back is difficult.</p>\n<p>The telephoto can focus as close as ten centimetres, which turns it into a surprisingly good lens for food, jewellery and flowers. Portraits at that focal length have a natural shape, without the stretched noses that wide lenses produce. Low light is a strength of the main camera and only acceptable on the other two. Video is good but not at iPhone level for smooth panning and steady focus.</p>\n<h2>Speed is still not an issue</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 with fast LPDDR5X memory and UFS 4.0 storage remains quicker than almost every mid-range chip sold today. Games run at high settings, and 12GB of memory keeps plenty of apps open. A small body warms up faster than a large one, so an hour of PUBG at maximum settings will make the top half hot to the touch and bring the frame rate down a step.</p>\n<h2>Global version or China version: check before you pay</h2>\n<p>This is the single most important thing about buying a Xiaomi 14 in Nepal. Two versions exist. The global version comes with Google services, international 4G and 5G bands, and IP68 protection against water and dust. The China version is cheaper, and many units entered Nepal that way. It may arrive with a Chinese system, with Google apps added afterwards by a shop, and with different network bands.</p>\n<p>Problems with a China unit include missing notifications until you change battery settings, banking apps that refuse to run on modified software, and no Android Auto. Never buy one on which a shop has installed an unofficial global ROM: the bootloader is usually unlocked or relocked badly, and an update can leave you with a dead phone. Look at the MIUI or HyperOS version in About phone. Global builds contain the letters MI after the version number, while Chinese builds contain CN.</p>\n<p>Xiaomi promised four Android upgrades and five years of security patches for the global 14. Service and parts in Nepal depend on the official distributor, and an imported unit may be refused warranty work, so ask at the service centre before you buy.</p>\n<h2>Living with HyperOS</h2>\n<p>The software is fast and full of options, and it also has more built-in apps and suggestions than a Pixel or an iPhone. Spend fifteen minutes after setup: remove the apps you do not need, turn off recommendations inside the file manager and security apps, and allow the apps that matter, such as WhatsApp, Viber and your bank, to run in the background. After that it stays out of your way.</p>\n<h2>Where it stands against phones you can buy today</h2>\n<p>The natural replacement inside the same family is the standard Xiaomi 17, with a newer chip and a bigger battery at a higher price. At the top is the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a>, which is really a camera that makes calls. If your budget is closer to a used Xiaomi 14, the new <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> is faster on paper and has a huge battery, and the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> has a periscope zoom lens. Neither is as small, and neither has three matched cameras or wireless charging.</p>\n<p>So the choice is simple to describe. Pay for a new phone if you want a warranty, a fresh battery and a long run of updates. Choose a used Xiaomi 14 if compact size and photography matter more to you than those things and you are willing to inspect the phone carefully.</p>\n<h2>Inspection list for a used unit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm the global model and IMEI, then check MDMS registration.</li>\n<li>Take a photo with each of the three lenses at night. Look for haze, which means a damaged lens cover or moisture.</li>\n<li>Plug in a charger and see whether the phone reports 90W or HyperCharge. Very slow charging can mean a damaged port or a replaced battery.</li>\n<li>Read the battery cycle count and health if the software version shows it.</li>\n<li>Check the screen for green lines, a fault that appears on many AMOLED phones after an update or a fall.</li>\n<li>Make sure Mi Account is signed out. A locked account cannot be removed by a local shop.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Xiaomi 14 is one of the best small phones of its generation, and its cameras have more character than most phones at twice its used price. Its risks come from how it is sold here, not from the hardware. Find a registered global unit in good health and you will enjoy it. If the seller cannot show you which version it is, walk away.</p>',8.7,'Three matched 50MP cameras with Leica looks\nCompact 6.36-inch body with a 4,610mAh battery\n90W wired and 50W wireless charging\nSnapdragon 8 Gen 3 is still very fast','China and global units are easily confused\nGets warm in long games\nWarranty on imported units is uncertain in Nepal','One of the best small phones of its generation. Buy only a registered global unit and inspect it carefully.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-05-18 11:33:58','2026-05-18 11:33:58'),(51,88,20,'iPhone 18 Pro Max Review: Is It Worth Rs 2,79,900 in Nepal?','iphone-18-pro-max-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the iPhone 18 Pro Max yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The iPhone 18 Pro Max went on sale in Nepal on 29 September 2026 at <strong>Rs 2,79,900</strong> for 256GB. That\'s Rs 20,000 more than the smaller <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a>, and Rs 54,900 more than a base <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a>. So the question isn\'t whether it\'s a good phone. It\'s whether it is good enough to justify that gap.</p>\n\n<h2>What the money gets you</h2>\n<p>The A20 Pro is Apple\'s first 2nm chip, and the 6.9-inch LTPO display reaches 3,000 nits with an anti-reflective coating. That coating is the upgrade you\'ll notice most outdoors in the Kathmandu sun. All three rear cameras are 48MP: a main camera with a variable aperture, an ultra wide and a 5x periscope telephoto. Charging finally reaches 50W wired, with 25W on MagSafe.</p>\n\n<h2>Pro Max or Pro?</h2>\n<p>The camera is the main reason to pick the Max. The 18 Pro uses a 4x telephoto, while the Max gets 5x, which helps at concerts, sports and wildlife shots in Chitwan. You also get the bigger screen. The cost is weight: at 249g, this is a heavy phone to hold up for a long video call or to carry in a shirt pocket.</p>\n\n<h2>Choosing storage</h2>\n<p>Storage upgrades are where Apple makes its margin. 512GB costs Rs 3,22,900 (Rs 43,000 more), 1TB costs Rs 4,07,900 and 2TB costs Rs 5,34,900. If you shoot a lot of 4K video, 512GB is the sensible step. Otherwise, 256GB plus an iCloud plan costs far less over the phone\'s life.</p>\n\n<h2>The alternatives</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy S26 Ultra gives you a built-in S Pen, a 200MP main camera and seven years of updates for much less money. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> (Rs 1,99,999) has a 200MP periscope and a 6,510mAh battery. If you\'re already in the Apple ecosystem with a Mac, AirPods and an Apple Watch, the iPhone is the easier choice. Coming from Android, it\'s harder to justify on value alone. <a href=\"/compare?g1=iphone-18-pro-max&g2=samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Compare it side by side with the S26 Ultra</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Full specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>iPhone 18 Pro Max</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.9-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1320×2868, LTPO 120Hz, 3,000 nits peak, anti-reflective coating</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB / 1TB / 2TB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>48MP main (variable aperture) + 48MP ultra wide + 48MP 5x periscope telephoto | 24MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>50W wired, 25W MagSafe wireless, 4.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>iOS 27</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>5G, Wi-Fi 7, Bluetooth 6.0, USB-C</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>249g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>163.4 × 78.0 × 8.75 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The screen, and why the coating matters more than the nits</h2>\n<p>Phone makers like to argue about peak brightness, and 3,000 nits is a big number. What makes the difference on a bright afternoon at Ratna Park is how much of the sky the glass throws back at your eyes. The anti-reflective coating on the 18 Pro Max reduces that mirror effect, so dark apps, maps and videos stay readable without pushing the panel to its limit. That in turn saves battery, because the screen does not have to fight the sun.</p>\n<p>There is a practical consequence. A cheap tempered glass protector covers the coating and cancels it. If you always use a screen guard, look for one that has its own anti-reflective layer, or you will have paid for a feature you never see.</p>\n<p>At 6.9 inches with a resolution of 1320 by 2868 and a variable 120Hz refresh rate, it is a comfortable size for reading PDFs, editing photos and watching football. It is too large for one hand. Try holding the phone in a shop for a full five minutes, not five seconds, because 249 grams only announces itself over time.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: what three 48MP sensors change</h2>\n<p>Earlier Pro models had one excellent camera and two good ones. Giving all three rear cameras a 48MP sensor means the ultra wide and the telephoto can now save detailed images as well, and cropping a photo later does less damage. The main camera has a variable aperture, which physically opens to let in light and closes to bring more of the scene into focus. It is useful for group photos, where a wide aperture often leaves the person at the edge slightly soft, and for documents and food shot from close range.</p>\n<p>The 5x periscope is the lens that justifies the Max. Think of a graduation at Tribhuvan University, where you are standing thirty metres from the stage, or rhinos on the far bank of the Rapti. A 4x lens on the smaller Pro will get you most of the way, and cropping from 48MP closes some of the gap, so be honest about how often you need it.</p>\n<p>The 24MP front camera gives more room to crop selfies and video calls. Creators who film themselves will find that more useful than any rear zoom.</p>\n<h2>Performance and heat</h2>\n<p>A 2nm chip is smaller and more efficient than the 3nm parts before it. For ordinary apps you will not feel a difference from an iPhone 17 Pro. Where it helps is in sustained work: long 4K recording, exporting a project in the field, games with ray-traced lighting, and the on-device parts of Apple Intelligence. The 12GB of memory helps with the last of those. If your heaviest task is Instagram, none of this is a reason to upgrade.</p>\n<h2>Charging at last</h2>\n<p>iPhones have charged slowly for years, and 50W by cable is a real change. You need a USB Power Delivery charger that can supply that power, and Apple does not include one in the box, so add it to your budget. MagSafe at 25W is enough for a desk or a car mount. Reverse charging at 4.5W by cable is meant for earbuds or a watch, not for rescuing a friend\'s phone.</p>\n<p>Apple does not publish battery capacity in the way Android brands do, so compare the hours of video playback it quotes and, better, ask someone who has owned the phone for a month. Any claim about an exact mAh figure usually comes from teardown reports.</p>\n<h2>What it costs to own for four years</h2>\n<p>The price on the sticker is only the start. Take the 256GB model at Rs 2,79,900. Add a charger, a case and a protector, roughly Rs 10,000 if you avoid counterfeits. Add AppleCare-style cover or put money aside for a screen, because an out-of-warranty screen on a Pro Max is one of the most expensive repairs in the phone business. Then subtract the resale value. iPhones hold their price better than almost any Android phone in Nepal, which is a large part of why people accept the higher entry cost.</p>\n<p>You can see the effect by looking at what a three-year-old Pro Max sells for and comparing it with an Android flagship of the same age. Our <a href=\"/tech-lab#resale\">resale value tool</a> gives a rough estimate. If you change phones every two years, the gap in running cost between this and a Galaxy S26 Ultra becomes smaller than the Rs 54,900 difference at the counter. If you keep a phone for six years, resale matters less and the cheaper phone wins.</p>\n<h2>Official unit, EMI and grey imports</h2>\n<p>Buy from a seller who gives a VAT bill and official warranty. Grey units from Dubai, Hong Kong or the United States can look attractive, but they come with their own problems: American models use eSIM only, some regional models have different bands, and any phone brought in has to be registered in MDMS with the duty paid. Use our <a href=\"/tech-lab\">customs calculator</a> to work out the real cost before you decide that an imported phone is cheaper.</p>\n<p>At this price many buyers use EMI. Ask three questions: is there a processing fee, is the price on EMI the same as the cash price, and what happens if you want to close the loan early? A zero per cent plan on a higher sticker price is not zero per cent.</p>\n<h2>How it compares at around this price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>iPhone 18 Pro Max</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,79,900</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>50W wired, 25W MagSafe wireless, 4.5W reverse wire...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold8\">Samsung Galaxy Z Fold 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,74,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy</td><td>4,800mAh; 45W wired, 20W wireless, 4.5W reverse wi...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-v5\">Honor Magic V5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,66,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 5820 mAh, non-removable; 65W Honor SuperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-n5\">Oppo Find N5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,49,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 5600 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro-max\">iPhone 17 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5088 mAh, non-removable; Up to 50% in aroun...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Foldables now live in the same bracket. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold-7\">Galaxy Z Fold 7</a> and <a href=\"/products/honor-magic-v5\">Honor Magic V5</a> give you a small tablet that folds into a phone, which is a bigger change to daily life than a better zoom. They also have softer inner screens and more fragile hinges. If you hand your phone to children or work on building sites, a slab is the safer choice.</p>\n<p>Last year\'s <a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro-max\">iPhone 17 Pro Max</a> at Rs 2,42,499 is the other obvious rival. You lose the coating, the faster charging and the newest chip, and you save over Rs 37,000. For many people that is the smarter iPhone to buy this year.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it, and who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Buy it</strong> if you shoot a lot of distant subjects or video, already own a Mac or Apple Watch, and plan to sell the phone in two or three years.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy the 18 Pro instead</strong> if you like the cameras but not the weight.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy the 17 Pro Max instead</strong> if the price feels like a stretch.</li>\n<li><strong>Look at Android</strong> if you want a built-in stylus, a bigger battery or more zoom for less.</li>\n</ul>',8.6,'5x periscope telephoto with 48MP sensor\nBright 3,000-nit display with anti-reflective coating\nA20 Pro is Apple\'s first 2nm chip\n50W wired charging is a real step up for iPhone','Heavy at 249g\nStorage upgrades are very expensive in Nepal\nCosts Rs 54,900 more than a base Galaxy S26 Ultra','The best iPhone you can buy in Nepal, and the zoom camera alone separates it from the 18 Pro. Buy it if you live in Apple\'s ecosystem. Android buyers get more for less with the Galaxy S26 Ultra.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(52,93,20,'Galaxy S26 Ultra Review: Still the Android Flagship to Beat in Nepal?','samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy S26 Ultra yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>Nine months after launch, the Galaxy S26 Ultra is still the Android phone most Nepali buyers ask about first. It now costs <strong>Rs 2,24,999</strong> for 12GB/256GB, up from Rs 2,12,999 earlier in the year. The 512GB model is Rs 2,59,999 and the 16GB/1TB model is Rs 2,93,999.</p>\n\n<h2>Why it\'s still the default pick</h2>\n<p>No other phone sold here combines this many things: a 6.9-inch QHD+ display with Samsung\'s Privacy Display, a 200MP main camera, both 3x and 5x telephoto lenses, a built-in S Pen and <strong>seven years</strong> of Android updates. That last point matters if you keep a phone for four or five years, as many people do here.</p>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy is among the fastest chips in any phone. At 214g and 7.9mm, it\'s also noticeably lighter and thinner than the <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Where rivals have caught up</h2>\n<p>Battery is the weak spot. Samsung stays at 5,000mAh, while Chinese flagships at a lower price have moved to silicon-carbon cells: the <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> has 6,510mAh. Charging is 60W wired and 25W wireless, which is fine but not class-leading. The 3x camera is only 10MP.</p>\n\n<h2>Buy now or wait for the S27 Ultra?</h2>\n<p>Leaks point to a <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra\">Galaxy S27 Ultra</a> in January or February 2027, with a bigger battery. Our estimate is that it will cost more in Nepal, around Rs 2,40,000. If you need a phone now, the S26 Ultra is a safe buy with years of updates ahead. If you can wait three months, wait: either for the S27 Ultra or for the S26 Ultra to drop in price.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Full specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.9-inch QHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 3120×1440, 1–120Hz, 2,600 nits peak, Privacy Display</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB / 1TB (UFS 4.0)</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>200MP f/1.4 main (OIS) + 50MP ultra wide + 10MP 3x telephoto + 50MP 5x periscope | 12MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>5,000mAh; 60W wired, 25W wireless, 4.5W reverse wireless</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, One UI 8.5 (7 years of updates)</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>5G, Wi-Fi 7, Bluetooth, UWB, USB-C</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>214g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>163.6 × 78.1 × 7.9 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Privacy Display explained</h2>\n<p>The feature people ask about most is the one that sounds least exciting. Privacy Display narrows the viewing angle of the screen so that someone sitting beside you sees a dark or blurred panel. You can switch it on for the whole screen, or only when particular apps or the PIN pad are open. In a microbus from Lagankhel, in a bank queue or at a shared desk, that is worth having, especially with mobile banking and eSewa on the phone.</p>\n<p>It has a cost. With the mode on, the screen is a little dimmer and colours shift if you are not looking straight at it. Leave it automatic for sensitive apps and off for video, and you will rarely notice the trade.</p>\n<h2>The camera system in plain words</h2>\n<p>Samsung uses four rear cameras, and each has a job. The 200MP main camera with an f/1.4 lens gathers a great deal of light, so indoor photos at a wedding or a puja look clean without flash. It usually combines pixels into 12MP or 50MP pictures; use the full 200MP only in strong daylight when you plan to crop or print.</p>\n<p>The 50MP ultra wide is good enough for landscapes and for close-up macro shots. The 50MP periscope with 5x zoom is the travel lens: peaks from Nagarkot, birds at Koshi Tappu, the details on a temple roof. The weak member is the 10MP 3x camera. It covers the portrait range that people use constantly, and it is the lowest-resolution sensor of the group, so portraits sometimes look softer than on rivals that put a large sensor there. In good light the phone often crops from the main camera instead, and you should let it.</p>\n<p>If portraits are your main interest, compare with the <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> before you buy. If you want one phone that does everything reasonably well, Samsung remains the safer answer.</p>\n<h2>S Pen: useful or decoration?</h2>\n<p>The stylus lives inside the phone and is always charged enough to write. People who use it tend to use it for small things: signing a PDF, marking up a screenshot, jotting a phone number on the locked screen, cropping a photo accurately. It no longer works as a Bluetooth remote for the camera, which Samsung removed in an earlier generation. If you never wrote on a phone before, try it for a week before telling yourself you need it.</p>\n<h2>Battery life, honestly</h2>\n<p>A 5,000mAh battery was large in 2020 and is ordinary in 2026. The efficient chip and the adaptive screen still get most people through a day, and the 60W wired charging makes a difference compared with older Ultras. But if you are comparing it with phones that carry 6,500 or 7,500mAh, you will charge the Samsung more often. Trekkers, delivery riders and people who live with load-shedding in mind should give that proper weight.</p>\n<p>Remember that the charger is sold separately. A 60W-capable USB PD charger with PPS is what you need. A cheap 65W laptop charger without PPS will fall back to a slower speed.</p>\n<h2>Seven years of updates: what it is worth</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s promise covers Android version upgrades and security patches into 2033. The obvious benefit is safety for banking apps, which stop supporting old Android versions. The hidden benefit is resale: a phone with four years of updates left sells faster than one with none. If you pass phones on to parents or younger siblings, as most families here do, the S26 Ultra will still be useful at the end of the chain.</p>\n<h2>Which storage, which memory?</h2>\n<p>The step from 256GB to 512GB costs Rs 35,000. There is no memory card slot. Record 4K video regularly, keep offline maps and games, and 512GB makes sense. Use Google Photos backup and mostly stream, and 256GB is enough. The 1TB model at Rs 2,93,999 includes 16GB of memory, and it is aimed at people who edit video on the phone or use DeX as a desktop. Very few buyers need it.</p>\n<h2>DeX, the underrated reason</h2>\n<p>Connect the phone to a monitor with a USB-C cable and it becomes a simple desktop, with resizable windows, a mouse and a keyboard. For students writing assignments, or for a shop owner who needs spreadsheets now and then, it can replace a cheap laptop. Our <a href=\"/products?category=monitor\">monitor list</a> includes models with USB-C that charge the phone at the same time.</p>\n<h2>What else costs about the same</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,24,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 for Galaxy (3nm)</td><td>5,000mAh; 60W wired, 25W wireless, 4.5W reverse wi...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro\">iPhone 17 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,21,299</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 4252, non-removable; Up to 50% in 20 minute...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Google Pixel 10 Pro XL</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,00,000</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging;...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reverse</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> is the stronger camera and the weaker all-rounder, with fewer service points. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15\">OnePlus 15</a> at Rs 1,89,999 has far better battery life and charging, with a plainer camera set. The <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Pixel 10 Pro XL</a> takes beautiful, consistent photos, but Google has no official presence in Nepal, so warranty means sending it abroad. Samsung\'s advantage is dull and important: authorised service, spare parts in Kathmandu and official pricing.</p>\n<h2>Price history and timing</h2>\n<p>It is unusual for a phone to cost more at the end of its first year than in the middle. The rise from Rs 2,12,999 to Rs 2,24,999 reflects the wider increase in memory prices and exchange rates that we covered in <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">why gadget prices rose</a>. With the S27 series expected early in 2027, shops will want to clear stock, and Samsung often bundles earbuds or exchange bonuses in that window. If your current phone works, January is a better month to buy than November.</p>\n<h2>Before you pay</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check that the box carries the official distributor\'s sticker and that the IMEI is registered in MDMS.</li>\n<li>Ask whether screen-protection cover is included. Samsung sells accidental-damage plans, and the curved corners of a large phone find the floor sooner or later.</li>\n<li>Test the Privacy Display, the S Pen and all four cameras in the shop.</li>\n<li>Compare the EMI price with the cash price on the same bill.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<p>Buy it if you want one phone to keep for a long time, use the stylus or DeX, and value local service. Choose something else if battery life, portraits or price come first. And if you already own an S24 or S25 Ultra, there is nothing here that should make you hurry.</p>',8.7,'Most complete feature set of any Android phone in Nepal\nSeven years of OS updates\nBuilt-in S Pen\nLighter and thinner than the iPhone 18 Pro Max','5,000mAh battery trails cheaper Chinese flagships\nPrice went up during 2026\nS27 Ultra is only a few months away','Still the safest high-end Android phone you can buy in Nepal. The update promise and the S Pen keep it ahead. Unless you need a phone right now, though, wait to see what the S27 Ultra does to its price.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(53,102,20,'Redmi Note 17 Pro Max Review: A 10,000mAh Battery for Rs 69,999','redmi-note-17-pro-max-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Redmi Note 17 Pro Max yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>Redmi Note phones are among the best-selling phones in Nepal every year, and the Note 17 Pro Max gives the series its biggest upgrade in years: a <strong>10,000mAh</strong> silicon-carbon battery. It costs <strong>Rs 69,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 79,999 for 12GB/256GB.</p>\n\n<h2>The battery changes how you use it</h2>\n<p>Ten thousand milliamp-hours is double what most phones had two years ago. Light users can go two days between charges, which helps on long bus journeys, treks and days with power cuts. When you do plug in, it charges at 100W. It can also charge another phone or earbuds at 27W over a cable.</p>\n\n<h2>Screen and build</h2>\n<p>The 6.83-inch 1.5K AMOLED reaches 3,500 nits and is covered with Gorilla Glass Victus 2. The phone carries IP68 and IP69K ratings, so rain and dust aren\'t a concern and it can handle high-pressure water jets. That\'s unusual protection at this price.</p>\n\n<h2>What you give up</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 is a mid-range chip. It handles social apps, YouTube and casual games without trouble, but it isn\'t a gaming phone. The cameras are a 50MP main with OIS and an 8MP ultra wide, with no telephoto. If zoom matters, the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> has a 3.5x periscope at the same Rs 69,999.</p>\n\n<h2>Rivals at this price</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a> (Rs 76,999) matches the battery with a 10,001mAh cell and adds a 144Hz screen. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> (Rs 54,999) has an 8,300mAh battery for Rs 15,000 less. And the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro</a> (Rs 57,999) keeps most of the Max\'s features with an 8,340mAh battery.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Full specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,500 nits peak, 12-bit, Gorilla Glass Victus 2</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main (OIS) + 8MP ultra wide | 32MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, HyperOS 3</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What ten thousand milliamp-hours feels like</h2>\n<p>Numbers this large are hard to picture, so think in days. A typical phone with 5,000mAh lasts a busy person from breakfast to bedtime. Twice that does not always mean two full days, because a bigger and brighter screen uses more, but it changes your habits. You stop carrying a power bank. You stop checking the percentage before a bus to Jhapa. You can leave the hotspot on for a laptop through a power cut without anxiety.</p>\n<p>Silicon-carbon chemistry is the reason a phone can hold this much without becoming a brick. Silicon stores more energy than the graphite used in older batteries. The technology is young, so long-term ageing is the open question. Xiaomi sets its own rating for charge cycles, and a battery this big goes through fewer cycles in the first place, since you charge it less often. That alone should slow the wear.</p>\n<p>Charging at 100W needs the right adapter. Check whether it is in your box, because bundles differ between shops and batches. With a generic 33W charger, an empty battery of this size will need well over two hours.</p>\n<h2>Is it heavy?</h2>\n<p>Yes, and you should hold it before ordering. A 6.83-inch phone with this battery cannot be light. Weight is not a flaw by itself, but it is the price of the headline feature. People with smaller hands, or anyone who reads in bed with the phone above their face, will be happier with the smaller <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>The screen is a class above the chip</h2>\n<p>A 1.5K AMOLED panel sits between Full HD+ and Quad HD+. Text looks crisper than on a Full HD+ phone of the same size, and the 120Hz refresh rate makes scrolling smooth. A peak of 3,500 nits is only reached on small parts of the picture, usually in HDR video, but general outdoor brightness is high as well. Twelve-bit colour is mostly a marketing line, as very little content uses it.</p>\n<p>Gorilla Glass Victus 2 on the front deserves more attention than it gets. It is the glass from flagship phones, and it handles drops onto rough surfaces better than the cheaper types normally used at this price.</p>\n<h2>IP68 and IP69K: what they cover</h2>\n<p>IP68 means protection against dust and against being under still, fresh water for a limited time. IP69K refers to hot, high-pressure water jets. Together they suggest a phone that will survive monsoon rain, a spilled cup of tea, a muddy trail and a rinse under the tap. They do not cover salt water, soap, steam or damage from a fall that loosened the seals, and water damage is usually excluded from warranty whatever the rating says. Enjoy the safety margin, but do not go swimming with it.</p>\n<h2>Performance for real tasks</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 belongs to Qualcomm\'s middle family. It is efficient, which suits the purpose of this phone. Facebook, TikTok, banking apps, Google Maps, split-screen messaging and video calls all run well. In PUBG Mobile and Free Fire you can expect smooth play at medium settings. Heavier games will need lower graphics, and after a long session you will feel the difference from a phone such as the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a>, which costs the same and has a much faster chip.</p>\n<p>That comparison is the heart of the decision. At Rs 69,999 you can have speed or endurance. The POCO gives you the first with a good battery. The Redmi gives you the second with an adequate chip.</p>\n<h2>Camera expectations</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera with optical stabilisation is the one to use. In daylight it produces sharp, slightly warm pictures, and stabilisation keeps indoor shots usable. The 8MP ultra wide is fine for a group photo in good light and weak after dark. There is no telephoto, so zoom beyond 2x is a digital crop. The 32MP selfie camera has plenty of detail for video calls and social media.</p>\n<p>If you photograph a lot, the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> with its 3.5x periscope is a better tool at the same price, and the <a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a> at Rs 67,999 leans heavily on its main camera. Neither will last as long between charges.</p>\n<h2>Software and support</h2>\n<p>HyperOS 3 on Android 16 brings plenty of features along with some clutter. After setup, remove the extra apps and switch off recommendations in the built-in tools. Xiaomi\'s update promise for the Note series is shorter than Samsung\'s, so ask for the exact number of years in writing if it matters to you. Service is one of the advantages of buying Redmi in Nepal: there are authorised centres and parts for popular Note models are easy to get.</p>\n<h2>Eight or twelve gigabytes?</h2>\n<p>Both versions have 256GB of storage. The extra Rs 10,000 buys 4GB of memory. On a phone with this chip, 8GB is enough for most people today. Twelve helps if you keep dozens of apps open or plan to use the phone for four years. If money is tight, spend the difference on a good case and a genuine 100W charger instead.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives at about Rs 70,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W reverse...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone (4a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>5,080mAh; 50W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;67,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Honor SuperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-15-pro\">Realme 15 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;66,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W wired</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Also look at the <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a>. It has nearly the same battery capacity and a 144Hz screen, and it costs about Rs 7,000 more. And consider whether you need the full 10,000mAh at all: the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> with 8,300mAh costs Rs 15,000 less and is already far ahead of ordinary phones.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Yes:</strong> riders, field workers, trekkers, students in hostels with unreliable power, and parents who forget to charge their phones.</li>\n<li><strong>Yes:</strong> anyone who runs a mobile hotspot for hours.</li>\n<li><strong>No:</strong> competitive gamers and zoom photographers.</li>\n<li><strong>No:</strong> people who want a light, pocketable phone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For its intended audience it is one of the easiest recommendations of the year. Everyone else should decide what they would do with the extra battery before carrying it around.</p>',8.4,'10,000mAh battery with 100W charging\nIP68 and IP69K protection\nBright 1.5K AMOLED with Gorilla Glass Victus 2\nReverse charging for other devices','No telephoto camera; ultra wide is only 8MP\nMid-range chip, not built for heavy gaming\nRealme P4 Power has a faster 144Hz screen','If battery life is your top priority, this is the phone to buy around Rs 70,000. Photographers who want zoom should look at the Nothing Phone 4a instead.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(54,117,20,'MacBook Air M5 Review: The Laptop Most People in Nepal Should Buy','macbook-air-m5-13-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the MacBook Air M5 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The 13-inch MacBook Air with M5 arrived in Nepal on 10 April 2026 at <strong>Rs 2,34,900</strong> for 16GB of memory and 512GB of storage. For students, writers, developers and office workers, it\'s the laptop we\'d point to first. That\'s not because it\'s cheap, but because it is hard to regret.</p>\n\n<h2>Why it works so well</h2>\n<p>It has no fan, so it makes no noise, and the M5 (10-core CPU, up to a 10-core GPU) stays fast for everyday work and light photo or video editing. The 16GB base memory is the right amount for the next five years, where 8GB machines will start to struggle. Battery life easily covers a working day away from a socket, which counts for a lot when the power goes out.</p>\n\n<h2>The compromises</h2>\n<p>The 13.6-inch display (2560×1664, 500 nits) is sharp and colour-accurate, but it runs at 60Hz rather than 120Hz, and it isn\'t bright enough for HDR work. Going from 512GB to 1TB costs Rs 55,000 (Rs 2,89,900 total). An external SSD is the cheaper fix if you only need space for archives.</p>\n\n<h2>What else to consider</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Less money:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> (Rs 1,34,999) uses an iPhone-class A18 Pro chip with 8GB of memory. That\'s fine for study and browsing, but the 256GB model has no Touch ID or keyboard backlight.</li>\n<li><strong>Windows with OLED:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3406ga\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED</a> (Rs 2,54,999) gives you 32GB and 1TB with an OLED touchscreen.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigger screen:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-15\">15-inch Air</a> costs Rs 2,69,900.</li>\n<li><strong>Heavy video work:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">MacBook Pro 14 (M5)</a> adds a fan and a 120Hz XDR display.</li>\n</ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Full specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Apple MacBook Air 13 (M5, 16GB/512GB)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>13.6-inch (diagonal) LED-backlit display with IPS technology;2 2560-by-1664 native resolution at 224 pixels per inch 500 nits brightness, Support for 1 billion...</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple M5 (10-core CPU, 8/10-core GPU)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X Unified Memory</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB NVMe SSD 6,473 MB/s Read speeds and 6,558 MB/s write speeds.</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>12MP Center Stage camera with support for Desk View 1080p HD video recording Advanced image signal processor with computational video</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Built-in 53.8‑watt‑hour lithium‑polymer battery , 11 to 13 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>macOS</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 7 (802.11be) and Bluetooth 6</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.24KG</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Who the Air is really for</h2>\n<p>The Air is easy to recommend because most laptop work is light. Writing a thesis, running Excel sheets with a few thousand rows, coding in VS Code, attending Zoom classes, editing photos, managing a small online shop: none of these stress an M5. What people notice is everything around the chip. The lid opens with one finger and the screen is already awake. The keyboard is comfortable. The trackpad is the best on any laptop. And the machine can sit in a bag asleep for a week and still have charge.</p>\n<p>In a place with power cuts, that last point changes how you work. A laptop that loses only a little overnight means you are not planning your day around a socket. Apple\'s own figure for video playback is optimistic, as every manufacturer\'s is; the listing in our catalogue quotes 11 to 13 hours, which is a fair expectation for mixed office use at sensible brightness.</p>\n<h2>No fan: what that means under load</h2>\n<p>With no fan, the aluminium body has to carry away the heat. For short bursts the Air is as quick as a MacBook Pro with the same chip. Keep it busy for fifteen or twenty minutes, exporting a long 4K video or compiling a large project, and it slows down to keep cool. In a warm room in Nepalgunj in May it will slow down sooner.</p>\n<p>So ask yourself how often you do work that lasts longer than a tea break. If the answer is daily, the <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">MacBook Pro 14</a> is worth the extra. If it is once a month, the Air will simply take a few minutes longer.</p>\n<h2>Is 16GB enough?</h2>\n<p>For almost everyone, yes. macOS handles memory well, and 16GB leaves room for a browser with thirty tabs, Office and a photo editor together. You cannot add memory later, though, so think about the next five years and not about this semester. People who run virtual machines, Docker containers, large Lightroom catalogues or local AI models should look at 24GB.</p>\n<p>Storage is the other permanent choice. The Rs 55,000 step from 512GB to 1TB is steep. An external NVMe drive of the same size costs a fraction of that and works well for photos and archives. Keep active projects on the internal drive and move the rest. Our <a href=\"/products?category=storage\">storage section</a> lists portable SSDs with current prices.</p>\n<h2>macOS if you come from Windows</h2>\n<p>Most popular software is available: Microsoft Office, Adobe\'s apps, AutoCAD, Chrome, Zoom, VS Code. The trouble spots are specific. Some accounting packages used in Nepal, certain government and bank portals that depend on old browser components, Tally without workarounds, many engineering tools and a large number of games are Windows only. Write down the five programs you cannot live without and check each one before buying. Windows can run in a virtual machine on Apple silicon, but that is an extra cost and it uses the Arm version of Windows.</p>\n<p>Nepali typing works without extra software. macOS includes Devanagari keyboard layouts, and Unicode text behaves properly in Pages, Word and the browser. Older Preeti-font documents will need converting.</p>\n<h2>Ports and accessories</h2>\n<p>You get two Thunderbolt USB-C ports, MagSafe charging and a headphone jack. There is no HDMI, no USB-A and no card reader. A small hub is close to compulsory for students who present in classrooms with HDMI projectors. MagSafe is worth using in shared rooms, because a foot caught in the cable pulls out the plug and not the laptop.</p>\n<p>The 12MP Center Stage camera keeps you in the middle of the picture during calls. For online teachers and remote workers it is a visible improvement over the poor webcams on most laptops at this price.</p>\n<h2>Price in Nepal: why the figure moves</h2>\n<p>The launch price was Rs 2,34,900 and current listings are around Rs 2,42,499, which reflects the exchange rate and stock. Discounts appear around Dashain and Tihar and for students at some resellers, so ask. Compare what the price includes: official units carry Apple\'s one-year warranty, honoured at authorised service providers in Kathmandu, while some imported machines have only a shop warranty. Check the serial number on Apple\'s coverage page before paying, and check the keyboard layout, as units from some countries have a different Enter key and extra symbols.</p>\n<h2>Windows alternatives in the same budget</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Apple MacBook Air 13 (M5, 16GB/512GB)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>Apple M5 (10-core CPU, 8/10-core GPU)</td><td>Built-in 53.8‑watt‑hour lithium‑polymer battery ,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-f16-fx608jhr-i7-14650hx-rtx-5050\">ASUS TUF Gaming F16 FX608JHR (Core i7-14650HX, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>Intel® Core™ i7 Processor 14650HX 2.2 GHz (30MB Cache, up to...</td><td>90WHrs, 4S1P, 4-cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9350-2024-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-512gb-134-fhd\">Dell XPS 13 9350 2024</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,39,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 256V (TSMC N3B), 8 cores / 8 threads (bas...</td><td>Li-Ion 55 Wh; 60W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-2024-intel-i5-12450hx-rtx-4050\">Lenovo LOQ 15 15IAX9 2024 (Intel Core i5-12450HX)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,45,900</td><td>Intel Core i5-12450HX (Intel 7), 8 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 60 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-prestige-16-studio-2023-i7-13700h-rtx-4050\">MSI Prestige 16 Studio A13V 2023 (Intel Core i7-13700H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,35,000</td><td>Intel Core i7-13700H (Intel 7), 14 cores / 20 threads</td><td>Li-Po 82 Wh</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For the same money, Windows laptops split into two groups. Gaming machines such as the <a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-a16-fa608umi-ryzen-7-260-rtx-5060\">ASUS TUF A16 with an RTX 5060</a> offer far more graphics power and far less battery life. Thin machines such as the <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-s3407ca-core-ultra-7-255h\">Vivobook S14</a> or <a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9350-2024-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-512gb-134-fhd\">Dell XPS 13</a> offer OLED or sharper screens, Windows software and often more ports, though not always the same silence and standby time.</p>\n<h2>Buying used or older instead</h2>\n<p>An M4 Air is still a very good laptop, and even an M2 will serve a student for years. If you go that way, insist on 16GB of memory, look at the battery cycle count in System Information, check that the machine is not enrolled in a company\'s device management, and make sure the previous owner has removed it from their Apple account. Avoid 8GB models now unless the price is very low.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> is the cheap way into macOS. It suits schoolwork and browsing, but the 8GB of memory is a ceiling you will reach. If you can afford the Air, buy the Air.</p>\n<h2>Common questions at the counter</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Midnight or silver?</strong> Dark finishes show fingerprints and chips around the ports. Silver and starlight age better.</li>\n<li><strong>13 or 15 inches?</strong> The 15-inch model gives you a bigger desk to work on and better speakers, at the cost of weight and Rs 35,000 or so. Take the 13 if you commute.</li>\n<li><strong>Do I need antivirus?</strong> Keep macOS updated, install software from the App Store or the developer\'s own site, and you will not need a paid one.</li>\n</ul>',9.0,'Silent fanless design\n16GB memory as standard\nBattery lasts a full working day\nM5 has headroom for years of updates','60Hz display, not suited to HDR editing\nRs 55,000 to go from 512GB to 1TB\nNo gaming to speak of on macOS','The easiest laptop recommendation in Nepal right now. Unless you game on Windows or edit video for a living, buy the base 16GB/512GB model and keep it for years.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(55,124,20,'HP Victus 15 Review: The Cheapest Real Gaming Laptop in Nepal','hp-victus-15-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the HP Victus 15 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>For a lot of students in Nepal, the first serious gaming laptop is set by budget, not specs. The HP Victus 15 is the cheapest laptop in our catalogue with a proper NVIDIA GPU. It comes in two versions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rs 1,49,000:</strong> AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS with an RTX 3050 6GB</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 1,60,999:</strong> Intel Core i5-13420H with an RTX 4050 6GB</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both have 16GB of DDR5-5600, a 512GB PCIe Gen4 SSD and a 15.6-inch 1080p 144Hz screen.</p>\n\n<h2>Spend the extra Rs 12,000</h2>\n<p>The RTX 4050 is a generation newer than the 3050 and supports DLSS 3 frame generation, which keeps new games playable at 1080p for longer. Over three or four years of ownership, the extra Rs 12,000 is the best money you\'ll spend on this laptop. The RTX 3050 version is fine for esports titles like Valorant and older games.</p>\n\n<h2>Plan for storage</h2>\n<p>512GB fills up fast. A few modern games can take up most of it once Windows and your apps are installed. Budget for a second SSD early.</p>\n\n<h2>If you can stretch the budget</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> (Rs 1,94,999) moves up to an RTX 5050 at 100W with a 100% sRGB screen. That\'s a better long-term buy if Rs 45,000 more is possible. If you mostly want to play <a href=\"/products/gta-6\">GTA VI</a>, note that it\'s only out on consoles for now, and a <a href=\"/products/sony-playstation-5\">PS5</a> costs less than either laptop.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Full specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>HP Victus 15</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 144Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What you can play, and at what settings</h2>\n<p>A 6GB graphics card in 2026 needs realistic expectations, so here is how to think about it. Esports games are the easy part. Valorant, Counter-Strike 2, League of Legends, Dota 2 and Rocket League are designed to run on modest hardware, and either version of the Victus should keep them above 100 frames per second at 1080p, which is what the 144Hz screen is for.</p>\n<p>Older big titles such as GTA V, Red Dead Redemption 2, The Witcher 3 and Forza Horizon 5 are comfortable at medium to high settings. New releases built on Unreal Engine 5 are the hard part. They often want 8GB of video memory for high textures, so plan on medium textures with DLSS set to Quality or Balanced. The RTX 4050 version adds frame generation in games that support it, which makes motion smoother once the base frame rate is already playable. It does not rescue a game running at 25 frames per second.</p>\n<h2>Power limits: the number that is missing from the box</h2>\n<p>Two laptops with the same RTX 4050 can perform quite differently, because manufacturers choose how many watts the chip may use. The Victus 15 is built to a budget, so its graphics power limit is lower than in thicker and more expensive machines. That keeps heat and noise under control, and it means you should not compare it with benchmark videos of a gaming laptop twice its price. When you watch reviews online, look for this exact model and the same graphics power figure.</p>\n<h2>Heat and noise in a Nepali room</h2>\n<p>Gaming laptops are tested in air-conditioned rooms, and most of us do not live in one. At 32 degrees in Biratnagar the fans will run near full speed during a game, the keyboard area above the number row will get hot, and the chip will slow down slightly to protect itself. Raise the back of the laptop by two or three centimetres with a stand or even a book, keep the rear vents away from the wall, and clean the dust out once a year. Those three habits do more than an expensive cooling pad.</p>\n<h2>The screen is the weakest part</h2>\n<p>The 144Hz panel is fast enough for games, but listings do not mention wide colour coverage, and budget Victus screens have traditionally been dim with muted colour. For gaming and coding that is acceptable. For photo editing, video grading or design work, it is not, and you should plan to use an external monitor. A 24-inch IPS display costs from around Rs 18,000, and our <a href=\"/products?category=monitor\">monitors page</a> shows what is available.</p>\n<h2>Upgrades that make sense</h2>\n<p>This is where the Victus earns its place. Memory sits in standard slots, so a machine that starts with 16GB can grow later. Check whether the 16GB is one stick or two: two sticks run in dual channel, which helps both games and integrated graphics. If you find a single stick, adding a matching one is the cheapest performance upgrade available.</p>\n<p>Storage is the first thing you will run out of. Windows, Office and three large games can fill a 512GB drive. Ask the shop whether your exact model has a second M.2 slot. If it does, a 1TB NVMe drive is a half-hour job. If not, replace the original and move it into a USB enclosure as a backup drive.</p>\n<h2>Battery and everyday use</h2>\n<p>Do not expect to sit through a full day of lectures without the charger. Gaming laptops of this class manage a few hours of browsing and documents when the dedicated graphics chip is switched off, and little more than an hour of gaming. Use the hybrid graphics mode in the HP app, drop the refresh rate to 60Hz on battery and lower the keyboard backlight.</p>\n<p>It is also a 15.6-inch machine with a large power brick. If you walk to college every day, carry both in a shop and then decide. Many students end up leaving it in the hostel and using a phone or tablet in class.</p>\n<h2>AMD or Intel version?</h2>\n<p>The Ryzen 5 8645HS is the newer and more efficient processor. The Core i5-13420H is older, but it comes with the better graphics card, and in games the graphics card decides. For gaming, take the Intel version with the RTX 4050. For programming, study and occasional Valorant, the AMD version saves Rs 12,000 and lasts longer away from a socket.</p>\n<h2>Service and warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>HP has authorised service in Kathmandu and partners in larger cities. Ask whether the warranty is from HP Nepal or from the importer, and get the period written on the bill. Gaming laptops have more moving parts than thin ones, and fans and hinges are the usual failures in year two, so a genuine two-year warranty is worth a few thousand rupees more than a vague one-year shop promise.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives at a similar price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>HP Victus 15</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-gaming-3-2023-i7-12700h\">Lenovo Ideapad Gaming 3 (12th Gen i7-12700H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>Intel Core i7-12700H, 14 cores / 20 threads</td><td>45 Wh Li-ion; 170W AC Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-book4-pro\">Samsung Galaxy Book4 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155H</td><td>76Wh, 65W PD Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-anl16-71g-2025-intel-core-i5-13420h-rtx-3050\">Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-71G 2025 (Intel Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,51,999</td><td>Intel Core i5-13420H (Intel 7), 8 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 53 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-m1407ka-2025\">ASUS VivoBook 14 M1407KA 2025</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,45,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen AI 5 340 (4nm), 6 cores / 12 threads (base configu...</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-anl16-71g-2025-intel-core-i5-13420h-rtx-3050\">Acer Nitro Lite 16</a> has a larger 16:10 screen that is nicer for study. Older laptops such as the IdeaPad Gaming 3 sometimes appear at the same price with a stronger processor but an ageing graphics card. Look past the CPU name and compare the GPU and its wattage. If you can wait and save, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> with an RTX 5050 is a generation ahead and has a much better screen.</p>\n<h2>A short checklist before buying</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Open Task Manager and confirm the exact GPU name and its memory.</li>\n<li>Check for dead pixels on a black and a white screen, and for backlight bleeding at the corners.</li>\n<li>Press every key, and listen to the fans at full speed for grinding.</li>\n<li>Make sure Windows is activated with a genuine licence and not an activation script.</li>\n<li>Ask for the original charger. A lower-watt replacement will limit performance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should choose the Victus 15</h2>\n<p>First-time buyers with a firm limit of about Rs 1.6 lakh, who play esports and older titles, study engineering or IT, and are prepared to add storage later. If you want to play the newest games at high settings for several years, or you need a laptop that lasts through the day, keep saving or choose a different type of machine.</p>',7.8,'Lowest price for an RTX laptop in our catalogue\n16GB DDR5 and a 144Hz screen as standard\nRTX 4050 version is good value','512GB SSD fills quickly\nRTX 3050 version is showing its age\nIntel version uses an older 13th-gen chip','A sensible first gaming laptop if your budget ends at about Rs 1.6 lakh. Buy the RTX 4050 version, and add storage early.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(56,153,20,'PS5 in Nepal (2026): Digital vs Slim Disc vs PS5 Pro — Which to Buy','ps5-digital-slim-pro-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of every PS5 model side by side yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>With <a href=\"/products/gta-6\">GTA VI</a> out, a lot of people in Nepal are buying their first PS5. As of 20 November 2026 the three models cost:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digital Edition (825GB):</strong> Rs 95,000</li>\n<li><strong>PS5 Slim Disc Edition (1TB):</strong> Rs 1,39,999</li>\n<li><strong>PS5 Pro (2TB):</strong> Rs 1,45,000</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The surprising result: the Pro beats the Slim Disc on value</h2>\n<p>At these listings, the Pro costs only about Rs 5,000 more than the Slim Disc. For that you get double the storage (2TB), a 16.7-TFLOPS GPU instead of 10.3, and better image quality in games with PS5 Pro enhancements. The catch is that the Pro has no disc drive. Sony\'s detachable drive is sold separately. If you only buy digital games, the Slim Disc is hard to recommend at this price.</p>\n\n<h2>The Digital Edition is the budget pick</h2>\n<p>At Rs 95,000 it\'s Rs 50,000 cheaper than the Pro, and it plays every PS5 game, including GTA VI. The trade-off is storage: 825GB doesn\'t last long with today\'s game sizes, so plan to add an M.2 SSD later. Every PS5 model has a slot for one.</p>\n\n<h2>RAM doesn\'t change between models</h2>\n<p>All three have 16GB of GDDR6. The Pro adds 2GB of DDR5 for system tasks, but the real upgrade is the GPU. If a shop advertises a \"higher RAM\" PS5, that isn\'t how Sony sells them.</p>\n\n<h2>Buying tips for Nepal</h2>\n<p>Retail prices vary with stock and the season. Compare at least two sellers, ask for the warranty terms in writing, and check that the console\'s region matches the PlayStation Store account you plan to use. A spare <a href=\"/products/sony-dualsense-wireless-controller\">DualSense controller</a> costs Rs 9,500.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The three models side by side</h2>\n<p>All PS5 consoles use the same family of processor: eight AMD Zen 2 cores and 16GB of fast GDDR6 memory. That is why every PS5 game runs on every PS5. The differences are the graphics section, the storage and the disc drive.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Listed price</th><th>Storage</th><th>Disc drive</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Digital Edition</td><td>Rs 95,000</td><td>825GB</td><td>No (sold separately)</td><td>Lowest entry price, downloads only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slim Disc Edition</td><td>Rs 1,39,999</td><td>1TB</td><td>Yes</td><td>People who buy and resell discs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PS5 Pro</td><td>Rs 1,45,000</td><td>2TB</td><td>No (sold separately)</td><td>4K TV owners who want the best picture</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Discs or downloads: do the sums first</h2>\n<p>Physical games matter more in Nepal than in many countries. Internet packages can make a 100GB download slow, and discs can be swapped with friends or sold when you finish. If you buy six games a year and sell each one for half of what you paid, a disc drive can save you tens of thousands of rupees over the life of the console.</p>\n<p>On the other side, digital stores run sales all year, and subscription plans include a large library. With a stable fibre connection and a habit of waiting for discounts, you may never miss the drive. There is a trap, though: a digital game is tied to your account for ever and has no resale value.</p>\n<p>If you are unsure, remember that Sony sells a detachable drive for both the Digital Edition and the Pro. Check that it is available and what it costs before you count on adding it later. At the moment availability here is irregular.</p>\n<h2>What the Pro improves</h2>\n<p>The Pro\'s larger graphics section, at 16.7 TFLOPS compared with about 10.3, does two things. It lets games run at higher resolution while keeping 60 frames per second, and it uses Sony\'s AI upscaling to sharpen the image. In games with a Pro patch you often get the quality mode\'s visuals at the performance mode\'s speed.</p>\n<p>Whether you can see that depends on your television. On a 4K set of 55 inches or more, viewed from two or three metres, the difference is visible in fine detail and steadier motion. On a 1080p TV or a 32-inch screen, most people will struggle to tell. If your TV is not 4K, put the money towards a better one before you think about the Pro.</p>\n<h2>TV features that matter</h2>\n<p>Look for HDMI 2.1, a 120Hz panel, variable refresh rate and a low input lag game mode. A basic 4K 60Hz TV still looks good with a PS5 and is all that many games need. Use the HDMI cable from the box, as it is certified for the full bandwidth, and turn on the TV\'s game mode and its enhanced HDMI setting. More disappointing first impressions come from a wrong TV setting than from the console.</p>\n<h2>Storage: plan for it on day one</h2>\n<p>System files take up a part of the drive, so the Digital Edition\'s 825GB leaves much less for games. Recent blockbusters take between 80GB and 200GB each. Every PS5 has a slot for an M.2 NVMe drive. Buy a PCIe Gen4 model with a read speed of at least 5,500MB/s and a heatsink that fits the slot. Installing it takes a screwdriver and ten minutes, and Sony\'s support page shows each step.</p>\n<p>An ordinary USB hard drive can store PS4 games and play them, but PS5 games can only be kept there as an archive and must be copied back before playing. It is a cheap way to avoid downloading a game twice.</p>\n<h2>GTA VI and the reason for the rush</h2>\n<p>The launch of GTA VI sent many buyers to the shops at once, and prices moved with demand. If that game is your only reason, any of the three consoles will run it. Wait a few weeks if you can. Launch-window stock is expensive, bundles appear later, and the first patches usually improve performance. Our <a href=\"/news/gta-6-launch-record-sales-how-to-buy-nepal\">GTA VI buying article</a> covers editions and regional accounts.</p>\n<h2>Accounts, regions and paying for games</h2>\n<p>There is no Nepal PlayStation Store, so most people use an account from another country. Choose the region carefully at the start, because it cannot be changed, and downloadable content must match the region of the game. Gift cards must match the account region as well. Buy them only from sellers you trust, as reversed or stolen codes can get an account banned. Games on disc work on any console, but extra content for them follows the region of the disc.</p>\n<p>Online play in most paid games needs a PlayStation Plus subscription. Free-to-play games such as Fortnite do not. Work out the yearly cost when you compare consoles with a gaming laptop.</p>\n<h2>Power, heat and safety</h2>\n<p>Use a proper surge protector, and better still a UPS that lets you shut down safely. A power cut in the middle of a system update or a save can damage data. Put the console in rest mode only when your power is reliable; otherwise shut it down fully. Leave at least ten centimetres of space around it, do not enclose it in a glass cabinet, and clean the dust ports with a soft brush and a vacuum cleaner every few months. Kathmandu dust is the most common cause of noisy fans.</p>\n<h2>Which one to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digital Edition:</strong> the right choice for a tight budget, a good internet connection and people who mostly play one or two big games. Add an SSD later.</li>\n<li><strong>Slim Disc:</strong> only if you already own PS4 or PS5 discs, or will buy and sell them regularly. At today\'s listing its price is too close to the Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Pro:</strong> the best hardware, and at a gap of about Rs 5,000 over the Slim Disc it is strangely good value. Make sure your TV deserves it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying used</h2>\n<p>A second-hand PS5 can be a good deal. Ask to see it running a demanding game for twenty minutes and listen for loud fans or coil whine. Check that the disc drive reads both a game and a Blu-ray if it has one, that the controller sticks do not drift, and that the console is not banned: sign in with your own account and go online. Look at the HDMI port for bent pins, since that repair is common and not cheap. Original boxes and bills help if you want to sell it on later.</p>',8.8,'Huge game library, now including GTA VI\nPS5 Pro is only about Rs 5,000 more than the Slim Disc at current listings\nM.2 SSD slot on every model','Prices rose during 2026\n825GB Digital Edition fills up quickly\nDisc drive costs extra on the Digital and Pro','Buy the Digital Edition on a budget, or the Pro if you have a 4K TV. At current Nepal prices the Slim Disc only makes sense if you rely on physical games.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-21 18:15:00','2026-11-21 18:15:00'),(87,97,20,'Galaxy A57 5G Review: The Safe Mid-Ranger, but Not the Best Value','samsung-galaxy-a57-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy A57 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Galaxy A57 5G costs <strong>Rs 96,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 1,07,999 for 12GB/256GB in Nepal. It\'s the phone many people buy because it\'s a Samsung, and that\'s not a bad reason: you get six years of Android updates, Samsung\'s service network and a well-made 6.9mm body.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 6.7-inch Super AMOLED at 120Hz is one of the better screens in this range, and the Exynos 1680 (4nm) is fast enough for everyday use and casual gaming. A 50MP main camera with a 12MP ultrawide covers most situations. The six-year update promise is the real selling point. Few rivals at this price match it.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The 5,000mAh battery and 45W charging are now ordinary. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">realme P4 Power</a> has twice the battery for Rs 20,000 less, and the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone (4a)</a> adds a 3.5x periscope camera for Rs 69,999. The 5MP macro camera is filler.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<p>If you value software support, resale value and Samsung service centres, it\'s a sound choice. If you want the most hardware for the money, look elsewhere. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=samsung-galaxy-a57-5g&g2=nothing-phone-4a\">Compare it with the Nothing Phone (4a)</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The full sheet</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy A57 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.7-inch FHD+ Super AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass Victus+</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Exynos 1680 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB / 512GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main + 12MP ultra wide + 5MP macro | 12MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, One UI 8.5 (6 years of updates)</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>About 179g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>6.9mm thick</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why so many people end up with this phone</h2>\n<p>Walk into a mobile shop in Butwal or Dharan and say you have about a lakh and want something reliable. The A57 is very likely the first box that comes out. Some of that is habit, and some of it is fair. Samsung has authorised service in more Nepali cities than any other Android brand, spare parts are stocked, and a shop can tell you the cost of a screen before you break it. A phone is a four-year purchase for most families, and support is part of what you are buying.</p>\n<p>The design helps as well. At 6.9mm and about 179 grams it feels closer to a flagship than to a mid-ranger. Many rivals with huge batteries weigh 210 grams or more. If you have ever owned a heavy phone, you know the difference on a long scooter ride with it in a front pocket.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 6.7-inch Super AMOLED is the part you will enjoy most. Colours are rich without being silly in the Natural mode, black is truly black for night reading, and 120Hz keeps scrolling steady. The glass is Gorilla Glass Victus+, which resists drops well. There is nothing exotic here, such as the 144Hz or 1.5K panels you find on Chinese phones, and nobody who is not comparing two phones next to each other will miss them.</p>\n<h2>Performance over time</h2>\n<p>The Exynos 1680 is a 4nm chip in the upper middle of the market. It handles Facebook, banking, Google Meet and ordinary photo editing without hesitation. Games such as Mobile Legends and Free Fire run well. Genshin Impact and similar titles need reduced settings.</p>\n<p>What matters more is how it behaves in year three. Samsung\'s One UI has become lighter, and 8GB of memory is still comfortable for this class. If you plan to use all six years of updates, the 12GB version for Rs 11,000 more is easier to live with at the end. If you change phones every three years, save the money.</p>\n<h2>Camera: good where it counts</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera is dependable. Daylight shots are sharp, faces look natural, and night mode brightens a street without turning it orange. The 12MP ultra wide is above average for the class, since most competitors use 8MP. It also has enough detail for group photos indoors. The 5MP macro is there to fill a space on the back.</p>\n<p>There is no telephoto. Two-times zoom, which crops the main sensor, is fine. Beyond that, detail goes quickly. A parent photographing a school programme from the back row will be happier with the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a>.</p>\n<p>The 12MP front camera sounds modest beside 32MP rivals. In practice it produces pleasing skin and handles backlight well, which counts for more on video calls.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging in a normal week</h2>\n<p>Five thousand milliamp-hours gets most people to bedtime. Heavy users on mobile data will need a top-up. The 45W charging refills about two-thirds of the battery in half an hour with a suitable charger, which is not supplied. Wireless charging is not available. If your house loses power for hours, rivals with 7,000mAh or more give you a wider safety margin, and no software feature makes up for that.</p>\n<h2>Six years of updates in plain terms</h2>\n<p>The phone launches with Android 16, and Samsung promises six generations of upgrades and six years of security patches. Banking apps and digital wallets drop old Android versions over time, so this directly affects how long the phone stays useful. It also affects resale. A two-year-old Galaxy A with four years of support left is easy to sell, while a similar phone from a brand with two years of support is not.</p>\n<h2>Price: the uncomfortable part</h2>\n<p>At Rs 96,999 the A57 costs nearly as much as phones with flagship chips.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy A57 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;96,999</td><td>Exynos 1680 (4nm)</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;98,499</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>7,400mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">Poco X8 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;94,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500s (3nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 8500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16f-5g\">OPPO Reno 16F</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Energy (4nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10a\">Google Pixel 10a</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;86,000</td><td>Google Tensor G4 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5100 mAh, non-removable; 30W charger; 10W wi...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a> and <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">POCO X8 Pro Max</a> are much faster. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17t\">Xiaomi 17T</a> has a stronger camera set. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">OnePlus Nord 6</a> is cheaper and lasts far longer on a charge. Each of them gives up something the Samsung has, usually years of updates, service reach or slimness. You have to decide what those are worth to you, and it is perfectly reasonable to decide that they are worth it.</p>\n<p>There is also competition from inside the family. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Galaxy A37 5G</a> costs around Rs 22,000 less, and shares the same software approach. If your use is light, the A37 is the smarter Samsung. And during festival sales, last year\'s Galaxy S models sometimes fall close to the A57\'s price, at which point they are the better phone.</p>\n<h2>Who it fits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parents and relatives</strong> who want something familiar, light and supported for years.</li>\n<li><strong>Office users</strong> who live in email, calls and documents and value local service.</li>\n<li><strong>People in smaller cities</strong> where Samsung is the only brand with a nearby service centre.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should look elsewhere</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Gamers, who get far more frames per rupee from POCO or OnePlus.</li>\n<li>Travellers and delivery workers, who need a bigger battery.</li>\n<li>Anyone who wants zoom.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying advice</h2>\n<p>Wait for an offer if you can. Samsung Nepal runs exchange bonuses and EMI schemes for most of the year, and the sticker price is rarely the final one. Ask for the official warranty sticker, check the IMEI in MDMS and confirm the memory version on the box, because the 8GB and 12GB models look identical. Finally, buy a charger from a known brand with PPS support, or you will pay for 45W charging and receive 15W.</p>',8.1,'Six years of Android updates\nBright 120Hz Super AMOLED\nSlim 6.9mm, 179g build\nSamsung service network','Battery and charging are average now\nPricey against Chinese rivals\n5MP macro adds little','A dependable, long-supported mid-ranger. Buy it for the Samsung ecosystem and updates, not for value.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(88,99,20,'Galaxy A17 5G Review: Samsung\'s Most Sensible Budget Phone','samsung-galaxy-a17-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy A17 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>At <strong>Rs 44,999</strong> (6GB/128GB), the Galaxy A17 5G is the cheapest way into a Samsung with a Super AMOLED screen and 5G. It\'s also one of the phones in the new Samsung–Ncell offers, with EMI and free data packs.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 6.7-inch FHD+ Super AMOLED gets bright (1,100 nits), the 50MP main camera has OIS, which is rare at this price, and there\'s a microSD slot. Exynos 1330 is modest but fine for social apps and YouTube.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The screen is 90Hz rather than 120Hz, charging is 25W, and it\'s only IP54. For the same money, the <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> gives you a 7,500mAh battery, and the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> costs Rs 10,000 more but brings a much bigger battery and better durability.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>A safe pick for parents, first-time Samsung buyers and anyone who wants a familiar interface and easy service. It isn\'t the spec-sheet winner.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.7-inch FHD+ Super AMOLED, 90Hz, 1,100 nits, Gorilla Glass Victus</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Exynos 1330</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>6GB / 8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB, microSD</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main (OIS) + 5MP ultra wide + 2MP macro | 13MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>5,000mAh; 25W wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, One UI 7</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>First phone, parents\' phone, spare phone</h2>\n<p>The A17 5G is bought by three groups of people more than any others: students getting their first decent phone, sons and daughters buying for their parents, and people who need a second number that will not let them down. It suits all three because of what it avoids. The interface is the same as on expensive Samsungs, the menus are available in Nepali, emergency settings are easy to find, and a relative who already owns a Galaxy can help over the phone.</p>\n<p>Samsung\'s Easy mode deserves a mention here. It makes icons and text larger, simplifies the keyboard and lengthens the time needed for a long press. Set that up, pin WhatsApp, the camera and the dialler to the home screen, and an older user can manage without lessons.</p>\n<h2>A proper screen for the money</h2>\n<p>Super AMOLED at Full HD+ is still rare at this price. Text is sharp, contrast is strong and 1,100 nits is bright enough for reading outdoors in shade. The glass is Gorilla Glass Victus, an unusually tough choice for a budget phone and a good one for people who drop things.</p>\n<p>The refresh rate is 90Hz. Phones at the same price offer 120Hz, and if you place the two next to each other you can see it while scrolling fast. On its own, 90Hz looks smooth. The notch at the top is the dated U shape, so the front of the phone will not impress anybody.</p>\n<h2>What the Exynos 1330 can and cannot do</h2>\n<p>This is a modest processor. It opens WhatsApp, Facebook, YouTube and eSewa without drama, keeps a handful of apps in memory, and plays casual games. Free Fire works at standard settings. PUBG Mobile works if you keep graphics low. Switching quickly between a video call, a browser and a map will show short pauses.</p>\n<p>For that reason, spend the extra Rs 4,000 on 8GB of memory if you can. On this class of chip, memory is the difference between a phone that feels acceptable in 2028 and one that does not. The 8GB/256GB version at Rs 56,999 is harder to justify, since it costs as much as clearly faster phones. The microSD slot is the cheaper answer to storage.</p>\n<h2>Cameras with one real advantage</h2>\n<p>Optical image stabilisation on a Rs 45,000 phone is the advantage. It moves the lens to cancel out small shakes of your hand, so photos taken indoors in the evening come out sharp more often. Older users with less steady hands benefit the most. Video looks calmer too, though it is limited to 1080p.</p>\n<p>The 5MP ultra wide is for daylight only, and you can ignore the 2MP macro. The 13MP selfie camera is fine for video calls.</p>\n<h2>Battery, charging and the missing charger</h2>\n<p>A 5,000mAh battery with an undemanding chip lasts a full day, and nearer a day and a half for light users. Charging at 25W is slow by current standards, taking about an hour and a half from empty, and the charger is usually not in the box. For someone who plugs in overnight, none of this matters.</p>\n<p>IP54 means dust and splashes. Light rain on the way to the bus is fine. Dropping it in a bucket is not.</p>\n<h2>5G in Nepal: is it worth paying for?</h2>\n<p>Commercial 5G in Nepal is limited, and whether you benefit depends on where you live and which operator you use. Our <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">5G status article</a> explains the situation. The fair way to see it is as insurance for the next four years and not as a reason to buy today. If you would never move off 4G, the 4G-only phones at this price sometimes have bigger batteries or faster charging.</p>\n<h2>Updates are the hidden feature</h2>\n<p>Samsung has been giving its cheaper A-series phones a long run of updates, far longer than most rivals at this price. For a phone that will be handed down, that keeps banking and wallet apps working, and it keeps the owner safer from malware. Ask the shop to confirm the exact number of years for this model, and look it up on Samsung\'s site.</p>\n<h2>Around the same price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Exynos 1330</td><td>5,000mAh; 25W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Motorola Moto G96 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4 nm)</td><td>Li-ion 5500 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Tecno Pova 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,990</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 70W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> has a faster feel and quicker charging. The <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> brings a smoother screen and cleaner software. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m17-5g\">Galaxy M17 5G</a> is nearly the same phone with small differences, so compare the two prices on the day you buy. If you can reach Rs 54,999, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> is in another league for battery life and toughness.</p>\n<h2>About the Ncell offer</h2>\n<p>The A17 is included in the Samsung and Ncell promotion, which combines monthly payments with data packs. Read the conditions before signing. Add up every instalment and compare the total with the cash price elsewhere, check how long you must keep the plan, and find out what happens if you lose the phone. We went through the details in our <a href=\"/news/samsung-ncell-partnership-galaxy-offers-nepal\">article on the offer</a>.</p>\n<h2>Should you buy it?</h2>\n<p>If the buyer is comfortable with technology and wants the most hardware for Rs 45,000, no. There are phones with bigger batteries and faster chips. If the buyer wants something dependable that the whole family already knows how to use, with service nearby and years of updates, yes. Choose 8GB of memory, add a good case and a 25W charger with PPS, and it will quietly do its job.</p>',7.6,'Super AMOLED screen at this price\nOIS on the main camera\nmicroSD slot\nSamsung software and service','90Hz, not 120Hz\nSlow 25W charging\nOnly IP54','The sensible budget Samsung. Rivals offer more battery, but few offer the same peace of mind.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(89,90,20,'iPhone 17 Review: The First Base iPhone With ProMotion','iphone-17-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the iPhone 17 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The iPhone 17 costs <strong>Rs 1,64,999</strong> for 256GB in Nepal (Rs 2,15,699 for 512GB). Apple fixed the base model\'s biggest weakness this year: it finally has a 120Hz ProMotion display.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 6.3-inch LTPO OLED reaches 3,000 nits and now scrolls as smoothly as the Pro. The A19 (3nm) is fast, and 256GB is now the starting storage. The two 48MP cameras (main and ultrawide) are a real upgrade, and the 18MP Center Stage selfie camera frames group shots automatically. It weighs 177g.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>There\'s still no telephoto lens, so zoom shots lose detail. 8GB of RAM is fine for iOS but not generous. At this price you could buy a <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a> and pocket Rs 66,000, though you\'d give up iOS, resale value and Apple\'s ecosystem.</p>\n<h2>Should you wait for the iPhone 18?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/iphone-18\">iPhone 18</a> is expected around March 2027 at a higher price. If you need a phone now, the iPhone 17 is a complete package.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications at a glance</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>iPhone 17</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1206×2622, LTPO 120Hz ProMotion, 3,000 nits peak</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>48MP main + 48MP ultra wide | 18MP Center Stage front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>25W MagSafe, 15W Qi2; about 50% in 20 minutes with a fast charger</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>iOS 26 (at launch)</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>5G, Wi-Fi 7, Bluetooth 6.0, USB-C</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>177g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>149.6 × 71.5 × 8.0 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>How 120Hz changes the base iPhone</h2>\n<p>For four years people compared a Rs 1.5 lakh iPhone with a Rs 40,000 Android and asked why the cheaper phone scrolled more smoothly. That conversation is over. The iPhone 17 uses the same kind of variable panel as the Pro, running from a very low refresh rate on a still page up to 120Hz when you scroll. It also makes an always-on lock screen possible, so the time and your widgets are visible on the desk.</p>\n<p>Go from an iPhone 13 or 14 to this and the screen is the first thing you will notice. Text stays readable while it moves, animations feel lighter, and the 3,000-nit peak makes maps usable at noon. If you already have a Pro from the last few years, you will see no difference.</p>\n<h2>The cameras in daily life</h2>\n<p>There are two lenses on the back, both 48MP. The main one covers ninety per cent of what people shoot: family, food, streets, documents, pets. It also provides a 2x setting by cropping the centre of the sensor, which is good enough for portraits and half-length shots. The ultra wide now has the resolution to capture a whole room or a mountain view with detail in the corners, and it works for close-ups of flowers or jewellery.</p>\n<p>What you do not get is reach. At 5x or 10x the phone is enlarging pixels. At a football match at Dasharath Stadium, or with birds across a lake, the Pro models and many Android phones will beat it easily. Think back over the last hundred photos on your current phone. If fewer than five were zoomed, you will not miss the lens.</p>\n<p>The front camera is the surprise of this generation. It has a square sensor, so you can hold the phone upright and still take a wide group selfie. When more people step into the frame, it widens on its own. For creators who film themselves, this is more useful than any change on the back.</p>\n<h2>Speed, memory and Apple Intelligence</h2>\n<p>The A19 is quicker than the chips in almost every Android phone at the same price, and the interface never hesitates. The 8GB of memory is the minimum for Apple Intelligence. It is enough today, and it is the one figure that could feel tight towards the end of the phone\'s life if on-device AI features grow. Apple usually supports an iPhone for six years or more, so this is a minor doubt and not a reason to avoid it.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Apple quotes a full day, and in mixed use that is realistic. Bright sun, hotspot use and mobile games will shorten it. The welcome change is charging speed: about half full in twenty minutes with a 40W-class USB-C charger. MagSafe at up to 25W is quick for a wireless system. There is no charger in the box, so plan for one, and buy a reputable brand. A fake charger is the cheapest way to damage an expensive phone.</p>\n<h2>Is the 512GB model worth it?</h2>\n<p>No, for most people. The jump from Rs 1,64,999 to Rs 2,15,699 is more than Rs 50,000 for 256GB of storage. At that point you are within sight of the <a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro\">iPhone 17 Pro</a>. Since the base model now starts at 256GB, it is enough for anyone who backs photos up to iCloud or a computer. Pay for an iCloud plan or buy an external SSD, which plugs straight into the USB-C port.</p>\n<h2>Cost of ownership</h2>\n<p>An iPhone costs more at the counter and gives more back later. Two years from now a clean iPhone 17 with its box and bill should sell for a larger share of its price than an Android phone bought on the same day. Repairs are the other side. Screen and back-glass replacements with genuine parts are expensive, so use a case, and consider what you would do if you cracked the screen in the first month.</p>\n<p>Official warranty matters too. Apple products sold through authorised resellers in Nepal carry a one-year warranty handled by authorised service providers in Kathmandu. Outside the valley, expect to send the phone.</p>\n<h2>Compared with phones at about this price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>iPhone 17</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>25W MagSafe, 15W Qi2; about 50% in 20 minutes with...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-plus\">Samsung Galaxy S26+</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,59,999</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 4900 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging;...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro\">Google Pixel 10 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,76,000</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 4870 mAh, non-removable; 30W USB-PD Chargin...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-15-pro\">iPhone 15 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>Apple A17 Pro</td><td>3274mAh, 27W Fast Charging, MagSafe</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">Realme GT 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>7,000mAh; 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> is the natural rival. It is Rs 25,000 cheaper, similar in size, and has a telephoto lens and seven years of updates. If you have no attachment to iOS, it is hard to argue against. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15\">OnePlus 15</a> costs Rs 25,000 more and offers a battery that lasts almost twice as long. The <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro\">Pixel 10 Pro</a> takes wonderful photos, but has no official support here.</p>\n<p>People choose the iPhone for reasons outside the spec sheet: iMessage and FaceTime with relatives abroad, AirDrop with a MacBook, an Apple Watch, steady app quality, and resale. If three or more of those apply to you, the comparison ends there.</p>\n<h2>Upgrading from an older iPhone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From an iPhone 11 or 12:</strong> a huge step in every respect. Do it.</li>\n<li><strong>From a 13 or 14:</strong> worthwhile for the screen, camera and USB-C, if your battery is tired anyway.</li>\n<li><strong>From a 15 or 16:</strong> wait. The difference is mainly the 120Hz display.</li>\n<li><strong>From any Pro since the 14:</strong> you would lose a lens. Skip it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Before you buy</h2>\n<p>Check that the box is sealed and the serial number matches Apple\'s coverage page. Confirm that the model has a physical SIM tray if your operator or habits need one, as some regions sell eSIM-only versions. Run the IMEI through MDMS. And compare at least two authorised sellers, because bundles with a charger or case can be worth several thousand rupees.</p>',8.5,'120Hz ProMotion display at last\nTwo 48MP rear cameras\n256GB base storage\nLight at 177g','No telephoto\nExpensive in Nepal\n8GB RAM','The best base iPhone in years. Unless you need zoom, there\'s little reason to pay for the Pro.',1,0,0,0,1,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(90,105,20,'OnePlus 15 Review: The Biggest Battery in a True Flagship','oneplus-15-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the OnePlus 15 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The OnePlus 15 sells for <strong>Rs 1,89,999</strong> (16GB/512GB) in Nepal. It pairs a flagship Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 with a <strong>7,300mAh</strong> battery, something Samsung and Apple don\'t offer.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>Battery life is the headline: comfortably two days for many people, with fast wired charging and 50W wireless. The 6.78-inch LTPO AMOLED goes up to 165Hz in supported games. All three rear cameras are 50MP, including a 3.5x telephoto. It\'s rated IP68 and IP69K.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>It\'s heavy at 211–215g, and the cameras are good rather than class-leading. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> and <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> both shoot better for Rs 10,000 more. Check whether a charger is in the box at your shop, as listings differ.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If battery life and speed matter more than the last bit of camera quality, this is the flagship to buy in Nepal. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=oneplus-15&g2=samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Compare it with the Galaxy S26 Ultra</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>OnePlus 15</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.78-inch AMOLED, LTPO 1–120Hz (up to 165Hz in games), Dolby Vision</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB / 16GB LPDDR5X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB UFS 4.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP Sony LYT-700 main (OIS) + 50MP ultra wide + 50MP 3.5x telephoto | 32MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>7,300mAh; up to 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, OxygenOS 16</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>211–215g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>161.4 × 76.7 × 8.1 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Living with 7,300mAh</h2>\n<p>Most flagships are designed around a thin body, and the battery gets whatever space is left. OnePlus did it the other way round. Silicon-carbon cells let it fit 7,300mAh into a phone of normal thickness, and the result is a flagship you stop thinking about. Leave home at seven with mobile data on, take photos, use maps, share a hotspot for an hour, watch highlights on the bus back, and there is still a comfortable amount left at night.</p>\n<p>If you are a lighter user, you can charge every second day. That has a hidden benefit. Batteries age by the number of full cycles, and a phone that needs half as many will hold its health for longer.</p>\n<p>Charging is fast in both forms: up to 120W with a compatible OnePlus SUPERVOOC charger and 50W on the matching wireless stand. With an ordinary USB PD charger it is slower. Ask the shop which charger is in the box, or whether one is included at all, as listings in Nepal do not agree.</p>\n<h2>Speed, and how long it lasts</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 is in the top group of phone chips, and paired with 16GB of fast memory and UFS 4.1 storage it makes everything feel immediate. The interesting question with any such chip is heat. A large phone with a large vapour chamber can keep its performance up longer than a compact one, which is one reason gamers like OnePlus. The 165Hz mode works in a list of supported titles and drops back to 120Hz elsewhere.</p>\n<p>In a hot room without a fan, no phone holds its peak speed for an hour. Take off the case, lower the brightness and avoid charging while you play. Those steps matter more than the chip\'s name.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 6.78-inch AMOLED adapts between 1Hz and 120Hz in normal use. It is flat, which makes it easy to fit a protector and reduces accidental touches. OnePlus has put effort into comfort at night with very low minimum brightness and flicker reduction, worth knowing if you read in bed or get headaches from some AMOLED phones.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: consistent and unfussy</h2>\n<p>Three 50MP cameras sounds impressive, and the point is balance. The main camera uses a Sony sensor with stabilisation, the ultra wide is better than usual, and the 3.5x telephoto covers portraits and moderate zoom. Pictures look natural, and the camera app is quick.</p>\n<p>What keeps it behind the leaders is sensor size. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> gathers more light with its one-inch sensor, and the <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> has far more detail at long zoom. At a concert or in a dim restaurant, you can tell. In daylight and on social media, most people cannot.</p>\n<h2>Toughness</h2>\n<p>IP68 and IP69K ratings together mean the phone resists dust, immersion in fresh water and jets of hot water. That is as high as consumer phones go. It is reassuring through the monsoon, but water damage is generally not covered by warranty, so treat it as a safety net.</p>\n<p>The weight of 211 to 215 grams is the cost of the battery. It is noticeable, though well balanced. Anyone coming from a compact phone should hold it for a while before buying.</p>\n<h2>Software</h2>\n<p>OxygenOS 16 is one of the more pleasant versions of Android: fast, not overloaded, with sensible shortcuts and good split-screen tools. Useful extras include the file dock and the ability to receive files from iPhones with a companion app. OnePlus promises several years of upgrades and security patches, which is shorter than Samsung\'s seven years. Ask for the exact figure for this model.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>OnePlus phones are sold through official partners with service in Kathmandu. Coverage outside the valley is limited compared with Samsung and Xiaomi. If you live in Surkhet or Dhangadhi, find out how a repair would be handled before you spend almost two lakh. Buy an official unit: Chinese-market OnePlus phones run a different system, ColorOS, and their warranty does not apply here.</p>\n<h2>What else is near Rs 1,90,000?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>OnePlus 15</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,89,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>7,300mAh; up to 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-air\">iPhone Air</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,90,699</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>MagSafe wireless and wired charging; 4.5W reverse...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip8\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm)</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reverse</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> is the all-rounder with a stylus and the longest support, at Rs 35,000 more and with a much smaller battery. The <a href=\"/products/honor-magic-8-pro\">Honor Magic 8 Pro</a> offers a large battery and strong cameras. The <a href=\"/products/iphone-air\">iPhone Air</a> sits at the same price and represents the opposite idea: thinness before endurance. Try both in your hand and you will know within a minute which kind of person you are.</p>\n<p>If the price is the problem, the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a> at about half the money keeps the speed and the big battery, and cuts the cameras. It is the value choice in the family.</p>\n<h2>One storage option, and what that means</h2>\n<p>In Nepal the listed version has 16GB of memory and 512GB of storage. That is generous and leaves nothing to upgrade, yet it also removes the cheaper entry point that exists elsewhere. If you would have been happy with 256GB, you are paying for space you may not use.</p>\n<h2>Verdict in practice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Choose it</strong> for travel, gaming, long shifts and anyone who resents charging.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose it</strong> if you like clean Android and fast everything.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose something else</strong> if your camera is the main reason to spend this much.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose something else</strong> if you need a service centre in your own city.</li>\n</ul>',8.7,'7,300mAh battery in a flagship\nSnapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5\n165Hz gaming mode\nIP68 and IP69K','Heavy\nCameras trail Xiaomi and vivo\nCharger availability varies','The best-battery flagship in Nepal. Camera fans should look at the Xiaomi 17 Ultra instead.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(91,100,20,'Xiaomi 17 Ultra Review: A Camera First, a Phone Second','xiaomi-17-ultra-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Xiaomi 17 Ultra yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Xiaomi 17 Ultra costs <strong>Rs 1,99,999</strong> (16GB/512GB) in Nepal. It is built around its cameras, and on paper it\'s the most capable camera phone you can buy here.</p>\n<h2>The camera system</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera uses a <strong>1-inch-type sensor</strong> with LOFIC HDR. That size of sensor is what gives natural background blur and cleaner low-light photos. The 200MP periscope offers continuous optical zoom from 3.2x to 4.3x, and the ultrawide is 50MP too.</p>\n<h2>The rest</h2>\n<p>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5, a 6.9-inch 12-bit LTPO OLED at 3,500 nits, a 6,000mAh battery with 90W wired and 50W wireless charging, and IP66/IP68/IP69 protection. It\'s a complete flagship, not just a camera with a phone attached.</p>\n<h2>Who it\'s for</h2>\n<p>If photos matter most, this or the <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> (same price, longer 3.7x periscope) are the two to choose between. If you want the S Pen and seven years of updates, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> remains the all-rounder.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Xiaomi 17 Ultra</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.9-inch LTPO OLED, 2608×1200, 1–120Hz, 12-bit, 3,500 nits peak, Dolby Vision</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB UFS 4.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP 1-inch Light Fusion 1050L main (f/1.67, OIS, LOFIC) + 200MP periscope (3.2x–4.3x optical) + 50MP ultra wide | 50MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reverse</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, HyperOS 3</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why sensor size is the whole story</h2>\n<p>Phone adverts talk about megapixels because large numbers are easy to print. Photographers look at the size of the sensor. A one-inch-type sensor has several times the area of the one in a mid-range phone, so it collects several times as much light in the same fraction of a second. That gives you three things: less grain in the dark, faster shutter speeds for moving children, and a background that blurs because of the lens and not because software guessed where the edges were.</p>\n<p>LOFIC, the technology Xiaomi mentions, is about contrast. Each pixel can store more light before it clips to white, so a bright window behind a person, or the sky above a shaded courtyard, keeps its detail in a single exposure. For anyone who photographs the mountains at sunrise, with dark foreground and blazing snow, this solves a real problem.</p>\n<h2>The zoom that moves</h2>\n<p>Most phones have fixed zoom lenses and crop between them. The 17 Ultra\'s 200MP periscope physically changes focal length between 3.2x and 4.3x. Inside that range you lose no quality as you frame a portrait or a detail, and the high resolution leaves room to crop well beyond it. It is the closest thing to a real lens on a phone.</p>\n<p>For long distances the <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> remains a strong competitor with its 200MP 3.7x camera. The two phones cost the same, so the choice is about style. Xiaomi is the better everyday and low-light camera. vivo is the better choice for the far end of the zoom and for stage photography.</p>\n<h2>Taking photos with it</h2>\n<p>You pick between two Leica looks. Authentic keeps shadows and restrained colour, Vibrant is closer to what other phones produce. A professional mode gives manual control over shutter, focus and white balance, and you can save RAW files for editing. People who already own a mirrorless camera often find that this phone replaces it for travel, while people who only want to point and shoot will be paying for a lot of capability that stays in the menus.</p>\n<p>Video has improved, with stable 4K and flat colour profiles for grading. The iPhone still leads for effortless, consistent clips, particularly inside apps such as Instagram and TikTok, whose cameras are usually tuned better for Apple and Samsung.</p>\n<h2>Everything else is flagship too</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 and 16GB of memory mean there is no task this phone struggles with. The 6.9-inch OLED is bright, accurate and adapts from 1Hz to 120Hz. A 6,000mAh battery is large for a camera phone and comfortably covers a day of shooting. It charges at 90W by cable and 50W wirelessly, and can send 22.5W to another device, enough to properly charge a small camera or earbuds.</p>\n<p>Protection is rated IP66, IP68 and IP69. The camera island is enormous, though, and it makes the phone top-heavy. Use a strap or a grip case. A drop onto that lens ring would be an expensive afternoon.</p>\n<h2>HyperOS and the things to change first</h2>\n<p>HyperOS 3 is fast and full of features. Spend a little time on it: uninstall the extra apps, turn off recommendations in the built-in tools, allow WhatsApp and Viber to run in the background, and set the camera to open with a double press of the volume key. Xiaomi\'s update promise for its top phones has grown, yet it remains shorter than Samsung\'s seven years.</p>\n<h2>Is this a good idea in Nepal?</h2>\n<p>There are practical points to consider. Xiaomi has official service here, but the Ultra is a low-volume model, and parts such as its camera module may need ordering. Ask how long a screen replacement would take. Resale is the second point: Android camera phones lose value faster than iPhones and Galaxy Ultras. If you buy it to keep and to photograph with, that does not matter. If you sell every year, it does.</p>\n<p>Third, check the version. Chinese units are common in the grey market and cost less, but they bring Chinese software, fewer Google conveniences and no local warranty. The official Nepali unit at Rs 1,99,999 is the one we price.</p>\n<h2>Compared with others at this price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Xiaomi 17 Ultra</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reverse</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-8-pro\">Honor Magic 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>7,100mAh; 100W wired, 80W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Google Pixel 10 Pro XL</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,00,000</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging;...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip8\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm)</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> does more non-camera things: a stylus, a privacy screen, DeX and long support. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15\">OnePlus 15</a> lasts much longer on a charge. The <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Pixel 10 Pro XL</a> has the most reliable point-and-shoot results, with no official service. Only the Xiaomi and the vivo feel like cameras that happen to make calls.</p>\n<h2>A question to ask yourself</h2>\n<p>Open the photo gallery on your current phone and look at the last month. Are there portraits, night scenes, travel and details that you wish were better? Then this phone will give you pleasure every week. Are they mostly screenshots, documents and food? Then the same money would be better spent on a phone with a longer battery, or half of it on something sensible and the other half on a trip worth photographing.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Enthusiasts, travellers and small studios who want one device for quick professional work.</li>\n<li>People who enjoy editing RAW files.</li>\n<li>Owners of older Leica-tuned Xiaomi phones who want a clear step up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It does not suit people who want a light phone, who need wide service coverage or who sell on quickly. Within its purpose it is outstanding, and it is refreshing to see a phone that knows exactly what it is for.</p>',9.0,'1-inch main camera sensor\nContinuous 3.2x–4.3x optical zoom\n6,000mAh with 50W wireless\nIP66/IP68/IP69','Rs 2 lakh\nLarge and heavy\nShorter update promise than Samsung','The camera phone to beat in Nepal. If photography is why you\'re spending Rs 2 lakh, start here.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(92,110,20,'vivo X300 Pro Review: The Zoom Specialist','vivo-x300-pro-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the vivo X300 Pro yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The vivo X300 Pro costs <strong>Rs 1,99,999</strong> (16GB/512GB) in Nepal, after a price rise on 1 September 2026. It\'s the phone to choose if you shoot distant subjects: concerts, wildlife in Chitwan, mountains from the valley.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The <strong>200MP periscope at 3.7x</strong> with optical stabilisation is the standout, and it can crop further while keeping detail. The MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm) is a true flagship chip, the 6,510mAh battery is large, and you get 90W wired and 40W wireless charging. The 6.78-inch BOE Q10+ panel reaches 4,500 nits.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The main sensor is smaller than the Xiaomi 17 Ultra\'s 1-inch type, so the Xiaomi has the edge for everyday shots and low light. vivo\'s OriginOS is polished but less familiar to many buyers here.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy it for zoom. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=vivo-x300-pro&g2=xiaomi-17-ultra\">Compare it with the Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> to choose between reach and the main camera.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>vivo X300 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.78-inch BOE Q10+ AMOLED, 2800×1260, 120Hz, 4,500 nits peak</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB UFS 4.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main (OIS) + 50MP ultra wide + 200MP periscope 3.7x (OIS) | 50MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, OriginOS 6</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What a 200MP periscope is good for</h2>\n<p>The headline camera on the X300 Pro is not the main one. It is the 200MP telephoto with 3.7x optical zoom and stabilisation. At its native length it takes portraits with the flattering perspective of an 85mm lens. Because the sensor has so many pixels, you can crop to seven or ten times and still have a photo worth keeping. vivo\'s work with ZEISS on the lenses shows in clean edges and controlled flare when shooting towards stage lights.</p>\n<p>In Nepal there are plenty of places to use that: rhinos and birds in Chitwan and Koshi, performers at a Dashain programme, a bride across a party palace, the face of Machhapuchhre from Pokhara lakeside. A hand-held 10x shot is something you can actually rely on here, whereas most phones treat it as a party trick. Beyond about 20x, atmospheric haze and shake take over, whatever the specification claims.</p>\n<h2>The main camera in comparison</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera is very good, but it uses a smaller sensor than the one-inch unit in the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a>. In daylight you would not know. In a candle-lit room or on a street at night, the Xiaomi collects more light and shows more natural background blur. Colour is where vivo wins some people over: skin tones are warm and flattering, and the portrait modes imitate classic lenses convincingly.</p>\n<p>The 50MP ultra wide and the 50MP front camera complete a set without a weak member. Video is improved, with good stabilisation on the zoom lens, which is rare.</p>\n<h2>Performance and heat</h2>\n<p>The Dimensity 9500 is MediaTek\'s flagship 3nm chip. It competes directly with Qualcomm\'s best, and in everyday use the two are impossible to tell apart. Games run at high settings, and 16GB of memory keeps everything open. Some emulators and a few games are better optimised for Snapdragon, so heavy emulator users should prefer a phone with that chip.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>At 6,510mAh this has one of the larger batteries among camera flagships. A long day of photography, maps and messaging is no problem. It charges at 90W with vivo\'s charger and 40W on a compatible wireless stand. With that combination you can arrive at a lunch stop with twenty per cent and leave with most of a charge.</p>\n<h2>Display</h2>\n<p>The 6.78-inch AMOLED reaches a claimed 4,500 nits on small bright areas, and is easily readable in strong sun. It is sharp, smooth at 120Hz and comfortable at night. It is flat, or very nearly so, which makes it easier to protect.</p>\n<h2>OriginOS instead of Funtouch</h2>\n<p>vivo moved its international phones to OriginOS, a redesign with smoother animation and more consistent menus. People who remember the older Funtouch software will find it a clear improvement. It still comes with vivo\'s own apps in duplicate, and the notification settings need a little attention so that messaging apps are not put to sleep. Ask about the number of Android upgrades promised, since this is an area where Samsung is ahead.</p>\n<h2>About that price rise</h2>\n<p>The phone moved up in September 2026 to Rs 1,99,999. Memory costs and the exchange rate pushed up almost every flagship this year, as we explained in <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">our look at 2026 prices</a>. The effect is that the X300 Pro now sits exactly level with its closest rival. Before the rise it was an easy recommendation as the cheaper option. Now it has to win on zoom alone.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>vivo X300 Pro</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reverse</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-8-pro\">Honor Magic 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>7,100mAh; 100W wired, 80W wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Google Pixel 10 Pro XL</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,00,000</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging;...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip8\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm)</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Service and resale</h2>\n<p>vivo has been in Nepal for many years and has service centres in the main cities, which is a point in its favour over brands that only sell here. The X series is sold in small numbers, however, so camera modules and screens may not be on the shelf. Resale follows the usual Android pattern: expect a steep drop in the first year. A well-kept unit with box, bill and remaining warranty will sell more easily.</p>\n<h2>Accessories worth considering</h2>\n<p>vivo sells a photography kit and a ZEISS tele-extender lens for this series in some countries. Availability in Nepal is uncertain, so do not plan around it. What you should buy locally is simpler: a case with a raised edge around the camera ring, a small tripod with a phone clamp and a USB-C card reader. A tripod turns the night and long-zoom modes from good to excellent.</p>\n<h2>X300 Pro or Xiaomi 17 Ultra?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>You photograph people on stages, wildlife, sport or distant scenery:</strong> vivo.</li>\n<li><strong>You photograph family, streets, food and night scenes:</strong> Xiaomi.</li>\n<li><strong>You want the longer battery:</strong> vivo, by about 500mAh.</li>\n<li><strong>You want the more adjustable, camera-like controls:</strong> Xiaomi.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> is the sensible third option for people who like both and need neither. It has good zoom, wider service and much longer software support for Rs 25,000 more.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<p>Buy the X300 Pro if you have missed photos because you could not get closer, and you enjoy taking them. If your zoom slider rarely moves past 2x, you would be paying two lakh for a feature you do not use. In that case, look at a good all-round phone and put the difference aside.</p>\n<h2>Practise for an afternoon</h2>\n<p>A camera phone like this rewards an hour of learning. Walk through a market and take every picture three times: at 1x, at 2x and at 3.7x, from the same place. At home, delete the weakest of each three. You will notice that the telephoto wins for faces and details, and the main camera for places. After one afternoon of that, you will choose the right lens without thinking, and the price will begin to make sense.</p>',8.8,'200MP 3.7x periscope with OIS\nDimensity 9500\n6,510mAh, 90W and 40W wireless\nVery bright display','Price went up in September\nSmaller main sensor than Xiaomi\nHeavy','The best zoom camera in Nepal. Pick the Xiaomi 17 Ultra if most of your photos are everyday shots.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(93,113,20,'realme P4 Power Review: A 10,001mAh Phone That Doesn\'t Cut Corners','realme-p4-power-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the realme P4 Power yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The realme P4 Power costs <strong>Rs 76,999</strong> (12GB/256GB) in Nepal. Its <strong>10,001mAh</strong> silicon-carbon battery is the reason to buy it, and unlike many big-battery phones, the rest of the package is good too.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>Light users can go three days between charges, and 80W charging refills it quickly. It also has 27W reverse charging to top up a friend\'s phone. The 6.8-inch 1.5K AMOLED runs at 144Hz and reaches 6,500 nits. It\'s rated IP66/IP68/IP69, and the main camera has OIS.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The Dimensity 7400 Ultra is upper mid-range, not flagship. The 8MP ultrawide is weak, and there\'s no telephoto. The quad-curved screen looks premium but picks up accidental touches more easily than a flat one.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For long trips, treks and heavy daily use, it\'s the best value big-battery phone in Nepal. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> (Rs 69,999, 10,000mAh) is the cheaper alternative.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Realme P4 Power</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.8-inch quad-curved 1.5K AMOLED, 144Hz, 6,500 nits peak</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB UFS 3.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP Sony IMX882 (OIS) + 8MP ultra wide | 16MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reverse</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, Realme UI 7</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Three days, if you let it</h2>\n<p>The extra one in 10,001mAh is a joke by the marketing team. The battery is not. With light use, meaning calls, messages, some browsing and an hour of video, this phone can go from Sunday morning to Tuesday night. Heavy users who stream, navigate and share a hotspot can expect close to two days. For trekking on routes where charging costs money at every teahouse, or for work in places with irregular electricity, that changes what you carry.</p>\n<p>Charging at 80W is slower than the 100W of the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a>, yet an hour or so on the plug fills it. Because you charge half as often, the slower figure rarely matters. The 27W reverse charging is strong enough to rescue a friend\'s phone properly, so the P4 Power doubles as a power bank.</p>\n<h2>The rest of the phone is not an afterthought</h2>\n<p>Big-battery phones used to be heavy slabs with poor screens. Here you get a 6.8-inch AMOLED with 1.5K resolution and a 144Hz refresh rate. The claimed 6,500-nit peak applies to small highlights, but general brightness outdoors is excellent as well. Gamers will like the touch response, and readers will like the sharpness.</p>\n<p>It is a quad-curved panel, meaning the glass bends slightly on all four sides. It feels expensive in the hand. It also makes tempered glass protectors harder to fit, catches reflections along the edges and registers the odd accidental touch when you hold the phone with one hand. People who prefer flat screens should try before buying.</p>\n<h2>Dimensity 7400 Ultra in use</h2>\n<p>This is an upper mid-range chip built for efficiency. Everything ordinary is smooth. PUBG Mobile, Mobile Legends and Call of Duty Mobile run comfortably at medium to high settings, and the 144Hz screen helps in titles that support high frame rates. Very heavy games need lower settings. Where this phone shines for players is staying power, since you can play for hours without hunting for a plug.</p>\n<p>If raw speed matters, the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> is faster and cheaper. It also has a large battery, though not on this scale.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>The 50MP Sony IMX882 main camera with stabilisation is a well-known and reliable sensor. Daylight results are detailed, and stabilisation keeps indoor photos sharp. The 8MP ultra wide is usable in good light and soft at night. There is no telephoto, so 2x is a crop of the main sensor and higher zooms lose detail. The 16MP selfie camera is ordinary.</p>\n<p>If photography is a priority at this price, look at the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a>, or the <a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">vivo V60 5G</a>, which is built around portraits.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>Ratings of IP66, IP68 and IP69 cover dust, heavy splashes, immersion in fresh water and high-pressure jets. For farmers, riders, site engineers and anyone who works outside, this is as useful as the battery. Remember that the ratings describe a new phone under test conditions, and that liquid damage is normally excluded from the warranty.</p>\n<h2>Software</h2>\n<p>Realme UI 7 on Android 16 is quick and customisable. It comes with a number of preinstalled apps, most of which can be removed. Turn off personalised recommendations during setup. Realme\'s update commitment for this series is moderate; confirm the number of Android versions with the seller if you plan to keep the phone for long.</p>\n<h2>Price and the obvious rival</h2>\n<p>At Rs 76,999 for 12GB and 256GB, it costs Rs 7,000 more than the base Redmi Note 17 Pro Max, which has 8GB, and about Rs 3,000 less than the 12GB version of that phone.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Realme P4 Power</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reverse</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">Vivo V60</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>7, 500mAh, non-removable; 80W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Motorola Edge 70 Fusion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 68W Fast Char...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>So compare like with like. Against the 12GB Redmi, the Realme is cheaper and has the faster screen. The Redmi has quicker charging, tougher front glass and a service network that is larger in Nepal. Against the 8GB Redmi, you pay for memory that you might not need.</p>\n<p>Others near this price take a different path. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Galaxy A37 5G</a> has half the battery and far longer software support. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-5\">OnePlus Nord 5</a> and <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">Nord CE 6</a> balance performance and endurance. The <a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Motorola Edge 70 Fusion</a> is slimmer and lighter.</p>\n<h2>Who it is made for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Long-distance travellers and trekkers</strong> who count charging points.</li>\n<li><strong>Delivery riders and drivers</strong> who run maps and calls all day.</li>\n<li><strong>Families in areas with power cuts</strong>, where one phone is also the hotspot.</li>\n<li><strong>Mobile gamers</strong> who value session length over maximum graphics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should pass</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone who wants a slim, light phone.</li>\n<li>Photographers who use zoom or ultra wide often.</li>\n<li>People who dislike curved screens.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tips for owners</h2>\n<p>Use the original charger for full speed. Set charge protection to stop at eighty or ninety per cent on ordinary days, because you have capacity to spare and it slows ageing. Buy a case that covers the curved edges. And keep the packing: big-battery phones sell well second-hand outside the cities, and a full box helps.</p>\n<h2>Travelling with a battery this large</h2>\n<p>At a little under 40 watt-hours it is well inside airline limits for a phone in the cabin. Keep it with you and not in checked luggage, as with any lithium battery. On a trek, sleep with it inside your sleeping bag, since cold temporarily reduces capacity. Reverse charging can rescue a friend\'s phone or a headlamp, but each transfer loses about a third as heat, so lend it sparingly. For a four-day walk with the phone in airplane mode and the camera in use, one full charge can be enough.</p>',8.3,'10,001mAh battery\n80W charging and 27W reverse\n144Hz 1.5K AMOLED\nIP69 rated','Mid-range chip\nWeak 8MP ultrawide\nCurved screen','The big-battery phone to buy if you can stretch past Rs 70,000.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(94,115,20,'Honor X9d Review: The Toughest Phone Under Rs 60,000','honor-x9d-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Honor X9d yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Honor X9d costs <strong>Rs 54,999</strong> (8GB/256GB) or Rs 59,999 (12GB/256GB). Honor sells it on durability: it\'s rated <strong>IP69K, IP68 and IP66</strong> and tested for 2.5-metre drops. That matters if you work outdoors, ride a bike daily or are simply hard on phones.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>An 8,300mAh silicon-carbon battery with 66W charging, a 6.79-inch AMOLED that reaches 6,000 nits, and a 108MP main camera with OIS. Despite the big battery, it weighs 193g and is 7.76mm thin.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 is fine for daily use but not for heavy gaming. The 5MP ultrawide is basic, and Honor\'s after-sales network in Nepal is smaller than Samsung\'s.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For under Rs 60,000, few phones combine this much battery, brightness and toughness. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=honor-x9d&g2=samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Compare it with the Galaxy A17 5G</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Honor X9d</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.79-inch AMOLED (2640×1200), 120Hz, 6,000 nits peak</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>108MP main (OIS, f/1.75) + 5MP ultra wide | 16MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, MagicOS 9</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>193g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>161.9 × 76.1 × 7.76 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What \"drop resistant\" should mean to you</h2>\n<p>Honor says the X9d is tested for falls from 2.5 metres and carries the top water and dust ratings. Laboratory tests use specific surfaces and angles, so treat them as evidence of strong design and not as a promise. What you can expect is that an ordinary accident, such as the phone sliding off a motorbike tank or falling from a ladder, is less likely to end with a broken screen than it would be with most phones.</p>\n<p>Two details help. The glass is reinforced and surrounded by cushioning material, and the battery and board are mounted so that shock spreads instead of concentrating at the corners. For masons, electricians, guides, traffic police, farmers and parents of small children, that is worth more than another camera.</p>\n<h2>IP66, IP68 and IP69K in monsoon terms</h2>\n<p>IP66 covers heavy jets of water, IP68 covers being under fresh water for a time, and IP69K covers hot, high-pressure spray. Riding in Asar rain with the phone on a mount, wiping off mud, or rinsing it under a tap are all within what it is built for. Avoid the sea, soap, steam and swimming pools, and dry the charging port before plugging in. Water damage is usually outside warranty terms, however the phone is rated.</p>\n<h2>Battery without the bulk</h2>\n<p>An 8,300mAh cell in a 193 gram phone that is under 8mm thick is a small engineering feat made possible by silicon-carbon chemistry. Two days between charges is realistic for moderate users. At 66W it fills reasonably fast, and it can pass charge to other devices.</p>\n<p>Compare that with the 10,000mAh phones at Rs 70,000 and above. The Honor gives you more than four-fifths of their stamina in a body that does not feel like a tool. For most people that is the better balance.</p>\n<h2>A screen for the outdoors</h2>\n<p>The 6.79-inch AMOLED has a resolution between Full HD+ and Quad HD+, a 120Hz refresh rate and a claimed peak of 6,000 nits. Outdoors it is one of the easiest screens to read at this price. Honor also uses very fast dimming at low brightness, which reduces flicker for sensitive eyes.</p>\n<h2>Everyday performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 is a balanced mid-range chip. It is quick for social apps, navigation, mobile banking and video. Games work at medium settings. Multitasking is fine on 8GB, and 12GB for Rs 5,000 more is a fair upgrade if you keep the phone for several years. Heavy gamers will find faster chips at the same money from POCO or Infinix, usually without the ruggedness.</p>\n<h2>Camera</h2>\n<p>The 108MP main camera with optical stabilisation takes detailed photos in good light, and stabilisation helps at dusk. A 3x setting crops from the high-resolution sensor, with acceptable results in daylight. The 5MP ultra wide is the cheapest part of the phone and looks it. The 16MP front camera is adequate. Nobody should buy this phone mainly for photos, but nobody will be embarrassed by them either.</p>\n<h2>MagicOS and Google</h2>\n<p>Some buyers still connect Honor with Huawei and worry about missing Google services. Honor has been a separate company since 2020, and its phones come with the Play Store, Gmail, Maps and everything else. MagicOS 9 on Android 15 is smooth and has helpful touches such as dragging content into a side panel of apps. Expect some preinstalled software that you can remove. The phone ships with Android 15 while some rivals already have 16, so ask about the upgrade schedule.</p>\n<h2>After-sales support</h2>\n<p>This is the one place where caution is needed. Honor\'s network in Nepal is smaller than Samsung\'s or Xiaomi\'s, and screens for a curved, reinforced model are not cheap. Ask the seller three things: where the nearest service point is, how much a replacement screen costs today, and whether a free screen replacement period is included. Honor has offered such cover on X9-series phones in some markets, and a Nepali seller should be able to say clearly whether it applies.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives around Rs 55,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Honor X9d</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse chargi...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">Nothing Phone 3a Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Pro (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging;...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60\">Infinix Note 60</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7400 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 6500 mAh; 45W Fast Charging; 30W wirel...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y500\">Vivo Y500</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>Unisoc T7300 (6nm)</td><td>Li-ion 8100 mAh, non-removable; 44W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-lite\">Honor 600 Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7100 Elite (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6520 mAh, non-removable; 45W HONOR SuperChar...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 15 Pro 5G</a> has a better camera set and wider service. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">Galaxy A27 5G</a> has longer software support. The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">Nothing Phone 3a Lite</a> has cleaner software and more style. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y500\">vivo Y500</a> is the closest match in spirit, with a very large battery and good water protection. None of them combine drop resistance, brightness and battery quite like the Honor.</p>\n<h2>Real-life buyers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A trekking guide</strong> who needs two days of GPS and a screen that can be read on snow.</li>\n<li><strong>A delivery rider</strong> whose phone lives on the handlebar in dust and rain.</li>\n<li><strong>A shopkeeper</strong> who drops the phone on concrete once a week.</li>\n<li><strong>A student in a hostel</strong> who forgets to charge.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Less suitable for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Serious gamers.</li>\n<li>People who take lots of wide group photos.</li>\n<li>Anyone far from a service point who needs fast repairs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our advice</h2>\n<p>Take the 8GB version if you use the phone for calls, social apps and maps. Take 12GB if you will keep it beyond three years. Use a light case anyway, as scratches still happen, and register the warranty on the day you buy. At this price the X9d has few direct rivals, and for people who work outdoors it is the first phone we would look at.</p>',8.2,'IP69K and 2.5m drop rating\n8,300mAh with 66W\n6,000-nit AMOLED\nSlim for its battery','Mid-range chip\nBasic ultrawide\nSmaller service network','The phone to buy if it needs to survive rain, dust and drops.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(95,104,20,'Redmi 17 5G Review: The Budget 5G Phone With a 7,500mAh Battery','redmi-17-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Redmi 17 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Redmi 17 5G starts at <strong>Rs 33,999</strong> (4GB/128GB). It\'s the cheapest phone we\'d call a good all-rounder: 5G, a very large battery and a smooth screen.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 7,500mAh battery lasts well over a day, with 45W charging and 22.5W reverse charging. The Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 (4nm) is efficient and supports 5G. The 6.9-inch display runs at 120Hz, and there\'s a microSD slot.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The screen is HD+ LCD, so text isn\'t as sharp as on an AMOLED. 4GB of RAM is tight. The Rs 37,999 4GB/256GB version is the better buy, and 6GB at Rs 45,999 is safer for the long run.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For students and anyone on a tight budget who wants 5G and long battery life, it\'s our pick under Rs 40,000. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">best phones under Rs 40,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi 17 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.9-inch HD+ LCD, 120Hz, 825 nits peak, Gorilla Glass 7i</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB, microSD</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main + auxiliary lens | 8MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>7,500mAh; 45W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, HyperOS 3</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Where the money went</h2>\n<p>At Rs 33,999 every phone is a set of choices. Xiaomi put its budget into the battery, the size of the screen, a modern 4nm chip and a tough front glass. It saved on resolution, memory and cameras. If you agree with those choices, this is a very satisfying cheap phone. If you care most about a crisp display or photos, it will annoy you from the first day.</p>\n<h2>The battery is the star</h2>\n<p>With 7,500mAh feeding an efficient chip and a low-resolution screen, the Redmi 17 5G can last two days without much effort. That is a lot of reassurance in a village where the power comes and goes. The 45W charging is quick for the price, and reverse charging at 22.5W is faster than some phones can charge themselves. You can top up earbuds, a feature phone or a friend\'s handset by cable.</p>\n<h2>About that HD+ screen</h2>\n<p>The panel measures 6.9 inches, yet it has an HD+ resolution, roughly 720 by 1600 pixels. On a screen this large, small text has slightly fuzzy edges, and you will notice it when reading Devanagari with its fine matras, or when looking at spreadsheets. Videos on Facebook and YouTube at 480p or 720p, which most people choose to save data, look fine.</p>\n<p>It is an LCD, so black areas look dark grey at night. Peak brightness of 825 nits is enough for shade and for the street if you are not in direct sun. The 120Hz refresh rate keeps movement smooth, and Gorilla Glass 7i is a better protection layer than budget phones usually have.</p>\n<h2>Memory: do not buy the cheapest one</h2>\n<p>Android 16 with HyperOS uses a large part of 4GB for itself. With that much memory, apps reload when you return to them, and the keyboard can lag while several things are happening. Virtual memory, which borrows storage, helps only a little.</p>\n<p>Here is how we would choose. The 4GB/128GB version at Rs 33,999 is for calls, WhatsApp and YouTube. The 4GB/256GB version at Rs 37,999 is for the same use with lots of photos and videos. The 6GB/256GB version at Rs 45,999 is the one that will still feel acceptable in three years, although at that price it meets strong competition, including phones with AMOLED screens.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 is an entry-level 5G chip on a modern process. It is more than enough for everyday apps and runs cool. Free Fire and Ludo are no problem. PUBG Mobile works at low settings. For students, Google Classroom, Teams and PDF readers are fine.</p>\n<h2>Camera and calls</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera takes decent daylight photos for social media. The second lens is an auxiliary sensor that adds nothing you will see. Night photos are noisy. The 8MP selfie camera is for video calls and not for creators. Voice calls are clear, and the speaker is loud, which is what many buyers at this price actually ask about.</p>\n<h2>5G at this price</h2>\n<p>Having 5G on a cheap phone is a bet on the future. The network in Nepal is still limited, as covered in our <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">5G article</a>. The practical gain today is the newer modem and chip, which handle 4G efficiently. If you live outside the cities, you may go years without seeing a 5G icon.</p>\n<h2>Service and spare parts</h2>\n<p>Redmi phones are everywhere in Nepal, and that is a feature. Screens, batteries and charging boards are easy to find, repairers know the models, and official centres exist in the main cities. Use official parts during warranty, and keep the bill. The microSD slot also makes it simple to move photos when you change phones.</p>\n<h2>What else costs about the same</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Redmi 17 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>7,500mAh; 45W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-4g\">Redmi Note 15 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultra (6nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-air\">ZTE nubia Air</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>Unisoc T8300 (6nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5000mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y11d\">Vivo Y11d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>Unisoc T7225 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 6500 mAh, non-removable; 44W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4x-4g\">Realme P4x 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-4g\">Redmi Note 15 4G</a> has an AMOLED screen and a much better main camera if you can live without 5G. For many people that is the nicer phone to look at every day. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x7d\">Honor X7d</a> is another big-battery choice. The <a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-70\">Infinix Hot 70</a> and <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a> give more memory for the money. Our guide to the <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">best phones under Rs 40,000</a> compares them in one place.</p>\n<h2>Set it up well on day one</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Remove the preinstalled games and apps you do not need.</li>\n<li>Turn off recommendations in the file manager, security and music apps.</li>\n<li>Move photos and WhatsApp media to a memory card.</li>\n<li>Set the screen to 60Hz if you want even more battery life.</li>\n<li>Install Find My Device and a screen lock, as cheap phones get stolen too.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<p>Students, parents, shopkeepers and drivers who want a large, loud phone with a battery that does not need attention. It is also a good choice for a household hotspot. People who read a lot on the phone, take pictures at night or play demanding games should save a little longer or choose an AMOLED model.</p>\n<h2>After the first month</h2>\n<p>Owners of very large phones tend to settle into habits: two hands for typing, a pop-up grip or ring on the back, and the one-handed mode for reaching the top. Set that mode up early, in Settings under additional options. Turn on the 80 per cent charge limit at night as well. With a battery this big you will rarely need the last fifth, and the cell will thank you in the third year. If the screen\'s softness bothers you, raise the font one step. Larger text hides the lower resolution surprisingly well.</p>',7.7,'7,500mAh battery\n5G at Rs 33,999\n120Hz screen\nmicroSD slot','HD+ LCD\n4GB RAM on the base model\nBasic second camera','The best budget 5G phone in Nepal right now. Get at least the 256GB version.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(96,109,20,'Nothing Phone (4a) Review: A Periscope Camera for Rs 69,999','nothing-phone-4a-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Nothing Phone (4a) yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Nothing Phone (4a) starts at <strong>Rs 69,999</strong> (8GB/128GB). It\'s the cheapest phone in Nepal with a proper <strong>3.5x periscope zoom</strong>, and it looks unlike anything else at the price.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 50MP periscope makes portraits and distant shots far better than the digital zoom on rivals. The Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 is quick, the 6.78-inch AMOLED runs at 120Hz, and Nothing OS is clean, with no bloatware.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The 5,080mAh battery and 50W charging are only average, the charger isn\'t in the box, and IP64 is the minimum we\'d want. 128GB on the base model fills up fast; the 256GB version costs Rs 79,999.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If you care about zoom photos and a clean interface, it beats the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g\">Galaxy A57</a> on value. If you care about battery, choose the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Nothing Phone (4a)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.78-inch AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass 7i</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main + 8MP ultra wide + 50MP 3.5x periscope | 32MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>5,080mAh; 50W wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, Nothing OS 4.1</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>205g</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>163.9 × 77.5 × 8.5 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The lens that decides the argument</h2>\n<p>A periscope camera bends light through a prism so that a long lens can lie sideways inside a thin phone. Until recently you had to spend well over a lakh to get one. On the Phone (4a) it gives you 3.5x optical zoom on a 50MP sensor for Rs 69,999.</p>\n<p>What does 3.5x do? It is the distance between a photo of a stage and a photo of the dancer. It makes portraits look natural, because you stand further back and faces keep their shape. It lets you pick out a temple roof strut, a sign across the road or a child on a swing. Crop it to 7x and the result is still clean in daylight. Phones that only have a main camera and an 8MP ultra wide cannot do any of that.</p>\n<p>The main camera is a solid 50MP unit. The ultra wide, at 8MP, is the weak lens. In low light, use the main camera and step closer, as the periscope gathers less light.</p>\n<h2>Design and the lights on the back</h2>\n<p>Nothing\'s transparent back and its Glyph lights split opinion, and that is the intention. The lights can act as a timer, show charging progress, flash for chosen contacts or serve as a soft fill light for photos. Some people switch them off after a week and others build routines around them. Either way, the phone does not look like any other at the price, which counts for something when every rival is a plain glass rectangle.</p>\n<p>At 205 grams it is on the heavy side, and the 6.78-inch screen makes it wide. Protection is IP64: dust-tight and splash-proof. That is fine for rain and less than the immersion ratings of the Honor, Redmi and Realme phones nearby.</p>\n<h2>Nothing OS, and why people like it</h2>\n<p>The software looks like a design project, with dotted fonts and monochrome icons, and behaves like clean Android. There are no duplicate app stores, no lock-screen adverts and no games installed without asking. It feels quicker than heavier systems on the same chip. Nothing has been steady with updates, although its promise is shorter than Samsung\'s. Features such as the Essential key and AI notes vary by region and may not all be useful here.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 is a sensible mid-range chip. Apps open fast, the interface stays smooth at 120Hz and casual games are easy. It is not the choice for heavy gaming, where the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> at the same price is far stronger. For photos, social media and work, you will not feel a lack.</p>\n<h2>Battery: the real weakness</h2>\n<p>Around 5,000mAh used to be large. Next to rivals with 7,000 to 10,000mAh it is small. A normal day is fine. A day of travel with maps, camera and mobile data will send you to a charger in the evening. Charging at 50W helps, with the first half taking a little over twenty minutes, but you must buy the charger. Choose a USB PD charger that supports PPS from a known brand.</p>\n<h2>Which version?</h2>\n<p>The 8GB/128GB model is the one that makes the headline price. If you shoot in high resolution and record video, 128GB fills quickly, and there is no microSD slot. The 8GB/256GB version costs Rs 10,000 more, which is a lot for storage. Before paying it, look at your current phone and see how much you use. If it is under 90GB after two years, the base model with cloud backup is enough.</p>\n<h2>Buying and service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Nothing is a young brand with a small network. Official units come through a local distributor, and service is concentrated in Kathmandu. The distinctive back panel is not something a corner shop will have in stock. Use a case, and ask what a replacement back and screen cost. Avoid units brought in from India or Dubai without MDMS registration, however tempting the price.</p>\n<h2>Compared with phones at Rs 70,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Nothing Phone (4a)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>5,080mAh; 50W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W reverse...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;67,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Honor SuperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-15-pro\">Realme 15 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;66,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinixgt-30-pro\">Infinix GT 30 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;65,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8350 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>45W Fast Charging; 30W Wireless Charging, 4.5W Rev...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The list shows how specialised this price band has become. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> is for endurance. The <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> and <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro\">POCO X8 Pro</a> are for speed. The <a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a> is for a big main camera. The Nothing is for zoom, software and style. None of them does everything, so decide on one priority and buy the phone that has it.</p>\n<h2>Questions people ask</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Is it good for TikTok and Reels?</strong> Yes. Stabilised video from the main camera and a 32MP front camera are both good, and the zoom adds a look other phones lack.</li>\n<li><strong>Does it work with Nepali banking apps?</strong> It is a normal Google-certified Android phone, so yes.</li>\n<li><strong>Can I use any earbuds?</strong> Any Bluetooth earbuds work. Nothing\'s own have tighter controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Will 5G work?</strong> It supports the common bands. Coverage depends on the operator and your area.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should choose it</h2>\n<p>Students, creators and design-minded buyers who take portraits and zoomed photos, like clean software and can charge once a day. If you are hard on phones, play heavy games or travel without power, choose one of the others in the table.</p>\n<h2>How to use the periscope well</h2>\n<p>The 3.5x lens works best in good light and at two to five metres from a face. Stand back further than feels natural, tap to focus on the eyes and keep the subject away from the wall behind. Indoors in the evening the phone may quietly switch to a crop from the main camera, so look at the zoom indicator and cover the lens with a finger to check. For stages and mountains, rest your elbows on something. Small movements are magnified too.</p>',8.2,'3.5x periscope at this price\nClean Nothing OS\nDistinctive design\nSnapdragon 7s Gen 4','Average battery\nNo charger in the box\nIP64 only','The best camera phone under Rs 80,000 if you take zoom shots.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(97,125,20,'Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025) Review: RTX 5050 Gaming Without the Premium','lenovo-loq-15-2025-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Lenovo LOQ 15 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Lenovo LOQ 15 (15AHP10) costs <strong>Rs 1,94,999</strong> with a Ryzen 5 220, an <strong>RTX 5050 at 100W</strong>, 16GB DDR5 and a 512GB SSD. It\'s the value step up from the <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a>.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The GPU runs at 100W, which is the figure that matters. Many cheaper laptops limit their GPUs to much less. The 15.6-inch 144Hz screen covers 100% sRGB, so it\'s also fine for photo editing, and 16GB of DDR5 is the right amount.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>300 nits is fine indoors but not in bright rooms. 512GB fills up after a few big games, so plan for a second SSD. The six-core Ryzen 5 220 is fine for gaming but slower than higher-end chips for heavy rendering.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Our value pick for gaming laptops around Rs 2 lakh. See all options in our <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptop guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare, 100% sRGB, 144Hz, G-SYNC®</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB L2 / 16MB L3)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>1x 16GB SO-DIMM DDR5-5600</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB SSD M.2 2242 PCIe® 4.0x4 NVMe®</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>60Wh</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi® 6, 802.11ax 2x2 + BT5.3, 100/1000RPM (RJ45)</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>359.86 x 258.7 x 21.9-23.9 mm (14.17 x 10.19 x 0.86-0.94 inches)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why 100 watts is the important number</h2>\n<p>Two laptops can carry the same RTX 5050 sticker and differ by a quarter or more in games. The reason is power. A graphics chip limited to 50 or 60 watts in a slim body runs slower than the same chip allowed 100 watts in a body with proper cooling. Lenovo gives this LOQ the full figure, and that is why we keep recommending it over prettier machines with a lower limit. When you compare laptops in a shop, ask for the TGP, which is the graphics power in watts. If the seller does not know, look up the exact model code, here 15AHP10.</p>\n<h2>What to expect in games</h2>\n<p>With 8GB of GDDR7 memory on the RTX 5050 and DLSS 4, the LOQ is a comfortable 1080p machine. Competitive games such as Valorant, CS2 and Apex Legends can fill the 144Hz screen at high settings. Story games run at high settings with DLSS on Quality. The newest Unreal Engine 5 titles need medium settings and frame generation to look smooth. Ray tracing is possible in lighter uses and not something to plan on.</p>\n<p>The six-core Ryzen 5 220 is well matched to the graphics chip. It holds back only in games that lean hard on the processor, like large strategy titles, and in heavy rendering or compiling. If you work in Blender or compile big projects daily, an eight-core model will repay the extra cost.</p>\n<h2>A screen you can work on</h2>\n<p>Budget gaming laptops often have panels that cover barely two-thirds of the sRGB colour space, so photos look washed out and red looks orange. The LOQ covers the whole of it. For students in design, architecture or media, that means one machine can do classwork and games. Brightness is 300 nits, adequate for a room and too low for a sunny veranda. G-SYNC keeps the picture free of tearing when frame rates change.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage: plan the first upgrades</h2>\n<p>The specification shows one 16GB stick. A single stick leaves half of the memory bandwidth unused, and that costs frames, particularly in processor-heavy games. Adding a second matching 16GB DDR5-5600 SO-DIMM is the best-value upgrade you can make, giving you 32GB and dual-channel mode together.</p>\n<p>The 512GB SSD is a short 2242 module. After Windows, Office and two large games it will be almost full. Check whether your unit has a second M.2 slot; most LOQ designs do. A 1TB Gen4 NVMe drive installed there takes half an hour and leaves the original for the system. Our <a href=\"/products?category=storage\">storage page</a> shows prices.</p>\n<h2>Battery, noise and weight</h2>\n<p>A 60Wh battery in a gaming laptop gives a few hours of light work if you use the integrated graphics and drop the screen to 60Hz. Do not expect to game unplugged. The charger is large, and the machine weighs well over two kilograms, so it belongs on a desk more than in a backpack.</p>\n<p>Under load the fans are loud. Lenovo gives you three performance modes on a key combination: quiet for lectures, balanced for daily use and performance for games. In the Terai summer, use a stand that lifts the rear edge, and clean the vents each year. Keyboard comfort is good, with a full number pad that helps accounting students.</p>\n<h2>Ports and connection</h2>\n<p>A wired Ethernet port is a welcome sight, because it gives online gamers a steadier connection than any Wi-Fi. HDMI and USB-C connect an external monitor. The Wi-Fi 6 card is a small economy compared with newer 6E or 7 cards and makes little difference on most home routers in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>How it compares around Rs 2 lakh</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB L2 / 16MB L3)</td><td>60Wh</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-summit-e16-flip-evo-2023-i7-1360p-165hz\">MSI Summit E16 Flip Evo A11UCT 2023 (Intel Core i7-1360P)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,95,000</td><td>Intel Core i7-1360P (Intel 7), 12 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 82 Wh; 65W AC Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-rl063ws-2026-ryzen-7-170-rtx-4050\">ASUS TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUQ (Ryzen 7 170, RTX 4050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 7 170 Processor 3.2GHz (20MB Cache, up to 4.75 GH...</td><td>56WHrs, 4S1P, 4-cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-anv16-71-2025-core-i7-14650hx-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga-165hz\">Acer Nitro V 16 ANV16-71 (Core i7-14650HX, RTX 4050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,96,499</td><td>Intel® Core™ i7 14650HX (16 cores, 24 Threads) 2.20 GHz with...</td><td>59 Wh Lithium (Li-Ion) 4 cell</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-5-14iph11-2026-intel-core-ultra-5-322\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 5 14IPH11 2026 (Intel Core Ultra 5 322)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,91,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 322 (Intel 18A), 6 cores / 6 threads</td><td>60 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Notice how many laptops at this price still use the older RTX 4050 with 6GB. They may have a faster processor or a larger screen, but the newer chip with 8GB will age better. Others in the table are thin work machines with no dedicated graphics at all. They are better for travel and battery life, and cannot play the same games.</p>\n<p>Going down, the <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a> saves Rs 35,000 to 45,000 and is a good esports machine. Going up, models with an RTX 5060 give a clear step in frame rates, at Rs 2,40,000 and more. The LOQ sits where each extra rupee still buys something you can feel.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and buying tips</h2>\n<p>Lenovo has an authorised network in Nepal. Ask whether your unit carries a Lenovo warranty, which you can check online with the serial number, or only the importer\'s. Accidental-damage plans are sometimes available for a few thousand rupees and are worth it for a student. Test the screen for uneven light, press every key, run a game for fifteen minutes while watching for crashes, and confirm the 170W or similar original charger is in the box.</p>\n<h2>Good choice for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Engineering, IT and design students who also play.</li>\n<li>First-time PC gamers with about two lakh to spend.</li>\n<li>Streamers and video editors who need an NVIDIA encoder.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Not the best choice for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who carry a laptop everywhere.</li>\n<li>Professionals who render for hours.</li>\n<li>Anyone who wants a bright screen for outdoor use.</li>\n</ul>',8.1,'RTX 5050 at full 100W\n100% sRGB 144Hz screen\n16GB DDR5\nGood value','300-nit screen\n512GB fills fast\nSix-core CPU','The sensible gaming laptop around Rs 2 lakh.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(98,135,20,'Sony WF-1000XM6 Review: The Quietest Commute Money Can Buy','sony-wf-1000xm6-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Sony WF-1000XM6 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>Sony\'s WF-1000XM6 cost <strong>Rs 64,999</strong> in Nepal, the most of any mainstream earbuds here. You\'re paying for some of the strongest noise cancellation available, plus LDAC for high-resolution audio on Android.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>Noise cancellation that handles bus engines and traffic, LDAC and LC3 codec support, and multipoint so they stay connected to your phone and laptop. Battery life is up to 24 hours with the case.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>At 6.2g per bud they\'re bigger than most, and IPX4 is only splash resistance, so they\'re not ideal for the gym. iPhone users lose LDAC, and <a href=\"/products/airpods-pro-3\">AirPods Pro 3</a> integrate better with iOS.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy them for flights, long bus rides and open offices. On a budget, the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro\">Soundcore Liberty 5 Pro</a> gets you most of the features for about half the price.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Sony WF-1000XM6</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Up to 24 hours (earbuds + case)</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3 with multipoint; AAC, SBC, LDAC, LC3</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>6.2g per earbud</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Who pays Rs 64,999 for earbuds?</h2>\n<p>At this price you could buy a decent phone, so it is fair to start with the reason anyone would. The people who get value from the XM6 share a pattern: they wear earbuds for three or more hours a day, in noisy places, and they care about either silence or sound quality. A consultant who flies weekly, a student who lives beside Ring Road and needs to study, a developer in an open office, a musician who travels. For them the cost spreads over a thousand hours of use a year. For someone who listens for twenty minutes at the gym, it is a poor purchase.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling: what is possible</h2>\n<p>Sony\'s approach is to use several microphones on each bud with a dedicated processor, and to adapt to the noise around you and the seal in your ear. It is at its best with constant low sound: bus and aircraft engines, air conditioners, generators, fans. It reduces chatter noticeably. It softens horns and barking, and cannot remove them, since they are too sudden.</p>\n<p>The seal decides how good it gets. Try every tip in the box, use the fit test in the app, and repeat it after a week as the foam settles. If one ear feels less isolated, try a different size on that side. Many people have ears that do not match.</p>\n<p>Please do not use noise cancelling while riding, driving or crossing roads.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>The XM line is tuned for a full, easy sound with deep bass and clear vocals. The app\'s equaliser can make it neutral in a minute. On Android you can use LDAC for higher-bitrate streaming, or LC3, the newer efficient codec, on phones that support Bluetooth LE Audio. On an iPhone you get AAC. The difference between LDAC and AAC is real but modest, and you need well-mastered music from a high-quality service to hear it. Free-tier streams will not reveal it.</p>\n<h2>Size and comfort</h2>\n<p>Each bud weighs 6.2 grams, a little more than the XM5\'s 5.9 grams, and the body is larger than an AirPod\'s. People with small ears should try them first. Foam tips are comfortable for long sessions, though they get dirty and need replacing every few months. Spare tips in Sony\'s sizes can be hard to find locally, so it is wise to buy a set when you see one.</p>\n<h2>Calls and multipoint</h2>\n<p>For people who spend their day in meetings, call quality may matter more than music. The microphones and voice pickup do a good job indoors and in moderate noise. Multipoint keeps a phone and a laptop connected together, so a call on one interrupts a video on the other without re-pairing. It is reliable once set up, though switching on LDAC may limit it depending on the phone.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Up to 24 hours in total with the case is enough for a working week of commuting. A short charge gives an hour or so of listening, and the case supports wireless charging. As with all earbuds, the cells inside are tiny and will lose capacity in two to three years of daily use. Treat that as part of the price.</p>\n<h2>Water resistance</h2>\n<p>IPX4 covers sweat and splashes. It is enough for walking in drizzle and light exercise. For running in the monsoon or hard gym sessions, choose a sports model with IP55 or better and hooks that hold it in place.</p>\n<h2>XM6, XM5 or something else?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm5\">WF-1000XM5</a> is about Rs 15,000 cheaper and remains excellent. The newer model improves noise cancelling, call pickup and the connection. If you already own the XM5, keep it until the battery fades. If you are buying your first pair of premium Sony earbuds, the XM6 will stay current for longer, but the older pair is better value whenever the gap is large.</p>\n<p>For iPhone owners the <a href=\"/products/airpods-pro-3\">AirPods Pro 3</a> at Rs 47,999 are the sensible alternative. They pair instantly with every Apple device, offer personalised spatial audio and cost Rs 17,000 less. On Android they lose most of those benefits.</p>\n<p>If you want quiet without the luxury price, the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro\">Soundcore Liberty 5 Pro</a> and similar models give you strong noise cancelling and a good app. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">guide to earbuds under Rs 20,000</a> goes lower still. And if comfort and battery matter more than pocket size, full-size headphones such as Sony\'s WH series cancel more noise for similar money.</p>\n<h2>Settings to change on the first day</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Run the ear-tip fit test.</li>\n<li>Turn off the feature that pauses music when you talk, unless you like it.</li>\n<li>Limit maximum volume. Noise cancelling means you can listen more quietly, which protects your hearing.</li>\n<li>Set one long press to the ambient mode for shop counters and announcements.</li>\n<li>Update the firmware before judging the sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying genuine ones</h2>\n<p>Counterfeit Sony earbuds are common and increasingly convincing. Buy from a seller who issues a VAT bill and an official warranty. Pair them with Sony\'s app in the shop: a genuine set is recognised, shows its serial and firmware and completes the fit test. Check that the serial number on the case matches the box. If the price is far below Rs 64,999, be suspicious.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<p>Wipe the tips and charging contacts weekly, dry them after sweat, and keep the case away from keys and coins. Do not leave them in a car in summer, since heat is the fastest way to age the batteries. With that small amount of care, they should give you several good years.</p>\n<h2>Two things to settle before paying</h2>\n<p>First, decide how you will replace the foam tips. They are part of why these earbuds are so quiet, and they wear out. Ask the dealer whether sets are in stock and what they cost, and buy a spare with the earbuds if you can.</p>\n<p>Second, think about loss. A pair at this price lives in pockets, on bus seats and beside tea cups. Switch on the find feature on the first evening, put your name on a small sticker inside the case, and make a habit of closing the lid with both buds in it before you stand up. It sounds fussy, and it is cheaper than a single replacement bud.</p>',8.6,'Class-leading noise cancellation\nLDAC and LC3\nMultipoint\n24-hour battery with case','Rs 64,999\nBulky buds\nIPX4 only','The best noise-cancelling earbuds in Nepal, if you can justify the price.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(99,130,20,'AirPods Pro 3 Review: The Default Choice for iPhone Owners','airpods-pro-3-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the AirPods Pro 3 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>AirPods Pro 3 cost <strong>Rs 47,999</strong> in Nepal. For iPhone users they\'re the easiest recommendation: instant pairing, automatic switching between Apple devices, and noise cancellation that copes well with Kathmandu traffic.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>Strong noise cancellation and an excellent Transparency mode. IP57 means sweat and rain aren\'t a worry. Battery life reaches up to 24 hours with the case and ANC on, and they\'re comfortable at 5.55g per bud.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>On Android you lose most of what makes them special, and they only support the AAC and SBC codecs. Android users get more from the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4-pro\">Galaxy Buds 4 Pro</a> or the <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">Sony WF-1000XM6</a>.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If you own an iPhone, buy these. If you don\'t, look elsewhere.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Apple AirPods Pro 3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple H2 with custom high-excursion driver</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Up to 24 hours with ANC (earbuds + case)</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3; AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>5.55g per earbud</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The case for them in one week of use</h2>\n<p>Feature lists do not explain why iPhone owners stop looking at other earbuds. A normal week does. On Monday you open the case beside a phone and they connect. On Tuesday a call arrives on your Mac during a video on your iPad, and the sound moves by itself. On Wednesday you take one bud out to talk to a shopkeeper and the music pauses. On Thursday you leave them in a teashop, and Find My shows the case on a map and lets you play a sound. None of this needs an app or a setting. Other brands match individual pieces. Nobody else gets all of it on an iPhone, since Apple does not give outside companies the same access.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling and Transparency</h2>\n<p>These are among the quietest earbuds you can buy. The new foam-infused tips matter as much as the electronics, because they block more sound physically. Apple includes a wider range of sizes than before. Run the fit test in Settings with each of two sizes, and choose the one that stays put when you chew.</p>\n<p>Transparency is the mode that other makers still chase. Voices sound natural, as if you were not wearing anything, and sudden loud sounds such as a horn are softened. For walking in city traffic it is the safer choice than full cancellation. Adaptive mode mixes the two and changes as you move, which works well in an office and is less predictable on a street.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>The balance is lively with stronger bass than the previous model and clear vocals. Spatial audio with head tracking is impressive with films on an iPad and a matter of taste with music. There is no high-resolution codec: iPhones send AAC over Bluetooth. At a decent bitrate AAC sounds very good, and few people can identify a difference in a blind test on the move. If you collect lossless music and listen in silence, wired headphones remain the better answer.</p>\n<h2>Health features</h2>\n<p>Apple has added sensors and hearing tools to this line, including a hearing test and an assistance mode in supported countries, along with heart-rate sensing during workouts. Availability of the medical functions depends on your region, and Nepal is often absent from those lists. Do not buy them as a hearing aid. The heart-rate reading is useful for people who train with an iPhone and do not own an Apple Watch.</p>\n<h2>Fit, sweat and rain</h2>\n<p>Each bud weighs about 5.5 grams, and the angle of the stem has been adjusted so that most people forget them after ten minutes. IP57 for buds and case is a real improvement. It covers dust and a short dip in fresh water, so running in drizzle or a spill at the gym is no concern. They are still not for swimming.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>You can expect roughly a full working day of on-and-off listening, with the case bringing the total to about 24 hours with cancellation on. Five minutes in the case gives around an hour. The case charges by USB-C, MagSafe, Qi and an Apple Watch charger. Like all small lithium cells, the ones inside will fade after two or three years of daily use. Apple offers a battery service, at a price that is sometimes close to a discounted new pair.</p>\n<h2>If you use Android</h2>\n<p>They will connect as ordinary Bluetooth earbuds. Noise cancelling and Transparency work by squeezing the stem. You lose automatic switching, customisation, spatial audio, the fit test, Find My and easy firmware updates. At Rs 47,999 that is a poor bargain. Look at the <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm5\">Sony WF-1000XM5</a> or the Galaxy Buds 4 Pro for Samsung phones.</p>\n<h2>AirPods Pro 2 owners</h2>\n<p>If your second-generation pair still lasts the day, the upgrade is nice and not necessary. You would gain stronger cancelling, better fit, improved water resistance and heart-rate sensing. If your battery is tired, this is the moment.</p>\n<h2>What else is at this price?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Apple AirPods Pro 3</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>Apple H2 with custom high-excursion driver</td><td>Up to 24 hours with ANC (earbuds + case)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm5\">Sony WF-1000XM5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;50,000</td><td>–</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 16 hours (case); Quick cha...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro-max\">Soundcore Liberty 5 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>–</td><td>Music: 6.5 hours (buds) / 28 hours (case), With AN...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For half the money, earbuds such as the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro\">Soundcore Liberty 5 Pro</a> cancel noise impressively and work the same on every phone. They will not switch between your Apple devices by magic, and on a tight budget you may not care.</p>\n<h2>Fakes are everywhere</h2>\n<p>AirPods are among the most copied products in Nepal. Good copies show the same pairing animation and even a serial number that belongs to a real pair. Check properly. Open Settings on an iPhone with the earbuds connected and look for the full feature page, including the ear-tip fit test, Adaptive audio and firmware version. Run the fit test and switch between all three noise modes. Enter the serial number on Apple\'s coverage page and make sure the product, purchase date and warranty make sense. Look at the print quality on the case. If a seller asks Rs 8,000 for \"original\", it is not.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Buy from an authorised reseller and keep the bill. One-year service is handled through authorised providers, and they may replace a single bud instead of the full set. Register the pair to your Apple account right away, so that nobody else can use them if they are lost.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>iPhone users who also own a Mac, iPad or Apple Watch.</li>\n<li>Commuters who want excellent quiet without studying settings.</li>\n<li>Runners who need water resistance and heart-rate data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should skip them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Android users.</li>\n<li>People who lose earbuds. A Rs 5,000 pair hurts less.</li>\n<li>Audiophiles who want high-bitrate wireless.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Before the return window closes</h2>\n<p>Use the first few days properly. Try all the tip sizes with the fit test in Settings. Take a call on a street, ride a bus, run or climb stairs with them, and wear them for two hours at a desk. Check that both buds charge to the same level and that the case speaker plays when you use Find My. If one ear keeps losing its seal, ask about exchanging them for AirPods 4, which have no tips and suit some ears better.</p>',8.7,'Seamless with iPhone, iPad and Mac\nStrong ANC and Transparency\nIP57\nComfortable','Limited on Android\nAAC/SBC only\nExpensive in Nepal','The best earbuds for iPhone owners in Nepal.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(100,146,20,'Galaxy Watch 9 Review: The Best Watch for Android, Charged Every Day and a Half','samsung-galaxy-watch9-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy Watch 9 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Galaxy Watch 9 costs <strong>Rs 66,499</strong> (40mm) or Rs 71,499 (44mm) in Nepal. For Android users, and Samsung owners in particular, it\'s the closest thing to an Apple Watch.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>A 3nm Snapdragon Wear Elite makes it fast, the AMOLED reaches 3,000 nits under sapphire glass, and dual-band GPS improves accuracy on runs and treks. Samsung\'s BioActive sensor adds body-composition readings to heart and temperature tracking. It\'s rated 5ATM, IP68 and MIL-STD-810H.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>Battery life is about a day and a half. That\'s normal for smartwatches but short next to the <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Huawei Watch GT 6</a> (up to three weeks). Some health features work best with a Samsung phone.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy it if you want apps, payments and a full smartwatch on Android. Buy the Huawei if you want weeks of battery.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy Watch 9</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>1.34-inch (40mm) or 1.47-inch (44mm) AMOLED, 3,000 nits, sapphire crystal</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon Wear Elite (3nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>2GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>32GB</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>390mAh (40mm) / 445mAh (44mm), about 1.5 days</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Wear OS 7, One UI Watch 9</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, dual-band GPS (L1 + L5)</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>31.5g (40mm) / 34g (44mm)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Smartwatch or fitness band: decide that first</h2>\n<p>Many people who say they want a smartwatch need a fitness tracker, and the other way round. A fitness watch counts steps, shows notifications, records exercise and lasts a week or more. A full smartwatch, such as the Galaxy Watch 9, runs apps, replies to messages by voice or keyboard, plays music without the phone, gives turn-by-turn directions and takes calls properly. In return it wants a charger every day or two.</p>\n<p>If you have never installed an app on a watch and only want health numbers, read our <a href=\"/reviews/huawei-watch-gt-6-review-nepal\">Huawei Watch GT 6 review</a> before spending Rs 66,499.</p>\n<h2>Speed and software</h2>\n<p>The 3nm Snapdragon Wear Elite is a big change from earlier chips. Menus respond straight away, voice replies process quickly and maps draw without waiting. Wear OS 7 with Samsung\'s One UI on top gives access to the Play Store on the wrist. Google Maps, WhatsApp, Spotify, Strava and calculators all have proper apps. Google and Samsung\'s assistants understand English well. Nepali voice typing is limited, so most replies will be in English or by quick phrases.</p>\n<h2>Screen and build</h2>\n<p>Sapphire crystal is far harder than ordinary glass and resists scratches from walls, zips and keys. At 3,000 nits the AMOLED is readable in strong sun. The two sizes suit different wrists: 40mm for slim ones, 44mm for most men and anyone who wants a bigger battery. At around 32 to 34 grams without the strap, both are light enough for sleep tracking.</p>\n<p>The watch is rated 5ATM and IP68, which covers pools, showers with care and monsoon rain, and it meets a military standard for shock and temperature. Rinse it in fresh water after a pool, since chlorine eats at seals.</p>\n<h2>Health tracking you can rely on, and what you cannot</h2>\n<p>Heart rate, sleep stages, step counts, blood oxygen and skin temperature trends are useful when you look at weeks of data. The BioActive sensor estimates body fat and muscle using a small current. It is fine for seeing a direction of change, and not a medical reading. ECG and blood pressure depend on regional approval and on using a Samsung phone, and they may not be enabled in Nepal. No watch replaces a doctor. If it warns of an irregular rhythm or high heart rate at rest, get checked.</p>\n<h2>GPS for runs and treks</h2>\n<p>Dual-band GPS listens to two frequencies, so it copes better with tall buildings and deep valleys. Recorded distances in places like Thamel or on the Langtang trail should be closer to the truth than with single-band watches. Battery is the limit. Continuous GPS with the screen on will drain the watch in several hours, so it suits a day walk but not a week\'s trek without a power bank.</p>\n<h2>Battery in practice</h2>\n<p>About a day and a half with normal settings means a routine: charge while you shower and dress, and wear it at night for sleep tracking. Turn off the always-on display and it stretches towards two days. The charger is a small magnetic puck and should be packed for every trip. If a nightly charge sounds like a chore, this is not your watch.</p>\n<h2>Which phones does it work with?</h2>\n<p>It needs an Android phone. Any recent model will do, but some health features and the smoothest experience require a Samsung. It does not work with iPhones at all. Bluetooth and LTE versions exist in other markets. Check which one you are buying, as cellular watch plans are not a normal product in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>How it compares</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy Watch 9</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;66,499</td><td>Snapdragon Wear Elite (3nm)</td><td>390mAh (40mm) / 445mAh (44mm), about 1.5 days</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-7-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 7 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;64,990</td><td>1.47 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 12 days (normal usage), Up to 21 days (low p...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-ultra\">Amazfit T-Rex Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,000</td><td>1.39 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 25 days (normal usage), Up to 50 days (low p...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-watch-series-11\">Apple Watch Series 11</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,000</td><td>42mm: 1.77 inches, 46mm: 1.96 inches, Retina LTPO3 OLED</td><td>Up to 24 hours (normal usage), Up to 38 hours (low...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The older <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-8\">Galaxy Watch 8</a> sometimes sells for a few thousand rupees less. It has a slower chip and is otherwise similar, so it is worth buying only at a clear discount. The <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-7-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 7 Pro</a> gives you premium materials and long battery life without apps. The <a href=\"/products/apple-watch-series-11\">Apple Watch Series 11</a> is the equivalent for iPhone users. The <a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-ultra\">Amazfit T-Rex Ultra</a> is for people who go off-grid.</p>\n<h2>Payments and local services</h2>\n<p>Samsung Wallet and Google Wallet on the watch are attractive features abroad. In Nepal, tap-to-pay support from local banks is limited, so do not count on leaving your wallet at home. Notifications from eSewa, Khalti and banking apps arrive normally, which is handy for OTP messages.</p>\n<h2>Everyday tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose which apps may send notifications on the first day. Ten useful alerts beat two hundred.</li>\n<li>Turn on fall detection and add an emergency contact for elderly users.</li>\n<li>Use a fabric or sport strap for exercise. Leather ruins quickly in summer.</li>\n<li>Keep the battery protection option on if you charge every night.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Should you buy it?</h2>\n<p>With a Samsung phone and a real interest in apps, replies and maps on your wrist, it is the best Android watch available here. With another Android phone it is still good, only a little less complete. With an iPhone, or with a dislike of daily charging, choose something else and keep Rs 20,000 or more in your pocket.</p>\n<h2>Living with daily charging</h2>\n<p>The trick is to attach it to something you already do. Put the charger beside the toothbrush and charge while you shower and dress. Thirty to forty minutes is enough for a day. Then sleep tracking works every night and the watch never dies at dinner. People who charge overnight lose the sleep data that they paid for. Keep a second charger at the office if you can, as genuine ones can be bought separately and the cheap copies charge slowly and grow warm.</p>',8.3,'Fast 3nm chip\nSapphire, 3,000-nit AMOLED\nDual-band GPS\nBioActive health sensor','About 1.5 days of battery\nBest with Samsung phones\nPricey','The best full smartwatch for Android users in Nepal.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(101,149,20,'Huawei Watch GT 6 Review: Three Weeks of Battery, Few Compromises','huawei-watch-gt-6-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Huawei Watch GT 6 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Huawei Watch GT 6 costs <strong>Rs 34,490</strong> in Nepal, and the GT 6 Pro (titanium, sapphire) costs Rs 54,490. The reason to buy one is battery life: up to <strong>21 days</strong> on the 46mm model.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>You charge it a couple of times a month. The 466×466 AMOLED reaches 3,000 nits, dual-band GPS records routes accurately on runs and treks, and it\'s rated 5ATM. It works with both Android and iPhone.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>HarmonyOS has no third-party app store like Wear OS or watchOS, so it\'s a fitness watch with smart features rather than a full smartwatch. Replying to messages is limited, especially on iPhone.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For trekkers, runners and anyone who hates charging, it\'s the best value watch in Nepal. If you want apps and payments, see the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch9\">Galaxy Watch 9</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Huawei Watch GT 6</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>1.32-inch (41mm) or 1.47-inch (46mm) AMOLED (466×466), 3,000 nits</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Up to 14 days (41mm) / 21 days (46mm)</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>HarmonyOS 5</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Dual-band GPS (L1 + L5)</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>37.5g (41mm) / 51.3g (46mm) / 54.7g (Pro)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Three weeks: where the number comes from</h2>\n<p>Huawei\'s 21-day claim belongs to the 46mm model under light use. Switch on the always-on display, record several GPS workouts a week and allow plenty of notifications, and it will be closer to ten or twelve days. The 41mm version, rated at 14, will be nearer to a week. Even those halved figures are far beyond any Wear OS or Apple Watch.</p>\n<p>The effect on daily life is larger than it sounds. You never think about the watch at night, so you sleep with it on and the sleep data has no holes. You can go on a ten-day trek without packing its charger. And because it is charged perhaps twice a month, the battery will stay healthy for longer.</p>\n<h2>For trekkers and runners</h2>\n<p>Dual-band GPS, an altimeter and a compass make it a capable outdoor companion. It records routes accurately in valleys, shows elevation gain and can guide you back along the track you walked. Offline maps and route import depend on the model and app version, so test that at home before relying on it. Runners get training load, recovery advice and pace coaching. Cyclists can see estimated power and cadence when paired with the phone.</p>\n<p>One thing to note about altitude: wrist blood-oxygen readings become less reliable when your hands are cold. If you are going above 3,500 metres, carry a fingertip oximeter and listen to your body.</p>\n<h2>Health features</h2>\n<p>Heart rate is tracked all day, with alerts for unusually high or low values. Sleep staging and stress estimates are sensible as trends. Huawei has added an emotional wellbeing indicator and arrhythmia analysis to this generation. Some of those features are limited by region and all of them are guidance and not diagnosis.</p>\n<h2>Screen and comfort</h2>\n<p>Both sizes use a sharp 466 by 466 AMOLED that reaches 3,000 nits. It looks like a watch and less like a gadget, with a rotating crown and a proper bezel. At about 37 and 51 grams, the smaller one is comfortable for slim wrists and sleep, and the larger one wears like a normal sports watch. The Pro, in titanium and sapphire, costs Rs 20,000 more. Materials are the main difference, along with a few diving and golf features, so buy it for how it feels and not for health data.</p>\n<h2>What it cannot do</h2>\n<p>There is no Play Store. A few third-party apps exist in Huawei\'s gallery, but you will not find WhatsApp, Spotify or Google Maps as full apps. Notifications show the text of messages. On Android you can send short quick replies in some apps. On an iPhone you can only read. Calls work over Bluetooth using the watch\'s speaker and microphone, which is useful with wet or dirty hands.</p>\n<p>Music can be stored on the watch and played to Bluetooth earbuds. Streaming without the phone is not possible. If those limits bother you, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch9\">Galaxy Watch 9</a> is what you are looking for.</p>\n<h2>Setting it up with a non-Huawei phone</h2>\n<p>On Android the Huawei Health app is best installed from Huawei\'s own website or AppGallery, because the Play Store version may be older. That means allowing an installation from outside the store. Download it only from the official address, and refuse copies sent by friends. On iPhone it is in the App Store. After pairing, allow the app to run in the background, as many phones stop it and then people complain about missing notifications.</p>\n<h2>Compared with others near Rs 35,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Huawei Watch GT 6</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;34,490</td><td>1.32-inch (41mm) or 1.47-inch (46mm) AMOLED (466×466), 3,000...</td><td>Up to 14 days (41mm) / 21 days (46mm)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-watch-6\">Honor Watch 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>1.46 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 17 days (Standard usage), Up to 35 days (Cas...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah\">Amazfit Cheetah</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;36,999</td><td>1.39 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (heavy usage), Up to 14 days (low pow...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-4-pro\">Huawei Watch Fit 4 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;38,990</td><td>1.82 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage), Up to 10 days (low po...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-watch-s5\">Xiaomi Watch S5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>1.48-inch AMOLED (480×480), 2,500 nits</td><td>Up to 21 days (light use)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/honor-watch-6\">Honor Watch 6</a> is close in spirit and price. The <a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah\">Amazfit Cheetah</a> is a lightweight running watch with strong training tools and a more open app for exporting data. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-watch-s5\">Xiaomi Watch S5</a> has an attractive round design. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-7\">Galaxy Watch 7</a> at a lower price is a full smartwatch with one-day battery. Each serves a different person.</p>\n<h2>Strava and data export</h2>\n<p>If you share workouts on Strava or Komoot, check the current state of syncing. Huawei Health supports linking to some services, and it has been less open than Garmin or Amazfit in the past. Serious runners who live in Strava should confirm this before buying.</p>\n<h2>Buying and warranty</h2>\n<p>Huawei wearables are sold officially in Nepal. Ask for a warranty card, check that the strap size fits, and make sure the box contains the magnetic charger for this model. Third-party straps use a standard width, so replacements are cheap and easy to find. A spare charging puck is worth having if you travel a lot.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Trekkers, runners and field workers.</li>\n<li>People who dislike charging things.</li>\n<li>iPhone and Android users who want health tracking with a classic look.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who it does not suit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone who wants to reply to messages or use apps on the wrist.</li>\n<li>People who want mobile payments.</li>\n<li>Athletes tied to other training platforms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For its purpose it is very hard to fault. It tells the time, watches over your health, records your walks and otherwise leaves you alone, for weeks at a stretch.</p>\n<h2>41mm or 46mm?</h2>\n<p>Measure the flat part of your wrist. Under about 52mm, the larger watch will overhang and rock when you type. The 41mm model is lighter and prettier on slim wrists, and gives up roughly a third of the battery life, which still leaves more than a week. If you plan to record long treks, choose the 46mm whatever your wrist, because GPS hours matter more than elegance on the trail. Straps differ in width between the two, so buy spares to match.</p>',8.4,'Up to 21 days of battery\nDual-band GPS\nBright AMOLED\nWorks with Android and iPhone','No third-party app store\nLimited replies\nPro is much pricier','The battery-life champion: our top smartwatch pick for trekkers.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(102,255,20,'Moto G64 Review: The Most Phone You Can Get for Rs 30,000','moto-g64-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Moto G64 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Moto G64 costs <strong>Rs 29,999</strong> for 8GB/128GB, and the 12GB/256GB model is only Rs 1,000 more at Rs 30,999. At this price most phones give you 4GB of RAM, an HD+ screen and a slow chip. The G64 gives you none of those compromises.</p>\n<h2>What you get for the money</h2>\n<p>The 6.5-inch screen is <strong>Full HD+ at 120Hz</strong>, sharper than the HD+ panels on most rivals here. The MediaTek Dimensity 7025 is a 6nm 5G chip that is clearly faster than the Unisoc and Helio chips in this class. A 6,000mAh battery with 30W charging and an 8MP ultra wide camera (rare under Rs 30,000) round it out.</p>\n<h2>The catches</h2>\n<p>It\'s an LCD, not AMOLED, so blacks look grey in a dark room. It launched on Android 14, and Motorola\'s update promise is short compared with Samsung\'s. The design is plain plastic with only IP52 splash resistance.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> (Rs 25,499) has longer software support but a slower chip and an HD+ screen. The <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50\">Tecno Spark 50</a> (Rs 29,999) has a bigger 7,000mAh battery but a much weaker processor. If you can stretch to about Rs 40,000, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> adds an AMOLED screen and a 7,000mAh battery.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Motorola Moto G64</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.5 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 560 nits (typ)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7025 (6nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB UFS 2.2</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8, OIS, PDAF + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, 118° FOV | 16MP, f/2.4 front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 6000 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 14, Android 14</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.3, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>192 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>161.6 x 73.8 x 8.9 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>First, check that it is still available</h2>\n<p>The G64 went on sale in April 2024 and has since been marked as discontinued in our catalogue, so prices in this review are the last ones we recorded. You may still find sealed stock in some shops and plenty of used ones. If a seller asks the same Rs 29,999 today, compare it with newer phones first, because what made it special was how much it offered at that price two years ago. At a discount it remains a good phone.</p>\n<h2>Why it became a favourite</h2>\n<p>Budget phones usually cut the same corners: a low-resolution screen, 4GB of memory and slow storage. Motorola refused. The G64 has a sharp Full HD+ panel, 8GB or 12GB of memory, UFS storage and a chip with 5G. Anyone upgrading from a three-year-old budget phone feels the difference in the first minute, as apps open without the usual pause.</p>\n<p>It is also one of the few phones in its class with optical stabilisation on the main camera. In dim rooms, that lets the phone use a slower shutter without blur. Budget rivals with the same 50MP number and no stabilisation produce softer pictures.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 6.5-inch LCD is smooth at 120Hz and sharp enough for long reading. Brightness of around 560 nits is fine indoors and in shade, and you will tilt the phone to read it at midday. Being an LCD, it cannot show true black. If you watch films in a dark room, an AMOLED phone such as the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> looks better.</p>\n<h2>Performance today</h2>\n<p>The Dimensity 7025 is a capable 6nm chip. Social apps, video calls, banking and office documents are smooth. PUBG Mobile and Free Fire run at medium settings. The 12GB version keeps many apps open, and since the price difference was only Rs 1,000, that is the one to look for.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>With 6,000mAh, it lasts well over a day for most people, and the 30W charger fills it in a little less than two hours. A used phone from 2024 will have lost some capacity. Android 15 and later show battery health on some models. If yours does not, charge to full, use it for a normal day and see how much is left.</p>\n<h2>Software: the honest weakness</h2>\n<p>Motorola promised one Android upgrade and three years of security patches for the G64. It launched with Android 14, so the upgrade to 15 is the last version it will get, with security patches ending around 2027. That does not stop the phone working, but banking apps raise their minimum requirements over time. For someone who plans to use it until 2029, a Samsung with longer support is the safer choice.</p>\n<p>In every other way the software is pleasant. It is close to plain Android, with few extra apps. The Moto gestures, a double chop for the torch and a double twist for the camera, are the kind of small idea you miss on other phones.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera is good in daylight and acceptable at night. The 8MP ultra wide has autofocus, so it also takes close-ups. The 16MP selfie camera is fine for calls and social media. Video tops out at 1080p, with steady results thanks to stabilisation.</p>\n<h2>Durability and connectivity</h2>\n<p>IP52 protects against dust and drips and nothing more. Carry it in a pouch during rain. There is a headphone jack, a microSD slot that shares space with the second SIM, side fingerprint reader and stereo speakers. For students, the headphone jack and card slot are more useful than they sound.</p>\n<h2>What to buy instead in late 2026</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For support:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a>, slower but with a long update promise.</li>\n<li><strong>For battery:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50\">Tecno Spark 50</a> with 7,000mAh.</li>\n<li><strong>For a better screen:</strong> spend a little more on the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> with AMOLED.</li>\n<li><strong>From the same brand:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Moto G35 5G</a>, a newer but simpler phone, or the <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> if you can reach Rs 45,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying a used G64</h2>\n<p>Motorola\'s service presence in Nepal is smaller than Samsung\'s or Xiaomi\'s, so condition matters. Look for even brightness, test both speakers, take a photo in a dark room to check that stabilisation works, try both SIM slots and the card slot, and verify MDMS registration. Ask when the battery was last replaced, if ever. Expect to pay clearly less than the original price, and walk away from a unit with a third-party screen, which usually shows bluish whites and poor touch at the edges.</p>\n<h2>Who should still consider it</h2>\n<p>A student who needs a fast phone for little money and changes phones every two years. A second phone for hotspot use, where the battery and 5G modem help. Someone who likes clean Android and a headphone jack. For parents and long-term owners, choose a phone with more update years ahead.</p>\n<h2>Final thoughts</h2>\n<p>The Moto G64 set a standard that many newer phones at Rs 30,000 still fail to reach, which says more about the market than about the phone. Memory prices have risen, and manufacturers have gone back to 4GB and HD+ screens to hold the line. If you find one at a fair price in good condition, it is a smart buy. If you find one at full price, let it go.</p>',7.8,'Full HD+ 120Hz screen at this price\nDimensity 7025 is quick for the class, with 5G\n12GB/256GB costs only Rs 1,000 more\n6,000mAh battery with 30W charging','LCD rather than AMOLED\nShort software support\nOnly IP52 splash resistance','The best all-rounder under Rs 30,000. Buy the 12GB/256GB version, since it costs just Rs 1,000 more.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(103,179,20,'Galaxy A07 Review: Samsung Reliability on a Tight Budget','samsung-galaxy-a07-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy A07 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Galaxy A07 starts at <strong>Rs 25,499</strong> for 4GB/64GB, with 4GB/128GB at Rs 29,499 and 6GB/128GB at Rs 33,499. It\'s Samsung\'s cheapest current phone in Nepal, and it\'s for buyers who want a known brand, local service centres and long software support more than the best specs.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The MediaTek Helio G99 is a proven 6nm chip that handles WhatsApp, Facebook, YouTube and light games without fuss. The 6.7-inch 90Hz screen is big, it has a microSD slot, and IP54 protects against dust and rain splashes. Samsung\'s software support for its recent A-series phones is far longer than most budget brands offer, so it should stay secure for years.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The screen is only HD+, so text is less sharp than on the <a href=\"/products/moto-g64\">Moto G64</a>. It\'s a 4G phone. That\'s fine on NTC and Ncell today, but it won\'t use 5G as coverage grows. The second rear camera is a 2MP filler, so in practice it\'s a single-camera phone.</p>\n<h2>Which version to buy</h2>\n<p>Skip the 64GB model: apps and photos fill it within a year. The 4GB/128GB at Rs 29,499 is the sensible choice, but at that price the Moto G64 gives you 8GB, 5G and a sharper screen.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy A07</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.7 inches PLS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (typ)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB / 6GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>64GB / 128GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8, PDAF, 1/2.76” S5KJN1 sensor + Depth: 2MP, f/2.4 | 8MP, f/2.0 front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, One UI 7</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.3, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>184 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>164.4 × 77.4 × 7.6 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Buying for someone else</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy A07 is rarely chosen by people who read spec sheets for fun. It is chosen by a daughter buying for her father in Palpa, by a small business that needs five identical phones, by a student\'s parents. Seen from that angle, the questions change. Will it last? Can it be repaired nearby? Will the apps still work in four years? Can I help over the phone when something goes wrong? On those questions the A07 does well.</p>\n<h2>The chip is the pleasant surprise</h2>\n<p>MediaTek\'s Helio G99 has been around for years and has earned a good reputation. It is built on a 6nm process, so it runs cool, and it has two strong cores that keep the interface responsive. Phones at the same price often use 12nm Unisoc or Helio G81 chips that feel noticeably slower when you open the camera or switch apps. For WhatsApp, Facebook, YouTube, maps, mobile banking and a little Ludo or Free Fire, this is enough.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage, in rupees</h2>\n<p>There are three versions. The 4GB/64GB model at Rs 25,499 looks tempting and is the wrong one. After the system takes its share you get roughly 40GB, and a few months of WhatsApp media will fill it. The 4GB/128GB model at Rs 29,499 is the sensible middle. The 6GB/128GB at Rs 33,499 is smoother over the years, but at that price other phones appear with AMOLED screens and bigger batteries.</p>\n<p>A memory card helps with photos and videos. It does not help with apps, which need internal space. So if money is tight, take 128GB of internal storage before anything else.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>At 6.7 inches it is large, at 90Hz it is reasonably smooth, and at HD+ it is not sharp. Older readers often increase the text size, in which case the lower resolution hardly shows. Brightness is moderate, so direct sunlight needs shading with a hand. The big panel makes video calls with grandchildren pleasant, which is arguably its main job.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>The 5,000mAh battery combined with an efficient chip and a low-resolution screen lasts more than a day for most people. It charges at up to 25W, which is faster than the 15W common at this price, but the charger is not in the box. Samsung\'s own 25W charger or another PPS model will do. An old 10W charger also works, only slowly.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera takes clear pictures in daylight and weak ones at night. The second lens is a 2MP depth sensor with no practical use. The 8MP selfie camera is good enough for video calls. If photography matters at all, this is not the phone.</p>\n<h2>4G only: does it matter?</h2>\n<p>For the next few years, no. NTC and Ncell\'s 4G covers the places where most people live, while 5G is limited, as described in our <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">5G article</a>. Someone who keeps a phone for five years in Kathmandu may wish for 5G at the end. Someone in a rural municipality probably will not.</p>\n<h2>Long support is the reason to pay for the badge</h2>\n<p>Samsung has extended support even on cheap A-series phones, to a length that almost no rival offers at this price. Security patches close holes used by scam apps and malware, which are a genuine danger for older users in Nepal who receive fake lottery and parcel messages. An up-to-date phone and Samsung\'s Auto Blocker feature, which stops apps from being installed outside the stores, make a good combination. Turn Auto Blocker on during setup.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>IP54 covers dust and rain splashes. The plastic back does not shatter. Add a simple silicone case and a screen protector and it will survive ordinary life.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy A07</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a77\">ZTE Nubia A77</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>Li-ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Chargin...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x5d\">Honor X5d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5260 mAh, non-removable; 15W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-50-pro-plus\">Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,999</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Other phones in the table offer bigger batteries or smoother screens. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y05\">vivo Y05</a> has 6,500mAh and a brighter display with a slower chip. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x5d\">Honor X5d</a> and <a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">Realme C71</a> compete on battery and design. A little above, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07s\">Galaxy A07s</a> is a variation worth comparing on the day. Read our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-30000-nepal\">best phones under Rs 30,000</a> for the full picture.</p>\n<h2>Set it up for a parent in fifteen minutes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Easy mode and a large font.</li>\n<li>Put the phone, WhatsApp, camera and torch on the first page and remove the rest.</li>\n<li>Turn on Auto Blocker, Find My Mobile and automatic updates.</li>\n<li>Add emergency contacts and medical details to the lock screen.</li>\n<li>Switch off notifications from shopping and news apps.</li>\n<li>Show them that a bank will never ask for an OTP by phone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict in plain words</h2>\n<p>It is an ordinary phone done carefully. Nothing about it will excite a young buyer, and that buyer should look at Redmi, Infinix or Tecno. For anyone who needs a dependable phone from a brand with service in many towns, the 4GB/128GB Galaxy A07 does its job and will keep doing it.</p>',7.3,'Samsung brand, service network and long updates\nReliable Helio G99 chip\nIP54 and microSD slot\nBig 6.7-inch 90Hz screen','HD+ resolution\n4G only\n64GB base storage is too small\nSecond camera adds nothing','A safe, long-lasting first smartphone or phone for parents. Buy the 128GB model. If specs matter more than the Samsung badge, the Moto G64 is better value.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(104,245,20,'Tecno Spark 50 Review: A 7,000mAh Battery Under Rs 30,000','tecno-spark-50-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Tecno Spark 50 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Tecno Spark 50 costs <strong>Rs 29,999</strong> for 4GB/128GB and Rs 32,999 for 6GB/128GB. Its main selling point is a <strong>7,000mAh battery</strong>, the biggest in this price range, alongside a 6.78-inch 120Hz screen and Android 16 out of the box.</p>\n<h2>Battery first</h2>\n<p>For most people a 7,000mAh cell means a day and a half, and two days for light users. That helps during load-shedding, on long bus journeys or for anyone away from a socket all day. The downside is 18W charging: a full charge takes well over two hours.</p>\n<h2>The weak spot is the chip</h2>\n<p>The MediaTek Helio G81 is a 12nm chip, slower than the Helio G99 in the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> and well behind the Dimensity 7025 in the <a href=\"/products/moto-g64\">Moto G64</a>. Social apps and video are fine, but heavier games and quick app switching will stutter, more so on the 4GB model. The screen is HD+ and the phone is 4G only.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy it for the battery, not for speed. If you want both, the Moto G64 costs the same with a 6,000mAh battery and a much faster chip.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Tecno Spark 50</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.78-inch IPS LCD, HD+, 120Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>MediaTek Helio G81 (12nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main | 8MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>7000mAh; 18W</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>HiOS 16 on Android 16</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What seven thousand milliamp-hours means on a small budget</h2>\n<p>In many Nepali homes the phone is also the television, the torch, the radio and the hotspot. A battery that lasts a day and a half is not a luxury in that situation. The Spark 50 can play downloaded films on a bus from Kathmandu to Mahendranagar and still have charge left to call for a ride at the end. With light use it reaches two days.</p>\n<p>The price of that size is the charging speed. At 18W, going from empty to full takes close to three hours. If you charge overnight, you will not care. If you are used to a quick top-up before leaving, you will be disappointed, as half an hour adds perhaps twenty per cent.</p>\n<h2>The processor, without sugar coating</h2>\n<p>The Helio G81 is an older 12nm design. It runs the basics, but you wait a little: a second for the camera, a second for a web page to settle, a moment when switching from a call to a chat. On the 4GB model those waits grow as you install more apps. Tecno\'s virtual memory feature borrows storage space to help, and it is much slower than real memory, so think of 4GB plus 4GB as 4GB.</p>\n<p>For Rs 3,000 more, the 6GB version is the one we would buy. It does not make the chip faster, but it reduces the number of times apps have to restart. Games such as Free Fire work at low settings. PUBG Mobile is possible and not enjoyable.</p>\n<h2>Screen and sound</h2>\n<p>A 6.78-inch panel at 120Hz looks smooth while scrolling. The resolution is HD+, so small letters have soft edges. For watching video at 480p, which most people use to save data, it looks fine. Colours are average, black is grey, and outdoors you will look for shade. Speakers are loud, a genuine strength of Tecno phones.</p>\n<h2>Camera expectations</h2>\n<p>There is one useful camera, the 50MP main one. Daylight photos of people and places are good enough to share. Indoors they turn grainy, and at night the phone struggles. The 8MP front camera comes with many beauty filters. Switch them off for video calls if faces look like plastic.</p>\n<h2>HiOS and how to calm it down</h2>\n<p>HiOS 16 on Android 16 is current, and that is a point in its favour. It also includes a lot: its own app store, theme store, browser, games and suggestions. Spend ten minutes removing what you can. In the settings, turn off personalised recommendations and notifications from the app store, phone manager and theme apps. Keep Google Play as your only source of apps. Tecno\'s update policy for the Spark series is short, so expect security patches and perhaps one version upgrade.</p>\n<h2>Who sells it and who fixes it</h2>\n<p>Tecno, Infinix and itel belong to the same group, which has built a wide sales network in Nepal, including small towns. Carlcare is the group\'s service arm. Ask where the nearest service point is, and keep the bill. Screens and batteries for popular Spark models are usually easy to find.</p>\n<h2>Compared with what Rs 30,000 buys elsewhere</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Tecno Spark 50</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>MediaTek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>7000mAh; 18W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-design\">ZTE Nubia V80 Design</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>Unisoc T7280</td><td>Li-Ion 5000mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x6d\">Honor X6d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 6300 (6 nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5260 mAh, non-removable; 15W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-30\">Tecno Spark 30</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G91 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07s\">Samsung Galaxy A07s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;30,499</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99+ (6nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07s\">Galaxy A07s</a> has a better-known brand and longer support with a smaller battery. The <a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Moto G35 5G</a> adds 5G and a sharper screen. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x6d\">Honor X6d</a> has a more efficient chip and slower charging. If you can find a <a href=\"/products/moto-g64\">Moto G64</a> in old stock, it beats all of these for speed.</p>\n<h2>Three kinds of buyer</h2>\n<p><strong>The traveller or driver.</strong> Long days, few sockets, mostly calls and maps. The Spark 50 fits well.</p>\n<p><strong>The student.</strong> Online classes, notes, social media and some games. The battery helps, but the slow chip will frustrate within a year. Choose the 6GB version or look at a phone with a Helio G99 or a Dimensity chip.</p>\n<p><strong>The parent.</strong> A loud speaker, large screen and long battery are just right. Spend time on the setup to remove clutter and scam risks.</p>\n<h2>Getting the best from the battery</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use 60Hz mode if you do not notice the difference. It adds hours.</li>\n<li>Keep the screen at automatic brightness.</li>\n<li>Charge with the supplied adapter and avoid cheap cables that slow it further.</li>\n<li>Try not to keep it at 100 per cent on the charger all day.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Things we would like to see improved</h2>\n<p>A faster charger would change how this phone feels more than anything else. After that, a Full HD+ screen and a 6nm chip would put it level with rivals. None of those are possible after purchase, so decide whether you can live with them now.</p>\n<h2>Final advice</h2>\n<p>Buy the Spark 50 if your first question about any phone is how long the battery lasts. In that respect it beats everything near it. For any other priority, whether speed, screen, camera or long support, a different phone at the same price will serve you better. And at any price, avoid the 4GB model if you keep phones for more than two years.</p>\n<h2>Making a basic chip feel quicker</h2>\n<p>Three changes help most on a phone like this. Set the refresh rate to automatic so that scrolling is smooth and the battery still lasts. Reduce animation scales to half in developer options. And keep no more than about forty apps installed, removing the ones that came with the phone and were never opened. After that, the Spark 50 is comfortable for calls, chat, maps and video, which is what its battery was made for.</p>',7.0,'7,000mAh battery: easily a full day and more\n120Hz screen\nAndroid 16 out of the box\n128GB storage as standard','Slow 12nm Helio G81 chip\nOnly 18W charging\nHD+ screen and 4G only','A good pick if battery life matters more than anything else. For a better all-rounder at the same price, get the Moto G64.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(105,234,20,'Honor X6d Review: Newest Software, Ordinary Hardware','honor-x6d-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Honor X6d yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Honor X6d costs <strong>Rs 29,999</strong> for 4GB/128GB. It runs MagicOS 10 on Android 16, one of the newest software versions at this price, on a MediaTek Dimensity 6300, a 6nm chip that\'s a step up from the Unisoc and Helio G81 chips in cheaper phones.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The chip is efficient and fine for everyday use, 128GB is a sensible amount of storage, and the 50MP main camera takes decent daylight photos. At 186g it\'s comfortable to hold for a 6.75-inch phone.</p>\n<h2>What holds it back</h2>\n<p>For Rs 30,000 the rest is ordinary: an HD+ LCD with no high refresh rate listed, a 5,260mAh battery with slow 15W charging, and only 4GB of RAM. The <a href=\"/products/moto-g64\">Moto G64</a> costs the same and has 8GB of RAM, a Full HD+ 120Hz screen, a bigger battery and twice the charging speed. The <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50\">Tecno Spark 50</a> has a 7,000mAh battery at the same price.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>It\'s fine, but Rs 30,000 is too much for what it offers. If it drops below Rs 25,000 it becomes worth considering.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Honor X6d</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.75 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz, 450 nits (peak)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 6300 (6 nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8 + Auxiliary lens | 5MP, f/2.2 front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Po 5260 mAh, non-removable; 15W charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, MagicOS 10</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.3, A2DP, LE, aptX HD</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>186 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>167 x 77 x 7.9 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A phone that arrived at the wrong price</h2>\n<p>There is nothing wrong with the Honor X6d as an object. It is well assembled, light, up to date and efficient. The problem is simple arithmetic. At Rs 29,999 a buyer can have more memory, a sharper screen, faster charging or a bigger battery from someone else. A review has to judge a phone against what else your money could do, and that is why the score is low while the phone itself is perfectly usable.</p>\n<h2>What works</h2>\n<p>The Dimensity 6300 is a 6nm chip with 5G, and it is the best part. It opens everyday apps without long pauses, stays cool and gets good mileage from the battery. In a price band full of 12nm processors, that is a meaningful advantage. If you place it beside a <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50\">Tecno Spark 50</a> and open the same five apps, the Honor finishes first.</p>\n<p>It also runs MagicOS 10 on Android 16, the newest version you will see on a budget phone this year. Google services are all present. Honor\'s interface is tidy compared with others at this price, with fewer adverts. Useful touches include a sidebar for opening a second app in a small window and a simple mode for older users.</p>\n<p>At 186 grams it feels lighter than most big-battery rivals, and the 6.75-inch screen fills the front nicely.</p>\n<h2>What does not</h2>\n<p>Four gigabytes of memory in a phone sold in late 2026 is the main limit. Android itself, the Google services and the keyboard use most of it, so switching between WhatsApp, a browser and the camera means waiting for apps to reload. There is no 6GB version listed in Nepal.</p>\n<p>The LCD is HD+ with a peak of about 450 nits. Indoors that is fine. In the afternoon sun at a bus stop you will be shading it with your palm. Charging at 15W is the slowest on the market, taking well over two hours for a full charge, and it makes little sense when rivals offer 30W.</p>\n<p>The main 50MP camera is respectable in daylight, and the 5MP selfie camera is the weakest in the group. Video calls in a dim room will look rough.</p>\n<h2>Battery life</h2>\n<p>The numbers are average and the result is better than average, because the chip and the low-resolution screen use so little. A day and a half of calls, chat and video is realistic. It is the refilling that tests your patience.</p>\n<h2>5G: the argument in its favour</h2>\n<p>If you want a light 5G phone from a known brand at about Rs 30,000, the list is short. The X6d is on it, as is the <a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Moto G35 5G</a>. Whether 5G is worth paying for is another matter. Coverage in Nepal is limited and mobile data packs are small, so most owners will run on 4G for years.</p>\n<h2>How it compares</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Honor X6d</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 6300 (6 nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5260 mAh, non-removable; 15W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-design\">ZTE Nubia V80 Design</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>Unisoc T7280</td><td>Li-Ion 5000mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-30\">Tecno Spark 30</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G91 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07s\">Samsung Galaxy A07s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;30,499</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99+ (6nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Motorola Moto G35 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;30,699</td><td>Unisoc T760 (6nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The Motorola gives you a sharper, smoother screen and near-stock Android. The Tecno and ZTE models give you larger batteries. Samsung\'s <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07s\">Galaxy A07s</a> brings longer software support and a wider service network. A leftover <a href=\"/products/moto-g64\">Moto G64</a> has twice the memory. Each of them is a better answer to a specific need.</p>\n<h2>When it becomes a good buy</h2>\n<p>Phones in this class often fall in price within months, and shops run bundles. Here is a guide to when it makes sense:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Around Rs 29,999:</strong> skip it.</li>\n<li><strong>Around Rs 26,000:</strong> fair, if you value a light body and 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 24,000:</strong> a good phone for parents and light users.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Service and warranty</h2>\n<p>Honor is growing in Nepal, but its network remains smaller than those of Samsung, Xiaomi and vivo. Find out the nearest service point and the price of a screen before buying. Check the IMEI in MDMS, and insist on a sealed box with the distributor\'s warranty card.</p>\n<h2>If you already own one</h2>\n<p>You can make it noticeably nicer to live with. Uninstall apps you do not use, especially those that run in the background. Use the lite versions of Facebook and Messenger. Restart the phone once a week to clear memory. Keep at least 15GB of storage free. Turn the refresh rate to 90Hz if scrolling feels rough, or 60Hz to save power. Buy a longer charging cable and charge overnight, since the slow speed is then no bother and is even gentle on the battery.</p>\n<h2>Who might still choose it</h2>\n<p>Someone who wants a light, tidy phone for calls, messaging and occasional video, likes Honor\'s software and has found a discount. Someone who needs 5G support for an office SIM or a hotspot on a budget. For games, photos or a phone to keep for four years, look elsewhere.</p>\n<h2>The wider lesson</h2>\n<p>Budget phones have become harder to judge by their price alone. Two phones at Rs 30,000 can be separated by years in speed and comfort. When comparing, look at four numbers before anything else: memory, screen resolution, charging speed and the age of the chip\'s process. The X6d wins one of those four. Most rivals win two or three.</p>',6.8,'Android 16 with MagicOS 10\nEfficient 6nm Dimensity 6300\n128GB storage\nLight at 186g','HD+ screen\nOnly 4GB RAM\nSlow 15W charging\nMoto G64 is far better value','Not bad, just overpriced. The Moto G64 gives you more of everything for the same money.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(106,186,20,'vivo Y05 Review: Big Battery and a Bright Screen, Basic Everything Else','vivo-y05-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the vivo Y05 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The vivo Y05 costs <strong>Rs 24,999</strong> for 4GB/64GB and Rs 28,499 for 4GB/128GB. It has a 6,500mAh battery, a 6.74-inch 120Hz screen that reaches 1,200 nits in bright light, and an IR blaster for controlling your TV or AC.</p>\n<h2>Good for the basics</h2>\n<p>The battery will comfortably last a full day of calls, WhatsApp and YouTube, and the bright 120Hz screen is easier to read outdoors than most budget LCDs. It ships with OriginOS 6 on Android 16, which is new for this price.</p>\n<h2>Where it cuts corners</h2>\n<p>The Unisoc T7225 is an entry-level 12nm chip, so expect pauses when opening apps or switching between them. The 8MP rear camera is basic: fine for documents and daylight snaps, poor in low light. Charging is 15W and it\'s a 4G phone. The 64GB model fills up quickly.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> (Rs 25,499) has a faster Helio G99 chip and a 50MP camera. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro\">Redmi A7 Pro</a> is cheaper at Rs 21,499 with a similar chip.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Vivo Y05</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1200 nits (HBM)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Unisoc T7225 (12nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>64GB / 128GB eMMC 5.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 8MP, f/2.0, PDAF | 5MP, f/2.2 front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 6500 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, OriginOS 6</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.2, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>209 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>167.4 x 77.1 x 8.4 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What you notice first</h2>\n<p>Pick up the Y05 and two things stand out for a phone at about Rs 25,000. The screen is easy to see, and the body feels solid. A 6.74-inch panel with a 1,200-nit high-brightness mode is rare at this price. Walk outside and you can read a message without cupping your hand over it. The 120Hz refresh rate makes scrolling smoother than on most budget phones, even though the chip cannot always keep up.</p>\n<h2>The infrared blaster deserves its own paragraph</h2>\n<p>It sounds like a relic, yet an IR transmitter is useful in Nepali homes. The phone can act as a remote for a television, set-top box, fan or air conditioner. When the DishHome remote goes missing between the sofa cushions, you will be glad of it. Set it up through vivo\'s remote app, choose the brand and test a few codes until one works.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>At 6,500mAh, with a low-resolution screen and an undemanding chip, two days are possible for light users and a full day is certain for heavy ones. The downside is 15W charging. It needs about three hours from empty, so overnight charging is the sensible routine. vivo says its batteries are designed to keep most of their capacity for several years, and slow charging does no harm there.</p>\n<h2>Where the savings were made</h2>\n<p>The Unisoc T7225 is an entry-level chip on a 12nm process. Opening the camera takes a moment, the keyboard sometimes trails your typing, and a heavy Facebook feed stutters. With 4GB of memory, apps in the background are closed quickly. The storage is eMMC, which is slower than the UFS storage in better phones, and that shows when installing or updating apps.</p>\n<p>The main camera has 8 megapixels. In good light it takes pictures that look acceptable on a phone screen. It is fine for photographing a document, a notice or a bill. Evening photos of people are blurry and noisy. The 5MP front camera is for video calls only.</p>\n<h2>64GB or 128GB</h2>\n<p>The gap is about Rs 3,500. Take 128GB. With 64GB, after the system and a few large apps, you will have perhaps 35GB for everything else, and WhatsApp will claim most of that within a year. When a phone\'s storage is nearly full, it slows down, and on this chip you cannot afford that.</p>\n<h2>Software</h2>\n<p>OriginOS 6 on Android 16 is a welcome change from older vivo systems, with a cleaner design. There are preinstalled apps and a second app store. Remove what you do not need, and turn off suggestions in settings. vivo\'s update plan for the cheapest Y phones is limited, so regard the version you get as close to the one you will keep, with security patches for a time.</p>\n<h2>Who it is really for</h2>\n<p>We think of three people. The first is a parent or grandparent who wants a bright, loud phone with a battery that lasts. The second is a worker who needs a tough-ish phone for calls, WhatsApp groups, eSewa and YouTube at lunch. The third is a family that wants a spare phone and a universal remote in one.</p>\n<p>It is not for students who run many apps at once, not for gamers, and not for anyone who likes taking photos.</p>\n<h2>The competition</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Vivo Y05</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,974</td><td>Unisoc T7225 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 6500 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,999</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a77\">ZTE Nubia A77</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>Li-ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Chargin...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x5d\">Honor X5d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5260 mAh, non-removable; 15W charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> at Rs 25,499 is the main rival. It has a far faster chip, a 50MP camera and long software support, with a dimmer and slower screen and a smaller battery. For most people the Samsung is the better all-round phone. The <a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a> is close to the vivo in character. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro\">Redmi A7 Pro</a> is cheaper with an even larger display. The <a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">Realme C71</a> adds a bit of toughness.</p>\n<p>If you can reach Rs 30,000, the list of good choices grows quickly, as our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-30000-nepal\">guide to phones under Rs 30,000</a> shows. And if you must stay below Rs 20,000, see <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-20000-nepal\">phones under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>vivo has service centres in the main cities and a dealer network that reaches smaller towns. Parts for Y-series phones are common and cheap. This is a real advantage over lesser-known brands that offer slightly better numbers.</p>\n<h2>Living with a slow phone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Facebook Lite, Messenger Lite-style apps and YouTube in the browser when you can.</li>\n<li>Set WhatsApp to not save every photo and video automatically.</li>\n<li>Turn on extended memory, and do not expect miracles from it.</li>\n<li>Restart weekly, and update apps only over Wi-Fi at night.</li>\n<li>Choose 60Hz if animation looks uneven. A steady 60 is nicer than a jumpy 120.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Safety for first-time users</h2>\n<p>Many budget phones are somebody\'s first smartphone. Before handing it over, set a PIN, add the fingerprint, enable Find My Device, and turn off installation of apps from unknown sources. Explain that prize messages, parcel fees and job offers on Viber or WhatsApp are usually scams, and that no bank or wallet asks for an OTP.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Y05 chooses brightness and battery over speed and cameras. If those are your priorities, and especially if an infrared remote appeals to you, the 128GB version is reasonable. Everybody else should put Rs 25,499 on the counter for the Galaxy A07, or wait until they can spend a little more.</p>',6.7,'6,500mAh battery\nBright 120Hz screen (1,200 nits)\nAndroid 16 out of the box\nIR blaster','Entry-level Unisoc chip\n8MP camera\n15W charging, 4G only\n64GB base storage','A good-enough phone for calls, social media and video. If you want a better camera and a faster chip for similar money, get the Galaxy A07.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(107,181,20,'Redmi A7 Pro Review: A Big-Screen Basic Phone for Rs 21,499','redmi-a7-pro-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Redmi A7 Pro yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Redmi A7 Pro costs <strong>Rs 21,499</strong> for 4GB/64GB and Rs 23,999 for 4GB/128GB. It\'s built to be a simple, dependable phone: a huge 6.9-inch 120Hz screen, a 6,000mAh battery and HyperOS 3 on Android 15.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<p>Parents, students getting their first phone, or anyone who needs a second phone for calls, WhatsApp, eSewa/Khalti and YouTube. The large screen makes text and video easy to see, and the battery lasts a full day of that kind of use.</p>\n<h2>Limits</h2>\n<p>The Unisoc T7250 (12nm) is an entry-level chip. Everyday apps run, but not quickly, and gaming beyond simple titles isn\'t realistic. The single 13MP camera is fine in daylight only. Charging is a slow 15W, and it\'s 4G only. Pay the extra Rs 2,500 for 128GB.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/realme-note-80\">Realme Note 80</a> (Rs 21,999) uses the same chip with a slightly bigger battery and a tougher, MIL-STD-rated body, but only 64GB. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> costs about Rs 4,000 more and is noticeably faster.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi A7 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.9-inch IPS LCD, HD+, 120Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>64GB / 128GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>13MP | 8MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>6000mAh; 15W</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>HyperOS 3 on Android 15</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The biggest screen for the least money</h2>\n<p>At 6.9 inches, the Redmi A7 Pro has a display as large as phones costing ten times as much. That is the whole idea. Text can be enlarged without every message spilling off the page, YouTube is comfortable from arm\'s length, and video calls show faces at a decent size. For older eyes this is a better feature than any processor.</p>\n<p>It also makes the phone wide. Someone with small hands will need both of them to type, and it will stick out of a trouser pocket. Try it in person if the owner is used to a compact phone.</p>\n<p>The resolution is HD+, spread over all those inches, so letters look soft when you hold it close. At normal viewing distance with large text, it is acceptable. The 120Hz setting gives smoother movement, though the chip often cannot deliver 120 frames, and locking it at 60Hz saves power with little loss.</p>\n<h2>Everyday speed</h2>\n<p>The Unisoc T7250 and 4GB of memory handle one thing at a time. WhatsApp, calls, a wallet payment, a YouTube video: each works. Doing several together shows the limits. The phone takes a moment to open the camera, and returning to an app often means waiting for it to load again. It is usable with patience. It is not suitable for someone who is used to a mid-range phone.</p>\n<h2>Storage is the choice that matters</h2>\n<p>Pay the additional Rs 2,500 for 128GB. The cheaper version\'s 64GB will fill with system files, apps and WhatsApp media, and a full phone becomes slow. A memory card, if your unit has a free slot, can hold photos and music.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>The 6,000mAh battery runs for a day and a half of typical use, with two days possible if the phone is mostly for calls. Charging is 15W. Leave it plugged in overnight. The charger may or may not be in the box depending on the batch, so ask.</p>\n<h2>Camera</h2>\n<p>A single 13MP camera is honest in one respect: there is no fake second lens. In daylight it records what you point it at. Indoors and at night it is poor. The 8MP front camera is actually better suited to its job, which is video calls.</p>\n<h2>Software and safety</h2>\n<p>HyperOS 3 on Android 15 looks like other Redmi phones, so anyone who has used one will feel at home. Remove the extra apps and turn off recommendations. Update support on the A series is short compared with Samsung, so this is a phone for two to three years and not for five.</p>\n<p>If you are giving it to a first-time user, set up a screen lock and Find My Device, disable unknown app sources, and install the official eSewa, Khalti or banking apps yourself from the Play Store. Fake wallet apps circulate in chat groups.</p>\n<h2>What else is near Rs 22,000?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Redmi A7 Pro</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,499</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>6000mAh; 15W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Unisoc T615 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 10000 mAh, non-removable; 18W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-80\">Realme Note 80</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6300 mAh, non-removable; 15W VOOC Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-play10\">Honor Play 10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;20,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 10W Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/realme-note-80\">Realme Note 80</a> uses the same chip, has a slightly larger battery and a tougher body, and comes with 64GB only. The <a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-20\">Infinix Smart 20</a> and <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-3\">Tecno Spark Go 3</a> compete closely. All of them are slow in a similar way, so choose by storage, service and screen.</p>\n<p>The real alternative costs about Rs 4,000 more. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> with its Helio G99 feels like a different class, and it receives updates for much longer. If there is any way to stretch, stretch.</p>\n<h2>Four situations where it makes sense</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A grandparent</strong> who wants to see family on a large screen.</li>\n<li><strong>A shop counter</strong> that needs a phone for QR payments, calls and a customer WhatsApp number.</li>\n<li><strong>A driver</strong> who uses it as a map and call phone on a mount.</li>\n<li><strong>A child\'s first phone</strong> with parental controls, where loss or breakage is likely.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Four situations where it does not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Online classes with many tabs and documents.</li>\n<li>Games beyond Ludo, carrom and simple puzzles.</li>\n<li>Taking photos at weddings or on trips.</li>\n<li>Replacing a three-year-old mid-range phone. You would be going backwards.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New or used?</h2>\n<p>For Rs 22,000 to 24,000, the used market offers phones that were far better when new: a Galaxy A52 or A53, a Redmi Note 11 Pro, a Poco X4. They have AMOLED screens and quicker chips. Against that, you get a worn battery, no warranty and a shorter future for updates. For a confident buyer who can check the phone and the MDMS registration, used can be the better deal. For a parent or a first-timer, new with a warranty is the safer choice.</p>\n<h2>Making it last</h2>\n<p>Use a case with raised corners, since a 6.9-inch screen is a large target. Keep 20GB of storage free. Update apps on Wi-Fi. Restart it every few days. And when the battery begins to fade after a couple of years, a replacement is inexpensive because Redmi parts are everywhere.</p>\n<h2>A note on the price of cheap phones</h2>\n<p>Memory has become more expensive through 2026, and entry-level phones feel it most, as our article on <a href=\"/news/electronic-price-hikes-nepal-ram-ssd-smartphones-analysis\">memory prices</a> explains. Two years ago Rs 22,000 bought 6GB and a faster chip. Today it buys 4GB. That is not the fault of this phone, but it does mean that waiting for a sale, or spending a little more once, pays off better than usual.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Redmi A7 Pro does one thing well, which is to put a very large screen and a long battery into ordinary hands for little money. Buy the 128GB version for someone whose phone life is calls, chats, payments and video. For everybody else it is a false economy.</p>',6.6,'Huge 6.9-inch 120Hz screen\n6,000mAh battery\nAffordable 128GB option\nSimple, familiar HyperOS','Slow Unisoc T7250\nBasic 13MP camera\n15W charging, 4G only','A sensible basic phone at Rs 23,999 for 128GB. Anyone who games or takes lots of photos should save for the Galaxy A07 or Moto G64.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(108,211,20,'Realme Note 80 Review: Tough and Long-Lasting, Not Fast','realme-note-80-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Realme Note 80 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Realme Note 80 costs <strong>Rs 21,999</strong> for 4GB/64GB, the only version sold here. It has a 6,300mAh battery in a 7.94mm body, a MIL-STD-810H durability rating, and can charge other devices at 6W over a cable.</p>\n<h2>Strengths</h2>\n<p>For a phone that may be dropped, carried on a bike or used outdoors all day, the toughness rating and big battery count for a lot. Reverse charging can top up earbuds or a friend\'s phone in a pinch.</p>\n<h2>Weaknesses</h2>\n<p>The chip is the same entry-level Unisoc T7250 as the <a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro\">Redmi A7 Pro</a>, the screen is HD+ at 90Hz, and the cameras are 8MP rear and 5MP front: fine for video calls, poor for photos. The biggest problem is <strong>64GB with no larger option</strong>. After the system and a few apps, there\'s little room for photos and videos.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy it as a rugged backup phone or for someone who mostly calls and chats. For a main phone, the Redmi A7 Pro with 128GB (Rs 23,999) is the better buy.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Realme Note 80</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 450 nits (typ)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>64GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP, f/2.0, AF | 5MP, f/2.2 front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Po 6300 mAh, non-removable; 15W VOOC Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, realme UI 5.0</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / WCDMA / LTE, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.2, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>197 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>167.2 x 76.6 x 7.94 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The tough-phone idea at Rs 21,999</h2>\n<p>Realme has noticed that many buyers at this price break their phones before they outgrow them. The Note 80 answers with a body tested to a military standard for shock and temperature. MIL-STD-810H is a set of laboratory procedures, and makers choose which of them to run, so it is not a guarantee that a fall onto a gravel road will leave no mark. Still, the corners are reinforced and the frame is stiff, and the result is a phone that should cope better than most with being dropped, sat on or rattled in a toolbox.</p>\n<p>It manages that at under 8mm thick and under 200 grams, with a 6,300mAh battery inside. Rugged phones usually look like bricks, and this one does not.</p>\n<h2>Battery and power sharing</h2>\n<p>Expect a day and a half to two days. The 15W charging is slow, as it is on every phone at this level. Reverse charging at 6W is slow too, but it can bring a dead feature phone or a pair of earbuds back to life, and that has value on a long day out.</p>\n<h2>A correction about the camera</h2>\n<p>Specifications for this phone differ between sources. Our own specification sheet lists a 50MP main camera, while some listings, including the summary above, mention 8MP. Ask the shop to open the camera settings and check the maximum photo resolution before you buy. Either way, expect modest results: usable pictures in daylight and weak ones after sunset. The 5MP front camera is for calls.</p>\n<h2>64GB and nothing else</h2>\n<p>This is the weakness that decides the review. Only one version is sold in Nepal, with 4GB of memory and 64GB of storage. The system and preinstalled apps take around 20GB. Add a wallet, banking, Facebook, TikTok and WhatsApp with a year of media, and you will be deleting things. A memory card can take photos and videos, so buy one on the first day and set the camera to save to it. WhatsApp will still use the internal storage.</p>\n<h2>Performance and screen</h2>\n<p>The Unisoc T7250 is the same entry-level chip as in the <a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro\">Redmi A7 Pro</a>. One app at a time is fine. Multitasking is slow. The 6.74-inch HD+ LCD runs at 90Hz and about 450 nits, so it is readable indoors and difficult in direct sun. That is a pity on a phone meant for outdoor use.</p>\n<h2>Who should consider it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Construction and farm workers</strong> who need a phone that can be dropped.</li>\n<li><strong>Trekking porters and drivers</strong> who want a long battery.</li>\n<li><strong>Anyone who needs a backup phone</strong> for a second SIM, hotspot or emergencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Parents of a teenager</strong> who has already broken two phones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should avoid it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone for whom this will be the only phone and who likes photos and videos.</li>\n<li>Students and gamers.</li>\n<li>People who keep phones for four years.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Alternatives</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Realme Note 80</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6300 mAh, non-removable; 15W VOOC Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Unisoc T615 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 10000 mAh, non-removable; 18W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-3\">Tecno Spark Go 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro-4g\">Redmi A7 Pro 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,499</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The Redmi A7 Pro with 128GB costs Rs 2,000 more and solves the storage problem with a bigger screen, though without the reinforced body. The <a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a> is a true rugged phone at the same price, with a thicker and heavier body. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x5c-plus\">Honor X5c Plus</a> and <a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-20\">Infinix Smart 20</a> are ordinary phones with their own small advantages. If toughness is the priority and your budget can grow, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> shows what is possible at more than twice the price: high-grade water protection, a bright AMOLED and a large battery.</p>\n<h2>Do you need a tough phone or a good case?</h2>\n<p>It is worth asking. A Rs 500 silicone case with raised lips and a Rs 300 tempered glass protect an ordinary phone from most falls. That leaves you free to buy the faster Galaxy A07. What a case cannot do is protect against bending, dust in the ports or extreme heat. If your phone lives in a back pocket on a construction site, the stronger body is worth having. If it lives on a desk, it is not.</p>\n<h2>Software</h2>\n<p>Realme UI 5 on Android 15 is easy to use. There are a number of preinstalled apps and suggestions to clean up, and a simple mode for older users. Do not expect more than one version upgrade on a phone at this price. Security updates tend to come every few months.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Realme shares much of its service arrangement with its sister brands, and centres exist in major cities. Screens and batteries for C and Note series phones are affordable. As always, ask for a VAT bill, the warranty card and MDMS registration, even on a cheap phone. Networks treat an unregistered Rs 20,000 phone the same as an unregistered flagship.</p>\n<h2>How to make 64GB bearable</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Insert a 128GB memory card and move the camera roll there.</li>\n<li>In WhatsApp, turn off automatic media download and clear large files monthly.</li>\n<li>Use Google Photos backup on Wi-Fi and free up space afterwards.</li>\n<li>Choose lite apps and uninstall what you do not use.</li>\n<li>Check that at least 10GB stays free, or updates will fail.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>As a second phone or a work phone, the Note 80 has a clear purpose: it lasts long and takes knocks. As a main phone, its storage holds it back so much that we cannot recommend it over cheaper-looking rivals with twice the space. If Realme brings a 128GB version at a small premium, the advice changes.</p>',6.5,'6,300mAh battery in a slim 7.94mm body\nMIL-STD-810H durability\n6W reverse charging','64GB only\n8MP camera\nEntry-level chip, HD+ 90Hz screen\n15W charging','A decent rugged second phone, but the 64GB limit makes it hard to recommend as anyone\'s main phone.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(109,957,20,'Poco F7 Review: Flagship-Class Speed for Rs 69,999','poco-f7-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Poco F7 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Poco F7 costs <strong>Rs 69,999</strong> in Nepal, and that gets you 12GB of RAM and 512GB of storage with no cheaper, smaller model. It\'s the fastest phone you can buy under Rs 80,000.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 is a near-flagship 4nm chip, paired with fast LPDDR5X memory and UFS 4.1 storage. PUBG Mobile, Free Fire and Genshin Impact run at high settings, and the phone will still feel quick in three or four years. Xiaomi promises four Android updates and six years of security patches.</p>\n<h2>Build, screen and battery</h2>\n<p>You get Gorilla Glass 7i on both sides, an aluminium frame and <strong>IP68</strong>, which is uncommon at this price. The 6.83-inch 120Hz AMOLED reaches 3,200 nits for HDR. The 6,500mAh silicon-carbon battery charges at 90W, and it has Wi-Fi 7.</p>\n<h2>The trade-off: cameras</h2>\n<p>The 50MP Sony IMX882 main camera with OIS is good, but the 8MP ultra wide is weak and there\'s no telephoto. If photos matter more than speed, the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone (4a)</a> (Rs 69,999) has a periscope zoom. If battery life matters most, look at the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> (Rs 69,999, 10,000mAh).</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Poco F7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 1700 nits (HBM), 3200 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB UFS 4.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.5, 26 mm, OIS, PDAF, 1/1.95&quot; Sony IMX882 sensor + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, 15 mm | 20MP, f/2.2, 1/4&quot; sensor front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, HyperOS 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / WCDMA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 7, Dual-band, Bluetooth 6.0, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>215.7 gm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What near-flagship means in numbers you can feel</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 borrows its design from Qualcomm\'s top tier and trims the peaks. In practice, apps open as quickly as on phones that cost twice as much. The gap appears in the heaviest games and in the final seconds of a video export. For everyone else the limiting factor is the network.</p>\n<p>Just as important are the parts around it. LPDDR5X memory and UFS 4.1 storage mean that installing a large game, scrolling a gallery of ten thousand photos or editing a clip happens without a pause. And with 12GB and 512GB as the only version, there is no trap model with too little of either.</p>\n<h2>Gaming for an hour, and not for five minutes</h2>\n<p>Benchmarks measure a burst. Gamers care about minute forty. A large phone with a sizeable vapour chamber holds its speed well, and Xiaomi\'s Game Turbo settings let you choose between cooler and faster. In a room at 30 degrees, expect the back to become hot and frame rates to settle a little lower after half an hour. Remove the case, lower the brightness and do not charge while you play.</p>\n<p>PUBG Mobile, Mobile Legends and Call of Duty Mobile can run at high frame-rate modes where the game allows. Genshin Impact and Wuthering Waves work at high settings with a steady frame rate if you drop one or two effects.</p>\n<h2>Screen and build</h2>\n<p>The 6.83-inch AMOLED is bright, smooth and sharp at 1.5K. HDR video reaches a peak of 3,200 nits. Glass on both sides with an aluminium frame and IP68 protection make it feel like a more expensive phone. At 216 grams it is solid and heavy. If you play lying down, you will feel it.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>A 6,500mAh silicon-carbon battery is large for a fast phone. A day of heavy use is safe, and light users will see nearly two. At 90W it goes from low to nearly full over lunch. Check that the adapter is included, since bundles vary between shops.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: know what you are getting</h2>\n<p>The 50MP Sony IMX882 is a respected sensor, and with stabilisation it takes crisp daylight photos and decent night shots. The 8MP ultra wide is weak. There is no telephoto. Xiaomi\'s processing is punchy, with a portrait mode that works best at 2x from a couple of metres. If you post a lot of photos of people, the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> or the <a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a> will please you more.</p>\n<p>Video recording goes to 4K at 60 frames per second with fair stabilisation. The 20MP front camera is good for calls and reels.</p>\n<h2>Software support has improved</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi promises four Android upgrades and six years of security patches for this phone. It launches with Android 15, so it should reach Android 19, and patches run into the next decade. That is a serious commitment at this price, and it makes the fast chip worth having, because the phone can still be useful when those updates arrive.</p>\n<p>HyperOS has many features and some noise. After setup, uninstall the bundled apps and games, switch off recommendations in the system apps, choose Google\'s keyboard and messages if you prefer them, and allow your chat apps to run in the background.</p>\n<h2>Weak points worth repeating</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It is big and heavy.</li>\n<li>The second camera is there for the specification sheet.</li>\n<li>No wireless charging.</li>\n<li>Poco\'s resale value is lower than that of Samsung or Apple.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Against others at Rs 70,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Poco F7</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W reverse...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone (4a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>5,080mAh; 50W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;67,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Honor SuperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-15-pro\">Realme 15 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;66,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W wired</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro\">POCO X8 Pro</a> is a newer design at the same price, so compare chips and prices on the day. The <a href=\"/products/realme-15-pro\">Realme 15 Pro</a> is slimmer with a camera focus. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> is the endurance champion. If you can wait or spend more, the <a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">POCO F8 Pro</a> at around Rs 1 lakh moves up to a flagship chip and a better camera set.</p>\n<h2>F7 or X7 Pro?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">X7 Pro</a> saves Rs 10,000. You lose the metal frame, some speed, Wi-Fi 7 and two years of security updates, and you keep 12GB, 512GB, IP68 and 90W charging. For a student whose limit is Rs 60,000 it is the smart choice. For someone who will keep the phone for five years, the F7\'s longer support is worth the difference.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy the F7</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mobile gamers who want high settings without a flagship price.</li>\n<li>People who keep phones for a long time and hate lag.</li>\n<li>Students and creators who need lots of storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Photographers who use zoom and wide lenses.</li>\n<li>Anyone who wants a small or light phone.</li>\n<li>Buyers who value resale above everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying tips for Nepal</h2>\n<p>Buy the official unit. Indian and Chinese versions of Poco and Redmi phones have different names, batteries and software, and the Indian F7 has a bigger battery with a different warranty. Check that the model number on the box matches the phone, register the IMEI through MDMS and keep the VAT bill. Service is handled by Xiaomi\'s authorised centres, which is one of the reasons to prefer Poco over similarly fast phones from smaller brands.</p>\n<h2>One last thought</h2>\n<p>It is easy to be blinded by speed. If you do not play games, edit video or keep a phone beyond three years, you will not use what this chip can do, and one of the other phones in the table will make you happier. If you do any of those things, the F7 is the best use of Rs 70,000 in Nepal right now.</p>',8.6,'Snapdragon 8s Gen 4: fastest under Rs 80,000\n12GB/512GB as standard\nIP68, metal frame, glass on both sides\n6,500mAh with 90W, Wi-Fi 7\n4 OS updates, 6 years of security','Weak 8MP ultra wide, no telephoto\nHeavy at 216g\nShips with preinstalled apps to remove','The phone to buy around Rs 70,000 if you game or want a phone that stays fast for years. Photographers should look at the Nothing Phone (4a).',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(110,956,20,'Poco X7 Pro Review: The Budget Gaming Pick at Rs 59,999','poco-x7-pro-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Poco X7 Pro yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Poco X7 Pro costs <strong>Rs 59,999</strong> for 12GB/512GB. Its MediaTek Dimensity 8400 Ultra is the fastest chip you can get at Rs 60,000. It\'s well ahead of the Snapdragon 6-series chips in Redmi Note, OPPO Reno F and Samsung A phones at this price.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>Games run smoothly at high settings, and 512GB of UFS 4.0 storage means you won\'t run out of space. The 6.67-inch 1.5K AMOLED is sharp and bright (3,200 nits peak). It has a 6,000mAh silicon-carbon battery with 90W charging and an IP68 rating, and the 50MP main camera with OIS is the same Sony IMX882 sensor used in the Poco F7.</p>\n<h2>What you give up</h2>\n<p>The frame and back are plastic (or faux leather), it has Wi-Fi 6 rather than 7, and software support is shorter: three Android updates and four years of security patches. The 8MP ultra wide is weak. It\'s also a phone from early 2025, so it isn\'t the newest model on the shelf.</p>\n<h2>X7 Pro or F7?</h2>\n<p>For Rs 10,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a> has a faster chip, an aluminium frame, Wi-Fi 7 and an extra year of updates. If Rs 60,000 is your limit, the X7 Pro is the faster choice than the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro</a> (Rs 57,999), which in turn has the bigger battery and IP69K. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=poco-x7-pro&g2=redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Compare them side by side</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Poco X7 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.67 inches CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 1400 nits (HBM), 3200 nits (peak)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8400 Ultra (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB UFS 4.0</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.5, 26 mm, OIS, PDAF, 1/1.95&quot; Sony IMX882 sensor + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, 15 mm | 20MP, f/2.2, 21 mm, 1/4.0&quot; sensor front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 90W HyperCharge</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, HyperOS 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / WCDMA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 6, Dual-band, Bluetooth 6.0, LE, HID</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>195 gm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why an older phone can still win</h2>\n<p>The X7 Pro came out in early 2025, and in phone years that is old. It remains on this list because chips at this price have barely moved. Memory became expensive, so manufacturers kept mid-range phones on mid-range processors. The Dimensity 8400 Ultra is a class above them. It uses eight large cores, with no small ones, and this shows in games and in heavy multitasking.</p>\n<p>So the choice at Rs 60,000 is between a new phone with a modest chip and a slightly older one with a fast chip. For players, the second is better. For people who want the latest design or the longest support, the first may be.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>Expect high settings and steady frame rates in PUBG Mobile, Mobile Legends and Free Fire, with high frame-rate modes available where supported. Genshin Impact runs well at medium to high. The 1.5K screen adds sharpness, and touch response is quick. The plastic body is a small advantage here, as it does not feel as hot as metal in the hand, though the chip inside will slow a little in a warm room.</p>\n<h2>Storage you will not fill quickly</h2>\n<p>Having 512GB of UFS 4.0 in a Rs 60,000 phone is remarkable when some rivals start at 128GB. You can keep several large games, a year of 4K video and offline films without thinking. There is no memory card slot, and with this much space you will not miss it.</p>\n<h2>Display and design</h2>\n<p>The 6.67-inch AMOLED is smaller than the near-7-inch panels that are now common, which makes the phone easier to handle at 195 grams. Brightness is high enough for outdoors. The flat sides and the choice of plastic or vegan leather backs give it some character, and the leather version hides fingerprints.</p>\n<p>IP68 protection on a plastic phone at this price was unusual at launch and remains so. Rain, dust and an accidental dip in fresh water should not be a problem while the seals are intact.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>The 6,000mAh battery gives a comfortable day for gamers and more for others. At 90W, a short stop refills most of it. Rivals with 8,000mAh or more last longer. Compare the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro</a> at Rs 57,999 if endurance matters more than frames.</p>\n<h2>Camera</h2>\n<p>You get the same 50MP Sony main sensor as the F7, with stabilisation. Daylight photos are sharp, with contrast set a little high, and night shots are fine. The 8MP ultra wide is below average, and no zoom lens exists. The 20MP front camera does its job. In short, one good camera.</p>\n<h2>Software and support</h2>\n<p>The phone launched with Android 15 and HyperOS 2. Three Android versions and four years of security updates were promised from early 2025, so about two years of versions and two and a half years of patches remain today. That is fine for someone who upgrades every two or three years and less good for long-term owners. Factor it into the price: a phone with less support left should cost less.</p>\n<h2>Compared with newer phones</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Poco X7 Pro</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 90W HyperCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8350 Apex (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 7100 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-8\">Tecno Pova 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-m8-pro\">POCO M8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;58,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-C 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperCharge; 22...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-11-pro\">OPPO Reno 11 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;62,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 8200</td><td>4600mAh, 80W SUPERVOOC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-m8-pro\">POCO M8 Pro</a> and <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE 5</a> are more recent, with larger batteries and slower chips. The <a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-8\">Tecno Pova 8</a> aims at gamers with bold design. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">Galaxy A27 5G</a> is the long-support choice and cannot match it for speed. For Rs 10,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> is the natural upgrade.</p>\n<h2>Is it risky to buy a phone near the end of its run?</h2>\n<p>Two practical checks remove most of the risk. Look at the manufacturing date on the box: batteries age on the shelf, so prefer a unit made within the past year. And ask for the full official warranty from the date of purchase, not from the date the shop received it. Then use it heavily for the first week, while exchange is easy.</p>\n<h2>Who will enjoy it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Gamers with a firm Rs 60,000 limit.</li>\n<li>People who install everything and delete nothing.</li>\n<li>Anyone who prefers a phone under 6.7 inches with a fast chip.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who will not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Owners who keep phones for five years.</li>\n<li>Photographers.</li>\n<li>Buyers who want premium materials.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Settings worth changing</h2>\n<p>Choose 120Hz for all apps. Turn off system app recommendations. In the battery menu, allow WhatsApp, Viber and your bank to run without restriction. Use the Game Turbo panel to block notifications while playing. If the phone warms up in summer, limit games to 60 frames per second. You will hardly notice, and the battery will thank you.</p>\n<h2>What about a used F6 or F5?</h2>\n<p>Second-hand Poco F phones are tempting at lower prices. They are fast, but their batteries have aged and their support is close to its end. If you find one with a healthy battery for half the price of the X7 Pro, it can make sense as a gaming device. As a main phone, the newer one with a warranty is the better bet.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Judged purely on what it does per rupee, the X7 Pro embarrasses most phones launched after it. Its age shows only in the remaining update years and in the plastic body. If you want speed and space for Rs 60,000, buy it while official stock lasts. If you plan further ahead, find Rs 10,000 more for the F7.</p>',8.3,'Dimensity 8400 Ultra: fastest at Rs 60,000\n12GB/512GB as standard\n6,000mAh with 90W charging\nIP68 and a sharp 1.5K AMOLED','Plastic frame\nShorter updates (3 OS, 4 years security)\nWeak 8MP ultra wide\nOlder than its rivals','The best phone for gaming at Rs 60,000. If you can stretch by Rs 10,000, the Poco F7 is the better long-term buy.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(111,103,20,'Redmi Note 17 Pro Review: Most of the Pro Max for Rs 12,000 Less','redmi-note-17-pro-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G costs <strong>Rs 57,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 64,999 for 12GB/256GB. It keeps the best parts of the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Note 17 Pro Max</a>: the same 6.83-inch 1.5K AMOLED, IP68 and IP69K protection, and a still-huge battery.</p>\n<h2>Battery and durability</h2>\n<p>The 8,340mAh battery should last most people well into a second day, and 67W charging refills it in reasonable time. It can also charge other devices at 22.5W over a cable. IP69K means it can survive high-pressure water jets as well as rain and dust. That\'s rare protection at this price.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 1.5K AMOLED reaches 3,500 nits and uses 3840Hz PWM dimming, which helps people who get eye strain from OLED screens at low brightness.</p>\n<h2>What\'s cut from the Pro Max</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 is a mid-range chip: fine for everything except long, heavy gaming sessions. Charging drops from 100W to 67W. The cameras are a 50MP main with OIS and an 8MP ultra wide.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>For gaming, the <a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">Poco X7 Pro</a> (Rs 59,999) has a much faster chip and 512GB, but a smaller 6,000mAh battery. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> (Rs 54,999) is cheaper with a similar focus on battery and toughness.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K AMOLED (2772×1280), 120Hz, 3,500 nits peak, 3840Hz PWM dimming</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main (OIS) + 8MP ultra wide | 16MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>8,340mAh; 67W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, HyperOS 3</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The Goldilocks model</h2>\n<p>The Redmi Note 17 family has three rungs. The plain Note 17 has a huge screen and an entry-level chip. The Pro Max has a 10,000mAh battery and a price near Rs 70,000. The Pro in the middle keeps what matters from the top: the same AMOLED, the same protection against water and dust, and a battery that few people will empty in a day. It is the one we would hand to a friend who says, just give me something that works.</p>\n<h2>Battery reality</h2>\n<p>With 8,340mAh, the arithmetic is friendly. Moderate users finish the first day above half. Heavy users reach bedtime with a margin. Charging at 67W is not class-leading, yet a battery this large needs filling less often. The 22.5W reverse charging is useful for earbuds, a watch puck or a friend.</p>\n<p>Use the charge limit setting. If you stop at eighty-five per cent on normal days, you still have more capacity than most phones have at full, and the battery will age more slowly.</p>\n<h2>A screen that is easy on the eyes</h2>\n<p>The 1.5K panel is sharp without wasting power. High-frequency dimming at 3,840Hz reduces the invisible flicker that gives some people headaches on AMOLED phones. If you have ever felt tired after reading on a phone at night, that is worth testing in the shop: lower the brightness to minimum and look at a white page for a minute.</p>\n<p>The peak of 3,500 nits applies to HDR highlights, and everyday outdoor visibility is very good.</p>\n<h2>Durability at an honest price</h2>\n<p>IP68 and IP69K ratings used to belong to flagships and rugged bricks. Here they come on a phone at Rs 57,999. They mean rain, dust, mud and a rinse are no concern. Add a case for drops, because water seals do nothing for glass.</p>\n<h2>The chip is fine, and it helps to know what fine means</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 is designed for efficiency. Scrolling, banking, video calls, navigation and photo editing are smooth. Games run at medium. A long session of PUBG is comfortable, and Genshin is a compromise. Compared with the Dimensity 8400 Ultra in the <a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">POCO X7 Pro</a>, it is about half as fast in heavy work. Most owners will never notice. Gamers will notice on the first evening.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>A 50MP main camera with optical stabilisation handles most scenes. Night photos of streets and indoor family pictures are dependable. The 8MP ultra wide is for daylight. There is no telephoto and the crop at 2x is usable. The 16MP front camera is adequate for calls and less flattering than the Pro Max\'s 32MP unit. Users who take a lot of selfies should take that into account.</p>\n<h2>8GB or 12GB</h2>\n<p>The 12GB version costs Rs 7,000 more with the same storage. On this chip, 8GB is the better value. Spend the difference on a genuine fast charger if yours is not included, or on screen insurance.</p>\n<h2>Where it sits among rivals</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;57,999</td><td>Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8,340mAh; 67W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-m8-pro\">POCO M8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;58,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-C 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperCharge; 22...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6s-pro\">OPPO A6s Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;56,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Max (6 nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh; 80W wired; 5W reverse wired...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A27 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;55,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8350 Apex (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 7100 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Fast...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> is cheaper by Rs 3,000, about as tough and slightly thinner, with a smaller service network. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">Galaxy A27 5G</a> has longer support and half the battery. The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">Nothing Phone 3a Lite</a> has cleaner software. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE 5</a> is faster, with a large battery and lower protection. If battery and toughness top your list, the Redmi and the Honor are the two to compare. Buy whichever has service near you.</p>\n<h2>Updates and service</h2>\n<p>Ask the seller for the exact number of Android versions and years of security patches for this model, as Xiaomi\'s policy differs across its ranges. Service is one of the stronger arguments for Redmi: centres in several cities, a wide choice of parts and technicians who know the phones. Out-of-warranty repairs on Note phones cost less than on Samsung\'s A5x series.</p>\n<h2>First-day checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update to the latest HyperOS build before installing apps.</li>\n<li>Remove bundled apps and turn off recommendations.</li>\n<li>Turn on anti-flicker or DC dimming if your eyes are sensitive.</li>\n<li>Set charge protection.</li>\n<li>Allow chat and banking apps to run in the background.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who want one phone for work, travel and family, without fuss.</li>\n<li>Anyone who spends days outdoors.</li>\n<li>Readers and viewers with sensitive eyes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Gamers, who should take the POCO X7 Pro.</li>\n<li>Zoom and selfie enthusiasts.</li>\n<li>People who prefer small or light phones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Common questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Is it better than the Note 17 Pro Max?</strong> Not better, but better value for most. The Max adds battery, charging speed and a sharper selfie camera for Rs 12,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I use it with a power bank to charge faster?</strong> Full speed needs Xiaomi\'s own charging system. With USB PD it charges more slowly.</p>\n<p><strong>Is it good for elderly parents?</strong> The battery and screen are excellent for them. Clean up the software first, and consider a lighter phone if they have weak hands.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>There is always a phone in the catalogue that does everything people actually complain about: battery, breakage, sunlight, rain. This year it is the Redmi Note 17 Pro. It will not win arguments about processors or cameras, and its owners will be too busy not charging it to care.</p>',8.2,'8,340mAh battery: easily a full day and more\nIP68 and IP69K\n3,500-nit 1.5K AMOLED with 3840Hz PWM dimming\n22.5W reverse charging','Mid-range chip for heavy gaming\nNo telephoto; 8MP ultra wide\nPoco X7 Pro is faster for Rs 2,000 more','The best Redmi for most people: big battery, tough build and a great screen. Gamers should get the Poco X7 Pro instead.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(112,184,20,'Redmi Note 17 5G Review: Huge Screen and Battery, Entry-Level Chip','redmi-note-17-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Redmi Note 17 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Redmi Note 17 5G costs <strong>Rs 47,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 52,999 for 12GB/512GB. It has a very large 6.99-inch 120Hz AMOLED and a 7,700mAh battery with 45W charging. It\'s a good phone for watching video and for long days away from a charger.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The AMOLED screen reaches 1,800 nits, the battery lets light users charge every other day, and 22.5W reverse charging can top up earbuds or another phone. IP65 covers rain and dust, and 256GB is a generous base storage.</p>\n<h2>The problem</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 is an entry-level chip, the kind usually found in Rs 30,000 phones. It\'s fine for social media and video, but slower than rivals at this price. There\'s only one useful rear camera (50MP), with no ultra wide, and at about 225g it\'s heavy.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> (Rs 44,999) is cheaper, lighter and faster, with a 144Hz curved pOLED, but a smaller 5,000mAh battery. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> (Rs 39,999) has a 7,000mAh battery and AMOLED for Rs 8,000 less, though it\'s 4G only. Spending Rs 10,000 more gets you the much better <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Note 17 Pro</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi Note 17 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.99 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1800 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 512GB UFS 2.2</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8, 26 mm, PDAF, 1/2.76&quot; sensor | 16MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Si-C Li-Ion 7700 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, HyperOS 3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, WiFi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.1, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>225 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>169.7 x 79.1 x 8.3 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Check the version and the price together</h2>\n<p>Listings for this phone have not been consistent. The summary above describes 8GB/256GB at Rs 47,999 and 12GB/512GB at Rs 52,999, while our price table records 6GB/128GB at Rs 47,999 and 8GB/256GB at Rs 52,999. Before paying, look at the sticker on the box and at About phone, and make sure you get the memory you were quoted. The difference decides whether this phone is merely expensive or a poor buy.</p>\n<h2>Almost seven inches of AMOLED</h2>\n<p>At 6.99 inches this is one of the largest phones in normal production. Watching cricket highlights, reading PDFs for an exam or following a recipe in the kitchen is pleasant, and AMOLED contrast makes a difference compared with the LCDs of cheaper phones. A 120Hz refresh rate and 1,800 nits in HDR round it off.</p>\n<p>It will not fit every pocket, and one-handed use is out of the question for most people. With 225 grams to hold, it feels more like a small tablet that makes calls. Try it before ordering online.</p>\n<h2>The chip explains the score</h2>\n<p>Qualcomm\'s Snapdragon 4 series is made for entry-level 5G phones. It is efficient and adequate, and it does not belong in a Rs 48,000 phone. Social feeds scroll well, but loading a heavy web page, switching between apps or editing a long video shows the limits. Games run at low to medium settings. A phone at this price should stay comfortable for four years, and this one starts closer to the edge.</p>\n<h2>Battery life is superb</h2>\n<p>The other side of a modest chip is endurance. With 7,700mAh, many owners will charge every second night. At 45W a full charge takes a little over an hour and a half. Reverse charging at 22.5W is fast enough to be useful.</p>\n<h2>Camera: one lens, no extras</h2>\n<p>There is a single 50MP camera with autofocus and, according to the sheet, no optical stabilisation. Daylight photos are good. At night you need steady hands. No ultra wide means stepping back for group photos, and that is awkward in small rooms. The 16MP selfie camera is fine.</p>\n<h2>Protection</h2>\n<p>IP65 covers dust and water jets, enough for rain. The more expensive Note 17 models add immersion and high-pressure ratings.</p>\n<h2>What we would buy instead</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Redmi Note 17 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-C Li-Ion 7700 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50-pro\">Tecno Spark 50 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>6000mAh Li-Po, non-removable; 60W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-30\">Infinix GT 30</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;46,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7400 (4nm)</td><td>45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Motorola Edge 50 Fusion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;46,499</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 68W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table shows the trouble. For the same money, you can have the <a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a> or <a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-30\">Infinix GT 30</a> with gaming-class speed, or the <a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Motorola Edge 50 Fusion</a> with a curved pOLED and two good cameras. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> is cheaper with stabilisation and longer support. And ten thousand more brings the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro</a>, which fixes nearly every complaint.</p>\n<h2>So who is it for?</h2>\n<p>There is a real audience. Think of someone whose phone is mostly a screen: a retired teacher who reads and watches all day, a shop owner who streams news at the counter, a student who studies from PDFs and lectures and does not play games. For them the display and the battery are the two features that count, and nothing else near this price has both at this size.</p>\n<h2>Tips for living with it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use one-handed mode, which shrinks the interface, when you need to reach the top.</li>\n<li>Set the font size once and the display size separately. Larger text with normal icons reads best.</li>\n<li>Use split screen: on seven inches it is useful, with a video above and notes below.</li>\n<li>Choose a case with a ring or strap to hold it safely.</li>\n<li>Keep 60Hz for reading and 120Hz for scrolling, or leave it on automatic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>HyperOS 3 on Android 16</h2>\n<p>You get the current software, which is good. Clean up the bundled apps, stop recommendations, and check the promised update period with the seller. Xiaomi\'s Note series generally receives several years of security patches, with fewer version upgrades than Samsung gives.</p>\n<h2>Tablet or big phone?</h2>\n<p>If reading and video are the only goals and you already own a working phone, a budget tablet may serve you better. A 10 or 11-inch tablet costs from about Rs 25,000, and our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-30000-nepal\">guide to tablets under Rs 30,000</a> lists good ones. You would have two devices for less than the price of this phone, with a much larger picture. If you want just one thing to carry, the Note 17\'s size makes sense.</p>\n<h2>Resale and service</h2>\n<p>Redmi Note phones sell easily second-hand because buyers know them. The unusual size may narrow the audience later. Service coverage is good, and screens for a 6.99-inch AMOLED will cost more than for smaller Redmis, so take out screen insurance if it is offered.</p>\n<h2>If the price falls</h2>\n<p>At around Rs 40,000 for the 8GB model this would be an easy phone to like. At that level the slow chip is what you expect and the screen and battery become a bonus. Festival sales and exchange offers sometimes bring Redmi phones down quickly, so if you are in no hurry, put it on your list in our <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a> and wait.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>A magnificent screen and battery have been attached to the wrong processor and then priced as though nobody would notice. Buy it only for reading and video, and only after comparing the version and price carefully. Everyone else should save for the Note 17 Pro or choose one of the faster phones in the table.</p>',7.4,'Big 6.99-inch 120Hz AMOLED\n7,700mAh battery, 45W charging\n256GB base storage\nIP65 and reverse charging','Entry-level Snapdragon 4 Gen 4\nNo ultra wide camera\nHeavy at about 225g','Good for video and battery life, but the chip is weak for the price. Most buyers will be happier with the Moto G96 or the Note 17 Pro.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(113,253,20,'Moto G96 Review: A Light, Fast Phone with a Lovely Screen','moto-g96-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Moto G96 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Moto G96 costs <strong>Rs 44,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB. Most phones at this price chase battery size. The G96 focuses on a good screen and a light body instead.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 6.67-inch <strong>curved pOLED at 144Hz</strong> is one of the smoothest screens under Rs 50,000. The Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4nm) is a proper mid-range chip, clearly faster than the Snapdragon 4 Gen 4 in the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-5g\">Redmi Note 17 5G</a>. At 178g it\'s light. Motorola\'s near-stock Android has few preinstalled apps, and the 32MP selfie camera is good for video calls.</p>\n<h2>What\'s ordinary</h2>\n<p>A 5,000mAh battery with 30W charging is now average when rivals offer 7,000mAh or more. The 8MP ultra wide is basic, and Motorola\'s update promise is shorter than Samsung\'s.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m17-5g\">Galaxy M17 5G</a> (Rs 43,999) has longer software support but a slower chip and 90Hz screen. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> (Rs 39,999) lasts much longer on a charge. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=moto-g96&g2=samsung-galaxy-m17-5g\">Compare the G96 and M17</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Motorola Moto G96 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.67 inches P-OLED, 144Hz, 1600 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4 nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB UFS 2.2</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.9, PDAF, OIS, 1/1.56&quot; LYT-700C sensor + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, 1/4.0&quot; sensor | 32 MP, f/2.2, 1/3.14&quot; sensor front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-ion 5500 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>178 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>161.9 x 73.3 x 7.9 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A phone for people who hold phones</h2>\n<p>Spec sheets reward big numbers. Hands reward other things. At 178 grams with a curved screen, the G96 is one of the few phones under Rs 50,000 that you enjoy picking up. The edges slope into the palm, the back is soft vegan leather on most versions, and it slides into a jeans pocket without dragging it down. If you have been carrying a 220 gram phone with an enormous battery, the difference on the first day is almost comic.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 6.67-inch pOLED at 144Hz is the headline. Motion is fluid, blacks are deep and colours are rich. The curved sides look expensive, with the usual costs: reflections along the edges, protectors that cost more and fit less well, and an occasional accidental touch. Use a case that covers the sides and those touches mostly go away.</p>\n<p>Peak brightness of 1,600 nits belongs to HDR video. In ordinary sunlight it is good, though phones such as the Honor X8d are brighter.</p>\n<h2>Battery: check the number</h2>\n<p>Our text above says 5,000mAh, while the specification sheet in our catalogue lists 5,500mAh. Motorola has sold the G96 with the larger figure, so expect that, and look at the label on the box. Either way it is a one-day phone. A commuter with moderate use will end the day with a quarter left. A student who streams on mobile data between classes will need a top-up. Charging at 30W takes a little over an hour.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 is two generations old by name and still a strong middle-class chip. Apps open quickly, multitasking on 8GB is smooth and light games run at 60 frames per second or more. PUBG Mobile is enjoyable at balanced settings. The UFS 2.2 storage is slower than the UFS 3.1 and 4.0 found in gaming phones, which you notice only when installing something large.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>A 50MP Sony LYT-700C main sensor with optical stabilisation is a better part than usual at this price. Photos have good detail and pleasing colour, and night shots are usable without a tripod. The 8MP ultra wide doubles as a macro camera with autofocus. The 32MP front camera is sharp, and it helps with video interviews and reels. Video goes to 4K at 30 frames per second on both sides.</p>\n<h2>Software: clean, with a short horizon</h2>\n<p>Motorola\'s Android feels calm. There are few extra apps, notifications behave, and gestures such as chopping the phone twice for the torch are addictive. Smart Connect lets you use the phone with a TV or PC like a small desktop. The weakness is support. Motorola promises one or two Android upgrades and three to four years of patches on most G phones, less than Samsung gives its cheaper A series. The G96 launched with Android 15. Ask the seller what the plan is for this model.</p>\n<h2>Toughness</h2>\n<p>The G96 is rated IP68 in Motorola\'s own material for several markets, which is a pleasant surprise in a light phone. Confirm it for the unit you are buying, as regional versions differ, and remember that no warranty covers water.</p>\n<h2>Compared with its neighbours</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Motorola Moto G96 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4 nm)</td><td>Li-ion 5500 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Exynos 1330</td><td>5,000mAh; 25W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Tecno Pova 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,990</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 70W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> is the closest all-round rival, with a brighter screen and wider service. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m17-5g\">Galaxy M17 5G</a> and <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">A17 5G</a> win on support and lose on speed and smoothness. The <a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Tecno Pova 6</a> has a bigger battery with bold styling. If endurance is the main goal, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> is cheaper and lasts far longer.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Motorola has fewer service points than Samsung, Xiaomi or vivo. Curved pOLED panels are not cheap, and a corner shop is unlikely to stock one. Before buying, ask the seller for the cost and waiting time of a screen replacement and where it would be done. If you live far from Kathmandu, weigh this seriously.</p>\n<h2>Who will love it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who want a light, good-looking phone.</li>\n<li>Users who dislike bloated software.</li>\n<li>Students who take photos and video more than they play games.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who will not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone away from a charger all day.</li>\n<li>Owners who keep a phone for five years.</li>\n<li>People who break screens.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Small print before you pay</h2>\n<p>Only the 8GB/256GB version is listed in Nepal, which is the right one to have. Check that a charger is included, that the IMEI appears in MDMS, and that the phone is not an Indian import with a different warranty. Try the curved edges with your own grip for a few minutes. And look at last year\'s <a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Edge 50 Fusion</a> at a slightly higher price, which brings better protection and a similar screen.</p>\n<h2>Verdict in a sentence or two</h2>\n<p>The Moto G96 proves that a phone under Rs 45,000 can feel expensive. It asks you to charge nightly and to accept shorter support, and in return it is nicer to use every hour in between.</p>\n<h2>One week after buying</h2>\n<p>Check how often the curved edge registers your palm, and adjust the grip rejection in display settings if it does. Look at the battery graph after three ordinary days. If it falls below 20 per cent before evening, set the refresh rate to automatic and limit background use for the two apps at the top of the list. Those two steps usually add an hour or more, which is the hour this phone needs.</p>',7.8,'144Hz curved pOLED screen\nSnapdragon 7s Gen 2 is quick for the price\nLight at 178g\nClean, near-stock Android\n256GB storage','5,000mAh and 30W are only average now\nBasic ultra wide\nShorter update promise than Samsung','A pleasant, fast all-rounder under Rs 45,000 if you charge every night. Heavy users should look at the Honor X8d\'s bigger battery.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(114,237,20,'Honor X8d Review: 7,000mAh in a 7.5mm Body for Rs 39,999','honor-x8d-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Honor X8d yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Honor X8d costs <strong>Rs 39,999</strong> for 8GB/128GB and Rs 43,999 for 8GB/256GB. It packs a <strong>7,000mAh battery into a 7.5mm, 188g body</strong>, which is unusually slim for that capacity, along with a bright AMOLED screen.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 6.77-inch AMOLED is Full HD+ at 120Hz and reaches 3,000 nits, so it\'s easy to read outdoors. The battery should last well into a second day, and 45W charging is quick enough. It runs MagicOS 10 on Android 16, and IP65 handles rain and dust. The 108MP main camera takes detailed daylight shots.</p>\n<h2>The catch: 4G only</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2 doesn\'t support 5G. Today that matters little on NTC and Ncell, but if you keep phones for four or five years it\'s worth weighing. Performance is fine for everyday use and casual games. The 5MP ultra wide adds little.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> (Rs 44,999) is faster, has 5G and a 144Hz screen, but a 5,000mAh battery. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-5g\">Redmi Note 17 5G</a> (Rs 47,999) has 5G and a 7,700mAh battery, but costs Rs 8,000 more with a weaker chip.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Honor X8d</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.77 inches AMOLED, 120 Hz, 3000 nits (Peak)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2 (6nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB / 512GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 108MP, f/1.8, 24 mm, OIS, PDAF, 1/1.67” CMOS BSI sensor + Ultrawide: 5 MP, f/2.2 | 16MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W HONOR SuperCharge</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, MagicOS 10</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / WCDMA / LTE, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.0, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>188 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>162.9 x 76.3 x 7.5 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Slim and long-lasting, which used to be a contradiction</h2>\n<p>For years you had to pick. Thin phones died in the afternoon, and phones that lasted were bricks. The X8d, at 7.5mm and 188 grams with a 7,000mAh battery, shows how far silicon-carbon cells have come. It is thinner than many flagships and holds almost half as much energy again.</p>\n<p>In daily life that means a day and a half of real use, or two days if your phone is mostly for messages and calls. Charging at 45W takes a little more than an hour. There is nothing to manage and nothing to remember, the best thing a battery can offer.</p>\n<h2>The screen is better than the price suggests</h2>\n<p>A Full HD+ AMOLED at 120Hz that reaches 3,000 nits belongs in phones costing Rs 60,000. Text is crisp, video is rich, and you can read it at noon on the way to Swayambhu. Honor includes high-frequency dimming for low brightness, helpful if AMOLED screens tire your eyes. Next to an HD+ LCD phone at the same price, the contrast is obvious from across a shop.</p>\n<h2>About the missing 5G</h2>\n<p>Honor saved money by using a 4G chip. Let us think about who loses out. NTC and Ncell have wide 4G coverage and limited 5G, so almost nobody will miss it in 2026. By 2029 or 2030 that could change in the large cities. If you replace your phone every three years, ignore this. If you keep one until it dies, and you live in the valley, put it on the scale.</p>\n<p>A 4G modem also has an advantage. It draws less power in areas with weak signal, which many of us deal with every day.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2 is a 6nm chip for everyday work. Facebook, TikTok, YouTube, banking, maps and office documents are fine, and 8GB of memory keeps things from reloading. Casual games are smooth and heavy ones are not the point. A student who plays PUBG for two hours a night should look at the <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> or save for a POCO.</p>\n<h2>Camera</h2>\n<p>The 108MP main camera has optical stabilisation, according to the sheet, and that is worth more than the pixel count. In daylight you can crop a long way into a picture. Indoors, stabilisation keeps family photos sharp. The 5MP ultra wide is there for emergencies. The 16MP front camera is fine for calls.</p>\n<h2>Protection and build</h2>\n<p>IP65 covers dust and jets of water. It is not meant for immersion. The frame is plastic and the back is smooth, so use a case to stop it from sliding. Honor sells a tougher sibling, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">X9d</a>, for Rs 15,000 more.</p>\n<h2>128GB or 256GB?</h2>\n<p>The gap is Rs 4,000, and it is money well spent. Video from a 108MP phone takes space, WhatsApp fills whatever it finds, and a memory card, which this phone accepts, is slower for apps. Take the 256GB model unless your budget ends at forty thousand exactly.</p>\n<h2>MagicOS</h2>\n<p>Google services are all there. The software is smooth and has useful side panels and gestures. A few bundled apps can be removed. Version information differs between listings: the review says Android 16 and our specification sheet lists Android 15 with MagicOS 10, so check About phone in the shop. Honor\'s update promise on the X series is moderate.</p>\n<h2>Competition at Rs 40,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Honor X8d</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2 (6nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W HONOR SuperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-m8s\">POCO M8s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s Gen 3 (6 nm)</td><td>Si-C 7000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Charging; 18W Re...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y32\">Vivo Y32</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Unisoc T7300 (6nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 8000 mAh, non-removable; 44W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c85\">Realme C85</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-rock-1-pro\">Blackview Rock 1 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>15000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-m8s\">POCO M8s</a> and <a href=\"/products/realme-c85-5g\">Realme C85 5G</a> add 5G at the same price with weaker screens or cameras. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y32\">vivo Y32</a> has a larger service network. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x7e\">Honor X7e</a> comes from the same family and is worth comparing on the shelf. For Samsung fans, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> at Rs 44,999 offers longer support with a smaller battery.</p>\n<p>None of these match the combination of brightness, sharpness and endurance here. The X8d loses only where 5G or long software support matter to you.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Ask where Honor service is available near you, and how much a screen costs. The brand is expanding, but it is not as common as Samsung or Redmi. A screen replacement offer may be included in the first months. Get it in writing.</p>\n<h2>Best suited to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Teachers, office workers and shopkeepers who want a phone that lasts.</li>\n<li>Readers and video watchers.</li>\n<li>Travellers who want a light phone with stamina.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Not suited to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>5G early adopters.</li>\n<li>Gamers.</li>\n<li>People who keep phones for five years or more.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Owner tips</h2>\n<p>Turn on smart charging to slow battery ageing. Use natural colour mode for skin tones that look right. Shoot at the default resolution and use the 108MP mode only in bright light. Let your chat apps run in the background from the battery settings. With those set, this is one of the easiest phones to live with at its price, and one of the few you can recommend to a parent and use yourself.</p>',7.6,'7,000mAh in a slim 7.5mm body\nBright 3,000-nit AMOLED\nAndroid 16 out of the box\nGood value at Rs 39,999','No 5G\nWeak 5MP ultra wide\nBase model has 128GB','The best battery phone around Rs 40,000, as long as you don\'t need 5G. Pay Rs 4,000 extra for 256GB if you shoot a lot of video.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(115,176,20,'Galaxy M17 5G Review: Samsung AMOLED and 5G for Rs 43,999','samsung-galaxy-m17-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy M17 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Galaxy M17 5G costs <strong>Rs 43,999</strong> for 6GB/128GB. It\'s the cheapest way to get a Samsung phone with a Super AMOLED screen and 5G in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 6.7-inch Full HD+ Super AMOLED looks noticeably better than the LCDs on Samsung\'s cheaper A0-series phones. You get Samsung\'s service network, One UI 7 and long software support, which is the real reason to choose Samsung at this price. The 50MP main camera is backed by a 5MP ultra wide.</p>\n<h2>Where it falls short</h2>\n<p>The Exynos 1330 is an older 5nm chip, fine for daily use but slower than the Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 in the <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a>. The screen is 90Hz rather than 120Hz, charging is 25W, and 6GB/128GB is modest for Rs 44,000. The 2MP macro is filler.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Choose it if you want Samsung\'s software support and service. If you care more about speed and screen smoothness, the Moto G96 is better for Rs 1,000 more. If you can wait for a sale, the Galaxy A-series is worth watching too. See the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy M17 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.7 inches Super AMOLED, 90Hz, 1100 nits (HBM)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1330 (5nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary : 50MP, f/1.8, OIS, 1/2.76&quot; sensor + Ultrawide: 5MP, f/2.2, 1/5.0&quot; sensor + Macro: 2MP, f/2.2 | 13 MP, f/2.0, 1/3.1&quot; sensor front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, One UI 7.0</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.0, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>192 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>164.4 x 77.9 x 7.5 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>M or A? Sorting out Samsung\'s twins</h2>\n<p>Samsung sells two nearly identical phones at this price. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> is the one in shop windows, and the M17 5G is usually sold online. Both use the Exynos 1330, a 6.7-inch 90Hz Super AMOLED, a 5,000mAh battery with 25W charging and the same trio of cameras. The M series is typically a little cheaper or offers a little more memory for the money. When the prices are within a thousand rupees, buy whichever has the better warranty and is easier to return.</p>\n<p>Listings for the M17 in Nepal also differ. Our price table records 6GB/128GB at Rs 43,999, whereas the specification sheet mentions 8GB. Confirm what is in the box before you pay.</p>\n<h2>What the AMOLED does for you</h2>\n<p>Many phones around Rs 40,000 still use LCDs. Super AMOLED brings deeper contrast, better viewing angles and an always-on clock. Full HD+ keeps small Devanagari text readable. At 1,100 nits in high-brightness mode, it copes with daylight. The 90Hz refresh rate is the weak point next to 120Hz and 144Hz rivals, though it is smooth enough when it is your only phone.</p>\n<h2>Day to day</h2>\n<p>The Exynos 1330 is built on a 5nm process and belongs to the entry level of 5G chips. WhatsApp, Facebook, YouTube, bank apps and video calls are fine. Opening many apps in a hurry will show small delays. Games run at low to medium settings. If you compare it with the Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 in the <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a>, the Motorola is clearly faster in everything that takes effort.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: the stabilised one counts</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera has optical stabilisation, which is uncommon here. It produces steady photos and calmer video. The 5MP ultra wide is limited to daylight, and the macro lens can be ignored. The 13MP selfie camera is ordinary. Samsung\'s processing brightens faces, and most people like the result straight from the camera.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>A full day is normal, with light users reaching into the second. At 25W a full charge takes about ninety minutes, and the charger is usually sold separately. A phone with 7,000mAh lasts noticeably longer, so if your power supply is uncertain, look at the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a>.</p>\n<h2>Software support is why this phone exists</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s promise for its recent cheaper phones covers several Android versions and years of security patches, far beyond the typical budget phone. There are two benefits. Your wallet and bank apps will keep working on a secure version, and the phone will be easier to sell. Samsung also adds Knox security, Secure Folder for private files and Auto Blocker against dangerous installs. For a family phone these are useful tools, and not just words on a box.</p>\n<h2>Service everywhere</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s service network is the widest in the country. You can get a screen in Pokhara, Biratnagar or Nepalgunj without sending the phone away. Genuine AMOLED parts cost more than LCD ones, so budget for a case and protector.</p>\n<h2>How it compares</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy M17 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1330 (5nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-lite-4g\">Vivo V60 Lite 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,499</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Tecno Pova 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,990</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 70W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>Exynos 1330</td><td>5,000mAh; 25W wired</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> at the same price has a smoother, brighter screen and faster charging. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a> is much faster. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-lite-4g\">vivo V60 Lite 4G</a> is slim, with quick charging and no 5G. If you would rather have hardware than support, choose among those. If the phone is going to a parent, a child or an employee, the Samsung is easier to defend.</p>\n<h2>A few honest scenarios</h2>\n<p><strong>You sell on Facebook and need a reliable work phone.</strong> The M17 is good. The main camera handles product photos, the screen shows colours accurately enough and repairs are easy.</p>\n<p><strong>You are a gamer with Rs 45,000.</strong> Skip it. The Realme P3 or a POCO on offer will serve you better.</p>\n<p><strong>You are buying for your mother.</strong> A fine choice, though the simpler <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> costs far less if she only calls and chats.</p>\n<p><strong>You want it for five years.</strong> Choose the version with the most memory, as the chip will not get any faster.</p>\n<h2>Setup advice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Turn on Auto Blocker and automatic updates.</li>\n<li>Use Secure Folder for bank and wallet apps if you share the phone with children.</li>\n<li>Set battery protection to 80 per cent if you charge every night.</li>\n<li>Uninstall or disable partner apps you do not use.</li>\n<li>Choose 90Hz, which Samsung sometimes leaves off.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Waiting for a sale</h2>\n<p>Online-focused phones are discounted often. Watch for Dashain, New Year and brand-day sales, and compare the final price after delivery and bank offers. At around Rs 38,000 to 40,000 the M17 becomes attractive. At Rs 44,000, the gap to a much better phone is small.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>It is a decent Samsung at a fair price, not a great phone at a great price. You are paying for AMOLED, stabilised photos, service in your own city and years of updates. If those four words describe what you need, buy it with confidence. If you stopped reading at the refresh rate, you already know your answer.</p>',7.2,'Super AMOLED and 5G at a Samsung entry price\nLong Samsung software support\nSamsung service network\n5,000mAh battery','Older Exynos 1330\n90Hz screen, 25W charging\n6GB/128GB only\nMacro camera adds nothing','A safe Samsung choice, but not the best value. The Moto G96 is faster with a better screen for nearly the same price.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(116,98,20,'Galaxy A37 5G Review: Solid, Safe and Now Overpriced','samsung-galaxy-a37-5g-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Galaxy A37 5G yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Galaxy A37 5G costs <strong>Rs 74,999</strong> for 8GB/128GB, Rs 86,999 for 8GB/256GB and Rs 96,999 for 12GB/256GB. It launched at Rs 64,999 before this year\'s price revisions, and that Rs 10,000 rise changes the verdict.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The important basics are all here: <strong>IP68</strong>, Gorilla Glass Victus 2 over a 6.7-inch 120Hz Super AMOLED, a 50MP main camera with OIS, 45W charging and <strong>six years of updates</strong>. It\'s a phone you can keep for a long time, with Samsung service centres across the country.</p>\n<h2>What\'s not</h2>\n<p>The Exynos 1480 is a year-old mid-range chip, fine for daily use but well behind what Rs 75,000 buys elsewhere. A 5,000mAh battery is now average. And 128GB in the base model is stingy at this price; the 256GB version costs Rs 12,000 more.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a> (Rs 69,999) costs less and has a far faster chip, 512GB, IP68 and a 6,500mAh battery. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-5\">OnePlus Nord 5</a> (Rs 73,999) is also much faster. Within Samsung\'s range, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g\">Galaxy A57</a> is faster and thinner if you can stretch.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.7-inch Super AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass Victus 2</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB / 256GB</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>50MP main (OIS) + 8MP ultra wide + 5MP macro | 12MP front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, One UI 8.5 (6 years of updates)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>How a good phone becomes a hard sell</h2>\n<p>At its launch price of Rs 64,999, the A37 was a simple recommendation: water resistance, strong glass, stabilised camera, six years of updates and service everywhere. At Rs 74,999 it is the same phone, and the conversation is different. A ten thousand rupee rise on a mid-range phone moves it into the territory of near-flagships. We explained why prices climbed in <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">this article</a>, and Samsung was not alone, but you still have to decide with today\'s price in front of you.</p>\n<h2>The parts that justify it</h2>\n<p><strong>Protection.</strong> IP68 and Gorilla Glass Victus 2 make this one of the tougher mainstream Samsungs. You can take it into the rain without a thought.</p>\n<p><strong>Support.</strong> Six Android upgrades and six years of patches lead into 2032. Almost nothing else at this price comes close.</p>\n<p><strong>Screen.</strong> The 6.7-inch Super AMOLED at 120Hz is bright, accurate and comfortable.</p>\n<p><strong>Main camera.</strong> A stabilised 50MP sensor with Samsung\'s mature processing gives dependable photos of family and food in most light.</p>\n<h2>The parts that do not</h2>\n<p>The Exynos 1480 is fine and not more. Everyday tasks are smooth, games run at medium, and the chip has less in reserve for the later years of that long support. The 5,000mAh battery is average in a market with 7,000mAh rivals. The 8MP ultra wide and the 5MP macro are ordinary. And 128GB in the base model without a generous path to more feels mean: the 256GB version is Rs 12,000 extra.</p>\n<h2>Which version makes sense?</h2>\n<p>Look at the price ladder. 8GB/128GB is Rs 74,999, 8GB/256GB is Rs 86,999 and 12GB/256GB is Rs 96,999. The top version costs exactly as much as the base <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g\">Galaxy A57 5G</a>, which has a newer chip, a thinner body and a better ultra wide. So if you want more than the base A37, buy the A57 instead. That leaves the 128GB model as the only A37 worth considering. Use Google Photos or a computer for backup and keep an eye on WhatsApp media.</p>\n<h2>Against the fast phones</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">Vivo V60</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>7, 500mAh, non-removable; 80W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reverse</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W reverse...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> is cheaper, far faster, has four times the storage, a larger battery and IP68 as well. It also has good support, with six years of patches. On paper there is no contest. What the POCO lacks is Samsung\'s service reach, resale value and lighter software. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-5\">OnePlus Nord 5</a> is quick with clean software, and the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">Nord CE 6</a> lasts for days. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">vivo V60 5G</a> takes better portraits.</p>\n<h2>Who still comes out ahead with the A37</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parents and older relatives</strong> who will use one phone for five or six years.</li>\n<li><strong>Offices</strong> that buy in quantity and need consistent service and security.</li>\n<li><strong>People outside Kathmandu</strong> with a Samsung centre nearby.</li>\n<li><strong>Buyers with an exchange offer</strong> that brings the price down to about Rs 65,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should walk away</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Gamers and heavy users.</li>\n<li>Anyone who needs more than 128GB.</li>\n<li>People who want the most for their money.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery habits for this phone</h2>\n<p>Expect a full day with moderate use. If you have a long day, turn on power saving in the morning and not at 15 per cent, since early saving stretches the most. Charging at 45W needs a PPS charger with a 5A cable. The phone also supports battery protection modes that stop at 80 per cent. Use them on nights at home.</p>\n<h2>Camera tips</h2>\n<p>Shoot in the main camera\'s 12MP mode for everyday pictures, as it combines pixels for cleaner results. Use 2x crop for people and avoid the ultra wide at night. Turn off the scene optimiser if colours look too strong. Samsung\'s editor can remove reflections and passers-by with one tap, and it works well on simple backgrounds.</p>\n<h2>Is the previous model a smarter buy?</h2>\n<p>Last year\'s Galaxy A36 5G, where clearance stock or clean used units exist, covers much of the same ground for far less. Compare chips and remaining update years, check the battery and MDMS registration, and ask for a bill. If it is Rs 25,000 cheaper, the difference in daily use does not justify a new A37.</p>\n<h2>Timing</h2>\n<p>Samsung prices in Nepal move with offers more than with lists. Exchange bonuses, bank cashback and EMI schemes run for most of the year. Ask for the final price with every offer applied, and compare that figure with the rivals. Our <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a> shows how it has moved.</p>\n<h2>Bottom line</h2>\n<p>The A37 is the phone you recommend to people who do not want to think about phones. For them, reliability over six years outweighs a benchmark score. For everyone who enjoys comparing, it is overpriced, and the fair price is the one it had at launch.</p>\n<h2>If you already own an A33, A34 or A35</h2>\n<p>Stay where you are unless the battery is failing. A new battery at a Samsung centre restores an A34 or A35 for a small fraction of this phone\'s price, and both still receive patches. The A37 improves durability and support length more than speed or photographs. The people who will feel a real difference are those coming from an A13, A14 or A23, where the screen, the stabilised camera and the smoothness are all a class higher.</p>',7.3,'IP68 with Gorilla Glass Victus 2\n50MP main camera with OIS\nSix years of updates\nSamsung service network','Rs 10,000 price rise since launch\nSlow chip for Rs 75,000\nOnly 128GB in the base model\nAverage 5,000mAh battery','A good phone at the wrong price. Buy it only if Samsung\'s update and service promise matters most. Otherwise the Poco F7 gives far more for less.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(117,226,20,'OnePlus Nord 5 Review: Fast, Smooth, and No Charger in the Box','oneplus-nord-5-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the OnePlus Nord 5 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The OnePlus Nord 5 costs <strong>Rs 73,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 79,999 for 12GB/512GB. It\'s a near-flagship phone in performance and screen, with OnePlus\'s clean OxygenOS software.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8s Gen 3 is a fast 4nm chip that handles demanding games well. The 6.83-inch 1.5K AMOLED runs at <strong>144Hz</strong> and is one of the smoothest screens under Rs 80,000. The 6,800mAh battery is large, and the 50MP front camera with autofocus is excellent for video calls and vlogging.</p>\n<h2>What to know before buying</h2>\n<p><strong>There\'s no charger in the box.</strong> To get the 80W speed you\'ll need a compatible OnePlus charger, which adds several thousand rupees. Protection is IP65, not IP68, and the 8MP ultra wide is weak.</p>\n<h2>Nord 5 or Poco F7?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a> costs Rs 4,000 less, includes 12GB/512GB, has a newer Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 and IP68. The Nord 5 has the 144Hz screen, a bigger battery, a much better selfie camera and cleaner software. <a href=\"/compare?category=mobile&g1=oneplus-nord-5&g2=poco-f7\">Compare them side by side</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>OnePlus Nord 5</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.83 inches Swift AMOLED, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ), 1400 nits (HBM), 1800 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 3 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB UFS 3.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8, 24 mm, OIS, PDAF, 1/1.56” LYT-700 sensor + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, 15 mm, 116° FOV, 1/4.0&quot; OV08D10 sensor | 50MP, f/2.0, 21 mm, 1/2.76&quot; S5K...</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Po 6800mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, OxygenOS 15</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / CDMA/ HSPA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 6, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.4, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>211 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>163.4 x 77 x 8.1mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What you are buying into</h2>\n<p>The Nord line takes most of the OnePlus experience and removes the expensive cameras. In the Nord 5 that means a chip close to flagship level, a huge smooth display, a battery that lasts and software that many people consider the most pleasant on Android. At Rs 73,999 it costs about a third of a premium flagship, and in normal use it does not feel like it.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8s Gen 3 is strong, with a big core that keeps the interface instant. Games run at high settings, and OnePlus provides a cooling system large enough to keep frame rates steady. In PUBG Mobile and Call of Duty Mobile, high frame-rate modes look great on the 144Hz screen. The newer Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 in the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a> is faster, but in the hand you would struggle to tell them apart outside long gaming sessions.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>At 6.83 inches with 1.5K resolution, it is sharp and immersive. Brightness is good outdoors. It is flat, so protectors are cheap and accidental touches are rare. For reading at night, low brightness and reduced flicker are handled well.</p>\n<h2>Battery and that missing charger</h2>\n<p>The 6,800mAh battery delivers a long day for heavy users and closer to two for light ones. It charges at 80W with a SUPERVOOC charger. The box does not contain one, so here is what to do. If you already own a OnePlus, OPPO or Realme SUPERVOOC charger, it will work. If not, add the price to your budget. A good USB PD charger also works, at lower speed. Beware of cheap copies with SUPERVOOC printed on them. They can damage the battery.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: the front is the star</h2>\n<p>A 50MP selfie camera with autofocus is rare in any phone. Video calls, vlogs and reels look clear, and you can hold the phone further away without losing focus. For creators this can be the deciding feature.</p>\n<p>On the back, the 50MP Sony LYT-700 main camera with stabilisation is reliable in most light. The 8MP ultra wide is average and there is no zoom lens. Video goes to 4K at 60 frames per second on both the main and the front camera.</p>\n<h2>OxygenOS</h2>\n<p>The launch version is OxygenOS 15 on Android 15, with upgrades promised for several years. It is fast and clean, with a customisable key on the side in place of the old alert slider and a set of AI tools for notes and screenshots. There is less clutter than on HyperOS or HiOS. OnePlus does not match Samsung\'s length of support, so ask for the number of years on this model.</p>\n<h2>Protection</h2>\n<p>IP65 is enough for rain and dust. At this price, several rivals have IP68, and it is fair to count that against the Nord. At 211 grams it is not light either.</p>\n<h2>Nord 5 against its rivals</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>OnePlus Nord 5</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;73,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Po 6800mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Fast C...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">Vivo V60</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>7, 500mAh, non-removable; 80W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reverse</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Compared with the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a>, you give up IP68, storage and some speed, and gain a faster screen, a bigger battery, a much better front camera and tidier software. Compared with the <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a>, you give up battery and gain speed. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">Nord CE 6</a> is the newer, cheaper-feeling sibling with more battery. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">vivo V60 5G</a> takes far better portraits with its zoom lens and is slower.</p>\n<h2>8GB/256GB or 12GB/512GB?</h2>\n<p>The step is Rs 6,000 for 4GB of memory and double the storage, which is one of the better deals in this class. If you plan to keep the phone for three years or more, take it. There is no card slot, and games and 4K video fill 256GB sooner than you think.</p>\n<h2>A year on sale: does age matter?</h2>\n<p>The Nord 5 launched in July 2025. That means its successor exists in the market as the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">Nord 6</a> at about Rs 89,999, with a newer chip and an even larger battery. The older model is still good value, provided the price is lower. If you see the gap shrink to Rs 10,000, the newer phone with longer support ahead is the better buy.</p>\n<h2>Buying and service</h2>\n<p>Choose an official unit with a Nepali warranty. OnePlus service is concentrated in Kathmandu, and Indian imports, common because of the border, do not carry local warranty. Verify MDMS before paying. Screens for this size of AMOLED are not cheap, so a case and a tempered glass are sensible.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vloggers, teachers and people who live on video calls.</li>\n<li>Gamers who want a smooth, large screen.</li>\n<li>Users who care about tidy software.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who it does not suit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Zoom photographers.</li>\n<li>People who want full water protection.</li>\n<li>Anyone who would be annoyed by buying a charger separately.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Settings to try</h2>\n<p>Set the side key to open the camera or the recorder. Choose 144Hz only in games and keep the automatic mode elsewhere. Enable the 80 per cent charging limit for nights. In the camera, turn on the front camera\'s 4K mode and use a small tripod: you will be surprised at how good it looks.</p>',8.1,'Snapdragon 8s Gen 3 performance\n144Hz 1.5K AMOLED\n6,800mAh battery\n50MP autofocus selfie camera\nClean OxygenOS','No charger included\nIP65 only\nWeak 8MP ultra wide\nPoco F7 is cheaper with more storage','A great phone for gamers and selfie-takers. Budget for a charger. For pure value, the Poco F7 edges it.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(118,201,20,'OPPO Reno 15 F Review: Nice Design and Selfies, Dated Chip','oppo-reno-15-f-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the OPPO Reno 15 F yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The OPPO Reno 15 F costs <strong>Rs 63,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 69,999 for 12GB/512GB. Like most Reno phones, it focuses on design, a compact 6.57-inch AMOLED and a good selfie camera.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The <strong>32MP autofocus front camera</strong> is better than most at this price for selfies and video calls. The 6.57-inch 120Hz AMOLED is easy to use one-handed. A 6,000mAh battery with 45W charging is solid, and it runs ColorOS 16 on Android 16.</p>\n<h2>The problem: the chip</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 is a 2022-era mid-range chip. It handles everyday use, but at Rs 64,000 it\'s well behind the <a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">Poco X7 Pro</a> (Rs 59,999, Dimensity 8400 Ultra) and the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a> (Rs 69,999, Snapdragon 8s Gen 4). The 2MP macro is filler, and there\'s no telephoto.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy it for the selfie camera and compact size if you find it on discount. At full price, the Poco X7 Pro is the better phone for less money.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>OPPO Reno 15F</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.59 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 1400 nits (HBM)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB UFS 3.1</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 50MP, f/1.8, AF, OIS, PDAF + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, FOV 112° + Macro: 2MP, f/2.4 | 50MP, f/2.0, AF front</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 16, ColorOS 16</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band, Bluetooth 5.1, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>195 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>158.2 x 74.9 x 8.1 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Read the label: two versions of this phone exist in listings</h2>\n<p>The figures published for the Reno 15 F do not agree with each other. The text above speaks of a 6.57-inch screen, a 6,000mAh battery with 45W charging and a 32MP front camera. The specification sheet in our catalogue, gathered later, lists a 6.59-inch screen, 7,000mAh with 80W charging and a 50MP autofocus front camera. OPPO often sells different versions under one name in different countries, so the correct answer for you is the one printed on the box in front of you. Check battery capacity and charging speed before paying, because the second version is a much better deal than the first.</p>\n<h2>The Reno recipe</h2>\n<p>Reno phones are built around how they look and how you look in them. The body is slim, the finish is attractive, and the front camera gets more attention than usual. At 195 grams with a screen under 6.6 inches, the 15 F is easier to hold than the near-7-inch phones around it. Many buyers, particularly those who found the last generation too large, will like it immediately.</p>\n<h2>Selfies and video calls</h2>\n<p>Autofocus on the front camera is the feature to appreciate. Most selfie cameras are fixed to focus at arm\'s length, so group shots and close-ups lose sharpness. With autofocus you can hold the phone near to talk or far away for a group, and faces stay crisp. OPPO\'s skin processing is smooth by default, and it can be reduced in the settings.</p>\n<h2>Rear cameras</h2>\n<p>The 50MP main camera with stabilisation handles daylight and ordinary indoor scenes well. The 8MP ultra wide is average. The 2MP macro has no purpose. There is no telephoto. Portrait mode blurs backgrounds convincingly around hair and glasses, one of OPPO\'s strengths.</p>\n<h2>The processor question</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 is a 4nm chip that first appeared in 2022. It still runs ordinary apps smoothly and keeps the phone cool. The issue is what it will feel like in 2029, and what others give you for Rs 63,999. The <a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">POCO X7 Pro</a> is several times faster in games and cheaper. If you do not play games or edit video, you may never notice. If you do, you will notice every day.</p>\n<h2>ColorOS 16</h2>\n<p>OPPO\'s software has become one of the smoothest on Android. Animations are fluid, the control centre is neat and the privacy tools are useful, for example a private safe and the option to give apps empty data. There are some bundled apps and a recommendation feed to switch off. Connection with iPhones for sharing photos through a companion app is handy in mixed families. OPPO\'s promised support for Reno F models is moderate. Ask for the number of Android versions.</p>\n<h2>Battery life</h2>\n<p>Either version will last a full day, and the 7,000mAh one closer to two. With 80W charging, a short stop fills most of it. Use the supplied charger. OPPO\'s fast charging is proprietary, and other chargers will be slower.</p>\n<h2>What else costs about the same</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>OPPO Reno 15F</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;63,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-pro-plus\">Redmi Note 15 Pro Plus 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;63,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v80-lite-5g\">Vivo V80 Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;63,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7360 Turbo (4nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 7050 mAh, non-removable; 44W FlashCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4b\">Nothing Phone 4b</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;64,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4 nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-11-pro\">OPPO Reno 11 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;62,999</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 8200</td><td>4600mAh, 80W SUPERVOOC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-pro-plus\">Redmi Note 15 Pro Plus</a> offers a strong main camera and tougher protection. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-15t\">Xiaomi 15T</a> brings Leica-tuned triple cameras and a much better chip. The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4b\">Nothing Phone 4b</a> has clean software and unusual design. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v70-fe\">vivo V70 FE</a> is the closest in spirit, aimed at portraits and looks. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> is about battery. The Reno\'s advantages over all of them are size, style and front camera focus.</p>\n<h2>Two honest profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Sita, 24, works in a bank and posts reels on weekends.</strong> She wants a slim phone with a good front camera, does not play games and changes phones every two years. The Reno 15 F is a reasonable choice if she gets the better version with a discount.</p>\n<p><strong>Bikash, 20, studies engineering and plays PUBG.</strong> He should not look at it twice.</p>\n<h2>Price and offers</h2>\n<p>OPPO dealers are flexible. Accessories, screen cover, exchange bonuses and cash discounts are common, and the price on the board is an opening bid. At around Rs 55,000 the 8GB/256GB model becomes fair. The 12GB/512GB version at Rs 69,999 is not, as it enters the range of far faster phones.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>OPPO has an established service network in Nepal with centres in major cities and reasonable parts prices. It is one of the better brands in that respect after Samsung, Xiaomi and vivo. Keep the bill and the warranty card.</p>\n<h2>Before you buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check the box for battery, charging and front camera details.</li>\n<li>Try the front camera indoors, where weak cameras show themselves.</li>\n<li>Hold it next to a larger phone. If compactness does not matter to you, the argument for it weakens.</li>\n<li>Confirm memory, storage and MDMS registration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Reno 15 F is a pleasant phone with a clear personality, carrying a processor that does not match its price. Choose it for size, style and selfies, at a discount, in the better-equipped version. If any of those conditions fails, there are stronger phones for less money.</p>',6.9,'32MP autofocus selfie camera\nCompact 6.57-inch AMOLED\n6,000mAh battery\nAndroid 16 with ColorOS 16','Old Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 for Rs 64,000\n2MP macro filler, no telephoto\nPoco X7 Pro is faster and cheaper','Good-looking with great selfies, but overpriced for its chip. Wait for a discount or buy the Poco X7 Pro.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(119,788,20,'Acer Aspire Lite 15 (Ryzen 5 7430U) Review: Best Laptop Under Rs 1 Lakh','acer-aspire-lite-15-ryzen-5-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Acer Aspire Lite 15 with an AMD Ryzen 5 7430U costs <strong>Rs 97,999</strong> with <strong>16GB of RAM and a 512GB SSD</strong>. Most laptops under Rs 1 lakh give you 8GB and 256GB. That makes this the best-specified laptop in the budget.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Ryzen 5 7430U has 6 cores and 12 threads, more than the 4-core chips in cheaper laptops. Office work, dozens of browser tabs, Zoom classes, coding and light photo editing all run comfortably. 16GB of memory means it won\'t slow down in a year or two the way 8GB machines do.</p>\n<h2>The rest</h2>\n<p>The 15.6-inch Full HD IPS screen is fine for study and office work, but it\'s 60Hz and not especially bright. The 58Wh battery is bigger than most at this price. At 1.59kg it\'s portable for a 15-inch laptop. Wi-Fi 5 rather than 6 is a small compromise.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1404vap-2025\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VAP</a> costs the same with only 8GB. The <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</a> (Rs 89,999) has a far better screen and build, but only 8GB.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 2024 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pixels), 60Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7430U (7nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 58 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 5, Dual-band (2.4 / 5 GHz), Bluetooth 5.1</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.59 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why memory decides laptops under one lakh</h2>\n<p>Open Task Manager on a typical Windows 11 laptop with a browser, Teams and Word running, and you will see seven or eight gigabytes in use. On an 8GB machine that is the ceiling, and Windows starts borrowing from the SSD. Everything still works, only with a hesitation each time you switch windows. On 16GB there is no hesitation. That is the practical reason this Acer beats prettier laptops at the same price, and it is the figure we would check first on any laptop for college or office.</p>\n<h2>Six cores that do real work</h2>\n<p>The Ryzen 5 7430U has six cores and twelve threads. Compiling a class project, running a database in the background, joining a video call while sharing a spreadsheet, exporting a batch of photos: these are tasks where extra cores help, and where four-core chips such as the Ryzen 5 7520U fall behind. Its graphics are modest. Expect Valorant or Minecraft at reduced settings and no modern big-budget games.</p>\n<p>The chip is based on an older design, so it is not as efficient as the newest processors. The 58Wh battery makes up for much of that. A morning of lectures and an afternoon in the library is possible with sensible brightness.</p>\n<h2>Screen and sound</h2>\n<p>A 15.6-inch Full HD IPS panel is easy to read and can be viewed from the side when two people share it. Brightness is adequate for rooms and poor near a sunny window. Colour coverage is limited, as on almost all laptops at this price, so it is not for design work. The speakers are for voices. Use headphones for music.</p>\n<h2>Build, keyboard and weight</h2>\n<p>The body is plastic with a metal-look lid on some versions. It flexes a little if you press it, and it will survive normal use in a padded bag. The keyboard includes a number pad, which accounting and management students will value. At 1.59kg it is light for its size, lighter than many 14-inch machines from a few years ago.</p>\n<h2>Ports and upgrades</h2>\n<p>You get the usual USB-A, USB-C and HDMI connections, enough for a projector and a mouse without adapters. Ask the shop about memory and storage. Aspire Lite models often allow the SSD to be replaced, and some have a spare memory slot. The wireless card is Wi-Fi 5, which is fine for home fibre packages up to a few hundred megabits.</p>\n<h2>Windows and software</h2>\n<p>It comes with Windows 11 Home. Check that it is activated. Uninstall trial antivirus programs, since Windows Security is enough, and use LibreOffice or Microsoft 365 for documents. Set the laptop to update at night so that it does not restart in the middle of a class.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives near Rs 1 lakh</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 2024 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;97,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7430U (7nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 58 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-core-i5-1334u\">Dell 15 DC15250 (Core i5-1334U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;96,499</td><td>13th Generation Intel® Core™ i5-1334U (10 cores, up to 4.60...</td><td>3-Cell Battery, 41WHr (Integrated)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-go-14-e1404fa-2025\">ASUS Vivobook Go 14 E1404FA (Ryzen 3 30 / Ryzen 5 40)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;95,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen 3 30 (4C/8T, up to 4.1GHz) or Ryzen 5 40 (4C/8T, u...</td><td>42WHrs, 3S1P, 3-cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-pavilion-x360-14-2022\">HP Pavilion x360 14 (2022)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;95,000</td><td>Intel Core i5-1235U</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15irh10-2025-intel-core-i5-13420h\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IRH10 (Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,01,499</td><td>Intel Core i5 13420H Processor (8 Cores, 12 Threads, 12MB In...</td><td>47Wh Integrated Battery</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Dell\'s 15-inch models come with Intel chips and a better-known badge, usually with less memory for the same money. The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-42p-ryzen-5-7430u\">AL15-42P version</a> of the same Acer differs in details such as ports and finish, so compare the two. Convertible HP Pavilions at Rs 95,000 are from 2022, with touchscreens and older processors. The <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Zenbook 14 OLED</a> is a nicer object with half the memory.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>BBA, BCA, BIT and law students who need one laptop for four years.</li>\n<li>Office workers who live in browsers and spreadsheets.</li>\n<li>Families that share a computer for school, bank work and films.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Gamers and 3D students, who need a dedicated graphics card.</li>\n<li>Designers and photographers, who need a better screen.</li>\n<li>People who travel daily and want something small and solid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Extending its life</h2>\n<p>Three inexpensive steps keep a laptop like this useful into its fifth year. Add a second stick of memory if a slot is free. Replace the SSD with a 1TB drive when space runs low and use the old one as a backup in a USB case. And clean the fan and replace the thermal paste in year three. Together they cost less than a tenth of a new machine.</p>\n<h2>Power protection</h2>\n<p>Voltage swings are hard on chargers. Use a surge protector, unplug the charger in thunderstorms, and avoid charging from a cheap inverter that produces a rough waveform. If the charger is lost, buy a genuine 65W replacement. An underpowered one will slow the laptop and wear its battery.</p>\n<h2>Checking it in the shop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm the exact processor name in Settings, System, About.</li>\n<li>Look at the memory in Task Manager: 16GB, and whether it is one stick or two.</li>\n<li>Open a white page and a black one to look for bright spots.</li>\n<li>Test the webcam, microphone, Wi-Fi and every port.</li>\n<li>Ask for an official Acer warranty with the period on the bill.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>There is nothing glamorous about the Aspire Lite 15, and that is fine. It puts its budget into memory, cores, storage and battery, the four things that determine whether a laptop is still pleasant in 2030. Under one lakh, that makes it our first recommendation for people who have work to do.</p>',7.8,'16GB RAM and 512GB SSD under Rs 1 lakh\n6-core, 12-thread Ryzen 5\n58Wh battery\nLight for a 15.6-inch laptop','Plain 60Hz screen\nWi-Fi 5\nPlastic build','The laptop to buy for students and office work under Rs 1 lakh. It has the memory and processor to last four or five years.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(120,301,20,'Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415 Review: Premium Feel for Rs 89,999','asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415 costs <strong>Rs 89,999</strong> with an Intel Core Ultra 5 125H, 8GB of RAM and a 512GB SSD. You get an OLED screen and Zenbook build quality for under Rs 1 lakh.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 14-inch <strong>OLED</strong> screen has deep blacks and rich colours, far nicer for films and photos than the IPS panels on budget laptops. The Core Ultra 5 125H is an H-series chip, quicker than the U-series processors common at this price, with capable Intel Arc integrated graphics. Zenbooks are slim and light.</p>\n<h2>What to check</h2>\n<p><strong>8GB of RAM</strong> is the weak point. Zenbook memory is soldered, so you can\'t add more later, and Windows 11 with many tabs open will feel tight in a couple of years. The screen is Full HD at 60Hz. \"Q\" models are made for US retailers, so ask the shop who handles warranty and whether the keyboard has the layout you expect.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15</a> (Rs 97,999) has 16GB of RAM but a plain screen and plastic build. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> (Rs 1,34,999) is the step up if you want a premium thin laptop with official warranty.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>14-inch FHD OLED, 60Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 125H</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What OLED brings to a laptop</h2>\n<p>On an OLED screen each pixel makes its own light, so black is truly off and colours have depth that LCD panels cannot show. Films look better at once. So do photographs, slides and even text in dark mode. At under Rs 90,000 you normally get a dim IPS panel that covers two-thirds of the standard colour range. The Q415\'s screen is the single best reason to choose it.</p>\n<p>OLED has two weaknesses. Static elements such as the taskbar can leave a faint ghost after years of use, and Asus includes tools to reduce that: shift pixels, dim the screen when idle and use dark mode. Some people also notice flicker at low brightness, which Asus lets you reduce in its settings. Set these up on the first day.</p>\n<h2>An H-series chip in a thin body</h2>\n<p>The Core Ultra 5 125H has fourteen cores of different types and a decent integrated Arc graphics unit. It is quicker than the U-series chips found in most laptops at this price. Coding, photo editing and even light video work go well. Arc graphics can run older games and esports titles at low to medium settings. A neural unit speeds up background blur in video calls and similar effects.</p>\n<h2>Eight gigabytes is the limit you must accept</h2>\n<p>The memory is soldered. It can never be increased. Windows 11 with a browser and Office uses most of it, so heavy multitasking means waiting. For writing, streaming, research and one big application at a time, it is fine. For programmers who run containers, for designers with several Adobe apps open, or for anyone who keeps forty tabs, it is not. If that sounds like you, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Aspire Lite 15 with 16GB</a> is less beautiful and more useful.</p>\n<h2>Where it comes from, and why that matters</h2>\n<p>Asus uses Q model numbers for machines made for a large American retailer. Units in Nepal have been imported, often as open-box or refurbished stock. That explains the low price, and it raises questions to ask before paying:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is it new, open-box or refurbished? Look for signs of use around the ports and keys.</li>\n<li>Who provides the warranty? Asus Nepal may not cover it, so get the shop\'s terms in writing.</li>\n<li>Is the charger original, and does the plug need an adapter?</li>\n<li>Is Windows activated with a licence tied to the machine?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use MyAsus or a battery report to check health. In a command window, type powercfg /batteryreport, and open the file it creates. Compare full charge capacity with design capacity. Anything below ninety per cent on a supposedly new machine deserves an explanation.</p>\n<h2>Build and portability</h2>\n<p>Zenbooks have metal lids, slim frames and a hinge that lies nearly flat. The keyboard is comfortable with a number pad built into the touchpad on some versions. It slips into any bag and wakes quickly. Compared with a plastic 15-inch laptop at the same price, it feels like the next class up.</p>\n<h2>Battery life</h2>\n<p>Expect a working day of light use if you keep brightness at half and use dark themes, which save power on OLED. Heavy work on the H-series chip drains it faster. It charges by USB-C, so a good phone charger of 65W or a power bank with laptop support can rescue you.</p>\n<h2>How it compares</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 125H</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB or 16GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 7520U (up to 4.3 GHz max boost clock, 4 MB L3 c...</td><td>3-cell, 41 Wh Li-ion polymer</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-e1504fa-ryzen-5-40\">ASUS Vivobook 15 E1504FA (Ryzen 5 40, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,000</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 40 Processor (4 Cores, 8 Threads, 2.8GHz Base C...</td><td>42WHrs, 3S1P, 3-cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2022-i5-1235u\">MSI Modern 14 C12M 2022 (12th Gen i5-1235U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;92,000</td><td>Intel Core i5-1235U, 10 cores / 12 threads</td><td>3-cell 39Whr Li-Po; 65W Slim Tip</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15amn8-2023\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AMN8 (Ryzen 5 40, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;87,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 40 (4C / 8T, 2.8 / 4.3GHz, 2MB L2 / 4MB L3)</td><td>47Wh</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Most of its neighbours are ordinary plastic machines with IPS screens. A few offer 16GB. The <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-e1504fa-ryzen-5-40\">Vivobook 15 E1504FA</a> is a larger, simpler Asus with official supply. The <a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">MSI Modern 14</a> has a fast processor and a plain display. Decide whether your priority is screen and feel, or memory and warranty, and the choice makes itself.</p>\n<h2>Good for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Writers, journalists, lawyers and teachers who read all day.</li>\n<li>Students who watch lectures and films on the same machine.</li>\n<li>Travellers who want something light and handsome.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Not good for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Software developers and heavy multitaskers.</li>\n<li>Gamers.</li>\n<li>Anyone who needs official service in their city.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to get the most from 8GB</h2>\n<p>Use Edge or Chrome with sleeping tabs on. Close Teams and Spotify when you do not need them. Disable startup apps in Task Manager. Choose web versions over desktop apps for chat. Keep at least 60GB of the SSD free, so that Windows has room for its swap file. Set the visual effects to best performance if it feels heavy. With these habits most people will be comfortable.</p>\n<h2>An alternative view</h2>\n<p>For Rs 45,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> also has 8GB, with longer battery life and official support, though macOS makes better use of that memory. For Rs 8,000 more, the Aspire Lite doubles the memory. The Zenbook sits between them as the one you would rather look at.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If your work is reading, writing and watching, the Q415 gives you a lovely screen, a quick chip and a premium body for the price of a budget laptop. Inspect the unit, sort out the warranty, and accept the fixed memory. If any of those worries you, spend the money on something plainer.</p>',7.6,'OLED screen under Rs 1 lakh\nFast H-series Core Ultra 5\nPremium, slim Zenbook build\n512GB SSD','8GB RAM, soldered\n60Hz Full HD panel\nUS-market model: check warranty','Excellent value if you mostly browse, write and watch video, and confirm the warranty before buying. Heavy multitaskers should get the 16GB Aspire Lite 15.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(121,873,20,'Lenovo IdeaPad 1 (Ryzen 5 7520U) Review: The Rs 81,000 Student Laptop','lenovo-ideapad-1-ryzen-5-7520u-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Lenovo IdeaPad 1 15AMN7 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Lenovo IdeaPad 1 with an AMD Ryzen 5 7520U costs <strong>Rs 80,999</strong> with 8GB of RAM and a 256GB SSD. It\'s a no-frills 15.6-inch laptop for assignments, online classes and office work.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The Ryzen 5 7520U is a 4-core, 8-thread 6nm chip. It\'s efficient and noticeably quicker than the Intel N100 and Celeron chips in cheaper laptops. The screen is a Full HD IPS panel, so viewing angles are fine, unlike the TN panels in some budget models. At 1.58kg it\'s carryable.</p>\n<h2>Limits</h2>\n<p>Laptops with the 7520U usually have soldered memory, so the 8GB you buy is likely the 8GB you keep. Check with the shop before buying. A 256GB SSD fills up fast, the 42Wh battery is small, and it has Wi-Fi 5. The screen is 60Hz and fairly dim.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>For Rs 17,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15</a> doubles the RAM and storage and adds a 6-core chip. That\'s the better long-term buy if you can stretch.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Lenovo IdeaPad 1 15AMN7 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB/256GB)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6-inch FHD (1920 x 1080) IPS Display with 16:9 aspect ratio, anti-glare technology and 250nits brightness</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U (4 cores, 8 threads, up to 4.3GHz max turbo boost and 4MB L3 cache) processor</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB LPDDR5-5500MHz RAM</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB PCIe NVMe SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>HD 720p, with privacy shutter</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Integrated Li-Polymer 42Wh battery</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi® 5, 802.11ac 2x2 Wi-Fi® + Bluetooth® 5.1, M.2 Card</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.58kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What the price looks like now</h2>\n<p>The text above quotes Rs 80,999, while our latest record is Rs 84,499. Prices of basic laptops have moved up with memory and storage costs through 2026. Ask two or three shops, and compare versions carefully, since the same model is sold with 256GB and 512GB drives and it is easy to pay the price of one for the other.</p>\n<h2>A tool for school and office</h2>\n<p>Think of the IdeaPad 1 as the Maruti 800 of laptops: simple, cheap to run and good enough for the journey. It types, prints, browses, joins classes and plays films. It does these things without warming your lap or making noise. Asking for more than that is where disappointment begins.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the Ryzen 5 7520U</h2>\n<p>AMD\'s naming suggests a mid-range chip. In reality the 7520U is a four-core part with an older core design on a newer process, built for efficiency. It is far better than an N100 or a Celeron, roughly equal to a modern Core i3, and well behind six-core Ryzens such as the 7430U. You will feel comfortable with a dozen tabs and a document, and less so with thirty tabs and a video call.</p>\n<h2>Memory that stays as it is</h2>\n<p>The 8GB of LPDDR5 is soldered to the board on this design. It is fast, which helps the integrated graphics, but it is final. For the next three years of ordinary study this is workable. Beyond that, expect to feel the squeeze. If you can find a 16GB version for a modest premium, take it.</p>\n<h2>Storage</h2>\n<p>A 256GB SSD leaves about 180GB after Windows and Office. That is enough for documents and not for a film collection or games. The SSD can normally be replaced with a larger M.2 drive, so storage is the upgrade you can plan for. Until then a portable drive or cloud storage does the job. Our <a href=\"/products?category=storage\">storage page</a> lists current prices.</p>\n<h2>Screen and webcam</h2>\n<p>The 15.6-inch Full HD IPS panel is better than the TN screens in some cheaper laptops, because you can tilt it without losing the picture. Its brightness is listed at 250 nits, dim by modern standards, so avoid sitting with a window behind you. The 720p webcam has a physical privacy shutter, a good touch, and its picture is grainy in the evening. A cheap desk lamp in front of you will help more than any setting.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>At 42Wh it is small for a 15-inch laptop. The efficient chip stretches it to a reasonable afternoon of notes and browsing. Plan to carry the charger for a full day. Lenovo\'s Rapid Charge can add a couple of hours of use in fifteen to thirty minutes on supported models.</p>\n<h2>Build and keyboard</h2>\n<p>The plastic body is light at 1.58kg and the lid bends a little. Lenovo\'s keyboards are comfortable even on cheap machines, with a number pad here. There is usually no backlight, which hostel students may miss. Look for it in the version you buy.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lenovo IdeaPad 1 15AMN7 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB/256GB)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;84,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U (4 cores, 8 threads, up to 4.3GHz max turb...</td><td>Integrated Li-Polymer 42Wh battery</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-v15-g4-iru-2023\">Lenovo V15 G4 IRU 2023 (Intel i3-1315U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;84,000</td><td>Intel Core i3-1315U (Intel 7), 6 cores / 8 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 45 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15x-oled-x1503za-2022\">Asus Vivobook 15X OLED X1503ZA 2022 (Intel Core i3-1220P)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;83,900</td><td>Intel Core i3-1220P (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 70 Wh; 90W Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-15-pv15255-2025\">Dell Pro 15 PV15255 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;86,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U (4 cores, 8 threads, up to 4.3GHz)</td><td>3 Cell Battery, 41 Wh, integrated</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-32p-39zh-2024-intel-core-i3-n355\">Acer Aspire Lite 14 AL14-32P (Core i3 N355, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,499</td><td>Intel Core i3 N355, 8 cores, 8 Threads, 6 MB Cache, Base Fre...</td><td>Li-Ion 45 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15iru8-intel-core-i3-1315u\">IdeaPad Slim 3 with Core i3-1315U</a> costs slightly less with a faster processor for short tasks. The <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Vivobook 15 with a Core i5-1334U and 16GB</a>, where it is available for about Rs 80,000, is a much stronger laptop and shows how uneven prices can be. Always look for leftover stock of better-specified machines before settling on a new basic one.</p>\n<p>If the budget can stretch to Rs 98,000, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15</a> with 16GB and six cores will be useful for years longer.</p>\n<h2>Windows tips for a basic laptop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Remove trial software on the first day.</li>\n<li>Turn off startup programs you do not need.</li>\n<li>Use Edge with efficiency mode or Firefox with a light set of extensions.</li>\n<li>Keep 20 per cent of the drive free.</li>\n<li>Let Windows Update run overnight once a week.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Used business laptops: the honest alternative</h2>\n<p>For Rs 45,000 to 65,000 you can find used ThinkPads, Latitudes and EliteBooks with 16GB, better keyboards and metal bodies. They are two to four years old and come from offices abroad. A clean one can outlast a new budget laptop. The risks are worn batteries, missing warranty and hidden faults. If you know someone who can inspect it, consider that route. If not, new with a warranty is calmer.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy the IdeaPad 1</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>School and bachelor-level students with a fixed budget.</li>\n<li>Small offices that need typing, email and billing.</li>\n<li>Parents who want a computer for bank work and video calls.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should wait or stretch</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>IT, engineering and design students.</li>\n<li>Anyone who expects to keep it beyond four years.</li>\n<li>Gamers and video editors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>As a first laptop, it is decent and unexciting. The processor is fine for study, the screen is fair, and the fixed memory sets a clear end date. If you find it near Rs 80,000 and need a computer now, it will serve. If you can wait for a sale or add Rs 15,000, you will be rewarded for years.</p>',7.0,'Efficient Ryzen 5 7520U\nFull HD IPS screen\nReasonable 1.58kg\nAffordable for a Ryzen 5','8GB RAM, usually not upgradeable\n256GB storage\nSmall 42Wh battery, Wi-Fi 5','Fine for school and office work on a tight budget. If you can find Rs 17,000 more, the Aspire Lite 15 will last much longer.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(122,299,20,'Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VAP Review: Fine, but Rs 97,999 Buys More','asus-vivobook-14-x1404vap-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VAP yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VAP costs <strong>Rs 97,999</strong> with an Intel Core 5 120U, 8GB of RAM and a 512GB SSD. It\'s a compact 14-inch laptop for study and office use.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The Core 5 120U is a 10-core chip that\'s quick for office apps, browsing and video calls. A 512GB SSD gives plenty of room, and the 14-inch size is easier to carry to college than a 15.6-inch laptop. The Full HD IPS screen has decent viewing angles.</p>\n<h2>What\'s not</h2>\n<p>At just under Rs 1 lakh, <strong>8GB of RAM</strong> is the issue. Windows 11 with a browser, Teams and a few documents open will already use most of it. The design and screen are basic for the price.</p>\n<h2>Rivals at the same price</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15</a> costs the same with 16GB of RAM and a 6-core Ryzen 5. The <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</a> costs Rs 8,000 less and adds an OLED screen and a faster H-series chip.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>ASUS Vivobook 14 X1404VAP (Core 5 120U, 8GB/256GB)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>14&quot; (1920 x 1080) FHD 60Hz, 250 nits, 45% NTSC, 83% Screen to Body Ratio</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core 5 Processor Series 1 120U 10 Cores (2P + 8E), 12 Threads, 12MB Cache</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB DDR4</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB NVMe PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>720p HD Camera</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>42 Wh, 3 Cell Battery</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 6(802.11ax) (Dual band) 1*1, Bluetooth v5.2</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.4 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Two configurations, one name</h2>\n<p>There is a trap here. The review text describes a version with a 512GB SSD at Rs 97,999. Our latest listing, at Rs 87,000, has 8GB of memory and a 256GB SSD. Both are called Vivobook 14 X1404VAP and both have the Core 5 120U. Read the sticker under the laptop or check Settings before paying, and judge the price against the version in front of you. At Rs 87,000 with 256GB it is an average deal. At Rs 97,999 it was a poor one even with 512GB.</p>\n<h2>The processor is better than the rest</h2>\n<p>The Core 5 120U has two fast cores and eight efficient ones, reaching high speeds for short tasks. Opening programs, loading web pages and working in spreadsheets feel quick. It is a low-power part, so long jobs such as video exports take their time. The integrated graphics are for video and simple games.</p>\n<p>It is frustrating to see a chip like this tied to 8GB of memory. With 16GB it would be one of the better student laptops in the country. Ask the seller to open the bottom or check the service manual for this model: many X1404 boards have some memory soldered and one free slot. If a slot is free, adding an 8GB stick turns the machine around for a few thousand rupees.</p>\n<h2>Screen: the part to see in person</h2>\n<p>The panel is Full HD at 60Hz with 250 nits and 45 per cent NTSC coverage. Translated, that means usable for documents, dull for photos and weak near a window. Colours look pale if you are used to a phone\'s AMOLED. If you study late at night in a hostel this will not trouble you. If you edit pictures for a page or a shop, look elsewhere or connect a monitor.</p>\n<h2>Size and comfort</h2>\n<p>A 14-inch machine of 1.4kg is the right size for a backpack. Asus gives it a comfortable keyboard, a fingerprint sensor on many units and a webcam shutter. The hinge opens flat, which is nice for sharing a screen at a table. The body is plastic and meets some military test items for shock and temperature, according to Asus.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>With 42Wh you can expect half a day of mixed work. The chip is efficient at idle, so reading and typing stretch it. A 45W or 65W USB-C charger can top it up if the model supports charging through that port. Confirm it.</p>\n<h2>Its rivals tell the story</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ASUS Vivobook 14 X1404VAP (Core 5 120U, 8GB/256GB)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;87,000</td><td>Intel Core 5 Processor Series 1 120U 10 Cores (2P + 8E), 12...</td><td>42 Wh, 3 Cell Battery</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15amn8-2023\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AMN8 (Ryzen 5 40, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;87,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 40 (4C / 8T, 2.8 / 4.3GHz, 2MB L2 / 4MB L3)</td><td>47Wh</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-15-pv15255-2025\">Dell Pro 15 PV15255 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;86,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U (4 cores, 8 threads, up to 4.3GHz)</td><td>3 Cell Battery, 41 Wh, integrated</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-e1504fa-ryzen-5-40\">ASUS Vivobook 15 E1504FA (Ryzen 5 40, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,000</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 40 Processor (4 Cores, 8 Threads, 2.8GHz Base C...</td><td>42WHrs, 3S1P, 3-cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB or 16GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 7520U (up to 4.3 GHz max boost clock, 4 MB L3 c...</td><td>3-cell, 41 Wh Li-ion polymer</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>At almost the same price, the <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</a> gives you a far better screen and a stronger chip with imported-unit questions. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15amn8-2023\">IdeaPad Slim 3</a> is larger with a Ryzen chip. The <a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15</a> is another plain machine. For about Rs 11,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15</a> offers 16GB and 512GB, and it is the one we recommend.</p>\n<h2>When the Vivobook 14 is the right answer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You want a small laptop for college and cannot go above Rs 90,000.</li>\n<li>You have confirmed a free memory slot and plan to add a stick.</li>\n<li>You value Asus\'s local service and an official warranty over a fancier import.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When it is not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You will not upgrade it and you multitask.</li>\n<li>You care about the screen.</li>\n<li>You are being charged close to a lakh.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to do with 256GB</h2>\n<p>After Windows, Office and a few programs, you have about 170GB. Keep coursework in OneDrive or Google Drive, store films on a portable drive, and uninstall games when you finish them. Replacing the SSD with a 1TB drive later is straightforward on this model and takes twenty minutes with a screwdriver and a USB stick with Windows on it.</p>\n<h2>Asus extras worth using</h2>\n<p>MyAsus lets you limit charging to 60 or 80 per cent, which extends battery life if the laptop is mostly on a desk. It also has fan modes, noise reduction for calls and a diagnostic tool. Register the warranty in the same app. If you get an option of extended or accidental cover in the first weeks, students should consider it.</p>\n<h2>Core 5 120U or Ryzen 5 7430U?</h2>\n<p>People ask this because the two are found in laptops at similar prices. For short, single tasks the Intel chip feels snappier. For work that uses many cores at once, the Ryzen keeps up better. In daily life the difference between 8GB and 16GB is much larger than the difference between these processors. Choose by memory first.</p>\n<h2>A quick inspection routine</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check processor, memory and drive size in Settings.</li>\n<li>Run a battery report and compare capacities.</li>\n<li>Test the keyboard, touchpad, webcam and ports.</li>\n<li>Look for pressure marks on the screen with a plain grey image.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>A quick processor and a handy size are held back by a dull screen and too little memory. It is an acceptable laptop at the right price with a small upgrade, and an expensive one otherwise. Let the version and the price decide, and do not pay a lakh for 8GB.</p>',6.8,'Quick Core 5 120U for office work\n512GB SSD\nCompact 14-inch size','Only 8GB RAM near Rs 1 lakh\nBasic screen and design\nRivals offer more at the same price','Nothing wrong with it, but at this price the Aspire Lite 15 (16GB) or the Zenbook 14 OLED is the better buy.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(123,304,20,'Asus Vivobook 14 (Core i3-1315U) Review: 128GB Holds It Back','asus-vivobook-14-x1404va-i3-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VA yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VA costs <strong>Rs 70,999</strong> with an Intel Core i3-1315U, 8GB of RAM and a <strong>128GB SSD</strong>. It\'s a 14-inch Full HD laptop for basic study and office use.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The Core i3-1315U handles Word, Excel, browsing and online classes without trouble, and 8GB of RAM is workable for that kind of use. The 14-inch size is easy to carry.</p>\n<h2>The problem</h2>\n<p>A 128GB SSD is too small in 2026. After Windows 11 and its updates you\'ll have well under 100GB left, which fills quickly with Office, a few apps and downloads. You\'ll need to replace the SSD or rely on cloud and external storage.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>For Rs 10,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-1-15amn7-2023-ryzen-5-7520u\">Lenovo IdeaPad 1 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a> has a faster chip and twice the storage. Under Rs 60,000, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ian8-2023-intel-n100\">IdeaPad Slim 3 (N100)</a> costs less but is weaker still.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VA 2023 (Intel Core i3-1315U)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pixels), 60Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core i3-1315U (Intel 7), 6 cores / 8 threads</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>WiFi 5, Dual-band (2.4 / 5 GHz), Bluetooth 5.1</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.4 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The 128GB problem in detail</h2>\n<p>A new Windows 11 installation takes around 30GB, updates and recovery files take more, and Windows needs free space to install its yearly version. Add Office, a browser profile, Zoom and a PDF reader, and a 128GB drive has perhaps 60GB left. That is before your files. When free space falls below about 15GB, updates fail and the laptop slows. Many owners of 128GB laptops meet that point in the first year.</p>\n<p>The fix is cheap. The drive is a standard M.2 module on this model. A 512GB NVMe SSD costs a few thousand rupees, and a technician can clone the old one onto it in an hour. So think of the price not as Rs 70,999 but as Rs 70,999 plus the cost of a drive. On that basis, compare it with laptops that start with 256GB or 512GB.</p>\n<h2>The processor is fine</h2>\n<p>The Core i3-1315U has two performance cores and four efficient ones. It handles Word, Excel, browsing, Zoom and YouTube smoothly, and it is faster than many older Core i5 chips. For schoolwork and office tasks nobody needs more. Memory, at 8GB, is the next limit, and is often soldered with one spare slot on this series. Ask.</p>\n<h2>Using it every day</h2>\n<p>At 1.4kg and 14 inches it is easy to carry. The Full HD screen is sharp enough, with the pale colours and modest brightness that are standard at this price. The keyboard is good, and the fingerprint reader on some versions speeds up sign-in. Asus\'s hinge and build are better than they look. The 42Wh battery gives an afternoon of work.</p>\n<h2>Who this laptop suits</h2>\n<p>It suits people whose work lives online. A teacher using Google Classroom, a small business using online accounting, a student writing in Google Docs and streaming lectures can live happily with 128GB, because almost nothing is stored locally. It also suits buyers who already have a spare SSD or know how to fit one.</p>\n<h2>Who it does not suit</h2>\n<p>Anyone who downloads films, edits photos, installs games or keeps a phone backup on the computer will run out of room. Engineering and IT students need more memory and cores as well.</p>\n<h2>Other options near Rs 71,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VA 2023 (Intel Core i3-1315U)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;70,999</td><td>Intel Core i3-1315U (Intel 7), 6 cores / 8 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-15-3520\">Dell Inspiron 15 3520</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>Intel Core i5-1235U</td><td>54Wh, 65W Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-45p-ryzen-3-5400u\">Acer Aspire Lite 14 AL14-45P (Ryzen 3 5400U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;73,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 3 5400U Processor (4 Cores / 8 Threads, up to 4.0...</td><td>58Wh, 5100mAh, 11.4V, 3-Cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-pavilion-15\">HP Pavilion 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7530U</td><td>41Wh, up to 8.5 hours</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-go-15-e1504ga-2023\">Asus Vivobook Go 15 E1504GA 2023 (Intel Core i3-N305)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>Intel Core i3-N305 (Intel 7), 8 cores / 8 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-45p-ryzen-3-5400u\">Acer Aspire Lite 14</a> with a Ryzen 3 and 512GB costs Rs 3,000 more and solves the storage problem. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-14iru8-core-i3-1315u\">IdeaPad Slim 3 14</a> has the same Core i3 with a better configuration at about Rs 75,000. The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-15-ag15-32p-39r2-2025-intel-core-3-n355-8gb-256gb-156-fhd\">Aspire Go 15</a> has a larger battery and 256GB with a slower chip. The <a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-15-3520\">Inspiron 15 3520</a> is a bigger machine with good service. For most people, one of these is the better purchase.</p>\n<h2>The upgrade plan, with numbers</h2>\n<p>If you get the Vivobook at a discount, say Rs 62,000 to 65,000, then a 512GB SSD and, if there is a slot, an 8GB memory stick bring the total to around Rs 75,000. For that you have a Core i3, 16GB and 512GB in a light body, which beats everything new at the price. That is the only scenario in which we would recommend it. Keep the old SSD in a USB enclosure as a backup.</p>\n<p>Opening the laptop may affect warranty terms, so ask Asus service to fit the parts or to confirm in writing that a standard upgrade is allowed.</p>\n<h2>How to stretch 128GB until you upgrade</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Storage Sense to delete temporary files automatically.</li>\n<li>Set OneDrive to online-only files.</li>\n<li>Turn off hibernation to regain several gigabytes.</li>\n<li>Install programs you seldom use on a memory card or an external drive, or use web versions.</li>\n<li>Move the Downloads folder to external storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Consider a Chromebook-style approach</h2>\n<p>If your needs are online, ChromeOS Flex or a light Linux system can make a low-storage laptop feel fresh. They need little space and start quickly. Some Windows-only programs will not work, so this is a choice for confident users. An inexpensive tablet with a keyboard is another path for reading and typing, as we discuss in <a href=\"/guides/laptop-vs-tablet-work-study-nepal\">laptop or tablet for study</a>.</p>\n<h2>Checks before buying</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm drive size. Some sellers advertise the 512GB version\'s price and photo.</li>\n<li>Look inside Task Manager for memory slots used.</li>\n<li>Check the battery report.</li>\n<li>Make sure the warranty is Asus\'s own.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Asus has official service in Nepal and parts for the Vivobook line are widely available. That is a fair advantage over unknown imports.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>A capable little laptop has been sold with a drive that belongs in 2015. At its listed price there are better choices on the same shelf. At a proper discount, with a new SSD, it becomes one of the more sensible student machines around. Do the sum before you decide, and do not let a low sticker price hide the second purchase.</p>\n<h2>What the upgrade day looks like</h2>\n<p>Back up your files, take the laptop and a new NVMe drive to a service centre, and ask for a clean installation of Windows with drivers, not a clone. A clean start avoids carrying over trial software. It takes about two hours. Windows activates by itself, since the licence is stored in the laptop. Put the old 128GB drive in a USB case for Rs 800 and use it for backups. If a memory slot is free, have the extra stick fitted in the same visit and save a second trip.</p>',6.2,'Capable Core i3 for office work\n8GB RAM\nPortable 14-inch size','128GB SSD is too small\nBasic screen and build\nIdeaPad 1 Ryzen 5 is better for Rs 10,000 more','Only worth it if you plan to upgrade the SSD yourself. Otherwise spend Rs 10,000 more on the IdeaPad 1 Ryzen 5.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(124,708,20,'Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 (Intel N100) Review: Only for the Lightest Use','lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-n100-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IAN8 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 with an Intel N100 is one of the cheapest new Windows laptops in Nepal at <strong>Rs 54,999</strong>, for <strong>4GB of RAM and a 128GB SSD</strong>.</p>\n<h2>What it can do</h2>\n<p>The Intel N100 is a low-power 4-core chip. It handles a browser with a few tabs, Google Docs, YouTube and Zoom classes. The 47Wh battery and efficient chip give reasonable battery life, and it has Wi-Fi 6. At 1.55kg it\'s light for a 15.6-inch laptop.</p>\n<h2>Why we can\'t recommend it for most people</h2>\n<p><strong>4GB of RAM is too little for Windows 11.</strong> Expect slowdowns with more than a handful of tabs, and check before buying whether the memory can be upgraded, since many laptops in this class have it soldered. 128GB of storage fills quickly. The 15.6-inch screen is a <strong>TN panel</strong>, so colours shift when you look at it from an angle.</p>\n<h2>What to buy instead</h2>\n<p>If you can stretch to Rs 81,000, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-1-15amn7-2023-ryzen-5-7520u\">IdeaPad 1 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a> doubles the RAM and storage, with a better IPS screen and a much faster chip. If not, a good tablet with a keyboard may serve light study better than this laptop.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IAN8 2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6 inches, TN LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pixels), 60Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel N100 (Intel 7), 4 cores / 4 threads (base configuration; see prices for other chips)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>4GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>128GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 47 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>WiFi 6, Dual-band (2.4 / 5 GHz), Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.55 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why we scored it so low</h2>\n<p>A low score does not mean a faulty product. It means that the money is likely to be regretted. Windows 11 officially asks for 4GB of memory as a minimum, in the same way that a bus needs four wheels as a minimum. With the system running, about a gigabyte is left for everything else. A browser with a few tabs is enough to send the laptop into constant swapping with its drive. You wait for the start menu, then for the browser, then for the page.</p>\n<p>At Rs 54,999 this is not a throwaway sum. A student who struggles with it for a year and then buys a better laptop has paid twice.</p>\n<h2>What the N100 does well</h2>\n<p>The chip is more capable than its name suggests. It has four efficient cores that play high-resolution video without effort, sip power and stay cool, usually without needing a loud fan. Put 8GB of memory beside it and it makes a pleasant basic computer. Our variant table shows a version with a Core i3-N305, 8GB and 256GB at Rs 67,999. That one is worth considering. The 4GB/128GB one is not.</p>\n<h2>Can the memory be upgraded?</h2>\n<p>This is the first question to ask the shop. On some IdeaPad Slim 3 models the memory is soldered, and on others there is a slot. If yours has a slot, replacing or adding a stick to reach 8GB or more would change our opinion of the machine. Do not accept \"probably\". Ask the seller to check the model code in Lenovo\'s PSREF document, or open it in front of you.</p>\n<h2>The TN screen</h2>\n<p>The 15.6-inch Full HD panel uses the older TN type. Seen straight on, it is acceptable. Tilt it, and colours invert or fade. Two people cannot comfortably watch the same film. For spreadsheets and text it does the job.</p>\n<h2>Storage</h2>\n<p>With 128GB you face the same trouble we describe in our <a href=\"/reviews/asus-vivobook-14-x1404va-i3-review-nepal\">Vivobook Core i3 review</a>: updates need room, and there is little to spare. An SSD upgrade is usually possible.</p>\n<h2>Realistic uses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A reception desk running a single billing page.</li>\n<li>A typing-practice machine for a training centre.</li>\n<li>A kitchen or prayer-room screen for YouTube.</li>\n<li>A light Linux computer for a hobbyist.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With Linux Mint or ChromeOS Flex in place of Windows, 4GB goes further, and the laptop becomes quite usable for browsing, documents and video. That is an honest way to give it a good life, as long as you do not need Windows programs.</p>\n<h2>Better ways to spend Rs 55,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IAN8 2023</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>Intel N100 (Intel 7), 4 cores / 4 threads (base configuratio...</td><td>Li-Ion 47 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-notebook-14-em0002wm-2023-amd-athlon-silver-7120u\">HP Notebook 14-em0002wm 2023 (AMD Athlon Silver 7120U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>AMD Athlon Silver 7120U (6nm), 2 cores / 2 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 45 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-wyse-5470-2022-celeron-n4100\">Dell Wyse 5470 2022 (Intel Celeron N4100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;58,999</td><td>Intel Celeron N4100 (14nm), 4 cores / 4 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-v15-g6-2025\">Lenovo V15 G6 2025 (Intel N100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;62,999</td><td>Intel N100 (Intel 7), 4 cores / 4 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 38 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-go-14-e410ka-2023-intel-celeron-n4500-8gb-256gb-14-fhd\">Asus Vivobook Go 14 E410KA 2023 (Intel Celeron N4500)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;45,999</td><td>Intel Celeron N4500 (10nm), 2 cores / 2 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 33W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The list of new laptops at this price is short and weak. Think wider.</p>\n<p><strong>A used business laptop.</strong> ThinkPad, Latitude and EliteBook models with a Core i5, 16GB and 256GB or more appear in the used market at around this price. They have better keyboards, screens and bodies. Have the battery and hinges checked and ask for at least a short shop warranty.</p>\n<p><strong>A tablet with a keyboard.</strong> For reading, notes and online classes, an 11-inch tablet with a Bluetooth keyboard is quicker and lasts longer. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-50000-nepal\">tablets under Rs 50,000</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Waiting two or three months.</strong> At Rs 70,000 to 75,000 you reach laptops with 8GB and 256GB or 512GB that will last through a degree. The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-45p-ryzen-3-5400u\">Acer Aspire Lite 14</a> is one of them. EMI can bridge the gap if the total cost is fair.</p>\n<p><strong>A desktop for home.</strong> If the computer will never move, a small desktop or a mini PC with a monitor gives far more power per rupee. Our <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a> can plan one.</p>\n<h2>If you already bought one</h2>\n<p>Do not despair. First, check for a memory slot and go to 8GB if you can. Second, install a 256GB or 512GB SSD. Third, remove everything that starts with Windows. Fourth, use a light browser such as Edge in efficiency mode with no more than five tabs. Fifth, use web versions of Office. Those steps can make it tolerable.</p>\n<h2>Advice for schools and offices</h2>\n<p>Cheap machines bought in bulk often cost more in the end, since staff time is lost and replacements come early. If you are buying ten computers for a lab, ask suppliers for refurbished business desktops with 8GB and SSDs. They are faster, easier to repair and frequently cheaper than new laptops of this class.</p>\n<h2>Who should consider it despite everything</h2>\n<p>Someone with a precise, tiny need and no other option, or someone who plans to run Linux. Everyone else should treat the Rs 54,999 sticker as a warning sign. The price looks like a bargain because the parts that make a laptop pleasant have been removed.</p>\n<h2>The rule to remember</h2>\n<p>For Windows in 2026: 8GB of memory is the floor, 16GB is comfortable, 256GB of storage is the floor, and an IPS screen is worth asking for. If a new laptop misses two of those, as this one misses three, leave it in the shop.</p>',5.6,'Very low price for a new Windows laptop\nWi-Fi 6\nLight at 1.55kg','4GB RAM is too little for Windows 11\n128GB SSD\nTN screen with poor viewing angles\nSlow for anything beyond basics','Only for very light use on the tightest budget. Saving for the IdeaPad 1 Ryzen 5 gives a far better laptop.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(125,122,20,'Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition Review: The Best Thin Windows Laptop Under Rs 1.7 Lakh','lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition costs <strong>Rs 1,64,999</strong> with an Intel Core Ultra 5 226V, 16GB of fast LPDDR5x memory and a 512GB SSD. It\'s the thin-and-light Windows laptop we\'d recommend to most students and office workers with this budget.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 14-inch WUXGA <strong>OLED</strong> covers 100% of the DCI-P3 colour range and reaches 600 nits. It\'s excellent for photos, video and long hours of reading, and much better than the IPS screens on most laptops at this price.</p>\n<h2>Performance and battery</h2>\n<p>The Core Ultra 5 226V is from Intel\'s efficiency-focused Lunar Lake generation, so it\'s quick for office work, coding and light photo editing, with very good battery life for a Windows laptop. The integrated Arc 130V graphics can handle light creative work, not serious gaming. The 16GB of memory is built into the processor package, so it can\'t be upgraded. 16GB is enough for most people for years.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> (Rs 1,34,999) is cheaper but has only 8GB. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air M5</a> costs Rs 70,000 more. For gaming at this price you need a different kind of laptop, such as the <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-2025-13th-gen-i7-13620h-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb\">Acer Nitro Lite 16</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>14-inch WUXGA OLED, 600 nits, 100% DCI-P3</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 226V (Intel Arc 130V graphics)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB LPDDR5x-8533</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The case for a quiet premium laptop</h2>\n<p>Between the budget machines at one lakh and the showpieces at two and a half there is a gap that few laptops fill well. The Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition does. It costs Rs 1,64,999, weighs little, has a beautiful screen and lasts through a working day. For lawyers, accountants, researchers, managers and postgraduate students, that combination is more valuable than a graphics card.</p>\n<h2>Lunar Lake, explained simply</h2>\n<p>Intel designed the Core Ultra 200V chips around efficiency. Memory sits on the same package as the processor, background tasks run on low-power cores, and standby uses very little. The result is a Windows laptop that behaves more like a MacBook: it wakes quickly, stays cool on your lap and keeps its charge overnight. The Core Ultra 5 226V is the entry model, and for office work you will not feel any lack.</p>\n<p>Two limits follow from the design. The memory cannot be changed, so 16GB is what you have for life. And heavy multi-core work, such as compiling a large code base or rendering, is slower than on H-series chips that use more power. Choose according to your work.</p>\n<h2>The OLED screen</h2>\n<p>A 14-inch panel with 600 nits and full DCI-P3 colour is far above the norm. Reading is comfortable, photographs look right, and films are a pleasure. Editors of pictures and short videos can trust the colours. The 16:10 shape shows more of each page.</p>\n<p>As with every OLED, use dark mode, hide the taskbar and let the pixel care run, to avoid uneven wear after some years.</p>\n<h2>Battery life in practice</h2>\n<p>With moderate brightness and typical office tools, a full working day away from a socket is realistic, and light use goes further. That is an advantage in a city with power cuts, and for people who travel between meetings. It charges by USB-C, so one charger can serve your phone as well.</p>\n<h2>Webcam, sound and keyboard</h2>\n<p>Lenovo\'s premium models have sharp webcams with a physical shutter, quality microphones and speakers that sound fuller than you expect from a thin machine. The keyboard has enough travel for long typing. Aura Edition features such as modes for focus, privacy and wellness can be ignored if you prefer a plain laptop.</p>\n<h2>Gaming and creative work</h2>\n<p>The integrated Arc 130V graphics can run older and lighter games at reduced settings. It can edit 4K clips on a short timeline. It is not for modern games or 3D work. If that is what you need at this price, a gaming laptop such as the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-15ahp9-2024-ryzen-7-7445hs\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> costs the same and is the opposite kind of machine: heavy, loud and powerful.</p>\n<h2>Compared with others at Rs 1,65,000</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 226V (Intel Arc 130V graphics)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5x-8533</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">HP Spectre x360 14-EU0060CA 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155H (Intel 4), 14 cores / 18 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 68 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-14-plus-2-in-1-db04250-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-1tb-14-fhd\">Dell 14 Plus 2-in-1 DB04250 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 256V)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 256V (TSMC N3B), 8 cores / 8 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 64 Wh; 65W adapter, Supports ExpressCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-15ahp9-2024-ryzen-7-7445hs\">Lenovo LOQ 15AHP9 (Ryzen 7 7445HS, RTX 3050 6GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,499</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 7 7445HS (6C / 12T, 3.55 / 4.7GHz, 6MB L2 / 16MB...</td><td>60Wh</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx607-2024\">Asus TUF Gaming F16 FX607 (2024)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,65,600</td><td>Intel Core 5 210H</td><td>8GB / 16GB</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">HP Spectre x360 14</a> is a luxurious convertible with a sharper OLED and an older, thirstier chip. The <a href=\"/products/dell-14-plus-2-in-1-db04250-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-1tb-14-fhd\">Dell 14 Plus 2-in-1</a> has a faster chip of the same family, twice the storage and a plain screen that folds. If you value a pen or tent mode, choose one of those. If you want the best screen and battery in a normal laptop, the Yoga wins.</p>\n<p>Two more to consider. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air M5</a> costs far more and lasts longer with macOS. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> is cheaper with half the memory.</p>\n<h2>Storage: plan ahead</h2>\n<p>Five hundred gigabytes is enough for documents and photos. Video makers will need an external SSD. The internal drive can be replaced on many Yoga models, though it may be a short 2242 size. Check before buying a spare.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and checking the unit</h2>\n<p>Premium imports appear in the market beside official stock. Enter the serial number on Lenovo\'s support site to see where the laptop was sold and which warranty applies. Ask the seller to write the period and provider on the bill. Look for the correct keyboard layout, a Windows licence that activates by itself, and a battery report close to design capacity.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Professionals who carry a laptop daily.</li>\n<li>Postgraduate students who read and write for hours.</li>\n<li>Photographers who need colour on the road.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who it does not suit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Gamers and 3D artists.</li>\n<li>Developers who need 32GB or more.</li>\n<li>Buyers who want to upgrade parts later.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Set it up well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Turn on conservation mode in Lenovo Vantage if you are mostly on a desk.</li>\n<li>Use 60 per cent brightness indoors, which is plenty on this screen.</li>\n<li>Set the power button to sleep and let the laptop wake by lid.</li>\n<li>Back up to an external drive or cloud from the first day.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Is it worth the jump from a one-lakh laptop?</h2>\n<p>If you use a computer for six or more hours a day, yes. The screen, battery, keyboard and weight pay you back in comfort, and a better-built machine lasts longer. If you use one for an hour in the evening, a cheaper laptop does the same work and leaves you Rs 65,000 for other things.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Not flashy and not cheap, the Yoga Slim 7i Aura is one of those rare laptops that get out of your way. It is the thin Windows machine we would choose for real work at this price, provided your idea of real work does not include rendering or games.</p>',8.5,'14-inch OLED with full DCI-P3 colour\nEfficient Core Ultra 5 226V: long battery life\n16GB fast LPDDR5x\nThin and light build','Memory can\'t be upgraded\nNot for gaming\n512GB fills up for video work','The best all-round thin Windows laptop around Rs 1.65 lakh for students, writers and office workers.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(126,848,20,'Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 (Ryzen 7 8840HS) Review: Most Power per Rupee','lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-ryzen-7-8840hs-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AHP10 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 with an AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS costs <strong>Rs 1,31,999</strong> with 16GB of RAM, a 512GB SSD and a 15.3-inch touchscreen. It\'s the most processor you can get in a non-gaming laptop at this price.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>The Ryzen 7 8840HS has <strong>8 cores and 16 threads</strong> and is built on a 4nm process. That\'s real power for programming, data work, compiling and photo or light video editing. Its integrated Radeon 780M graphics can also run esports games and older titles at modest settings, which most integrated graphics can\'t.</p>\n<h2>The rest</h2>\n<p>The 15.3-inch WUXGA (16:10) IPS touchscreen gives more vertical room for documents and code than a standard 16:9 panel. It has Wi-Fi 6 and weighs 1.59kg. The weak points: the screen is 60Hz and not especially bright, and the 50Wh battery is modest for a chip this powerful.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> (Rs 1,64,999) has a much better OLED screen and battery life but less raw power. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> costs about the same with only 8GB.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AHP10 2025 (Ryzen 7 8840HS)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.3 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1900 x 1200 pixels), 60Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 6, Dual-band (2.4 / 5 GHz), Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.59 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A sleeper in a plain body</h2>\n<p>From the outside this is an ordinary IdeaPad. Inside sits a chip that was used in premium and gaming laptops a year earlier. The Ryzen 7 8840HS has eight Zen 4 cores and sixteen threads, and it runs in this thin chassis at moderate power. For students who compile code, run simulations, train small models or process data, it is the cheapest way to get this much speed in a new laptop.</p>\n<h2>What eight cores change</h2>\n<p>Consider a third-year computing student. Android Studio with an emulator, a database and a browser can bring a four-core laptop to its knees. With eight cores and 16GB, it is workable. A civil engineering student running structural analysis, a data student with large Excel and Python files, a media student exporting short films: each waits less. Over four years, less waiting is worth money.</p>\n<h2>Radeon 780M: surprisingly able</h2>\n<p>The integrated graphics are the best AMD offered in this generation. Valorant, CS2 at low settings, Rocket League, Minecraft with modest shaders, GTA V at medium and many indie games run smoothly at 1080p. It depends on memory speed, so check whether the laptop has two sticks or one. Dual-channel memory can raise frame rates by a third or more.</p>\n<p>New big-budget games are out of reach. If those matter, look at laptops with an RTX chip from about Rs 1,50,000.</p>\n<h2>The screen</h2>\n<p>At 15.3 inches with a 16:10 shape and touch, it shows more of a document than a normal 15.6-inch panel. Touch is useful for scrolling and signing, less so for drawing. It runs at 60Hz, with average brightness and colour. It is the part where Lenovo saved money, and for code and text it is fine.</p>\n<h2>Battery and heat</h2>\n<p>A 50Wh battery is small for an HS-class chip. In light use, the laptop is efficient and lasts an afternoon. Under heavy load it drains fast and the fan becomes audible. Use the quiet or balanced mode for classes and performance mode when plugged in. A thin chassis cannot keep maximum speed for long, so a thirty-minute render will settle below its peak.</p>\n<h2>Build and weight</h2>\n<p>At 1.59kg it is easy to carry. The shell is a mix of plastic and metal depending on colour, and the keyboard is comfortable with a number pad. Wi-Fi 6, USB-C with charging and display output, HDMI and a card reader on most versions cover the needs of a student.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage</h2>\n<p>Sixteen gigabytes is right. Find out whether it is soldered or in slots, as this differs between IdeaPad versions. If there is a free slot, going to 24GB or 32GB later will keep the machine current. The SSD is replaceable.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives near Rs 1.3 lakh</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AHP10 2025 (Ryzen 7 8840HS)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-5441-2024-snapdragon-x-plus-x1p-42-100\">Dell Inspiron 14 5441 2024 (Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100 (3nm), 8 cores / 8 thr...</td><td>Li-Ion 54 Wh; 65W adapter, Supports ExpressCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-sfl14-41m-r4qm-2025-amd-ryzen-7-260\">Acer Swift Lite 14 SFL14-41M-R4QM 2025 (Ryzen 7 260)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,32,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 260 (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,499</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td><td>8GB unified memory</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-envy-x360-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">HP Envy x360 ES1023DX 2024 (Intel Core 7 150U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,30,000</td><td>Intel Core 7 Processor 150U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 59 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> is quieter, lasts longer and has 8GB. The <a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-sfl14-41m-r4qm-2025-amd-ryzen-7-260\">Acer Swift Lite 14</a> with a Ryzen 7 260 uses a refreshed version of the same chip in a smaller body, so compare price and screen. The <a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">EliteBook 655</a> has a stronger business build and a slower processor. The Dell with Snapdragon X is the battery champion, although Arm compatibility is a concern for engineering software, as we explain in <a href=\"/news/qualcomm-oryon-snapdragon-x-elite-laptops-nepal-battery-benchmarks\">our Snapdragon X article</a>.</p>\n<h2>For which courses?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Computer engineering, BCA, CSIT:</strong> excellent.</li>\n<li><strong>Civil and electrical engineering:</strong> very good for AutoCAD 2D, ETABS and MATLAB. Heavy 3D work needs a dedicated GPU.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science:</strong> good for learning. Large model training belongs in the cloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Architecture:</strong> workable for 2D and light SketchUp. Rendering in Lumion requires an RTX laptop.</li>\n<li><strong>Management and law:</strong> more than you need. Spend on a better screen instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tuning it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update the BIOS and chipset drivers in Lenovo Vantage.</li>\n<li>Allocate more memory to graphics in the BIOS only if you play games.</li>\n<li>Set the charge limit to 80 per cent for desk use.</li>\n<li>Elevate the rear of the laptop for heavy work.</li>\n<li>Use the integrated Windows dev tools, such as WSL, on this chip. It handles Linux workloads well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Warranty notes</h2>\n<p>Check the model code 15AHP10 and the serial number on Lenovo\'s site. Official units have local warranty. Others come through importers with their own cover. Ask about accidental damage protection if the laptop goes to a hostel.</p>\n<h2>Compared with the Yoga Slim 7i</h2>\n<p>Our other recommendation, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a>, costs Rs 33,000 more with a lovely OLED and far better battery life and has less multi-core power. Choose the IdeaPad if your work makes the fan spin. Choose the Yoga if your work makes your eyes tired.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Few laptops give you this much processor for the money. The plain screen and small battery are fair compromises for students and developers who sit near a socket. If your course depends on computing power and your budget ends at Rs 1.4 lakh, this is where we would put it.</p>',8.2,'8-core Ryzen 7 8840HS\nRadeon 780M handles light gaming\n16GB RAM, 512GB SSD\n16:10 touchscreen','60Hz, average-brightness screen\nModest 50Wh battery\nPlain design','The best pick under Rs 1.4 lakh for engineering, IT and computing students who need performance more than a premium screen.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(127,116,20,'MacBook Neo Review: The Cheapest Mac, With Clear Limits','macbook-neo-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the MacBook Neo yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The MacBook Neo costs <strong>Rs 1,34,999</strong> for 8GB/256GB and Rs 1,54,000 for 8GB/512GB. It brings a Mac within reach for about Rs 1 lakh less than the <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air M5</a>.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>It runs on the A18 Pro, the chip from the iPhone 16 Pro, which is fast and efficient for notes, assignments, browsing, Zoom and light photo editing. The 13-inch Liquid Retina screen is sharp (2408×1506) and reaches 500 nits. You get macOS, the build quality Apple is known for, and easy syncing with an iPhone.</p>\n<h2>The limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>8GB of memory, fixed.</strong> Fine for study and writing; tight for coding with heavy tools, video editing or long multitasking.</li>\n<li><strong>The 256GB model drops Touch ID and the keyboard backlight.</strong> You\'ll miss the backlight when typing in a dim room or during load-shedding.</li>\n<li>256GB of storage fills quickly. The 512GB model fixes that and adds Touch ID, but costs nearly Rs 20,000 more.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026\">Dell XPS 13 (2026)</a> costs the same on Windows, also with 8GB. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> (Rs 1,64,999) has 16GB and an OLED screen. <a href=\"/compare?category=laptop&g1=macbook-neo&g2=dell-xps-13-2026\">Compare the Neo and the XPS 13</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>Liquid Retina display, 13.0-inch (diagonal) LED-backlit display with IPS technology; 2408-by-1506 native resolution at 219 pixels per inch, 500 nits brightness,...</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB unified memory</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>1080p FaceTime HD camera 1080p HD video recording Advanced image signal processor with computational video</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>macOS</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 6E (802.11ax), Bluetooth 6</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>1.23 Kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The price, and what it changes</h2>\n<p>When we first reviewed it, the 256GB Neo was Rs 1,34,999. The current figure in our catalogue is Rs 1,33,499, and the 512GB version has been around Rs 1,54,000. Until this year, a new Mac in Nepal meant saving for an Air at well over Rs 2 lakh or buying a used one. The Neo puts a new Mac beside ordinary Windows laptops, and for students that is a genuine change.</p>\n<h2>An iPhone chip in a laptop: does it work?</h2>\n<p>Yes, for what this laptop is meant to do. The A18 Pro has two very fast cores and four efficient ones. Single tasks, the kind you do all day, feel as quick as on any laptop at this price: opening apps, loading pages, typing, editing a photo. It is silent because there is no fan.</p>\n<p>Sustained work is different. Exporting a long 4K video, compiling a large project or running several heavy apps will take longer than on an Air. The chip has fewer cores and less cooling. It can do those jobs occasionally and should not be hired for them.</p>\n<h2>8GB on macOS</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s system is better than Windows at living in little memory. Compression and a quick SSD hide the limit for typical study. A dozen Safari tabs, Pages, Music and Zoom are fine. Chrome with thirty tabs plus Figma plus Teams is not. Use Safari, which is lighter, and close what you do not need.</p>\n<p>Since memory can never be added, ask yourself what you will be doing in the third year of your course. If the answer involves Xcode, Docker, virtual machines or Premiere, buy a different Mac.</p>\n<h2>256GB or 512GB?</h2>\n<p>The more expensive version brings twice the storage and Touch ID, for roughly Rs 20,000. The base model also lacks a keyboard backlight, according to the published details, and in a country where the lights still go out, that is more annoying than it sounds. External SSDs are cheap, so storage can be worked around. Touch ID and the backlight cannot. If you can afford it, the 512GB model is nicer to live with. If not, the base model with a portable drive is the value choice.</p>\n<h2>Screen, sound and camera</h2>\n<p>The 13-inch Liquid Retina panel is sharp and reaches 500 nits. It covers sRGB, a step below the wider colour of the Air, and that is fine for nearly everyone. The 1080p camera is much better than the webcams in most cheap laptops, and calls look clean. At 1.23kg, it disappears in a bag.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>An efficient chip in a laptop body means a long day. Classes from morning to evening without a charger are realistic. It charges by USB-C, and a phone charger of 30W or more will do. In power cuts this is a calm machine to own.</p>\n<h2>Software in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, Zoom, Adobe apps and most study tools run well. Problems appear with local accounting packages, some government signing tools, AutoCAD add-ons, and Windows-only engineering software. If your college or employer uses those, ask first. For Nepali typing, macOS includes Devanagari layouts, and Preeti-based documents need font conversion.</p>\n<h2>Compared with Windows at the same price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,499</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td><td>8GB unified memory</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-spin-5-2023-i7-1355u-14-inch-touch\">Acer Spin 5 72SG 2023 (Intel Core i7-1355U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,000</td><td>Intel Core i7-1355U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 56 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-5441-2024-snapdragon-x-plus-x1p-42-100\">Dell Inspiron 14 5441 2024 (Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100 (3nm), 8 cores / 8 thr...</td><td>Li-Ion 54 Wh; 65W adapter, Supports ExpressCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AHP10 2025 (Ryzen 7 8840HS)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-envy-x360-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">HP Envy x360 ES1023DX 2024 (Intel Core 7 150U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,30,000</td><td>Intel Core 7 Processor 150U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 59 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">IdeaPad Slim 3 with Ryzen 7 8840HS</a> gives twice the memory and storage and far more power, with shorter battery life and a plain screen. The <a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-sfl14-41m-r4qm-2025-amd-ryzen-7-260\">Swift Lite 14</a> is similar in a smaller body. The <a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026\">Dell XPS 13</a> is the closest in spirit, with more storage and the same memory on a system that needs more. For raw work, choose AMD. For comfort, quiet and longevity, choose the Mac.</p>\n<h2>Or a used MacBook Air?</h2>\n<p>This is the comparison many buyers should make. A clean M2 or M3 Air with 16GB can cost about the same. It has wider colour, more cores and more memory, along with a used battery and no warranty. Check battery cycles, the Activation Lock status and the keyboard. If you know the seller, a used Air with 16GB is the better computer. If you want new and worry-free, choose the Neo.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Students in management, humanities, medicine, law and media basics.</li>\n<li>iPhone owners who want messages, photos and calls across devices.</li>\n<li>Writers, teachers and small-business owners.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who it does not suit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Computing and engineering students.</li>\n<li>Video editors and music producers.</li>\n<li>Gamers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check the serial on Apple\'s coverage page and the date of first activation.</li>\n<li>Ask whether the seller is an authorised reseller with a Nepali warranty. Apple\'s international cover depends on country and product.</li>\n<li>Ask for education offers.</li>\n<li>Budget for a USB-C hub, since projectors use HDMI.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The MacBook Neo does one thing very well: it makes a pleasant, long-lasting, well-built laptop available at the price of an ordinary one. Its limits are printed on the box, and they are permanent. If your work fits inside 8GB, you will enjoy it every day. If it does not, no amount of polish will help.</p>',7.9,'Cheapest way into a Mac\nFast, efficient A18 Pro\nSharp 500-nit screen\nApple build and iPhone integration','8GB memory can\'t be upgraded\n256GB model lacks Touch ID and keyboard backlight\n256GB fills fast','A great first laptop for students who already use an iPhone. Coders and video editors should save for the MacBook Air M5.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(128,121,20,'Dell XPS 13 (2026) Review: Premium Build, Not Enough Memory','dell-xps-13-2026-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Dell XPS 13 (2026) yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The 2026 Dell XPS 13 costs <strong>Rs 1,34,999</strong> with an Intel Core 5 320, 8GB of LPDDR5x and a 512GB SSD. That\'s the same price as the <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a>, which makes this the year\'s most interesting Windows-versus-Mac choice.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The XPS 13 has one of the best builds of any Windows laptop: compact, solid and light. The 13.4-inch 2.5K touchscreen is sharp and reaches 500 nits. A 512GB SSD gives twice the storage of the base MacBook Neo.</p>\n<h2>The problem: 8GB, soldered</h2>\n<p>Windows 11 uses more memory than macOS, and the XPS\'s 8GB is soldered, so you can\'t upgrade it. For writing, browsing and email it\'s fine. For heavy multitasking, coding or photo editing it will feel tight within a year or two. The Core 5 320 is a mid-range chip, quick for everyday work but not built for heavy loads.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>For about Rs 30,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> has 16GB and an OLED screen. For raw power at a lower price, the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">IdeaPad Slim 3 (Ryzen 7 8840HS)</a> costs Rs 1,31,999 with 16GB.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Dell XPS 13 (2026)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>13.4-inch 2.5K (2408×1506) IPS touch, 500 nits</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core 5 320 (6 cores, up to 4.6GHz)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>8GB LPDDR5x-7467 (soldered)</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>An XPS for less, and what was removed</h2>\n<p>XPS has long been Dell\'s showcase: a small aluminium laptop that cost two lakhs or more in Nepal and felt it. The 2026 model at Rs 1,34,999 opens the door to many more people. Dell kept the part you touch and reduced the part you do not see. The body, hinge, trackpad and screen are still special. The memory is 8GB and the chip is mid-range.</p>\n<p>Whether that is a fair exchange depends on how you use a computer, so let us be specific.</p>\n<h2>What 8GB means on Windows 11</h2>\n<p>After starting up, Windows and its services occupy three to four gigabytes. Outlook, Teams and a browser with ten tabs take the rest. From there, Windows compresses memory and writes to the SSD. It works, and a fast SSD hides much of it. You notice small delays in switching, tabs that reload and the fan waking up. By 2029, with heavier software, you would notice more. The memory is soldered, so no technician can help.</p>\n<p>If you write, read, send email and watch lectures, you will be fine for years. If you work in large spreadsheets, code, edit photos in Lightroom or keep dozens of tabs, choose a 16GB machine, however plain.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>The 13.4-inch touchscreen is sharp and reaches 500 nits, enough for a bright cafe. It is an IPS panel with good colour. Our specification sheet lists 2408 x 1506, an unusual resolution for an XPS and identical to the MacBook Neo\'s panel, so confirm it on the machine. Touch is useful for scrolling and signing.</p>\n<h2>Processor</h2>\n<p>The Core 5 320 has six cores and boosts to 4.6GHz. It belongs to Intel\'s economical line, quick for short tasks and efficient. It is not made for long exports or engineering work.</p>\n<h2>Feel, weight and sound</h2>\n<p>Here the XPS justifies its name. It is rigid, compact and beautifully finished. The keyboard takes a day to learn and is pleasant after that. The trackpad is accurate. Speakers are better than in most small laptops. If you carry a computer to meetings, the impression matters, and this one gives a good one.</p>\n<h2>Ports</h2>\n<p>XPS laptops use USB-C only. Projectors in Nepali classrooms and offices still expect HDMI, and pen drives expect USB-A. Buy a small hub on the first day, and keep it in the bag.</p>\n<h2>Rivals at the same price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dell XPS 13 (2026)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,34,999</td><td>Intel Core 5 320 (6 cores, up to 4.6GHz)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5x-7467 (soldered)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-16-dc16255-2025-ryzen-7-250-16gb-1tb-16-fhd\">Dell 16 DC16255 2025 (Ryzen 7 250)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,34,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 250 (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 54 Wh; 65W adapter, Supports ExpressCharge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,499</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td><td>8GB unified memory</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-spin-5-2023-i7-1355u-14-inch-touch\">Acer Spin 5 72SG 2023 (Intel Core i7-1355U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,000</td><td>Intel Core i7-1355U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 56 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AHP10 2025 (Ryzen 7 8840HS)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The neighbours are awkward for it. The <a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026-intel-core-ultra-5-320\">XPS 13 with a Core Ultra 5</a> is listed at only Rs 1,000 more, so ask what memory it comes with. The <a href=\"/products/dell-16-dc16255-2025-ryzen-7-250-16gb-1tb-16-fhd\">Dell 16 with a Ryzen 7 250</a> has 16GB and a terabyte for the same money in a larger, plainer body. The <a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-sfl14-41m-r4qm-2025-amd-ryzen-7-260\">Acer Swift Lite 14</a> gives a powerful chip in a portable shell. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> has the same memory, where macOS manages it better, and far longer battery life, with 256GB of storage.</p>\n<h2>XPS 13 or MacBook Neo?</h2>\n<p>Choose the Neo if you have an iPhone, want the longest battery life and your software exists on macOS. Choose the XPS if you need Windows, want twice the storage and a touchscreen, and like its keyboard and shape. Neither is for heavy work. A <a href=\"/compare?g1=macbook-neo&g2=dell-xps-13-2026\">side-by-side comparison</a> shows the remaining details.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Executives and consultants who mostly use email, documents and calls.</li>\n<li>Writers and journalists who travel.</li>\n<li>Students in non-technical courses who value a small, strong laptop.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Developers, analysts, designers and engineers.</li>\n<li>Anyone who plans to keep one laptop for five years or more.</li>\n<li>People who need HDMI and USB-A every day.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Getting the best from it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Edge with sleeping tabs, and web versions of Teams and WhatsApp.</li>\n<li>Uninstall trial antivirus and disable startup apps.</li>\n<li>Keep 100GB of the SSD free, as Windows will lean on it.</li>\n<li>Set the charge limit in Dell\'s power manager for desk use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>Dell\'s service in Nepal is among the better ones for business models. XPS units, however, are often parallel imports. Enter the service tag on Dell\'s support page and see the country and the end date. If the warranty belongs to another region, ask whether it can be transferred before you buy. Parts for an XPS are expensive, and a screen assembly can cost a third of the laptop.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>There is real pleasure in using a laptop this well made, and for light work it will serve nicely. But memory is the one specification that decides how long a Windows laptop stays comfortable, and 8GB is the wrong answer at Rs 1.35 lakh. Buy it with open eyes for light duties, or spend a little more on the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> and stop thinking about it.</p>\n<h2>If you love it anyway</h2>\n<p>Some people will buy this laptop for how it feels, and there is nothing wrong with that. Make the 8GB stretch. Use one browser and not two. Keep chat in browser tabs. Restart at lunch. Choose the web versions of Office for quick edits and the desktop ones only for long documents. And ask about the Core Ultra 5 listing at almost the same price before you pay: if it comes with more memory, take it without a second thought.</p>',7.3,'Premium, compact XPS build\nSharp 2.5K touchscreen, 500 nits\n512GB SSD\nLight and easy to carry','8GB soldered RAM on Windows\nMid-range Core 5 chip\nRivals give 16GB for similar money','Lovely to carry and use for writing and browsing, but 8GB limits its lifespan. Most buyers should get the Yoga Slim 7i Aura instead.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(129,296,20,'Asus Vivobook S15 (Snapdragon X Plus) Review: OLED, 1TB and All-Day Battery','asus-vivobook-s15-snapdragon-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Asus Vivobook S15 costs <strong>Rs 1,42,800</strong> with a Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus, 16GB of RAM and a <strong>1TB SSD</strong>. It also has a 15.6-inch 3K OLED screen. On paper, that\'s a lot of laptop for the money.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The 3K OLED is sharp and vivid, great for video and photos. 16GB of RAM and 1TB of storage are generous at this price. Snapdragon X chips are known for long battery life and running cool and quiet, and they include a capable AI processor for Windows Copilot+ features.</p>\n<h2>Know what Arm means</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon X Plus is an <strong>Arm</strong> processor. Microsoft Office, browsers, Zoom, Teams, Spotify and most popular apps run natively, and many others run through emulation. But some older software, many games (especially those with anti-cheat), and some printer, scanner or VPN drivers may not work. If your course, office or bank needs specific Windows software, check before buying.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> (Rs 1,64,999) has a smaller OLED and an Intel chip with full app compatibility. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">IdeaPad Slim 3 (Ryzen 7)</a> is cheaper and runs everything, but with a plain screen.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6-inch 3K OLED</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>1TB</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The laptop that behaves like a phone</h2>\n<p>Close the lid on Friday and open it on Monday: it wakes at once, with nearly the same charge. Work on a sofa for an afternoon and the base stays cool. Those are the everyday advantages of Arm-based processors, and on Windows the Snapdragon X family is the first that delivers them convincingly. The Vivobook S15 pairs the chip with a splendid screen and generous memory for Rs 1,42,800.</p>\n<h2>Before anything else: your software list</h2>\n<p>Write down every program, device and website you depend on. Then sort them.</p>\n<p><strong>Safe:</strong> Microsoft 365, Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Zoom, Teams, WhatsApp, Spotify, VLC, Photoshop, Lightroom, DaVinci Resolve, Visual Studio Code, Python, Git. These have native Arm versions or work well.</p>\n<p><strong>Usually fine under emulation:</strong> many older 32-bit and 64-bit Windows programs, with some loss of speed and battery.</p>\n<p><strong>Risky:</strong> accounting packages with hardware keys, government signing tools, older scanners and receipt printers, some VPN clients, AutoCAD and other engineering suites, Android emulators, and games with kernel-level anti-cheat such as Valorant.</p>\n<p>In Nepal the practical tests are often local: digital signature drivers, Nepali accounting software, the bank token tool your office uses and the printer in the corner. If one of them fails, the best battery in the world will not help. Ask the shop for a demo or a return window and try them.</p>\n<h2>The screen is the showpiece</h2>\n<p>A 15.6-inch OLED at about 3K resolution is sharp enough that you stop noticing pixels. Blacks are true, colours cover the cinema range and motion is smooth. Reading, film and photo work all benefit. Use dark mode, auto-hide the taskbar and keep Asus\'s pixel care enabled to protect the panel over the years.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>Snapdragon X Plus has enough speed for anything a manager, writer, student of humanities, teacher or marketer does. It is strong in browsers and Office, and it holds the same speed on battery, unlike many Intel and AMD laptops that slow down when unplugged. Its graphics are modest. Light photo and short video editing are fine.</p>\n<p>The neural processor runs Windows features such as live captions, background effects in calls and local image tools without draining the battery. They are pleasant, and no reason by themselves to buy.</p>\n<h2>Battery life</h2>\n<p>Expect a full working day with room to spare in browser and document work, and long local video playback. OLED at high brightness with white pages consumes more, so dark mode helps twice. USB-C charging means that a 65W phone-and-laptop charger can serve for both.</p>\n<h2>Is there a catch in the configuration?</h2>\n<p>Sixteen gigabytes and a terabyte are excellent at this price. The memory is soldered, as on most thin laptops. Our listing shows another entry for the same machine, the <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-oled-x-plus-16gb-1tb-15-6-3k-oled\">Vivobook S15 OLED X Plus</a>, at the same price. A faster <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-2024-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">X Elite version</a> costs about Rs 12,000 more and is worth it only for heavier multitasking.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,42,800</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon X Plus</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-v15-gen-4-ryzen-5-7520u\">Lenovo V15 Gen 4 AMN (AMD Ryzen 5 7520U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,41,900</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U, 4 cores / 8 threads</td><td>38Wh Li-Polymer; 65W round-tip Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-ai-sfl14-54m-786p-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-155u-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga-oled-60hz\">Acer Swift Lite 14 AI SFL14-54M-786P 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 155U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,44,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155U (Intel 4), 12 cores / 14 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-x-flip-14-14-fk0033-2025-ryzen-ai-7-350-24gb-1tb-2k-ips-touch\">HP OmniBook X Flip 14 14-FK0033 2025 (Ryzen AI 7 350)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen Al 7 350 (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 59 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-m1407ka-2025\">ASUS VivoBook 14 M1407KA 2025</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,45,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen AI 5 340 (4nm), 6 cores / 12 threads (base configu...</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-swift-go-14-sfg14-74t-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-225h-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga-touch\">Acer Swift Go 14</a> with a Core Ultra 5 225H runs all software and has stronger multi-core speed with a plainer screen. The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Acer Aspire AI 14</a> with a Core Ultra 7 258V is a particularly good alternative: Intel\'s efficient chip, long battery life and full compatibility, for slightly less. The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> has similar ease and battery with half the memory.</p>\n<p>That Acer deserves emphasis. If you came for battery and not for the OLED, it removes the compatibility risk entirely.</p>\n<h2>Who will be delighted</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Writers, teachers, marketers, consultants and managers.</li>\n<li>Students of management, law, arts and education.</li>\n<li>People who watch a lot of films and work away from sockets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should avoid it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Engineers, architects and anyone with specialised Windows tools.</li>\n<li>Gamers.</li>\n<li>Accountants until their software has been tested.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Service and resale</h2>\n<p>Arm laptops are still new to technicians in Nepal. Windows reinstallation is different, and parts are less common. Choose official stock with Asus warranty. Resale is harder to predict, since many buyers are cautious about Arm, so buy it to keep.</p>\n<h2>Setup tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Install Arm64 versions of apps where offered. Check the download page.</li>\n<li>Use the manufacturer\'s printer driver page to confirm Arm support, or choose a printer that supports driverless printing.</li>\n<li>Set the battery limit to 80 per cent in MyAsus.</li>\n<li>Keep Windows updated. Emulation has improved with each release.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For a person whose work happens in a browser, Office and a media player, this is one of the most enjoyable laptops under Rs 1.5 lakh. The screen is lovely, the battery is freeing and the storage is ample. Just do not buy it on hope. Test the one odd program you cannot live without.</p>\n<h2>A sensible way to try it</h2>\n<p>Agree on a three-day trial with the shop, in writing. On the first day install everything you use and print a page on the printer you use. On the second, work a full day without the charger and see what remains at six. On the third, open the awkward things: the bank\'s signing page, the VPN, the old accounting file. If all three days pass quietly, you have found a wonderful laptop. If one does not, return it without embarrassment, because that is what the trial was for.</p>',7.5,'15.6-inch 3K OLED\n16GB RAM and 1TB SSD\nLong battery life, cool and quiet\nCopilot+ AI features','Some apps, games and drivers don\'t run on Arm\nNot for gaming\nCheck software you depend on first','Great value for browser-, Office- and media-heavy users who want battery life. If you depend on niche Windows software, choose an Intel or AMD laptop.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(130,821,20,'Acer Nitro Lite 16 (RTX 4050) Review: Affordable 16-inch Gaming','acer-nitro-lite-16-rtx-4050-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-51 yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Acer Nitro Lite 16 costs <strong>Rs 1,69,999</strong> with an Intel Core i7-13620H, an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4050, 16GB of RAM and a 512GB SSD. It\'s one of the cheapest ways to get an RTX 40-series GPU and a 16-inch screen in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>Gaming performance</h2>\n<p>The RTX 4050 is a clear step up from the RTX 3050 in cheaper gaming laptops. It has more video memory and supports DLSS 3 frame generation, so current games run well at 1080p on medium-high settings. The i7-13620H has 10 cores and 16 threads, which is plenty for gaming, streaming and editing.</p>\n<h2>The \"Lite\" part</h2>\n<p>It ships with a 135W charger, which suggests the GPU runs at lower power than in thicker gaming laptops, so expect less performance than the same GPU elsewhere. The 57Wh battery won\'t last long away from a socket, and at 2.2kg it\'s a desk machine. The 16-inch 165Hz screen is smooth for games.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a15-fa506ncg-2024\">Asus TUF A15 (RTX 3050)</a> costs Rs 14,000 less with a much weaker GPU. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> (Rs 1,94,999) and <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a> are the other names to compare.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-51 2025 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1080), 165Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Intel Core i7-13620H (Intel 7), 10 cores / 16 threads</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 57 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 6, Dual-band (2.4 / 5 GHz), Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>2.2 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The wattage question, explained</h2>\n<p>Two laptops can both say RTX 4050 on the sticker and differ by a quarter in speed. Nvidia lets manufacturers choose how much power the chip may draw, anywhere from about 35 to 115 watts. Thick laptops with big chargers use the upper end. Thin ones use the lower.</p>\n<p>The Nitro Lite\'s 135W adapter has to feed the processor, screen, drives and graphics together. That leaves a moderate allowance for the GPU. It will still beat any RTX 3050 comfortably, but do not expect the frame rates you see in videos of a full-power RTX 4050. Ask the seller to open Nvidia Control Panel, System Information, and read the maximum graphics power on the actual machine. It takes thirty seconds and tells you more than any brochure.</p>\n<h2>What it plays</h2>\n<p>At 1080p with sensible settings, esports titles such as Valorant, CS2, Dota 2 and League run far beyond sixty frames and make use of the 165Hz panel. Story games from recent years run at medium to high with DLSS. The 6GB of video memory is the long-term limit. Very new titles with large textures will want medium textures, whatever the GPU\'s raw speed. Ray tracing is possible in small doses.</p>\n<h2>The processor is more than a match</h2>\n<p>An i7-13620H has six performance cores and four efficient ones. It is comfortable with game streaming, video editing in DaVinci Resolve or Premiere, and engineering software such as SolidWorks at student level. It runs hot under sustained load, as most H-series Intel chips do, so a stand and a room that is not stuffy will help it keep its speed.</p>\n<h2>Screen and body</h2>\n<p>Sixteen inches with a 16:10 shape gives you more height for code and timelines than a 15.6-inch panel. The catalogue lists the resolution as 1920 x 1080 under a WUXGA label, which would normally mean 1920 x 1200. Check on the machine. Colour coverage on budget gaming screens is usually modest. If you edit photos for clients, plan for an external monitor.</p>\n<p>At 2.2kg plus the charger, you are carrying close to three kilos. That is fine for a hostel room and tiring for a daily bus ride.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage</h2>\n<p>Sixteen gigabytes is right for games today. Find out whether it is one stick or two, as dual-channel memory improves frame rates. The 512GB drive holds Windows, a few programs and three or four big games. A second M.2 slot is common on Nitro machines, so a 1TB drive is the first upgrade to budget for.</p>\n<h2>What else is on the shelf</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-51 2025 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,69,999</td><td>Intel Core i7-13620H (Intel 7), 10 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 57 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-15-2025-intel-core-5-210h-rtx-4050\">Acer Nitro V 15 ANV15-52-5302 2025 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,70,000</td><td>Intel Core 5 210H (Intel 7), 8 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 57.5 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1407ca-2025\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1407CA 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 225H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,70,905</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 225H (TSMC N3B), 14 cores / 14 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-440-g10-2023\">HP ProBook 440 G10 (2023)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,68,000</td><td>Intel Core i7-1355U</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-5-2-in-1-14akp10-2025-ryzen-ai-7-350\">Lenovo IdeaPad 5 2-in-1 14AKP10 2025 (Ryzen AI 7 350)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,67,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen AI 7 350 (4nm), 8 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 57 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-i5-13420h-rtx-4050\">HP Victus 15 with RTX 4050</a> is about Rs 2,500 cheaper with a lesser processor, and its graphics power allowance may be higher, which matters more in games. The <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-15-2025-intel-core-5-210h-rtx-4050\">Nitro V 15</a> is Acer\'s own alternative at the same price. Watch for the <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-nl16-71g-i7-13620h-rtx-3050\">Nitro Lite 16 with an RTX 3050</a>: same name, weaker graphics, and in our listing it even costs more. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> is the better gaming chassis for those who can reach Rs 1,94,999.</p>\n<h2>Heat, noise and Nepali summers</h2>\n<p>A gaming laptop in Birgunj in May is working in a forty-degree room. Performance drops when the chip reaches its thermal limit. Raise the back of the laptop by a few centimetres, clean the vents every few months, and cap the frame rate at what your eyes can enjoy. A cap of 90 in story games lowers heat, noise and power use with little loss. In winter in Kathmandu, the same machine will feel like a different one.</p>\n<h2>Power and battery</h2>\n<p>With 57Wh, count on three or so hours of light use. During load-shedding it acts as its own UPS for a while, but games on battery run at a fraction of their speed. Use a proper surge protector. Some small inverters cannot supply 135W cleanly alongside a router and lights, so check the rating.</p>\n<h2>Who it is for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Students who want one machine for engineering software and games.</li>\n<li>First-time gaming laptop buyers who stay mostly at a desk.</li>\n<li>Beginner video editors who need CUDA acceleration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should look elsewhere</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Competitive players who need every frame from the GPU.</li>\n<li>Daily commuters.</li>\n<li>Anyone who could use a desktop. The same money buys a stronger PC through our <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Inspection list</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Read the maximum graphics power in Nvidia Control Panel.</li>\n<li>Run a game or a stress test for fifteen minutes and listen to the fans.</li>\n<li>Check for dead pixels and backlight bleed in a dark corner of the shop.</li>\n<li>Confirm the memory sticks, free M.2 slot and warranty period.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Nitro Lite 16 offers a modern GPU, a strong processor and a roomy screen for less than most rivals ask. The charger tells you where the saving was made. Go in knowing that, test before paying, add storage, and it is one of the better first gaming laptops in the country.</p>',7.9,'RTX 4050 with DLSS 3 under Rs 1.7 lakh\n10-core i7-13620H\n16-inch 165Hz screen\n16GB RAM','135W charger suggests a lower-power GPU\nHeavy at 2.2kg\nShort battery life\n512GB fills fast with games','Good value for a first gaming laptop. Plan for a second SSD, because modern games quickly fill 512GB.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(131,314,20,'Asus TUF Gaming A15 (RTX 3050) Review: Entry-Level Gaming That Shows Its Age','asus-tuf-gaming-a15-rtx-3050-review-nepal','<p><em>How we rated it:</em> We haven\'t run our own hands-on test of the Asus TUF Gaming A15 FA506NCG yet. This review weighs the official specifications and current Nepal prices against the rivals you can buy here. We\'ll update it once we\'ve spent time with one.</p><p>The Asus TUF Gaming A15 costs <strong>Rs 1,55,999</strong> with an AMD Ryzen 7 7445HS, an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050, 16GB of RAM and a 512GB SSD, plus a 15.6-inch 144Hz screen.</p>\n<h2>What\'s good</h2>\n<p>The Ryzen 7 7445HS is a strong 6-core chip, and 16GB of RAM is the right amount for gaming. TUF laptops are built to take knocks. The 144Hz screen suits esports titles like Valorant, CS2 and Dota 2, which the RTX 3050 runs well.</p>\n<h2>Where it shows its age</h2>\n<p>The <strong>RTX 3050</strong> is an older entry-level GPU with limited video memory, and newer games increasingly need more. Expect to drop to low-medium settings in demanding releases, and it lacks the DLSS 3 frame generation that RTX 40-series cards have.</p>\n<h2>Rivals</h2>\n<p>For Rs 14,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-2025-13th-gen-i7-13620h-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb\">Acer Nitro Lite 16</a> has an RTX 4050 and a bigger 165Hz screen, which is the better long-term buy. The <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a> is the other RTX 3050 option to compare.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Asus TUF Gaming A15 FA506NCG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels), 144Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 7445HS (4nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>16GB</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>512GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Ion 48 Wh; 180W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Windows 11 Home</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi 6, Dual-band (2.4 / 5 GHz), Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>2.3 kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>An old graphics chip in a good laptop</h2>\n<p>The RTX 3050 for laptops appeared in 2021. It was entry level then. In late 2026 it still runs the games most Nepali students play, and that keeps it on shelves. What has changed is the price around it. At Rs 1,55,999 you are only a short step from laptops with chips two steps newer, and that is the whole difficulty with this TUF.</p>\n<h2>Where it is perfectly happy</h2>\n<p>Valorant, CS2, Dota 2, League of Legends, Rocket League, FIFA, GTA V and Minecraft all run well at 1080p, and several will fill the 144Hz display. Older story games from the PS4 period play nicely on medium or high. For someone who plays these and studies, the laptop feels quick.</p>\n<h2>Where it struggles</h2>\n<p>Video memory is the bottleneck. Depending on the version, the 3050 laptop carries 4GB or 6GB, and current releases list 6GB or 8GB as a comfortable amount. With too little, the game shows blurry textures or stutters when it loads new areas. DLSS upscaling helps the frame rate and does little for memory. Frame generation, which makes newer cards feel smoother, is not available here. Ask which memory size this unit has. A 6GB version is clearly the one to want.</p>\n<h2>The processor and the rest</h2>\n<p>AMD\'s Ryzen 7 7445HS is a six-core Zen 4 part with a generous name. It is efficient, strong in single tasks and fine for programming and editing. With 16GB of memory and a PCIe 4.0 drive, general use is fast. The 180W charger gives the components enough power to work near their limits, something the Nitro Lite\'s smaller adapter cannot promise.</p>\n<h2>Built for hostels</h2>\n<p>TUF laptops are tested against parts of a military standard for drops, vibration and temperature, according to Asus. It does not make them indestructible, but hinges and corners survive student life better than most. The keyboard has full-size keys and clear WASD markings. The cooling system has self-cleaning fans that push dust out, which is a real help in our cities. The 48Wh battery is the weak link, good for about two hours of lectures. At 2.3kg it is not a commuter\'s machine.</p>\n<h2>The screen</h2>\n<p>A 15.6-inch Full HD IPS panel at 144Hz gives smooth motion. Colour coverage on many TUF panels is limited, around two-thirds of sRGB, so colours look muted. Gamers adjust to it in a day. Designers should not buy it for colour work.</p>\n<h2>Rivals by price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Asus TUF Gaming A15 FA506NCG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,55,999</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 7445HS (4nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 48 Wh; 180W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-7-a715-59g-76rm-2026-intel-core-7-240h-rtx-3050\">Acer Aspire 7 A715-59G-76RM 2026 (Intel Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,54,999</td><td>Intel Core 7 240H (Intel 7), 10 cores / 16 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 54 Wh; 120W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-2024-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">ASUS Vivobook S15 OLED S5507QA (Snapdragon X Elite, 16GB/1TB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,54,999</td><td>Snapdragon® X Elite X1E 78 100 Processor (12 cores, 12 Threa...</td><td>3-cell Li-ion ,70WHrs, 3S1P</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-5-2-in-1-16ial10-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-255u-16gb-1tb-16-wuxga-ips-touch\">Lenovo IdeaPad 5 2-in-1 16IAL10 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,54,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 255U (Intel 3), 12 cores / 14 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 57 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-640-g11-intel-core-ultra-7-165u\">HP EliteBook 640 G11 (Intel Core Ultra 7 165U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,54,999</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 165U (Intel 4), 12 cores / 14 threads</td><td>Li-Ion 56 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-7-a715-59g-76rm-2026-intel-core-7-240h-rtx-3050\">Acer Aspire 7</a> offers the same graphics for a similar price in a body that can pass for an office laptop. Asus\'s own <a href=\"/products/asus-gaming-v16-2025-core-7-240h-rtx-3050-16gb-1tb-16-wuxga-144hz\">Gaming V16</a> adds a bigger screen and twice the storage for Rs 2,000 more, which makes the TUF look poor. The <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-i5-13420h-rtx-4050\">HP Victus 15</a> with an RTX 4050 is about Rs 11,500 more and is the purchase that will still feel right in 2029.</p>\n<h2>How to judge the gap</h2>\n<p>Imagine keeping the laptop for four years. An extra Rs 11,500 works out at about Rs 240 a month. For that you gain roughly half as much speed again, more or better-used video memory, frame generation and a higher resale price. Very few upgrades are such a clear bargain. If the difference has to be found, EMI can spread it, or you can wait for the next festival sale.</p>\n<h2>When the TUF is still the right buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A dealer offers it at or below Rs 1,40,000.</li>\n<li>You only play esports titles and want a sturdy machine.</li>\n<li>You need strong official service in Nepal and prefer Asus.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When it is not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You want to play new single-player releases.</li>\n<li>You edit video or photos with colour in mind.</li>\n<li>You carry a laptop every day.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Upgrades worth making</h2>\n<p>Most A15 boards have two memory slots and two M.2 slots, which is a strength. Confirm on your unit, then add a 1TB drive for games when the first fills up. If it ships with a single 16GB stick, a matching second stick brings dual-channel speed. Both jobs need only a small screwdriver and some patience, or fifteen minutes at a service centre.</p>\n<h2>Settings that help</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Armoury Crate\'s Turbo mode only when plugged in and gaming.</li>\n<li>Limit the battery to 80 per cent if it stays on a desk.</li>\n<li>Set textures one step lower than the preset suggests.</li>\n<li>Turn on DLSS Quality in supported games.</li>\n<li>Cap frames at 144 in esports to keep heat down.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Used market note</h2>\n<p>RTX 3050 laptops from 2022 and 2023 are sold used for much less. If your budget is under a lakh and you only want esports, a clean used TUF with a healthy battery is a better deal than this new one. Check fan noise, temperature under load and hinge movement before paying.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>A solid laptop tied to a tired graphics chip. Nothing about it is bad, and at the right discount it is a sensible esports machine. At Rs 1,55,999, though, the best advice we can give is to look one shelf up.</p>',7.4,'Ryzen 7 7445HS and 16GB RAM\nTough TUF build\n144Hz screen for esports','Older RTX 3050 with limited video memory\nNo DLSS 3 frame generation\nRTX 4050 laptops cost only a little more','Fine for esports and older games. If you want to play new releases for the next few years, spend the extra on an RTX 4050 laptop.',1,0,0,0,0,0,0,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00');
