INSERT INTO `tech_guides` VALUES (1,20,'How to Choose a Smartphone in Nepal (2026)','how-to-choose-smartphone-nepal-2024','buying-guide','<p>Buying a phone in Nepal is mostly about three decisions: how much to spend, what you\'ll use it for most, and where you buy it. Get those right and the spec sheet looks after itself.</p>\n<h2>1. Pick a budget band</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 25,000:</strong> calls, WhatsApp, YouTube and a big battery. Expect slower charging and an LCD screen. See <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">phones under Rs 25,000</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 30,000–60,000:</strong> where phones get genuinely good, with AMOLED screens, 5G and batteries that last well over a day. See <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">under Rs 40,000</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 60,000–1,50,000:</strong> better cameras, faster charging and longer software support.</li>\n<li><strong>Above that:</strong> flagships like the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> and <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. Decide what matters most</h2>\n<p>Few phones are best at everything. If you\'re out all day, put <strong>battery</strong> first. Silicon-carbon batteries of 7,000mAh and more are now common under Rs 60,000. If you take a lot of photos of family and festivals, prioritise the <strong>main camera with OIS</strong> over extra lenses. For gaming, look at the chip and cooling.</p>\n<h2>3. Don\'t skimp on storage</h2>\n<p>WhatsApp media fills 64GB faster than people expect. Treat 128GB as the minimum, and choose 256GB if you shoot video.</p>\n<h2>4. Check the software support</h2>\n<p>Samsung and Google now promise around seven years of updates on many phones, and iPhones get long support too. Cheaper brands often promise far less. Over three or four years, that matters more than a spec bump.</p>\n<h2>5. Buy an official unit</h2>\n<p>Grey phones can be blocked on Nepali networks and usually come without a valid warranty. It only takes a few minutes to <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">check that a phone is official</a> before you pay. For current picks at every price, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">best phones by budget</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Spend twenty minutes before you spend thirty thousand rupees</h2>\n<p>Most people choose a phone in the shop, in ten minutes, with a salesperson who earns more on one model than on another. A little preparation at home changes the conversation. Take a piece of paper and answer five questions.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What annoys me about my present phone?</li>\n<li>How many hours a day do I use it, and for what?</li>\n<li>How long do I want to keep the next one?</li>\n<li>Where is the nearest service centre for each brand I am considering?</li>\n<li>What is the most I will pay, including a case and charger?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Those answers are your specification. Take the paper with you.</p>\n<h2>Turning annoyances into features</h2>\n<p><strong>\"It dies by evening.\"</strong> Look for 6,000mAh or more, and 33W charging at least.</p>\n<p><strong>\"It is slow.\"</strong> Memory of 8GB, UFS storage and a modern chip. More cores or megapixels will not help.</p>\n<p><strong>\"Photos of my children are blurred.\"</strong> Optical stabilisation on the main camera, and a larger sensor.</p>\n<p><strong>\"I cannot read it outside.\"</strong> A high brightness mode above 1,000 nits.</p>\n<p><strong>\"Storage is always full.\"</strong> 256GB, and the habit of stopping WhatsApp downloads.</p>\n<p><strong>\"The screen broke twice.\"</strong> A flat display, a stronger glass and a good case. Curved screens are lovely and fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>\"It stopped getting updates.\"</strong> A brand with a published promise of five years or more.</p>\n<h2>What each budget changes</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">Infinix Smart 10 Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;15,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>6.7 inches PLS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 1300 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 17...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;98,499</td><td>6.83-inch AMOLED, 165Hz, Dolby Vision, HDR10+</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,400mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Samsung Galaxy S26</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>6.3-inch FHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm) in most markets; Snapdragon...</td><td>12GB</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table climbs from Rs 16,000 to Rs 1.4 lakh. The jump from the first to the third row is the biggest you will ever feel in daily use. From the fifth row upwards you pay for cameras, materials and years of support.</p>\n<h2>Chips in plain words</h2>\n<p>You do not need to study processors. Use three groups. <strong>Basic:</strong> Unisoc T-series, Helio G81 and similar, fine for messaging. <strong>Comfortable:</strong> Snapdragon 6 and 7 series, Dimensity 7000 series, Helio G99 and G100 at the lower end. <strong>Fast:</strong> Snapdragon 8 series, Dimensity 8000 and 9000 series. Choose comfortable if you do not play demanding games. Our piece on <a href=\"/news/snapdragon-8-gen-4-debut-qualcomm-summit-2024\">chip names</a> decodes the rest.</p>\n<h2>Screens: three things to check</h2>\n<p>AMOLED or LCD, brightness, and resolution. At sizes above 6.5 inches, HD+ looks soft, especially for small Devanagari text. Full HD+ is right for nearly everyone. 120Hz is pleasant and no longer rare. If you are sensitive to flicker, look for high-frequency dimming or prefer an LCD.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: how to test in a shop</h2>\n<p>Take the same picture with two phones. First of a face near the window. Then of the darkest corner. Then of someone walking. Zoom in on both. It takes three minutes and beats every specification. Record a short video while walking to see stabilisation. Ignore macro and depth lenses.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Capacity is the tank, and the chip and screen are the engine. A 5,000mAh phone with an efficient chip can match 6,000mAh with an old one. Charging above 45W is a luxury, and below 18W is a nuisance. Check if the charger is in the box. A proper one costs Rs 1,500 to 4,000.</p>\n<h2>5G, NFC, eSIM: do you need them?</h2>\n<p><strong>5G:</strong> a tie-breaker in Nepal today. <a href=\"/guides/top-reasons-buy-5g-phone-nepal\">Here is our full answer</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>NFC:</strong> limited use for payment here so far, useful for pairing and some cards.</p>\n<p><strong>eSIM:</strong> handy for travellers. Check with your operator.</p>\n<p><strong>IR blaster:</strong> an old-fashioned joy for controlling the TV and AC. Xiaomi and Infinix often include it.</p>\n<p><strong>Headphone jack and card slot:</strong> disappearing, and still found on cheaper phones.</p>\n<h2>Brand by brand, briefly</h2>\n<p>Samsung for service and updates. Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO for hardware. vivo and OPPO for portraits and dealer networks. Realme for charging and battery. Honor for slim phones with big batteries. Motorola and Nothing for clean software. OnePlus for smoothness. Infinix and Tecno for low prices. Apple for video, longevity and resale. Each has good and poor models, so judge the phone.</p>\n<h2>New, old stock or used</h2>\n<p>A flagship from last year, sold new with warranty, is often the best buy in the shop. A used flagship can be better still if you know how to check it. A phone that has been in a box for three years is neither: its battery has aged and its updates have run out. Ask for the launch date and the date on the box.</p>\n<h2>Ownership cost: an example</h2>\n<p>Two friends need a phone for four years. One buys a Rs 18,000 phone, and replaces it after two years with another at Rs 20,000. Total Rs 38,000, with slow phones throughout. The other buys a Rs 36,000 phone, changes the battery in year three for Rs 3,000, and sells it for Rs 8,000 at the end. Total Rs 31,000, with a better phone every day. Cheap is not always cheaper.</p>\n<h2>The shop visit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ask to see the sealed box opened.</li>\n<li>Compare the IMEI on box, phone and bill, and check MDMS.</li>\n<li>Confirm memory and storage in Settings.</li>\n<li>Test cameras, speaker, call, charging and fingerprint.</li>\n<li>Ask what a screen costs and where it is replaced.</li>\n<li>Get the VAT bill and the warranty card.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The detailed list is in <a href=\"/blog/5-things-to-check-before-buying-a-smartphone-in-nepal\">five things to check before buying</a>.</p>\n<h2>Bargaining without conflict</h2>\n<p>Know the list price and one competing quotation. Ask for the best cash price, then for a case and glass. If you exchange an old phone, ask for its value separately from the discount, so one is not hidden in the other. Weekday mornings are best.</p>\n<h2>After you buy</h2>\n<p>Update the software, remove apps you do not need, set up backup and Find My Device, stop automatic media downloads, and photograph the bill and IMEI. Use the charge limit at night. These ten minutes add a year to its life.</p>\n<h2>If you are still choosing between two</h2>\n<p>Put them side by side in the <a href=\"/compare\">comparison tool</a>, and read the rows that match your list of annoyances. If they tie, choose the one with more memory. If they still tie, choose the one whose service centre is closer to your home.</p>','guides/guide-1.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(2,20,'Laptop or Tablet for Work and Study in Nepal? How to Decide','laptop-vs-tablet-work-study-nepal','buying-guide','<p>A tablet with a keyboard can look like a cheaper, lighter laptop. Sometimes it is. Often it turns out to be a compromise you notice every day. Here\'s how we\'d decide.</p>\n<h2>A tablet makes sense if…</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You mostly read, watch lectures and take handwritten notes. A tablet with a pen, such as the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a> with the S Pen in the box, is better at this than any laptop.</li>\n<li>Your college work happens in Google Docs, Canva and the browser.</li>\n<li>You want something light for the bus and long battery life.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Get a laptop if…</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You study engineering, IT or architecture, or your course needs Windows software such as AutoCAD, MATLAB or Tally.</li>\n<li>You write long documents and spreadsheets every day. A proper keyboard and trackpad make a big difference.</li>\n<li>You edit video, code or run anything heavier than a browser.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Watch the total cost</h2>\n<p>Add the keyboard cover and pen before you compare prices. An <a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-11-m4-2026\">iPad Air 11 (M4)</a> plus Apple\'s keyboard costs more than a <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> (Rs 1,33,499), which is a full laptop. On Windows, laptops like the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-5-14agp11-ryzen-ai-5-430\">IdeaPad Slim 5 14AGP11</a> (Rs 1,48,999) come with a touchscreen and pen, so you get both.</p>\n<p>Still unsure? For most students, a good laptop plus a cheap tablet for reading, such as the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2</a>, works better than one device trying to do everything. See also our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">laptop guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The same money, two very different tools</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 11-inch</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ), 600...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultra (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>11.2 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 8850 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>10.8 - 10.9 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (t...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1580 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">Apple iPad (A16)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;83,499</td><td>11-inch Liquid Retina IPS display, 2360 × 164...</td><td>Apple A16 chip (5-core CPU, 4-core GPU, 16-co...</td><td>6 GB LPDDR5</td><td>non-removable; 20W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 2024 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;97,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7430U (7nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 58 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,499</td><td>Liquid Retina display, 13.0-inch (diagonal) L...</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td><td>8GB unified memory</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A table like this makes tablets look cheap. Add a keyboard at Rs 8,000 to 35,000 and a pen at Rs 6,000 to 18,000, and the picture changes. Always compare complete kits.</p>\n<h2>Try this experiment for a week</h2>\n<p>Keep a note of every task you do on a screen larger than a phone. Beside each, write R for reading or watching, H for handwriting, T for typing more than a paragraph, and S for special software. At the end of the week count the letters. Mostly R and H points to a tablet. Mostly T and S points to a laptop. If T and H are close, you are one of the people who benefit from both, or from a convertible laptop with a pen.</p>\n<h2>By level of study</h2>\n<p><strong>Class 5 to 10.</strong> A tablet with parental controls handles lessons, reading and practice apps. Typing can wait. Choose a tough case.</p>\n<p><strong>Plus two.</strong> Science students draw diagrams and solve numericals, where a pen is excellent. Computer science students need a laptop from the first day.</p>\n<p><strong>Bachelor in management, humanities, law.</strong> A laptop for assignments, reports and presentations. A cheap tablet for reading is a fine addition.</p>\n<p><strong>Engineering, IT, architecture.</strong> A laptop, without debate.</p>\n<p><strong>Medicine and nursing.</strong> The best case for a tablet with a pen: atlases, PDFs and annotated slides. A basic laptop at home for typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Postgraduate research.</strong> A laptop for writing and data, with a tablet for reading papers if the budget allows.</p>\n<h2>By job</h2>\n<p><strong>Teachers:</strong> a laptop for preparation, a tablet with a pen for explaining on screen. Many do well with a pen tablet connected to a laptop, at Rs 5,000 to 8,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Sales and field staff:</strong> a tablet with LTE, catalogues and signatures.</p>\n<p><strong>Accountants:</strong> a laptop. Large spreadsheets and local software do not run properly on tablets.</p>\n<p><strong>Doctors:</strong> a tablet for rounds and records, if the hospital\'s system runs in a browser.</p>\n<p><strong>Writers and journalists:</strong> a laptop with a good keyboard.</p>\n<p><strong>Designers:</strong> an iPad for sketching and a computer for finishing.</p>\n<p><strong>Shop owners:</strong> a tablet at the counter, a laptop in the back.</p>\n<h2>What tablets still do badly</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Long documents with footnotes, tracked changes and complex tables.</li>\n<li>Excel beyond the basics: macros, pivot tables, add-ins.</li>\n<li>Nepali accounting, tax and registration portals that expect a desktop browser or a driver.</li>\n<li>Programming, apart from simple exercises.</li>\n<li>Managing many files from different places.</li>\n<li>Connecting to projectors and printers, which depends on the model.</li>\n<li>Working on your lap. Keyboard covers are wobbly on a sofa or bus.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What laptops do badly</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading in bed or standing on a bus.</li>\n<li>Handwriting and drawing.</li>\n<li>Lasting two days on a charge, with few exceptions.</li>\n<li>Starting instantly for a thirty-second task.</li>\n<li>Being shared with a four-year-old.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Samsung DeX, iPad windows and other bridges</h2>\n<p>DeX turns a Galaxy Tab into something like a desktop with windows and a taskbar. iPadOS offers resizable windows on recent models. With a mouse and keyboard, email, documents and browsing are comfortable. They remain mobile apps underneath. Before buying for this reason, sit with the actual apps you need and try to finish a real piece of work in the shop.</p>\n<h2>Convertible laptops</h2>\n<p>A 2-in-1 laptop with a pen folds into a thick tablet. It runs full Windows, so there are no software limits. It weighs 1.3 to 1.8kg, too heavy to hold as a tablet for long, but fine on a desk for notes. If you need typing, special software and handwriting, it is the honest single-device answer, starting at around Rs 1.2 to 1.5 lakh.</p>\n<h2>Budgets and our suggestions</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 30,000:</strong> Redmi Pad 2 for reading. A new laptop at this price is not worth having. A used business laptop may be.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 50,000 to 60,000:</strong> Xiaomi Pad 7 with pen for notes, or a used ThinkPad for typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 75,000 to 80,000:</strong> Galaxy Tab S10 FE for handwriting, or a Vivobook 15 with 16GB for everything else.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 1,00,000:</strong> Acer Aspire Lite 15. Add a pen tablet if you draw.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 1,30,000 to 1,50,000:</strong> MacBook Neo, IdeaPad Slim 3 Ryzen 7, or a Vivobook plus a Redmi Pad 2. The last pair covers more of a student\'s life than any single device.</p>\n<h2>Two cheap devices or one good one?</h2>\n<p>A Rs 80,000 laptop with a Rs 30,000 tablet gives you full software, a reading screen and a spare when one breaks. A Rs 1,10,000 tablet kit gives you one beautiful screen and several limits. For study, we would take the pair nearly every time.</p>\n<h2>Power and internet</h2>\n<p>Tablets run longer during cuts and can be charged from a power bank. Many laptops now charge by USB-C, and a 65W power bank can extend them. A tablet with LTE has its own connection. A laptop can use your phone\'s hotspot, at the cost of the phone\'s battery. In hostels with poor Wi-Fi, think about which of these you will depend on.</p>\n<h2>Durability and repair</h2>\n<p>A broken tablet screen can cost half the tablet. Laptop screens are cheaper to replace, and keyboards, batteries and drives can be changed. Over five years a laptop is easier to keep alive. Use a case for the tablet and a padded sleeve for the laptop, and insure neither with hope.</p>\n<h2>Resale</h2>\n<p>iPads hold value well. Android tablets lose it fast. Business laptops and MacBooks hold value, and consumer laptops fall quickly in the first two years. If you expect to sell after college, that difference can be Rs 15,000 to 20,000.</p>\n<h2>Questions to settle before you pay</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Which programs must run, by name?</li>\n<li>Will I type more than one page a day?</li>\n<li>Will I write by hand every day?</li>\n<li>Where will I use it: desk, bed, bus, field?</li>\n<li>What is the total with accessories?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Our conclusion</h2>\n<p>Choose a laptop if you create, and a tablet if you consume and annotate. If you are unsure, you probably need a laptop, as it can do everything a tablet does in an awkward way, while a tablet cannot do everything a laptop does at any price. For models, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1 lakh</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>','guides/guide-laptop-vs-tablet.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(3,20,'How to Choose Wireless Earbuds in Nepal (2026)','true-wireless-earbuds-buying-guide-nepal-2024','buying-guide','<p>You can spend Rs 2,000 or Rs 60,000 on earbuds in Nepal, and the difference isn\'t always obvious from the box. These are the things that actually change what you hear and how long the earbuds last.</p>\n<h2>Fit comes first</h2>\n<p>Earbuds that don\'t seal properly sound thin and let in traffic noise, whatever they cost. Try the different ear-tip sizes in the box. Most people never do, and it\'s free.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancellation</h2>\n<p>ANC is now available at budget prices, but it varies a lot. Cheap ANC takes the edge off bus and engine noise. The premium earbuds, like Sony\'s <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">WF-1000XM6</a> and the <a href=\"/products/airpods-pro-3\">AirPods Pro 3</a>, are in a different league on a long flight or in a noisy office.</p>\n<h2>Battery and case</h2>\n<p>Look at the earbuds\' own battery life, not just the total with the case. Six hours or more per charge covers a long commute. Cases that charge over USB-C are easier to live with.</p>\n<h2>Features worth paying for</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Multipoint</strong>: connected to your phone and laptop at the same time. Very useful for work calls.</li>\n<li><strong>Codecs</strong>: AAC for iPhone, and LDAC for higher quality on many Android phones.</li>\n<li><strong>Water resistance</strong>: IPX4 or better if you walk or exercise with them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where to start</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a> (Rs 1,999) are a good first pair. The <a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a> (Rs 2,599) and <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">OnePlus Nord Buds 4</a> (Rs 4,999) add ANC and multipoint. iPhone owners will get on easily with <a href=\"/products/airpods-4\">AirPods 4</a> (Rs 24,999). For more picks, see <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The three prices that matter</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,999</td><td>Up to 30 hours without ANC (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4; SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,599</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 1.66 hours playbac...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">OnePlus Nord Buds 4</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Up to 54 hours with ANC (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1 with multipoint; AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/airpods-4\">AirPods 4</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,999</td><td>Music: 5 hours (buds) / 30 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/airpods-pro-3\">Apple AirPods Pro 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>Up to 24 hours with ANC (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3; AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">Sony WF-1000XM6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;64,999</td><td>Up to 24 hours (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3 with multipoint; AAC, SBC, LDAC...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The gap between Rs 2,000 and Rs 5,000 is enormous. The gap between Rs 5,000 and Rs 15,000 is clear. The gap between Rs 15,000 and Rs 65,000 is for people who know exactly what they are paying for. Decide which of these you are, and most of the noise disappears.</p>\n<h2>Where do you listen?</h2>\n<p><strong>On a bus or bike taxi.</strong> Noise cancelling and a secure fit. For bikes, remember that blocking traffic is dangerous if you are the one riding.</p>\n<p><strong>At a desk.</strong> Comfort and multipoint. Consider headphones too, as we explain in <a href=\"/blog/earbuds-vs-headphones-which-should-you-buy\">earbuds or headphones</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>In the gym or on a run.</strong> Ear hooks or wing tips, IPX4 or more, physical buttons.</p>\n<p><strong>In bed.</strong> Small, flat buds, or a sleep headband. Ordinary buds press when you lie on your side.</p>\n<p><strong>In meetings.</strong> Microphones first. Test them.</p>\n<h2>Fit: how to get it right</h2>\n<p>Insert the bud, rotate it slightly back, and smile. If it loosens, use a larger tip. If it aches after ten minutes, use a smaller one. Play a tone with bass: with a good seal, it is full. Without one, it is thin. Two ears may need two sizes. Foam tips seal better and last about six months. If nothing seals, choose an open design such as AirPods 4 and accept more outside noise.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling by the numbers, and why to distrust them</h2>\n<p>Boxes say 35dB, 48dB or 55dB. These are maximum values at one frequency, usually a low hum. What matters is how wide the effect is and whether it works on voices. The order of quality in Nepal\'s market, roughly, is Sony and Apple and Bose at the top, Samsung, Soundcore Liberty, Redmi Pro and CMF Pro in the middle, OnePlus Nord, Realme and Soundcore P series below, and most Rs 2,000 pairs at the bottom.</p>\n<h2>Battery: the number to look for</h2>\n<p>Hours per charge with ANC on. Five is the minimum, seven is comfortable, ten is excellent. The case total tells you how often you need a socket. Fast charging saves you on the mornings you forgot. Batteries shrink by perhaps a fifth each year of heavy use, so start with more than you need.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Stems bring microphones closer to your mouth, so stemmed buds usually sound clearer than round ones. Wind is the enemy. Bone-conduction sensors help in premium pairs. Ask a friend to judge, from the footpath outside the shop.</p>\n<h2>Codecs without the jargon</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SBC:</strong> works everywhere, fine for most.</li>\n<li><strong>AAC:</strong> best for iPhones.</li>\n<li><strong>LDAC:</strong> high quality on many Androids, uses more battery.</li>\n<li><strong>aptX family:</strong> on phones with Qualcomm chips that support it.</li>\n<li><strong>LC3 and LE Audio:</strong> new, better calls and lower power, when both devices support it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you stream music on a normal plan while walking, the codec is not your bottleneck.</p>\n<h2>Multipoint, find my and other comforts</h2>\n<p>Multipoint keeps two devices connected. In-ear detection pauses when you remove a bud. Find my buds plays a sound. Wireless charging is pleasant. An app with an equaliser and firmware updates is essential. Spatial audio is fun for films. Live translation needs the internet and patience.</p>\n<h2>Water and dust</h2>\n<p>IPX4 covers sweat and drizzle. IP54 and IP55 add dust. IPX7 survives a drop in water. The case usually has less protection. Dry the buds before you put them back.</p>\n<h2>Which brand for which phone?</h2>\n<p><strong>iPhone:</strong> AirPods for switching and find my, anything with AAC otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Samsung:</strong> Galaxy Buds for the codec and switching between Galaxy devices.</p>\n<p><strong>OnePlus, OPPO, Realme:</strong> their own buds share features.</p>\n<p><strong>Xiaomi, POCO:</strong> Redmi Buds.</p>\n<p><strong>Nothing, CMF:</strong> CMF Buds, with controls in the phone\'s settings.</p>\n<p><strong>Any phone:</strong> Soundcore, Sony, JBL.</p>\n<h2>Copies and how to avoid them</h2>\n<p>Fake AirPods are sold across the country for Rs 1,500 to 4,000, and some are made well enough to show the pairing animation. Check the serial number on Apple\'s coverage page, look for the exact name in Bluetooth settings, and test noise cancelling. For JBL, Sony and Samsung, open the official app: it will not recognise a copy. A bill from an authorised shop is the best protection.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in practice</h2>\n<p>Most brands offer six to twelve months. Ask whether a faulty bud is replaced individually, and how long it takes. Lost buds are not covered. For expensive pairs, ask the price of a single bud and a case before you buy.</p>\n<h2>How long will they last?</h2>\n<p>Two to three years of daily use. The battery goes first. Clean the mesh monthly with a dry brush, wipe the tips with a damp cloth, keep the case out of the sun, and charge it every week or two even when idle.</p>\n<h2>A quick decision path</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Under Rs 3,000: Redmi Buds 5A or Kick Phantom Buds X Pro.</li>\n<li>Rs 5,000: OnePlus Nord Buds 4. For most people, stop here.</li>\n<li>Rs 9,000: CMF Buds 2 Plus.</li>\n<li>Rs 15,000 to 18,000: Redmi Buds 8 Pro or Soundcore Liberty 5.</li>\n<li>iPhone and Rs 48,000: <a href=\"/reviews/airpods-pro-3-review-nepal\">AirPods Pro 3</a>.</li>\n<li>The quietest: <a href=\"/reviews/sony-wf-1000xm6-review-nepal\">Sony WF-1000XM6</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Before you leave the shop</h2>\n<p>Pair them with your phone, play a song, make a call, try every tip, switch ANC on and off at the door, and check that both buds and the case charge. Then take the bill. It is fifteen minutes, and it saves a return trip.</p>\n<h2>Look after your ears</h2>\n<p>Keep the volume below 60 per cent, take a break each hour, and do not sleep with music on at high volume. Ringing after listening is a warning. Good earbuds last three years. Your ears have to last much longer.</p>','guides/guide-earbuds.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(4,20,'How to Read a Gadget Spec Sheet: A Plain-English Guide','how-to-read-gadget-spec-sheet-beginners-guide','how-to','<p>Spec sheets are written to impress, not to inform. Here\'s how to read the lines that matter, using real examples from our catalogue.</p>\n<h2>Screen</h2>\n<p>\"6.9-inch LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, 3000 nits\" means: the screen measures 6.9 inches corner to corner; AMOLED gives deep blacks; 120Hz makes scrolling smooth; and the nits figure is peak brightness. For use outdoors in Nepal\'s sun, brightness matters more than resolution. LTPO lets the refresh rate drop when nothing moves, which saves battery.</p>\n<h2>Chip</h2>\n<p>Names like \"A19 Pro\" or \"Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5\" tell you the generation. Newer is faster, but even last year\'s flagship chip is quick for daily use. On laptops, look at the letters: Intel and AMD chips ending in <strong>U</strong> are efficient, <strong>H</strong> is faster, and <strong>HX</strong> is desktop-class power that needs good cooling.</p>\n<h2>RAM vs storage</h2>\n<p>RAM (8GB, 12GB, 16GB) is working memory. More RAM means more apps stay open without reloading. Storage (128GB, 256GB, 1TB) is where your photos and apps live. On most phones and many laptops, neither can be upgraded later.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>mAh is capacity, and watts are charging speed. Bigger isn\'t automatically better, because a well-tuned 5,000mAh phone can outlast a poorly tuned 6,000mAh one. Also check whether a charger is in the box.</p>\n<h2>Laptop graphics</h2>\n<p>For gaming laptops, the GPU\'s wattage (TGP) matters as much as its name. An \"RTX 5050\" at 100W is far faster than one at 45W. See <a href=\"/news/nvidia-rtx-50-series-blackwell-gpus-nepal-guide\">our RTX 50-series explainer</a>.</p>\n<p>When two products look identical on paper, put them side by side with <a href=\"/compare\">Compare</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The three kinds of number on a spec sheet</h2>\n<p>Some numbers are <strong>facts</strong>: weight, screen size, memory, storage. You can check them with a scale, a ruler or a settings page. Some are <strong>laboratory results</strong>: battery hours, charging minutes, water resistance. They are true in conditions you will never repeat. Some are <strong>records</strong>: peak brightness, maximum speed, AI operations. They were reached once, for a moment, by a small part of the device. Read them with trust in that order.</p>\n<h2>Screens: what each word promises</h2>\n<p><strong>IPS LCD, PLS, TFT:</strong> a backlight behind a filter. IPS and PLS look right from the side. Plain TFT or TN on cheap laptops does not.</p>\n<p><strong>AMOLED, OLED, pOLED:</strong> each pixel makes light. Real black, rich colour, possible flicker at low brightness.</p>\n<p><strong>Mini-LED:</strong> an LCD with thousands of small lights. Very bright, with faint halos.</p>\n<p><strong>nits (typ):</strong> ordinary brightness. 400 to 600 is indoors. <strong>nits (HBM):</strong> what you get in sunlight. Above 1,000 is good. <strong>nits (peak, HDR):</strong> a small highlight in a video. Ignore it.</p>\n<p><strong>Hz:</strong> how often the picture refreshes. 60 is basic, 120 is smooth, above that is for games.</p>\n<p><strong>LTPO:</strong> can slow to 1Hz when nothing moves, which saves battery.</p>\n<p><strong>sRGB, DCI-P3, NTSC:</strong> how many colours. For laptops, \"45% NTSC\" means dull, \"100% sRGB\" means accurate enough for design.</p>\n<h2>Resolution names</h2>\n<p>HD+ is about 720 pixels wide on a phone and looks soft above 6.5 inches. FHD+ is 1080 and right for most. 1.5K is around 1220 and excellent. QHD+ is 1440 and rarely noticed. On laptops, FHD is 1920 x 1080, WUXGA is 1920 x 1200 with more height, and 2.5K to 3K is sharp at 14 to 16 inches.</p>\n<h2>Processors</h2>\n<p>Look at the year and class, not the gigahertz. A core at 2.4GHz from 2025 can beat one at 3GHz from 2019. \"8 cores\" may be two fast and six slow. \"6nm\" or \"4nm\" describes manufacturing: smaller is usually cooler. For laptops, the suffix tells you the power class: V and U are frugal, H is strong, HX is very strong and hot. For phone chips, see <a href=\"/news/snapdragon-8-gen-4-debut-qualcomm-summit-2024\">this explainer</a>.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage types</h2>\n<p><strong>LPDDR4X, LPDDR5X:</strong> phone and thin-laptop memory. Newer is faster and more efficient, and it is soldered.</p>\n<p><strong>DDR4, DDR5 SO-DIMM:</strong> laptop memory in slots, which you can replace.</p>\n<p><strong>8GB + 8GB extended:</strong> eight real, eight pretend.</p>\n<p><strong>eMMC:</strong> slow storage. Acceptable on a Rs 15,000 phone, not on a laptop.</p>\n<p><strong>UFS 2.2, 3.1, 4.0:</strong> increasingly fast phone storage.</p>\n<p><strong>SATA SSD, NVMe PCIe 3.0, 4.0, 5.0:</strong> laptop drives. Any NVMe drive feels quick.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p><strong>MP:</strong> how many dots. <strong>Sensor size (1/1.56\"):</strong> how much light. A smaller second number is a bigger sensor: 1/1.3\" is larger than 1/1.95\". <strong>f/1.8:</strong> the opening. Smaller numbers let in more light. <strong>OIS:</strong> optical stabilisation, the most valuable three letters on a mid-range phone. <strong>PDAF:</strong> fast focus. <strong>Telephoto 3x:</strong> real zoom. <strong>100x zoom:</strong> not real. <strong>2MP macro, 2MP depth:</strong> decoration.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p><strong>mAh</strong> for phones, <strong>Wh</strong> for laptops. To compare a power bank with a laptop, multiply mAh by 3.7 and divide by 1,000. A 20,000mAh bank holds 74Wh, and delivers about 65 per cent of it. <strong>W</strong> after charging is the highest speed, for a few minutes, with the maker\'s own charger. <strong>Silicon-carbon</strong> is a newer type that holds more. <strong>\"Up to 18 hours\"</strong> is video at low brightness in airplane mode. Halve it.</p>\n<h2>Water and dust</h2>\n<p>In IP68 the first digit is dust, up to 6, and the second is water, up to 9. X means not tested. IPX4: splashes. IP54: some dust, splashes. IP65: dust-tight, jets. IP67: immersion to a metre. IP68: deeper, as the maker states. IP69: hot high-pressure spray. Ratings apply to new devices in fresh water, and no warranty covers leaks. A high second digit does not include the lower tests automatically, so a phone that says IP68 and IP66 has passed both.</p>\n<h2>Connectivity</h2>\n<p><strong>Wi-Fi 5, 6, 6E, 7:</strong> newer is faster if your router matches. <strong>Bluetooth 5.x:</strong> the decimal rarely matters. <strong>Codecs:</strong> AAC for iPhone, LDAC for many Androids. <strong>Multipoint:</strong> two devices together. <strong>NFC:</strong> short-range, for pairing and cards. <strong>USB-C 2.0 and 3.2:</strong> the same plug at very different speeds. Cheap phones are 2.0 and cannot send a picture to a monitor. <strong>Thunderbolt and USB4:</strong> fast docks and drives.</p>\n<h2>Graphics</h2>\n<p><strong>Integrated:</strong> inside the processor, shares memory. <strong>Dedicated:</strong> its own chip and video memory. On laptops, <strong>TGP in watts</strong> decides speed as much as the name. <strong>VRAM:</strong> 6GB is the minimum and 8GB comfortable for games. <strong>DLSS, FSR, XeSS:</strong> clever upscaling. <strong>Frame generation:</strong> smoother picture, same response.</p>\n<h2>Audio</h2>\n<p><strong>Driver size (10mm, 40mm):</strong> means little across brands. <strong>ANC 45dB:</strong> a best case at one frequency. <strong>IPX4:</strong> sweat-proof. <strong>50 hours with case:</strong> look for hours per charge. <strong>Frequency 20Hz to 20kHz:</strong> printed on everything.</p>\n<h2>Televisions and monitors</h2>\n<p><strong>HDR10, Dolby Vision:</strong> formats, useful only with real brightness. <strong>Motion rate 240:</strong> marketing. Look for the panel\'s actual Hz. <strong>1ms:</strong> measured in the kindest way. <strong>HDMI 2.1:</strong> needed for 4K at 120Hz. <strong>VRR, FreeSync, G-Sync:</strong> no tearing in games.</p>\n<h2>A worked example</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Redmi Note 15 5G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Display</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td></tr><tr><td>Processor</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>256GB / 512GB UFS 2.2</td></tr><tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Primary: 108MP, f/1.7, 24 mm, OIS, PDAF, 1/1.67” CMOS sensor + Ultrawide: 8MP, f/2.2, 16 mm, 1/4.4&quot; sensor + Macro: 2MP, f/2.4 | 20MP, f/2.2, 21 mm, 1/4.0&quot; Pure...</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Android 15, HyperOS 2</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>GSM / HSPA / LTE / 5G, WiFi 6, Dual-Band, Bluetooth 5.3, A2DP, LE</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>178 gm</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>164 x 75.4 x 7.4 mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Read it as we would. Screen: large AMOLED, FHD+ class, very high peak brightness, so good outdoors. Chip: Snapdragon 6 Gen 3, comfortable class, 4nm, fine. Memory: 6 or 8GB, so choose 8. Storage: UFS 2.2, adequate. Camera: 108MP with OIS on a 1/1.67\" sensor, the important part is the OIS. Battery: about 5,500mAh, a full day and some. Conclusion: an all-rounder with no serious weakness at its price.</p>\n<h2>What the sheet never tells you</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>How warm it gets.</li>\n<li>How the cameras treat your skin tone.</li>\n<li>How many unwanted apps it carries.</li>\n<li>Whether the haptics, speaker and buttons feel good.</li>\n<li>How it behaves after two years.</li>\n<li>Where it is repaired in your city.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For those you need a shop, a friend who owns one, or a long review.</p>\n<h2>Five questions that beat any sheet</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the memory enough for how long I will keep it?</li>\n<li>Can I read the screen where I will use it?</li>\n<li>Does the battery cover my longest day?</li>\n<li>Who fixes it, and what does a screen cost?</li>\n<li>What did I not like when I held it?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can answer them, you have understood the specification better than most salespeople. Then put your two finalists in the <a href=\"/compare\">comparison tool</a> and look only at the rows you now know how to read.</p>','guides/guide-spec-sheet.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(5,20,'Should Your Next Phone Be 5G? An Honest Answer for Nepal','top-reasons-buy-5g-phone-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Many guides list the \"top reasons to buy 5G\". Here\'s a more honest answer: in Nepal today, 5G is a nice extra, not the main thing to pay for. Here\'s when it matters and when it doesn\'t.</p>\n<h2>When 5G is worth choosing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The price gap is small.</strong> 5G phones like the <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> start around Rs 34,000, often close to similar 4G models.</li>\n<li><strong>You keep phones for four or five years.</strong> Commercial 5G may reach your area during that time.</li>\n<li><strong>You want a newer chip anyway.</strong> Many 5G phones use more efficient chips than older 4G models at the same price.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When it doesn\'t matter</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Your budget is tight.</strong> A 4G phone with a better screen, battery or camera will serve you better day to day, because your phone runs on 4G on NTC and Ncell anyway. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">under Rs 25,000 picks</a> are mostly 4G for this reason.</li>\n<li><strong>The seller uses \"5G\" to justify a much higher price.</strong> Compare the rest of the specs first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We explain the current situation in more detail in <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">5G in Nepal: where things stand</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Ten seconds to your own answer</h2>\n<p>Do you live in a place where you can buy a 5G plan today? If not, 5G has no effect on your life this month. Will you keep the phone for more than three years? If yes, there is a fair chance it will matter before you replace it. Does the 5G model cost less than about Rs 3,000 more than an equally good 4G one? If yes, take it. If not, spend the difference on memory or battery. That is the whole guide, and the rest is the reasoning.</p>\n<h2>What 5G phones cost in Nepal</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.9-inch HD+ LCD, 120Hz, 825 nits peak, Goril...</td><td>Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td><td>7,500mAh; 45W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">Motorola Moto G57 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;37,499</td><td>6.72 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1050 nits (HBM)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy M17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.7 inches Super AMOLED, 90Hz, 1100 nits (HBM...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1330 (5nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (Typ), 20...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>6.79-inch AMOLED (2640×1200), 120Hz, 6,000 ni...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse c...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What you give up at the bottom of the range</h2>\n<p>Makers have a fixed budget for a cheap phone. When they add a 5G modem and the licences that come with it, they take the money from somewhere else. Compare phones between Rs 33,000 and 40,000. The 5G ones tend to have HD+ LCD screens, 4GB of base memory and plain cameras. The 4G ones at the same price, such as the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a> or <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a>, offer AMOLED panels, 8GB and sometimes stabilised cameras. (The Infinix has 5G in some versions, so check the label.) For the next two years, the second group will be nicer to use every single day.</p>\n<p>Above Rs 45,000 this problem fades. There, 5G phones are good at everything, and a 4G-only phone needs an excuse.</p>\n<h2>Efficiency: the argument nobody makes</h2>\n<p>There is a better reason to choose some 5G phones than 5G. They use newer chips made on 4nm and 6nm processes, while many 4G phones still rely on 12nm designs. A newer chip runs cooler, lasts longer and stays quick. So look at the chip and not the icon. A Snapdragon 4 Gen 2 or 6 Gen 3 phone is a better phone even in airplane mode.</p>\n<h2>Where 5G helps first</h2>\n<p>If you travel to the Gulf, Malaysia, Japan, Korea, India or Europe, 5G is ordinary there. Students and workers going abroad should choose it, with attention to bands. A phone with wide band support bought here will work better in Doha or Delhi than a cheap regional one. Look at the list of bands on the maker\'s page for the exact model number.</p>\n<p>In Nepal, the first practical benefits will be in crowded places: stadiums, bus parks, festivals, campuses. 4G runs out of capacity in a crowd, and 5G has more.</p>\n<h2>Where 5G will not help</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>In a village with one bar of 4G.</li>\n<li>Inside a concrete building, where higher frequencies struggle.</li>\n<li>When the website you want is slow.</li>\n<li>When your data pack is nearly empty. 5G empties it sooner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery life</h2>\n<p>A 5G phone in a 4G country behaves like a 4G phone, with one exception. If it keeps looking for a 5G signal at the edge of a test site, it wastes power. In SIM settings choose 4G/LTE as the preferred network. When service comes to your area, change it back.</p>\n<h2>Which bands should a Nepali buyer look for?</h2>\n<p>Nobody can say for certain until licences are issued. Around the world, n78 at about 3.5GHz is the main mid-band, with n41, n28, n1, n3 and n5 in support. A phone with those six is well prepared. Official models for South Asia usually have them. Phones made for the United States or China may miss some. And for daily life, 4G bands 3, 8, 20 and 40 and VoLTE support matter more.</p>\n<h2>Standalone, non-standalone, carrier aggregation</h2>\n<p>You will read these words in forums. Non-standalone 5G uses 4G for control and is how many countries started. Standalone is a full 5G network. Most phones sold since 2022 support both. Aggregation joins several bands for speed. None of it should decide a purchase under Rs 1 lakh.</p>\n<h2>Will my 5G phone need an update to work here?</h2>\n<p>Possibly. In India, several brands had to release software updates to enable 5G on phones that already had the hardware. Brands with active support did so quickly. It is one more reason to prefer an official unit of a brand that updates its phones. A grey import running another region\'s software may wait for ever.</p>\n<h2>What operators have said</h2>\n<p>NTC and Ncell have run trials, and the subject returns in the news every few months. Cost, spectrum and demand are the obstacles, and they are described in <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">our article on 5G in Nepal</a>. A reasonable planning assumption is that limited commercial service in the largest cities is possible in the next few years, and wide coverage will take much longer.</p>\n<h2>Advice by type of buyer</h2>\n<p><strong>Parent, light use, keeps phones long.</strong> Choose service and battery. A 4G Galaxy A07 is fine.</p>\n<p><strong>Student with Rs 35,000.</strong> Screen, speed and storage first. 5G if it is free.</p>\n<p><strong>Professional with Rs 50,000 or more.</strong> 5G will be included. Do not pay attention to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Going abroad within a year.</strong> Choose 5G with wide bands.</p>\n<p><strong>Reseller of phones every year.</strong> 5G models hold value a little better.</p>\n<p><strong>Gamer.</strong> Chip and cooling. Delay depends on the network and not on the letter G.</p>\n<h2>Four myths</h2>\n<p><strong>\"4G will be switched off.\"</strong> Not for many years. Operators switch off 2G and 3G first, and even those remain in places.</p>\n<p><strong>\"5G makes Wi-Fi faster.\"</strong> They are unrelated.</p>\n<p><strong>\"A 5G SIM is needed, so buy now.\"</strong> Operators will tell customers if a new SIM is required. Do not buy one from a stranger.</p>\n<p><strong>\"5G is harmful.\"</strong> Exposure limits are set by international bodies, and phones must follow them.</p>\n<h2>Better ways to get faster internet today</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn on VoLTE and set the network to 4G preferred.</li>\n<li>Restart the phone weekly. It helps it find a better cell.</li>\n<li>Compare NTC and Ncell at your home and office with a friend\'s SIM.</li>\n<li>Use Wi-Fi at home on 5GHz, with the router in the middle.</li>\n<li>Remove apps that use background data.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>In the end</h2>\n<p>5G is like a spare room. Nice to have if it comes with the house, and a poor reason to buy a worse house. Choose the phone you would buy if the icon did not exist, and if it happens to say 5G, smile. Our guides to phones <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">under Rs 40,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-50000-nepal\">under Rs 50,000</a> follow that rule.</p>','guides/guide-5g.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(6,20,'How to Use Git Infosys to Find the Right Price','how-to-use-price-tracker-git-infosys','how-to','<p>Prices in Nepal move around more than most people realise, especially in 2026. Here\'s how to use the site to check you\'re paying a fair price before you buy.</p>\n<h2>1. Search the way you\'d ask a friend</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>Ctrl K</strong> (or tap the search bar) and type what you want, for example \"gaming laptop under 2 lakh\", \"4k monitor\" or \"iphone 17 pro\". Search understands budgets in lakh and k, specs like \"32gb\" or \"oled\", and copes with typos.</p>\n<h2>2. Check the price history on the product page</h2>\n<p>Every product page has a <strong>Price history</strong> section showing the dated prices we\'ve recorded. If today\'s price is well above what it was recently, it may be worth waiting.</p>\n<h2>3. Pick the right configuration</h2>\n<p>Many devices come in several versions, with more storage or a different graphics card. Choose one on the product page and the price updates, so you can see exactly what the extra storage costs.</p>\n<h2>4. Watch recent changes</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/price-tracker\">Price Tracker</a> page lists devices whose prices have moved recently, up or down.</p>\n<h2>5. Compare before you commit</h2>\n<p>Stuck between two models? <a href=\"/compare\">Compare</a> lines them up spec by spec. Product pages also link to the store\'s own listing, where the stock status is shown.</p>\n<p>One tip: around Dashain and Tihar, shops often run offers. Check the price history first, so a \"discount\" really is one.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Why prices need tracking at all</h2>\n<p>In a country where most goods are imported, a price is not a fixed thing. It moves with the dollar, with customs rules, with the size of a distributor\'s last shipment and with the mood before a festival. Two shops on the same street may hold stock bought three months apart at different costs. So the useful question is not \"what does it cost?\" but \"what has it cost lately, and where does today\'s number sit?\"</p>\n<h2>Reading a product page</h2>\n<p>Every page is arranged in the same order, which makes it quick to scan.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Name and price.</strong> If there are several versions, the price shown belongs to the one selected.</li>\n<li><strong>Versions.</strong> Memory, storage, graphics card or size. Click each to see the difference in rupees.</li>\n<li><strong>Status.</strong> On sale, expected, announced or discontinued. An expected price is our estimate and is marked so.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy button.</strong> Where a partner shop stocks it, the button opens that shop\'s page. We say who the shop is.</li>\n<li><strong>Specification.</strong> Grouped, with the date it was last checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Price history.</strong> Dated entries. When a price changes we add a line and leave the old one.</li>\n<li><strong>Similar devices.</strong> Others near the same price.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>How to judge a history</h2>\n<p><strong>A flat line</strong> means a stable official price. Discounts will come as gifts or exchange bonuses.</p>\n<p><strong>A step up</strong> usually follows a new shipment or a change in cost. It rarely reverses.</p>\n<p><strong>A step down</strong> can be a permanent cut, a sale, or a clearance before a new model. Look at the date. If a successor has just been announced, expect more.</p>\n<p><strong>A single entry</strong> means that we have recorded it once. Use it as a reference, and compare with two shops.</p>\n<h2>Using search like a conversation</h2>\n<p>Type what you would say to a friend. \"Phone under 40k with big battery\", \"gaming laptop 2 lakh\", \"earbuds under 5000\", \"iphone 17 256\". The search reads lakh and k, memory sizes and common misspellings. If you are looking for a kind of thing and not a model, start with a category and use the filters for brand, price and specification.</p>\n<h2>Comparing two or more devices</h2>\n<p>Open <a href=\"/compare\">Compare</a> and choose your finalists. Look first at the rows that differ, and among those at the rows that matter to you. We suggest five: memory, storage, battery, main camera or graphics chip, and years of updates. If a row is blank, the maker has not published it or we have not confirmed it. Do not read it as a zero.</p>\n<h2>A worked example: buying a laptop for college</h2>\n<p>Suppose your budget is Rs 1,00,000. Search for \"laptop under 1 lakh 16gb\". Open the three that look right. Check which versions you are seeing, as a name can hide 8GB and 16GB models. Read the price history of each. If one has fallen by Rs 5,000 in the last month, ask why. Compare the three. Read our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">guide for that budget</a>. Then call two shops with the exact model code and ask for a cash price and the warranty terms. The whole exercise takes forty minutes and can save you Rs 10,000.</p>\n<h2>A worked example: waiting for a phone</h2>\n<p>You want a Galaxy S26 Ultra and are not in a hurry. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">product page</a> shows its history. The <a href=\"/upcoming-launches\">upcoming launches</a> page shows that a successor is expected in the first months of next year. Past generations fell after a launch. So add the phone to your wishlist, check the <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a> every week or two, and set a number at which you will buy. Having the number in advance stops you from being talked into something else.</p>\n<h2>Wishlist and account</h2>\n<p>With a free account you can save devices and return to them. We do not sell your details, and you can read what we store in the <a href=\"/privacy-policy\">privacy policy</a>. You can use the rest of the site without signing in.</p>\n<h2>EMI estimates</h2>\n<p>On product pages you can see what a price means per month for different periods. It is arithmetic, not an offer from a bank. Real plans add a processing fee or interest, and need approval. Use the estimate to decide whether a price is comfortable. As a rule, if the instalment is more than a tenth of your monthly income, the gadget is too expensive.</p>\n<h2>The PC builder</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">custom PC builder</a> adds up parts and warns about common mismatches, such as a processor that does not fit a board or a power supply that is too small. Use it to see what a budget can do, then ask a shop to match the list.</p>\n<h2>Upcoming and estimated prices</h2>\n<p>For devices that are not yet sold in Nepal we sometimes give an estimate. It is based on the foreign price, the usual taxes and margins, and what the previous model cost here. It is there to help you plan. When the official price is announced, it replaces the estimate and the date is shown. Please do not plan a purchase to the last thousand on an estimate.</p>\n<h2>Why a shop may quote something different</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It has older or newer stock.</li>\n<li>It is another version or region.</li>\n<li>The quotation includes or excludes VAT.</li>\n<li>A bank, exchange or festival offer has been counted.</li>\n<li>It is a grey unit without local warranty.</li>\n<li>Our entry is out of date.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the last case, tell us through the <a href=\"/contact\">contact page</a> with the shop\'s name and the date, and we will check.</p>\n<h2>Festival discounts</h2>\n<p>Before Dashain and Tihar, open the history of what you want and write down the price. When the banners appear, subtract. The method is explained, with the tricks to look out for, in <a href=\"/blog/dashain-tihar-tech-deals-how-to-spot-a-real-discount\">how to spot a real discount</a>.</p>\n<h2>What the site does not do</h2>\n<p>We cannot guarantee that a shop will honour a listed price, and we do not give scores for devices we have not used as though we had tested them. Where an opinion is based on specifications and price, we say so. Business relationships, such as with our partner ONIN Infosys, are named where they matter.</p>\n<h2>On a phone</h2>\n<p>The site works on slow connections. Tables scroll sideways. On a phone the filters open from the bottom. You can add the site to your home screen from the browser menu and use it like an app in the shop, which is where it is most useful: search the model while the seller is talking.</p>\n<h2>Five habits of people who pay less</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>They decide on needs before models.</li>\n<li>They know the price of the last two months.</li>\n<li>They compare the exact version.</li>\n<li>They ask two shops and are polite to both.</li>\n<li>They are ready to wait a week.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The tracker helps with the second and third. The rest is up to you.</p>','guides/guide-price-tracker.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(7,20,'How to Choose a Smartwatch or Fitness Band in Nepal (2026)','smartwatch-buying-guide-nepal-2024','buying-guide','<p>The first question isn\'t which watch to buy. It\'s what you want it for, because the answers point to very different watches.</p>\n<h2>If you mainly want fitness and sleep tracking</h2>\n<p>A band or a simple watch from Xiaomi, Amazfit or Huawei does this well, often lasts one to two weeks per charge, and costs a fraction of a premium smartwatch. Built-in GPS is the feature worth paying a little more for. Then your runs and walks are tracked accurately without carrying your phone.</p>\n<h2>If you want a \"smart\" watch</h2>\n<p>Replying to messages, paying, and using apps work best when the watch matches your phone: an <strong>Apple Watch with an iPhone</strong>, or a <strong>Galaxy Watch with a Samsung phone</strong>. The trade-off is battery life, usually a day or two.</p>\n<h2>If you trek or travel</h2>\n<p>Look for dual-band GPS, a tough build and a long battery life. Watches like the Amazfit T-Rex series are built for multi-day treks where you won\'t see a charger.</p>\n<h2>Things to check in the shop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The screen is readable in sunlight (AMOLED helps).</li>\n<li>The strap is comfortable and can be swapped for a standard one.</li>\n<li>The companion app works with your phone, and you\'re comfortable with the data it collects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For specific models and prices, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">best smartwatches under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Start with a notebook, not a shop</h2>\n<p>Write down what annoyed you this month. Missed calls in the kitchen? A phone that comes out of your pocket forty times a day? Poor sleep? A running route you could not record? Each of those points to a feature. A list of three annoyances is a better shopping guide than a list of thirty features.</p>\n<h2>The four families of watch</h2>\n<p><strong>Bands.</strong> Slim, light, a week or two of battery, Rs 3,500 to 9,000. Excellent for sleep and steps. The <a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Galaxy Fit 3</a> and <a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Huawei Band 10</a> are typical.</p>\n<p><strong>Fitness watches.</strong> Larger screen, GPS from about Rs 10,000, ten days or more of battery. Redmi, CMF, Amazfit and Huawei. They show messages but cannot run many apps.</p>\n<p><strong>Smartwatches.</strong> Galaxy Watch, Apple Watch and Wear OS models. Replies, apps, maps and assistants. One to two days of battery. New ones start at about Rs 22,000 with the Galaxy Watch FE.</p>\n<p><strong>Sports and expedition watches.</strong> Garmin, Coros, Amazfit T-Rex and Huawei Ultimate. Long GPS life, offline maps, physical buttons. From Rs 30,000 to more than a lakh.</p>\n<p>Nearly every disappointment comes from buying one family and expecting another.</p>\n<h2>Your phone decides half of it</h2>\n<p>An Apple Watch works only with an iPhone. A Galaxy Watch works only with Android and is best with a Samsung. Others work with both, but on an iPhone none of them can reply to messages. If you plan to change phone brand next year, choose a watch that does not care.</p>\n<h2>Features explained without marketing</h2>\n<p><strong>GPS.</strong> Built-in records your route without a phone. Connected uses the phone. Dual-band is more accurate among buildings and trees.</p>\n<p><strong>AMOLED.</strong> Brighter, with an always-on option. Worth paying for.</p>\n<p><strong>Heart rate.</strong> Good at rest, variable in exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>SpO2.</strong> A rough indication. A fingertip device is better for trekking.</p>\n<p><strong>ECG.</strong> On premium watches, sometimes disabled by region.</p>\n<p><strong>Barometer.</strong> Counts climb and warns of weather changes.</p>\n<p><strong>NFC.</strong> Contactless payment support in Nepal is limited, so do not pay extra for it.</p>\n<p><strong>LTE.</strong> Watches with a SIM need operator support. Check before assuming that it will work here.</p>\n<h2>What Nepali conditions ask for</h2>\n<p>Bright sun, dust, sweat, rain, and long days away from a socket. That means at least 800 nits, 5ATM water resistance, a strap that dries quickly and a battery of five days or more. For hills, a barometer and dual-band GPS. For motorbike commuters, vibration strong enough to feel through a jacket.</p>\n<h2>Reading accuracy claims</h2>\n<p>Every maker says its sensor is precise. In practice, accuracy depends on fit. Wear it snugly, a finger\'s width above the wrist bone, with clean skin. Warm up before judging heart rate. Use trends over weeks and not single readings. Ignore calories. And never use a watch to diagnose yourself.</p>\n<h2>Battery life, real figures</h2>\n<p>Divide the maker\'s number by two for always-on use with daily exercise. If the result is still above five days, you will not think about charging. If it is under two, decide when you will charge it every day: many people use the time they shower and dress.</p>\n<h2>Size and comfort</h2>\n<p>Watches under 35 grams disappear. Above 50 grams you notice them in bed. Case sizes near 42mm suit most wrists. Try on the actual watch, as lug shape matters. If you have sensitive skin, look for titanium or plastic backs and a fabric strap.</p>\n<h2>Apps and data</h2>\n<p>You will open the companion app more than you expect. Check that it is in Play Store or App Store, that it does not show advertisements, that it exports to Strava or Apple Health, and that your history survives if you change phones. Zepp, Mi Fitness, Huawei Health and Samsung Health are mature. Many unbranded apps are not, and they collect more than they should.</p>\n<h2>Total cost</h2>\n<p>Add a spare strap (Rs 500 to 1,500), a glass protector for round faces (Rs 300) and a second charger (Rs 800 to 2,000). Some platforms put deeper analysis behind a subscription. Look at what is free.</p>\n<h2>Three ready answers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"I just want steps, sleep and calls.\"</strong> Redmi Watch 5 Active at about Rs 4,500, or Honor Watch X5i at Rs 9,000.</li>\n<li><strong>\"I run and want to leave the phone.\"</strong> Redmi Watch 6 Active at Rs 11,000, CMF Watch 3 Pro at Rs 13,000.</li>\n<li><strong>\"I want to reply and use apps.\"</strong> Galaxy Watch with a Samsung, Apple Watch with an iPhone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>In the shop: seven checks</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Raise your wrist. Does the screen wake every time?</li>\n<li>Take it to the door. Can you read it?</li>\n<li>Pair it with your own phone.</li>\n<li>Send yourself a message in Nepali.</li>\n<li>Make a call from the wrist.</li>\n<li>Start an outdoor walk and see how long GPS takes to lock.</li>\n<li>Ask for the warranty card and the service address.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Watches for elders</h2>\n<p>Large text, loud vibration, simple faces and emergency features. Fall detection and SOS on Samsung and Apple devices can alert family. Set it up yourself, add contacts, and test it. A long battery matters more than features, as an uncharged watch helps nobody.</p>\n<h2>Watches for children</h2>\n<p>Ordinary smartwatches are not trackers. Children\'s watches with a SIM allow calls and location, and need a data plan and an honest look at privacy. Choose a brand with a real company behind its servers.</p>\n<h2>When not to buy one</h2>\n<p>If you already feel interrupted all day, a watch may add to it. If you dislike charging things, a band or a mechanical watch will make you happier. And if you only want motivation, try counting steps on your phone for a month first.</p>\n<h2>Where to go next</h2>\n<p>Pick your budget: <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-5000-nepal\">Rs 5,000</a>, <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-10000-nepal\">Rs 10,000</a>, <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal\">Rs 20,000</a> or <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">Rs 30,000</a>. Each page compares current models and prices in Nepal.</p>\n<h2>The first month</h2>\n<p>Give a new watch four weeks before judging it. In the first, turn almost everything off and add notifications one by one. In the second, look at sleep and nothing else. In the third, set one goal, such as a daily walk after dinner. In the fourth, decide whether it changed anything. If it did, you chose well, whatever the price. If it did not, sell it while it still looks new, and be glad to know yourself a little better.</p>','guides/guide-7.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(31,20,'Best Phones in Nepal by Budget (November 2026)','best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget','buying-guide','<p>These picks use prices checked on 22 November 2026. Each one links to the full specs, every storage option and the price history we\'ve recorded. Prices change often, so check the product page before you buy.</p>\n\n<h2>Under Rs 40,000</h2>\n<p><strong><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> — from Rs 33,999.</strong> A 7,500mAh battery and a 6.9-inch 120Hz screen are rare at this price. The screen is HD+ LCD rather than AMOLED, which is the main compromise.</p>\n\n<h2>Rs 40,000 – 60,000</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> — Rs 44,999.</strong> A Super AMOLED screen, an OIS main camera and Samsung\'s software support. The safe brand choice.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> — Rs 54,999.</strong> An 8,300mAh battery, a 6,000-nit screen and drop protection rated to 2.5m. The toughest phone here.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro</a> — Rs 57,999.</strong> An 8,340mAh battery with 67W charging and IP68/IP69K protection.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Rs 60,000 – 80,000</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> — Rs 69,999.</strong> 10,000mAh and 100W charging. Read our <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-pro-max-review-nepal\">review</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> — Rs 69,999.</strong> The only phone in this range with a 3.5x periscope zoom.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Galaxy A37 5G</a> — Rs 74,999.</strong> IP68 and a 50MP OIS camera with long Samsung support.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a> — Rs 76,999.</strong> A 10,001mAh battery and a 144Hz screen.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Rs 80,000 – 1,50,000</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a> — Rs 98,499.</strong> Flagship-level speed for under Rs 1 lakh.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3\">Nothing Phone 3</a> — Rs 1,09,999.</strong> For people who want something that looks different.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a> — Rs 1,32,499.</strong> The cheapest current iPhone.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> — Rs 1,39,999.</strong> A compact Samsung flagship.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Flagships</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> — Rs 2,24,999.</strong> The most complete Android phone. <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra-review-nepal\">Review</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> or <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a> — Rs 1,99,999 each.</strong> Camera-first flagships for less than the Samsung.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a> — Rs 2,79,900.</strong> The best iPhone. <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-18-pro-max-review-nepal\">Review</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Worth waiting for?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s27-ultra\">Galaxy S27 Ultra</a> is expected in January–February 2027, and the <a href=\"/products/iphone-18\">iPhone 18</a> in spring 2027. If you\'re buying at the very top end and can wait a few months, it may be worth it. Browse all <a href=\"/products?status=upcoming\">upcoming phones</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>How we choose</h2>\n<p>We do not score phones by adding up specifications. Our picks come from three questions. Is the price in Nepal fair compared with everything else on the shelf today? Does the phone have a weakness that would annoy an ordinary owner every week? And can it be serviced here? A phone that wins on paper and cannot be repaired in Pokhara does not make the list, and neither does one that was a bargain at launch and has since become expensive. Prices come from published dealer lists and brand pages, and each device page shows its history.</p>\n<h2>How much should you spend?</h2>\n<p>A useful rule is to think in months. Divide the price by the number of months you will keep the phone. A Rs 36,000 phone kept for three years is Rs 1,000 a month. A Rs 1,40,000 phone kept for five years is about Rs 2,300, and a Rs 15,000 phone that becomes unbearable after eighteen months is about Rs 830. Seen this way, the cheapest phone is rarely much cheaper, and the middle of the market is where most of us should look.</p>\n<h2>The middle of the market in detail</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.9-inch HD+ LCD, 120Hz, 825 nits peak, Goril...</td><td>Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td><td>7,500mAh; 45W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>6.79-inch AMOLED (2640×1200), 120Hz, 6,000 ni...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse c...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 17...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,50...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;98,499</td><td>6.83-inch AMOLED, 165Hz, Dolby Vision, HDR10+</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,400mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Samsung Galaxy S26</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>6.3-inch FHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm) in most markets; Snapdragon...</td><td>12GB</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,32,499</td><td>6.1-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1,200 nits</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>4,005mAh; 15W MagSafe/Qi2 wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Read this before you pick a brand</h2>\n<p><strong>Samsung</strong> has the widest service and the longest updates, with modest batteries and slower charging. <strong>Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO</strong> give the most hardware for your money, and software that needs some tidying. <strong>vivo and OPPO</strong> make handsome phones with flattering cameras and large dealer networks. <strong>Realme</strong> leans towards battery and charging. <strong>Honor</strong> makes slim, tough phones with huge batteries, and its service is expanding. <strong>OnePlus and Nothing</strong> have the cleanest software among the Chinese-owned makers and fewer service points. <strong>Infinix and Tecno</strong> are strongest under Rs 40,000. <strong>Apple</strong> offers longevity, video quality and resale, at a price.</p>\n<p>Our brand guides have full price lists, for example <a href=\"/guides/samsung-phone-price-in-nepal\">Samsung</a>, <a href=\"/guides/xiaomi-phone-price-in-nepal\">Xiaomi</a>, <a href=\"/guides/vivo-phone-price-in-nepal\">vivo</a> and <a href=\"/guides/apple-phone-price-in-nepal\">Apple</a>.</p>\n<h2>Match the phone to the person</h2>\n<p><strong>For a parent:</strong> a big, bright screen, loud speaker, long battery and nearby service. Galaxy A07 or A17, vivo Y series.</p>\n<p><strong>For a college student:</strong> a smooth screen, 8GB of memory and enough speed for games. Infinix Note 50s, Redmi Note 15 5G, POCO F7.</p>\n<p><strong>For someone who sells online:</strong> a stabilised main camera and good front camera. Moto G96, vivo V series, Galaxy A37.</p>\n<p><strong>For a traveller or guide:</strong> battery and toughness. Honor X9d, Redmi Note 17 Pro, Realme P4 Power.</p>\n<p><strong>For a photographer:</strong> vivo X300 Pro, Xiaomi 17 Ultra, OPPO Find X9 Pro.</p>\n<p><strong>For someone who never wants to think about it:</strong> iPhone 17, Galaxy S26, OnePlus 15R.</p>\n<h2>Features that matter, ranked</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Memory and storage. They decide how the phone feels in year three.</li>\n<li>Battery. Above 6,000mAh, most people stop worrying.</li>\n<li>Main camera with optical stabilisation.</li>\n<li>Screen brightness, then smoothness.</li>\n<li>Years of security updates.</li>\n<li>Charging speed, from 33W upwards.</li>\n<li>Water and dust protection.</li>\n<li>5G, if your area is likely to get it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Features that matter less than advertised</h2>\n<p>Macro and depth cameras. Memory extension. Peak brightness in HDR. Ultra-fast charging above 80W. The number of AI tools. Benchmark scores. A processor two names higher than the one you can afford. None of these will change your Tuesday.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Prices follow a calendar. Samsung launches its S series in the first months of the year and its foldables in July, and older models fall before those dates. iPhones change in September, with discounts on the previous series from October. Dashain and Tihar bring broad offers, described in <a href=\"/blog/dashain-tihar-tech-deals-how-to-spot-a-real-discount\">our festival deals article</a>. In 2026, component costs pushed many prices upwards, so waiting is not always rewarded. If your phone is broken, buy now. If it works, wait for a sale.</p>\n<h2>Buying safely</h2>\n<p>Whichever phone you choose, three checks protect your money. The IMEI must be registered, the bill must carry it, and the warranty must belong to the brand\'s distributor in Nepal. Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">checking an official phone</a> shows how in five minutes.</p>\n<h2>New, used or refurbished?</h2>\n<p>A one-year-old flagship often beats a new mid-range phone at the same price on camera and build, and loses on battery and warranty. If you can judge a used phone or know the owner, it is worth considering above Rs 50,000. Below that, new phones are so capable that the saving is rarely worth the risk. Refurbished phones sold by shops vary widely. Ask who refurbished it, what was replaced and how long the guarantee lasts.</p>\n<h2>Keeping it longer</h2>\n<p>The best way to save money on phones is to buy fewer of them. A case, a screen protector, a charge limit of 80 per cent at night, 20GB of free space and a battery replacement in the third year can stretch almost any phone here by a year or two. At Rs 4,000 to 8,000, a new battery is the best value upgrade in the whole market.</p>\n<h2>How often this page changes</h2>\n<p>We revisit it as prices move and new phones arrive in Nepal. If you spot a price that no longer matches your local shop, tell us through the <a href=\"/contact\">contact page</a> and we will check it. For phones announced but not yet on sale, see <a href=\"/upcoming-launches\">upcoming launches</a>.</p>','guides/guide-best-phones.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(32,20,'Best Laptops in Nepal for Students, Work and Gaming (2026)','best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers','buying-guide','<p>Laptop prices in Nepal rose during 2026, which makes choosing the right one first time more important. Prices below were checked on 22 November 2026.</p>\n\n<h2>Three rules before you buy</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>16GB of RAM is the minimum</strong> for a laptop you want to keep for four or more years. 8GB is fine only for study and browsing.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy from an authorised seller</strong> and get the warranty terms in writing. Grey imports may be cheaper, but repairs can be a problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Think about power cuts.</strong> Battery life matters more here than spec sheets suggest.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>For students on a budget</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> — Rs 1,34,999.</strong> Apple\'s cheapest laptop, with an A18 Pro chip and 8GB of memory. Fine for classes, writing and browsing. The 256GB model has no Touch ID or keyboard backlight.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026\">Dell XPS 13 (2026)</a> — Rs 1,34,999.</strong> A premium build and a 2.5K touchscreen, but only 8GB of RAM.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition</a> — Rs 1,64,999.</strong> Our value pick: 16GB, an OLED screen and Intel\'s efficient Core Ultra 5 226V.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>For work that lasts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air 13 (M5)</a> — Rs 2,34,900.</strong> Our top recommendation for most people. <a href=\"/reviews/macbook-air-m5-13-review-nepal\">Read the review</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3406ga\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED</a> — Rs 2,54,999.</strong> 32GB and 1TB with an OLED touchscreen. The Windows alternative.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>For gaming</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a> — from Rs 1,49,000.</strong> The cheapest way into RTX gaming. Pick the RTX 4050 version. <a href=\"/reviews/hp-victus-15-review-nepal\">Review</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> — Rs 1,94,999.</strong> An RTX 5050 at 100W and a 100% sRGB screen.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai\">Acer Nitro V 16 AI</a> — Rs 2,30,999</strong> and <strong><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Lenovo Legion 5</a> — Rs 2,59,999.</strong> The mid-range step up.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>For video editors and developers</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">MacBook Pro 14 (M5)</a> (Rs 3,76,500) adds a fan and a 120Hz XDR display. Apple has also announced an <a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m6\">M6 model</a>, expected from around Rs 3,85,999, which isn\'t in Nepal yet.</p>\n<p>Not sure between two? <a href=\"/compare\">Compare any two laptops</a> side by side.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Where the price list stands today</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,499</td><td>Liquid Retina display, 13.0-inch (diagonal) L...</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td><td>8GB unified memory</td><td>256GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026\">Dell XPS 13 (2026)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,34,999</td><td>13.4-inch 2.5K (2408×1506) IPS touch, 500 nit...</td><td>Intel Core 5 320 (6 cores, up to 4.6GHz)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5x-7467 (soldered)</td><td>512GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>14-inch WUXGA OLED, 600 nits, 100% DCI-P3</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 226V (Intel Arc 130V graph...</td><td>16GB LPDDR5x-8533</td><td>512GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">Apple MacBook Air 13 (M5, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>13.6-inch (diagonal) LED-backlit display with...</td><td>Apple M5 (10-core CPU, 8/10-core GPU)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X Unified Memory</td><td>512GB NVMe SSD 6,473 MB/s Read speeds and 6,5...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3406ga\">ASUS Zenbook 14 OLED UM3406GA (Ryzen AI 7 445, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,999</td><td>14.0-inch, WUXGA (1920 x 1200) OLED 16:10 asp...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ AI 7 445 Processor 2.0GHz (14MB Ca...</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X on board</td><td>512GB M.2 NVMe™ PCIe® 4.0 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>512GB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>15.6&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare,...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB...</td><td>1x 16GB SO-DIMM DDR5-5600</td><td>512GB SSD M.2 2242 PCIe® 4.0x4 NVMe®</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai\">Acer Nitro V 16 AI</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,30,999</td><td>16-inch WUXGA IPS, 180Hz, 300 nits, 100% sRGB</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 350</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>1TB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Lenovo Legion 5 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,999</td><td>15.3-inch WUXGA IPS, 165Hz, 300 nits, 100% sR...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 260 (8 cores, up to 5.1GHz)</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>512GB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table uses current catalogue prices, so a few figures differ slightly from those quoted in the picks above. Where they differ, trust the table and the product page.</p>\n<h2>Begin with your course or job</h2>\n<p><strong>Management, humanities, law, education.</strong> Documents, presentations, video calls. A processor of any recent type, 16GB if possible, a comfortable keyboard and a good battery. Rs 80,000 to 1,00,000 is enough. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">one-lakh guide</a> is the right page for you.</p>\n<p><strong>Computing and data.</strong> Cores and memory. Six cores at the least, eight if you can, 16GB or more. The Ryzen 7 8840HS machines at Rs 1.3 lakh are very good value.</p>\n<p><strong>Civil, mechanical, architecture.</strong> A graphics card from the third year onwards. Start with an RTX laptop such as the LOQ 15 if you can afford to buy once. Otherwise use a 16GB laptop for two years and upgrade.</p>\n<p><strong>Medicine and nursing.</strong> Light weight, long battery, good screen for images. Yoga Slim 7i Aura, MacBook Neo or a used MacBook Air.</p>\n<p><strong>Media, film and design.</strong> Colour-accurate screen, 16 to 32GB, fast drive. MacBook Air M5, or a Windows laptop with an RTX chip and 100% sRGB.</p>\n<p><strong>Office and remote work.</strong> Webcam, microphone, keyboard, USB-C and a monitor at home. Business lines such as ProBook, Latitude and ThinkPad are better value than they look.</p>\n<h2>Understanding processor names in 2026</h2>\n<p>Intel now sells Core and Core Ultra in several series that are unrelated to each other. Letters at the end tell you more than the numbers. <strong>V</strong> means very efficient with memory built in. <strong>U</strong> is low power. <strong>H</strong> is stronger and hotter. <strong>HX</strong> is desktop-class. AMD uses Ryzen 200, Ryzen AI 300 and 400, along with many renamed older chips such as the 7520U and 7430U. When in doubt, count the cores and check the launch year.</p>\n<h2>Windows, Mac or Arm?</h2>\n<p>Windows on Intel or AMD runs everything and is the safe choice for Nepali software, from Tally to government signing tools. A Mac lasts longer on battery and holds its value. Some Windows-only programs will not run. Windows on Snapdragon gives Mac-like battery with more limits, described in <a href=\"/news/qualcomm-oryon-snapdragon-x-elite-laptops-nepal-battery-benchmarks\">our Snapdragon laptop article</a>. Write down the programs you must have before choosing.</p>\n<h2>Screen size and weight</h2>\n<p>Fourteen inches is the best compromise if you carry the laptop daily. Fifteen or sixteen inches is easier on the eyes at a desk and brings a number pad. Below 1.4kg you stop noticing it in a bag. Above 2kg you leave it at home. Also think about the charger: a USB-C brick of 65W is light, and a gaming brick is as heavy as a book.</p>\n<h2>How long should it last?</h2>\n<p>A well-chosen laptop should serve five years. The parts that end its life are usually memory, battery and hinges. So buy enough memory on the first day, choose a model whose battery can be replaced, and open the lid from the middle. A yearly clean and a thermal paste change in the third year keep it quiet and quick.</p>\n<h2>Buying new against buying used</h2>\n<p>Up to Rs 80,000 the used business market often beats new laptops. From a lakh upwards, new machines make more sense because of warranty, batteries and efficiency. Used MacBook Airs with 16GB are an exception and remain attractive. Used gaming laptops are a risk, as heat ages them.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>HP, Dell, Lenovo, Asus and Acer have official service here. MSI and others work through distributors. Apple has authorised resellers and service providers. Ask who repairs the laptop, how long the parts take and whether the warranty is printed in the maker\'s system. Enter the serial number on the brand\'s site in the shop. Our <a href=\"/about\">partners</a> and many other shops sell both official and imported stock, and an honest seller will tell you which one you are looking at.</p>\n<h2>What the prices do through the year</h2>\n<p>In 2026 laptop prices moved upwards with the cost of memory and storage. Configurations with 16GB that were Rs 85,000 in January are nearer a lakh now. New sessions in the colleges, Dashain and the western calendar\'s year end bring offers. If you need a laptop for a course, buy at least two weeks before classes start, as stock of good configurations disappears first.</p>\n<h2>Things to buy with it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A padded bag or sleeve.</li>\n<li>A wireless mouse. Your wrist will thank you.</li>\n<li>A 1TB external drive for backup. Two copies of every important file, one of them away from the laptop.</li>\n<li>A surge protector.</li>\n<li>A USB-C hub if your laptop lacks HDMI.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ten-minute test in the shop</h2>\n<p>Type a paragraph. Scroll a long page with the touchpad. Play a video at full volume. Look at a white and a black screen. Turn on the webcam. Open Task Manager and read the memory and processor. Run the battery report. Plug in the charger and see that it charges. Close the lid, open it and see how quickly it wakes. Feel the base after all that. If nothing annoyed you, it is a good laptop for you.</p>\n<h2>If you are still undecided</h2>\n<p>Tell us your course, budget and what you carry every day through the <a href=\"/contact\">contact page</a>, or line up two candidates in the <a href=\"/compare\">comparison tool</a>. Nine times out of ten the answer is the one with more memory.</p>','guides/guide-best-laptops.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(33,20,'How to Check a Phone Is Official in Nepal Before You Buy','how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal','how-to','<p>Nepal blocks mobile phones that aren\'t registered with the Nepal Telecommunications Authority (NTA). That\'s the job of its Mobile Device Management System (MDMS). A cheap phone from an unofficial seller can stop working on Nepali networks, and it usually comes without a valid local warranty. These checks take five minutes in the shop.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Find the IMEI</h2>\n<p>Dial <strong>*#06#</strong> on the phone. It shows the IMEI number, or two numbers on a dual-SIM phone. Check that it matches the IMEI printed on the box, and the one under Settings › About phone.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Check the IMEI with NTA</h2>\n<p>Enter the IMEI on NTA\'s MDMS website (<a href=\"https://mdms.nta.gov.np\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">mdms.nta.gov.np</a>). An officially imported phone should already be registered. If it isn\'t, ask the seller why before you pay.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Ask for a VAT bill and the warranty card</h2>\n<p>An official retailer gives you a VAT bill and the brand\'s warranty card, usually from the brand\'s authorised distributor in Nepal. Without these, warranty repairs can be refused.</p>\n\n<h2>4. For iPhones, check Apple\'s coverage page</h2>\n<p>Enter the serial number at <a href=\"https://checkcoverage.apple.com\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">checkcoverage.apple.com</a>. It confirms the model and whether the warranty has already started. If it began months ago, the phone isn\'t new.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Phones bought abroad</h2>\n<p>If you bring a phone back from abroad, register it through the MDMS system so it keeps working on Nepali SIM cards. Follow the current instructions on NTA\'s site, because the process has changed over the years.</p>\n\n<h2>Signs of a risky deal</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A price far below what our product pages show. Compare, for example, the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> or <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> pages.</li>\n<li>The seller won\'t let you check the IMEI before paying.</li>\n<li>The box has been resealed, or the IMEI on the box doesn\'t match the phone.</li>\n</ul>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>What \"official\" means, and what it does not</h2>\n<p>An official phone was imported by a company authorised by the brand, taxes were paid, its IMEI was registered, and the brand\'s local service network will repair it under warranty. A grey phone was brought in some other way. It can be new and genuine. It may also be refurbished, locked, stolen or made for another market. The word \"original\", which sellers love, says nothing about any of this. A grey phone is original too.</p>\n<h2>How MDMS works</h2>\n<p>Every phone that uses a SIM has one or two IMEI numbers. Importers submit the numbers of the phones they bring. The system compares them with the phones that appear on the networks. One that is not registered can be warned, limited or blocked according to the rules in force. The regulator has tightened and relaxed enforcement at different times, so the fact that a grey phone works today does not promise that it will work next year.</p>\n<h2>The check, step by step</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Dial <strong>*#06#</strong>. Write down both numbers, or take a photo.</li>\n<li>Turn the box over. The sticker must show the same numbers.</li>\n<li>Open Settings, About phone, Status. The numbers must match again. A mismatch between dialler and settings suggests tampering.</li>\n<li>Open the MDMS site at mdms.nta.gov.np and enter the IMEI.</li>\n<li>Read the result carefully. It should say that the device is registered.</li>\n<li>For a dual-SIM phone, check the second IMEI as well.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the website does not open in the shop, ask a friend to check from home, or wait. Do not accept \"the site is always down\".</p>\n<h2>Clues on the box</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Importer\'s sticker.</strong> Official phones carry the distributor\'s name, address and often a MRP in Nepali rupees.</li>\n<li><strong>Model code.</strong> Brands use suffixes for regions. Ask the brand\'s service centre which ones are sold here.</li>\n<li><strong>Plug type.</strong> A charger with a British or American plug and an adapter is a clue.</li>\n<li><strong>Languages.</strong> A box in Chinese with a separate English leaflet is a clue.</li>\n<li><strong>Seal.</strong> Look for glue marks or a second seal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Brand by brand</h2>\n<p><strong>Samsung.</strong> Open the Samsung Members app or the warranty page and enter the IMEI. Check the start date and the country. In Settings, the service provider software version shows a three-letter region code.</p>\n<p><strong>Apple.</strong> checkcoverage.apple.com shows the purchase date and coverage. In Settings, General, About, the model number ends in a region code, such as HN/A for India, LL/A for the United States or ZP/A for Hong Kong. American models from recent years have no SIM tray.</p>\n<p><strong>Xiaomi, Redmi, POCO.</strong> Mi\'s verification page accepts the IMEI. In About phone, the version should say Global or the region, not China.</p>\n<p><strong>vivo, OPPO, Realme, OnePlus.</strong> Their support sites accept an IMEI for warranty status. Indian units show an Indian warranty.</p>\n<p><strong>Honor, Motorola, Nothing, Infinix, Tecno.</strong> Use the maker\'s support app, and the distributor\'s sticker.</p>\n<h2>Is the phone really new?</h2>\n<p>A sealed box can contain a phone that was activated months ago. Look at the activation or warranty date on the brand\'s site. On Android, dialling a brand-specific code or opening the battery information may show a manufacture date and cycle count. On iPhones 15 and newer, Settings, Battery shows the cycle count and the date of first use. A new phone should show a handful of cycles at most.</p>\n<h2>Buying used: extra checks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ask for the original bill and an identity document of the seller. A photo of both, with permission, protects you if the phone turns out to be stolen.</li>\n<li>Check that no account is signed in. Reset the phone yourself and set it up in front of the seller.</li>\n<li>Look for water indicators inside the SIM tray.</li>\n<li>On iPhones, look in About for \"Unknown Part\".</li>\n<li>Test every camera, the microphone, speaker, proximity sensor, fingerprint, charging and both SIM slots.</li>\n<li>Check the IMEI in MDMS and make a call with your own SIM.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Bringing a phone from abroad</h2>\n<p>Travellers and returning workers may bring a phone for personal use within the customs rules, and can register it through MDMS. Rules on the number of devices, time limits and fees have changed several times, so read the current notice on the NTA and customs sites before travelling. Keep the purchase bill, your passport with the arrival stamp and your visa or labour permit. Register early, as a phone that has already been blocked takes longer to restore.</p>\n<h2>What to do if your phone is not registered</h2>\n<p>First, find out why. If you bought it recently, go back to the seller with the result and ask for registration or a refund. Your bill is your evidence. If you brought it from abroad, use the individual registration. If it was a gift, the person who brought it may need to provide documents. Do not pay an agent on social media who promises to \"register any IMEI\". Some change the IMEI, which is illegal and can get the phone blocked permanently.</p>\n<h2>Why changing an IMEI is a bad idea</h2>\n<p>Altering the identity of a phone is an offence in many countries, and it makes the device untraceable if stolen. It also breaks updates and banking apps that check for integrity. If a seller offers it as a service, leave.</p>\n<h2>Is a grey phone ever sensible?</h2>\n<p>Sometimes a model is not sold officially here, such as Google\'s Pixels. In that case you depend on an importer. Choose one with a shop, a bill and a written warranty, confirm registration before paying, check network bands and VoLTE, and accept that repairs may take weeks. For models that are sold officially, the saving on a grey unit is rarely worth what you lose below a lakh.</p>\n<h2>What a proper bill contains</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The shop\'s name, address and PAN or VAT number.</li>\n<li>Date, model, colour, memory and storage.</li>\n<li>IMEI numbers.</li>\n<li>Price and VAT.</li>\n<li>Warranty period and provider.</li>\n<li>Signature or stamp.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a seller offers two prices, with and without a bill, choose the one with. It is your warranty, your proof of ownership and, if the phone is stolen, the document the police will ask for.</p>\n<h2>Online purchases</h2>\n<p>Before accepting a parcel, film the opening. Check the IMEI on the box against the listing and the MDMS site while the courier waits, if allowed. Pay on delivery where possible. Choose listings from official stores. Keep the packaging for the return period.</p>\n<h2>Laptops, tablets and watches</h2>\n<p>Tablets and watches with a SIM have an IMEI and need the same check. Wi-Fi tablets and laptops do not, but their warranty has a region as well. Enter the serial number on the maker\'s site to see the country and the dates.</p>\n<h2>One minute version</h2>\n<p>Dial *#06#. Match the box. Check MDMS. Ask for the distributor\'s name. Get the bill with the IMEI. If any of the five is missing, the price should be lower or the answer should be no. For the wider list of checks, read <a href=\"/blog/5-things-to-check-before-buying-a-smartphone-in-nepal\">five things to check before buying a smartphone</a>.</p>','guides/guide-check-official-phone.jpg',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-24 07:52:34'),(34,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 25,000 in Nepal (2026)','best-phones-under-25000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 25,000 you are choosing between battery life, screen smoothness and a slightly faster chip, rarely all three. These are the phones we would shortlist.</p>\n\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">Realme C71</a> — from Rs 20,999.</strong> The best balance here: a 6,300mAh battery, 45W charging (rare at this price) and a 120Hz screen.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a> — from Rs 25,499.</strong> Just over budget, but its Helio G99 is the quickest chip in this group, and you get Samsung\'s service network.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a> — from Rs 24,999.</strong> A 7,000mAh battery for people who hate charging. Charging is slow at 15W, so plug it in overnight.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05\">vivo Y05</a> — from Rs 24,999.</strong> A 6,500mAh battery and a 120Hz screen that gets bright enough outdoors.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Under Rs 20,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Realme Note 70</a> (from Rs 15,499) and <a href=\"/products/honor-choice-t10\">Honor Choice T10</a> (from Rs 16,499) cover calls, messaging and social apps. Expect HD screens and slow charging. If you can stretch to Rs 21,000, the Realme C71 is a big step up.</p>\n\n<h2>What to check before you buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Storage:</strong> 64GB fills up fast with WhatsApp media. Pay the extra Rs 3,000–5,000 for 128GB if you can.</li>\n<li><strong>4G vs 5G:</strong> every phone above is 4G. That is fine on NTC and Ncell today. If you want 5G, look at the <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> from Rs 33,999.</li>\n<li><strong>Official units:</strong> check the IMEI on NTA\'s MDMS site before paying. Here\'s <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">how to check a phone is official</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More options: <a href=\"/products?category=mobile&max_price=25000&sort=price_asc\">all phones under Rs 25,000</a>, sorted by price.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Prices move, so start here</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">Realme C71</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 750 nits (Peak)</td><td>UNISOC T7250</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB</td><td>45W Fast Charge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>6.7 inches PLS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 9...</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05\">Vivo Y05</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,974</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1200 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7225 (12nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 6500 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Realme Note 70</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,999</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 563 nit (typ)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6300 mAh, non-removable; 15W VOOC Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 560 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The table above is drawn from our live catalogue, so it may differ from the prices mentioned in our picks, which were written with the cheapest version in mind. The Realme C71, for example, has been sold from about Rs 21,000 for the smaller configuration and is listed higher for the larger one. Always compare the exact memory and storage.</p>\n<h2>The four phones, honestly</h2>\n<p><strong>Realme C71: the all-rounder.</strong> Fast charging is what you notice. At 45W, half an hour while you get ready fills most of a 6,300mAh battery, and neighbours on this page take two hours. Realme phones at this price sometimes appear with different screens in different listings. Our sheet says AMOLED for one version, while most units sold here use an LCD, so look at a black screen in a dark corner of the shop before you pay. If you see a grey glow, it is an LCD.</p>\n<p><strong>Samsung Galaxy A07: the safe one.</strong> The Helio G99 is the most capable chip in the group, and Samsung supports its cheap phones with updates for years. The screen is only HD+ and the charger is extra. If the buyer is a parent, or the phone must last four years, this is the one we would choose. Read <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a07-review-nepal\">our full review</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>OPPO A6c: the marathon runner.</strong> Two days of light use from 7,000mAh. The price of that is slow charging and only 64GB in the base model. It suits people who travel or live with long cuts.</p>\n<p><strong>vivo Y05: easy to recommend, easy to outgrow.</strong> A bright 120Hz screen and a strong service network. The 8MP camera is the weakest here, and 4GB of memory is modest. <a href=\"/reviews/vivo-y05-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains who it suits.</p>\n<h2>The wildcard: Infinix Note 50x</h2>\n<p>It does not appear among our main picks, as Infinix has fewer service points, but at around Rs 22,000 its Dimensity 7300 Ultimate is in another league for speed. Games that stutter on every other phone here run properly. It also has 6 or 8GB of memory with faster UFS storage. If you live near an Infinix service point and you are under 25, look at it first.</p>\n<h2>What you can expect at this budget</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smooth enough.</strong> Social apps, maps, wallets and video calls are fine with 6GB or more.</li>\n<li><strong>Good daylight photos.</strong> A 50MP main camera is common. Night photos remain noisy.</li>\n<li><strong>A day and a half of battery.</strong> Often two.</li>\n<li><strong>4G only, with few exceptions.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>No water resistance to rely on.</strong> IP54 or IP64 means splashes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What you should not pay for</h2>\n<p>Ignore 2MP macro and depth lenses. Ignore \"AI camera\" stickers. Ignore memory extension. Ignore the number of megapixels if the aperture and sensor are not mentioned. And do not pay for a 5G badge at this price. The phones that have it give up screen, battery or storage to afford the modem.</p>\n<h2>128GB is the line</h2>\n<p>If there is one decision to get right, it is this. Phones with 64GB are cheaper by Rs 3,000 or so. They also fill up in a year. When storage is nearly full, Android slows down, apps fail to update and the camera refuses to record. Spread over three years, 128GB costs you about Rs 85 a month. It is the best upgrade in this price band.</p>\n<h2>Is a used phone better?</h2>\n<p>At Rs 25,000 the used market has Galaxy A5-series phones, Redmi Note Pros and OnePlus Nords from two or three years ago, with AMOLED screens and stabilised cameras. They can be lovely. They also come with worn batteries and no warranty. If you know how to check one, or you trust the seller, consider it. If not, a new phone with a one-year warranty is worth the plainer screen.</p>\n<h2>Accessories to budget for</h2>\n<p>Keep Rs 1,500 for a case, tempered glass and, in Samsung\'s case, a 25W charger. A cheap unbranded charger can damage a battery or worse. Our <a href=\"/products?category=accessory\">accessory listings</a> show what genuine ones cost.</p>\n<h2>Questions readers ask</h2>\n<p><strong>Is Realme or Samsung better at this price?</strong> Realme gives more hardware, Samsung more years. Decide how long you keep phones.</p>\n<p><strong>Can these phones run PUBG?</strong> The A07 and C71 manage it at smooth graphics with a lower frame rate. For comfortable play, get the Note 50x.</p>\n<p><strong>Which has the best camera?</strong> The A07 and C71, in daylight. None is good at night. For family photos, saving for a phone with optical stabilisation makes a bigger difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Which lasts longest away from a charger?</strong> The OPPO A6c, then the vivo Y05 and Realme C71.</p>\n<p><strong>Which charges fastest?</strong> The C71 and the Note 50x, both at 45W.</p>\n<h2>How long will it last?</h2>\n<p>With care, expect three years. The battery loses some capacity, apps grow heavier and updates stop. A phone with 6GB of memory ages noticeably better than one with 4GB. If you can, buy that version in preference to extra storage, because you can add a memory card and you can never add memory.</p>\n<h2>The decision in one paragraph</h2>\n<p>Choose the Galaxy A07 if you value support and service. Choose the Realme C71 if you value quick charging and balance. Choose the OPPO A6c if you hate chargers. Choose the Infinix Note 50x if you play games. If none of these feels right and you can wait for the next salary, the jump to <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-30000-nepal\">Rs 30,000</a> brings AMOLED screens, and at <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">Rs 40,000</a> you reach 5G and real cameras.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(35,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 40,000 in Nepal (2026)','best-phones-under-40000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 30,000 to Rs 40,000 is where phones get genuinely good: 5G chips, AMOLED screens and batteries that last well past a day. Here is what we would buy.</p>\n\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> — from Rs 33,999.</strong> The best all-rounder: 5G on a Snapdragon 4 Gen 5, a 7,500mAh battery and 45W charging. The screen is HD+ LCD, which is the compromise.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">Moto G57 Power</a> — Rs 37,499.</strong> The fastest chip in this group (Snapdragon 6s Gen 4), a 7,000mAh battery and near-stock Android. Note that the charger is not in the box.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a> — Rs 33,999.</strong> If the screen matters most: a 144Hz AMOLED panel and 45W charging for under Rs 35,000.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">Realme C85 Pro</a> — Rs 36,999.</strong> An AMOLED screen with IP66, IP68, IP69 and IP69K ratings. It is the toughest phone here.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/honor-x7d\">Honor X7d</a> — from Rs 33,999.</strong> 8GB/256GB as standard and a 108MP main camera, but it is 4G only.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Worth stretching for</h2>\n<p>For Rs 44,999 the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> adds a Super AMOLED screen, OIS on the main camera and Samsung\'s software support. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a> (Rs 44,999) gets you a Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 and IP69.</p>\n\n<p>See also: <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">best phones under Rs 25,000</a> and our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The main contenders</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.9-inch HD+ LCD, 120Hz, 825 nits peak, Goril...</td><td>Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td><td>7,500mAh; 45W wired, 22.5W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">Motorola Moto G57 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;37,499</td><td>6.72 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1050 nits (HBM)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 4500 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">Realme C85 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;36,999</td><td>6.8 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (Typ), 120...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x7d\">Honor X7d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.77 inches TFT LCD, 120Hz, 850 nits (HBM)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6500 mAh, non-removable; 35W Honor Supe...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 1300 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why Rs 30,000 to 40,000 is the sweet spot</h2>\n<p>If you asked us how much an ordinary person should spend on a phone in Nepal, we would say about Rs 35,000. Below that you keep bumping into limits. Above it you pay for refinements. In this band you can have a battery that lasts two days, or a brilliant screen, or a quick chip, or serious water resistance, and sometimes two of those in one phone.</p>\n<h2>Screens: LCD or AMOLED</h2>\n<p>Three of the phones here use LCD panels: the Redmi 17 5G, the Moto G57 Power and the Honor X7d. Three use AMOLED: the Spark 40 Pro, the Realme C85 Pro and the Note 50s. If you watch video at night, read in bed or like an always-on clock, AMOLED is worth choosing. If your phone is mainly for work, calls and maps, an LCD with a huge battery is fine and often kinder to the wallet when it breaks.</p>\n<p>Resolution matters as well. The Redmi 17\'s panel is HD+ stretched over 6.9 inches, so small text is a little soft. Look at a page of Devanagari news on it before buying. Some people do not see the difference and some cannot stop seeing it.</p>\n<h2>Chips: a wide spread</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 in the Moto G57 Power and the Dimensity 7300 Ultimate in the Note 50s are modern 4nm designs and comfortable with games. The Snapdragon 4 Gen 5 in the Redmi 17 is a newer entry chip, good for daily work. The Snapdragon 685 in the Realme and Honor, and the Helio G100 in the Tecno, are older 4G parts. They run apps without complaint and games at medium.</p>\n<p>Our advice: if you play anything heavier than Ludo, choose the Note 50s or Moto. If you do not, ignore this paragraph.</p>\n<h2>Battery life ranking</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Redmi 17 5G, 7,500mAh with an HD+ screen: two full days for many people.</li>\n<li>Moto G57 Power, 7,000mAh.</li>\n<li>Realme C85 Pro, 7,000mAh with an AMOLED.</li>\n<li>Honor X7d, 6,500mAh.</li>\n<li>Spark 40 Pro and Note 50s, 5,200mAh: a day and a bit.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Charging reverses the order in places. The 45W phones refill in an hour or so, the 30W Motorola takes longer and ships without a charger.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>The Note 50s has the only stabilised main camera under Rs 40,000 in this list, and it shows indoors. The Moto\'s larger sensor does well in daylight. The Honor\'s 108MP can crop into distant subjects when there is light. The rest are ordinary. If photos of children and evenings with friends are what you take, the Note 50s is the one.</p>\n<h2>Toughness</h2>\n<p>The Realme C85 Pro carries a long list of ratings up to IP69K, which covers rain, dust, immersion and hot spray in laboratory conditions. It is the phone for trekkers, kitchen staff and motorbike riders. Remember that no maker covers water damage under warranty, whatever is printed on the box.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<p><strong>Field worker or driver:</strong> Redmi 17 5G. Big screen for maps, big battery for the road.</p>\n<p><strong>University student who games:</strong> Infinix Note 50s. Fast, smooth, good camera.</p>\n<p><strong>Film and reels lover:</strong> Tecno Spark 40 Pro. A bright 144Hz AMOLED with 256GB.</p>\n<p><strong>Someone who breaks things:</strong> Realme C85 Pro.</p>\n<p><strong>Someone who likes clean Android:</strong> Moto G57 Power.</p>\n<p><strong>Someone who never deletes anything:</strong> Honor X7d, with 256GB as standard and 512GB available.</p>\n<h2>5G: does it decide?</h2>\n<p>Only the Redmi and Moto in our main list have it. In a country where most connections are 4G, that should be a tie-breaker. If you keep phones for four years or live in the valley, lean towards 5G. Otherwise choose on screen, battery and camera. We say more in <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">5G in Nepal</a>.</p>\n<h2>Should you add Rs 5,000?</h2>\n<p>At Rs 44,999 the market changes again. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> brings Samsung\'s support. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a> is fast with IP69. The <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96</a> has a gorgeous curved screen. The <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> at about Rs 44,000 combines a bright AMOLED, a stabilised 108MP camera and 5G, and is perhaps the best all-round phone under Rs 50,000. If you can wait a month to reach it, do.</p>\n<h2>Traps at this price</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Half-memory versions.</strong> The Redmi 17 starts at 4GB. Buy the 6GB model.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage type.</strong> The Spark 40 Pro\'s 256GB is eMMC, slower than UFS. You notice when installing apps.</li>\n<li><strong>Grey imports.</strong> Motorola and POCO phones from India are common. They have no local warranty.</li>\n<li><strong>Last year\'s phone at this year\'s price.</strong> Compare the launch date on the product page.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Practical checks</h2>\n<p>In the shop, open the camera and take a picture of your own hand in the dimmest corner. Play a video at full volume. Put the brightness at maximum and walk to the door. Type a message with one thumb. Ask about the price of a screen replacement. These five minutes tell you more than a page of specifications.</p>\n<h2>Our overall choice</h2>\n<p>If we had Rs 36,000 and no other information about the buyer, we would choose the Infinix Note 50s for its balance of screen, speed and camera, as long as service is available nearby. If service is a concern or battery is the priority, the Redmi 17 5G with 6GB is the sensible alternative. Read <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-17-5g-review-nepal\">our Redmi 17 5G review</a> to see whether its screen would bother you.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(36,20,'Best Gaming Laptops in Nepal by Budget (2026)','best-gaming-laptops-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Gaming laptop prices in Nepal run from about Rs 1.5 lakh to well over Rs 10 lakh. The GPU decides most of what you can play, so we\'ve grouped our picks by graphics card.</p>\n\n<h2>Entry level: RTX 3050 / RTX 4050 (Rs 1.5–2 lakh)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a> — from Rs 1,49,000.</strong> The cheapest way in. Choose the RTX 4050 version. <a href=\"/reviews/hp-victus-15-review-nepal\">Our verdict</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a15-fa506ncg-2024\">ASUS TUF Gaming A15</a> — Rs 1,55,999.</strong> Ryzen 7 7445HS, RTX 3050 and 16GB, in a sturdy TUF chassis.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-2026\">ASUS TUF Gaming A16</a> — Rs 1,83,999.</strong> An RTX 4050 on a bigger 16-inch 144Hz screen.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Mid-range: RTX 5050 / RTX 5060 (Rs 2–3 lakh)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a> — Rs 1,94,999.</strong> An RTX 5050 at 100W. The value pick of the new generation.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai\">Acer Nitro V 16 AI</a> — Rs 2,30,999</strong> and <strong><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Lenovo Legion 5</a> — Rs 2,59,999.</strong> Better cooling and screens.</li>\n<li>Skip the <a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx608jmr-2025\">TUF Gaming F16 FX608JMR</a> unless it\'s discounted. It costs too much for its configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>High end: RTX 5070 and above</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-2025\">ASUS ROG Strix G18</a> — from Rs 5,37,900.</strong> An 18-inch 240Hz screen with an RTX 5070.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-2025\">MSI Vector 16 HX AI</a> — Rs 6,25,000.</strong> The most affordable RTX 5090 laptop in our catalogue.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Before you buy</h2>\n<p>Ask for the GPU wattage (TGP), because the same RTX chip can be much slower at low power. 16GB of RAM is the minimum for current games. And budget for a second SSD: 512GB fills up after a handful of big games. Not sure about a laptop at all? A <a href=\"/reviews/ps5-digital-slim-pro-review-nepal\">PS5</a> costs less and plays <a href=\"/products/gta-6\">GTA VI</a>, which isn\'t on PC yet.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The ladder, rung by rung</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>512GB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a15-fa506ncg-2024\">Asus TUF Gaming A15 FA506NCG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,55,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 7445HS (4nm), 6 cores / 12 thread...</td><td>16GB</td><td>512GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-2025-13th-gen-i7-13620h-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb\">Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-51 2025 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,69,999</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1080), 165H...</td><td>Intel Core i7-13620H (Intel 7), 10 cores / 16...</td><td>16GB</td><td>512GB PCIe 4.0 NVMe M.2 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-2026\">Asus TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUQ (2026)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,83,999</td><td>16-inch FHD+, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 170</td><td>16GB</td><td>512GB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>15.6&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare,...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB...</td><td>1x 16GB SO-DIMM DDR5-5600</td><td>512GB SSD M.2 2242 PCIe® 4.0x4 NVMe®</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai\">Acer Nitro V 16 AI</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,30,999</td><td>16-inch WUXGA IPS, 180Hz, 300 nits, 100% sRGB</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 350</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>1TB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Lenovo Legion 5 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,999</td><td>15.3-inch WUXGA IPS, 165Hz, 300 nits, 100% sR...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 260 (8 cores, up to 5.1GHz)</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>512GB PCIe Gen4 SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-2025\">Asus ROG Strix G18 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,37,900</td><td>18-inch WQXGA, 240Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 9 8940HX or Intel Core i9-14900HX</td><td>16GB / 32GB</td><td>1TB / 2TB</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-2025\">MSI Vector 16 HX AI (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,25,000</td><td>16-inch QHD+ IPS, 240Hz</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 275HX</td><td>16GB</td><td>1TB</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The three numbers that decide everything</h2>\n<p>A gaming laptop\'s sticker is full of words. Only three numbers matter at first.</p>\n<p><strong>The graphics chip and its watts.</strong> An RTX 4050 at 100W beats an RTX 4060 at 45W in many games. Makers are not required to print the figure in large type. Ask, or open Nvidia Control Panel, System Information, and look for maximum graphics power.</p>\n<p><strong>Video memory.</strong> Games store textures here. 6GB is the practical minimum in 2026 and 8GB is comfortable at 1080p. An RTX 3050 with 4GB will run out before it runs slow.</p>\n<p><strong>System memory.</strong> Sixteen gigabytes, ideally as two sticks. A single stick can cost you a tenth of your frames.</p>\n<p>Processor, refresh rate and storage come after these.</p>\n<h2>What each class plays</h2>\n<p><strong>RTX 3050 (about Rs 1.4 to 1.6 lakh).</strong> Valorant, CS2, Dota 2, League, Rocket League, Minecraft, GTA V, older story games. New releases at low settings, if at all.</p>\n<p><strong>RTX 4050 and 5050 (Rs 1.65 to 2 lakh).</strong> Nearly everything at 1080p with medium to high settings and DLSS. The 5050 adds newer frame generation and, usually, 8GB.</p>\n<p><strong>RTX 5060 (Rs 2.2 to 3 lakh).</strong> High settings at 1080p, good 1440p with DLSS. The sensible upper limit for most people.</p>\n<p><strong>RTX 5070 and 5070 Ti (Rs 3.5 lakh upward).</strong> 1440p with high settings and some ray tracing. More important for creators who use the extra video memory.</p>\n<p><strong>RTX 5080 and 5090 (Rs 5 lakh and beyond).</strong> For professionals and people who do not ask the price.</p>\n<h2>The same game on a desktop</h2>\n<p>It is only fair to say it: a desktop gives more for less. A Rs 1,80,000 desktop with a full RTX 5060 outperforms most laptops costing Rs 2,60,000, runs cooler, and can be upgraded for years. If you play in one room, look at the <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a>. If you move between hostel and home, a laptop is the right answer.</p>\n<h2>Consoles are part of the question</h2>\n<p>A PlayStation 5 costs about as much as an entry gaming laptop\'s graphics card alone, connects to any TV and plays big releases without settings menus. GTA VI is on consoles first. If you do not need a computer for study, a console plus a Rs 70,000 laptop may be better than one Rs 1,80,000 machine. See <a href=\"/news/ps5-price-in-nepal-november-2026\">PS5 prices</a>.</p>\n<h2>Cooling in a warm country</h2>\n<p>Reviews online are done in cool rooms. In the Terai, or on a top floor in Kathmandu in June, the same laptop reaches its thermal limit earlier and slows. Choose thicker chassis with more vents: Legion and TUF A16 over slim designs. Keep the machine on a hard surface, raise the rear, clean it every few months, and cap frames to what your screen and eyes can use.</p>\n<h2>Screens</h2>\n<p>144Hz is plenty. 165Hz and above is for competitive players. More valuable is brightness of 300 nits and 100% sRGB colour, as on the LOQ and Legion. 16-inch 16:10 displays are better for work. If you connect an external monitor, check that the HDMI or USB-C port is wired to the graphics chip and supports the refresh rate you want.</p>\n<h2>Power at home</h2>\n<p>Gaming laptops use 135 to 330W chargers. Confirm that your inverter or UPS can carry it along with the router and lights. Use a surge protector. Never replace the charger with a lower-rated one: the laptop will drain its battery while plugged in.</p>\n<h2>Why the MUX switch matters</h2>\n<p>In normal mode, the picture from the Nvidia chip passes through the processor\'s graphics, which costs some performance. A MUX switch or Advanced Optimus bypasses it. If two laptops are otherwise equal, choose the one that has it.</p>\n<h2>Which one should you buy?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Budget under Rs 1.5 lakh:</strong> HP Victus 15 with the best graphics chip you can get. See also <a href=\"/news/best-gaming-laptops-nepal-under-150000-2024\">gaming laptops under Rs 1.5 lakh</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 1.7 lakh:</strong> <a href=\"/reviews/acer-nitro-lite-16-rtx-4050-review-nepal\">Nitro Lite 16</a> or the Victus with RTX 4050.</li>\n<li><strong>Under two lakhs:</strong> <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-loq-15-2025-review-nepal\">Lenovo LOQ 15</a>. It is our value pick of the whole list.</li>\n<li><strong>Rs 2.3 to 2.6 lakh:</strong> Legion 5 for cooling and finish, Nitro V 16 AI for storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Above five lakhs:</strong> Strix G18 for a desktop replacement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New, used or open-box</h2>\n<p>Gaming laptops age faster than office ones. A used machine may have spent two years at ninety degrees. If you buy used, run a stress test for twenty minutes, watch temperatures, listen to the fans, and inspect the hinges and the area around the vents. RTX 3060 laptops from 2022 can be excellent value at under a lakh if they pass. Ask when the thermal paste was last replaced.</p>\n<h2>Budget for what comes after</h2>\n<p>Keep aside Rs 8,000 to 12,000 for a 1TB drive, Rs 3,000 for a cooling stand, and something for a headset and mouse. Games cost money too. Plan for the subscription or the discs.</p>\n<h2>Setting it up well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update graphics drivers from Nvidia, and the BIOS from the maker.</li>\n<li>Use performance mode plugged in and balanced mode on battery.</li>\n<li>Turn on DLSS Quality first, then raise other settings.</li>\n<li>Keep 15 per cent of the SSD free.</li>\n<li>Limit the battery to 80 per cent if it lives on a desk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A word about timing</h2>\n<p>Prices of memory and drives went up during 2026, so discounts are smaller than before. The best moments remain Dashain, the new year and the weeks before a new generation. If a machine you like drops by ten per cent with the same warranty, that is a good day to buy.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(37,20,'Best Tablets in Nepal for Study, Work and Entertainment (2026)','best-tablets-nepal','buying-guide','<p>A tablet can be a cheap video screen, a note-taking machine for college or a laptop replacement. Here are our picks for each job.</p>\n\n<h2>For videos, reading and online classes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2</a> — from Rs 24,999.</strong> An 11-inch 2.5K screen, quad speakers and a 9,000mAh battery. Hard to beat for the price.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/oneplus-pad-lite\">OnePlus Pad Lite</a> — from Rs 29,999.</strong> There is an LTE version at Rs 34,999 if you need data away from Wi-Fi.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>For students and note-taking</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a> — from Rs 44,999.</strong> A 3.2K 144Hz screen and Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3. The best performance under Rs 50,000.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a> — Rs 74,999.</strong> The S Pen comes in the box, along with IP68 and seven years of updates. Good for handwritten notes.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">iPad 11 (A16)</a> — from Rs 86,000.</strong> The simplest choice for iPhone owners, with the biggest app library.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>To replace a laptop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-11-m4-2026\">iPad Air 11 (M4)</a> — from Rs 1,39,000</strong> and <strong><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11\">Galaxy Tab S11</a> — Rs 1,29,999.</strong> Both work well with a keyboard. Samsung\'s DeX gives a desktop-style layout.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-11-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 11 (M5)</a> — from Rs 2,29,500.</strong> An OLED screen and M5 chip, for artists and video editors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Comparing two? <a href=\"/compare?category=tablet\">Compare tablets side by side</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>All the picks, with current prices</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 11-inch</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ), 600...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultra (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-pad-lite\">OnePlus Pad Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;34,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9340 mAh, non-removable; 33W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>11.2 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 8850 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>10.8 - 10.9 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (t...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1580 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">Apple iPad (A16)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;83,499</td><td>11-inch Liquid Retina IPS display, 2360 × 164...</td><td>Apple A16 chip (5-core CPU, 4-core GPU, 16-co...</td><td>6 GB LPDDR5</td><td>non-removable; 20W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S11</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>11 inches Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 120Hz, 1000 nits...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9400+ (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 8400 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-11-m4-2026\">Apple iPad Air 11-inch (M4)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>LED backlit Multi‑Touch display with IPS tech...</td><td>Apple M4 chip 8-core CPU with 3 performance c...</td><td>12GB Unified Memory</td><td>Li-Po 28.93 Wh, non-removable; 20W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-11-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 11 M5 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,29,500</td><td>11 inches Ultra Retina Tandem OLED, 120Hz, 10...</td><td>Apple M5</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>Li-Po 8160 mAh, non-removable; 60W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Some prices have moved since the picks above were written. The Redmi Pad 2, for example, starts higher for the version most shops stock, and the iPad 11 has come down a little. The table follows the live catalogue.</p>\n<h2>What will it do at eight in the evening?</h2>\n<p>That is a better question than \"which tablet is best\". In most homes a tablet plays video in the kitchen, entertains a child, shows a PDF to a student or holds a recipe. For those, a Rs 25,000 to 35,000 tablet is as good as one that costs a lakh. If the honest answer is \"I will write notes for three hours\" or \"I will draw\", you need a pen and a better screen. If it is \"I will work on it\", you need a keyboard, and perhaps a laptop.</p>\n<h2>For watching and reading</h2>\n<p>Size, resolution and speakers matter most. Eleven inches with a 2K or better screen, four speakers and a battery of 8,000mAh or more is the formula, and the Redmi Pad 2 and OnePlus Pad Lite follow it. Check Widevine L1 support if you use Netflix or Prime Video, since without it films play in low resolution. The seller can show it with a free app such as DRM Info.</p>\n<h2>For students</h2>\n<p>A pen changes what a tablet is for. Handwritten notes over lecture slides, diagrams, signatures and maths are where it beats a laptop. Three approaches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Galaxy Tab S10 FE:</strong> the pen is in the box, it never needs charging, and the tablet is water resistant. Our choice for most students.</li>\n<li><strong>iPad 11 with Apple Pencil (USB-C):</strong> the best note apps, with a pen that costs extra.</li>\n<li><strong>Xiaomi Pad 7 with Xiaomi\'s pen:</strong> the smoothest screen for the price. Check availability of the pen before buying the tablet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Add a case with a stand. A tablet without one is tiring in class.</p>\n<h2>For artists</h2>\n<p>iPad with Procreate remains the easiest start. The base iPad 11 is fine for learning. The Air adds a better pencil with pressure and hover features, and a laminated display that removes the gap between tip and line. The Pro\'s OLED is for professionals. On the Samsung side, the Tab S11\'s AMOLED and included S Pen with Clip Studio or Infinite Painter make a strong alternative. Beginners who only want to draw at a desk can also use a pen tablet with their computer for much less.</p>\n<h2>For work</h2>\n<p>iPadOS with windows and Samsung\'s DeX have made tablets better at multitasking. They can replace a laptop for email, documents, meetings and browsing. They still cannot run full Excel add-ins, accounting packages, coding tools or many government sites properly. Add the price of the keyboard before deciding. An iPad Air with keyboard and pencil is far above the price of a decent laptop. Our piece on <a href=\"/guides/laptop-vs-tablet-work-study-nepal\">laptop or tablet</a> works it through.</p>\n<h2>For children</h2>\n<p>Choose a smaller, tougher and cheaper device, with a rubber case. The 8.7-inch Galaxy Tab A11 and 9.7-inch Redmi Pad 2 are right. Set up Google Family Link or Samsung Kids on the first day, with a time limit and app approvals. Cheap tablets with unknown names often carry old Android versions, poor screens and no security updates. Avoid them for children in particular.</p>\n<h2>Wi-Fi or SIM?</h2>\n<p>A SIM slot adds Rs 5,000 to 20,000. Most people can use their phone as a hotspot. Field workers, people who travel and those using a tablet in a vehicle benefit from LTE. Check that the tablet with a SIM is registered in MDMS, as devices with cellular modems have IMEI numbers. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">This guide</a> shows how.</p>\n<h2>How long do tablets last?</h2>\n<p>Longer than phones. iPads receive updates for seven years or so. Samsung\'s S series and FE have similar promises. Xiaomi, OnePlus and Honor offer fewer. A tablet that is used for video can live happily without the newest Android, so support matters most if you use banking or work apps on it.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage</h2>\n<p>Avoid 4GB unless it is for a child and video. Six is fine, and eight is comfortable. 128GB is enough for most, as films can be streamed or placed on a card. iPads have no card slot, so choose with care. The base 128GB is usually sufficient for notes and study.</p>\n<h2>Screen facts worth knowing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>90Hz is fine, and 120Hz or 144Hz makes writing feel more natural.</li>\n<li>400 to 500 nits is enough indoors and weak outdoors.</li>\n<li>Laminated screens look better for pens.</li>\n<li>Matte paper-like protectors improve writing and reduce sharpness.</li>\n<li>LCD is fine for reading, AMOLED for films.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buying tips</h2>\n<p>Look for official units with a Nepali warranty. Screen replacement on a tablet can cost half of its price, so a case is essential. Ask whether the charger is included and its wattage, as a 10,000mAh battery on a 10W charger takes all night. Bundles with keyboards are often good value at launch and during Dashain.</p>\n<h2>Our recommendations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Family:</strong> Redmi Pad 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Student:</strong> Galaxy Tab S10 FE.</li>\n<li><strong>Student on a smaller budget:</strong> Xiaomi Pad 7.</li>\n<li><strong>iPhone household:</strong> iPad 11.</li>\n<li><strong>Artist:</strong> iPad Air 11 or Galaxy Tab S11.</li>\n<li><strong>Child:</strong> Redmi Pad 2 9.7 or Galaxy Tab A11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For tighter budgets, see tablets <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-30000-nepal\">under Rs 30,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-50000-nepal\">under Rs 50,000</a>.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(38,20,'Best Smartwatches Under Rs 30,000 in Nepal (2026)','best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>You don\'t need an Apple Watch budget to get good fitness tracking. Under Rs 30,000 the trade-offs are screen quality, GPS and how many days the battery lasts.</p>\n\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a> — Rs 4,499.</strong> A first smartwatch for steps, heart rate, SpO2 and notifications, with up to 18 days of battery.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/amazfit-bip-5\">Amazfit Bip 5</a> — Rs 11,499.</strong> Built-in GPS for runs and walks without your phone, plus a big 1.91-inch screen.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/amazfit-active\">Amazfit Active</a> — Rs 22,499.</strong> An AMOLED screen, five-system GPS and a light aluminium frame. Our pick for most people.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5\">Huawei Watch Fit 5</a> — Rs 27,990.</strong> A slim rectangular design with a large AMOLED screen and up to 10 days of battery.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-2\">Amazfit T-Rex 2</a> — Rs 27,999.</strong> Built for trekking: MIL-STD-810G toughness, dual-band GPS and up to 24 days of battery.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Worth knowing</h2>\n<p>These watches pair with both Android and iPhone, but replies and app support are more limited than on an Apple Watch or Galaxy Watch. If you mainly want health tracking and long battery life, that trade-off is usually worth it. Need more? The <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Huawei Watch GT 6</a> (Rs 34,490) lasts up to three weeks.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Current prices and specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>2.0-inch LCD (320×385), 500 nits</td><td>Up to 18 days normal use</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;10,999</td><td>1.85 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 18 days (light usage), Up to 12 days (n...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, BDS, Gal...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-watch-3-pro\">CMF Watch 3 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;12,999</td><td>1.43 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 13 days (normal usage), Up to 60 days (...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5),...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">Amazfit GTR Mini</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;17,499</td><td>1.28 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 14 days (normal usage); 280 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5\">Huawei Watch Fit 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;27,990</td><td>1.82-inch AMOLED (Fit 5) / 1.92-inch AMOLED w...</td><td>Up to 10 days</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Huawei Watch GT 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;34,490</td><td>1.32-inch (41mm) or 1.47-inch (46mm) AMOLED (...</td><td>Up to 14 days (41mm) / 21 days (46mm)</td><td>Dual-band GPS (L1 + L5)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A note on Amazfit. Several models mentioned in our picks, including the Bip 5, the Active and the T-Rex 2, no longer have a confirmed price in our catalogue. They still appear in shops, and prices vary, so treat the figures above as a guide and check with the seller. Newer Amazfit models are on our <a href=\"/guides/amazfit-smartwatch-price-in-nepal\">Amazfit price page</a>.</p>\n<h2>Three kinds of buyer</h2>\n<p>People come to this budget for different reasons. The first wants a better-looking version of a fitness band. The second has started running, cycling or trekking and needs data that can be trusted. The third wants a smartwatch in the full sense, with replies and apps. The first two are served very well under Rs 30,000. The third should know that full smartwatches mostly begin above it.</p>\n<h2>For the well-dressed: Huawei Watch Fit 5</h2>\n<p>A slim rectangle with a large AMOLED screen and ten days of battery. It is one of the most comfortable watches we know of, light enough for sleep. Huawei\'s tracking of swimming, running and sleep is excellent. The limits are the same as ever: the app is installed from Huawei\'s site on Android, and there are no third-party apps to speak of.</p>\n<h2>For the outdoors: Amazfit T-Rex line</h2>\n<p>Built to military test standards, with dual-band GPS and weeks of battery, T-Rex watches are made for trails. They are thick and heavy on a small wrist. For trekking guides, cyclists and people who break things, they are ideal. If you go above 3,000 metres often, look for offline maps in the newer models.</p>\n<h2>For everyday value: CMF Watch 3 Pro or Redmi Watch 6 Active</h2>\n<p>You do not have to spend the whole budget. Both have GPS and AMOLED at about half of Rs 30,000, and do the basic job of a Rs 25,000 watch. <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal\">Our previous guide</a> compares them.</p>\n<h2>Just above: Huawei Watch GT 6</h2>\n<p>At Rs 34,490 it adds sapphire or tough glass depending on the version, a 3,000-nit screen, advanced running metrics and up to three weeks of battery in the larger size. If you can stretch, it is the best fitness watch you can buy in Nepal for the money. <a href=\"/reviews/huawei-watch-gt-6-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains.</p>\n<h2>Are Galaxy Watch and Apple Watch possible?</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy Watch FE and older Galaxy Watch models are sometimes sold under Rs 30,000. They give replies, apps and tight links with Samsung phones, and need charging every day or two. The Apple Watch SE sits above the budget new. For iPhone owners, a used SE with a healthy battery is worth considering: nothing else lets you reply to messages from the wrist on iOS.</p>\n<h2>What better sensors mean</h2>\n<p>More light sources and receivers on the back give steadier heart-rate readings when you move. Dual-band GPS keeps a straight line between buildings and under trees. A barometer measures climb, which matters in a country of hills. A skin temperature sensor helps cycle tracking and shows illness early. If you do not recognise a need for any of these, you can save money.</p>\n<h2>ECG and health alerts</h2>\n<p>Some watches record a single-lead ECG. Availability depends on region and approvals, and the function may be disabled on units sold here. Ask before paying for it. High and low heart-rate alerts and irregular rhythm notifications are useful, and no substitute for a doctor.</p>\n<h2>Choosing a size</h2>\n<p>Round watches come in 41 to 47mm. Measure your wrist: below 160mm, choose about 41 to 43mm, and above 175mm you can wear anything. Lugs should not hang over the edge. Rectangular watches look smaller and show more text.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>With GPS for an hour each day and an always-on display, expect three to six days from watches that claim two weeks. Most use their own magnetic charger. Keep a spare: they are easily left in hotel rooms, and a replacement takes time to find.</p>\n<h2>Data and privacy</h2>\n<p>Your watch knows your sleep, location and heart. Choose an app with export options to Strava, Google Health Connect or Apple Health, so that your history stays yours. Turn off public profiles in fitness apps, because morning runs start at your front door.</p>\n<h2>Straps, glass and care</h2>\n<p>Quick-release 20 or 22mm straps keep options open. Sapphire resists scratches, mineral glass does not. Rinse the watch after sweat or sea. Dry the charging pins. Clean the sensor with a soft cloth.</p>\n<h2>Good questions for the seller</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Is it an official unit, and who services it?</li>\n<li>Which functions are not available in Nepal?</li>\n<li>Can the strap be replaced with a standard one?</li>\n<li>Does it support my phone fully?</li>\n<li>Can I return it if the app does not work with my phone?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Recommendations in short</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Comfort and looks:</strong> Huawei Watch Fit 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Trekking:</strong> Amazfit T-Rex series.</li>\n<li><strong>Value:</strong> Redmi Watch 6 Active.</li>\n<li><strong>Style for less:</strong> CMF Watch 3 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Runner who can add Rs 4,500:</strong> Huawei Watch GT 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Samsung owner who wants apps:</strong> a discounted Galaxy Watch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Final thought</h2>\n<p>The best watch is the one you wear. Try the weight, sleep with a band for a week if a friend can lend one, and be honest about whether you will charge it. A Rs 11,000 watch on the wrist is worth more than a Rs 30,000 watch in a drawer.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(39,20,'ASUS Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','asus-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>ASUS has the widest laptop range in Nepal by some distance. It runs from a Rs 45,999 Vivobook Go with a Celeron chip all the way to the dual-screen <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-duo-gx651ax-2026\">ROG Zephyrus Duo</a> at over Rs 13 lakh. That breadth is useful, but it also makes the line-up confusing, because similar names hide very different machines.</p>\n<p>A rough map of the families: <strong>Vivobook</strong> is the everyday range, <strong>Zenbook</strong> the thin premium one, <strong>TUF</strong> and <strong>ROG</strong> are for gaming, and <strong>ProArt</strong> is for creative work. Each product page shows when its price was last checked.</p>\n<h2>ASUS laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-go-14-e410ka-2023-intel-celeron-n4500-8gb-256gb-14-fhd\">Asus Vivobook Go 14 E410KA 2023 (Intel Celeron N4500)</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 45,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1404va-2023\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VA 2023 (Intel Core i3-1315U)</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 70,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-go-15-e1504ga-2023\">Asus Vivobook Go 15 E1504GA 2023 (Intel Core i3-N305)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Asus VivoBook 15 X1504VA 2023 (Intel Core i5-1334U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15x-oled-x1503za-2022\">Asus Vivobook 15X OLED X1503ZA 2022 (Intel Core i3-1220P)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 83,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415-core-ultra-5-125h-8gb\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415 Core Ultra 5 125H, 8GB, 512GB, 14\" FHD, 60Hz OLED</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 125H, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1405vap-2024\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1405VAP 2024 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 93,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-go-e1504-2025-ryzen-3-30\">Asus Vivobook Go E1504 2025 (Ryzen 3 30)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 93,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1404vap-2025\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1404VAP 2025 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 97,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504vap-2024\">Asus Vivobook 15 X1504VAP 2024 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,15,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-2023-intel-core-i5-13420h-8gb-512gb-15-6-fhd\">Asus VivoBook 15 X1502VA 2023 (Intel Core i5 13420H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,27,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-oled-x-plus-16gb-1tb-15-6-3k-oled\">Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA-MA004W (Snapdragon X Plus)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,42,800</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-m1407ka-2025\">ASUS VivoBook 14 M1407KA 2025</a></td><td>Ryzen AI 5 340, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,45,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Ryzen AI 5 330, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,94,599</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-16-m1607ka-mb117w-2025-ryzen-ai-5-340-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga\">Asus Vivobook 16 M1607KA-MB117W 2025 (Ryzen AI 5 340)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,45,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-m3407ha-2025\">Asus Vivobook S14 M3407HA 2025</a></td><td>Ryzen 5 220, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,52,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Ryzen 7 260, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,21,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a15-fa506ncg-2024\">Asus TUF Gaming A15 FA506NCG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,55,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-gaming-v16-2025-core-7-240h-rtx-3050-16gb-1tb-16-wuxga-144hz\">Asus Gaming V16 V3607VJ 2025 (Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,57,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-2024-core-5-rtx-3050-4gb\">Asus TUF Gaming F16 FX607VB-RL015W 2024 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,65,600</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx607-2024\">Asus TUF Gaming F16 FX607 (2024)</a></td><td>Core 5 210H · RTX 3050, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,65,600</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Core 5 210H · RTX 4050, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,91,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1407ca-2025\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1407CA 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 225H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,70,905</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-x1405va-2023\">Asus Vivobook 14 X1405VA 2023 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,74,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-rl063ws-2026-ryzen-7-170-rtx-4050\">Asus TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUQ-RL063WS 2026 (Ryzen 7 170)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,83,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-2026\">Asus TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUQ (2026)</a></td><td>Ryzen 7 170 · RTX 4050, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,83,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nug-2024-ryzen-7-7445hs\">Asus TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,84,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-256v\">Asus Vivobook 14 Flip TP3407 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 256V)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,87,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-2024-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA 2024 (X Elite X1E 78 100)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,88,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-2024-core-5-rtx-4050\">Asus TUF Gaming F16 FX607VU 2024 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,91,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-flip-tp3407sa-2025\">Asus Vivobook 14 Flip TP3407SA 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 226V)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,04,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-s5406sa-2025\">ASUS VivoBook S 14 S5406SA 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,04,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-v16-2025-core-7-rtx-5050\">Asus Gaming V16 V3607VH 2025 (Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,07,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s-16-oled-s5606ma-2025-core-ultra-9-285h-16gb-1tb-16-32k-oled\">Asus Vivobook S 16 OLED S5606MA 2025 (Core Ultra 9 285H)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-2025-ryzen-ai-9-hx-370\">Asus Vivobook S 15 M5506WA 2025 (Ryzen AI 9 HX 370)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-s3407ca-2025\">Asus Vivobook S14 2025 S3407CA</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 210H, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,21,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 225H, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,48,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 255H, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,62,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-m3407ga-2026\">Asus Vivobook S14 M3407GA 2026 (Ryzen 7 445)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,36,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3405ga-2026\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED UM3405GA 2026 (Ryzen AI 9 465)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3406ga\">ASUS Zenbook 14 OLED (UM3406GA)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-flip-tp3407aa-sg065w-2026-intel-core-ultra-7-355\">Asus Vivobook 14 Flip TP3407AA-SG065W 2026 (Intel Core Ultra 7 355)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,66,949</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-14-flip-tp3407aa-2026\">Asus Vivobook 14 Flip TP3407AA (2026)</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 355, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,66,949</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-s-16-um5606ga-2026\">Asus Zenbook S 16 UM5606GA 2026 (Ryzen 7 445)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,75,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx608jmr-2025\">Asus TUF Gaming F16 FX608JMR (2025)</a></td><td>Core i7-14650HX · RTX 5050, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,91,649</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Core i7-14650HX · RTX 5060, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,52,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-ux3405ca-2025\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED UX3405CA 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,96,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-s-14-oled-ux5406sa-2024\">Asus Zenbook S 14 OLED UX5406SA 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 7 256V)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,25,755</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g815lm-2025-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5060\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G815LM 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g814fp-2025-ryzen-9-9955hx-rtx-5070\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G814FP 2025 (Ryzen 9 9955HX)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,99,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g16-g614fm-2025-ryzen-9-9955hx-rtx-5060\">Asus ROG Strix G16 G614FM 2025 (Ryzen 9 9955HX)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,18,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g16-g614pm-2025-ryzen-9-8940hx-rtx-5060\">Asus ROG Strix G16 G614PM 2025 (Ryzen 9 8940HX)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,19,899</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g815lm-2025-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5070\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G815LP 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,59,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g815jmr-2025-14th-gen-i9-14900hx-rtx-5060\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G815JMR 2025 (Intel Core i9-14900HX)</a></td><td>32GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 4,88,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu605cm-2025\">Asus ROG Zephyrus G16 GU605CM 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 5,05,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g814pp-2025-amd-ryzen-9-8940hx-rtx-5070\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G814PP 2025 (Ryzen 9 8940HX)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 5,37,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-scar-18-g835-2025-2026\">Asus ROG Strix Scar 18 G835 (2025-2026)</a></td><td>Core Ultra 9 275HX · RTX 5070 Ti, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 5,92,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Core Ultra 9 290HX Plus · RTX 5090, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 10,49,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g815jpr-2025-14th-gen-i9-14900hx-rtx-5070\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G815JPR 2025 (Intel Core i9-14900HX)</a></td><td>32GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 5,95,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-scar-16-2025\">ASUS ROG Strix SCAR 16 (2025)</a></td><td>RTX 5080, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 7,15,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-scar-16-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5080\">Asus ROG Strix SCAR 16 G635LW 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>32GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 7,15,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-gu606aw-tb103x-2026-intel-core-ultra-9-386h-rtx-5080\">Asus ROG Zephyrus G16 GU606AW-TB103X 2026 (Intel Core Ultra 9 386H)</a></td><td>32GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 7,49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-proart-p16-h7606wx-2025\">Asus ProArt P16 H7606WX (2025)</a></td><td>Ryzen AI 9 370 · RTX 5090, 64GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 8,31,249</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-zephyrus-duo-gx651ax-2026\">ASUS ROG Zephyrus Duo GX651AX 2026 (Intel Core Ultra 9 386H)</a></td><td>64GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 13,38,899</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What we\'d look at first</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For study and office work on a budget:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Vivobook 15 X1504VA</a> at Rs 79,999 pairs a Core i5 with 16GB of RAM, which is rare at this price and the main thing that keeps a laptop usable for years.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid the very cheapest models</strong> unless the job is light. The Celeron Vivobook Go 14 with 4GB of RAM struggles with modern Windows.</li>\n<li><strong>For gaming,</strong> the TUF line is the sensible start, while ROG Strix and Zephyrus cost more for better screens, cooling and build.</li>\n<li><strong>For video and 3D work,</strong> the <a href=\"/products/asus-proart-p16-h7606wx-2025\">ProArt P16</a>, with an RTX 5090, 64GB of RAM and an OLED screen, is the most capable ASUS here, priced like a workstation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">best laptops guide</a> compares the top ASUS picks with other brands at each budget.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Representative models with specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Asus VivoBook 15 X1504VA 2023 (Intel Core i5-1334U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>Intel Core i5-1334U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-m3407ha-2025\">Asus Vivobook S14 M3407HA 2025</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,52,999</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 220, 6 cores / 12 threads (base c...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 70 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-um3405ga-2026\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED UM3405GA 2026 (Ryzen AI 9 465)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,39,999</td><td>14 inches, OLED, FHD+ (1920 x 1200 pixels), 6...</td><td>AMD Ryzen AI 9 465 (4nm), 10 cores / 20 threa...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 75 Wh; 68W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nug-2024-ryzen-7-7445hs\">Asus TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUG 2024 (Ryzen 7 7445HS)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,84,999</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, FHD+ (1920 x 1200 pixels)...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 7445HS (4nm), 6 cores / 12 thread...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 56 Wh; 240W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-v16-2025-core-7-rtx-5050\">Asus Gaming V16 V3607VH 2025 (Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,07,999</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200), 144H...</td><td>Core 7 240H, 10 cores / 16 threads</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 63 Wh; 150W AC Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g814fp-2025-ryzen-9-9955hx-rtx-5070\">Asus ROG Strix G18 G814FP 2025 (Ryzen 9 9955HX)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,99,999</td><td>18 inches, IPS LCD, WQXGA (2560 x 1600 pixels...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 9 9955HX (4nm), 16 cores / 32 threa...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 90 Wh; 280W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>How to read an Asus model number</h2>\n<p>X1504VA looks like noise, and it is a small description. The first letter is the family: X for Vivobook, UX or UM for Zenbook with Intel or AMD, FX and FA for TUF with Intel or AMD, G for ROG. The next two digits are the screen size, 15 or 14. The following digits are the generation, and the letters at the end describe the platform. So X1504VA is a 15-inch Vivobook with a 13th generation Intel chip. Write down the whole code before comparing prices. \"Vivobook 15\" alone describes a dozen machines.</p>\n<h2>Vivobook: choose by memory</h2>\n<p>Asus sells Vivobooks with 8GB at Rs 90,000 and with 16GB at Rs 80,000, depending on age and importer. The 16GB X1504VA is the bargain of the range, as we explain in <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-80000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 80,000</a>. The Vivobook S versions are slimmer with bigger batteries and better screens. The <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s14-m3407ha-2025\">S14 with a Ryzen 5 220</a> and 70Wh is a fine student machine, at a price that should be negotiated.</p>\n<h2>Zenbook: the thin ones</h2>\n<p>Zenbooks have metal bodies, OLED screens and good keyboards. The cheap one, the <a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Q415</a>, has 8GB and an imported past, described in <a href=\"/reviews/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415-review-nepal\">our review</a>. The current ones cost Rs 2 lakh and more and compete with the MacBook Air. Look for 16GB or more and at least 70Wh.</p>\n<h2>TUF: sturdy gaming</h2>\n<p>TUF laptops take knocks, cool well and cost less than ROG. The A15 with an RTX 3050 is getting old, as <a href=\"/reviews/asus-tuf-gaming-a15-rtx-3050-review-nepal\">our review</a> says. The A16 and F16 with RTX 4050 to 5060 are the ones to consider. In this family, ask for the graphics wattage and the panel\'s colour coverage, since both vary.</p>\n<h2>ROG: from fast to absurd</h2>\n<p>Strix models are thick and powerful. The <a href=\"/products/asus-rog-strix-g18-g814fp-2025-ryzen-9-9955hx-rtx-5070\">Strix G18 with a Ryzen 9 and RTX 5070</a> at about Rs 4 lakh with 32GB is better value than several others with the same graphics chip at Rs 4.6 lakh or more. Zephyrus models are thin and lovely, and our <a href=\"/news/asus-rog-zephyrus-g16-2025-rtx-5080-benchmarks-leaked\">Zephyrus G16 article</a> compares them. The Zephyrus Duo has two screens and costs as much as a small car.</p>\n<h2>V16 and Gaming V16</h2>\n<p>A newer family that puts gaming chips in plainer bodies. The <a href=\"/products/asus-v16-2025-core-7-rtx-5050\">V16 with an RTX 5050</a> at about Rs 2,08,000 is close to a Lenovo LOQ in idea. Check the watts and the screen.</p>\n<h2>ProArt and others</h2>\n<p>ProArt laptops are for creators, with accurate OLED screens and dials. Few reach Nepal. If one does, look at who provides the warranty. Chromebooks and ExpertBooks appear now and then.</p>\n<h2>Which Asus for which person?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>School or first laptop:</strong> Vivobook Go with a Core i3 or Ryzen 3, 8GB and an SSD of 256GB or more. Avoid the Celeron.</li>\n<li><strong>Bachelor student:</strong> Vivobook 15 X1504VA with 16GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Office and travel:</strong> Vivobook S14 or Zenbook 14.</li>\n<li><strong>Engineering and games:</strong> TUF A16 or V16 with an RTX 5050.</li>\n<li><strong>Creators:</strong> Zephyrus or ProArt with 32GB.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Traps in the Asus range</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>The Vivobook Go E410 with a Celeron and 64GB of eMMC. It is slow the day you buy it.</li>\n<li>The <a href=\"/reviews/asus-vivobook-14-x1404va-i3-review-nepal\">X1404VA with 128GB</a>, which needs a new drive at once.</li>\n<li>The <a href=\"/reviews/asus-vivobook-14-x1404vap-review-nepal\">X1404VAP</a>, sold with two different drives under one name.</li>\n<li>16GB in laptops above Rs 4 lakh with soldered memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Screens</h2>\n<p>Asus uses everything from dull 45% NTSC panels to reference-class OLED. On Vivobooks, look at the panel with your own eyes. On OLED models, set pixel care and dark mode, as static toolbars over years can leave a mark.</p>\n<h2>MyAsus: use it</h2>\n<p>The app registers the warranty, limits charging to 60 or 80 per cent, updates drivers and runs diagnostics. On gaming models, Armoury Crate controls fans and power. Remove the trial antivirus, as Windows Security is enough.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Asus has an authorised distributor and service in Nepal. Official units usually carry one or two years, and some include accidental cover if registered within a period. Units with codes for other regions, such as Q-series Zenbooks or North American ROG models, are imports with a shop warranty. Enter the serial number on the Asus site to see. Ask for the answer in writing.</p>\n<h2>Power and heat</h2>\n<p>Use the original charger. Some Vivobooks take 45W by a round plug, others USB-C. TUF and ROG machines need 150 to 280W, more than many small inverters supply cleanly. Clean the fans each year, and raise the rear of gaming models in summer.</p>\n<h2>Upgrades</h2>\n<p>Many Vivobooks have some memory soldered and one slot. TUF and Strix models have two slots and two M.2 bays, making them the most upgradeable. Zenbooks and Zephyrus are fixed except for the SSD. Check before buying, since it changes how much memory you need on the first day.</p>\n<h2>Asus or another brand?</h2>\n<p><strong>Lenovo</strong> has better keyboards and the best-value gaming laptop in the LOQ. <strong>HP</strong> and <strong>Dell</strong> have stronger office service. <strong>Acer</strong> is cheaper for similar parts. <strong>MSI</strong> is for gaming with bolder designs. Asus offers the widest choice, OLED at lower prices, and sturdy gaming machines.</p>\n<h2>Price behaviour</h2>\n<p>With so many models there is always something being cleared. Older Vivobooks and TUFs with good specifications appear at Rs 5,000 to 20,000 below newer, weaker ones. Ask what is old in stock. Discounts are larger after Tihar and before the new college sessions.</p>\n<h2>Checks in the shop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Full model code on the base matches the box and bill.</li>\n<li>Processor, memory and drive in Task Manager.</li>\n<li>Battery report with powercfg.</li>\n<li>Screen on plain white, grey and black.</li>\n<li>Hinge, keyboard, webcam and all ports.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>In short</h2>\n<p>Asus has a right laptop for nearly everyone and a wrong one beside it on the shelf. Let memory, screen and model code guide you, and not the family name. For advice that crosses brands, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">laptop guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/asus-proart-p16-h7606wx-2025.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(40,20,'Acer Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','acer-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Acer\'s Nepal line-up is mostly sensible, value-first laptops, from the Rs 72,990 <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-15-ag15-32p-39r2-2025-intel-core-3-n355-8gb-256gb-156-fhd\">Aspire Go 15</a> to the slim <a href=\"/products/acer-swift-edge-14-ai-sfe14-51t-960e-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-288v\">Swift Edge 14 AI</a> at Rs 3,60,000. The Aspire range covers everyday laptops, Swift is the light premium range, and Nitro and Predator are for gaming.</p>\n<h2>Acer laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-15-ag15-32p-39r2-2025-intel-core-3-n355-8gb-256gb-156-fhd\">Acer Aspire Go 15 AG15-32P-39R2 2025 (Intel Core 3 N355)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 72,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-15-ag15-21pt-2024-ryzen-5-7520u\">Acer Aspire Go 15 AG15-21PT 2024 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 76,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-32p-39zh-2024-intel-core-i3-n355\">Acer Aspire Lite 14 AL14-32p-39ZH 2024 (Intel Core i3-N355)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 78,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 2024 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 97,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-spin-14-asp14-51mtn-2024-intel-core-5-120u\">Acer Aspire Spin 14 ASP14-51MTN 2024 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-travelmate-p2-14-2024-tmp214-intel-core-5-120u\">Acer TravelMate P2 14 TMP214 2024 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,07,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-extensa-15-ex215-57-2024-intel-core-i5-13420h\">Acer Extensa 15 EX215-57 2024 (Intel Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,07,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-16-al16-54p-586n-2024-intel-core-i5-1334u\">Acer Aspire Lite 16 AL16-54P-586N 2024 (Intel Core i5-1334U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,09,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-spin-5-2023-i5-1335u\">Acer Spin 5 A5SP14-51MTN-58HT 2023 (13th Gen i5-1335U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,15,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-14-g1471m55l0-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-115u\">Acer Aspire Go 14 AG14‑71M‑55L0 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 115U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,15,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-16-ai-2025-snapdragon-x-x1-26100-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga\">Acer Aspire 16 AI A16-11M-X0LW 2025 (Snapdragon X X1-26100)</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-52h-2024-intel-core-i7-13620h-16gb-1tb-156-fhd\">Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-52H 2024 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-16-a16-52p-intel-core-i5-1334u\">Acer Aspire Lite 16 AL16-52P 2024 (Intel Core i5-1334U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,23,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-14-ai-a14-52m-51s1-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-226v-16gb-512tb-14-wuxga-touch\">Acer Aspire 14 AI A14-52M-51S1 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 226V)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-neo-14-sfn14-54h-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-115u\">Acer Swift Neo 14 SFN14-54H 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 115U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,26,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-sfl14-41m-r4qm-2025-amd-ryzen-7-260\">Acer Swift Lite 14 SFL14-41M-R4QM 2025 (Ryzen 7 260)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,32,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-spin-5-2023-i7-1355u-14-inch-touch\">Acer Spin 5 72SG 2023 (Intel Core i7-1355U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,33,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Acer Aspire 14 AI A14-52MT 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-go-14-sfg14-74t-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-225h-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga-touch\">Acer Swift Go 14 SFG14-74T 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 225H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-ai-sfl14-54m-786p-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-155u-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga-oled-60hz\">Acer Swift Lite 14 AI SFL14-54M-786P 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 155U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-anl16-71g-2025-intel-core-i5-13420h-rtx-3050\">Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-71G 2025 (Intel Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,51,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-7-a715-59g-76rm-2026-intel-core-7-240h-rtx-3050\">Acer Aspire 7 A715-59G-76RM 2026 (Intel Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-lite-16-2025-13th-gen-i7-13620h-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb\">Acer Nitro Lite 16 ANL16-51 2025 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-15-2025-intel-core-5-210h-rtx-4050\">Acer Nitro V 15 ANV15-52-5302 2025 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,70,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-15-anv15-52-57dy-2025-intel-core-5-210h-rtx-5050\">Acer Nitro V 15 ANV15-52-57DY 2025 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,82,400</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-14-ai-a14-52mt-94h5-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-288v-32gb-1tb-14-wuxga-touch\">Acer Aspire 14 AI A14-52MT-94H5 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 288V)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,84,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v15-anv15-52-7287-2025-intel-core-7-240h-rtx-4050\">Acer Nitro V15 ANV15-52-7287 2025 (Intel Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,89,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-anv16-71-2025-core-i7-14650hx-rtx-4050-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga-165hz\">Acer Nitro V 16 ANV16-71 2025 (Core i7-14650HX)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,99,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v15-anv15-52-73m0-2025-intel-core-7-240h-rtx-5050\">Acer Nitro V15 ANV15-52-73M0 2025 (Intel Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16s-ai-anv16s-41-2025-ryzen-7-260\">Acer Nitro V 16S AI ANV16S-41 2025 (Ryzen 7 260)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,28,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai\">Acer Nitro V 16 AI</a></td><td>Ryzen 7 350 · RTX 5060, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,30,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16s-2025-core-7-240h-rtx-5060\">Acer Nitro V 16S AI ANV16S-71 2025 (Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-edge-14-ai-sfe14-51t-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-256v\">Acer Swift Edge 14 AI SFE14-51T 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 256V)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,00,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-predator-helios-neo-16s-ai-phn16s7191aw-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5060\">Acer Predator Helios Neo 16S AI PHN16S‑71‑91AW 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-edge-14-ai-sfe14-51t-960e-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-288v\">Acer Swift Edge 14 AI SFE14-51T-960E 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 288V)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,60,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Where Acer is strongest</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lots of laptop for the money in the middle.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-52h-2024-intel-core-i7-13620h-16gb-1tb-156-fhd\">Aspire Lite 15 AL15-52H</a> gives you a Core i7-13620H, 16GB and a 1TB SSD for Rs 1,19,999. That\'s a good fit for engineering students who need real processing power.</li>\n<li><strong>AI laptops with long battery life.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Aspire 14 AI with Core Ultra 7 258V</a> includes 32GB and 1TB for Rs 1,39,999.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry gaming.</strong> The <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16-ai\">Nitro V 16 AI</a> with an RTX 5060 is one of the better-value gaming laptops at around Rs 2.3 lakh.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cheapest Aspire Go models use efficiency chips and 8GB of RAM. They\'re fine for classes and browsing, but not for heavier software. If your budget is under a lakh, also see our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">best laptops under Rs 1,00,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Acer\'s range at a glance</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-45p-ryzen-3-5400u\">Acer Aspire Lite 14 AL14-45P (Ryzen 3 5400U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;73,999</td><td>14&quot; WUXGA IPS Display (1920×1200, 300 nits, 1...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 3 5400U Processor (4 Cores / 8 Thr...</td><td>8GB DDR4 RAM</td><td>58Wh, 5100mAh, 11.4V, 3-Cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 2024 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;97,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7430U (7nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 58 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-extensa-15-ex215-57-2024-intel-core-i5-13420h\">Acer Extensa 15 EX215-57 2024 (Intel Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,07,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core i5-13420H (Intel 7), 8 cores / 12...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 53 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Acer Aspire 14 AI A14-52MT 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 258V (TSMC N3B), 8 cores /...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 65 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-15-anv15-52-57dy-2025-intel-core-5-210h-rtx-5050\">Acer Nitro V 15 ANV15-52-57DY 2025 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,82,400</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core 5 210H (Intel 7), 8 cores / 12 thr...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 57.5 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-16s-2025-core-7-240h-rtx-5060\">Acer Nitro V 16S AI ANV16S-71 2025 (Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,34,999</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core 7 240H (Intel 7), 10 cores / 16 th...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 76 Wh; 135W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The honest engineer of laptop brands</h2>\n<p>Acer spends little on advertising and much on parts. Its laptops are rarely beautiful, and at a given price they often have more memory, a bigger battery or a better processor than the HP or Dell beside them. That is why three of our general recommendations in Nepal are Acers. The weak points are plain designs, average speakers and a confusing list of names.</p>\n<h2>Families in plain words</h2>\n<p><strong>Aspire Lite</strong> and <strong>Aspire Go</strong> are budget machines for study. <strong>Aspire</strong> is the main line. <strong>Aspire AI</strong> uses efficient modern chips. <strong>Extensa</strong> and <strong>TravelMate</strong> are for business. <strong>Swift</strong> is thin and light. <strong>Nitro</strong> is for gaming and <strong>Predator</strong> for serious gaming. The code after the name, such as AL15-41, identifies the exact version.</p>\n<h2>Aspire Lite and Go</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Aspire Lite 15</a> with a six-core Ryzen, 16GB and a 58Wh battery at Rs 97,999 is our first choice under a lakh. <a href=\"/reviews/acer-aspire-lite-15-ryzen-5-review-nepal\">Our review</a> says why. Beware of the AL15-42P version at nearly the same price: it has a 36Wh battery. Two letters in the code and a third less battery.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-45p-ryzen-3-5400u\">Aspire Lite 14</a> with a Ryzen 3 is a good school laptop at about Rs 74,000. The Go 15 models vary from weak N-series chips to a decent Ryzen 7 7730U at Rs 1,14,999.</p>\n<h2>Extensa and TravelMate</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/acer-extensa-15-ex215-57-2024-intel-core-i5-13420h\">Extensa 15</a> with a Core i5-13420H and 16GB at about Rs 1,08,000 is one of the strongest processors near a lakh. For offices and computing students who sit at a desk, it is excellent and completely ignored by fashion.</p>\n<h2>Aspire 14 AI: the one we like most</h2>\n<p>With a Core Ultra 7 258V, 32GB and 1TB at Rs 1,39,999, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Aspire 14 AI</a> has all-day battery and memory for years. The screen is ordinary. If you want one laptop for five years of work or study and do not play, buy this. A Snapdragon version, the <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-16-ai-2025-snapdragon-x-x1-26100-16gb-512gb-16-wuxga\">Aspire 16 AI</a>, costs less and has the software limits explained in <a href=\"/news/qualcomm-oryon-snapdragon-x-elite-laptops-nepal-battery-benchmarks\">our article</a>.</p>\n<h2>Swift</h2>\n<p>Swift Neo and Swift Lite bring OLED screens for Rs 1.2 to 1.5 lakh. The processors in the cheaper ones, such as the Core Ultra 5 115U, are modest. Choose the OLED if your day is reading and film, and the Aspire 14 AI if it is work.</p>\n<h2>Aspire 7</h2>\n<p>A normal-looking laptop with an RTX 3050 at about Rs 1,55,000. Handy for engineering students who do not want a gaming look. The graphics chip is old, so ask which memory version it has.</p>\n<h2>Nitro: the popular choice</h2>\n<p>Nitro V 15 and V 16 cover RTX 3050 to 5060. The <a href=\"/products/acer-nitro-v-15-anv15-52-57dy-2025-intel-core-5-210h-rtx-5050\">Nitro V 15 with an RTX 5050</a> at Rs 1,82,400 is among the cheapest 50-series laptops. The catch is in the charger. A 135W adapter means lower graphics power than a Lenovo LOQ with the same chip. The <a href=\"/reviews/acer-nitro-lite-16-rtx-4050-review-nepal\">Nitro Lite 16</a> has the same story. The V 16S with an RTX 5060 and a 76Wh battery is slimmer and better finished.</p>\n<h2>Predator</h2>\n<p>Helios Neo machines are fast, heavy and well cooled, from about Rs 3 lakh. An older model with an i9-14900HX and an RTX 4080 at Rs 3,69,000 is unusually good value for pure graphics power. Check its warranty and how long it has been in stock.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which Acer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>School:</strong> Aspire Lite 14.</li>\n<li><strong>Bachelor student:</strong> Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41.</li>\n<li><strong>Computing on a budget:</strong> Extensa 15.</li>\n<li><strong>Professional:</strong> Aspire 14 AI.</li>\n<li><strong>First gaming laptop:</strong> Nitro V 15 or Nitro Lite 16.</li>\n<li><strong>Serious gaming:</strong> Predator, or a Legion.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Things to check on an Acer</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>The battery in watt-hours. It varies within a family.</li>\n<li>The charger\'s wattage on Nitro models.</li>\n<li>The panel: IPS, brightness and colour.</li>\n<li>Memory slots. Many Aspires have one free.</li>\n<li>The full model code on the base.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Acer has an authorised distributor and service in Kathmandu with partners elsewhere. Parts for Aspire and Nitro are common, and independent technicians know them well. Official units carry a one-year warranty, sometimes two. Imported Predators and Swifts may have only a shop\'s promise. Enter the serial number on Acer\'s site.</p>\n<h2>Software</h2>\n<p>AcerSense or Acer Care Center sets the 80 per cent charge limit, performance modes and updates. NitroSense controls fans. Remove trial antivirus, shopping shortcuts and the browser bars that sometimes come with them.</p>\n<h2>Build quality</h2>\n<p>Budget Aspires are plastic and flex a little. They last if carried in a padded bag. Hinges are the usual failure after three years. Open the lid from the middle. Keyboards are decent with number pads on 15-inch models.</p>\n<h2>Upgrades</h2>\n<p>Acer is friendly here. Most Aspire Lite, Extensa and Nitro models allow an extra stick of memory and a second SSD. It is a good way to buy a cheaper version and improve it. Have the service centre fit parts within the warranty period.</p>\n<h2>Acer compared</h2>\n<p><strong>Lenovo:</strong> better keyboards and more refined. <strong>HP, Dell:</strong> wider office service. <strong>Asus:</strong> more choice and better screens. <strong>MSI:</strong> flashier gaming. Acer tends to win on the sheet for the money.</p>\n<h2>Price and bargaining</h2>\n<p>Because Acer competes on value, list prices have less padding. Ask for memory or an SSD upgrade at cost, which helps you more than a bag. The best time is after festivals, when remaining Nitros are cleared.</p>\n<h2>In the end</h2>\n<p>If you read specifications and do not mind plain looks, start with Acer and make the others prove that they are better. At Rs 98,000 and Rs 1,40,000 in particular, most cannot. Compare with <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1 lakh</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-150000-nepal\">under Rs 1.5 lakh</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/acer-aspire-14-ai-a14-52mt-94h5-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-288v-32gb-1tb-14-wuxga-touch.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(41,20,'Amazfit Smartwatch Price in Nepal (November 2026)','amazfit-smartwatch-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Amazfit makes watches for people who want long battery life and serious fitness tracking without paying Apple or Samsung prices. In Nepal the current range runs from the compact <a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">GTR Mini</a> at Rs 17,499 to the rugged <a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-ultra\">T-Rex Ultra</a> at Rs 69,000.</p>\n<h2>Amazfit smartwatches price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">Amazfit GTR Mini</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 17,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah\">Amazfit Cheetah</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 36,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah-pro\">Amazfit Cheetah Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-ultra\">Amazfit T-Rex Ultra</a></td><td>47mm</td><td>Rs 69,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which one is for you?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday wear and a first proper watch:</strong> the GTR Mini. It has an AMOLED screen and built-in GPS, and lasts up to about two weeks per charge, in a small round case that suits smaller wrists.</li>\n<li><strong>Runners:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah\">Cheetah</a> and <a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah-pro\">Cheetah Pro</a> are built around running. They track routes accurately, and the Pro adds a tougher build and more training features.</li>\n<li><strong>Treks and outdoor trips:</strong> the T-Rex Ultra is the one for multi-day hikes. It has a tough case, AMOLED screen and GPS, and Amazfit rates it at up to 25 days of normal use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of them work with Android phones and iPhones through the Zepp app, though replying to messages is more limited than on an Apple Watch. For cheaper options from other brands, see <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">smartwatches under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Models with a confirmed price</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">Amazfit GTR Mini</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;17,499</td><td>1.28 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 14 days (normal usage); 280 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah\">Amazfit Cheetah</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;36,999</td><td>1.39 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (heavy usage), Up to 14 days (lo...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah-pro\">Amazfit Cheetah Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,999</td><td>1.45 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage), Up to 14 days (l...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-ultra\">Amazfit T-Rex Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,000</td><td>1.39 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 25 days (normal usage), Up to 50 days (...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.0, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Amazfit has many more models in the world than in this table. Others, such as the Bip 5, Active and T-Rex 2, appear in shops here from time to time without a steady price. If you find one, use the notes below to judge it.</p>\n<h2>What Amazfit stands for</h2>\n<p>Long battery, honest GPS, a good app and prices below Garmin. The company behind it, Zepp Health, concentrates on tracking and leaves the phone-like features to others. If your interest is running, trekking, cycling or sleep, it deserves a place on your list. If you want to answer WhatsApp at length from your wrist, it does not.</p>\n<h2>The families</h2>\n<p><strong>Bip</strong> is cheap and simple. <strong>Active</strong> and <strong>Balance</strong> are daily watches. <strong>GTR</strong> is round and classic, <strong>GTS</strong> square. <strong>Cheetah</strong> is for runners. <strong>T-Rex</strong> and <strong>Falcon</strong> are for the outdoors. Numbers show the generation.</p>\n<h2>GTR Mini</h2>\n<p>At Rs 17,499 the <a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">GTR Mini</a> is a small steel watch for slim wrists, with GPS and two weeks of battery. It is one of the few good-looking round watches for people who find 46mm absurd. The AMOLED is bright and the weight is low.</p>\n<h2>Cheetah and Cheetah Pro</h2>\n<p>Made for runners: very light, with dual-band GPS, offline maps on the Pro, a training coach and accurate tracking in cities. The <a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah\">Cheetah</a> at Rs 36,999 is round or square depending on version. The <a href=\"/products/amazfit-cheetah-pro\">Cheetah Pro</a> at Rs 49,999 adds a better screen, titanium bezel and speaker. Against a Garmin Forerunner of similar price, Amazfit gives a better display and Garmin better analysis and more dependable heart rate in intervals.</p>\n<h2>T-Rex Ultra</h2>\n<p>At Rs 69,000 the <a href=\"/products/amazfit-t-rex-ultra\">T-Rex Ultra</a> is built for mud, cold and water, with offline maps, dual-band GPS and up to 25 days in normal use. It has a steel body and works down to very low temperatures according to Amazfit. For guides and serious trekkers it competes with Garmin\'s Instinct and Fenix at a lower price. It is heavy at close to 90 grams.</p>\n<h2>T-Rex 2 and 3</h2>\n<p>If a dealer offers the T-Rex 2 or the newer T-Rex 3 near Rs 28,000 to 45,000, those are the value choices for trekking. The T-Rex 3 has a brighter screen and better maps. Ask about the exact model and warranty.</p>\n<h2>Bip and Active</h2>\n<p>The Bip 5 has GPS and a big LCD for around Rs 11,000 when available. The Active has AMOLED and GPS in a light frame near Rs 20,000. Today the Redmi Watch 6 Active and CMF Watch 3 Pro compete hard with them. Amazfit\'s advantage is the app.</p>\n<h2>Zepp: the app</h2>\n<p>Zepp is among the better companion apps. It shows readiness, sleep quality, training load and trends clearly. It syncs with Strava, Google\'s Health Connect, Apple Health and others. Zepp Coach creates training plans without a fee on many models, and some deeper insights are sold as a subscription. You can ignore the subscription.</p>\n<h2>Zepp OS on the watch</h2>\n<p>There is a small store of mini apps and watch faces. Notifications work on Android and iPhone. Quick replies exist on Android. Zepp Flow, a voice assistant, works on newer models with an internet connection. Music storage and Bluetooth calling are on several.</p>\n<h2>GPS in the hills</h2>\n<p>Dual-band models hold a line under trees and in valleys. Update the satellite data in the app before a trek. With the highest accuracy, battery falls to 20 to 40 hours of recording depending on model. In endurance mode it can stretch to several days. For Everest Base Camp, record each day separately and charge every third or fourth night.</p>\n<h2>Offline maps</h2>\n<p>On Cheetah Pro, T-Rex Ultra and newer watches you can download map tiles and import GPX routes. Prepare at home on Wi-Fi. A watch map is a guide and not a substitute for a paper map and local advice.</p>\n<h2>Heart rate and health</h2>\n<p>Good at rest and steady pace. Cold hands at altitude reduce accuracy for all optical sensors, so wear the watch over a thin sleeve or use a chest strap. SpO2 readings at altitude are indicative. Use a fingertip oximeter for decisions, and descend if you feel unwell, whatever the watch says.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>T-Rex models pass a range of military tests and resist 10ATM. GTR and Cheetah are 5ATM. Glass varies. Buttons on T-Rex are excellent with gloves.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Amazfit is sold through distributors, with warranty usually handled by replacement. Ask for one year in writing. Some shops sell Indian or Chinese units, with Chinese-language boxes. Check the region in the app.</p>\n<h2>Used Amazfits</h2>\n<p>Battery wear is slow, so a used T-Rex can be good. Check that it can be unpaired from the previous account. Look for scratches, test GPS outside and press every button.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small wrist, daily wear:</strong> GTR Mini.</li>\n<li><strong>Runner:</strong> Cheetah, or Pro if maps matter.</li>\n<li><strong>Trekker or guide:</strong> T-Rex 3 if found, T-Rex Ultra for extremes.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget GPS:</strong> Bip 5 or Redmi Watch 6 Active.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with rivals</h2>\n<p><strong>Huawei:</strong> better sensors and style, harder app installation, fewer rugged choices. <strong>Garmin:</strong> best analysis and support for athletes, costlier. <strong>Xiaomi:</strong> cheaper, simpler. <strong>Samsung and Apple:</strong> smarter and charged daily.</p>\n<h2>Setting up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Install Zepp and update the firmware.</li>\n<li>Download satellite data and, if supported, maps.</li>\n<li>Choose sports you use and hide the rest.</li>\n<li>Connect Strava.</li>\n<li>Set sleep mode and raise-to-wake hours.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Prices fall when a new number arrives, and older Amazfits are often discounted heavily online. Stock in Nepal is uneven. If a model you want appears at a fair price with a warranty, take it, as it may not be there next month.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Amazfit is for people who move. The GTR Mini is a lovely small watch, the Cheetah line suits runners, and T-Rex is the trekking choice for those who cannot justify a Garmin. For the general field, start with our <a href=\"/guides/smartwatch-buying-guide-nepal-2024\">smartwatch buying guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/amazfit-t-rex-ultra.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(42,20,'Anker Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','anker-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Anker sells its earbuds under the <strong>Soundcore</strong> name, and they\'ve become a safe pick in Nepal because even the cheap models tend to have decent apps and sensible sound. The range here runs from the Rs 2,999 <a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-p20i\">P20i</a> to the Rs 39,999 <a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro-max\">Liberty 5 Pro Max</a>.</p>\n<h2>Anker earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-p20i\">Soundcore P20i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-p30i\">Soundcore P30i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r50i-nc\">Soundcore R50i NC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-r50i\">Soundcore R50i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r60i-nc\">Soundcore R60i NC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-c50i\">Soundcore C50i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,799</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-p41i\">Soundcore P41i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 8,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-liberty-4-nc\">Soundcore Liberty 4 NC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 12,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-buds\">Soundcore Liberty Buds</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-liberty-4-pro\">Soundcore Liberty 4 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5\">Soundcore Liberty 5</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 17,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro-max\">Soundcore Liberty 5 Pro Max</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>How to choose</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 5,000:</strong> the P20i is a fine starter pair. If you can spend a little more, the R50i NC and P30i add noise cancellation, which matters on noisy buses.</li>\n<li><strong>Best all-rounder:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5\">Liberty 5</a> (Rs 17,499) has strong noise cancellation, LDAC for higher-quality audio on Android, and multipoint, so it stays connected to your phone and laptop at once.</li>\n<li><strong>The Liberty 5 Pro Max</strong> adds a smart case with a screen and more features. It\'s impressive, but most people won\'t miss those for the extra Rs 22,500.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you\'re comparing across brands, our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">best earbuds under Rs 5,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">under Rs 20,000</a> guides include Soundcore alongside the alternatives.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Soundcore earbuds compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-p20i\">Soundcore P20i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 30 hours (case); Qui...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-p30i\">Soundcore P30i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 45 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r60i-nc\">Soundcore R60i NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,499</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 50 hours (case); Qui...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-p41i\">Soundcore P41i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,499</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 192 hours (case), Wi...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-liberty-4-nc\">Soundcore Liberty 4 NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;12,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 50 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5\">Soundcore Liberty 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;17,499</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 48 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5-pro-max\">Soundcore Liberty 5 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Music: 6.5 hours (buds) / 28 hours (case), Wi...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Anker, Soundcore: the same thing</h2>\n<p>Anker makes chargers, and Soundcore is its audio brand. Both names appear on boxes and in shops. What sets Soundcore apart from other affordable brands is the software. The app gives a proper equaliser, control settings, firmware updates and, on better models, a hearing test that shapes the sound to your ears. A Rs 3,000 pair with a good app can sound better than a Rs 5,000 pair without one.</p>\n<h2>The letters</h2>\n<p><strong>P</strong> is the bass-focused budget line. <strong>R</strong> is value with features. <strong>C</strong> clips to the ear and leaves it open. <strong>Liberty</strong> is premium. <strong>NC</strong> in the name means noise cancelling. <strong>i</strong> is simply part of the name. So R50i and R50i NC are different products at the same price, and the second is the better buy.</p>\n<h2>Under Rs 5,500</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-p20i\">P20i</a> at Rs 2,999 is the starter: no ANC, strong bass, ten hours per charge. The <a href=\"/products/soundcore-p30i\">P30i</a> at Rs 4,999 has ANC and a case that turns into a phone stand. The R50i NC has ANC with a smaller body. The <a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r60i-nc\">R60i NC</a> at Rs 5,499 adds LDAC. If you can reach Rs 5,499, that is the one. It offers nearly everything that a Rs 9,000 pair did a year ago.</p>\n<h2>Rs 7,800 to 8,500</h2>\n<p>The C50i is an open clip-on for people who dislike things in the ear canal and want to hear traffic. Bass is light and sound leaks a little. The <a href=\"/products/soundcore-p41i\">P41i</a> has an enormous case that doubles as a power bank, with a listed 192 hours. It is bulky, and for travellers it removes one gadget from the bag.</p>\n<h2>Liberty: Rs 13,000 to 17,500</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-liberty-4-nc\">Liberty 4 NC</a> is a former favourite at a falling price. The Liberty Buds are semi-open for comfort. The Liberty 4 Pro has better drivers and a case with a small display. The <a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5\">Liberty 5</a> is the newest all-rounder and our choice in the class. If the 4 NC is offered near Rs 10,000, it is the bargain.</p>\n<h2>Liberty 5 Pro and Pro Max</h2>\n<p>At Rs 35,000 to 40,000 these meet Samsung and Apple. They are good, and at that price the AirPods Pro 3 or Galaxy Buds 4 Pro give owners of those phones more. Choose Soundcore\'s best only if you switch between phone brands and want LDAC with strong ANC.</p>\n<h2>How to tune them</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update the firmware.</li>\n<li>Run the HearID test in a quiet room, if your model has it.</li>\n<li>Start from the Soundcore Signature preset and reduce the lowest band by two steps.</li>\n<li>Raise 2 to 4kHz slightly for speech.</li>\n<li>Set touch controls: single tap off, to avoid accidents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Noise cancelling modes</h2>\n<p>Adaptive mode changes with the surroundings. Manual lets you choose strength. On the street use transparency. In a bus use full. If you feel pressure, lower it by a step.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Good indoors, fair outside. The stemmed Liberty and P30i are clearer than round buds. Wind is hard for all. For all-day calling, a headset is better.</p>\n<h2>LDAC on a budget</h2>\n<p>LDAC improves sound on Android with good sources at the cost of battery. Turn it on in the app, not only in the phone. When LDAC is on, some models cannot use multipoint at the same time. Decide which you need.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Soundcore\'s claims are fairly honest: 8 to 12 hours without ANC, 6 to 8 with. Ten minutes of charging gives a couple of hours. Cases on higher models charge wirelessly.</p>\n<h2>iPhone or Android</h2>\n<p>They work well with both. iPhones use AAC. Find features depend on the model, and some newer ones appear in Google\'s Find Hub network. Automatic switching like AirPods is not available, but multipoint does a similar job between two devices.</p>\n<h2>Fit and comfort</h2>\n<p>The boxes include three to five tips. Liberty models have soft wings on some versions. If one ear is smaller, use two sizes. For sport, the P and R lines with light bodies stay in well. For open ears, look at the C series.</p>\n<h2>Water resistance</h2>\n<p>Between IPX4 and IP55 depending on model. Enough for sweat and drizzle. The case is not protected.</p>\n<h2>Copies</h2>\n<p>Soundcore is copied less than JBL and Apple, but it happens. A real pair appears in the Soundcore app at once, has a serial number and an importer\'s sticker. Prices far below our list deserve suspicion.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Anker\'s distributor gives warranty on official units, commonly 18 months abroad and 6 to 12 months here, depending on the seller. Ask, and keep the bill. A single lost bud is rarely available. Some shops sell only complete sets.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First pair:</strong> P20i.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> R60i NC.</li>\n<li><strong>Traveller:</strong> P41i.</li>\n<li><strong>Open ear:</strong> C50i.</li>\n<li><strong>Best Soundcore for most budgets:</strong> Liberty 5, or Liberty 4 NC on sale.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Against rivals</h2>\n<p><strong>OnePlus Nord Buds 4</strong> has stronger ANC than the P30i at the same price. <strong>CMF Buds 2 Plus</strong> at Rs 8,999 beats everything Soundcore sells between Rs 6,000 and 12,000. <strong>Redmi Buds 8 Pro</strong> at Rs 14,999 is the Liberty 5\'s closest rival. Soundcore\'s app remains the best among them.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Soundcore discounts often. Online brand days take 15 to 25 per cent off. The Liberty line drops when a successor is announced.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy the R60i NC if you want to spend a little, and the Liberty 5 if you want to spend once. Skip the models in between unless one of their special features is precisely what you need. For the whole market, see <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/soundcore-liberty-5.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(43,20,'Anker Headphones Price in Nepal (November 2026)','anker-headphones-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Soundcore\'s over-ear headphones are some of the best value noise-cancelling headphones you can buy in Nepal. They often cost a third of what Sony and Bose charge, and they get you most of the way there. Prices run from Rs 4,999 for the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Q11i</a> to Rs 25,499 for the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-one-pro\">Space One Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Anker headphones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q20i\">Soundcore Q20i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 9,199</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-one\">Soundcore Space One</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 12,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-q45\">Soundcore Space Q45</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-2\">Soundcore Space 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 24,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-one-pro\">Soundcore Space One Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 25,499</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our take</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For long trips:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-q45\">Space Q45</a> (Rs 19,999) is the one we\'d buy. It has good noise cancellation, LDAC support, multipoint and up to 65 hours of playback, so a Kathmandu–Pokhara round trip won\'t come close to emptying it.</li>\n<li><strong>On a budget:</strong> the Q11i and Q20i are comfortable, fun-sounding headphones for music at home. The Q20i adds noise cancellation.</li>\n<li><strong>The Space One and Space 2</strong> sit between those two, with lighter builds and newer chips. Compare them on the product pages before you choose.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For other brands, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-10000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 10,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Soundcore headphones</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 60 hours, With ANC off; 2 hours, Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q20i\">Soundcore Q20i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,199</td><td>Music: 40 hours, With ANC on, 60 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-one\">Soundcore Space One</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;12,999</td><td>Music: 40 hours, With ANC on, 55 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-q45\">Soundcore Space Q45</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;19,999</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 65 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-2\">Soundcore Space 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,499</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 70 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-one-pro\">Soundcore Space One Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>Music: 40 hours, With ANC on, 60 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Six headphones, three purposes</h2>\n<p>Soundcore\'s over-ear range divides neatly. The <strong>Q</strong> models are cheap and cheerful. The <strong>Space One</strong> family is the modern middle. The <strong>Space Q45</strong> and <strong>Space 2</strong> are for people who need quiet all day. All share the same excellent app, long batteries and multipoint, so you are choosing noise cancelling, comfort and finish.</p>\n<h2>Q11i: Rs 4,999</h2>\n<p>No noise cancelling, sixty hours of battery, soft pads. For music at home and online lessons it is the best under Rs 5,000. Because there is no ANC circuit to pay for, the money goes into sound and battery.</p>\n<h2>Q20i: Rs 9,199</h2>\n<p>The cheapest headphones in Nepal whose noise cancelling we would call real. Fans, engines and air conditioners fade by a clear amount. The body is plastic and a bit large. LDAC is not supported. A 3.5mm cable lets you use them when the battery is flat.</p>\n<h2>Space One: Rs 12,999</h2>\n<p>Here is the sweet spot. Better adaptive ANC that works on some voices, LDAC, 40 hours with ANC, a lighter body and a softer headband. It folds for travel. Against the CMF Headphone Pro at Rs 13,999 it is a close fight: CMF has physical controls and replaceable pads, Soundcore has the more detailed app. Try both for comfort.</p>\n<h2>Space Q45: Rs 19,999</h2>\n<p>A little older and still strong, with fifty hours with ANC on and a comfortable fit for long flights. At its list price the Space One makes it hard to justify. At Rs 15,000 to 16,000 on sale it is excellent.</p>\n<h2>Space 2: Rs 24,499</h2>\n<p>The newest. It aims at all-day wear: light, gentle clamp, strong cancelling in the low frequencies that make bus and plane journeys tiring. If you wear headphones for six or more hours a day, comfort is worth the price.</p>\n<h2>Space One Pro: Rs 25,499</h2>\n<p>A clever design that folds very small, with premium materials. Sound is refined. Its ANC is close to that of the Space 2. Choose it for travel and looks.</p>\n<h2>How noise cancelling really differs</h2>\n<p>All reduce low hum. Moving up the range, three things improve: how much of the voice range is reduced, how well the cancelling adapts when you move your head or wear glasses, and how natural transparency sounds. If your noise is a road or generator, the Q20i does most of the work. If it is people talking, spend more, and know that nothing under Rs 60,000 erases voices.</p>\n<h2>Comfort notes</h2>\n<p>Glasses break the seal of the pads. Thin frames suffer less. Test with yours. In summer, pads become warm. Take a break every hour. Pads on most models can be replaced with third-party ones for under Rs 1,500, and fresh pads restore both comfort and ANC.</p>\n<h2>Sound and equaliser</h2>\n<p>Default tuning is bass-heavy. In the app, the Acoustic or Podcast presets are good starting points. HearID on Space models makes a personal curve. Save two: one for music, one for voices.</p>\n<h2>Working with a laptop</h2>\n<p>Multipoint connects laptop and phone. On Windows, choose the headphones as the output and the laptop\'s microphone as the input, or call quality and music quality both fall. With LDAC turned on, multipoint may be limited. Update the Bluetooth driver from the laptop maker\'s site.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Voices come through clearly indoors. In wind they are poor. If your income depends on calls, buy a headset with a boom microphone for Rs 5,000 and keep these for music.</p>\n<h2>Battery facts</h2>\n<p>Figures are close to the truth at moderate volume. With LDAC and ANC, subtract a third. Five minutes of charging gives two to four hours. Charge once a week and forget.</p>\n<h2>Wired use</h2>\n<p>Most have a 3.5mm input, and the cable may be included. Wired mode without power works on some and sounds thinner. For editing and instruments, choose proper wired headphones.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>A game mode reduces delay for casual play. For shooters use a wired or 2.4GHz headset. With a PlayStation, connect by cable to the controller.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>Plastic hinges are the weak point of the Q models. Open them with two hands. Use a case. The Space models have metal sliders. Expect three to five years.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and where to buy</h2>\n<p>Buy from a seller who shows Anker\'s distributor sticker. Warranty is usually one year or a little more. Check that the app recognises the headphones before leaving. Online brand stores run deep discounts, especially on Q45 and Q20i.</p>\n<h2>Which one for which person</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Student in a quiet room:</strong> Q11i.</li>\n<li><strong>Student near a road:</strong> Q20i.</li>\n<li><strong>Office worker:</strong> Space One.</li>\n<li><strong>Remote worker wearing them all day:</strong> Space 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Frequent flyer with a small bag:</strong> Space One Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Bargain hunter:</strong> Q45 on sale.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with other brands</h2>\n<p><strong>JBL:</strong> more energetic, higher prices for ANC. <strong>Sony:</strong> the CH720N at Rs 27,000 is lighter, and the Space 2 cancels more. The XM5 and XM6 are in another class and price. <strong>CMF:</strong> Space One\'s equal. <strong>boAt:</strong> cheaper without ANC.</p>\n<h2>A note on volume</h2>\n<p>The greatest benefit of noise cancelling is that you can listen quietly. Set the limit in the app or phone to 85 decibels. If the person beside you can name the song, turn it down.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n<p><strong>Do they work with a TV?</strong> Yes, if it has Bluetooth. A slight delay can be adjusted on many TVs.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I sleep in them?</strong> Not comfortably. Use a headband or small buds.</p>\n<p><strong>Are they good for children?</strong> Only with a volume limit. Better to buy children\'s headphones.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Space One is the Soundcore to buy. Go down to the Q20i if money is short, up to the Space 2 if your ears live in them. And compare prices on the day, since the Q45 sometimes drops below the Space One and then becomes the answer. Others at these prices are in <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/soundcore-space-q45.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(44,20,'Apple Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','apple-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Apple\'s laptop range in Nepal is simpler than it looks: the <strong>MacBook Neo</strong> for students, the <strong>MacBook Air</strong> for most people, and the <strong>MacBook Pro</strong> for heavy work. Prices start at Rs 1,34,999 for the <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> and go past Rs 9 lakh for a fully loaded 16-inch Pro.</p>\n<h2>Apple laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Apple MacBook Neo</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,54,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">Apple MacBook Air 13 (M5)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,34,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,89,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-15\">Apple MacBook Air 15 (M5)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,69,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,29,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">Apple MacBook Pro 14 (M5)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,76,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>24GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,15,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,56,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-14-inch-m5\">Apple MacBook Pro 14-inch M5 (2025)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,76,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>24GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,15,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,56,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-14-inch-m5-pro-2026\">Apple MacBook Pro 14-inch M5 Pro (2026)</a></td><td>24GB - 1TB - 15-core - 16-core</td><td>Rs 4,74,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>24GB - 2TB - 15-core - 16-core</td><td>Rs 5,69,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>24GB - 2TB - 18-core - 20-core</td><td>Rs 6,06,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-16-inch-m5-pro-2026\">Apple MacBook Pro 16-inch M5 Pro (2026)</a></td><td>48GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 6,77,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-14-inch-m5-max-2026\">Apple MacBook Pro 14-inch M5 Max (2026)</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,76,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-16-inch-m5-max-2026\">Apple MacBook Pro 16-inch M5 Max (2026)</a></td><td>36GB - 2TB - 18-core - 32-core GPU</td><td>Rs 8,33,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>48GB - 2TB - 18-core - 40-core GPU</td><td>Rs 9,46,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which MacBook to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For most people:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">MacBook Air 13 (M5)</a>. It\'s silent, has 16GB as standard and lasts a full workday on battery. Choose the <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-15\">15-inch</a> only if you want the bigger screen.</li>\n<li><strong>On a tight budget:</strong> the MacBook Neo is a real Mac for less, but memory is fixed at 8GB and the 256GB model lacks Touch ID and a backlit keyboard.</li>\n<li><strong>For video editors and developers:</strong> the MacBook Pro adds a fan, a 120Hz XDR display and more ports. The <a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-14-inch-m5-pro-2026\">14-inch M5 Pro</a> is the sweet spot. The <a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-16-inch-m5-max-2026\">16-inch M5 Max</a> is only worth it for very heavy 3D or video work.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One tip: MacBook memory and storage can\'t be upgraded later, so buy what you\'ll need in three years, not what you need today.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>MacBooks side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro, 8GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,33,499</td><td>Liquid Retina display, 13.0-inch (diagonal) L...</td><td>Apple A18 Pro (6-core CPU, 5-core GPU)</td><td>8GB unified memory</td><td>256GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">Apple MacBook Air 13 (M5, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,42,499</td><td>13.6-inch (diagonal) LED-backlit display with...</td><td>Apple M5 (10-core CPU, 8/10-core GPU)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X Unified Memory</td><td>512GB NVMe SSD 6,473 MB/s Read speeds and 6,5...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-15\">Apple MacBook Air 15 (M5, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,79,999</td><td>Liquid Retina display 15.3-inch (diagonal) LE...</td><td>Apple M5 (10-core CPU, 10-core GPU)</td><td>16GB unified memory</td><td>512GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5\">Apple MacBook Pro 14 (M5, 16GB/1TB, Space Black)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,64,999</td><td>14.2-inch 3024 × 1964 resolution, 254 PPI 1,0...</td><td>Total CPU Cores 10 cores consisting of 4 high...</td><td>16GB Unified Memory</td><td>1TB SSD Storage</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/macbook-pro-14-m5-pro\">Apple MacBook Pro 14 (M5 Pro, 24GB/1TB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,63,499</td><td>Liquid Retina XDR display 14.2-inch (diagonal...</td><td>Apple M5 Pro (up to 18-core CPU, 20-core GPU)</td><td>24GB unified memory</td><td>1 TB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-macbook-pro-16-inch-m5-pro-2026\">Apple MacBook Pro 16-inch M5 Pro (2026)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,77,000</td><td>16.2 inches, IPS LCD, 3.5K (3456 x 2234 pixel...</td><td>Apple M5 Pro (3nm)</td><td>48GB</td><td>1TB SSD</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Three ladders in one shop</h2>\n<p>For the first time Apple sells MacBooks at three clearly different levels. The <strong>Neo</strong>, from about Rs 1,33,499, is for study and writing. The <strong>Air</strong>, from about Rs 2,42,499, is for nearly all work. The <strong>Pro</strong>, from about Rs 3,65,000, is for people who export, compile and render for a living. Decide which of those you are before looking at colours.</p>\n<p>You will notice two listings for the same Pro in some places, such as the 14-inch M5 at Rs 3,64,999 and Rs 3,76,500. They come from different sellers and dates. Ask for the exact configuration, and compare the two.</p>\n<h2>MacBook Neo: for whom?</h2>\n<p>Students in non-technical courses, writers, teachers, parents and small businesses. It is silent, light and lasts all day. Its 8GB can never grow, and the base model has no Touch ID or keyboard backlight. <a href=\"/reviews/macbook-neo-review-nepal\">Our review</a> helps you decide between 256GB and 512GB.</p>\n<h2>MacBook Air M5: the default</h2>\n<p>Sixteen gigabytes, a superb screen and enough power for photo work, web development and 4K editing of short films. The 13-inch is the better traveller. The 15-inch costs about Rs 37,500 more, with the same speed and a larger display, and is kinder to people who work in spreadsheets. <a href=\"/reviews/macbook-air-m5-13-review-nepal\">Our review</a> names it the best laptop for most people with the budget.</p>\n<h2>MacBook Pro: what the money adds</h2>\n<p>A fan, so it keeps its speed. An XDR display that is far brighter, with 120Hz. More ports, including HDMI and an SD slot. Better speakers. Longer battery life. With M5 Pro and Max, more cores, more memory and faster video engines. If you do not export or render at least weekly, you will not use what you paid for.</p>\n<h2>M5, M5 Pro or M5 Max?</h2>\n<p><strong>M5</strong> is right for developers, photographers and short-form video. <strong>M5 Pro</strong> is for multicam 4K, large code bases, many virtual machines and several displays. <strong>M5 Max</strong> is for 3D, 8K, heavy effects and local AI models with large memory. Each step costs more than a lakh here. Let a slow task in your present work decide, and not ambition.</p>\n<h2>Memory: buy once</h2>\n<p>Unified memory is shared by processor and graphics and cannot be changed. 16GB is comfortable for most. 24GB is right for developers with Docker and for photographers with large catalogues. 36GB and above for professionals. On the Neo, 8GB is what there is.</p>\n<h2>Storage: buy outside</h2>\n<p>Apple charges a great deal for internal storage. 512GB inside, with a 1 or 2TB external SSD on USB-C, is the sensible combination. See <a href=\"/products?category=storage\">storage prices</a>.</p>\n<h2>M6 and the OLED MacBook Pro</h2>\n<p>Apple has announced newer Pros that are not officially in Nepal yet. The estimated prices and whether to wait are covered in <a href=\"/news/macbook-pro-m6-oled-macbook-pro-expected-price-nepal\">this article</a>. If your Mac works, waiting a couple of months may bring a newer chip or a cheaper M5.</p>\n<h2>Will my software run?</h2>\n<p>Most will. Office, Google tools, Adobe, AutoCAD for Mac, MATLAB, SPSS, Python, web tools. Some will not: Tally and several Nepali accounting programs, ETABS and SAP2000, SolidWorks, Revit, certain government signing drivers and Windows-only exam software. Parallels can run Windows for Arm, at extra cost and with its own limits. Make a list before you buy.</p>\n<h2>Nepali typing</h2>\n<p>macOS includes Devanagari keyboards, romanised and traditional. Preeti and other legacy fonts can be installed, though documents in those fonts need converters for sharing. Unicode works perfectly.</p>\n<h2>Buying official</h2>\n<p>Authorised resellers sell with a VAT bill and a proper warranty. Apple\'s one-year limited warranty on Macs is generally honoured internationally through authorised service, which makes a Mac safer to import than a phone. Check the serial at checkcoverage.apple.com. Keyboard layout, plug and language are the usual differences. A US layout is the most common here.</p>\n<h2>Used MacBooks</h2>\n<p>An M1 Air at Rs 75,000 to 95,000, an M2 Air at Rs 1 to 1.3 lakh and an M1 Pro 14-inch at Rs 1.6 to 2 lakh are all good computers in 2026. Prefer 16GB. Check:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Battery cycles and condition in System Information.</li>\n<li>Activation Lock: erase it and set it up in front of the seller.</li>\n<li>No MDM profile from a company or school. Look for \"remote management\" during set-up.</li>\n<li>Keyboard, trackpad, screen coating, hinge, ports, speakers.</li>\n<li>Liquid marks and dented corners.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avoid Intel MacBooks now. They are hot, slow by comparison and near the end of support.</p>\n<h2>Repairs</h2>\n<p>Screens and logic boards are expensive, and liquid damage is not covered. Use a sleeve, keep tea away, and never close the lid on anything, not even a thin webcam cover. Batteries can be replaced by authorised providers in a few days.</p>\n<h2>EMI and education offers</h2>\n<p>Resellers offer zero-interest EMI through banks and occasional student discounts. Ask for the cash price too. On Rs 2,42,499, twelve months is about Rs 20,208 a month before fees.</p>\n<h2>Cost over time</h2>\n<p>A MacBook Air kept six years and sold for a quarter of its price costs about Rs 2,500 a month. A Rs 1,00,000 Windows laptop kept four years and sold for little costs about Rs 1,900. The gap is smaller than the stickers suggest, and it buys battery life, screen and quiet.</p>\n<h2>Mac or Windows?</h2>\n<p>Choose a Mac for battery, build, video and music work, and life with an iPhone. Choose Windows for games, engineering, local accounting, upgrades and price. There is no winner, only a list of programs.</p>\n<h2>First-day set-up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update macOS.</li>\n<li>Turn on FileVault and Find My.</li>\n<li>Set up Time Machine to an external drive.</li>\n<li>Install a window manager and Rectangle-style shortcuts if you come from Windows.</li>\n<li>Leave optimised charging on.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Study and writing:</strong> Neo 512GB, or a used M2 Air with 16GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Most professionals:</strong> Air M5 13-inch.</li>\n<li><strong>Spreadsheets and design:</strong> Air M5 15-inch.</li>\n<li><strong>Paid video and code:</strong> Pro 14 M5 or M5 Pro.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For alternatives at each budget, see <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">best laptops in Nepal</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/apple-macbook-pro-16-inch-m5-pro-2026.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(45,20,'Apple Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','apple-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Apple sells more iPhone models in Nepal right now than ever, because its 2026 launches were split across the year. The cheapest is the <a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-15\">iPhone 15</a> at Rs 1,27,499, and the most expensive is the foldable <a href=\"/products/iphone-duo\">iPhone Duo</a>, from Rs 4,30,900.</p>\n<h2>Apple phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-15\">iPhone 15</a></td><td>128GB</td><td>Rs 1,27,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,32,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,75,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-16\">iPhone 16</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 1,46,599</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,15,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-air\">iPhone Air</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,90,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,32,899</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,75,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro\">iPhone 17 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,21,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,63,599</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,05,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro-max\">iPhone 17 Pro Max</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,42,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,84,599</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,27,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 4,11,399</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,59,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,01,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,86,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 5,13,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,79,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,22,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,07,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 5,34,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-duo\">iPhone Duo</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 4,30,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 4,72,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 5,57,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 6,84,900</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which iPhone makes sense</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest current model:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a> (Rs 1,32,499) has the A19 chip, Apple Intelligence and 256GB as standard. For most people it\'s a better buy than the older iPhone 15, despite the price being close.</li>\n<li><strong>The balanced choice:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a> (Rs 1,64,999) adds a 120Hz screen and a second camera.</li>\n<li><strong>Best camera and battery right now:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro-max\">iPhone 17 Pro Max</a> (Rs 2,42,499) has a 4x telephoto and the biggest battery of the 17 series.</li>\n<li><strong>The newest:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a> and <a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">18 Pro Max</a> start at Rs 2,59,900. The regular iPhone 18 is expected in spring 2027.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before you pay, <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">check the phone is an official unit</a>. Grey iPhones can be blocked on Nepali networks.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The present range</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,32,499</td><td>6.1-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1,200 nits</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>4,005mAh; 15W MagSafe/Qi2 wireless</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-16\">iPhone 16</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,46,599</td><td>6.1 inches Super Retina XDR OLED, 60Hz, 1000...</td><td>Apple A18 (3nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Ion 3561 mAh, non-removable; Wired, PD2.0,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-17\">iPhone 17</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1206×2622, LT...</td><td>Apple A19 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>25W MagSafe, 15W Qi2; about 50% in 20 minutes...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-air\">iPhone Air</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,90,699</td><td>6.5-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1260×2736, LT...</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>MagSafe wireless and wired charging; 4.5W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-17-pro\">iPhone 17 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,21,299</td><td>6.3 inches LTPO Super Retina XDR OLED, 120Hz,...</td><td>Apple A19 Pro (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Ion 4252, non-removable; Up to 50% in 20 m...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro\">iPhone 18 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,900</td><td>6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Up to 36 hours video playback; 50W wired (abo...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-18-pro-max\">iPhone 18 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,79,900</td><td>6.9-inch Super Retina XDR OLED, 1320×2868, LT...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>50W wired, 25W MagSafe wireless, 4.5W reverse...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/iphone-duo\">iPhone Duo</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,30,900</td><td>7.6-inch inner LTPO OLED (1878×2670, 120Hz, n...</td><td>Apple A20 Pro (2nm) with vapour chamber</td><td>12GB</td><td>Dual-cell; up to 31 hours video (inner screen...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Which iPhone is for whom?</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s range looks long, and for most people it contains two phones. The <strong>iPhone 17</strong> is for almost everyone: a 120Hz screen, two good cameras, long battery, years of support. The <strong>18 Pro</strong> or <strong>Pro Max</strong> is for those who film and photograph seriously. The others serve a particular wish: lowest price (17e), thinness (Air), novelty (Duo) or a saving on an older flagship (17 Pro and Pro Max).</p>\n<h2>Cheapest sensible iPhone</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">17e</a> at Rs 1,32,499 has a new chip, 8GB of memory and a strong battery with one rear camera. It is better than a new iPhone 15 at Rs 1,27,499, which is older by two generations, has 6GB, and does not support Apple Intelligence. The difference of Rs 5,000 decides itself. The 16 at Rs 1,46,599 sits in between and is hard to justify when the 17 is Rs 18,000 more.</p>\n<h2>iPhone 17: the one we recommend</h2>\n<p>For the first time the regular iPhone has the smooth display that used to be reserved for the Pro. It starts with 256GB. The front camera lets you take wide group selfies in portrait. <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-17-review-nepal\">Our review</a> finds little to complain about except slow wired charging compared with Android and the missing telephoto.</p>\n<h2>Pro models: 17 or 18?</h2>\n<p>The 18 Pro and Pro Max are faster, more efficient and better at zoom. The 17 Pro Max is about Rs 37,000 cheaper and remains superb. If you keep phones for five years or more, the newer one gives you an extra year. If not, look for a sealed 17 Pro Max. The full prices by storage are in <a href=\"/news/iphone-18-pro-pro-max-iphone-duo-nepal-price\">this article</a>.</p>\n<h2>Why iPhones cost what they do here</h2>\n<p>The American price excludes tax. Then come freight, duty, VAT and the reseller\'s margin. A phone at $799 ends up near Rs 1,65,000. It is why cousins abroad are so popular in September. Bringing one for personal use is possible within customs rules, and it must be registered in MDMS. Consider the warranty and SIM type before asking for the favour.</p>\n<h2>Regions and their quirks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>United States:</strong> eSIM only. Some carriers sell locked phones.</li>\n<li><strong>China and Hong Kong:</strong> two physical SIMs. Some features differ.</li>\n<li><strong>Japan and Korea:</strong> camera shutter sound.</li>\n<li><strong>India and Dubai:</strong> close to the version sold here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The letters at the end of the model number in Settings, General, About tell you the region. An unlocked phone shows \"No SIM restrictions\" on the same page.</p>\n<h2>Official sellers</h2>\n<p>Apple has authorised resellers and service providers in Nepal. They give a VAT bill, a genuine unit and a route for repairs. Their prices are similar, so compare bank offers, exchange value and waiting time. Elsewhere you can find lower prices on units with a shop warranty. Decide knowingly.</p>\n<h2>How to check an iPhone before paying</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Enter the serial number at checkcoverage.apple.com. Look at the model and the coverage date.</li>\n<li>Dial *#06# and check the IMEI in MDMS.</li>\n<li>In General, About, look for \"Unknown Part\" notices.</li>\n<li>On 15 and newer, read the battery cycle count and first use.</li>\n<li>Test Face ID, True Tone, all cameras, both speakers and charging.</li>\n<li>Make sure that no Apple account is signed in.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Storage: how much?</h2>\n<p>128GB, on older models, is tight. 256GB is right for most. Videographers should take 512GB or record to an external SSD over USB-C on Pro models. iCloud at 50GB or 200GB costs little and protects photos.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Battery life is good on the 17, better on the Pro Max, average on the Air. Use a USB-C PD charger of 20W or more, and 40W or above for the newer Pros. The 80 per cent limit in Settings slows ageing. After three years a genuine battery restores an iPhone better than any new feature would.</p>\n<h2>Buying used in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Used iPhones are a large market, with large risks. Prices depend on battery health, storage, SIM type and whether the phone has been opened. A 15 Pro in good condition is a lovely purchase. Avoid phones with non-genuine screens, missing Face ID or any account lock. If the seller says \"it only needs an update\", leave. Ask for the bill and a copy of identification.</p>\n<h2>Repairs</h2>\n<p>Screen and back glass repairs are expensive, and Face ID is tied to the board. Third-party screens reduce brightness and may trigger warnings. Use a case and a glass, and turn on Stolen Device Protection.</p>\n<h2>EMI</h2>\n<p>At zero interest over twelve months, an iPhone 17 is about Rs 13,750 a month. Add the bank\'s fee. If the cash price is lower than the EMI price, the difference is the true interest. Longer plans cost more in total. Our product pages show the monthly arithmetic.</p>\n<h2>iPhone or Android?</h2>\n<p>Choose iPhone for video, app quality, privacy controls, resale, and family on FaceTime and iMessage. Choose Android for battery, fast charging, zoom, price and freedom. Both are secure when updated. At Rs 1.6 lakh, Android offers more hardware, and the iPhone will be worth more in three years.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Prices of older models soften after September and again when spring models arrive. The <a href=\"/news/iphone-18-iphone-18e-spring-2027-what-we-know\">iPhone 18 and 18e</a> are expected around spring 2027, and a discounted 17 may be the best purchase then. For Pro buyers, October to December is the calm season, with stock available and bank offers about.</p>\n<h2>Accessories worth having</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A 20 to 40W USB-C charger and a good cable.</li>\n<li>A MagSafe case with raised edges.</li>\n<li>A tempered glass from a known brand.</li>\n<li>A 256GB USB-C flash drive or SSD for moving video.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest:</strong> 17e.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for most:</strong> 17.</li>\n<li><strong>Value flagship:</strong> 17 Pro Max on offer.</li>\n<li><strong>Newest compact flagship:</strong> 18 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Best iPhone:</strong> <a href=\"/reviews/iphone-18-pro-max-review-nepal\">18 Pro Max</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Apple Intelligence</h2>\n<p>If you care about Apple\'s AI features, choose a 16 or newer, or a 15 Pro. <a href=\"/news/apple-intelligence-nepal-iphone-users-guide\">This guide</a> explains what works in Nepal and how to switch it on.</p>','gadgets/2026/apple-iphone-17-pro-max.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(46,20,'Apple Tablet Price in Nepal (November 2026)','apple-tablet-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Choosing an iPad comes down to what you\'ll do with it: watching and note-taking, schoolwork with a keyboard, or professional art and video. Nepal prices run from Rs 86,000 for the <a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">iPad 11</a> to Rs 3,27,000 for the <a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-13-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 13 (M5)</a>.</p>\n<h2>Apple tablets price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">iPad 11 (2025)</a></td><td>6GB - 128GB - WiFi only</td><td>Rs 86,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB - 256GB - WiFi only</td><td>Rs 1,05,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-11-m4-2026\">iPad Air 11 M4 (2026)</a></td><td>8GB - 128GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 1,39,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB - 256GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 1,59,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB - 128GB - WiFi + 5G</td><td>Rs 1,69,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB - 256GB - WiFi + 5G</td><td>Rs 1,89,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-13-m4-2026\">iPad Air 13 M4 (2026)</a></td><td>8GB - 128GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 1,79,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB - 256GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 1,99,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-11-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 11 M5 (2025)</a></td><td>12GB - 256GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 2,29,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB - 512GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 2,67,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB - 1TB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 3,45,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-13-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 13 M5 (2025)</a></td><td>12GB - 512GB - WiFi Only</td><td>Rs 3,27,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our recommendations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For most families and students:</strong> the iPad 11 with the A16 chip. It handles video, reading, notes with an Apple Pencil (USB-C), and the occasional game without trouble.</li>\n<li><strong>If it will replace a laptop:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-11-m4-2026\">iPad Air 11 (M4)</a> from Rs 1,39,000, or the <a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-13-m4-2026\">13-inch Air</a> for more screen. The M4 chip and Apple Intelligence make it a capable work device with a keyboard.</li>\n<li><strong>For artists and video editors:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-11-m5-2025\">iPad Pro (M5)</a>. Its OLED screen is the best on any tablet, but it\'s more than most people need.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember to add the cost of a keyboard and pencil when comparing. Our <a href=\"/guides/laptop-vs-tablet-work-study-nepal\">laptop or tablet guide</a> can help if you\'re unsure.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>iPads compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">Apple iPad (A16)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;83,499</td><td>11-inch Liquid Retina IPS display, 2360 × 164...</td><td>Apple A16 chip (5-core CPU, 4-core GPU, 16-co...</td><td>6 GB LPDDR5</td><td>non-removable; 20W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-11-m4-2026\">Apple iPad Air 11-inch (M4)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>LED backlit Multi‑Touch display with IPS tech...</td><td>Apple M4 chip 8-core CPU with 3 performance c...</td><td>12GB Unified Memory</td><td>Li-Po 28.93 Wh, non-removable; 20W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-air-13-m4-2026\">iPad Air 13 M4 (2026)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,79,000</td><td>13 inches Liquid Retina IPS LCD, 60Hz, 600 ni...</td><td>Apple M4</td><td>8GB</td><td>Li-Po 28.93 Wh, non-removable; 20W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-11-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 11 M5 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,29,500</td><td>11 inches Ultra Retina Tandem OLED, 120Hz, 10...</td><td>Apple M5</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>Li-Po 8160 mAh, non-removable; 60W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-ipad-pro-13-m5-2025\">iPad Pro 13 M5 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,27,000</td><td>13 inches Ultra Retina Tandem OLED, 120Hz, 10...</td><td>Apple M5</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>Li-Po 10290 mAh, non-removable; 60W Fast Char...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why iPads outlast everything else</h2>\n<p>An iPad bought in 2018 still opens, still updates most apps and still sells for a third of its price. No Android tablet in Nepal can say that. The reasons are long software support, chips that are far more powerful than tablets need, and apps made for a large screen. If you plan to keep a tablet for five years or more, an iPad is often the cheaper choice, though the sticker says otherwise.</p>\n<h2>iPad 11: enough for most people</h2>\n<p>At about Rs 83,500 the basic <a href=\"/products/ipad-11-2025\">iPad</a> has the A16 chip, an 11-inch screen and 128GB. It handles notes, films, drawing lessons, video calls and games. It lacks Apple Intelligence, the laminated display and the better pencil. For a school or college student who will read, annotate and watch, it is the right one, and spending more should be a deliberate choice.</p>\n<h2>iPad Air: for people who make things</h2>\n<p>The Air at about Rs 1,40,000 adds the M4, more memory, a laminated screen that removes the gap under the glass, and the Apple Pencil Pro with squeeze and hover. Illustrators, architecture students and video editors feel these every day. The 13-inch Air is for people who want a whole A4 page on the screen. If you only take notes, you will not notice most of it.</p>\n<h2>iPad Pro: for professionals</h2>\n<p>Tandem OLED, 120Hz, M5 and a thinness that surprises. At Rs 2,29,500 and above before accessories, it competes with a MacBook Air plus a basic iPad. Choose it if you are paid to draw, grade colour or edit on location.</p>\n<h2>The accessories question</h2>\n<p>Prices of Apple\'s accessories change the whole calculation. There are three pencils, and each works with certain iPads only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Apple Pencil (USB-C):</strong> cheapest, no pressure sensitivity. Good for notes.</li>\n<li><strong>Apple Pencil Pro:</strong> pressure, squeeze, hover. For Air and Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Older pencils:</strong> for older iPads. Check compatibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Third-party pencils at Rs 2,500 to 5,000 work well for writing, with palm rejection and no pressure. For lecture notes they are the sensible purchase. Keyboards from other makers at Rs 5,000 to 10,000 do most of what the Magic Keyboard does.</p>\n<h2>Will it replace a laptop?</h2>\n<p>For email, documents, presentations and browsing, with a keyboard, yes. For Excel beyond the basics, programming, accounting software and many government portals, no. An Air with Apple\'s keyboard and pencil costs more than a MacBook Neo and nearly as much as a good Windows laptop. Read <a href=\"/guides/laptop-vs-tablet-work-study-nepal\">laptop or tablet</a> before deciding.</p>\n<h2>Storage</h2>\n<p>128GB is comfortable for study. Procreate files, 4K video and offline films fill it. There is no card slot, but USB-C drives work in the Files app. Choose 256GB if you edit video or if children share it.</p>\n<h2>Wi-Fi or cellular?</h2>\n<p>Cellular versions cost about Rs 20,000 to 30,000 more. A phone hotspot does the same job for most. Cellular iPads also have GPS and need MDMS registration. Recent models use eSIM only, so confirm with your operator.</p>\n<h2>Buying official or imported</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s limited warranty on iPads is usually honoured through authorised service in many countries, and cellular models are the exception to check. Enter the serial number on Apple\'s coverage page. American, Emirati and Indian units are all common in Nepal. Screen replacement is very costly, so a case is essential.</p>\n<h2>Used iPads</h2>\n<p>A used iPad Air M1 at Rs 60,000 to 75,000 or an iPad 9 or 10 at Rs 30,000 to 50,000 is a good buy. Check:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activation Lock is off. Erase it and set it up yourself.</li>\n<li>No remote management from a school or company.</li>\n<li>Touch works in every corner. Drag an icon along all edges.</li>\n<li>The pencil connects and charges.</li>\n<li>Battery lasts as the seller claims. Watch video for twenty minutes.</li>\n<li>The frame is straight. Lay it on a table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>For children</h2>\n<p>Use Screen Time with a passcode, Guided Access to lock a child into one app, and Ask to Buy for purchases. Buy a thick foam case. A base iPad, used or new, outlives several cheap tablets and is safer on the internet, because you control which apps are allowed.</p>\n<h2>For artists</h2>\n<p>Procreate is a single purchase and remains the best reason to own an iPad. Start with the base iPad and the cheapest compatible pencil that has pressure sensitivity, if your model supports one, or a used Air with the second-generation Pencil. A matte protector helps the hand and softens the picture.</p>\n<h2>For students of medicine and science</h2>\n<p>GoodNotes, Notability and Apple\'s own Notes app with handwriting search change how revision works. Split view with a PDF on one side and notes on the other is the best study tool we know. The base iPad is enough. Buy the 13-inch Air only if you read two-column papers all day.</p>\n<h2>iPadOS and updates</h2>\n<p>Expect five to seven years of new versions and some years of patches after that. Newer iPadOS versions have proper windows on most recent models. Features tied to Apple Intelligence need an M-series chip or newer A-series with enough memory.</p>\n<h2>Battery and care</h2>\n<p>About ten hours of use is typical. Keep it between 20 and 80 per cent when convenient, and set the 80 per cent limit on newer models if it is always on a stand. Do not leave it in a hot car. Clean the screen with a damp microfibre cloth.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>New iPads appear irregularly, often in spring and autumn. In 2026 memory costs pushed prices up, so clearance is modest. Look for older stock of the previous Air, and ask about education prices at authorised resellers.</p>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most people and students:</strong> iPad 11, 128GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Artists and architects:</strong> iPad Air 11.</li>\n<li><strong>Large documents and music scores:</strong> iPad Air 13.</li>\n<li><strong>Professionals:</strong> iPad Pro 11.</li>\n<li><strong>Tight budget:</strong> used iPad 10 or Air M1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Android alternatives</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a> includes a pen and costs less. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a> has a smoother screen for half the price. They are good tablets with shorter lives and fewer large-screen apps. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a> compares them all.</p>','gadgets/2026/apple-ipad-pro-13-m5-2025.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(47,20,'Blackview Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','blackview-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Blackview is best known for <strong>rugged phones</strong>: thick, drop-resistant handsets with huge batteries, popular with field workers, drivers and trekkers. Its Nepal range runs from the Rs 10,999 <a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-2c\">Wave 2C</a> to the Rs 39,999 <a href=\"/products/blackview-rock-1-pro\">Rock 1 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Blackview phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-2c\">Blackview Wave 2C</a></td><td>3GB/32GB</td><td>Rs 10,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-10c\">Blackview Wave 10C</a></td><td>4GB/32GB</td><td>Rs 14,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 17,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-7c\">Blackview Wave 7C</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 18,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-bv4800-se\">Blackview BV4800 SE</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 20,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-100\">Blackview Fort 100</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-rock-1-pro\">Blackview Rock 1 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Who should buy one</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>If you drop phones or work outdoors:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Fort 1</a> (Rs 21,999) combines IP68 water and dust protection with a 10,000mAh battery that lasts several days.</li>\n<li><strong>The Wave series</strong> is Blackview\'s cheap everyday line: basic phones for calls and messaging, not rugged ones.</li>\n<li><strong>The Rock 1 Pro</strong> is the most capable model, with 8GB of RAM and 256GB of storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rugged phones are heavier and the cameras are ordinary, so if you don\'t need the toughness, a regular phone gives you more for the money. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">phones under Rs 25,000</a> guide covers the alternatives.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Rugged and simple: the models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-2c\">Blackview Wave 2C</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;10,999</td><td>5 inches TFT LCD, 60Hz, 350 nits (typ), 400 n...</td><td>Unisoc SC9863A1 (28nm)</td><td>3GB LPDDR3</td><td>Li-Po 3000 mAh, non-removable; 5W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-10c\">Blackview Wave 10C</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,499</td><td>6.88 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz, 430 nits</td><td>Unisoc T310 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 10W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-7c\">Blackview Wave 7C</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,999</td><td>6.56 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz, 430 (typ)</td><td>Unisoc Tiger T310 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 10W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-bv4800-se\">Blackview BV4800 SE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;20,999</td><td>6.56 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz</td><td>Unisoc Tiger T615 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 5100 mAh, non-removable; 10W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>6.56 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 400 nits (typ) / 4...</td><td>Unisoc T615 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 10000 mAh, non-removable; 18W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-100\">Blackview Fort 100</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>6.56 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 450 nits</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>6GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging;...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-rock-1-pro\">Blackview Rock 1 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>6.56 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 450 nits</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>15000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Two brands in one</h2>\n<p>Blackview makes two very different kinds of phone. The <strong>Wave</strong> series is made of ordinary cheap smartphones. The <strong>BV, Fort and Rock</strong> series is rugged, with rubber corners, sealed ports, loud speakers and big batteries. The first has many better rivals. The second has almost none in Nepal, and is the reason to read further.</p>\n<h2>What rugged means</h2>\n<p>A rugged phone is built to be dropped on concrete, rinsed under a tap and used with wet or gloved hands. It usually has IP68 and IP69K ratings, a frame that stands above the glass, and covers over the ports. Makers also quote MIL-STD-810H, a list of American military test methods for shock, heat, cold and vibration. The maker chooses which tests to perform, so it is a good sign and not a certificate of immortality.</p>\n<p>The price is size and weight. These phones weigh 270 to 500 grams and are 13 to 24mm thick. They go in a jacket or on a belt.</p>\n<h2>Fort 1: the value choice</h2>\n<p>For Rs 21,999 the <a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Fort 1</a> has a 10,000mAh battery, IP68 and a simple chip. Two or three days between charges is normal. The screen is small and dim by modern standards, the camera is for documentation, and 18W charging means an overnight fill. For a site supervisor, trekking porter, farmer or driver, it does exactly what is needed.</p>\n<h2>Fort 100 and BV4800 SE</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-100\">Fort 100</a> at Rs 25,999 is thinner, with 6GB and a normal battery. It is for people who want toughness without the brick. The <a href=\"/products/blackview-bv4800-se\">BV4800 SE</a> at Rs 20,999 is the entry rugged phone. Between the three, the Fort 1\'s battery makes it the most useful.</p>\n<h2>Rock 1 Pro: the power station</h2>\n<p>With 15,000mAh, 8GB and 33W charging at Rs 39,999, the <a href=\"/products/blackview-rock-1-pro\">Rock 1 Pro</a> is as much a power bank as a phone. Expect close to a week of light use. It is heavy. For expedition leaders, field scientists and people posted where there is no supply, it can replace a phone and two power banks.</p>\n<h2>Wave 2C, 10C, 7C</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-2c\">Wave 2C</a> at Rs 10,999 is a tiny 3G-era phone in spirit, with a 5-inch screen and a chip from another decade. It works for calls and little else. The 10C and 7C are large and slow. At Rs 14,000 to 17,000, the Tecno Spark Go 2 and Infinix Smart 10 Plus are better in every way that matters. We would not buy a Wave.</p>\n<h2>Who really needs a rugged phone?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Construction, hydropower and road workers.</li>\n<li>Trekking guides, porters, rafting and climbing crews.</li>\n<li>Electricians, plumbers, mechanics.</li>\n<li>Farmers and livestock keepers.</li>\n<li>Delivery riders in the monsoon.</li>\n<li>Security staff and police.</li>\n<li>Anyone on a third screen replacement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A normal phone in a tough case: the alternative</h2>\n<p>For many people, an IP68 phone in a thick case is better. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> at Rs 54,999 has drop protection, a bright screen, a fast chip and 8,300mAh. The <a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">Realme C85 Pro</a> at Rs 36,999 has high water ratings and an AMOLED. They are phones you can also enjoy. Choose Blackview when budget is tight, abuse is constant or battery must last for days.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>All of these use basic chips: Unisoc T310 to T615, or Helio G81. Calls, WhatsApp, maps, forms and photos are fine. Heavy social apps are slow on 4GB. Games are out. If the phone is for work software such as survey or delivery apps, test the app before buying several.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Good enough for site photos, meter readings and delivery proof. Colours are flat and nights are poor. Some rugged models elsewhere add thermal or night-vision cameras. Those are expensive and rare here.</p>\n<h2>Screens and gloves</h2>\n<p>At 400 to 480 nits, the displays are hard to read in direct sun. Use a cap or shade and the high contrast theme. Glove mode helps in winter. Physical buttons for push-to-talk or torch on some models are a pleasure in the cold.</p>\n<h2>Software and security</h2>\n<p>DokeOS is light, based on Android 15 or 16. Updates are few. Use the phone for work and not as the only device for banking. Keep Play Protect on, install apps from the Play Store only, and set a screen lock.</p>\n<h2>Network and MDMS</h2>\n<p>Check that VoLTE works with your SIM, and that the IMEI is registered. Some rugged phones arrive through small importers. The routine is in <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">our guide</a>. Ask which 4G bands are supported if you work in remote areas, where band 20 or 8 may be the only signal.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>There are few points. The good news is that rugged phones break less. The bad news is that when they do, parts are scarce and the body is glued. Ask for a one-year warranty and what it excludes. Water damage is excluded even on waterproof phones unless the maker says otherwise.</p>\n<h2>Care that keeps the rating</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Close the port covers firmly. A grain of sand defeats them.</li>\n<li>Rinse in fresh water after mud, and dry before charging.</li>\n<li>Do not take it into hot springs or the sea.</li>\n<li>Check the seals once a year.</li>\n<li>After a hard fall, assume that the water rating is gone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>For companies</h2>\n<p>If you equip a team, buy one first and test it for a month. Ask the distributor for a bulk price, spare batteries or covers and a replacement promise. Label phones, enable Find My Device, and use a management app to limit installs.</p>\n<h2>Tablets too</h2>\n<p>Blackview sells rugged and ordinary tablets. Our <a href=\"/guides/blackview-tablet-price-in-nepal\">Blackview tablet page</a> covers them. For field forms, a rugged tablet is often better than a phone.</p>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value rugged phone:</strong> Fort 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Extreme endurance:</strong> Rock 1 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Thinner and tough:</strong> Fort 100.</li>\n<li><strong>Skip:</strong> the Wave series.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Final word</h2>\n<p>Blackview\'s rugged phones are tools, like a good hammer. Nobody buys a hammer for its camera. If your work destroys phones, Rs 21,999 spent on a Fort 1 will save you more than any premium handset. If your phone lives in a handbag, choose something prettier.</p>','gadgets/2026/blackview-fort-1.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(48,20,'Blackview Tablet Price in Nepal (November 2026)','blackview-tablet-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Blackview sells a very large range of budget Android tablets in Nepal, from kids\' tablets around Rs 12,000 to the Rs 59,999 <a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-12-5g\">MEGA 12 5G</a>. The names are confusing (Tab, Zeno, Link, Mega, Active), so here\'s how to make sense of them.</p>\n<h2>Blackview tablets price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-link-mini-kids-wifi\">Blackview Link Mini Kids WiFi</a></td><td>3GB/32GB</td><td>Rs 11,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-50-wifi\">Blackview Tab 50 WiFi</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 12,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-70-wifi\">Blackview Tab 70 Wi-Fi</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 13,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-link-1-kids-wifi\">Blackview Link 1 Kids WiFi</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 13,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-zeno-1\">Blackview Zeno 1</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 15,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 17,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-50-kids\">Blackview Tab 50 Kids</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-link-2-wifi\">Blackview Link 2 WiFi</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-20-wifi\">Blackview Tab 20 WiFi</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-20-kids-wifi\">Blackview Tab 20 Kids WiFi</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 17,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-60-wifi\">Blackview Tab 60 WiFi</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 18,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-zeno-2\">Blackview Zeno 2</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-a6-kids\">Blackview Tab A6 Kids</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-60\">Blackview Tab 60</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 20,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-80\">Blackview Tab 80</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 22,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-90\">Blackview Tab 90</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 22,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-9-wifi\">Blackview Tab 9 WiFi</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 23,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-link-5\">Blackview LINK 5</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-active-5\">Blackview Active 5</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-13-pro\">Blackview Tab 13 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 26,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-60-pro\">Blackview Tab 60 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 27,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-16-pro\">Blackview Tab 16 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 27,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-zeno-5\">Blackview Zeno 5</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-2-wifi\">Blackview Mega 2 WiFi</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 31,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-zeno-10-5g\">Blackview Zeno 10 5G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 31,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-active-7\">Blackview Active 7</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-mega-2\">Blackview Tab MEGA 2</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 37,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-18\">Blackview Tab 18</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 37,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-8\">Blackview MEGA 8</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 39,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-link-8-wifi\">Blackview Link 8 WiFi</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-5\">Blackview MEGA 5</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 51,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-12-5g\">Blackview MEGA 12 5G</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>How to choose</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For children:</strong> the \"Kids\" models, such as the <a href=\"/products/blackview-link-mini-kids-wifi\">Link Mini Kids</a>, come with protective cases and parental controls. They\'re fine for cartoons and learning apps.</li>\n<li><strong>For video and reading at home:</strong> the mid-range Tab and Zeno models with 8GB of RAM, around Rs 23,000–30,000, are the sweet spot.</li>\n<li><strong>For a bigger screen and battery:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/blackview-link-8-wifi\">Link 8</a> (Rs 39,999) has 12GB of RAM and an 8,400mAh battery.</li>\n<li><strong>For rough use:</strong> the Active models are rugged, and the MEGA 12 5G adds IP68 and a 10,000mAh battery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before buying a Blackview over Rs 25,000, compare it with the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2</a> and other picks in our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>. Mainstream brands often have better screens and longer software support at similar prices.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Representative models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-link-1-kids-wifi\">Blackview Link 1 Kids WiFi</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;13,999</td><td>8.68 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz, 300 nits(typ)</td><td>Rockchip RK3562 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 10W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-50-kids\">Blackview Tab 50 Kids</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,499</td><td>8 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz, 320 nits (typ)</td><td>Rockchip RK3562 (12nm)</td><td>3GB</td><td>Li-Ion 5580 mAh, non-removable; 5W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-90\">Blackview Tab 90</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;22,999</td><td>10.92 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz</td><td>Unisoc T606 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 8200 mAh, non removable; 10W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-active-5\">Blackview Active 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>8.68 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 300 nits(typ)</td><td>Unisoc T615 (12nm)</td><td>6GB</td><td>Li-Po 6600 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-60-pro\">Blackview Tab 60 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;27,999</td><td>10.1 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz</td><td>Unisoc T606 (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 7700 mAh, non removable; 10W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-2-wifi\">Blackview Mega 2 WiFi</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;31,999</td><td>12 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz, 300 nits(typ)</td><td>Unisoc T615 (12nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Li-Po 9000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-8\">Blackview MEGA 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,000</td><td>13 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz</td><td>Unisoc Tiger T620 (12nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Li-Ion 11000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-12-5g\">Blackview MEGA 12 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>12.2 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 350 nits (typ)</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7200 (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>10000 mAh, non-removable; 55W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Thirty tablets and one question</h2>\n<p>Blackview has more tablets on sale here than any other brand, with names that differ by a digit or the word Kids. Most use a small set of basic chips and LCD screens at 60Hz. The question to ask of each is simple: what is it better at than a Redmi Pad 2 or Galaxy Tab A11 at the same price? For the children\'s, rugged and very large models there is an answer. For the rest there usually is not.</p>\n<h2>Kids tablets: when they make sense</h2>\n<p>From Rs 12,000 the Kids models come with a foam case, a handle and a child interface already installed. The hardware is slow, with Rockchip or Allwinner processors, 3 to 4GB and dim screens. For a child of three to seven who watches downloaded cartoons and plays simple learning games, that is enough, and a fall costs you less. For a school-age child who needs Zoom, a browser and YouTube to work smoothly, they are frustrating. A Galaxy Tab A11 in a Rs 1,000 foam case is a better investment.</p>\n<p>If you do buy one, choose 4GB over 3GB, an 8-inch or larger screen, and a model with a recent Android version. Set up Google Family Link as well, as the built-in child mode is not a substitute.</p>\n<h2>Standard tablets</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-90\">Tab 90</a> and <a href=\"/products/blackview-tab-60-pro\">Tab 60 Pro</a> at Rs 23,000 to 28,000 use the Unisoc T606. They have LTE in many versions, which is the argument in their favour: a tablet with a SIM slot and calls at this price is rare. For a salesperson or a family without fibre, that matters. For anyone with Wi-Fi, the Honor Pad X9a and Redmi Pad 2 have better screens.</p>\n<h2>Active: rugged tablets</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/blackview-active-5\">Active 5</a> and its relatives are built for work sites, with sealed bodies and thick edges. Surveyors, engineers, warehouse staff and clinics in the field are the buyers. Ordinary tablets in a rugged case are an alternative, though a sealed port and a hand strap are worth a lot in mud. Check the IP rating, the brightness outdoors and whether your company\'s app runs on 6GB and a T615.</p>\n<h2>Mega: very large screens</h2>\n<p>The Mega series offers 12 to 13 inches from about Rs 32,000. The <a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-8\">Mega 8</a> at Rs 39,000 has a 13-inch screen, 12GB and an 11,000mAh battery. As a kitchen television, a music stand for scores, a sign in a shop or a screen for elderly eyes, size beats everything. The chips are basic, and the resolution is modest for the size. Do not buy it as a laptop.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/blackview-mega-12-5g\">Mega 12 5G</a> at Rs 59,999 is the exception: a Dimensity 7200, 120Hz and 55W charging. It is a proper tablet. At that price the Xiaomi Pad 8 is faster and better supported, without the larger screen or 5G.</p>\n<h2>Real and claimed memory</h2>\n<p>Boxes say 24GB or 36GB of RAM. That is real memory plus storage borrowed as memory. Count only the real part. Look at the specification on our product pages, or in Settings, About tablet. Storage is usually eMMC, so apps open slowly.</p>\n<h2>Screens</h2>\n<p>Many are 1280 x 800 on 10-inch panels, with brightness around 320 to 400 nits. Text is a little soft. Full HD+ models such as the Tab 90 are better. Look at a newspaper PDF in Devanagari before buying.</p>\n<h2>Streaming quality</h2>\n<p>Widevine level varies. Some models are L1, many are L3, which limits Netflix to about 480p. YouTube is unaffected. Ask the seller to open DRM Info.</p>\n<h2>Software and updates</h2>\n<p>DokeOS is simple, based on recent Android. Expect few or no version updates. Do not use such a tablet for banking. Keep apps updated, and use it on a guest Wi-Fi network if it belongs to a child.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Capacities are decent, from 5,000 to 11,000mAh. Charging at 10 to 18W is slow: three to six hours. Leave it overnight. Kids tablets come back empty every evening, so fix a place and time for charging.</p>\n<h2>Accessories</h2>\n<p>Some bundles include a keyboard, pen, case and earphones. The keyboards are small, and the pens are capacitive. Treat them as free extras and not as reasons to buy.</p>\n<h2>Service and warranty</h2>\n<p>Ask the dealer for the distributor and the service address. Tablets of this kind are cheaper to replace than repair, so the replacement policy in the first months matters most. Cellular models must be registered in MDMS. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">Here is how to check</a>.</p>\n<h2>Best uses by person</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toddler:</strong> Link 1 Kids or Tab 50 Kids.</li>\n<li><strong>Field worker:</strong> Active 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Shop sign or kitchen screen:</strong> Mega 2 or Mega 8.</li>\n<li><strong>Salesperson who needs a SIM:</strong> Tab 60 Pro LTE.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Better choices for common needs</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>School student:</strong> <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reading and films:</strong> <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x9a\">Honor Pad X9a</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe child tablet:</strong> <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Galaxy Tab A11</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Notes by hand:</strong> <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Testing in the shop</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open three apps and switch between them.</li>\n<li>Play a YouTube video at 1080p.</li>\n<li>Turn brightness to maximum and walk to the door.</li>\n<li>Join a test call with the front camera.</li>\n<li>Check memory, storage and Android version in Settings.</li>\n<li>Press every edge of the screen.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Bargaining</h2>\n<p>Margins are comfortable. A tablet marked Rs 22,999 can often be had for Rs 20,000 with a case. Compare with online listings of the exact model name and memory.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Blackview\'s tablets are like a hardware shop: full of things you do not need, and one or two that solve your exact problem. If that problem is a toddler, a building site or a very large cheap screen, you are in the right place. If it is study, reading or work, see <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-30000-nepal\">tablets under Rs 30,000</a> first.</p>','gadgets/2026/blackview-mega-12-5g.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(49,20,'Dell Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','dell-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Dell\'s Nepal range covers everything from basic office laptops to Alienware gaming machines. The renamed line-up takes a moment to learn: plain <strong>Dell 14/15/16</strong> models are everyday laptops, <strong>Dell Pro</strong> is the business range, <strong>XPS</strong> is the premium one, and <strong>Alienware</strong> is for gaming. Prices run from about Rs 59,000 to Rs 4,94,499.</p>\n<h2>Dell laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-wyse-5470-2022-celeron-n4100\">Dell Wyse 5470 2022 (Intel Celeron N4100)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 58,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-2025\">Dell 15 DC15250 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core 3 100U, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 78,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel i5-1334U, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,08,205</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-15-pv15255-2025\">Dell Pro 15 PV15255 2025 (AMD Ryzen 5 7520U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 85,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-16-dc16251-2025-intel-core-5-120u\">Dell 16 DC16251 2025 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 94,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-14-plus-pc14250-2025-amd-ryzen-5-pro-230-16gb-512gb-14-fhd\">Dell Pro 14 Plus PC14250 2025 (AMD Ryzen 5 Pro 230)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-14-pc14250-2025\">Dell Pro 14 PC14250 2025 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,22,550</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026-intel-core-ultra-5-320\">Dell XPS 13 DX13260 2026 (Intel Core Ultra 5 320)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-14-dc14255-2025-ryzen-ai-5-330\">Dell 14 DC14255 2025 (Ryzen AI 5 330)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-5441-2024-snapdragon-x-plus-x1p-42-100\">Dell Inspiron 14 5441 2024 (Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,31,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026\">Dell XPS 13 (2026)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-16-dc16255-2025-ryzen-7-250-16gb-1tb-16-fhd\">Dell 16 DC16255 2025 (Ryzen 7 250)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-16-plus-pb16250-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-235u-16gb-256gb-16-fhd\">Dell Pro 16 Plus PB16250 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core 5 120U, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 235U, 16GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-14-plus-2-in-1-db04255-2025-ryzen-ai-7-350-16gb-1tb-14-fhd\">Dell 14 Plus 2-in-1 DB04255 2025 (Ryzen AI 7 350)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-14-plus-2-in-1-db04250-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-1tb-14-fhd\">Dell 14 Plus 2-in-1 DB04250 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 256V)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-plus-7440-intel-core-ultra-9-185h\">Dell Inspiron 14 Plus 7440</a></td><td>Intel Core 7 155H, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 185H, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-14-plus-pb14250-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-235u-16gb-512gb-14-fhd\">Dell Pro 14 Plus PB14250 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 235U, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,88,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 255U, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-alienware-15-da15260-2026-intel-core-7-240h-rtx-4050\">Dell Alienware 15 DA15260 2026 (Intel Core 7 240H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9345-2024-snapdragon-x-plus-16gb-256gb-134-qhd-touch\">Dell XPS 13 9345 2024 (Snapdragon X Plus)</a></td><td>16GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-13-pb13255-2025-ryzen-ai-7-pro-350\">Dell Pro 13 PB13255 2025 (Ryzen AI 7 Pro 350)</a></td><td>32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,09,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-14-plus-db14250-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v-32gb-1tb-14-wqxga-oled-90hz\">Dell 14 Plus DB14250 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9350-2024-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-512gb-134-fhd\">Dell XPS 13 9350 2024</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 256V, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 258V, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,98,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-14-premium-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-255h\">Dell 14 Premium DA14250 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,80,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-alienware-16x-aurora-2025\">Dell Alienware 16X Aurora (2025)</a></td><td>Core Ultra 9 275HX · RTX 5070, 16GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 4,94,499</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Good places to start</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest sensible Dell:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/dell-pro-15-pv15255-2025\">Dell Pro 15 PV15255</a> (from Rs 85,999) has a Ryzen 5, a 120Hz screen and an SD slot. The 8GB of soldered memory is the main limit.</li>\n<li><strong>Everyday home and study:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-2025\">Dell 15</a> and <a href=\"/products/dell-16-dc16251-2025-intel-core-5-120u\">Dell 16</a> are straightforward choices with Dell\'s service network behind them.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaming:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/dell-alienware-16x-aurora-2025\">Alienware 16X Aurora</a> with an RTX 5070 is the top of the range, and an Alienware with an RTX 5060 is also sold through ONIN Infosys.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Skip the Wyse 5470 at the bottom of the list unless you specifically need a thin-client office terminal. It\'s an older Celeron machine. For business laptops with long warranties, compare the Dell Pro models with ThinkPads in our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-150000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1,50,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Dell models compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-2025\">Dell 15 DC15250 2025</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;78,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core 3 100U (Intel 7), 6 cores / 8 thre...</td><td>8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 41 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-core-i5-1334u\">Dell 15 DC15250 (Core i5-1334U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;96,499</td><td>15.6&quot;, Non-Touch, FHD, 120Hz, WVA, IPS, Anti-...</td><td>13th Generation Intel® Core™ i5-1334U (10 cor...</td><td>8 GB, 1 x 8 GB, DDR4, 2666 MT/s</td><td>3-Cell Battery, 41WHr (Integrated)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-14-pc14250-2025\">Dell Pro 14 PC14250 (Core 5 120U, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,27,499</td><td>14&quot; FHD+ (1920x1200), 300 nits, anti-glare, n...</td><td>Intel® Core™ 5 120U (10 cores, up to 5.0 GHz)</td><td>16GB (2x8GB) DDR5</td><td>3-cell, 45 Wh, ExpressCharge™ Capable, Expres...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-x360-intel-core-5-120u\">Dell Inspiron 14 2-in-1 7440 (Core 5 120U, 16GB/1TB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,500</td><td>14.0-inch FHD+ (1920x1200) Anti-Glare Touch 3...</td><td>Intel Core 5 120U, 10 Cores, 12 Threads, 12MB...</td><td>16GB DDR5, up to 5600 MT/s</td><td>4-Cell Battery, 54Whr (Integrated)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-plus-7440-intel-core-ultra-9-185h\">Dell Inspiron 14 Plus 7440</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,74,999</td><td>14.0 inches, IPS, 2.2K (2240 x 1400 pixels),...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 185H (Intel 4), 16 cores /...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 64 Wh; 100W AC Adapter, Supports Expre...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9350-2024-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-512gb-134-fhd\">Dell XPS 13 9350 2024</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,39,999</td><td>13.4 inches, IPS LCD, FHD+ (1920 x 1200 pixel...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 256V (TSMC N3B), 8 cores /...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 55 Wh; 60W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-alienware-16x-aurora-2025\">Dell Alienware 16X Aurora (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,94,499</td><td>16-inch WQXGA IPS, 240Hz</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX</td><td>16GB</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The new names</h2>\n<p>Dell retired Inspiron, Vostro and Latitude for a simpler system, then brought XPS back. Today, <strong>Dell</strong> followed by a number is for home. <strong>Dell Pro</strong> is for business. <strong>Dell Pro Max</strong> is a workstation. <strong>Plus</strong> and <strong>Premium</strong> mean better versions. Shops still hold Inspirons and Latitudes, so you will see both systems side by side. The names changed, and the questions remain: processor, memory, screen, battery.</p>\n<h2>Why people choose Dell</h2>\n<p>Service. Dell\'s business support in Nepal is among the best, with on-site options for companies, and parts available for years. Offices know it, banks buy it, and used Latitudes keep their value. On consumer models the specification for the price is average: you often find 8GB at Rs 90,000. You are paying for the badge and the counter behind it.</p>\n<h2>Under Rs 80,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-2025\">Dell 15 DC15250</a> with a Core 3 100U, 8GB and a 120Hz screen is a fair home and office machine. Add a stick of memory if the slot is free. The Wyse 5470 with a Celeron, at Rs 58,999, is a thin client and not a laptop for a student. Avoid it.</p>\n<h2>Around one lakh</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-core-i5-1334u\">DC15250 with a Core i5-1334U</a> at Rs 96,499 has a good processor with 8GB in one stick. With another 8GB fitted it becomes a solid laptop at Rs 1,03,000 or so. Compare with the Acer Aspire Lite 15, which comes ready with 16GB. The Dell Pro 15 uses the weaker Ryzen 5 7520U.</p>\n<h2>Rs 1.25 to 1.35 lakh</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/dell-pro-14-pc14250-2025\">Dell Pro 14</a> is the business choice: 16GB in two sticks, a 300 nit 16:10 screen and a proper keyboard. The <a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-x360-intel-core-5-120u\">Inspiron 14 2-in-1</a> folds and accepts a pen. The <a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-2026\">XPS 13 (2026)</a> is beautiful with only 8GB, as <a href=\"/reviews/dell-xps-13-2026-review-nepal\">our review</a> warns. The Snapdragon Inspiron 14 5441 lasts all day with software limits. Our choice here is the Pro 14.</p>\n<h2>Rs 1.75 to 2.4 lakh</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-plus-7440-intel-core-ultra-9-185h\">Inspiron 14 Plus</a> with a sixteen-core Core Ultra 9 and 32GB at about Rs 1,75,000 is powerful for developers. The 14 Plus 2-in-1 with a Ryzen AI 7 is balanced. The <a href=\"/products/dell-xps-13-9350-2024-intel-core-ultra-7-256v-16gb-512gb-134-fhd\">XPS 13 9350</a> with a Core Ultra 7 256V and 16GB is the real XPS: light, efficient and well made.</p>\n<h2>Alienware</h2>\n<p>Alienware 15 starts near Rs 1.9 lakh with an RTX 4050, and the <a href=\"/products/dell-alienware-16x-aurora-2025\">16X Aurora</a> is close to Rs 5 lakh. They cool well and look distinctive. A Lenovo Legion or Asus Strix usually gives more graphics for the same money. Choose Alienware for design and, where official, for Dell\'s service.</p>\n<h2>Used Latitudes: the quiet bargain</h2>\n<p>Thousands of Latitude laptops arrive from foreign offices. For Rs 45,000 to 80,000 you can find a 7000-series machine with a Core i5 or i7, 16GB, a backlit keyboard and a metal body. For writing, office and programming they beat new consumer laptops at the price. Check the battery report, hinges, screen, BIOS password and whether the device is enrolled with a company (a message at first start). Buy from a shop that gives at least three months.</p>\n<h2>Reading the service tag</h2>\n<p>Every Dell has a seven-character tag on the base. Enter it at Dell\'s support site. You will see the original configuration, the country, the ship date and the warranty with its end date. It is the best honesty test in the laptop market. If the seller\'s story does not match the tag, believe the tag.</p>\n<h2>Warranty types</h2>\n<p><strong>Basic:</strong> carry in. <strong>On-site:</strong> a technician visits, after remote diagnosis. <strong>ProSupport:</strong> faster, for business. <strong>Accidental damage:</strong> extra. Ask which you have. Companies should ask for three years on-site, which costs a few per cent more and saves arguments.</p>\n<h2>Screens</h2>\n<p>Dell\'s budget screens are 250 nits with limited colour. Business panels reach 300 to 400. XPS and Premium models are excellent. The 120Hz panels on cheap models are smoother, not more colourful.</p>\n<h2>Keyboards and build</h2>\n<p>Business models have the best keyboards, with spill resistance. Consumer models are plain plastic. XPS keyboards have zero gap between keys, which takes a day to learn.</p>\n<h2>Ports</h2>\n<p>XPS machines have USB-C only. Buy a hub with HDMI and USB-A. Dell Pro machines have everything, including Ethernet on some.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which Dell</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Home and school:</strong> DC15250 with added memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Office:</strong> Dell Pro 14 or 15 with 16GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Developer:</strong> Inspiron 14 Plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Executive:</strong> XPS 13 9350.</li>\n<li><strong>Careful buyer:</strong> a used Latitude 7000.</li>\n<li><strong>Gamer:</strong> Alienware on discount, otherwise another brand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Set-up tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Install Dell Command Update or SupportAssist and update the BIOS.</li>\n<li>Set charging to \"Primarily AC use\" if it lives on a desk.</li>\n<li>Remove trial antivirus.</li>\n<li>Turn on BitLocker or device encryption and save the key.</li>\n<li>Create a recovery drive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Dell or HP, Lenovo, Acer?</h2>\n<p><strong>HP</strong> is the closest rival in service. <strong>Lenovo ThinkPads</strong> have better keyboards. <strong>Acer</strong> gives more hardware. If your company will maintain it, choose Dell or HP. If you will maintain it yourself, choose by memory and price.</p>\n<h2>Bargaining</h2>\n<p>For a single machine, ask for memory at cost. For five or more, ask for a quotation from two dealers with the same configuration, three-year warranty and delivery date. Prices follow the dollar, so quotations expire quickly. The EMI estimate on product pages helps with cash flow.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Dell is a service company that also makes laptops. If you value that, the Dell Pro 14 and the used Latitudes are excellent. If you want the most computer for a fixed sum, look at Acer and Lenovo first, and come back if you need the counter. For a wider view, see <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">best laptops in Nepal</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/dell-14-plus-2-in-1-db04255-2025-ryzen-ai-7-350-16gb-1tb-14-fhd.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(50,20,'Google Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','google-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Google\'s Pixels are sold in Nepal through importers rather than an official Google channel, so prices vary more between shops than for Samsung or Apple. The current range runs from the Rs 86,000 <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10a\">Pixel 10a</a> to the Rs 2,00,000 <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Pixel 10 Pro XL</a>.</p>\n<h2>Google phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10a\">Google Pixel 10a</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 86,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10\">Google Pixel 10</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,28,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro\">Google Pixel 10 Pro</a></td><td>16GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,76,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Google Pixel 10 Pro XL</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,00,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Why people choose a Pixel</h2>\n<p>The camera software, clean Android with fast updates, and Google\'s AI features. All the Pixel 10 models run the Tensor G5 chip and are rated IP68.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10\">Pixel 10</a> (Rs 1,28,000) has 12GB of RAM, 256GB of storage and a 120Hz screen. It\'s the one we\'d pick for most Pixel fans.</li>\n<li><strong>Best camera:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro\">Pixel 10 Pro</a> and Pro XL add a periscope zoom and more RAM.</li>\n<li><strong>The Pixel 10a</strong> is the cheapest way in, but at Rs 86,000 it faces strong competition from Samsung and Xiaomi.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because Pixels are imported, check that the unit is registered and ask who handles warranty repairs. Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">checking a phone is official</a> explains how.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Pixel models in our listing</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10a\">Google Pixel 10a</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;86,000</td><td>6.3 inches OLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HBM), 3000...</td><td>Google Tensor G4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>Li-Po 5100 mAh, non-removable; 30W charger; 1...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10\">Google Pixel 10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,28,000</td><td>6.3 inches OLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HDR), 3000...</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 4970 mAh, non-removable; 30W USB-PD Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro\">Google Pixel 10 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,76,000</td><td>6.3 inches LTPO OLED, 120Hz, 2200 nits (HDR),...</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 4870 mAh, non-removable; 30W USB-PD Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10-pro-xl\">Google Pixel 10 Pro XL</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,00,000</td><td>6.8 inches LTPO OLED, 120Hz, 2200 nits (HDR),...</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Pixels in Nepal: start with the problem</h2>\n<p>Google does not sell Pixel phones officially in Nepal. Every unit here comes through an importer, from India, the Gulf, the United States, Europe or Japan. That affects warranty, network features, MDMS registration and repairs. If you accept it, you get some of the best phones made. If you need a service counter in your city, choose something else and save yourself trouble.</p>\n<h2>Why people still want them</h2>\n<p><strong>The camera.</strong> Pixels take reliable photos with natural skin and controlled highlights. You press the button once, and it is right.</p>\n<p><strong>The software.</strong> Pure Android, the first to receive new versions, with call screening, voice recorder with transcripts, Now Playing and thoughtful details.</p>\n<p><strong>The support.</strong> Seven years of Android and security updates on recent models.</p>\n<p><strong>The size.</strong> At 6.3 inches, the 10a, 10 and 10 Pro suit a hand.</p>\n<h2>The models</h2>\n<p><strong>Pixel 10a, about Rs 86,000.</strong> The sensible one. The Tensor G4, a 5,100mAh battery, a very good main camera, and the same seven years. It lacks the telephoto and some AI features.</p>\n<p><strong>Pixel 10, about Rs 1,28,000.</strong> Adds the G5, 12GB, a 5x telephoto, which used to be reserved for the Pro, and magnetic wireless charging.</p>\n<p><strong>Pixel 10 Pro, about Rs 1,76,000.</strong> Larger sensors, 16GB and a brighter LTPO screen in a small body.</p>\n<p><strong>Pixel 10 Pro XL, about Rs 2,00,000.</strong> The same with a 6.8-inch screen and bigger battery.</p>\n<h2>Which to choose</h2>\n<p>The 10a is the best value if you want a Pixel for photos and updates. The 10 is the best of them overall at this price, thanks to its telephoto. The Pros compete with the Galaxy S26 Ultra, vivo X300 Pro and Xiaomi 17 Ultra, all of which have local warranty. It is hard to spend Rs 1.76 lakh on a phone that no one here officially repairs.</p>\n<h2>Tensor: good and not fast</h2>\n<p>Google\'s chip is designed for speech, photo and AI work. Everyday speed is fine. In long games it runs slower and warmer than Snapdragon rivals. If you play Genshin, a OnePlus 15R at less than the price of a Pixel 10 will please you more.</p>\n<h2>Network: check before you pay</h2>\n<p>Put your own SIM in the phone in the shop and test these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A normal call on 4G. Does it stay on 4G (VoLTE) or fall to an older network?</li>\n<li>Mobile data speed.</li>\n<li>Whether VoLTE can be turned on in SIM settings for your operator.</li>\n<li>eSIM, if it is a United States model without a physical SIM slot. Recent American Pixels may have no tray at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On unsupported carriers, Google may not enable VoLTE by default. Enthusiasts use tools to turn it on, but you should not have to.</p>\n<h2>MDMS</h2>\n<p>As there is no official importer, registration depends on the seller. Dial *#06#, check the IMEI in the system, and do not accept a promise. Our <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">guide</a> shows the steps. An unregistered phone can lose service later.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and repair</h2>\n<p>Google\'s warranty applies in the country of sale. The shop\'s warranty is what you get here. Ask for at least six months in writing, with what is covered. Screens and batteries are available through some independent technicians, at a price and after a wait. There have been battery programmes for certain older A-series Pixels that reduce capacity through updates, so research the model before buying used.</p>\n<h2>Regional differences</h2>\n<p>Japanese and Korean units may have a shutter sound. American ones support certain 5G bands and may be carrier locked. Indian ones are the closest match for our networks. Ask for the model code and look it up on Google\'s specification page.</p>\n<h2>Features that may not work here</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Call Screen, Hold for Me and some Phone app features, depending on region.</li>\n<li>Google Wallet tap to pay.</li>\n<li>Satellite SOS.</li>\n<li>Some Gemini features by language and account.</li>\n<li>Google One extras and Pixel Care.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Photography, transcription in supported languages, Circle to Search, Magic Editor and updates work.</p>\n<h2>Nepali language</h2>\n<p>Gboard supports Nepali typing, romanised input and voice typing well. Google Translate offers Nepali offline. The assistant understands some Nepali. For language support, a Pixel is as good as any phone and better than most.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Battery life is average to good. Charging is slow by local standards, at 23 to 30W, and 45W on the XL. Use a PD charger with PPS. The Pixel 10 series supports Qi2 magnetic charging.</p>\n<h2>Used Pixels</h2>\n<p>Used Pixel 7, 8 and 9 phones appear at tempting prices. Check for carrier locks and blacklisted IMEIs from abroad, since phones with unpaid contracts are exported. Open the bootloader menu or developer options: if \"OEM unlocking\" is greyed out with a carrier note, it may be locked. Look for screen burn, a green line and poor battery. Confirm MDMS. Prefer Indian units of the 8 series or newer for their long support.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives with local support</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Chip or screen</th><th>Battery or memory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Google Pixel 10</strong></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,28,000</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 4970 mAh, non-removable; 30W USB-PD Chargin...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/apple-iphone-15\">iPhone 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,27,499</td><td>Apple A16 (4nm)</td><td>Li-Ion 3349 mAh, non-removable; 20W Charging; 15W...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Honor Sup...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,25,999</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>Si-Ca 7500 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-fe\">Samsung Galaxy S26 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,24,999</td><td>Samsung Exynos 2500 (3nm)</td><td>Li-Po 4900 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charging;...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For a similar feel with a warranty, consider the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> with seven years of updates, or the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> for clean software and zoom at almost half the price. For cameras, the <a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a> is powerful.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy a Pixel in Nepal</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Photographers who like a natural look and do not want to edit.</li>\n<li>Developers and Android enthusiasts.</li>\n<li>People who travel often to a country where Google offers service.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone who breaks phones.</li>\n<li>Heavy gamers.</li>\n<li>Buyers outside Kathmandu.</li>\n<li>Those who want a bargain. Imports carry a premium.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Questions for the seller</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Which country is this unit from?</li>\n<li>Is the IMEI registered now?</li>\n<li>Does VoLTE work on my SIM? Show me.</li>\n<li>What is your warranty, in writing?</li>\n<li>What does a screen cost, and how long does it take?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Summary</h2>\n<p>A Pixel 10 or 10a is a pleasure to use and a small gamble to own here. If the seller answers all five questions and the price is within Rs 10,000 of the foreign one, it can be worth it. If there is any hesitation on registration or VoLTE, buy a Galaxy S26 and enjoy your sleep.</p>','gadgets/2026/google-pixel-10-pro-xl.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(51,20,'HP Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','hp-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>HP is one of the most common laptop brands in Nepali offices and homes, partly because its basic HP 15 models are easy to find and easy to get serviced. The current range runs from about Rs 55,000 to the Rs 1,98,000 <a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-ultra-g1q-2025\">EliteBook Ultra G1q</a>.</p>\n<p>The families: <strong>HP 14/15</strong> are the everyday models, <strong>Pavilion</strong> and <strong>Envy</strong> are nicer consumer laptops (several are 2-in-1s), <strong>ProBook</strong> and <strong>EliteBook</strong> are for business, and <strong>Victus</strong> is HP\'s gaming line.</p>\n<h2>HP laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-notebook-14-em0002wm-2023-amd-athlon-silver-7120u\">HP Notebook 14-em0002wm 2023 (AMD Athlon Silver 7120U)</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-notebook-15-fc0124nia-ryzen-3-7320u\">HP Notebook 15 FC0124NIA 2024 (Ryzen 3 7320U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 78,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0124nia-2024\">HP 15-fc0124nia (2024)</a></td><td>AMD Ryzen 3 7320U, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 78,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15-FC0043NQ 2024 (AMD Ryzen 5 7520U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 90,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 99,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-pavilion-x360-14-2022-i5-1235u\">HP Pavilion x360 DV2053TU 2022 (Intel Core i5-1235U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 95,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-pavilion-x360-14-2022\">HP Pavilion x360 14 (2022)</a></td><td>Intel Core i5-1235U, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 95,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-15-fd0154wm-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-8gb-512gb-156-fhd\">HP 15-FD0154WM 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-envy-x360-2024-intel-core-5-120u\">HP Envy x360 ES0013DX 2024 (Intel Core 5 120U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,05,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-notebook-14-ep-2024\">HP NoteBook 14-ep 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,09,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-445-g11-ryzen-5-7535u\">HP ProBook 445 G11 (Ryzen 5 7535U)</a></td><td>32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">HP EliteBook 655 G10 (Ryzen 5 Pro 7530U)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-envy-x360-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">HP Envy x360 ES1023DX 2024 (Intel Core 7 150U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,30,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-x-flip-14-14-fk0033-2025-ryzen-ai-7-350-24gb-1tb-2k-ips-touch\">HP OmniBook X Flip 14 14-FK0033 2025 (Ryzen AI 7 350)</a></td><td>24GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-2023-intel-core-i5-13420h-rtx-3050-8gb-512gb-156-fhd-144hz\">HP Victus 15 FA1093DX 2023 (Intel Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-2025-ryzen-5-8645hs-rtx-3050\">HP Victus 15 15‑FB3009AX 2025 (Ryzen 5 8645HS)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,48,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a></td><td>Ryzen 5 8645HS · RTX 3050 6GB, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,49,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Core i5-13420H · RTX 4050 6GB, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,60,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-640-g11-intel-core-ultra-7-165u\">HP EliteBook 640 G11 (Intel Core Ultra 7 165U)</a></td><td>32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-x-flip-14-14-fm0013dx-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-226v\">HP OmniBook X Flip 14 14-fm0013dx 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 226V)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-440-g11\">HP ProBook 440 G11 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,60,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">HP Spectre x360 14-EU0060CA 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-5-flip-14-fp0023-2025-intel-core-7-150u-16gb-512gb-14-2k-ips-touch\">HP OmniBook 5 Flip 14-fp0023 2025 (Intel Core 7 150U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,65,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-5-flip-14-2025\">HP OmniBook 5 Flip 14 (2025)</a></td><td>Intel Core 7 150U, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,65,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-440-g10-2023\">HP ProBook 440 G10 (2023)</a></td><td>Intel Core i7-1355U, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,68,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-ultra-g1q-b16xzua-2025-snapdragon-x-elite-x1e-78-100\">HP EliteBook Ultra G1Q B16XZUA 2025 (Snapdragon X Elite X1E-78-100)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,88,100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-ultra-g1q-2025\">HP EliteBook Ultra G1q (2025)</a></td><td>Snapdragon X Elite X1E-78-100, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,98,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Where to start</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15 with Ryzen 5 7520U</a>. Pay the extra for the 16GB version, because the memory can\'t be upgraded later.</li>\n<li><strong>Office laptops with plenty of memory:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/hp-probook-445-g11-ryzen-5-7535u\">ProBook 445 G11</a> and <a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">EliteBook 655 G10</a> come with 32GB here, unusual at their prices and great for heavy multitasking.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaming on a budget:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">Victus 15</a> is one of the cheapest RTX laptops around. Choose the RTX 4050 version if you can.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Skip the HP 14 at the bottom of the list, with an Athlon chip and 4GB/64GB, unless it\'s for very light use. For other options at your budget, see <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1,00,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>From Notebook to Spectre</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-pavilion-15\">HP Pavilion 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 1920×1080, Anti-glare</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7530U</td><td>8GB DDR4</td><td>41Wh, up to 8.5 hours</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-ryzen-7-7730u-8gb\">HP 15 (Ryzen 7 7730U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,06,000</td><td>39.6 cm (15.6″) diagonal, FHD (1920 x 1080),...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 7 7730U (up to 4.5 GHz max boost c...</td><td>8GB SODIMM DDR4 SDRAM</td><td>3-cell, 41 Wh Li-ion polymer</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-445-g11-ryzen-5-7535u\">HP ProBook 445 G11 (Ryzen 5 7535U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,14,999</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7535U (6nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 56 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">HP EliteBook 655 G10 (Ryzen 5 Pro 7530U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 Pro 7530U, 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-2023-13th-gen-i5-13420h-rtx-4050\">HP Victus 15 2023 (13th Gen i5-13420H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,65,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080),...</td><td>Intel Core i5-13420H, 8 cores / 12 threads</td><td>16GB</td><td>52.5 Wh LiPo; 150W AC Adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-x-flip-14-14-fm0013dx-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-226v\">HP OmniBook X Flip 14 14-fm0013dx 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 5 226V)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,59,999</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, 2K (1920 x 1200 pixels),...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 226V (TSMC N3B), 8 cores /...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 59 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">HP Spectre x360 14-EU0060CA 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>14 inches, OLED, 2.8K (2880 x 1800 pixels), 1...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155H (Intel 4), 14 cores /...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 68 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>HP\'s families, old and new</h2>\n<p>HP is moving to the OmniBook name for consumer laptops, replacing Pavilion, Envy and Spectre. You will see both in shops for some time. <strong>HP 14/15</strong> are basic. <strong>Pavilion</strong> and <strong>OmniBook 5</strong> are the middle. <strong>OmniBook X</strong>, <strong>Envy</strong> and <strong>Spectre</strong> are premium. <strong>ProBook</strong> and <strong>EliteBook</strong> are for business. <strong>Victus</strong> and <strong>OMEN</strong> are for games.</p>\n<h2>The best thing HP sells here: ProBook and EliteBook</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,14,999 the <a href=\"/products/hp-probook-445-g11-ryzen-5-7535u\">ProBook 445 G11</a> has 32GB, a 16:10 screen, a 56Wh battery and Windows 11 Pro. The <a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">EliteBook 655 G10</a> at Rs 1,29,999 adds a terabyte and a stronger chassis. The Ryzen chips are not the newest, but the rest is superb for office work, coding and study. People walk past them to buy glossy consumer laptops with 8GB. Do not be one of them.</p>\n<h2>Under Rs 80,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/hp-pavilion-15\">Pavilion 15</a> with a Ryzen 5 7530U at about Rs 75,000 is the strongest, with six cores and 8GB of replaceable memory. Add a stick and it lasts. The HP 14 with an Athlon and 4GB at Rs 54,999 belongs in the same drawer as the N100 machines: avoid. The HP 15 with a Ryzen 3 7320U is slow for its price.</p>\n<h2>Around one lakh</h2>\n<p>The HP 15 with a Ryzen 7 7730U or Core Ultra 5 125H looks tempting, until you see the 8GB. The processor is fine, the battery at 41Wh is small. With another 8GB these are good, so ask whether a slot exists. If it must be perfect out of the box, the Acer Aspire Lite 15 is cheaper with 16GB.</p>\n<h2>Convertibles</h2>\n<p>The Pavilion x360 from 2022 at Rs 95,000 is an old design. The <a href=\"/products/hp-omnibook-x-flip-14-14-fm0013dx-2025-intel-core-ultra-5-226v\">OmniBook X Flip 14</a> with a Core Ultra 5 226V is the modern choice: efficient, light and fine with a pen. The <a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">Spectre x360 14</a> with its 2.8K OLED is the most beautiful. Its listing mentions two different processors, so check the sticker.</p>\n<h2>Victus: gaming for students</h2>\n<p>Victus 15 is the most popular first gaming laptop in Nepal, and with reason. It looks normal and is sold everywhere. Versions differ a lot: RTX 3050 with 6GB, RTX 4050, different processors, and in some listings DDR4 memory. The <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15-2023-13th-gen-i5-13420h-rtx-4050\">RTX 4050 version</a> at about Rs 1,66,000 is the one to aim for. <a href=\"/reviews/hp-victus-15-review-nepal\">Our Victus review</a> lists the questions.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which HP</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Home:</strong> Pavilion 15 Ryzen 5 with added memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Student in any course except 3D:</strong> ProBook 445 G11.</li>\n<li><strong>Office fleet:</strong> ProBook or EliteBook with a three-year warranty.</li>\n<li><strong>Artist or note-taker:</strong> OmniBook X Flip.</li>\n<li><strong>Luxury:</strong> Spectre x360.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaming:</strong> Victus with RTX 4050.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>HP\'s model codes</h2>\n<p>Codes such as 15-fd0154wm carry meaning at the end. \"wm\", \"dx\", \"cl\" and \"nr\" mark machines made for American retailers, so they are imports. \"ne\", \"nia\" and \"tu\" are for our part of the world. An American unit is not bad, but its warranty and keyboard may differ, and the original price was set for another market.</p>\n<h2>Warranty check</h2>\n<p>Enter the serial and product number at HP\'s warranty page. Business units show the type and the end date. Ask the seller to match it on the bill. HP has authorised service in Nepal, with the best coverage for ProBook and EliteBook. Batteries are often covered for one year only.</p>\n<h2>Screens and speakers</h2>\n<p>Budget HPs have 250 nit panels and quiet speakers. Business models offer 300 nits or more, and matte finish. The Spectre and OmniBook X are bright and colourful. If you present to others often, ask about brightness first.</p>\n<h2>Security features</h2>\n<p>EliteBooks have useful extras: privacy shutters, BIOS protection, smart card and fingerprint readers, and in some a built-in privacy screen. For lawyers, doctors and accountants these are worth more than a faster chip.</p>\n<h2>Battery care</h2>\n<p>In the BIOS, HP Battery Health Manager can hold the charge near 80 per cent. On consumer models look in the HP Support Assistant. Use the original charger. HP business laptops warn about underpowered adapters, and they are right.</p>\n<h2>Used EliteBooks</h2>\n<p>An EliteBook 840 or 845 from two or three years ago at Rs 55,000 to 85,000 with 16GB is a pleasure to type on and solid to carry. Check for BIOS administrator passwords, as they cannot be removed easily, company enrolment, battery health and screen marks. With a new battery for Rs 5,000 to 8,000 it will serve you for years.</p>\n<h2>Printers and ecosystem</h2>\n<p>Many offices buy HP laptops to match HP printers. There is no technical need. If you are buying a printer too, compare ink costs. See <a href=\"/products?category=printer\">printers</a>.</p>\n<h2>HP compared</h2>\n<p><strong>Dell:</strong> equal in business, similar prices. <strong>Lenovo:</strong> ThinkPad keyboards and LOQ gaming are ahead, HP\'s convertibles are nicer. <strong>Acer:</strong> more hardware on consumer models. <strong>Asus:</strong> more OLED choice.</p>\n<h2>Set-up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update Windows and the BIOS through HP Support Assistant.</li>\n<li>Remove McAfee trials and shopping shortcuts.</li>\n<li>Set battery protection.</li>\n<li>Turn on device encryption and save the key.</li>\n<li>On Victus, set the performance mode in OMEN Gaming Hub and leave the rest.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Consumer HPs are discounted at festivals and session starts. Business models are cleared when a new generation arrives. Right now G10 and G11 machines are excellent value because of that. Ask specifically for \"older ProBook or EliteBook with 16GB or 32GB\".</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>HP\'s consumer range in Nepal is average for the money, and its business range is outstanding. Buy the ProBook 445 G11 or EliteBook 655 for work and study, the Victus RTX 4050 for games, and the OmniBook X Flip or Spectre if you want to enjoy looking at your laptop. Compare all with <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-150000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1.5 lakh</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/hp-omnibook-5-flip-14-fp0023-2025-intel-core-7-150u-16gb-512gb-14-2k-ips-touch.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(52,20,'Honor Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','honor-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Honor has quietly become one of the most interesting phone brands in Nepal, mainly because of its batteries and tough builds. The range covers almost every budget, from the Rs 16,499 <a href=\"/products/honor-choice-t10\">Honor Choice T10</a> to the Rs 2,66,999 <a href=\"/products/honor-magic-v5\">Magic V5</a> foldable.</p>\n<h2>Honor phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-choice-t10\">HONOR CHOICE T10</a></td><td>3GB/32GB</td><td>Rs 16,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 19,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x5c\">Honor X5c</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 20,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-play10\">Honor Play 10</a></td><td>3GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 20,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 22,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x5c-plus\">Honor X5c Plus</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 22,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x5d\">Honor X5d</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 25,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x6c-4g\">Honor X6C 4G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 26,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 28,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x5d-plus\">Honor X5d Plus</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 28,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x6d\">Honor X6d</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x6e\">Honor X6e</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 32,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x7d\">Honor X7d</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x7e\">Honor X7e</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 43,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-lite\">Honor 600 Lite</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 67,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600\">Honor 600</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 84,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-8-pro\">Honor Magic 8 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,99,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-v5\">Honor Magic V5</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,66,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>The Honors worth knowing about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> (from Rs 39,999). It has a 7,000mAh battery, an AMOLED screen and OIS on the main camera, a combination usually found at higher prices.</li>\n<li><strong>Toughest mid-ranger:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a> (from Rs 54,999) has an 8,300mAh battery and drop protection. It\'s a favourite with people who are hard on their phones.</li>\n<li><strong>Flagship performance for less:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a> (from Rs 1,29,999) pairs a Snapdragon 8 Elite with a 7,000mAh battery, well below what Samsung and Apple charge for similar speed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Honor\'s cheapest X5 and Choice models are basic phones with small storage, so aim for at least 128GB. Compare against other brands in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Key models compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x6d\">Honor X6d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>6.75 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz, 450 nits (peak)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 6300 (6 nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 5260 mAh, non-removable; 15W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x7d\">Honor X7d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.77 inches TFT LCD, 120Hz, 850 nits (HBM)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6500 mAh, non-removable; 35W Honor Supe...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>6.77 inches AMOLED, 120 Hz, 3000 nits (Peak)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2 (6nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W HONOR Supe...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-x9d\">Honor X9d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>6.79-inch AMOLED (2640×1200), 120Hz, 6,000 ni...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>8,300mAh silicon-carbon; 66W wired, reverse c...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;67,999</td><td>6.55 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 5000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Honor Supe...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600\">Honor 600</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;84,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 8000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Hono...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 8000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Hono...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-magic-8-pro\">Honor Magic 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>6.71-inch LTPO OLED, 120Hz, 6,000 nits peak</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>7,100mAh; 100W wired, 80W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A brand that came back</h2>\n<p>Honor began as Huawei\'s younger line and became independent in 2020. That matters for one reason: its phones have Google services, unlike Huawei\'s. In Nepal it has grown quickly by putting big batteries in slim, tough bodies and offering large storage. The ladder runs from about Rs 16,500 to the foldable Magic V5 at Rs 2,66,999.</p>\n<h2>The series</h2>\n<p><strong>Choice and Play:</strong> very basic. <strong>X5 and X6:</strong> entry phones. <strong>X7, X8, X9:</strong> the middle, where Honor is strongest. <strong>Number series (400, 600):</strong> cameras and style. <strong>Magic:</strong> flagships. The letter after the X number is the generation, so X9d is newer than X9c.</p>\n<h2>Under Rs 30,000: choose carefully</h2>\n<p>The X5 and X6 phones use the Helio G81, a basic chip, with HD screens and 15W charging in most. They are pleasant to look at and slow to use. Storage is generous in some versions, which helps. The <a href=\"/products/honor-x6d\">X6d</a> has a better chip, and <a href=\"/reviews/honor-x6d-review-nepal\">our review</a> covers it. At Rs 22,000 to 26,000 an Infinix Note 50x or Galaxy A07 is usually the better purchase.</p>\n<h2>X7d and X7e</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/honor-x7d\">X7d</a> at Rs 33,999 comes with a 108MP camera, 8GB and 256GB or more. For people who store everything, it is excellent. The chip is an older 4G one and the screen an LCD. The X7e at Rs 39,999 has 7,500mAh, and a slower chip than the X7d, so look twice.</p>\n<h2>X8d: the one for reading and video</h2>\n<p>An AMOLED that reaches high brightness, a 7,000mAh battery and a slim body at Rs 39,999. No 5G, modest speed. We like it a lot for ordinary people, as <a href=\"/reviews/honor-x8d-review-nepal\">our review</a> says.</p>\n<h2>X9d: the best Honor for most</h2>\n<p>For Rs 54,999 you get 8,300mAh, 66W charging, a very bright screen, a capable chip and drop protection that competitors do not attempt. It is the phone to give someone who works outdoors, travels or has small children. <a href=\"/reviews/honor-x9d-review-nepal\">Read our review</a>.</p>\n<h2>400 and 600: the camera line</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/honor-400\">Honor 400</a> at about Rs 68,000 and the <a href=\"/products/honor-600\">600</a> at Rs 84,999 are compact, with 200MP main cameras. The 600\'s 7,000mAh battery in a 6.57-inch body is hard to find anywhere else. The <a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">600 Pro</a> at Rs 1,29,999 adds a flagship chip and a telephoto, and it belongs on any list of small powerful phones. If you dislike large phones, Honor deserves your attention before Samsung.</p>\n<h2>Magic 8 Pro and Magic V5</h2>\n<p>Flagships with excellent cameras and batteries. They compete with vivo, Xiaomi and Samsung at about Rs 2 lakh, where resale and service count for more, and Honor is weaker there. Buy the Magic 8 Pro if you have held it and prefer it. The <a href=\"/products/honor-magic-v5\">Magic V5</a> is among the thinnest foldables. Ask about repair costs.</p>\n<h2>MagicOS</h2>\n<p>MagicOS is smooth, with clever gestures such as dragging text or images to a side panel to send them to another app. It includes some extra apps. On flagships, Honor has promised long support, in line with the best. On X models expect a couple of Android versions and several years of patches. Ask for the exact promise.</p>\n<h2>Batteries: what Honor learned first</h2>\n<p>Honor was early with silicon-carbon cells, and its big batteries come without the thickness you would expect. Charging speeds are moderate on X models, at 35 to 66W, and fast on Magic. The charger is usually included. Use the smart charging option at night.</p>\n<h2>Toughness, honestly</h2>\n<p>The X9 line\'s drop resistance comes from a layered screen structure and cushioned corners. It survives falls that break others, though not every fall. Honor has offered free screen replacement in the first months on some models in Nepal. Ask whether that applies to yours and get it written on the bill.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Honor has authorised service in Kathmandu and some other cities, and the network is growing. In smaller towns you may have to send the phone. Parts for X models are reasonably priced. Before buying an expensive Honor outside Kathmandu, ask where it goes when it breaks and for how long.</p>\n<h2>Official and grey</h2>\n<p>Honor sells different versions of the same name around the world, with different batteries and memory. Official Nepali units have the distributor\'s sticker and MDMS registration. Phones from the Gulf or China appear too. Chinese ones have MagicOS without Nepali and with fewer Google functions. Check the model code.</p>\n<h2>Price behaviour</h2>\n<p>Honor phones launch with bundles such as earbuds, watches or an extra year of warranty, and fall in price within four to six months. The X9 series keeps its price best. If you see an earlier X9c at Rs 40,000 to 45,000 new, it is a bargain.</p>\n<h2>Compared with the rest</h2>\n<p><strong>Against Redmi:</strong> Honor is slimmer and tougher, Redmi faster with more service points. <strong>Against vivo and OPPO:</strong> more battery and storage for the price, fewer shops. <strong>Against Samsung:</strong> much better batteries, shorter support on X models, lower resale. <strong>Against Realme:</strong> better screens and build, slower charging.</p>\n<h2>Our Honor shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Storage on a budget:</strong> X7d.</li>\n<li><strong>Screen and battery under Rs 40,000:</strong> X8d.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for most:</strong> X9d.</li>\n<li><strong>Compact camera phone:</strong> 600, or 600 Pro if the budget allows.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not choose Honor</h2>\n<p>Serious gamers on a budget, people who sell their phone every year, and buyers far from a service centre. For them POCO, Samsung and whichever brand has the nearest counter are better.</p>\n<h2>What is next</h2>\n<p>A <a href=\"/news/honor-13500mah-battery-phone-certified\">13,500mAh battery</a> has been certified in China, and a successor to the X9d will come in time. Neither is a reason to wait if your phone is dying. The X9d already lasts two days.</p>\n<h2>In short</h2>\n<p>Honor is the brand to look at when you want a slim phone that lasts. Start at the X8d, stop at the X9d unless you love cameras, and settle the service question before you pay.</p>','gadgets/2026/honor-600-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(53,20,'Honor Tablet Price in Nepal (November 2026)','honor-tablet-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Honor\'s tablets are an affordable alternative to Samsung and Xiaomi for video, reading and online classes. The range runs from the Rs 21,999 <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x7\">Pad X7</a> to the Rs 69,999 <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-v9\">Pad V9</a>.</p>\n<h2>Honor tablets price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x7\">Honor Pad X7</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x9a\">Honor Pad X9a</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x8b\">Honor Pad X8b</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 32,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-v9\">Honor Pad V9</a></td><td>8GB - 256GB - Pencil 3 + Keyboard (Free) options *</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which one?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For watching and reading:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x8b\">Pad X8b</a> (from Rs 29,999) has a 10,100mAh battery, so it lasts for days of casual use. Pick the 8GB version if you\'ll keep many apps open.</li>\n<li><strong>As a work and study tablet:</strong> the Pad V9 has a much faster Dimensity 8350 chip. ONIN and other sellers often bundle the pencil and keyboard, so check what\'s in the box.</li>\n<li><strong>The Pad X7</strong> is the cheapest option. It\'s fine for a child\'s videos and games, but noticeably slower.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At Rs 25,000–30,000, also look at the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2</a>. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-30000-nepal\">tablets under Rs 30,000</a> guide compares them.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Honor\'s tablets</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x7\">Honor Pad X7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>8.7 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ), 625...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 680 (6nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 7020 mAh, non-removable; 10W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x9a\">Honor Pad X9a</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>11.5 inches TFT LCD, 120Hz, 400 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 8300 mAh, non-removable; 35W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x8b\">Honor Pad X8b</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 680 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 10100 mAh, non-removable</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-v9\">Honor Pad V9</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>11.5 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 500 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8350 Elite (4nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>Si-Ca 10100 mAh, non-removable; 35W Fast Char...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Big batteries, slim bodies</h2>\n<p>Honor\'s tablets follow its phones: they are light for their size, they last a long time, and the cheaper ones use older chips. There are three for watching and reading, and one that is seriously fast. None includes a pen, and support is shorter than Samsung\'s. Within those limits they are good value.</p>\n<h2>Pad X7: small and cheap</h2>\n<p>At Rs 21,999 the <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x7\">Pad X7</a> has an 8.7-inch screen, 4GB and a large 7,020mAh battery for its size. It is a child\'s tablet or a reader, and it competes with the Galaxy Tab A11. The Honor lasts longer, the Samsung has Samsung Kids and longer support. For a child, we lean to the Samsung. For an adult who reads on buses, to the Honor.</p>\n<h2>Pad X9a: the one with the nice screen</h2>\n<p>At Rs 25,999 the <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x9a\">X9a</a> has an 11.5-inch 120Hz screen, a Snapdragon 685, up to 8GB and 35W charging. It is our favourite of the cheap ones. It costs Rs 4,000 less than the Redmi Pad 2 and has a slightly bigger, smoother screen with more memory in the top version. The Redmi\'s resolution is higher and its speakers better. Look at both.</p>\n<h2>Pad X8b: the marathon runner</h2>\n<p>A 10,100mAh battery at Rs 29,999. The Snapdragon 680 inside is from 2021, and the base version has 4GB. Buy it if hours away from a socket are the reason, and take 8GB. Otherwise the X9a is cheaper and quicker.</p>\n<h2>Pad V9: the grown-up</h2>\n<p>At Rs 69,999 the <a href=\"/products/honor-pad-v9\">Pad V9</a> has a Dimensity 8350 Elite, a 144Hz 11.5-inch screen and 10,100mAh in a thin metal body. It is fast enough for anything on Android and lasts for days of study. It competes with the Xiaomi Pad 8 at Rs 59,999, which has a similar chip for less, and the Galaxy Tab S10 FE at Rs 74,999, with a pen, water resistance and seven years of updates. If you need battery above all, choose the Honor. If you write, choose the Samsung.</p>\n<h2>Which chip does what</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 680 and 685 are for video, reading, light browsing and simple games. Two apps at once are possible with patience. The Dimensity 8350 is for everything. There is nothing between them in the range, and that gap is where the Xiaomi Pad 7 at Rs 44,999 sits, unchallenged by Honor.</p>\n<h2>MagicOS on a tablet</h2>\n<p>Split screen, floating windows and a side panel for dragging content work well. If you also use an Honor phone, you can share files, mirror the phone and use the tablet as a second screen for some laptops. A handful of extra apps can be removed. Updates: expect one or two Android versions on the X models, more on the V9. Ask.</p>\n<h2>Pens and keyboards</h2>\n<p>The V9 supports Honor\'s active pen and a keyboard, sold separately and in small quantities here. Confirm that both are available. The X models work with basic capacitive pens only. For handwriting on a budget there is no good Honor option.</p>\n<h2>Eye comfort</h2>\n<p>Honor pays attention to flicker and blue light. Its reading mode with a paper-like texture and the hardware low blue light certification on several models are pleasant for long evenings. Warmth and brightness adjust automatically.</p>\n<h2>Speakers and films</h2>\n<p>Most have four speakers, two on the Pad X7. Check Widevine L1 for HD streaming with the DRM Info app. On older imported units it may be L3.</p>\n<h2>Battery life in hours</h2>\n<p>Local video at medium brightness runs for about ten hours on the X7, twelve or so on the X9a and around fifteen on the X8b and V9. Charging is slow on the X7 at 10W, so leave it overnight. The others charge at 22 to 35W.</p>\n<h2>For online classes</h2>\n<p>The X9a with 8GB is comfortable with Zoom or Meet and a PDF beside it. Prop it on a stand at eye level, sit facing a window and use earphones with a microphone. The front camera sits on the side that suits landscape on newer models. Check.</p>\n<h2>For children</h2>\n<p>Honor has a Kids mode, and Google Family Link works. Get a thick case. Prefer 6GB or more, since children open everything. Avoid giving a child the only device with your wallet or bank app on it.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Honor service is available in Kathmandu and some cities. Tablet screens may have to be ordered. Ask about time and price. Official units have a distributor\'s sticker. Tablets with a SIM need MDMS registration, Wi-Fi ones do not.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy an Honor tablet</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reader or film lover with Rs 26,000:</strong> Pad X9a.</li>\n<li><strong>Traveller:</strong> Pad X8b, 8GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Power user who wants endurance:</strong> Pad V9.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Students who write by hand.</li>\n<li>People who want updates until 2032.</li>\n<li>Buyers without service nearby.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Comparisons that matter</h2>\n<p><strong>X9a or Redmi Pad 2?</strong> Honor for smoothness and price. Redmi for sharpness and speakers.</p>\n<p><strong>X8b or OnePlus Pad Lite?</strong> OnePlus for cleaner software and the LTE option. Honor for battery.</p>\n<p><strong>V9 or Xiaomi Pad 8?</strong> Xiaomi for price, Honor for battery and slimness.</p>\n<p><strong>V9 or Tab S10 FE?</strong> Samsung for pen, water and support.</p>\n<h2>Bargaining</h2>\n<p>Honor dealers often add a case, earbuds or a keyboard. Ask for the case at least. Tablets are slow sellers outside of school admission season, so prices are flexible.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Store at half charge if unused for weeks.</li>\n<li>Use the charger supplied.</li>\n<li>Protect the corners.</li>\n<li>Restart weekly.</li>\n<li>Keep 15 per cent of storage free.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Pad X9a is a quiet bargain for reading and watching. The Pad V9 is a fine tablet in a hard neighbourhood. The other two are for particular needs. Compare all of them with our picks for <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-30000-nepal\">under Rs 30,000</a> and the wider <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/honor-pad-v9.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(54,20,'Huawei Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','huawei-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Huawei\'s earbuds stand out more for their designs than their prices. The range includes the open-ear <strong>FreeArc</strong> hooks and the <strong>FreeClip</strong> earrings-style buds, which clip onto your ear rather than sitting inside it. Prices run from Rs 7,490 to Rs 33,990.</p>\n<h2>Huawei earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freebuds-se-4-anc\">Huawei FreeBuds SE 4 ANC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,490</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freearc\">Huawei FreeArc</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freebuds-7i\">Huawei FreeBuds 7i</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 17,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freeclips\">Huawei FreeClip</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 29,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freeclips-2\">Huawei FreeClip 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 33,990</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Pick by how you\'ll wear them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Classic earbuds with noise cancellation:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/huawei-freebuds-se-4-anc\">FreeBuds SE 4 ANC</a> (Rs 7,490) is the sensible buy, with long battery life of up to about 50 hours with the case. The FreeBuds 7i adds better sound and stronger ANC.</li>\n<li><strong>For walking and exercise:</strong> open-ear designs like the FreeArc let you hear traffic and people around you. That\'s safer on Kathmandu roads, but you lose bass and noise blocking.</li>\n<li><strong>The FreeClip and FreeClip 2</strong> are comfortable to wear all day and look like jewellery. They\'re expensive for what they do, so buy them for the comfort, not the sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Huawei earbuds work with any phone, but some features need Huawei\'s AI Life app. Compare other brands in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 10,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The five models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freebuds-se-4-anc\">Huawei FreeBuds SE 4 ANC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,490</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 50 hours (case); 1 h...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freearc\">Huawei FreeArc</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,990</td><td>Music: 7 hours (buds) / 28 hours (case); 40 m...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freebuds-7i\">Huawei FreeBuds 7i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;17,990</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freeclips\">Huawei FreeClip</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,990</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 36 hours (case); Quic...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-freeclips-2\">Huawei FreeClip 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,990</td><td>Music: 9 hours (buds) / 38 hours (case); 40 m...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Two ideas of what an earbud is</h2>\n<p>Huawei sells ordinary in-ear buds and, more interestingly, buds that do not enter the ear at all. The FreeClip and FreeArc sit outside, so you hear the world along with your music. For people who find ear canals sore, who walk in traffic or who want to hear a child in the next room, they are worth understanding. For people who want silence on a bus, they are the wrong product.</p>\n<h2>FreeBuds SE 4 ANC: Rs 7,490</h2>\n<p>The sensible pair. Noise cancelling, up to ten hours in the buds, fifty with the case, stems that make controls easy. Sound is balanced by Huawei\'s standards. Rivals such as the CMF Buds 2 at Rs 7,499 and Buds 2 Plus at Rs 8,999 offer more features, LDAC among them. The Huawei\'s strength is comfort and battery.</p>\n<h2>FreeBuds 7i: Rs 17,990</h2>\n<p>Better drivers and stronger ANC. At this price the Soundcore Liberty 5 and Redmi Buds 8 Pro are hard to beat. Choose the 7i if you own a Huawei tablet or watch and like the common app.</p>\n<h2>FreeArc: Rs 16,990</h2>\n<p>An ear-hook design for sport. A soft arm goes over the ear and a speaker points at the canal. It stays put while running, and with IP57 resistance it copes with sweat and rain. Bass is better than most open designs and still lighter than in-ear. For running on roads, this is the safest way to listen.</p>\n<h2>FreeClip and FreeClip 2: Rs 29,990 and 33,990</h2>\n<p>A ball, a bean and a bendable bridge. They clip to the edge of the ear like jewellery and weigh about five grams. You forget that they are there, which is their whole point. Left and right are interchangeable. Sound is clear for voices and light in bass. They leak little at moderate volume. The second version improves sound and battery. They are a luxury, and people who wear glasses, hearing aids or earrings should try them first.</p>\n<h2>Open or sealed? A short guide</h2>\n<p><strong>Choose sealed</strong> for buses, flights, offices and music with bass.</p>\n<p><strong>Choose open</strong> for walking, running, cycling where allowed, cooking, childcare, long calls at a desk, and sore ears.</p>\n<p>Many people end up with both: a Rs 5,000 ANC pair for travel and an open pair for life.</p>\n<h2>Safety on our roads</h2>\n<p>Motorbikes come from every side. Sealed buds with ANC remove the warning. If you walk or run beside a road, use open buds or transparency and keep the volume low. On a motorbike or bicycle, do not use earbuds.</p>\n<h2>The app</h2>\n<p>AI Life, now part of Huawei\'s Audio Connect on some phones, sets controls, equaliser and ANC, and updates firmware. On Android it is best installed from Huawei\'s site. On iPhone use the App Store. Without the app the buds work with default settings.</p>\n<h2>Pairing and multipoint</h2>\n<p>They pair with any Bluetooth device. Most support two at once. On Huawei and Honor devices there is a pop-up. Codecs are AAC and SBC on the cheaper models, with L2HC or LDAC on some higher ones. Check the sheet if you care.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Huawei\'s call quality is better than average, with bone-conduction sensors in several models. The FreeClip is surprisingly good for calls in quiet places. In wind, the in-ear models win.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Seven to ten hours on a charge for most. Open designs have no ANC to feed. The cases are small, and higher models charge wirelessly.</p>\n<h2>Comfort through the day</h2>\n<p>FreeClips can be worn for hours without pressure. FreeArc\'s hook may touch the arms of glasses. SE 4 and 7i are light with soft tips. If your ears are small, try the 7i\'s smallest tip before buying.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Huawei has a distributor and gives a year on official units. Single buds may be ordered for some models. The unusual shapes are copied widely: there are Rs 1,500 clip buds that look like FreeClips from a metre away. A real pair shows its name in the app.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Wipe clips after sweat and let them dry before casing.</li>\n<li>Do not bend the bridge more than needed.</li>\n<li>Keep hair spray and perfume away from the speakers.</li>\n<li>Charge the case once a fortnight even if unused.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday, budget:</strong> FreeBuds SE 4 ANC.</li>\n<li><strong>Runner:</strong> FreeArc.</li>\n<li><strong>All-day wearer, style:</strong> FreeClip 2, or the first on sale.</li>\n<li><strong>Huawei device owner:</strong> FreeBuds 7i.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Cheaper open alternatives</h2>\n<p>The Soundcore C50i clip at Rs 7,799, the CMF Clip Pro at Rs 14,999 and JBL\'s Soundgear Clips at Rs 23,000 follow the same idea. Huawei\'s clips are the most comfortable we know of by reputation and design. The CMF is the value choice. Try before paying three times more.</p>\n<h2>Cheaper sealed alternatives</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">Nord Buds 4</a> at Rs 4,999 and <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a> at Rs 8,999 have better noise cancelling than the SE 4. Those who do not care about the brand should look there.</p>\n<h2>Buying tips</h2>\n<p>Huawei often bundles earbuds with watches. If you want a GT 6 as well, ask for a combined price. FreeClip colours differ in price. Test for leakage by playing at your normal volume and asking someone to stand beside you.</p>\n<h2>Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Do FreeClips fall off?</strong> Rarely. Jumping and headscarves can move them.</p>\n<p><strong>Can they be used with a helmet?</strong> Uncomfortable, and unsafe while riding.</p>\n<p><strong>Do they suit people with hearing loss in one ear?</strong> Mono mode works. Open buds can be heard along with hearing aids in some cases. Ask an audiologist.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Huawei\'s open earbuds are the reason to visit this page. The FreeArc for sport and FreeClip for daily life do something in-ear buds cannot. Its normal earbuds are good and face cheaper, better-equipped rivals in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/huawei-freebuds-se-4-anc.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(55,20,'Huawei Smartwatch Price in Nepal (November 2026)','huawei-smartwatch-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Huawei\'s watches have a reputation for one thing: battery life. Many of them last one to three weeks between charges, which is a big part of why they\'re popular in Nepal. The range runs from the Rs 7,990 <a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Band 10</a> to the Rs 64,990 <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-7-pro\">Watch GT 7 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Huawei smartwatches price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Huawei Band 10</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-11\">Huawei Band 11</a></td><td>Durable Polymer</td><td>Rs 9,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Aluminium Alloy</td><td>Rs 11,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-11-pro\">Huawei Band 11 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 14,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-4\">Huawei Watch Fit 4</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 22,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5\">Huawei Watch Fit 5</a></td><td>Watch Fit 5</td><td>Rs 27,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Watch Fit 5 Pro</td><td>Rs 43,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Huawei Watch GT 6</a></td><td>Watch GT 6 (41mm / 46mm)</td><td>Rs 34,490</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Watch GT 6 Pro (46mm titanium)</td><td>Rs 54,490</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-4-pro\">Huawei Watch Fit 4 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 38,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5-pro\">Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 43,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-7\">Huawei Watch GT 7</a></td><td>46 mm</td><td>Rs 44,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-5-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 5 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 49,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 6 Pro</a></td><td>Brown Composite Woven Strap</td><td>Rs 54,490</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Black Flurolastomer Strap</td><td>Rs 54,490</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Titanium Strap</td><td>Rs 74,490</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-d2\">Huawei Watch D2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 64,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-7-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 7 Pro</a></td><td>Fluoroelastomer Strap</td><td>Rs 64,990</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Huawei watch?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For basic fitness and sleep tracking:</strong> the Band 10 or Band 11. They\'re light and cheap, and last around two weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>A slim, smartwatch-style design:</strong> the Watch Fit 4 and Fit 5 have big rectangular AMOLED screens and built-in GPS.</li>\n<li><strong>Our pick for most people:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Watch GT 6</a> (from Rs 34,490). It has a round AMOLED screen and accurate GPS, and lasts up to about two weeks (41mm) or three weeks (46mm).</li>\n<li><strong>The GT 7 Pro and Watch D2</strong> are premium models. The Watch D2 includes a blood-pressure cuff in the strap, a rare feature.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Huawei watches work with Android and iPhone, but replying to messages is more limited than on an Apple Watch or Galaxy Watch. See also our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">smartwatches under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The range</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Huawei Band 10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,990</td><td>1.47 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 8 days (normal usage), Up to 14 hours (...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.0</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-11-pro\">Huawei Band 11 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,990</td><td>1.62 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 8 days (normal usage), Up to 14 days (l...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, GPS: Yes, with GNSS tracking</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-4\">Huawei Watch Fit 4</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;22,990</td><td>1.82 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage), Up to 10 days (l...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5),...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5\">Huawei Watch Fit 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;27,990</td><td>1.82-inch AMOLED (Fit 5) / 1.92-inch AMOLED w...</td><td>Up to 10 days</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Huawei Watch GT 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;34,490</td><td>1.32-inch (41mm) or 1.47-inch (46mm) AMOLED (...</td><td>Up to 14 days (41mm) / 21 days (46mm)</td><td>Dual-band GPS (L1 + L5)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-5-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 5 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,990</td><td>41 mm: 1.32 inches, 46 mm: 1.43 inches, AMOLE...</td><td>Up to 9 days (normal usage), Up to 14 days (l...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5),...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6-pro\">Huawei Watch GT 6 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,490</td><td>1.47 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 12 days (normal usage), Up to 21 days (...</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-d2\">Huawei Watch D2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;64,990</td><td>1.82 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Li-Ion 524 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, NFC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Huawei\'s phones left. Its watches stayed</h2>\n<p>In Nepal, Huawei is now mainly a watch and earphone brand, and a very good one. Its watches have excellent sensors, handsome bodies, bright screens and batteries that last one to three weeks. They work with Android and iPhone. What they lack is an app store of any depth, replies on iPhone, contactless payment here, and an easy installation on Android.</p>\n<h2>Installing the app on Android</h2>\n<p>The version of Huawei Health in the Play Store is outdated or missing. Scan the code in the watch\'s manual or visit Huawei\'s site, download the app and allow the installation. It takes three minutes. Update it from inside the app later. On Samsung phones, turn off Auto Blocker for that moment and back on afterwards. On iPhone, the App Store version is fine.</p>\n<h2>Bands: 10, 11, 11 Pro</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Band 10</a> at Rs 7,990 is among the best cheap trackers, especially for sleep and swimming. The 11 has a larger screen. The <a href=\"/products/huawei-band-11-pro\">Band 11 Pro</a> at Rs 14,990 adds its own GPS, so runners with slim wrists may like it. At that price, a Redmi Watch 6 Active is a full watch with GPS for less.</p>\n<h2>Watch Fit 4 and Fit 5</h2>\n<p>Rectangular, thin and light, with large AMOLED screens, GPS and barometers on newer models. The <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-4\">Fit 4</a> at Rs 22,990 is excellent value. The <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5\">Fit 5</a> at Rs 27,990 is brighter and newer. The Fit 5 Pro adds sapphire and titanium with diving and trail functions. For people who find round watches bulky, the Fit line is the most comfortable smartwatch of all.</p>\n<h2>Watch GT 6: the one we recommend</h2>\n<p>At Rs 34,490 the <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">GT 6</a> offers a classic look, a 3,000-nit screen, dual-band GPS and up to 21 days in the 46mm size. <a href=\"/reviews/huawei-watch-gt-6-review-nepal\">Our review</a> calls it the best fitness watch for the money in Nepal. The newer GT 7 at Rs 44,990 improves details. Unless it is discounted, the 6 is the better buy.</p>\n<h2>The Pro models</h2>\n<p>GT 5 Pro, GT 6 Pro and GT 7 Pro use titanium, sapphire and ceramic backs, with golf maps, diving and ECG. The <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-5-pro\">GT 5 Pro</a> at about Rs 50,000 is the value among them. ECG availability depends on region, so ask to see it working.</p>\n<h2>Watch D2: blood pressure</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-d2\">Watch D2</a> at Rs 64,990 contains a tiny inflating cuff in the strap. It measures blood pressure in the same way as a home monitor and can take readings through the day. It is the only watch in this list that measures it properly, and Huawei has obtained medical device approvals for it in several regions. It is still no substitute for a doctor, and a Rs 4,000 arm cuff is accurate for home checks. For people advised to monitor pressure over 24 hours, it is a serious tool.</p>\n<h2>Training features</h2>\n<p>Running plans, training load, recovery time, VO2 max estimates and route import. For ordinary runners and cyclists there is more than enough. Serious athletes still prefer Garmin for its ecosystem. Huawei syncs to Strava, and to Apple Health on iPhone.</p>\n<h2>Trekking</h2>\n<p>GT models record routes, show contour maps on newer versions when downloaded, and have barometers. Battery with GPS lasts 20 to 40 hours depending on the mode. For the Annapurna Circuit, record in economy mode and carry a small power bank. The watch charger is proprietary, so do not forget it.</p>\n<h2>Sleep and health</h2>\n<p>Huawei\'s sleep tracking has long been among the most consistent. The TruSense sensors handle wrist movement and varied skin tones well. SpO2, stress and emotional state are indicators. Women\'s health tracking is good.</p>\n<h2>Notifications</h2>\n<p>Text and emojis appear well, with Devanagari supported. On Android you can send quick replies to some apps. On iPhone you cannot. Keep the Huawei Health app allowed in the background. Phones from Xiaomi, OPPO and vivo need it to be locked in recent apps.</p>\n<h2>iPhone owners</h2>\n<p>Huawei\'s are some of the best watches for an iPhone user who dislikes daily charging. You lose replies, Apple Pay and deep integration. You gain two weeks between charges and a round case.</p>\n<h2>Sizes</h2>\n<p>GT watches come in 41 and 46mm. The smaller are elegant and have roughly two-thirds of the battery life. Fit models are 43 to 48mm tall and slim. Try them on.</p>\n<h2>Straps</h2>\n<p>Standard 22mm on 46mm GT, 18 or 20mm on the smaller ones. Fit models use their own clips. Leather is lovely and dislikes sweat. Fluoroelastomer is better for sport.</p>\n<h2>Official units and warranty</h2>\n<p>Huawei has a distributor in Nepal, with a one-year warranty. Gulf and Chinese units exist. Chinese versions may lack languages. Check the box and the sticker.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheap and accurate:</strong> Band 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Light and comfortable:</strong> Watch Fit 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for most:</strong> Watch GT 6, 46mm.</li>\n<li><strong>Dress watch:</strong> GT 5 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood pressure:</strong> Watch D2, with a doctor\'s advice.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should look elsewhere</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who want apps and replies: Galaxy Watch, Apple Watch.</li>\n<li>Those who cannot install apps from outside the Play Store, for instance on managed work phones.</li>\n<li>Elite runners: Garmin, Coros, Amazfit Cheetah.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Price behaviour</h2>\n<p>Older generations stay on sale beside newer ones, and that is where bargains live. GT 5 Pro and Fit 4 fall at each launch. Bundles with FreeBuds are common.</p>\n<h2>Privacy</h2>\n<p>Health data is stored in Huawei\'s cloud for your region if you turn on syncing. You can use the watch with syncing off. Read the permissions and decline what you do not need.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If you can install the app, Huawei gives you more watch for the money than anyone else between Rs 8,000 and Rs 50,000. The GT 6 is the heart of the range. The Fit 4 is its comfortable cousin. Everything else is taste. See the wider field in our <a href=\"/guides/smartwatch-buying-guide-nepal-2024\">smartwatch guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/huawei-watch-gt-6.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(56,20,'Infinix Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','infinix-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Infinix sells budget and mid-range phones that often have better screens or faster charging than their price suggests. The Nepal range runs from the Rs 14,999 <a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10\">Smart 10</a> to the Rs 84,999 <a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-50-pro\">GT 50 Pro</a> gaming phone.</p>\n<h2>Infinix phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10\">Infinix Smart 10</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">Infinix Smart 10 Plus</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 15,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-60i-4g\">Infinix Hot 60i 4G</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 18,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-20\">Infinix Smart 20</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 23,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-50-pro-plus\">Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-70\">Infinix Hot 70</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 36,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 35,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-edge\">Infinix Note Edge</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 41,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 43,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 47,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-30\">Infinix GT 30</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 46,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-70-pro\">Infinix Hot 70 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 52,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 56,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60\">Infinix Note 60</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60-pro\">Infinix Note 60 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 62,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinixgt-30-pro\">Infinix GT 30 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 65,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-50-pro\">Infinix GT 50 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 84,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>The ones to look at</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Note 50s</a> (Rs 35,999) has a 120Hz AMOLED screen, a Dimensity 7300 Ultimate and OIS on the main camera, which is a lot of phone for the money.</li>\n<li><strong>A step up:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60\">Note 60</a> (from Rs 54,999) has a faster Dimensity 7400 Ultimate and the same strong screen.</li>\n<li><strong>For mobile gamers:</strong> the GT 30 Pro and GT 50 Pro add gaming shoulder triggers, better cooling and RGB lighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Smart and Hot series are basic phones. They\'re fine for calls and social media, but go for at least 128GB of storage. Software updates are shorter than on Samsung or Xiaomi, so if you keep phones a long time, weigh that up. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">phones under Rs 40,000</a> for alternatives.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The range with specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">Infinix Smart 10 Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;15,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-60i-4g\">Infinix Hot 60i 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,999</td><td>6.7 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (HBM)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 Ultimate (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5160 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 560 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-50-pro-plus\">Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 550 nits (typ), 13...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 1300 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-30\">Infinix GT 30</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;46,999</td><td>6.78 Inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 700 nits (typ), 16...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7400 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>45W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60-pro\">Infinix Note 60 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;62,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 700 nits (typ), 1,...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 6500 mAh; 90W Fast Charging; 30W...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-50-pro\">Infinix GT 50 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;84,999</td><td>6.78 Inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 700 nits (typ), 16...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8400 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>45W Fast Charging; 30W Wireless Charging, 4.5...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why Infinix keeps appearing in our picks</h2>\n<p>Infinix belongs to Transsion, the company behind Tecno and itel. It sells huge numbers of phones in Africa and South Asia and has learned to make a small budget go far. Under Rs 40,000, its best models offer chips and screens that others sell for Rs 10,000 more. The trade is found in service reach, update length and resale. If you know that and choose the right model, you get a great deal of phone.</p>\n<h2>Four families</h2>\n<p><strong>Smart</strong> is entry level. <strong>Hot</strong> is the young, affordable line. <strong>Note</strong> is the one with the best hardware. <strong>GT</strong> is for games, with a bold back and sometimes lights. Odd letters such as i, x and s mark variations. In Infinix\'s case they can matter a lot: the Note 50x and the Note 50s are very different phones.</p>\n<h2>Smart 10 and 10 Plus</h2>\n<p>At Rs 15,000 to 16,000 these are basic phones done properly. The <a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">10 Plus</a> is the better buy, with 128GB and 6,000mAh. The newer Smart 20 at Rs 21,999 is poor value beside the Note 50x at the same price, a good example of why the family name matters.</p>\n<h2>Note 50x: the bargain</h2>\n<p>At about Rs 21,999 the <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Note 50x</a> has a Dimensity 7300 Ultimate, the same chip class you find in Rs 45,000 phones. Games that choke on other phones at this price run properly. The screen is an LCD and the cameras are plain. For a student who plays, nothing else comes close.</p>\n<h2>Hot 50 Pro Plus: the screen</h2>\n<p>An AMOLED at 120Hz with 8GB for Rs 25,999. If films matter more than games, choose it over the Note 50x. The chip is fine for daily use.</p>\n<h2>Note 50s: the complete one</h2>\n<p>For Rs 35,999 you get speed, a 144Hz AMOLED, fast memory and storage, and a stabilised 64MP camera. It is our general recommendation between Rs 35,000 and 40,000. See how it compares in <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">phones under Rs 40,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Above Rs 40,000: be pickier</h2>\n<p>The Note Edge, GT 30 and Hot 70 Pro enter a range where Redmi, Realme, Motorola and Samsung are strong. The <a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-30\">GT 30</a> at Rs 46,999 is good for gaming with shoulder triggers on some versions. The Hot 70 Pro at Rs 52,999 with an LCD is hard to understand. The <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60\">Note 60</a> at Rs 54,999 competes with the Honor X9d, which has a much larger battery and tougher body.</p>\n<p>At Rs 63,000 to 85,000, the <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-60-pro\">Note 60 Pro</a>, GT 30 Pro and <a href=\"/products/infinix-gt-50-pro\">GT 50 Pro</a> offer fast chips, wireless charging and gaming features. The POCO F7 and X8 Pro at Rs 69,999 are faster or better supported. Buy the GT line only if you want its triggers, lights and magnetic cooler.</p>\n<h2>XOS: tidy it once</h2>\n<p>XOS has improved, and it still comes with many apps and notification channels. On the first evening:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Remove or disable the app store recommendations, games and browsers you will not use.</li>\n<li>Turn off Palm Store and Hot Apps notifications.</li>\n<li>Switch off lock-screen content.</li>\n<li>Keep the useful ones: game mode, social turbo, the IR remote, and XArena.</li>\n<li>Allow your chat apps to run in the background.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Updates</h2>\n<p>Entry models get little. Note and GT models are promised two or three Android versions and a few years of patches, depending on the phone. If you keep a phone for five years or do sensitive work on it, Samsung is the better choice.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Good in daylight, weak at night until the Note 50s and above. Infinix likes high-contrast colours, so choose a natural mode where it exists. Video on the cheaper models is limited to 1080p or 2K without stabilisation.</p>\n<h2>Charging</h2>\n<p>45W on the Note and some Hot models is generous, and the charger is in the box. Bypass charging on GT and some Note phones lets you play while plugged in without heating the battery. It is worth learning where the switch is.</p>\n<h2>Service and resale</h2>\n<p>Carlcare serves Infinix, Tecno and itel. There are centres in Kathmandu and several other cities. Parts are inexpensive. Resale is low, so plan to keep the phone or give it to someone in the family.</p>\n<h2>Official units</h2>\n<p>Check for the distributor\'s sticker and MDMS registration. Indian Infinix phones are common and may differ in memory or chip, even under the same name. The Note 50s in particular exists in 4G and 5G versions in different countries. Make sure the box matches the specification you read.</p>\n<h2>Infinix against its rivals</h2>\n<p><strong>Tecno:</strong> sister brand with more attention to design and cameras. Compare both, as prices cross.</p>\n<p><strong>Redmi:</strong> wider service, better resale, weaker chips at the bottom.</p>\n<p><strong>Realme:</strong> tougher, faster charging on some, slower chips under Rs 36,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Samsung:</strong> longer life, lower speed.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy Infinix</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Students with Rs 20,000 to 40,000 who want speed.</li>\n<li>Film lovers on a budget.</li>\n<li>People who change phones every two or three years.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Parents who will keep a phone for six years.</li>\n<li>Buyers far from a Carlcare centre.</li>\n<li>Anyone for whom night photos are important.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our Infinix shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 16,000:</strong> Smart 10 Plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Best chip for the money:</strong> Note 50x.</li>\n<li><strong>Best screen for the money:</strong> Hot 50 Pro Plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Best overall:</strong> Note 50s.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How to bargain</h2>\n<p>Infinix dealers often add earbuds, a watch or a power bank. These cost little and are sometimes worth having. Ask what the phone costs without them. Online brand days can be Rs 1,500 to 3,000 cheaper, and the price history on our pages shows whether an offer is new.</p>\n<h2>Final thought</h2>\n<p>Infinix rewards people who read one line of the sheet, the processor. The Note 50x and Note 50s are the reason the brand matters in Nepal. Buy those and you will feel clever. Buy a Smart or Hot at the wrong price and you will wonder what the fuss was about.</p>','gadgets/2026/infinix-note-60.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(57,20,'JBL Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','jbl-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>JBL is known for bass-heavy, fun-sounding earbuds and a strong presence in Nepali electronics shops. Its range here runs from the Rs 8,900 <a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-buds-2\">Wave Buds 2</a> to the Rs 28,999 <a href=\"/products/jbl-live-beam-3\">Live Beam 3</a>.</p>\n<h2>JBL earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-buds-2\">JBL Wave Buds 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 8,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-beam-2\">JBL Wave Beam 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 11,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-beam-2\">JBL Tune Beam 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 13,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-buds-2\">JBL Tune Buds 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 13,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-flex-2\">JBL Wave Flex 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 13,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-endurance-race-2\">JBL Endurance Race 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 14,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-flex-2\">JBL Tune Flex 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-sense-lite\">JBL Sense Lite</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 18,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-endurance-peak-4\">JBL Endurance Peak 4</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 20,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-soundgear-sense\">JBL Soundgear Sense</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 22,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-soundgear-clips\">JBL Soundgear Clips</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 23,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-live-flex-3\">JBL Live Flex 3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 28,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-live-beam-3\">JBL Live Beam 3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 28,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which JBL earbuds?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Entry level:</strong> the Wave series. Good bass for music and videos, but noise cancellation is basic.</li>\n<li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> the Tune series (Tune Buds 2, Tune Beam 2, Tune Flex 2) adds stronger ANC and a better app.</li>\n<li><strong>For the gym or running:</strong> the Endurance models hook securely over your ears and are built to handle sweat.</li>\n<li><strong>The best JBL:</strong> the Live Beam 3 and <a href=\"/products/jbl-live-flex-3\">Live Flex 3</a>, with LDAC, multipoint and good noise cancellation. The Beam 3\'s case even has a small touchscreen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>JBL\'s prices in Nepal are higher than Soundcore or Redmi for similar features, so you\'re partly paying for the sound signature and the brand. If you mostly want ANC on a budget, compare our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>JBL\'s line-up</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-buds-2\">JBL Wave Buds 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,900</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-beam-2\">JBL Wave Beam 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;11,499</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 40 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-beam-2\">JBL Tune Beam 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;13,000</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 48 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-endurance-race-2\">JBL Endurance Race 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,000</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 48 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-flex-2\">JBL Tune Flex 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,500</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 36 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-endurance-peak-4\">JBL Endurance Peak 4</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;20,000</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 48 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-soundgear-clips\">JBL Soundgear Clips</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;23,000</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 32 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-live-beam-3\">JBL Live Beam 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;28,999</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 36 hours (case); Qui...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Paying for a name that means something</h2>\n<p>JBL has been in sound for generations, and its consumer tuning is instantly recognisable: big bass, bright treble, plenty of energy. In Nepal its earbuds start at Rs 8,900, where other brands already offer noise cancelling, LDAC and long batteries. So you pay a premium for the sound signature, sturdy build and a brand whose warranty is real. Whether that is worth it depends on your ears.</p>\n<h2>Four families</h2>\n<p><strong>Wave</strong> is entry. <strong>Tune</strong> is the middle. <strong>Live</strong> is premium. <strong>Endurance</strong> is for sport. Within each, <strong>Buds</strong> are round, <strong>Beam</strong> have stems and sealed tips, <strong>Flex</strong> have stems and sit semi-open. The number is the generation. Once you know this, the list becomes readable: Tune Flex 2 is a mid-range, semi-open, second-generation pair.</p>\n<h2>Wave: Rs 8,900 to 13,500</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-buds-2\">Wave Buds 2</a> and <a href=\"/products/jbl-wave-beam-2\">Wave Beam 2</a> are solid with basic noise cancelling. At these prices the <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a> has more of everything. Buy a Wave if you have heard both and prefer JBL.</p>\n<h2>Tune: Rs 13,000 to 16,500</h2>\n<p>The Tune Beam 2 and <a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-flex-2\">Tune Flex 2</a> add better ANC and drivers, with 48 hours in total. The Flex is for people who dislike a tight seal, and it can be worn sealed or open with the tips provided. It is the most versatile of the range.</p>\n<h2>Endurance: the sport pair</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/jbl-endurance-race-2\">Endurance Race 2</a> at Rs 14,000 and <a href=\"/products/jbl-endurance-peak-4\">Peak 4</a> at Rs 20,000 are built for sweat and rain, with high water ratings. The Race uses a twist-lock fit, the Peak has ear hooks that will not move in a marathon. For gyms and trails they are among the best available here, and the strongest argument for JBL.</p>\n<h2>Sense Lite and Soundgear Clips: open designs</h2>\n<p>The Sense Lite hooks over the ear and the Soundgear Clips clip on. Both leave the canal free. They suit runners, cyclists in safe places and office workers. Huawei and CMF are the rivals.</p>\n<h2>Live: Rs 29,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/jbl-live-beam-3\">Live Beam 3</a> and Live Flex 3 come with a touchscreen on the case, adaptive ANC and LDAC. The case is fun: you can change settings without a phone. The sound is powerful and can be tuned. They compete with Galaxy Buds 4 and AirPods 4, which suit their own phones better, and with the Liberty 5 at Rs 17,499, which costs far less.</p>\n<h2>The JBL Headphones app</h2>\n<p>Equaliser, Personi-Fi hearing profile, ANC modes, find my buds and firmware. Use the equaliser to bring down the bass by a couple of steps. The music opens up.</p>\n<h2>Fakes: a serious problem</h2>\n<p>JBL is the most copied audio brand in Nepal. A Rs 2,000 \"JBL TWS\" in a market stall is always fake. Protect yourself:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Buy from an authorised seller, with a VAT bill.</li>\n<li>Check the serial number with JBL or Harman\'s verification.</li>\n<li>Open the JBL Headphones app. A fake will not connect.</li>\n<li>Look at printing, hinge and weight. Real cases feel dense.</li>\n<li>Be suspicious of any price far below our list.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Noise cancelling</h2>\n<p>Adequate on Wave, good on Tune, very good on Live. It is not at the level of Sony or Apple. TalkThru and Ambient Aware let in voices, and they sound natural on newer models.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Four to six microphones depending on model. Beam and Flex stems help. Outdoors they are average.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Ten to twelve hours without ANC, seven or eight with it. Cases add three or four charges. Ten minutes gives two to four hours. Wireless charging is available on Tune and Live in some versions.</p>\n<h2>Water resistance</h2>\n<p>From IPX2 for some cases to IP68 for sport models. JBL prints both bud and case ratings. Read them, since many people ruin the case and not the buds.</p>\n<h2>Fit</h2>\n<p>Three sizes of tip. The Endurance models include enhancers. Round Buds suit small ears better than stems for some people. Try.</p>\n<h2>With each phone</h2>\n<p>Works with all. Google Fast Pair on Android, AAC on iPhone, LDAC on Live. Multipoint on most newer models, and new ones support LE Audio and Auracast, which lets you listen to broadcasts from compatible JBL speakers and TVs.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Authorised distributors provide a year on genuine products, with replacement policies that vary. Keep the box. Single buds can sometimes be arranged.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy JBL</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bass lovers</strong> who have compared: Tune Beam 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Sport:</strong> Endurance Peak 4 or Race 2.</li>\n<li><strong>People who like semi-open comfort:</strong> Tune Flex 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Owners of JBL speakers:</strong> any with Auracast.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Value seekers: CMF, OnePlus, Soundcore, Redmi.</li>\n<li>People who want the quietest: Sony, Apple.</li>\n<li>Anyone buying from a pavement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Is it worth the premium?</h2>\n<p>Here is a test. Listen to a song you love on a Rs 8,999 CMF and a Rs 13,000 JBL in the same shop at the same volume. If the JBL makes you grin, buy it. Sound is personal, and a grin is worth Rs 4,000. If you cannot tell, keep the money.</p>\n<h2>Price and timing</h2>\n<p>JBL discounts well at Dashain, 11.11 and New Year, often 15 to 30 per cent on the previous generation. The first-generation Tune and Wave, when genuine and new, are good buys at such times.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For sport, JBL\'s Endurance line is easy to recommend. For everything else it is a matter of taste at a premium. Check that it is real, tame the bass a little, and you will have a pair that makes walking to work feel like a film. Other options are in <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(58,20,'JBL Headphones Price in Nepal (November 2026)','jbl-headphones-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>JBL\'s over-ear headphones in Nepal range from the Rs 9,499 <a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530c\">Tune 530C</a> to the premium Rs 39,999 <a href=\"/products/jbl-tour-one-m2\">Tour One M2</a>.</p>\n<h2>JBL headphones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530c\">JBL Tune 530C</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 9,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530bt\">JBL Tune 530BT</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 9,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-730bt\">JBL Tune 730BT</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 11,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-680nc\">JBL Tune 680NC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 15,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-780nc\">JBL Tune 780NC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 19,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tour-one-m2\">JBL TOUR ONE M2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Battery life on a budget:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530bt\">Tune 530BT</a> (Rs 9,500) lasts up to about 76 hours and supports multipoint. A week of commuting on one charge is realistic.</li>\n<li><strong>Noise cancellation:</strong> the Tune 680NC and <a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-780nc\">Tune 780NC</a> add ANC. The 780NC is the better choice for travel.</li>\n<li><strong>Premium:</strong> the Tour One M2 is JBL\'s flagship, with strong ANC and comfortable padding. It\'s up against Sony and Soundcore\'s best, so compare before you buy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 530C uses USB-C audio rather than Bluetooth, so check that your device supports it. More choices are in our <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-10000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 10,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">under Rs 30,000</a> guides.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>JBL headphones compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530c\">JBL Tune 530C</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,499</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530bt\">JBL Tune 530BT</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,500</td><td>Music: 76 hours; 2 hours, Quick charge: 5 min...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-730bt\">JBL Tune 730BT</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;11,000</td><td>Music: 76 hours; 2 hours, Quick charge: 5 min...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, LC3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-680nc\">JBL Tune 680NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;15,000</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 76 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-780nc\">JBL Tune 780NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;19,500</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 76 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tour-one-m2\">JBL TOUR ONE M2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>Music: 30 hours, With ANC on, 50 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Reading a JBL name</h2>\n<p>Tune is the affordable family, Live sits above it and Tour is the best. The first digit of the number tells the size: 5 and 6 are on-ear, 7 is over-ear. BT means Bluetooth without noise cancelling. NC means noise cancelling. C means a USB-C cable. So the Tune 730BT is an over-ear Bluetooth pair, the 680NC is an on-ear pair with noise cancelling. Once you know this, you can read the shelf.</p>\n<h2>Tune 530C: the one with a wire</h2>\n<p>At Rs 9,499 it costs the same as the Bluetooth version, which looks odd until you consider what a wire gives: no charging, no delay, no pairing, and sound that does not depend on a codec. For laptops, tablets and phones with USB-C audio, it is a practical tool. Teachers, editors and students in exam halls that forbid Bluetooth may prefer it.</p>\n<h2>Tune 530BT: light and long</h2>\n<p>At Rs 9,500, on-ear, foldable, with about 76 hours of battery. It is made for music while moving. On-ear pads press after an hour, so it is not for a working day.</p>\n<h2>Tune 730BT: the comfortable one</h2>\n<p>For Rs 11,000 you move to over-ear pads and the same battery. It is the better choice for study. Without ANC it relies on its pads, which block a fair amount. Compare it with the Soundcore Q20i at Rs 9,199, which has real noise cancelling for less, and the Space One at Rs 12,999.</p>\n<h2>Tune 680NC and 780NC</h2>\n<p>The 680NC at Rs 15,000 is on-ear with ANC, a strange combination: on-ear pads leak, so cancelling has more to do. The <a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-780nc\">780NC</a> at Rs 19,500 is over-ear with adaptive ANC, 50 hours and multipoint. It is the Tune to buy for travel and offices. The CMF Headphone Pro at Rs 13,999 and Soundcore Space One are cheaper rivals with similar quiet. JBL\'s answer is its lively sound.</p>\n<h2>Tour One M2</h2>\n<p>At Rs 39,999 the <a href=\"/products/jbl-tour-one-m2\">Tour One M2</a> is the premium pair: True Adaptive ANC, hi-res sound, excellent microphones, and comfortable pads. It sits between the Rs 27,000 Sony CH720N and the Rs 57,600 XM5. For calls and sound it is strong, and for silence the Sony is ahead. There is a newer M3 abroad, so ask for a discount on the M2.</p>\n<h2>The JBL sound</h2>\n<p>Bass first, then sparkle. It flatters pop, hip-hop, dohori with drums and films. For classical, ghazal and acoustic music it can be too much. In the app, reduce the low bands by two or three steps and it becomes balanced. The saved setting stays in the headphones.</p>\n<h2>App features</h2>\n<p>Equaliser, ANC control, ambient modes, VoiceAware to hear yourself in calls, and firmware updates. Personi-Fi on higher models shapes the sound by a hearing test. Turn off touch controls if you often adjust your hair.</p>\n<h2>Comfort and fit</h2>\n<p>Tune headphones are light. The clamp is firm at first and eases with time. People with glasses should choose the 730BT or 780NC. Headscarves and caps fit better with on-ear pairs.</p>\n<h2>Calls and work</h2>\n<p>Fine indoors. The Tour One M2 is the only one we would choose for a day of meetings. On Windows use the computer\'s microphone for your voice to keep music quality. Multipoint on the Bluetooth models handles laptop and phone.</p>\n<h2>Auracast and LE Audio</h2>\n<p>Newer Tune models support LE Audio and Auracast, for listening together from compatible sources. It is early, and few phones and TVs use it. Consider it a small bonus.</p>\n<h2>Fakes and how to spot them</h2>\n<p>JBL over-ear copies at Rs 1,200 to 2,500 are sold everywhere under names like \"JBL 950\". JBL never made most of them. A real pair has a serial number, an importer\'s sticker, proper printing and appears in the JBL Headphones app. If the box lists features such as an FM radio and memory card slot, it is fake.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Seventy-six hours is at half volume without ANC. Expect fifty in real life, still more than a week of use. Five minutes of charging gives about three hours. Batteries in headphones last many years if not left empty.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>Tune hinges are plastic. Fold them gently, carry them in a case and do not stretch the headband. Ear pads flake after two years of sweat. Replacements are cheap. The Tour comes with a case.</p>\n<h2>For gaming</h2>\n<p>These are not gaming headsets. JBL\'s Quantum line is, with boom microphones and 2.4GHz dongles. For a console, the wired 530C works only with USB-C sources, so check your controller.</p>\n<h2>For children</h2>\n<p>JBL makes children\'s models limited to 85 decibels. They are the right choice for a seven-year-old, and a Tune 530BT is not.</p>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>One year from authorised sellers. Ask who handles it and whether you will receive a replacement or a repair. Keep the box with the serial.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laptop user:</strong> Tune 530C.</li>\n<li><strong>Commuter with music:</strong> Tune 530BT.</li>\n<li><strong>Student:</strong> Tune 730BT, or Soundcore Q20i for ANC.</li>\n<li><strong>Traveller who loves bass:</strong> Tune 780NC.</li>\n<li><strong>Professional caller:</strong> Tour One M2 on discount.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with other brands</h2>\n<p><strong>Soundcore:</strong> cheaper ANC, better equaliser. <strong>Sony:</strong> better ANC at the top, calmer sound. <strong>CMF:</strong> best value near Rs 14,000. <strong>boAt:</strong> half the price without the refinement.</p>\n<h2>Prices and offers</h2>\n<p>JBL cuts prices at festivals and when a model number changes. Prices in our list are for official units. Online, choose the brand\'s store. A Tune 730BT at Rs 8,500 or 780NC at Rs 15,500 is a good day.</p>\n<h2>Looking after your hearing</h2>\n<p>JBL headphones go loud easily. Use the 85 decibel limit in your phone, take breaks, and do not use volume to cover traffic. That is what noise cancelling is for.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If you love the JBL sound, the 730BT and 780NC are the two to hear. If you are undecided, listen to a Soundcore or CMF beside them at the same volume before paying. Both lists are in <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-10000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 10,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(59,20,'Kick Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','kick-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Kick is a value earbud brand, and all four of its current models cost under Rs 3,000 in Nepal. Prices run from Rs 1,499 for the <a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x2-neo\">Phantom Buds X2 Neo</a> to Rs 2,599 for the <a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Phantom Buds X Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Kick earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x2-neo\">Kick Phantom Buds X2 Neo</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-elite\">Kick Phantom Buds X Elite</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x2\">Kick Phantom Buds X2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,026</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,599</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which to buy</h2>\n<p>If you can stretch to Rs 2,599, the <strong>Phantom Buds X Pro</strong> is the one to get. It\'s the only Kick pair here with noise cancellation and multipoint, and battery life is up to about 66 hours with the case. That\'s unusual at this price.</p>\n<p>The X2 Neo, X Elite and X2 are simple everyday earbuds for calls, YouTube and music on the bus. Don\'t expect much bass or noise blocking.</p>\n<p>At budget prices, fit and microphone quality vary a lot between brands. If calls matter to you, also look at the <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a> and the picks in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 3,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The four pairs</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x2-neo\">Kick Phantom Buds X2 Neo</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,499</td><td>–</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-elite\">Kick Phantom Buds X Elite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,999</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 2 hours playback</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x2\">Kick Phantom Buds X2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,026</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 100 hours (case); Qui...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,599</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 1.66 hours playbac...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A local favourite at pocket-money prices</h2>\n<p>Kick has become familiar in Nepal by selling earbuds between Rs 1,500 and Rs 2,600 that do what people ask at that price: pair quickly, last long, make a decent thump, and, in one case, cancel some noise. Its advantages are price, availability and a distributor close at hand. Nobody should expect the finish or longevity of brands that charge twice as much.</p>\n<h2>Phantom Buds X2 Neo: Rs 1,499</h2>\n<p>The cheapest. It is a basic pair for calls, YouTube and songs. If your alternative is an unbranded pair from a stall, this is better because it comes with a box, a name and a warranty. If you can add Rs 500, a Redmi Buds 5A has a better app and wider service.</p>\n<h2>Phantom Buds X Elite: Rs 1,999</h2>\n<p>A step up in battery and sound. It competes directly with the Redmi Buds 5A. Pick whichever fits your ears.</p>\n<h2>Phantom Buds X2: about Rs 2,000</h2>\n<p>Its headline is a very large case: 100 hours in total by the maker\'s figure, with eight in the buds. For people who travel, guard a shop at night or forget to charge, that is attractive. The case is bigger in a pocket.</p>\n<h2>Phantom Buds X Pro: Rs 2,599</h2>\n<p>The one that put Kick on our lists. Active noise cancelling and connection to two devices under Rs 3,000 is unusual. The cancelling is modest, as you would expect, and it takes the edge off a bus engine. Multipoint is more valuable still for anyone who works between a laptop and a phone. It leads our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 3,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to expect from sound</h2>\n<p>Strong bass, recessed voices, bright top. If there is an app or preset that reduces bass, use it. Otherwise use the equaliser in your phone\'s settings or music player. Choose \"vocal\" or lower the first two bands.</p>\n<h2>Fit decides the result</h2>\n<p>At this price half of the reviews that say \"no bass\" describe a poor seal. Try all the tips. If none works, Rs 300 foam tips will change your opinion.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Good enough indoors. Outside, people will ask you to repeat. Put one bud in, cup your hand if it is windy, or hold the phone.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>Game modes lower the delay for PUBG Mobile and Free Fire. They are fine for casual play. Wired earphones are still the best value for serious players.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling versus ENC</h2>\n<p>Boxes mix the two. ANC reduces what <em>you</em> hear. ENC reduces what your <em>caller</em> hears. Many cheap buds advertise ENC in large letters, and people think it is ANC. Among Kick\'s present models only the X Pro has the first. Check by turning it on near a fan.</p>\n<h2>Battery facts</h2>\n<p>Numbers such as 100 hours count the case. What matters on a given day is hours in the buds: five to eight, less with ANC. Quick charge gives about an hour and a half to two hours in ten minutes.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>Expect IPX4 or similar. Sweat and drizzle are fine. Dry the pins before charging, as corrosion is the commonest failure.</p>\n<h2>How long will they last?</h2>\n<p>A year to two years of daily use. The battery fades, the hinge loosens, one side goes quiet from wax. Clean the mesh monthly. At Rs 2,000, plan to replace and not repair.</p>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>Ask for six months or a year on the bill. Kick\'s local distribution makes replacement quicker than with imported brands. Keep the box and all tips. Do not open the buds.</p>\n<h2>Shopping online</h2>\n<p>Look for the official store. Read the one-star reviews first: if several say that one side stopped, ask the seller about replacement. Film the opening of the parcel.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy Kick</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Students with Rs 2,000 to 2,600.</li>\n<li>People who lose earbuds regularly.</li>\n<li>Anyone wanting multipoint for the lowest price.</li>\n<li>Night-shift workers who like the X2\'s battery.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Music lovers with Rs 5,000. The Nord Buds 4 are a different class.</li>\n<li>Professionals on calls.</li>\n<li>iPhone owners who want switching and find features.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with neighbours</h2>\n<p><strong>Redmi Buds 5A (Rs 1,999):</strong> steadier app and service. <strong>boAt Airdopes 212 (Rs 2,199):</strong> long battery, bass. <strong>Realme Buds T200x (Rs 2,999):</strong> better tuned ANC and app. <strong>Soundcore P20i (Rs 2,999):</strong> the best equaliser.</p>\n<p>Against these, the X Pro wins on features for money and the T200x on quality. If you can add Rs 400, hear both.</p>\n<h2>Getting more from them</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forget and re-pair if one side disconnects.</li>\n<li>Update firmware if an app is provided.</li>\n<li>Keep the case above 20 per cent.</li>\n<li>Use volume at 60 per cent or less.</li>\n<li>Store with the tips clean.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Children and hearing</h2>\n<p>Cheap buds go loud, and their bass invites it. Set a volume limit in the phone for children. Do not let them sleep in earbuds. Lithium batteries and pillows do not mix.</p>\n<h2>E-waste</h2>\n<p>Millions of cheap buds end in rivers. When yours die, give them to a shop or collector that takes batteries. If you find yourself buying a third pair in two years, the next one should be a better pair with a longer life. It costs less.</p>\n<h2>Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Do they work with an iPhone?</strong> Yes, as ordinary Bluetooth buds.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I use one bud?</strong> Yes, either side on most models.</p>\n<p><strong>Are they waterproof for swimming?</strong> No.</p>\n<p><strong>Do they support LDAC?</strong> No, and at this price it would not help.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Phantom Buds X Pro is the Kick to buy. The others are for tighter budgets or huge batteries. They are honest products at their price, as long as you do not ask them to be something else. If your budget grows, our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a> show what the next step brings.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(60,20,'Lenovo Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','lenovo-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Lenovo\'s Nepal range is huge, from basic IdeaPads around Rs 55,000 to the Legion Pro 7i at over Rs 7 lakh, and many models come in several configurations. Here\'s how the families divide up. <strong>IdeaPad</strong> and <strong>V-series</strong> are everyday and office laptops, <strong>ThinkPad</strong> and <strong>ThinkBook</strong> are for business, <strong>Yoga</strong> is the premium thin range, and <strong>LOQ</strong> and <strong>Legion</strong> are for gaming.</p>\n<h2>Lenovo laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ian8-2023-intel-n100\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IAN8 2023</a></td><td>Intel N100, 4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core i3-N305, 8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 67,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-v15-g6-2025\">Lenovo V15 G6 2025 (Intel N100)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 62,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-14amn8-2023\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 14AMN8 2023 (AMD Athlon 7120U)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 66,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-1-15amn7-2023-ryzen-5-7520u\">Lenovo IdeaPad 1 15AMN7 2023 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 80,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15iru8-intel-core-i3-1315u\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IRU8 (Intel Core i3-1315U)</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 81,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-v15-g4-iru-2023\">Lenovo V15 G4 IRU 2023 (Intel i3-1315U)</a></td><td>8+512</td><td>Rs 84,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-1-15iru7-2025-intel-core-i5-1335u\">Lenovo IdeaPad 1 15IRU7 2025 (Intel Core i5-1335U)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15irh10-2025-intel-core-i5-13420h\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IRH10 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core i5-13420H, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core i5-13420H, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,17,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core i7-13620H, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,36,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-2025-13th-gen-i5-13420h-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3i 14IRH10 2025 (Intel Core i5-13420H)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,01,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7-carbon-2022-i5-1240p-90hz\">Lenovo Yoga Slim 7 Carbon 13IAP7 2022 (Intel Core i5-1240P)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,10,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-5-2025-16irh10r-intel-core-5-210h\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 5 16IRH10R 2025 (Intel Core 5 210H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,15,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-v14-13th-gen-intel-core-i5-13420h\">Lenovo V14 G4 IRU 2023</a></td><td>13th Gen Intel Core i5-13420H, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,17,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core i7-13620H, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15irh10r-2025-intel-core-5-210h\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15IRH10R 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core 5 210H, 16GB/512GB</td