novo-legion-5-15iax10-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-255hx-rtx-5060\">Lenovo Legion 5 15IAX10 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 255HX, RTX 5060, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,84,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX, RTX 5070, 32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 5,39,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-thinkpad-l16-gen-2-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-255h\">Lenovo ThinkPad L16 Gen 2 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255H)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,89,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-pro-5-16iah10-2025\">Lenovo IdeaPad Pro 5 16IAH10 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 285H)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-thinkpad-x13-gen-6-2025\">Lenovo ThinkPad X13 Gen 6 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 255H)</a></td><td>32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-15irx10-intel-i7-13650hx-rtx-5050\">Lenovo LOQ 15 15IRX10 2025 (Intel Core i7-13650HX)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,31,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-15ahp11-2026-ryzen-7-250-rtx-5060\">Lenovo LOQ 15 15AHP11 2026 (Ryzen 7 250)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,45,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-7i-16iax10-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5060\">Lenovo Legion 7i 16IAX10 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-9-2-in-1-gen-10-2025\">Lenovo Yoga 9 2-in-1 Gen 10 (2025)</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 258V, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,60,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-9-14ill10-2025-core-ultra-7-258v\">Lenovo Yoga 9 Gen 5 14ILL10 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 3,69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5a-g11-15agp11-2026-amd-ryzen-ai-9-465-rtx-5060\">Lenovo Legion 5a G11 15AGP11 2026 (AMD Ryzen AI 9 465)</a></td><td>32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 4,76,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-pro-5i-16iax10-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5060\">Lenovo Legion Pro 5i 16IAX10 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 4,95,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-pro-5-16afr10-2025-ryzen-9-9955hx-rtx-5070\">Lenovo Legion Pro 5 16AFR10 2025 (Ryzen 9 9955HX)</a></td><td>32GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 5,23,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-2025\">Lenovo Legion Pro 7i (2025)</a></td><td>RTX 5080, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 7,73,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>RTX 5090, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 9,93,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-16iax10h-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5090\">Lenovo Legion Pro 7i 16IAX10H 2025</a></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX, RTX 5080, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 7,73,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX, RTX 5090, 32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 9,93,900</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Where we\'d start</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Students on a budget:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15irh10-2025-intel-core-i5-13420h\">IdeaPad Slim 3 15IRH10</a> uses a faster H-series Core i5. Pick a 16GB configuration.</li>\n<li><strong>Business:</strong> ThinkPads come with 3-year warranties through ONIN Infosys. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-thinkpad-e14-gen-7-core-ultra-5-225u\">ThinkPad E14 Gen 7</a> is a solid start.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaming value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">LOQ 15 with RTX 5050</a> at 100W is one of the best-value gaming laptops here.</li>\n<li><strong>High-end gaming:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-2025\">Legion Pro 7i</a> with an RTX 5080 has an OLED screen and top-tier cooling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avoid the cheapest IdeaPads with Intel N100 chips or Athlon processors unless the laptop is for very light use. More picks are in our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-150000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1,50,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>A selection from the range</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 15AHP10 2025 (Ryzen 7 8840HS)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,31,999</td><td>15.3 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1900 x 1200 pixe...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS (4nm), 8 cores / 16 thread...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-5i-gen-9-2024-core-ultra-5\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 5i Gen 9 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,900</td><td>14 inches, OLED, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels),...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 125H (Intel 4), 14 cores /...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 57 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>14-inch WUXGA OLED, 600 nits, 100% DCI-P3</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 226V (Intel Arc 130V graph...</td><td>16GB LPDDR5x-8533</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>15.6&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare,...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB...</td><td>1x 16GB SO-DIMM DDR5-5600</td><td>60Wh</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Lenovo Legion 5 (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,59,999</td><td>15.3-inch WUXGA IPS, 165Hz, 300 nits, 100% sR...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 260 (8 cores, up to 5.1GHz)</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-thinkpad-l16-gen-2-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-255h\">Lenovo ThinkPad L16 Gen 2 (Core Ultra 7 255H, 16GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,66,499</td><td>16&quot; WUXGA (1920x1200) Non-touch IPS 400nits A...</td><td>Core Ultra 7 255H 16 (6 P-core + 8 E-core + 2...</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>57Wh Rechargeable Li-ion Battery</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-16iax10h-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5090\">Lenovo Legion Pro 7i 16IAX10H 2025</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,73,900</td><td>16 inches, OLED, WQXGA (2560 x 1600 pixels),...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX (TSMC N3B), 24 cores...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 80 Wh; 400W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Six families worth knowing</h2>\n<p><strong>IdeaPad</strong> is for home and study. <strong>V series</strong> is cheap business. <strong>ThinkPad</strong> is proper business. <strong>Yoga</strong> is premium and thin. <strong>LOQ</strong> is entry gaming. <strong>Legion</strong> is serious gaming. Codes such as 15AHP10 give the size (15), the chip maker and platform (A for AMD, I for Intel), and the generation (10). IdeaPad 1, 3 and 5 are ranks: higher is better.</p>\n<h2>What Lenovo does best</h2>\n<p>Keyboards, value in gaming laptops, and a habit of putting strong processors into plain bodies. Two of our favourite machines in Nepal are Lenovos: the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">LOQ 15</a> for games and technical courses, and the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Yoga Slim 7i Aura</a> for work.</p>\n<h2>IdeaPad: the best and the worst</h2>\n<p>The same family contains the <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-n100-review-nepal\">Slim 3 with an N100 and 4GB</a>, which we ask people not to buy, and the <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-ryzen-7-8840hs-review-nepal\">Slim 3 with a Ryzen 7 8840HS</a>, which is the cheapest powerful laptop in the country. Read the processor and the memory, always. The <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-ideapad-1-ryzen-5-7520u-review-nepal\">IdeaPad 1</a> is a basic study tool.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-5i-gen-9-2024-core-ultra-5\">IdeaPad Slim 5i</a> with a Core Ultra 5 125H, 16GB and OLED at about Rs 1,30,000 is an attractive middle choice: better screen than the Slim 3, more affordable than the Yoga.</p>\n<h2>V series: simple office machines</h2>\n<p>V14 and V15 laptops are plain and dependable. The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-v14-13th-gen-intel-core-i5-13420h\">V14 with a Core i5-13420H and 16GB</a> at about Rs 1,18,000 is fast for its class. One listing of a V15 with a Ryzen 5 7520U at Rs 1,41,900 is far too expensive for the chip. Prices of imported stock can be strange.</p>\n<h2>ThinkPad</h2>\n<p>For typists, engineers in the field and companies, ThinkPads remain the standard. The keyboard, the TrackPoint and the tolerance for abuse are worth paying for. New ones are expensive here, for instance the <a href=\"/products/lenovo-thinkpad-l16-gen-2-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-255h\">L16 Gen 2</a> at about Rs 2,66,000. Used T14, T14s and X1 Carbons at Rs 55,000 to 1,00,000 are where the value lies. Check the battery, the supervisor password and the keyboard.</p>\n<h2>Yoga</h2>\n<p>Thin, light, OLED. The Slim 7i Aura is our choice at Rs 1,65,000, and <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition-review-nepal\">our review</a> explains its limits. The Yoga 9 convertible at about Rs 3,70,000 is gorgeous, with a soundbar hinge and 32GB, for people who want one beautiful object.</p>\n<h2>LOQ: the first gaming laptop to consider</h2>\n<p>The LOQ 15 with an RTX 5050 at its full 100W and an accurate screen at Rs 1,94,999 sets the standard. <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-loq-15-2025-review-nepal\">Read our review</a>. Other LOQs in shops range from an ageing RTX 2050 at Rs 1,81,900, which makes no sense, to an i7-13650HX with an RTX 5050 at Rs 3,31,900, which makes even less. There is one LOQ to buy, and it is the cheaper one.</p>\n<h2>Legion</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-legion-5-15ahp10\">Legion 5</a> at about Rs 2,60,000 has an RTX 5060 at high power with good cooling. Legion Pro models go up to the RTX 5090 at nearly Rs 7,74,000. For heat and noise, Legion is among the best of the gaming brands.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which Lenovo</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>School:</strong> IdeaPad Slim 3 with Core i3 and 8GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Bachelor student:</strong> V14 or IdeaPad Slim 5i with 16GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Computing:</strong> IdeaPad Slim 3 Ryzen 7 8840HS.</li>\n<li><strong>Office and travel:</strong> Yoga Slim 7i Aura.</li>\n<li><strong>Engineering, architecture, gaming:</strong> LOQ 15, then Legion 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Writers and coders on a budget:</strong> used ThinkPad.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Traps to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>4GB or 128GB in anything.</li>\n<li>TN screens on IdeaPad Slim 3 and V models. Tilt the lid and look.</li>\n<li>Old RTX 2050 laptops at new prices.</li>\n<li>Soldered 8GB in slim machines.</li>\n<li>Names without codes. Ask for the full code and look it up in Lenovo\'s PSREF documents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>PSREF: a gift to buyers</h2>\n<p>Lenovo publishes a specification reference for each model, with memory slots, screen options, battery sizes and ports. Search the model code with the word PSREF. It ends arguments in shops.</p>\n<h2>Vantage</h2>\n<p>Lenovo Vantage sets conservation mode (charging to 80 per cent), rapid charge, fan modes and updates. On Legion and LOQ, Fn+Q changes the performance mode. Purple or red is for games plugged in, white is for class.</p>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>Lenovo\'s warranty can be checked by serial number, with country and dates. Official units in Nepal are covered by authorised service. Imported ones may be covered only where they were sold. Some warranties can be upgraded online within the period, sometimes cheaply. Batteries are covered for a year.</p>\n<h2>Upgrades</h2>\n<p>LOQ and Legion have two memory slots and two M.2 slots. IdeaPads vary. Yogas are fixed. Note that some Lenovos use short 2242 SSDs, so check the length before buying a drive.</p>\n<h2>Lenovo compared</h2>\n<p><strong>Acer</strong> is cheaper in plain machines. <strong>Asus</strong> has more OLED and more models. <strong>HP and Dell</strong> have stronger consumer service networks for offices. Lenovo leads in keyboards and gaming value.</p>\n<h2>Price behaviour</h2>\n<p>Gaming models follow graphics card prices, and IdeaPads follow memory. Both rose in 2026. Older LOQ stock with RTX 4050 sometimes falls below Rs 1,65,000 and is worth catching. Watch the <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a>.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Lenovo is the brand where the code matters most. Choose the right one and you have the best laptop in your budget. Choose the wrong one and you have an N100. Now you know how to tell them apart. Compare across brands in our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">laptop guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-2025.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(61,20,'MSI Laptop Price in Nepal (November 2026)','msi-laptop-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>MSI is best known for gaming laptops, but its Nepal range also includes the Modern series of office laptops and the Prestige and Summit lines for creators and business users. Prices run from Rs 92,000 to Rs 6,25,000 for the <a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-2025\">Vector 16 HX AI</a>.</p>\n<h2>MSI laptops price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2022-i5-1235u\">MSI Modern 14 C12M 2022 (12th Gen i5-1235U)</a></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 92,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">MSI Modern 14 F1MG 2024</a></td><td>Intel Core 5 120U, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 93,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core 7 150U, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,25,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-15-2023-i5-1335u\">MSI Modern 15 B13M 2023</a></td><td>Intel Core i5-1335U, 8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,05,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core i7-1355U, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,27,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-15-h-2023-i5-13420h\">MSI Modern 15 H B13M 2023</a></td><td>Intel Core i5-13420H, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,13,050</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Intel Core i7-13700H, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,30,150</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-summit-e13-ai-evo-a1mtg-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-155h\">MSI Summit E13 AI Evo A1MTG 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 155H)</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-summit-e16-flip-evo-2023-i7-1360p-165hz\">MSI Summit E16 Flip Evo A11UCT 2023 (Intel Core i7-1360P)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,95,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-katana-15-2023-i7-13620h-rtx-4060\">MSI Katana 15 B13VEK 2023 (Intel Core i7-13620H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,09,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-pulse-15-b13v-i7-13700h-rtx-4060-360hz\">MSI Pulse 15 B13V 2023 (Intel Core i7-13700H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,23,250</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-prestige-16-studio-2023-i7-13700h-rtx-4050\">MSI Prestige 16 Studio A13V 2023 (Intel Core i7-13700H)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,35,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-stealth-16-ai-studio-intel-core-ultra-7-155h-rtx-4060\">MSI Stealth 16 AI Studio A1VFG 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 7 155H)</a></td><td>32GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,60,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-vector-gp68-hx-2023-i9-13980hx-rtx-4070\">MSI Vector GP68 HX 13VG 2023 (Intel Core i9-13980HX)</a></td><td>32GB/2TB</td><td>Rs 3,47,700</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-a2xwjg-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-275hx-rtx-5090\">MSI Vector 16 HX AI A2XWJG 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX)</a></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 6,10,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-2025\">MSI Vector 16 HX AI (2025)</a></td><td>Core Ultra 7 275HX · RTX 5090, 16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 6,25,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What to look at</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Office work:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">Modern 14 F1MG</a> is good value in its Core 7 150U, 16GB, 1TB configuration. MSI\'s Modern laptops are simple but well built.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry gaming:</strong> the Katana and Cyborg lines. Check the GPU wattage, because some newer Cyborg models limit the RTX 5050 to 45W.</li>\n<li><strong>For creators:</strong> the Prestige 16 Studio and Stealth 16 AI Studio combine good screens with dedicated GPUs in slimmer bodies.</li>\n<li><strong>High end:</strong> the Vector 16 HX AI with an RTX 5090 at 175W is among the most affordable RTX 5090 laptops in Nepal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some MSI listings here are older 2022–2023 models. They\'re fine if discounted, but compare them with newer laptops at the same price in our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-200000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 2,00,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>MSI\'s laptops in Nepal</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">MSI Modern 14 F1MG 2024</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;93,000</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pixe...</td><td>Intel Core 7 150U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 th...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Po 46.8 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-15-h-2023-i5-13420h\">MSI Modern 15 H B13M 2023</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,13,050</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>Intel Core i7-13420H (Intel 7), 8 cores / 12...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Po 53.8 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-venture-14-ai-a1mg-core-ultra-5-125h\">MSI Venture 14 AI A1MG (Core Ultra 5 125H, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,19,999</td><td>14&quot; Full HD+ (1920 x 1200), IPS</td><td>Intel® Core™ Ultra 5-125H (14 Cores, 18 Threa...</td><td>8GB DDR5 5600MHz (Max. 96GB, 2 Slots</td><td>4-Cell 55.2Whr Battery (Whr)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-cyborg-15-b2rwekg-core-7-240h-rtx-5050\">MSI Cyborg 15 B2RWEKG (Core 7 240H, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,14,999</td><td>15.6&quot; Full HD (1920 x 1080), IPS, 144Hz</td><td>Intel® Core 7-240H (10 Cores, 16 Threads, 24M...</td><td>16GB DDR5 5600MHz (Max. 64GB, 2 Slots)</td><td>4-Cell 55.2Whr Battery (Whr)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-pulse-15-b13v-i7-13700h-rtx-4060-360hz\">MSI Pulse 15 B13V 2023 (Intel Core i7-13700H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,23,250</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>Intel Core i7-13700H (Intel 7), 14 cores / 20...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Po 90 Wh; 240W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-stealth-16-ai-studio-intel-core-ultra-7-155h-rtx-4060\">MSI Stealth 16 AI Studio A1VFG 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 7 155H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,60,000</td><td>16 inches, IPS LCD, QHD+ (2560 x 1600 pixels)...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155H (Intel 4), 16 cores /...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Po 99.9 Wh; 240W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-2025\">MSI Vector 16 HX AI (2025)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,25,000</td><td>16-inch QHD+ IPS, 240Hz</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 275HX</td><td>16GB</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A gaming company with office clothes</h2>\n<p>MSI is known for graphics cards and motherboards, and its laptops follow from that: strong components, bold designs, and gaming at the centre. It also sells simple work laptops, some of which are among the best value in the country. The network of service is smaller than HP\'s or Asus\'s, and that is the main thing to weigh.</p>\n<h2>Names</h2>\n<p><strong>Modern</strong> and <strong>Venture</strong> are for study and work. <strong>Prestige</strong> and <strong>Summit</strong> are premium business. <strong>Cyborg</strong> and <strong>Katana</strong> are entry gaming. <strong>Pulse</strong> and <strong>Sword</strong> are the middle. <strong>Stealth</strong> is thin gaming. <strong>Vector</strong>, <strong>Raider</strong> and <strong>Titan</strong> are the heavyweights. <strong>Creator</strong> and <strong>Studio</strong> are for content work.</p>\n<h2>Modern 14: our favourite MSI</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">Modern 14 F1MG with a Core 7 150U</a>, 16GB and 1TB at Rs 93,000 is hard to match. It weighs little and handles office work and code quickly. The screen is ordinary and the battery below 47Wh is average. Be careful: an older Modern 14 with a Core i5-1235U, 8GB and a 39Wh battery sits beside it at Rs 92,000. A thousand rupees separate a bargain from a poor buy.</p>\n<h2>Modern 15 and 15 H</h2>\n<p>The Modern 15 with 8GB at Rs 1,05,000 is overpriced. The <a href=\"/products/msi-modern-15-h-2023-i5-13420h\">Modern 15 H</a> with an H-class processor and 16GB at about Rs 1,13,000 is stronger than most laptops at that price, good for computing students who work at a desk.</p>\n<h2>Venture 14 AI</h2>\n<p>A newer design with a Core Ultra 5 125H and a 16:10 screen at about Rs 1,20,000. The base has 8GB, so check for a slot. With 16GB it is a sensible modern laptop.</p>\n<h2>Summit and Prestige</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/msi-summit-e16-flip-evo-2023-i7-1360p-165hz\">Summit E16 Flip</a> at Rs 1,95,000 is a large convertible with a 165Hz QHD+ screen and an 82Wh battery, an unusual and useful thing for people who present and sketch. The Summit E13 is small and neat. They are older stock, so negotiate and check the battery.</p>\n<h2>Cyborg and Katana</h2>\n<p>Cyborg 15 models with an RTX 5050 cost about Rs 2,15,000. The translucent plastic is fun, the graphics power is low: Cyborgs have traditionally limited the chip to 45W or so. The Lenovo LOQ with the same chip at 100W is cheaper and much faster. Ask for the TGP before looking at anything else. The Katana 15 with an RTX 4060 at Rs 2,09,000 is older and, if the wattage is above 100W, quicker in games.</p>\n<h2>Pulse</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/msi-pulse-15-b13v-i7-13700h-rtx-4060-360hz\">Pulse 15</a> with an i7-13700H, RTX 4060 and a 90Wh battery at Rs 2,23,250 is an interesting older machine, with a 360Hz panel for competitive players. Compare it with the Nitro V 16 AI and Legion 5, which have newer chips.</p>\n<h2>Stealth 16 Studio</h2>\n<p>At Rs 2,60,000 the <a href=\"/products/msi-stealth-16-ai-studio-intel-core-ultra-7-155h-rtx-4060\">Stealth 16</a> has 32GB, a QHD+ screen, a 99.9Wh battery and an RTX 4060 in a slim body. For creators who move, it is far cheaper than a Zephyrus and worth a serious look.</p>\n<h2>Vector</h2>\n<p>Vector models are desktop replacements. At Rs 6,10,000 to 6,25,000 the <a href=\"/products/msi-vector-16-hx-ai-2025\">Vector 16 HX</a> is the lowest price for an RTX 5090 laptop in our catalogue. It is heavy, loud and extremely fast. It ships with 16GB in some versions, which is odd for such a machine, so budget for 32GB.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which MSI</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Student, office, travel:</strong> Modern 14 with the Core 7.</li>\n<li><strong>Computing at a desk:</strong> Modern 15 H.</li>\n<li><strong>Presenter:</strong> Summit E16 Flip on discount.</li>\n<li><strong>Creator on the move:</strong> Stealth 16.</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum power:</strong> Vector.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry gaming:</strong> consider Lenovo and HP first.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The wattage lesson</h2>\n<p>MSI publishes the maximum graphics power on its specification pages. Use that. A thin or cheap model can carry an impressive chip at half power. In a shop, Nvidia Control Panel, System Information shows it.</p>\n<h2>MSI Center</h2>\n<p>It sets performance modes, fan speed and battery mode: balanced, for mobility or best for battery at 60 per cent. Use the last at a desk. On gaming models, Cooler Boost runs the fans at full. It is loud and useful for rendering.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>MSI works through a distributor with service in Kathmandu. Parts for popular models are available, others are ordered. Ask about the warranty period, whether it is MSI\'s or the shop\'s, and register the product online. Keep the box.</p>\n<h2>Build and keyboard</h2>\n<p>Modern models are light with some flex. Gaming models are plastic with metal lids. Keyboards are good, some by SteelSeries on higher models. Webcams are ordinary.</p>\n<h2>Upgrades</h2>\n<p>Gaming MSIs usually have two memory slots and two M.2 slots. Modern models have some soldered memory with one slot, or are fixed. Check the exact code. Opening the case may break a factory seal, so ask the service centre first.</p>\n<h2>Old stock: good and bad</h2>\n<p>MSI shelves in Nepal hold models from 2022 to 2025. Old stock with strong specifications at a discount is a fine purchase. Ask for the manufacture date, run a battery report, and look for a warranty that starts on the day you buy.</p>\n<h2>MSI against others</h2>\n<p><strong>Asus:</strong> wider range, stronger service. <strong>Lenovo:</strong> better-value gaming. <strong>Acer:</strong> cheaper entry gaming. <strong>HP:</strong> more normal-looking. MSI wins with the Modern 14 F1MG, with big batteries on Stealth and Pulse, and at the very top for price.</p>\n<h2>Before you pay</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Full model code.</li>\n<li>Memory: size, sticks, slots.</li>\n<li>Graphics power.</li>\n<li>Battery report.</li>\n<li>Warranty in writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Most buyers should look at one MSI, the Modern 14 with the Core 7 150U. Creators should look at the Stealth 16. People who need an RTX 5090 already know what they are doing. For the rest, the alternatives in our <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptop guide</a> are easier to service and better value.</p>',NULL,1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(62,20,'Motorola Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','motorola-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Motorola phones are popular with people who want clean, close-to-stock Android without extra apps, plus solid batteries. The Nepal range runs from the Rs 30,699 <a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Moto G35 5G</a> to the Rs 79,999 <a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Edge 70 Fusion</a>.</p>\n<h2>Motorola phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Motorola Moto G35 5G</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 30,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g06-power\">Motorola Moto G06 Power</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 31,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">Motorola Moto G57 Power</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 37,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Motorola Moto G96 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Motorola Edge 50 Fusion</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 46,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Motorola Edge 70 Fusion</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Moto?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Moto G96 5G</a> (Rs 44,999) has a 120Hz AMOLED screen, OIS on the main camera and IP68 water resistance, which is rare at this price.</li>\n<li><strong>Battery first:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">Moto G57 Power</a> (Rs 37,499) has a big battery and the fastest chip in its group. Note that it ships without a charger.</li>\n<li><strong>The best Motorola here:</strong> the Edge 70 Fusion, with a 7,000mAh battery, a 120Hz AMOLED and a Snapdragon 7s Gen 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The G06 Power and G35 are basic phones. Look at Samsung\'s and Xiaomi\'s options at the same price in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">phones under Rs 40,000</a> guide before you decide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>What is available</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-moto-g35-5g\">Motorola Moto G35 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;30,699</td><td>6.72 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Unisoc T760 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g06-power\">Motorola Moto G06 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;31,999</td><td>6.88 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 450 nits (typ), 6...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G81 Ultra (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 18W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">Motorola Moto G57 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;37,499</td><td>6.72 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1050 nits (HBM)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Motorola Moto G96 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.67 inches P-OLED, 144Hz, 1600 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4 nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-ion 5500 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Motorola Edge 50 Fusion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;46,499</td><td>6.7 inches P-OLED, 144Hz, 1600 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 68W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-70-fusion\">Motorola Edge 70 Fusion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 5, 200 nits (peak)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 68W Fast...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Motorola\'s place in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Motorola, owned by Lenovo, has a small but loyal following here. People come for the software, which is close to Google\'s, and for designs with soft vegan-leather backs and curved screens that feel dearer than they are. The official range is short. Many more models arrive from India, and that is where care is needed.</p>\n<h2>G or Edge?</h2>\n<p><strong>Moto G</strong> phones cover Rs 30,000 to 45,000. \"Power\" means a larger battery. Two-digit numbers show rank and not age: the G96 is the best G and the G06 the simplest. <strong>Edge</strong> phones are slim and more expensive, and \"Fusion\" is the affordable Edge.</p>\n<h2>G35 5G and G06 Power</h2>\n<p>Both hover around Rs 31,000 and neither is easy to recommend. The G06 Power has a 7,000mAh battery with a basic chip. The G35 has 5G, 5,000mAh and 18W charging. At the same price a Redmi 15 4G, Spark 40 Pro or Redmi 17 5G gives more. Choose them only for the clean software.</p>\n<h2>G57 Power: the sensible one</h2>\n<p>For Rs 37,499 the <a href=\"/products/moto-g57-power\">G57 Power</a> offers a modern Snapdragon 6s Gen 4, 7,000mAh, 8GB and Android 16. Its screen is an LCD and its charger is not in the box. It is the best Motorola for value, and it appears in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">phones under Rs 40,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>G96: the beautiful one</h2>\n<p>A curved 144Hz pOLED, a light body and a stabilised Sony camera at Rs 44,999. <a href=\"/reviews/moto-g96-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains why we enjoy it and why people who travel all day should not buy it.</p>\n<h2>Edge 50 Fusion: the older bargain</h2>\n<p>At Rs 46,499 the <a href=\"/products/motorola-edge-50-fusion\">Edge 50 Fusion</a> shares the chip of the G96 and adds 68W charging and IP68. It is older, so ask about remaining updates. If you find it at Rs 40,000, it is excellent.</p>\n<h2>Edge 70 Fusion</h2>\n<p>At Rs 79,999 it moves to a Snapdragon 7s Gen 4, 12GB and 7,000mAh in a slim body. It competes with the OnePlus Nord 6, Honor 600 and POCO X8 Pro Max, which are faster or better with photos. Its attraction is elegance and endurance together. Look for an offer.</p>\n<h2>The software experience</h2>\n<p>Motorola phones feel light. The launcher is simple, notifications are respected, and useful gestures are built in: chop twice for the torch, twist for the camera, lift to unlock. Smart Connect links the phone with a PC, tablet or TV and is better than most similar tools. There are a few preinstalled apps and a lock-screen feed on cheaper models that you can turn off.</p>\n<h2>The update problem</h2>\n<p>Here is the weak spot. Many G phones get one or two Android versions and three to four years of security patches, and they arrive late. Edge models do better. If you keep a phone five years or use it for sensitive work, Samsung and others are safer. Read the promise for your model on Motorola\'s site.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Power models last two days. Others are ordinary. Charging ranges from 18 to 68W. Motorola uses USB PD widely, so common chargers work. Boxes in Nepal do not always include one.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Motorola\'s cameras have improved, with Sony sensors and stabilisation from the G96 upwards. Colour is natural, portraits are decent, and nights are average. The front cameras are sharp and good for video calls.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>Vegan leather does not show fingerprints or crack, and it gives grip. Curved glass is fragile at the edge. Several models carry IP64 to IP68 ratings and some pass military tests, but ratings differ by region, so check the exact model.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>This is the question to ask first. Official service is limited, mostly to Kathmandu. A curved pOLED can take weeks to arrive and cost a third of the phone. Outside the valley, choose a brand with a nearby centre, or accept the risk and use a good case.</p>\n<h2>Indian imports</h2>\n<p>Because Motorola sells a great variety online in India, border shops carry models that have no official presence here. They are cheap and tempting. There is no Nepali warranty, the IMEI needs to be registered, and returns are difficult. If you buy one, verify MDMS before paying, and check that the charger and bill are included.</p>\n<h2>Resale</h2>\n<p>Low. Few buyers look for a used Motorola. Keep it for three years or more, or buy it already discounted.</p>\n<h2>How Motorola compares</h2>\n<p><strong>With Nothing:</strong> both are clean. Nothing has longer support and a zoom camera, Motorola is lighter and cheaper.</p>\n<p><strong>With Samsung:</strong> Motorola gives nicer hardware and Samsung gives years and service.</p>\n<p><strong>With Redmi:</strong> Redmi gives more hardware and clutter.</p>\n<p><strong>With OnePlus:</strong> OnePlus is faster and dearer.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy a Motorola</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who value simple software and a light phone.</li>\n<li>Those who live in Kathmandu and take care of their things.</li>\n<li>Users who connect the phone to a PC or TV.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Buyers who need service in other cities.</li>\n<li>Long-term owners.</li>\n<li>Gamers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Value:</strong> G57 Power.</li>\n<li><strong>Design:</strong> G96.</li>\n<li><strong>Older bargain:</strong> Edge 50 Fusion at a discount.</li>\n<li><strong>Premium feel:</strong> Edge 70 Fusion on offer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>First-day settings</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Open the Moto app and turn on the gestures you like.</li>\n<li>Switch off the lock-screen feed.</li>\n<li>Set the refresh rate to auto.</li>\n<li>Turn on battery care or overcharge protection.</li>\n<li>Check for updates until none remain.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Motorola makes some of the most pleasant phones to hold and use in the middle of the market, and supports them less than it should. If three years is your horizon and you live near service, the G57 Power and G96 are both good buys. If not, admire them in the shop and take the Samsung.</p>','gadgets/2026/motorola-edge-70-fusion.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(63,20,'Noise Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','noise-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Noise is an Indian brand that\'s popular in Nepal for cheap, stylish earbuds. Its current range here is small and tightly priced, from the Rs 2,699 <a href=\"/products/noise-buds-trance-2\">Buds Trance 2</a> to the Rs 3,999 <a href=\"/products/noise-aura-buds\">Aura Buds</a>.</p>\n<h2>Noise earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-buds-trance-2\">Noise Buds Trance 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-buds-n1\">Noise Buds N1</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,799</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-mvp-102-gaming\">Noise MVP 102 Gaming</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,399</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-aura-buds\">Noise Aura Buds</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What to expect</h2>\n<p>All four models are everyday earbuds for calls, YouTube and music on the go. The <strong>Aura Buds</strong> have the most eye-catching design, the <a href=\"/products/noise-buds-n1\">Buds N1</a> are a plain, reliable choice, and the <strong>MVP 102 Gaming</strong> buds have a low-latency mode, so mobile game sound doesn\'t lag behind the action.</p>\n<p>The gap to better brands is small at this price. Before you buy, compare them with the <a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a> (Rs 2,599), which adds noise cancellation, and with the other picks in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a> guide. Noise cancellation and a good mic matter more day to day than the look of the case.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Noise models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-buds-trance-2\">Noise Buds Trance 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,699</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 120 minutes playba...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-buds-n1\">Noise Buds N1</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,799</td><td>–</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-mvp-102-gaming\">Noise MVP 102 Gaming</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,399</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 150 minutes playba...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/noise-aura-buds\">Noise Aura Buds</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,999</td><td>45 minutes (buds) / 1.5 hours (case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>An Indian value brand in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Noise is one of India\'s big sellers of cheap wearables, and its earbuds arrive here through a distributor and, in larger numbers, across the border. They are stylish, inexpensive and aimed at young buyers. Four models have a steady price in our listing, from Rs 2,699 to Rs 3,999. Despite the name, most do not cancel noise.</p>\n<h2>Buds Trance 2 and Buds N1</h2>\n<p>At Rs 2,699 and 2,799 both are simple pairs with long battery, quick charge and strong bass. They sit in the hardest part of the market. The Kick Phantom Buds X Pro at Rs 2,599 has ANC and multipoint, and the Redmi Buds 5A at Rs 1,999 does the basics for less. Choose a Noise here for its look and fit.</p>\n<h2>MVP 102 Gaming</h2>\n<p>At Rs 3,399, with a low-latency mode and gaming style. The delay is better than that of ordinary buds and still higher than a wire. If you play on a phone in a hostel room and dislike cables, it is fun. Competitive players should use wired earphones or a USB-C dongle headset.</p>\n<h2>Aura Buds</h2>\n<p>At Rs 3,999 it is the most polished of the four, with a nicer case and fuller sound. For Rs 1,000 more the OnePlus Nord Buds 4 have real ANC, a better app and better sound. That is a strong argument.</p>\n<h2>How to choose among them</h2>\n<p>Put each in your ear and shake your head. Make a call. Play a song with vocals. If two sound alike, take the one with longer battery. There is not much else between them.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>Noise tunes for bass and loudness. Dance music is lively, and voices are a little behind. Use your phone\'s equaliser to bring forward the middle. Do not push the volume to the top: distortion begins early.</p>\n<h2>ENC, ANC and labels</h2>\n<p>Environmental noise cancellation helps your caller. Active noise cancellation helps you. Read the box carefully. If ANC is not written, it is not there. Labels such as \"hyper sync\" and \"instacharge\" describe quick pairing and quick charging.</p>\n<h2>App and firmware</h2>\n<p>NoiseFit Audio or similar apps support some models with equaliser presets and controls. They ask for permissions that are not all needed. If yours has no app, controls are fixed, so learn them on the first day.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Around five to seven hours in the buds and 30 to 60 with the case. Ten minutes of charge gives two hours or more. The case charges by USB-C on current models. Older stock may use micro USB, so look.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>IPX4 to IPX5 covers sweat and light rain. Dry before charging.</p>\n<h2>Fit</h2>\n<p>Three pairs of tips. Stems are comfortable for most. For small ears, the round ones are better. A seal is worth more than any feature.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and the border</h2>\n<p>Official Nepali units come with a distributor\'s sticker and a warranty of six to twelve months. Many Noise products here were bought in India, where their warranty applies and where the online price is lower. They are fine as long as you know that a fault means a trip. Ask the seller which it is, and expect a lower price for an Indian unit.</p>\n<h2>Registration</h2>\n<p>Noise asks buyers to register online for warranty. Use the serial number on the box. For units sold in Nepal, follow the distributor\'s instructions and keep the bill.</p>\n<h2>Compared with others near Rs 3,000</h2>\n<p><strong>Realme Buds T200x:</strong> ANC and multipoint at Rs 2,999. The strongest rival. <strong>Soundcore P20i:</strong> best app. <strong>boAt Airdopes 212:</strong> similar character. <strong>Kick X Pro:</strong> most features. <strong>Redmi Buds 6 Play:</strong> cheap and light.</p>\n<p>On features, Noise is behind. On design, some will prefer it.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy Noise</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who like the design and have tried the fit.</li>\n<li>Casual mobile gamers: MVP 102.</li>\n<li>Anyone with a discount that brings them near Rs 2,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Commuters who want quiet.</li>\n<li>Laptop users who need multipoint.</li>\n<li>People who call for work.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to check in the shop</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Seal, sticker and serial number.</li>\n<li>Both sides play and charge.</li>\n<li>The microphone: record a voice note.</li>\n<li>Case hinge and magnet.</li>\n<li>USB-C port.</li>\n<li>Return or replacement terms in writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Solving common problems</h2>\n<p><strong>One side silent:</strong> put both in the case, forget the device on the phone, reset the buds by holding the case button as the manual says, and pair again.</p>\n<p><strong>Low volume:</strong> clean the mesh with a soft dry brush. Check the media volume and the phone\'s hearing protection limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Disconnecting:</strong> carry the phone on the same side as the main bud, and turn off battery saving for Bluetooth.</p>\n<p><strong>Not charging:</strong> wipe the gold pins with a dry cloth.</p>\n<h2>Looking after them</h2>\n<p>Keep them in the case, away from coins and water bottles. Charge the case weekly. Do not leave it in a parked vehicle. Clean the tips with a damp cloth.</p>\n<h2>Sound for children</h2>\n<p>Loud cheap buds can harm young ears quickly. Use a limit of 85 decibels on the phone, or buy on-ear children\'s headphones.</p>\n<h2>Noise also makes watches</h2>\n<p>ColorFit watches from the same brand are sold here. They follow the same idea: good looks, calls, modest tracking. If you want a matching set, ask for a bundle price. For tracking, Xiaomi and Huawei are better, as our <a href=\"/guides/smartwatch-buying-guide-nepal-2024\">smartwatch guide</a> explains.</p>\n<h2>Value over time</h2>\n<p>A Rs 2,700 pair that lasts 18 months costs Rs 150 a month. A Rs 4,999 pair that lasts three years costs under Rs 140 and sounds better every day. That is why we so often suggest saving for the next step.</p>\n<h2>Price watch</h2>\n<p>Noise products are discounted constantly. Compare with online brand stores on the day. If a shop\'s price is above our listing, ask for a match.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Noise makes pleasant, fashionable earbuds in a crowded field, and at their listed prices others do more. If one of them fits you perfectly and costs Rs 500 less than its rivals, enjoy it. If not, our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 3,000</a> list the stronger choices.</p>','gadgets/2026/noise-aura-buds.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(64,20,'Nothing Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','nothing-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Nothing\'s earbuds in Nepal are sold under its cheaper <strong>CMF</strong> sub-brand, and they\'re some of the best value around. You get modern features like noise cancellation and multipoint without paying flagship prices. The range runs from Rs 5,999 for the <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2a\">CMF Buds 2a</a> to Rs 14,999 for the <a href=\"/products/cmf-clip-pro\">CMF Clip Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Nothing earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2a\">CMF Buds 2a</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2\">CMF Buds 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 8,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-pro-2\">CMF Buds Pro 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 9,599</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-clip-pro\">CMF Clip Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 14,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best all-rounder:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a> (Rs 8,999) has noise cancellation, LDAC for higher-quality audio on Android, and multipoint, so it switches between your phone and laptop.</li>\n<li><strong>On a tighter budget:</strong> the Buds 2a and <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2\">Buds 2</a> cover the basics well, with ANC included.</li>\n<li><strong>Open-ear comfort:</strong> the Clip Pro clips onto your ear instead of sealing it, so you can still hear traffic and conversations. It\'s good for walking and long wear, but weaker for blocking noise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CMF earbuds work with any phone through the Nothing X app. For other brands at similar prices, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Nothing and CMF earbuds</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2a\">CMF Buds 2a</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,999</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 35.5 hours (case), Wi...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2\">CMF Buds 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,499</td><td>Music: 13.5 hours (buds) / 55 hours (case); Q...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,999</td><td>Music: 14 hours (buds) / 61.5 hours (case), W...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-pro-2\">CMF Buds Pro 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,599</td><td>Music: 11 hours (buds) / 43 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-ear-3a\">Nothing Ear (3a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;13,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 42 hours (case); 70...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-ear-a\">Nothing Ear (a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>Music: 9.5 hours (buds) / 42.5 hours (case);...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-clip-pro\">CMF Clip Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 32.5 hours (case), W...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>One company, two labels</h2>\n<p>CMF by Nothing is the affordable label, and Nothing is the dearer one with the transparent design. They use the same app and similar engineering, and at present the CMF models are so good that the Nothing ones have trouble explaining themselves. That is the short version of this page.</p>\n<h2>CMF Buds 2a: Rs 5,999</h2>\n<p>ANC, a tidy design and good battery. It faces the OnePlus Nord Buds 4 at Rs 4,999 and Soundcore R60i NC at Rs 5,499. All three are good, and the CMF\'s app and look are its edge.</p>\n<h2>CMF Buds 2: Rs 7,499</h2>\n<p>Longer battery, stronger noise cancelling and better water resistance. Fine, and only Rs 1,500 from the next one.</p>\n<h2>CMF Buds 2 Plus: Rs 8,999</h2>\n<p>The one to buy. LDAC, strong adaptive ANC, a hearing test to personalise sound, multipoint, 14 hours in the buds and more than sixty with the case. We have put it first in <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 10,000</a>, and nothing has displaced it.</p>\n<h2>CMF Buds Pro 2: Rs 9,599</h2>\n<p>Two drivers per side and a dial on the case. Livelier sound, shorter battery. If you enjoy music more than you dislike charging, choose it. The dial is a better control than any touch pad.</p>\n<h2>Nothing Ear (a) and Ear (3a): Rs 14,000 to 15,000</h2>\n<p>Transparent stems, nicer cases and balanced sound. The Ear (a) is the older, at Rs 14,999, the 3a newer at Rs 13,999, so the choice between them is easy unless you love yellow. Against the Buds 2 Plus you pay Rs 5,000 for design and a small step in refinement.</p>\n<h2>CMF Clip Pro: Rs 14,999</h2>\n<p>An open clip design that leaves the ear free. For walkers, parents and desk workers who want awareness. It costs half of Huawei\'s FreeClip. Bass is light, as in all clips. Try the fit on your ear shape.</p>\n<h2>Nothing X: the app</h2>\n<p>Clean and fast, with equaliser, ANC strength, control mapping, low-latency mode, find my buds and firmware. On Nothing and CMF phones the controls are inside the system settings. ChatGPT integration on some models lets you ask questions by a pinch, with a Nothing phone and an internet connection. It is a curiosity.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>CMF is tuned with strong but controlled bass. The personal sound test on the Plus helps more than presets. With LDAC on, use a good source or leave it off to save battery.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling</h2>\n<p>For Rs 9,000 it is impressive: buses become a murmur, offices calmer. High-pitched sounds and voices pass more than on Sony or Apple. Adaptive mode is good, and fixed high is stronger on a bus.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Better than average in the class, with wind handling on the Plus and Pro 2. In a quiet room people will not know that you are on earbuds.</p>\n<h2>Fit and comfort</h2>\n<p>Light, with three tip sizes. The stems are short. The Pro 2 is slightly heavier. For sport they are secure enough for running, and for intense workouts consider hooks.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>From IP54 to IP55 for buds, with lower or no rating for cases. Sweat and drizzle are fine.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>USB-C only on most, with no wireless charging under Rs 13,000. Ten minutes gives several hours. The 2 Plus is the champion.</p>\n<h2>Compatibility</h2>\n<p>Any phone. Fast Pair on Android, AAC on iPhone. Multipoint is reliable. On iPhone, use the Nothing X app from the App Store.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>An authorised distributor provides a year on official units. Indian units are common with Indian warranty. Look for the sticker. Single buds are sometimes available through service. Ask for the price.</p>\n<h2>Copies</h2>\n<p>Less common than for JBL and Apple, but transparent-stem fakes exist. The Nothing X app will not accept them.</p>\n<h2>Which to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best overall:</strong> CMF Buds 2 Plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Music and fun:</strong> CMF Buds Pro 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Tight budget:</strong> Buds 2a, or the Nord Buds 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Open ear:</strong> CMF Clip Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Design lovers:</strong> Nothing Ear (3a).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with rivals</h2>\n<p><strong>Realme Buds Air 8 (Rs 8,499):</strong> similar battery, slightly weaker ANC. <strong>Soundcore R60i NC (Rs 5,499):</strong> most of it for less. <strong>Redmi Buds 8 (Rs 10,499):</strong> good with Xiaomi phones. <strong>Soundcore Liberty 5 (Rs 17,499):</strong> better ANC and extras. <strong>Samsung Buds 3 FE (Rs 19,999):</strong> for Galaxy owners.</p>\n<h2>Who should look elsewhere</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>iPhone owners who want automatic switching: AirPods.</li>\n<li>Galaxy owners who want Samsung\'s codec: Galaxy Buds.</li>\n<li>Frequent flyers who need maximum quiet: Sony or AirPods Pro.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Set-up in five steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Install Nothing X and update.</li>\n<li>Run the fit and hearing tests where available.</li>\n<li>Map a double pinch to play and pause, a long pinch to ANC.</li>\n<li>Turn on multipoint and pair the laptop.</li>\n<li>Choose adaptive ANC for daily use.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Headphones too</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/cmf-headphone-pro\">CMF Headphone Pro</a> at Rs 13,999 and <a href=\"/products/nothing-headphone-a\">Nothing Headphone (a)</a> at Rs 26,999 follow the same logic: CMF for value, Nothing for design. They appear in <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Price and timing</h2>\n<p>CMF prices drop by Rs 500 to 1,500 in sales. When a Buds 3 generation appears, the present ones should fall further. At Rs 7,500 the 2 Plus would be impossible to ignore.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<p>Clean the mesh monthly, dry after sweat, charge the case weekly, and keep the dial of the Pro 2 free from dust.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If you came here to buy Nothing earbuds, buy the CMF Buds 2 Plus. They cost less, last longer and do the important things as well. Let your heart choose the transparent ones if it insists. It is only money, and they are pretty.</p>','gadgets/2026/cmf-buds-2-plus.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(65,20,'Nothing Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','nothing-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Nothing\'s phones are easy to spot thanks to their see-through backs and Glyph lights, but there\'s more to them than looks. The software is clean and fast, and the prices are sensible. The Nepal range runs from Rs 54,999 for the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">Phone 3a Lite</a> to Rs 1,09,999 for the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3\">Phone (3)</a> and <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a-pro\">Phone 4a Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Nothing phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">Nothing Phone 3a Lite</a></td><td>8/128GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4b\">Nothing Phone 4b</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3\">Nothing Phone (3)</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,09,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a-pro\">Nothing Phone 4a Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,09,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Nothing phone?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The value pick:</strong> the Phone 3a Lite has a 120Hz AMOLED screen and a Dimensity 7300 Pro, a smooth everyday phone with Nothing\'s design.</li>\n<li><strong>A camera with zoom:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Phone 4a</a> is one of the cheapest phones in Nepal with a periscope zoom. Compare it in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a> guide.</li>\n<li><strong>Flagship speed:</strong> the Phone (3), with a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4, IP68 and 16GB/512GB, is the most powerful Nothing phone here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nothing promises several years of updates on these models, which is reassuring at this price. The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4b\">Phone 4b</a> sits between the Lite and the 4a if you want something in the middle.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Nothing phones side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">Nothing Phone 3a Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;54,999</td><td>6.77 inches Super AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (ty...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Pro (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4b\">Nothing Phone 4b</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;64,999</td><td>6.77 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 12...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4 nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone (4a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.78-inch AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass 7i</td><td>Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>5,080mAh; 50W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a-pro\">Nothing Phone 4a Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,09,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 16...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4 nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 5080 mAh, non-removable; 50W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3\">Nothing Phone (3)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,09,999</td><td>6.67-inch FHD+ AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass 7...</td><td>Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>5,150mAh; 65W wired, 15W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What makes a Nothing phone different</h2>\n<p>Two things. The back, with its transparent look and pattern of lights, and the software, which is calm, monochrome and consistent. In a market where phones are hard to tell apart, that has been enough to build a following. Underneath are ordinary mid-range parts, chosen sensibly. You pay a little for design, and unlike a coloured glass back, the design includes the way the phone behaves.</p>\n<h2>Which model is which</h2>\n<p>Nothing\'s numbers have become complicated. <strong>3a Lite</strong> is the cheapest. <strong>4b</strong> is a simpler 4-series phone. <strong>4a</strong> is the main model. <strong>4a Pro</strong> is the larger version with better materials. <strong>Phone 3</strong> is the flagship from the previous year. A higher number does not mean a more expensive phone: the Phone 3 and 4a Pro cost the same today.</p>\n<h2>3a Lite: Rs 54,999</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3a-lite\">3a Lite</a> gives you the software and a decent chip with an average battery. At this price, rivals such as the Honor X9d, Realme 14 5G and Redmi Note 15 Pro have more hardware. Choose it only if Nothing OS is what you want.</p>\n<h2>4b: Rs 64,999</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4b\">4b</a> costs Rs 5,000 less than the 4a and gives up the zoom camera. In our view that is the wrong place to save. Add the Rs 5,000, or look at the OnePlus Nord CE5.</p>\n<h2>4a: the one to buy</h2>\n<p>At Rs 69,999 the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">4a</a> has something nobody else offers for the money: a 50MP periscope with 3.5x optical zoom. Portraits at the right distance, stages, birds, signs across the street. Add the clean software and it becomes the most interesting phone under Rs 70,000 for people who take pictures. <a href=\"/reviews/nothing-phone-4a-review-nepal\">Our review</a> notes the average battery and 50W charging.</p>\n<h2>4a Pro: Rs 1,09,999</h2>\n<p>Price increases have been unkind to the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a-pro\">4a Pro</a>. It has a better build, a larger screen and a faster chip than the 4a, and it stands beside a POCO F8 Pro and OnePlus 15R with flagship silicon. Fans of the design may not care. Everyone else should.</p>\n<h2>Phone 3: Rs 1,09,999</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-3\">Phone 3</a> has a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4, wireless charging, three 50MP cameras and a tiny round display on the back called the Glyph Matrix. It is a better phone than the 4a Pro at the same price. Its odd camera arrangement divides opinion. If you want a premium Nothing, this is it.</p>\n<h2>Nothing OS</h2>\n<p>It is near to plain Android with its own typeface, icons and widgets. There are few unwanted apps, though the company has added some suggestions on cheaper models that can be removed. The Essential Key and Essential Space save screenshots and voice notes and try to organise them. Some people find it useful, some never press it. Updates run to several Android versions and up to six years of patches on recent models, depending on the phone. Check yours.</p>\n<h2>Are the lights useful?</h2>\n<p>More than you expect, for a small number of things. The phone can lie face down and still tell you who is calling, show a timer, a delivery\'s progress or the charging level. The fill light is kind to faces in close video. After a month most people use two patterns. They are charming, and they draw attention in public.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>About 5,000 to 5,200mAh across the range, in a market moving to 7,000. A day of mixed use is fine. Long travel days need a top-up. Charging is moderate, and the charger is usually not in the box. Any good USB-C PD charger with PPS works, a practical advantage over brands with private standards.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Nothing\'s processing is natural, sometimes a little dark, with pleasing skin. The telephoto on the 4a and 4a Pro is the star. Ultra wides are ordinary at 8MP on the cheaper ones. Video is steady at 4K30. Black and white presets are lovely for street pictures in Asan.</p>\n<h2>Repairs and service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Nothing is sold through a distributor with service in Kathmandu. Screens and transparent backs must be ordered for some models. Ask about availability and cost. Use a clear case, or you will hide what you paid for. A frame scratch on the transparent back cannot be polished away.</p>\n<h2>Official and imported units</h2>\n<p>Indian units are common, as the brand is popular there. They are a few thousand rupees cheaper, with Indian warranty. Check MDMS, the sticker and the bill. The IMEI is also printed on the SIM tray area in some models.</p>\n<h2>Who will love it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People tired of cluttered phones.</li>\n<li>Designers, students and anyone who enjoys objects.</li>\n<li>Portrait photographers with Rs 70,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who will not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Heavy gamers.</li>\n<li>People who need two-day batteries.</li>\n<li>Buyers outside Kathmandu without access to service.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CMF: the cheaper sister</h2>\n<p>CMF by Nothing makes earbuds, watches and phones with the same software at lower prices. The <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a> and <a href=\"/products/cmf-watch-3-pro\">Watch 3 Pro</a> integrate neatly with a Nothing phone, with controls in the settings. If you own the phone, they are natural companions.</p>\n<h2>Compared with its rivals at Rs 70,000</h2>\n<p><strong>POCO F7:</strong> much faster, bigger battery, no zoom. <strong>Redmi Note 17 Pro Max:</strong> twice the battery. <strong>vivo V70 FE:</strong> slim, 200MP, wide service. <strong>Galaxy A37:</strong> longest support. Only the Nothing has a periscope.</p>\n<h2>Resale and longevity</h2>\n<p>Resale is fair among enthusiasts and weak in general shops. With long security support and tidy software, a Nothing phone ages gracefully. A new battery in year three will be the main need.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Prices have mostly gone up this year. Discounts come at festivals and when a new number is announced. If the 4a falls to Rs 62,000 to 65,000, it is an easy yes.</p>\n<h2>Our advice</h2>\n<p>Buy the 4a. If you want luxury, buy the Phone 3 and not the 4a Pro. If neither appeals when you hold it, the whole idea is not for you, and you will be happier with a phone that spends its money on battery. Try it with your own photos before you decide.</p>','gadgets/2026/nothing-phone-3.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(66,20,'OPPO Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','oppo-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>OPPO has a big presence in Nepal, with phones from the Rs 22,999 <a href=\"/products/oppo-a5\">A5</a> to the Rs 2,49,999 <a href=\"/products/oppo-find-n5\">Find N5</a> foldable. The <strong>A series</strong> covers budget phones, the <strong>Reno</strong> series is mid-range with a focus on portrait cameras, and <strong>Find</strong> is the flagship range.</p>\n<h2>OPPO phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a5\">OPPO A5</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 22,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 27,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 30,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6x\">OPPO A6x</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 27,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 32,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6k-4g\">OPPO A6k 4G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 38,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 43,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6\">OPPO A6</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 40,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 46,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6-pro-4g\">OPPO A6 Pro 4G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 51,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6s-pro\">OPPO A6s Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 56,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-15-f\">OPPO Reno 15F</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 63,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-15-5g\">OPPO Reno 15</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16f-5g\">OPPO Reno 16F</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16-5g\">OPPO Reno 16</a></td><td>12GB - 256GB - Phone Only</td><td>Rs 1,09,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB - 256GB - With OPPO Bubble</td><td>Rs 1,19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,35,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,42,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 1,58,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-n5\">Oppo Find N5</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,49,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Worth a look</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A tough phone on a budget:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/oppo-a6\">OPPO A6</a> (from Rs 40,999) has a 7,000mAh battery and IP68 water resistance, a rare combination at this price.</li>\n<li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16-5g\">Reno 16</a> and <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16f-5g\">Reno 16F</a> are good for people who take a lot of portrait and social photos. The 16F\'s 7,000mAh battery is a highlight.</li>\n<li><strong>Flagship:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">Find X9 Pro</a> (Rs 1,25,999) pairs a Dimensity 9500 with a 7,500mAh battery, and costs less than most rival flagships.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cheapest A-series models (A6c, A6x) come with as little as 4GB/64GB, so aim for 128GB of storage. The new <a href=\"/news/oppo-reno-17-launched-8000mah-battery\">Reno 17</a> has launched in China and is expected here later.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Where each series sits</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a5\">OPPO A5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;22,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 850 nits (typ), 10...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 1 (11nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 9...</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6\">OPPO A6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;40,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 1...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 6300 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6-pro-4g\">OPPO A6 Pro 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;51,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 14...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 (6 nm) CPU</td><td>8GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-15-f\">OPPO Reno 15F</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;63,999</td><td>6.59 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 14...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 1 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16-5g\">OPPO Reno 16</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,09,999</td><td>6.32 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 18...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 6700 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPE...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,25,999</td><td>6.78 inches LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>Si-Ca 7500 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-n5\">Oppo Find N5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,49,999</td><td>Inner: 8.12 inches (Foldable), Cover: 6.62 in...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 5600 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A, Reno, Find</h2>\n<p><strong>A</strong> phones are for endurance and toughness at modest prices. <strong>Reno</strong> phones are for style and people pictures. <strong>Find X</strong> is the flagship, and <strong>Find N</strong> folds. Letters after the number, such as c, x, k and s, mark small variations that differ by market. Some of them are the same phone with another battery or charger, so read the sheet and not the name.</p>\n<h2>The A series: look at two things</h2>\n<p>With OPPO\'s cheaper phones, check storage and charging speed. The <a href=\"/products/oppo-a5\">A5</a> at about Rs 23,000 has a Snapdragon chip and 45W charging, and is better than it looks. The <a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">A6c</a> at Rs 24,999 has a huge battery with 64GB and 15W. The A6x models at about Rs 28,000 fall between. If we were choosing one for a parent, it would be the A5 in the larger memory version.</p>\n<p>Higher up, the <a href=\"/products/oppo-a6\">A6</a> at Rs 40,999 has 5G-class silicon with 7,000mAh, and the <a href=\"/products/oppo-a6-pro-4g\">A6 Pro 4G</a> at Rs 51,999 adds an AMOLED, 80W charging and strong water resistance, held back by a 4G chip that others sell at Rs 25,000. At that price the Honor X9d or Realme 14 5G are better rounded.</p>\n<h2>The Reno puzzle</h2>\n<p>Reno phones are attractive, and their prices confuse. The <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-15-f\">Reno 15 F</a> at Rs 63,999 has a 2022 chip. <a href=\"/reviews/oppo-reno-15-f-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains why the sheet and the advertisements disagree. The Reno 15 5G and Reno 16F both sit at Rs 89,999. The first has a faster Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 and 12GB, and the second is newer. The <a href=\"/products/oppo-reno-16-5g\">Reno 16</a> at Rs 1,09,999 is remarkable for one thing: a 6.32-inch screen with a 6,700mAh battery, making it one of the very few small phones with real endurance.</p>\n<p>And an older model, the Reno 11 Pro, is still listed at about Rs 63,000 from remaining stock. Check its update status before buying.</p>\n<h2>Find X9 Pro: the odd bargain</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,25,999, OPPO\'s true flagship costs only Rs 16,000 more than a Reno 16. For that you move to a Dimensity 9500, a large main sensor, a high-resolution telephoto and 7,500mAh. If you can reach it, there is no reason to buy a Reno above Rs 1 lakh unless you want a small phone. We put it first in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-150000-nepal\">phones under Rs 1,50,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Find N5</h2>\n<p>At Rs 2,49,999 the <a href=\"/products/oppo-find-n5\">Find N5</a> is one of the thinnest book-style foldables, with a wide and useful outer screen. Hinges have matured, but repairs have not become cheap. Ask for the price of both screens in writing. For service in many cities, Samsung\'s Fold is easier to live with.</p>\n<h2>ColorOS</h2>\n<p>OPPO\'s software is among the smoothest on Android. Animations stay fluid when the phone is busy, the private safe and app lock are useful, and file sharing with iPhones and Macs through a companion app works well. Two things need attention on the first day: turn off the recommendation page and lock-screen magazine, and remove the bundled games. Update promises are moderate on A and Reno, and good on Find.</p>\n<h2>SUPERVOOC and chargers</h2>\n<p>OPPO, Realme and OnePlus share a family of chargers. An 80W brick from one usually works with the others, which is convenient in a household. With ordinary PD chargers OPPO phones charge at lower speeds. Keep the cable that came with it, since the high speeds need a special one. Genuine replacements cost Rs 2,500 to 5,000. Cheap copies are dangerous.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Reno phones are tuned for faces. The result is bright, smooth and ready for social media. If it looks too smooth, lower the retouch level. Autofocus on the front camera, found on the better models, is worth having. For zoom, only the Reno 16 and Find models have proper telephoto lenses. Choose those if you photograph stages, children at play or mountains.</p>\n<h2>Toughness</h2>\n<p>OPPO has put real effort into water and drop protection on the A6 line, with ratings up to IP69 on some models. These are laboratory results and not a warranty. Still, for riders and kitchen workers they are welcome.</p>\n<h2>Buying and bargaining</h2>\n<p>OPPO sells through a large dealer network. Board prices have a margin for negotiation, and exchange bonuses are common. A Reno six months after launch can be had for 10 to 15 per cent less than its list price. Ask for the cash price with no gifts. Then decide if the gifts are worth more.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Service centres in the major cities keep parts for A and Reno models, with published prices for screens and batteries. Find models may wait for parts. A battery replacement in the third year costs a few thousand rupees and is the best money you can spend on an ageing OPPO.</p>\n<h2>Official units</h2>\n<p>Look for the distributor\'s sticker, check the IMEI in MDMS and keep the bill. Indian units have Indian warranty. Chinese Find phones run a different ColorOS with Google services that must be enabled, and they do not receive global updates.</p>\n<h2>OPPO compared</h2>\n<p><strong>With vivo:</strong> cousins in style. Choose by the phone in front of you. <strong>With Realme:</strong> same charging family, faster chips for less, plainer cameras. <strong>With Samsung:</strong> OPPO offers battery and charging, and Samsung longevity and resale. <strong>With OnePlus:</strong> shared software roots. OnePlus gives more speed, OPPO more camera and toughness.</p>\n<h2>Our OPPO shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 25,000:</strong> A5.</li>\n<li><strong>Long battery, small budget:</strong> A6c with 128GB, if found.</li>\n<li><strong>Small phone with stamina:</strong> Reno 16, on discount.</li>\n<li><strong>Best OPPO for the money:</strong> Find X9 Pro.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What is next</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/news/oppo-reno-17-launched-8000mah-battery\">Reno 17</a> has been launched in China with 8,000mAh and three 50MP cameras. It may reach Nepal in some form next year. Until then it is a good reason to ask for a better price on the 15 and 16.</p>\n<h2>In a sentence</h2>\n<p>Buy OPPO at the bottom for battery and charging, at the top for the Find X9 Pro, and in the middle only with a discount.</p>','gadgets/2026/oppo-find-x9-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(67,20,'OnePlus Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','oneplus-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>OnePlus phones in Nepal offer a lot of speed for the money, with big batteries and fast charging. The range runs from the Rs 59,999 <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">Nord CE5</a> to the Rs 1,89,999 <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15\">OnePlus 15</a>.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-5\">OnePlus Nord 5</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 73,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE6</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">OnePlus Nord 6</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 89,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 98,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,06,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">OnePlus 13s</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15\">OnePlus 15</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,89,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which OnePlus?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value in the range:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a> (from Rs 98,499) has flagship-level performance for under a lakh. For most people, it\'s the one to buy.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget OnePlus:</strong> the Nord CE5 has a 7,100mAh battery and a Dimensity 8350 Apex, which is plenty fast for under Rs 60,000. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">Nord CE6</a> is the newer model.</li>\n<li><strong>The flagship:</strong> the OnePlus 15, with a Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 and a 7,300mAh battery, rivals Samsung\'s best on speed and battery life.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">OnePlus 13s</a> is a compact phone with 16GB/512GB, a good fit if you prefer a smaller handset.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>All the current models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>6.77 inches Fluid AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (ty...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8350 Apex (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 7100 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-5\">OnePlus Nord 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;73,999</td><td>6.83 inches Swift AMOLED, 144Hz, 800 nits (ty...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 6800mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC F...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">OnePlus Nord CE6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ), 18...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>7, 500mAh, non-removable; 80W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">OnePlus Nord 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>6.78-inch 1.5K AMOLED, 165Hz, 1,800 nits HBM,...</td><td>Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,500mAh; 80W wired, 27W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;98,499</td><td>6.83-inch AMOLED, 165Hz, Dolby Vision, HDR10+</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,400mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">OnePlus 13s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,19,999</td><td>6.32 inches LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 5850 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15\">OnePlus 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,89,999</td><td>6.78-inch AMOLED, LTPO 1–120Hz (up to 165Hz i...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,300mAh; up to 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A short list is a virtue</h2>\n<p>OnePlus sells seven phones in Nepal at present. You can understand the whole brand in five minutes, and none of the seven is bad. That cannot be said of makers with thirty. The range runs from about Rs 60,000 to 1,90,000, and it stands for three things: speed, smooth software and big batteries. Cameras are good and rarely the best. Service is the weak point.</p>\n<h2>Reading the names</h2>\n<p><strong>Nord</strong> is the mid-range. <strong>CE</strong> is its cheaper branch. <strong>R</strong> is a flagship with a simpler camera. <strong>S</strong> is the compact one. The plain number is the full flagship. An odd feature this year is that a newer CE costs more than an older Nord, so read the chip as well as the number.</p>\n<h2>Nord CE5: balance at Rs 60,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">CE5</a> pairs a quick Dimensity 8350 Apex with 7,100mAh and 80W charging. Nothing about it is weak. It is our general recommendation at Rs 60,000 for people who are not chasing one particular feature.</p>\n<h2>Nord 5 or Nord CE6?</h2>\n<p>They cost nearly the same. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-5\">Nord 5</a> has a faster chip, a sharper screen and a superb 50MP selfie camera with autofocus. The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-6\">CE6</a> has a larger 7,500mAh battery, newer software and a slower chip. Choose the Nord 5 for games and video calls, the CE6 for endurance. <a href=\"/reviews/oneplus-nord-5-review-nepal\">Our Nord 5 review</a> covers the missing charger, which also matters.</p>\n<h2>Nord 6: the sweet spot</h2>\n<p>At Rs 89,999 the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">Nord 6</a> has a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4, a 165Hz display and 7,500mAh. It is close to the 15R in every way you can feel, and cheaper. For most people who like OnePlus, it is the one.</p>\n<h2>15R: nearly a flagship</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">15R</a> at about Rs 98,500 adds the Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 and 12GB as standard. Buy it if you play long sessions or keep a phone for five years. It leads our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-100000-nepal\">phones under Rs 1 lakh</a>.</p>\n<h2>13s: for small hands</h2>\n<p>With a 6.32-inch screen, a Snapdragon 8 Elite, 16GB and 5,850mAh, the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">13s</a> at Rs 1,19,999 is a rare thing. It has a telephoto and no ultra wide. If you have been waiting for a small powerful Android, look at it before a Galaxy S26.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus 15</h2>\n<p>The full flagship at Rs 1,89,999, with a 7,300mAh battery, the newest chip and three 50MP cameras. <a href=\"/reviews/oneplus-15-review-nepal\">Our review</a> finds it the best Android for stamina and speed, and behind vivo and Xiaomi for photography. At that price, those matter.</p>\n<h2>OxygenOS</h2>\n<p>OnePlus built its name on software, and it is still the reason many people stay. It is fast, quiet and free of most clutter. The Plus Key on recent models can open the camera, recorder, torch or an AI note tool. Updates run to four Android versions and six years of patches on recent mid and upper models. Check the promise for yours.</p>\n<h2>Chargers: read this before leaving the shop</h2>\n<p>OnePlus has begun leaving the charger out of some boxes. SUPERVOOC chargers from OPPO and Realme work. A genuine 80W brick costs Rs 2,500 to 4,000. A normal PD charger works more slowly. Add it to the price when comparing.</p>\n<h2>Service in Nepal</h2>\n<p>There is official service in Kathmandu through the distributor, and limited coverage elsewhere. AMOLED screens on these phones are expensive. If you live far from the capital, ask for the cost and waiting time of a screen before buying. Independent shops can handle batteries and ports for older models.</p>\n<h2>Indian units</h2>\n<p>OnePlus is very popular in India, so imported units are everywhere, usually Rs 4,000 to 10,000 cheaper. They carry Indian warranty only. In the past some OnePlus screens developed green lines, and Indian customers had free replacements that were not available elsewhere. An official Nepali unit with a local warranty is worth the difference on a phone you plan to keep. Check MDMS in every case.</p>\n<h2>Cameras, in practice</h2>\n<p>In daylight all models take pleasing photos. At night the better sensors in the 15R and 15 pull ahead. The 8MP ultra wide on Nord and R models is the weakest part, so step back instead of switching lens. Portraits look natural at 2x. Video is stable at 4K60 on most.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>High refresh rates, good cooling and steady frame rates make OnePlus a favourite among mobile players. The 15R and Nord 6 support high frame-rate modes in several games. For tournament-level play, dedicated gaming phones with fans are better, and for value the POCO F7 is cheaper.</p>\n<h2>Compared with others</h2>\n<p><strong>POCO:</strong> more speed and storage per rupee, busier software. <strong>Samsung:</strong> better service and resale, smaller batteries. <strong>Nothing:</strong> similar clean software, more style, better zoom at Rs 70,000, weaker batteries. <strong>Realme and OPPO:</strong> same charging family, OnePlus is cleaner.</p>\n<h2>Resale</h2>\n<p>Average. A OnePlus loses about a third of its value in the first year. It is a reason to buy models a few months after launch, or to keep them long.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Prices soften before a successor, usually around mid-year for Nord and winter for the flagship. Dashain offers and bank cashback apply widely. Ask for earbuds as a bundle, as the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">Nord Buds 4</a> are excellent and cost dealers less than you.</p>\n<h2>Our OnePlus shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 60,000:</strong> Nord CE5.</li>\n<li><strong>Selfies and calls:</strong> Nord 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for most:</strong> Nord 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Long-term and gaming:</strong> 15R.</li>\n<li><strong>Small:</strong> 13s.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Set it up well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Turn on smart charging or the 80 per cent limit.</li>\n<li>Choose auto refresh rate, and the highest only in games.</li>\n<li>Assign the Plus Key to something you use daily.</li>\n<li>Enable the private safe for documents.</li>\n<li>Turn off the Glance-style lock screen if it appears.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>If you want an Android phone that feels quick for years and does not nag you, OnePlus remains one of the best answers in Nepal. Confirm the service, add a charger to the budget and choose the Nord 6 unless you have a reason not to.</p>','gadgets/2026/oneplus-15.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(68,20,'Realme Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','realme-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Realme\'s earbuds offer a lot of features for little money. The range here runs from the Rs 2,499 <a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t100\">Buds T100</a> to the Rs 8,499 <a href=\"/products/realme-buds-air-8\">Buds Air 8</a>.</p>\n<h2>Realme earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t100\">Realme Buds T100</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200x\">Realme Buds T200x</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200\">Realme Buds T200</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t500-pro\">Realme Buds T500 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-air-8\">Realme Buds Air 8</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 8,499</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200\">Buds T200</a> (Rs 4,499) has noise cancellation, LDAC for better audio on Android, and multipoint. That\'s a lot for under Rs 5,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Cheapest with good basics:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200x\">Buds T200x</a> (Rs 2,999) includes ANC and a long battery life.</li>\n<li><strong>Best Realme earbuds:</strong> the Buds Air 8, with stronger noise cancellation and better sound tuning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The T100 is a simple pair for calls and music. If you\'re buying under Rs 3,000, also compare the <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a> in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 3,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Realme\'s earbuds</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t100\">Realme Buds T100</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,499</td><td>Music: 6 hours (buds) / 28 hours (case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200x\">Realme Buds T200x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>Music: 5 hours (buds) / 30 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200\">Realme Buds T200</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 5 hours playback</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t500-pro\">Realme Buds T500 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,500</td><td>Music: 13.5 hours (buds) / 56 hours (case), W...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-air-8\">Realme Buds Air 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,499</td><td>Music: 14 hours (buds) / 58 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A tidy range under Rs 9,000</h2>\n<p>Realme sells five pairs in Nepal, all below Rs 9,000, and each adds one clear thing. It is a pleasant contrast with brands that sell thirty. The buds share a family sound, a decent app and the same quick pairing with Realme, OPPO and OnePlus phones. If you own one of those, look here first.</p>\n<h2>Buds T100: Rs 2,499</h2>\n<p>The oldest and simplest, with no ANC. It did well in its day. Now the T200x costs Rs 500 more and does much more. Skip it unless it is discounted to Rs 1,700 or so.</p>\n<h2>Buds T200x: Rs 2,999</h2>\n<p>Real noise cancelling, multipoint and an app at this price. It is the best-balanced pair under Rs 3,000, and only the Kick X Pro gives comparable features for less. The sound is more even than most cheap buds. For a student with a laptop, it is the one.</p>\n<h2>Buds T200: Rs 4,499</h2>\n<p>LDAC, better ANC and longer battery. Against the OnePlus Nord Buds 4 at Rs 4,999, the Realme is lighter and has LDAC, and the OnePlus cancels more noise. If you listen at home to good files, choose the T200. If you ride a bus, choose the OnePlus.</p>\n<h2>Buds T500 Pro: Rs 5,500</h2>\n<p>A newer model with long battery, up to 13.5 hours in the buds, and stronger ANC. It is good value just above the crowd. The Soundcore R60i NC at Rs 5,499 is its twin in price. Realme has longer battery, Soundcore the better equaliser.</p>\n<h2>Buds Air 8: Rs 8,499</h2>\n<p>The flagship, with comfortable stems, 14 hours per charge, good ANC and call quality. It sits beside the CMF Buds 2 Plus at Rs 8,999, which has slightly better noise cancelling and a hearing test. With a Realme phone, the Air 8\'s integration may tip the balance. It is often bundled with phones at half price, when it becomes an easy yes.</p>\n<h2>The app: realme Link</h2>\n<p>Equaliser, ANC levels, controls, dual connection, game mode, find my buds and updates. On Realme phones these appear in the system\'s Bluetooth settings. On others install the app. On OnePlus and OPPO phones much of it works through HeyMelody or the system.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>Bass forward, with clear voices on the T200 and above. The Clear preset helps podcasts and Nepali songs. The \"N Bass\" preset is for those who like their ears shaken.</p>\n<h2>Game mode</h2>\n<p>Realme has paid attention here, with low-latency modes around 45 to 80 milliseconds on the sheet. In PUBG Mobile on a Realme phone it feels decent. For tournaments, a wire.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling expectations</h2>\n<p>T200x: takes the edge off. T200 and T500 Pro: useful on buses. Air 8: calm in offices. None is silent.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Good in rooms, with AI noise reduction on newer pairs. In traffic, acceptable on the T500 Pro and Air 8.</p>\n<h2>Fit</h2>\n<p>All are light, between four and five grams. Three tips. Small ears do well with the T200x. The Air 8\'s stems are rounder and rest comfortably.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>From 5 hours with ANC on the cheapest to 8 or more on the Air 8. Ten minutes gives three to five hours on most. The cases are compact and charge by USB-C.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>IPX4 to IP55. Fine for the gym. Cases are not protected.</p>\n<h2>With an iPhone or Samsung</h2>\n<p>They work as normal Bluetooth buds, with the app for settings. LDAC works on Samsungs that support it, and not on iPhones.</p>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>Realme\'s service points handle earbuds, with six to twelve months, usually by replacement. Look for a Nepali sticker, as Indian units are common. Keep the bill.</p>\n<h2>Fakes</h2>\n<p>There are some, especially of the Air series. A real pair pops up by name on a Realme or OPPO phone and appears in the app.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best cheap pair:</strong> T200x.</li>\n<li><strong>Android listener with good files:</strong> T200.</li>\n<li><strong>Battery and ANC for little:</strong> T500 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Realme phone owner, or bundle:</strong> Air 8.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with others</h2>\n<p><strong>OnePlus:</strong> the Nord Buds 4 is the best all-rounder at Rs 5,000. <strong>CMF:</strong> Buds 2 Plus edges the Air 8. <strong>Redmi:</strong> Buds 8 Lite for Xiaomi phones. <strong>Soundcore:</strong> better apps. <strong>boAt, Noise:</strong> cheaper looks, weaker tuning.</p>\n<h2>Problems and fixes</h2>\n<p><strong>Delay in video:</strong> turn game mode on, or off and on again. <strong>One bud quiet:</strong> brush the mesh. <strong>Will not switch between devices:</strong> enable dual connection and disconnect one of three. <strong>LDAC missing:</strong> turn it on in the app, then reconnect.</p>\n<h2>Looking after them</h2>\n<p>Dry after use, charge the case weekly, replace tips each year, and update firmware. Expect two to three years.</p>\n<h2>Bundles</h2>\n<p>When a new Realme number or P phone is launched, earbuds are offered at a deep discount to the first buyers. If you plan to change phone, plan for the buds at the same time. If the buds are \"free\", check that the phone\'s price has not risen to pay for them.</p>\n<h2>Volume and safety</h2>\n<p>Cheap ANC lets you listen more quietly. Use it that way. On roads use transparency, which the T200 and above have, and keep one ear free.</p>\n<h2>Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Is the T200x better than Redmi Buds 5A?</strong> Yes, with ANC and multipoint, for Rs 1,000 more.</p>\n<p><strong>T200 or T500 Pro?</strong> T500 Pro for battery and ANC. T200 for LDAC at a lower price. Check if LDAC is supported on the T500 Pro unit you see.</p>\n<p><strong>Do they support wireless charging?</strong> Generally not.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The T200x is the bargain, and the Air 8 the one to own with a Realme phone. The rest fill the steps in between, well. For a comparison across brands, read <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal\">under Rs 3,000</a>, <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">under Rs 5,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/realme-buds-t200.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(69,20,'Realme Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','realme-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Realme sells phones at almost every price in Nepal, from the Rs 15,999 <a href=\"/products/realme-note-60x\">Note 60x</a> to the Rs 1,49,999 <a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">GT 8 Pro</a>. The <strong>Note</strong> and <strong>C</strong> series are budget phones, <strong>P</strong> is the value range, and <strong>GT</strong> is the performance range.</p>\n<h2>Realme phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-60x\">Realme Note 60x</a></td><td>3GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 15,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Realme Note 70</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 16,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 20,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 23,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-80\">Realme Note 80</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">Realme C71</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c100i\">Realme C100i</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 28,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-lite-4g\">Realme P4 Lite 4G</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 31,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4x-4g\">Realme P4x 4G</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c100x\">Realme C100x</a></td><td>4GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 35,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">Realme C85 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 36,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c85-5g\">Realme C85 5G</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c85\">Realme C85</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-4g\">Realme P4 4G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 45,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-16-5g\">Realme 16</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3-ultra\">Realme P3 Ultra</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 65,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-15-pro\">Realme 15 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 66,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 71,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 76,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">Realme GT 8 Pro</a></td><td>Standard (Wind Moves White), 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Aston Martin Edition, 16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,59,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Highlights from the range</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tough and affordable:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/realme-c85-5g\">Realme C85 5G</a> (from Rs 39,999) has a 7,000mAh battery and IP68. The <a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">C85 Pro</a> adds an AMOLED screen and even higher water ratings.</li>\n<li><strong>Battery champion:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a> has a 10,001mAh battery. It\'s one of the longest-lasting phones on sale.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance:</strong> the GT 8 Pro, with a Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 and 7,000mAh, competes with flagships costing much more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Realme\'s cheapest Note models have small storage. Pick at least 128GB, as covered in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">phones under Rs 25,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The line-up with specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Realme Note 70</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,999</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 563 nit (typ)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6300 mAh, non-removable; 15W VOOC Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">Realme C71</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 750 nits (Peak)</td><td>UNISOC T7250</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB</td><td>45W Fast Charge</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c100x\">Realme C100x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,999</td><td>6.8 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 90...</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>6GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">Realme C85 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;36,999</td><td>6.8 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (Typ), 120...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 7000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (Typ), 20...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3-ultra\">Realme P3 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;65,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (typ), 12...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 8350 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>6.8-inch quad-curved 1.5K AMOLED, 144Hz, 6,50...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reve...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-gt-8-pro\">Realme GT 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,999</td><td>6.79-inch QHD+ AMOLED (1440×3136), 144Hz, 7,0...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,000mAh; 120W wired, 50W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Six series in one brand</h2>\n<p>Realme names too many phones. Here is the map. <strong>Note</strong> is the cheapest. <strong>C</strong> is basic with a big battery or tough body. <strong>P</strong> is for performance at a price. <strong>Number series</strong>, such as 14, 15 and 16, is for style and camera. <strong>GT</strong> is the flagship. \"X\", \"Lite\", \"Pro\" and \"Ultra\" tell you the rank. \"4G\" in the name is a useful warning that a similar phone exists with a better chip.</p>\n<h2>What Realme does well</h2>\n<p>Battery and charging. No brand under Rs 50,000 is as generous with both. 45W charging appears at Rs 25,000, and batteries of 7,000 to 8,000mAh are ordinary. The company also puts strong dust and water ratings on mid-range models, which few others do. What it does less well is restraint: too many models, a crowded app list and modest update promises on cheap phones.</p>\n<h2>Under Rs 25,000: Note 60x, Note 70, Note 80</h2>\n<p>All three are for light use with the same family of basic chips. The <a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Note 70</a> has the best balance with 6,300mAh. The <a href=\"/products/realme-note-80\">Note 80</a> costs about Rs 5,000 more for small changes, as our <a href=\"/reviews/realme-note-80-review-nepal\">review</a> points out. Choose 128GB versions.</p>\n<h2>Rs 25,000 to 36,000: C71, C100i, P4 Lite, P4x, C100x</h2>\n<p>Here lies a trap. Several of these phones use the same Unisoc T7250 found in Rs 15,000 models, dressed with a bigger battery and a nicer name. The C100x costs Rs 35,999 with that chip. A battery of 8,000mAh is attractive, yet at the same price a Redmi 17 5G or Infinix Note 50s is several times faster. Buy these only if endurance is the single thing you want. The <a href=\"/products/realme-c71\">C71</a> is the sensible one for its charging.</p>\n<h2>Rs 37,000 to 50,000: where Realme shines</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/realme-c85-pro\">C85 Pro</a> is an AMOLED phone with extreme water ratings. The <a href=\"/products/realme-c85-5g\">C85 5G</a> adds 5G and 144Hz with an LCD. Then comes the pair that makes Realme worth visiting: the <a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">P3</a> at Rs 44,999 and the <a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">14 5G</a> at Rs 49,999. Both use the Snapdragon 6 Gen 4, with AMOLED screens, 6,000mAh and 45W. The 14 5G adds a stabilised camera and stronger memory. For students who play, they are among the best purchases in the country.</p>\n<p>Be careful with the P4 4G at Rs 45,999. It shares a number with better phones and has an older 4G chip.</p>\n<h2>Rs 65,000 to 77,000: 16 5G, P3 Ultra, 15 Pro, P4 Power</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/realme-p3-ultra\">P3 Ultra</a> has the fastest chip of the four and 80W charging. The <a href=\"/products/realme-15-pro\">15 Pro</a> balances a 144Hz screen, 7,000mAh and a decent camera. The <a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">P4 Power</a> is for battery, and <a href=\"/reviews/realme-p4-power-review-nepal\">our review</a> explains how far 10,001mAh goes. The 16 5G at Rs 64,999 has a weaker chip for the price. At this level, look at the POCO F7 as well before paying.</p>\n<h2>GT 8 Pro</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,49,999 it has a top chip, a QHD+ screen at 144Hz, 7,000mAh with 120W charging and a camera system that can stand beside the established names. The weak points are resale and the length of support. It sits among the OPPO Find X9 Pro, Galaxy S26 and OnePlus 15. For pure hardware it is strong. Try for a discount, since flagships from value brands fall quickly.</p>\n<h2>Realme UI</h2>\n<p>It shares its base with OPPO\'s ColorOS: smooth, with many options. On cheaper models expect preinstalled apps and recommendation folders. On the first day, uninstall what you can, turn off App Market recommendations and the lock-screen magazine, and limit notifications. Updates: two to three Android versions on most P and number models, less on Note and C, more on GT.</p>\n<h2>Charging</h2>\n<p>Realme\'s chargers are the SUPERVOOC family, interchangeable with OPPO and OnePlus. They are usually in the box. At full speed the battery warms, so keep it out of the sun and off the bed. For overnight charging an old slow adapter is kinder.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Treat everything below Rs 45,000 as a daylight camera. From the 14 5G upwards, stabilisation helps indoors. For portraits with a real zoom, Realme\'s mid-range has little to offer: look at the Nothing Phone 4a or a vivo V. The GT 8 Pro is the exception.</p>\n<h2>Service and parts</h2>\n<p>Realme has service points in the larger cities, and its popular models are easy for independent technicians. Parts prices are moderate. For the GT line, ask about stock.</p>\n<h2>Where to buy and how to bargain</h2>\n<p>Online brand stores and large dealers run frequent flash discounts. C and P models drop by 8 to 15 per cent within months. Ask for the box charger, a case and glass. Exchange values for old Realmes are low, so sell privately.</p>\n<h2>Checking the unit</h2>\n<p>Indian units are common. Their boxes show an Indian MRP and importer. They carry no Nepali warranty. Check MDMS, the distributor\'s sticker, and confirm in Settings that the region and model match. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">Here is how</a>.</p>\n<h2>Realme or...</h2>\n<p><strong>Redmi or POCO?</strong> Redmi has better cameras in the middle, POCO more speed above Rs 60,000. Realme has tougher bodies and faster charging below Rs 50,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Infinix?</strong> Infinix is faster for the money under Rs 40,000. Realme has wider service.</p>\n<p><strong>Samsung?</strong> Support and resale against battery and charging.</p>\n<h2>Our Realme shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest worth having:</strong> Note 70, 128GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Quick charging on a budget:</strong> C71.</li>\n<li><strong>Toughest:</strong> C85 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> P3.</li>\n<li><strong>Best all-rounder:</strong> 14 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Longest lasting:</strong> P4 Power.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>One rule for shopping Realme</h2>\n<p>Read the processor line before the battery line. If it says Unisoc T7250 and the price is above Rs 28,000, move on. If it says Snapdragon 6 Gen 4, sit down and ask for tea.</p>','gadgets/2026/realme-gt-8-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(70,20,'Samsung Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','samsung-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Samsung\'s Galaxy Buds work with any phone, but they make the most sense with a Galaxy phone. Switching between devices, Galaxy AI interpretation and the best audio quality all depend on Samsung\'s own software. Prices in Nepal run from Rs 7,999 for the <a href=\"/products/galaxy-buds-core\">Galaxy Buds Core</a> to Rs 35,799 for the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4-pro\">Galaxy Buds 4 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Samsung earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/galaxy-buds-core\">Galaxy Buds Core</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds3-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 3 FE</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sasmung-galaxy-buds3\">Samsung Galaxy Buds3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 22,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 4</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 26,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds3-pro\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 3 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 31,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4-pro\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 4 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 35,799</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Galaxy Buds?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>On a budget:</strong> the Buds Core. It has noise cancellation, a secure fit, and easy pairing with Galaxy phones.</li>\n<li><strong>The middle of the range:</strong> the Buds 3 FE and <a href=\"/products/sasmung-galaxy-buds3\">Buds 3</a>. The Buds 3 has an open, stem design that some people find more comfortable than a sealed fit.</li>\n<li><strong>The best:</strong> the Buds 4 Pro has Samsung\'s strongest noise cancellation and its best sound. Pair it with a recent Galaxy S phone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you don\'t use a Samsung phone, you\'ll get similar features for less from Soundcore or CMF. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Galaxy Buds in Nepal</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/galaxy-buds-core\">Galaxy Buds Core</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,999</td><td>Music: With ANC off</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds3-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 3 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;19,999</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sasmung-galaxy-buds3\">Samsung Galaxy Buds3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;22,999</td><td>Music: 6 hours (buds) / 30 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 4</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;26,099</td><td>Up to 24 hours with ANC / 30 hours without (e...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1; AAC, SBC, SSC HiFi, SSC UHQ, L...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds3-pro\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 3 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;31,999</td><td>Music: 5 hours (buds) / 22 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4-pro\">Samsung Galaxy Buds 4 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,799</td><td>Up to 26 hours with ANC / 30 hours without (e...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1; AAC, SBC, SSC HiFi, SSC UHQ, L...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Are Galaxy Buds for you?</h2>\n<p>If you own a Galaxy phone, tablet or watch, read on. If you do not, you can stop here and buy something else, as the special features need Samsung\'s software. That is not a criticism. Apple does the same. It is simply how these products are built.</p>\n<h2>What you gain with a Galaxy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Pairing by opening the lid.</li>\n<li>Automatic switching between Galaxy devices.</li>\n<li>Samsung\'s own codec with higher-quality sound on recent models.</li>\n<li>360 Audio with head tracking.</li>\n<li>Live translation in the ear, with a supported phone.</li>\n<li>Controls in the quick panel, and SmartThings Find.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Buds Core: Rs 7,999</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/galaxy-buds-core\">Buds Core</a> is small, light, has basic ANC and long battery. Sound is pleasant. The CMF Buds 2 Plus at Rs 8,999 is better equipped. Choose the Core for the Samsung conveniences, or when offered in a bundle. In November it was sold with large discounts during a pre-order campaign.</p>\n<h2>Buds 3 FE: Rs 19,999</h2>\n<p>Stems, sealed tips, good ANC. A solid mid-range pair with a price that meets the Soundcore Liberty 5 and Redmi Buds 8 Pro. On features outside Samsung\'s world they win. Inside it, the FE is convenient.</p>\n<h2>Buds 3 and Buds 4: Rs 22,999 and 26,099</h2>\n<p>Open-fit stems without tips. They are comfortable for hours and let in the world, and ANC cannot do much without a seal. People who dislike in-ear tips love them. Compare with AirPods 4 in idea. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-buds4\">Buds 4</a> improve sound, microphones and controls. The difference of about Rs 3,000 is fair.</p>\n<h2>Buds 3 Pro: Rs 31,999</h2>\n<p>Last year\'s best, with two-way drivers, strong ANC and 24-bit sound with Galaxy phones. At a discount to around Rs 25,000 they are excellent value. At list price, the Buds 4 Pro are only Rs 3,800 more.</p>\n<h2>Buds 4 Pro: Rs 35,799</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s best noise cancelling, best sound and best calls. With a Galaxy S phone they are the natural choice. Against AirPods Pro 3 at Rs 47,999 they are cheaper. Against Sony\'s XM6 at Rs 64,999 they are much cheaper, and a step behind in silence.</p>\n<h2>Sealed or open</h2>\n<p>Pro, FE and Core models seal with tips. Buds 3 and 4 do not. This is the first decision. On a bus, sealed. In an office where you must hear your name, open. With sensitive ears, open. For bass, sealed.</p>\n<h2>Sound settings</h2>\n<p>In the Wearable app, choose Balanced and adjust with the nine-band equaliser. Turn on UHQ or 24-bit audio on supported phones and files. Adaptive EQ on Pro models compensates for fit. Turn off 360 Audio for music if voices feel distant.</p>\n<h2>ANC and ambient</h2>\n<p>Adaptive Noise Control on the Pros adjusts to sirens and voices. Conversation detect lowers the music when you speak. It is useful, and irritating if you sing along. Ambient sound is natural on the Pros.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Among the better ones, especially the Pros with super-wideband calls between Galaxy devices. In wind they manage.</p>\n<h2>Controls</h2>\n<p>Pinch and swipe on the stems. Swiping changes volume. It takes a day to learn. Customise the long pinch for ANC or an assistant.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Five to seven hours with ANC, 24 to 30 with the case. Wireless charging on higher models. Turn off always-listening assistant features to gain half an hour.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>Newer Pros carry IP57, others IP54 or so. Do not put wet buds in the case.</p>\n<h2>With non-Samsung phones</h2>\n<p>On other Androids, the Wearable app gives ANC and equaliser, with AAC. On iPhones there is hardly any support. No automatic switching, no Samsung codec.</p>\n<h2>Fit problems and tips</h2>\n<p>Run the fit test on Pro models. Replace tips yearly. Samsung\'s tips are a special shape, so buy genuine ones. On the first batches of Buds 3 Pro there were reports of torn tips abroad, so remove them gently with the pads of your fingers.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and lost buds</h2>\n<p>Samsung service in Nepal covers official units for a year and can supply single buds or cases for many models. Ask for the price: usually a third of the set. Register in Members. Use SmartThings Find, and remember it shows the last known location.</p>\n<h2>Fakes</h2>\n<p>Galaxy Buds are copied. Genuine ones show the correct name in the Wearable app and can update firmware. Check the serial inside the case and the sticker on the box.</p>\n<h2>Which to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Budget Galaxy owner:</strong> Buds Core on offer.</li>\n<li><strong>Dislikes tips:</strong> Buds 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for the money:</strong> Buds 3 Pro discounted.</li>\n<li><strong>Best Samsung:</strong> Buds 4 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Not a Galaxy owner:</strong> CMF Buds 2 Plus or Soundcore Liberty 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Trade-ins and bundles</h2>\n<p>Samsung gives buds at half price or free during phone pre-orders. If you expect to buy an S27 or a fold in the coming year, wait for that. During Dashain, watch and buds combinations are discounted.</p>\n<h2>Galaxy Buds or Galaxy Watch first?</h2>\n<p>If your budget allows one, think about which you will use daily. Commuters and callers gain more from buds. Walkers and people who miss calls gain more from a watch. The <a href=\"/guides/samsung-smartwatch-price-in-nepal\">watch page</a> has those prices.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Wipe stems and sensors weekly.</li>\n<li>Clean mesh with a dry brush.</li>\n<li>Keep firmware current.</li>\n<li>Charge the case every week.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For Galaxy owners with the money, the Buds 4 Pro are the ones to want and the Buds 3 Pro the ones to find on sale. For everybody else, the same rupees buy more from brands that treat all phones equally. See <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/samsung-galaxy-buds4-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(71,20,'Samsung Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','samsung-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Samsung has the broadest phone range in Nepal and the biggest service network, which is a big reason many people stick with Galaxy phones. Prices run from Rs 25,499 for the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Galaxy A07</a> to Rs 2,99,999 for the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold-8-ultra\">Galaxy Z Fold 8 Ultra</a>. The <strong>A and M</strong> series are budget and mid-range, <strong>S</strong> is the flagship range, and <strong>Z</strong> covers the foldables.</p>\n<h2>Samsung phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 25,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 33,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07s\">Samsung Galaxy A07s</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 30,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a08\">Samsung Galaxy A08</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 34,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 40,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 47,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m36-5g\">Samsung Galaxy M36 5G</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 41,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 47,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy M17 5G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 43,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 48,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 56,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A27 5G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 55,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 86,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 96,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A57 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 96,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,07,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-fe\">Samsung Galaxy S26 FE</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip7-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 7 FE</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,35,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Samsung Galaxy S26</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip-7\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip7</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S25 Ultra</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,84,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip8\">Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 8</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,94,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,24,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 2,59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>16GB/1TB</td><td>Rs 2,93,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold-7\">Samsung Galaxy Z Fold 7</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold8\">Samsung Galaxy Z Fold 8</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-fold-8-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy Z Fold 8 Ultra</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 2,99,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 3,39,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks by budget</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under Rs 50,000:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> (from Rs 44,999) has a Super AMOLED screen and OIS camera, plus Samsung\'s long software support. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m36-5g\">Galaxy M36 5G</a> (Rs 41,999) is a strong alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Galaxy A37 5G</a> adds IP68 and a better camera.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value flagship:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">Galaxy S25 Ultra</a> (Rs 1,84,999) is now Rs 40,000 cheaper than the S26 Ultra. <a href=\"/news/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra-leak-roundup-snapdragon-8-elite-nepal\">Our comparison</a> explains what you give up.</li>\n<li><strong>The newest:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> and <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-fe\">S26 FE</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cheapest A0x models are basic, and 4GB/64GB fills up quickly. Samsung Nepal often runs EMI and data offers, covered in <a href=\"/news/samsung-ncell-partnership-galaxy-offers-nepal\">our Samsung–Ncell report</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The middle of the range, with specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>6.7 inches PLS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m36-5g\">Samsung Galaxy M36 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;41,999</td><td>6.7 inches Super AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (HBM...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1380 (5nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.7-inch FHD+ Super AMOLED, 90Hz, 1,100 nits,...</td><td>Exynos 1330</td><td>6GB / 8GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 25W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A27 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;55,999</td><td>6.7 inches Super AMOLED, 120Hz</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>6.7-inch Super AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass V...</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Samsung Galaxy S26</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>6.3-inch FHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm) in most markets; Snapdragon...</td><td>12GB</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy S25 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,84,999</td><td>6.9 inches Dynamic LTPO AMOLED 2X, 120Hz, 260...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Understanding the letters</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s naming is tidy once someone explains it. The letter is the family. A is for everyone, M and F are versions of A sold mainly online, S is the flagship line, Z folds. The first digit is the rank inside the family, from 0 to 5 in the A series. The second digit is the year. So the A07 is the basic model of the 7 generation and the A57 its best. A phone called A08 is newer than an A07 and not better than an A17.</p>\n<p>\"FE\" means a simplified flagship. \"Plus\" means a larger one. \"Ultra\" means everything, with a pen.</p>\n<h2>Why Samsung costs more for the same hardware</h2>\n<p>Set a Galaxy A17 5G beside a Redmi Note 15 5G at nearly the same price, and the Redmi wins most rows: brighter screen, faster chip, larger battery, better zoom. Samsung asks you to pay for other things.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Service.</strong> Centres in most cities, with parts in stock.</li>\n<li><strong>Updates.</strong> Six years on recent A models, seven on S and Z.</li>\n<li><strong>Security.</strong> Knox, Secure Folder, Auto Blocker.</li>\n<li><strong>Resale.</strong> A used Galaxy sells quickly at a better price.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you change phones every eighteen months, these are worth little. If you keep one for five years, they are worth more than a faster chip.</p>\n<h2>Up to Rs 35,000: A07, A07s, A08</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">A07</a> is the one to know. It is a plain, dependable phone, and <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a07-review-nepal\">our review</a> explains its limits. The A07s and A08 cost more for small changes, and at over Rs 34,000 you should look at other brands unless support is the reason. Take 6GB if you can.</p>\n<h2>Rs 40,000 to 57,000: M36, M17, A17, A27</h2>\n<p>This is where Samsung\'s own range competes with itself. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m36-5g\">M36 5G</a> at Rs 41,999 has a faster chip, a 120Hz screen and 8GB, and is the best value Samsung sells in Nepal at present. It is found online more than in shops. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">A17 5G</a> is everywhere and a little weaker. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a27-5g\">A27 5G</a> has a Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 and 120Hz, and at Rs 55,999 it meets the Honor X9d and others with far larger batteries.</p>\n<h2>Rs 75,000 to 1,08,000: A37 and A57</h2>\n<p>After this year\'s increases these are hard to recommend at list price. The A37 in its base version makes sense for people who want IP68 and six years of support. The A57 is thin and pleasant, with a price that now touches phones with flagship chips. Both become reasonable with an exchange bonus. See our <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g-review-nepal\">A37</a> and <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a57-5g-review-nepal\">A57</a> reviews.</p>\n<h2>Rs 1,25,000 to 1,60,000: S26 FE, S26, S26+</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">S26</a> is the one we like: small, fast and beautifully made, with a battery that asks for a midday top-up from heavy users. The FE is bigger and slower. The Plus brings a larger screen and battery for Rs 20,000 more and is quietly the most comfortable phone in the line for people who do not need a pen.</p>\n<h2>Rs 1,85,000 and above: Ultra and foldables</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra\">S25 Ultra</a> has become the sensible Ultra, as we argue in <a href=\"/news/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra-leak-roundup-snapdragon-8-elite-nepal\">this comparison</a>. The <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra-review-nepal\">S26 Ultra</a> is the complete one. Among foldables, the Flip models are stylish and compact, and the Fold models are small tablets. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-z-flip7-fe\">Z Flip7 FE</a> at Rs 1,35,999 is the cheapest way to own a folding phone from a brand with service here. Ask about the price of an inner screen before you fall in love.</p>\n<h2>When Samsung prices move</h2>\n<p>S phones launch in the first quarter and fold in summer. The months before a launch are for bargains on the outgoing model, and the pre-order window is for exchange bonuses on the new one. A phones change in spring. Dashain and Tihar bring cashback. Between these, the price in the shop is often a few thousand below the list, so ask.</p>\n<h2>Exynos or Snapdragon?</h2>\n<p>Some Galaxy S models use Samsung\'s own chip in our region and Qualcomm\'s elsewhere. In ordinary use they are equal. In long games the Snapdragon has usually been cooler. Do not buy an imported unit for this reason alone. You would lose the local warranty for a difference you may never feel.</p>\n<h2>Checking an official Galaxy</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Dial *#06# and check the IMEI in MDMS.</li>\n<li>Open Samsung Members and read the warranty start.</li>\n<li>Look for the distributor\'s sticker on the box.</li>\n<li>Check the model code and region in Settings, Software information.</li>\n<li>Make sure the Knox warranty bit has not been tripped on a used phone.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The complete routine is in <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">how to check that a phone is official</a>.</p>\n<h2>What is not in the box</h2>\n<p>No charger, on any model. The right one depends on the phone: 25W for the A0 and A1 series and the small S, 45W for the A37, A57 and Plus, and 60W for the S26 Ultra. Buy genuine or a reputable PD charger with PPS. A cheap one charges slowly and warms the phone.</p>\n<h2>Three settings for every new Galaxy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Battery protection at 80 per cent for nights at home.</li>\n<li>Auto Blocker on, to stop unknown apps.</li>\n<li>Motion smoothness at its highest, as some models ship with it lowered.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Samsung or something else?</h2>\n<p>Choose Samsung for a parent, an employee, a long-term purchase or a town with a Samsung centre. Choose Xiaomi, Realme or Infinix for hardware, OnePlus for smoothness, Honor for battery, vivo for portraits, Apple for video. There is no shame in either answer. If you are still between two, use <a href=\"/compare\">Compare</a>, then read <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a>.</p>\n<h2>Our Samsung shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parent:</strong> A07 6GB, or A17 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Value:</strong> M36 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Small flagship:</strong> S26.</li>\n<li><strong>Best overall for the money:</strong> S25 Ultra.</li>\n<li><strong>No compromise:</strong> S26 Ultra.</li>\n</ul>','gadgets/2026/samsung-galaxy-s25-ultra.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(72,20,'Samsung Smartwatch Price in Nepal (November 2026)','samsung-smartwatch-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Galaxy Watches are the natural choice for Samsung phone owners. They handle notifications, replies, payments and health tracking in a way most other watches can\'t match on Android. The range in Nepal runs from the Rs 7,999 <a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Galaxy Fit 3</a> band to the Rs 1,23,499 <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-ultra-2\">Galaxy Watch Ultra 2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Samsung smartwatches price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Samsung Galaxy Fit 3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 7,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Watch FE</a></td><td>40mm</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-7\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 7</a></td><td>40mm (Wi-Fi only)</td><td>Rs 28,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>44mm (Wi-Fi only)</td><td>Rs 31,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-6\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 6</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-8\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 8</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 61,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-6-classic\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 6 Classic</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch9\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 9</a></td><td>40mm</td><td>Rs 66,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>44mm</td><td>Rs 71,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch8-classic\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 8 Classic</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 82,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-ultra\">Samsung Galaxy Watch Ultra</a></td><td>47mm</td><td>Rs 1,15,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-ultra-2\">Samsung Galaxy Watch Ultra 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,23,499</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Galaxy watch?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fitness tracking only:</strong> the Galaxy Fit 3. It\'s a simple band with long battery life.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value smartwatch:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-fe\">Galaxy Watch FE</a> (Rs 21,999) gives you the full Wear OS experience, GPS and an AMOLED screen at the lowest price.</li>\n<li><strong>The newest:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-8\">Galaxy Watch 8</a> and Watch 9 add better health sensors and brighter screens.</li>\n<li><strong>Outdoors and trekking:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-ultra\">Watch Ultra</a> and Ultra 2 have titanium cases and the longest battery life in the range.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Battery life on Galaxy Watches is usually one to two days, much shorter than Huawei or Amazfit. If that matters more to you than smart features, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">smartwatches under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Galaxy Watches and the Fit</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Samsung Galaxy Fit 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,999</td><td>1.6 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 13 days (normal usage); 208 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Watch FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>1.2 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 40 hours (normal usage); Li-Ion 247 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.0, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, Beidou,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-7\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;28,500</td><td>40 mm: 1.3 inches, 44 mm: 1.5 inches, Super A...</td><td>Up to 40 hours (normal usage), Up to 30 hours...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, Beidou,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-8\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;61,999</td><td>42 mm: 1.34 inches, 46 mm: 1.47 inches, Super...</td><td>Up to 40 hours (normal usage); 42 mm: Li-Ion...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, Beidou,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch9\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 9</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;66,499</td><td>1.34-inch (40mm) or 1.47-inch (44mm) AMOLED,...</td><td>390mAh (40mm) / 445mAh (44mm), about 1.5 days</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, dual-band GPS (L1 + L5)</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch8-classic\">Samsung Galaxy Watch 8 Classic</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;82,999</td><td>1.34-inch AMOLED (438×438), 3,000 nits, sapph...</td><td>445mAh, about 3 days</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-ultra-2\">Samsung Galaxy Watch Ultra 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,23,499</td><td>1.52-inch AMOLED (498×498), 5,000 nits, sapph...</td><td>800mAh, about 5 days</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>First question: do you own an Android phone?</h2>\n<p>Galaxy Watches do not work with iPhones. They work with any recent Android phone, and best with a Samsung. Some health functions, such as ECG and blood pressure where available, need the Samsung Health Monitor app, which officially supports Galaxy phones only. If you own a Xiaomi or OnePlus, you get most of the watch. If you own a Galaxy, you get all of it.</p>\n<h2>Fit 3: the modest one</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Galaxy Fit 3</a> at Rs 7,999 is a band with a wide AMOLED, up to 13 days of battery and fall detection. It is not a smartwatch: no apps, no replies, no GPS of its own. For steps, sleep, notifications and an elder\'s safety, it is the best small purchase in Samsung\'s catalogue.</p>\n<h2>Watch FE: the cheapest real Galaxy Watch</h2>\n<p>At Rs 21,999 the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-fe\">Watch FE</a> runs Wear OS with apps, replies, GPS and Samsung\'s sensors. It uses older hardware with a 40mm size and a battery that lasts a day and a half at best. For someone who wants to try a smartwatch with a Samsung phone, it is enough.</p>\n<h2>Watch 7: the value choice</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-7\">Watch 7</a> at about Rs 28,500 has a newer, more efficient chip, dual-band GPS and better sensors. It is the Galaxy Watch we would buy today. Listings also show the older Watch 6 at Rs 44,999, a good example of why you should compare before paying: the newer watch is cheaper.</p>\n<h2>Watch 8 and Watch 9</h2>\n<p>The 8 introduced a thinner cushion shape and a brighter screen. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch9\">Watch 9</a> moves to a new chip and improves battery, as our <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-watch9-review-nepal\">review</a> explains. At Rs 62,000 to 66,500 they cost more than double the Watch 7, with a modest difference in daily use. Buy the 9 if you keep a watch for four years or more.</p>\n<h2>Classic: the rotating ring</h2>\n<p>Scrolling with a physical bezel is a pleasure that touch cannot match. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch8-classic\">Watch 8 Classic</a> at about Rs 83,000 brings it back in steel. If you love the ring, nothing else will do. If you have never used one, you will not miss it.</p>\n<h2>Ultra</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-watch-ultra-2\">Watch Ultra 2</a> at about Rs 1,23,500 is for mountains and long races, with titanium, strong GPS and around five days of battery by Samsung\'s figures. Trekkers should compare it with Garmin and Amazfit watches that last for weeks and cost less.</p>\n<h2>What works in Nepal</h2>\n<p><strong>Works:</strong> notifications and replies, calls, GPS, sleep coaching, heart rate, body composition, music, maps with the phone, fall detection.</p>\n<p><strong>Depends:</strong> ECG and blood pressure, on region and phone. Blood pressure needs calibration with a cuff every month and is not a medical replacement. Ask the seller to show it working.</p>\n<p><strong>Limited:</strong> Samsung Wallet payments, LTE versions without operator support, some Galaxy AI features.</p>\n<h2>Battery: the honest figure</h2>\n<p>Plan to charge every day or every other day for all models except the Ultra and Fit. With always-on display, GPS for an hour and sleep tracking, most reach about 24 to 30 hours. A quick charge while you shower keeps it going. If charging daily sounds unbearable, look at Huawei or Amazfit.</p>\n<h2>Size</h2>\n<p>40mm suits wrists under 165mm, 44mm suits most others. Watch 8 and 9 have a cushion case that looks larger than its number. Try it with your sleeve. Straps on newer models use a quick system that does not accept standard pins, so accessories must match.</p>\n<h2>Sleep, heart and body composition</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s sleep coaching is friendly and useful over a month. Heart rate is good at rest and in steady exercise. Body composition gives a trend, and numbers vary with hydration. Use it in the morning, always at the same time.</p>\n<h2>LTE or Bluetooth?</h2>\n<p>LTE watches need an operator that supports a shared number on eSIM. That is not generally available in Nepal at present. Do not pay extra for LTE unless your operator confirms it in writing.</p>\n<h2>Official and imported watches</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s Nepali warranty can be checked in Samsung Members or Galaxy Wearable. Indian and Gulf units are common and have no local cover. Older models such as the Watch 4 and 5 appear new in boxes: their batteries have aged, and their updates are ending.</p>\n<h2>Used Galaxy Watches</h2>\n<p>Check the battery after a full charge, the screen for burn-in from always-on faces, the crystal for scratches, the buttons, and that no account is locked to it. Reset and pair it yourself. A used Watch 6 or 7 at half price is sensible.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elder or first band:</strong> Fit 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Trying a smartwatch:</strong> Watch FE.</li>\n<li><strong>Most Samsung owners:</strong> Watch 7.</li>\n<li><strong>Long-term:</strong> Watch 9.</li>\n<li><strong>Ring lovers:</strong> Classic.</li>\n<li><strong>Outdoors with a Samsung:</strong> Ultra 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Alternatives</h2>\n<p>With another Android, or if battery matters, look at the <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Huawei Watch GT 6</a> or <a href=\"/products/cmf-watch-3-pro\">CMF Watch 3 Pro</a>. With an iPhone, look at Apple. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">watches under Rs 30,000</a> cover the cheaper choices.</p>\n<h2>Setting up well</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update the watch and the Galaxy Wearable plug-in.</li>\n<li>Choose five apps that may notify you.</li>\n<li>Set emergency contacts and fall detection.</li>\n<li>Turn off always-on display if battery disappoints.</li>\n<li>Enter your body data and wear it snugly for the first week.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s new watches arrive in summer, with the previous ones falling over the following months. Bundles with phones during pre-orders often give a watch at half price. Dashain offers apply as well. The Watch 7\'s present price is already the result of such a fall.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For a Samsung owner who wants replies and apps, the Watch 7 is the sweet spot by a wide margin. For everyone who wants only tracking, the Fit 3 does it for a quarter of the money. The rest of the range is for people with a particular love.</p>','gadgets/2026/samsung-galaxy-watch-fe.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(73,20,'Samsung Tablet Price in Nepal (November 2026)','samsung-tablet-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Samsung\'s Galaxy Tabs run from simple family tablets to laptop replacements. In Nepal they cost from Rs 21,999 for the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Galaxy Tab A11</a> to Rs 1,29,999 for the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11\">Galaxy Tab S11</a>.</p>\n<h2>Samsung tablets price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab A11</a></td><td>WiFi</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>5G+WiFi</td><td>Rs 36,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-lite\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 lite</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 63,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S11</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,29,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our recommendations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For kids and videos:</strong> the Tab A11 is affordable and has Samsung\'s long update support. There\'s a 5G version if you need data away from Wi-Fi.</li>\n<li><strong>For students who take notes:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Tab S10 FE</a> (Rs 74,999) includes the S Pen in the box and is rated IP68. It\'s a great note-taking tablet. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-lite\">Tab S10 Lite</a> is a cheaper option with a pen.</li>\n<li><strong>To replace a laptop:</strong> the Tab S11 has an AMOLED screen and a Dimensity 9400+ chip, and DeX mode gives you a desktop-style layout with a keyboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Compare with iPads and Xiaomi tablets in our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The five tablets</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab A11</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>8.7 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5100 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11-plus\">Samsung Galaxy Tab A11+</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;41,999</td><td>11 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 7040 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-lite\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;63,499</td><td>10.8 - 10.9 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (t...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1380 (5nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>10.8 - 10.9 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (t...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1580 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S11</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>11 inches Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 120Hz, 1000 nits...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9400+ (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 8400 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Samsung\'s advantage in one word: pen</h2>\n<p>From the S10 Lite upwards, a Galaxy Tab comes with an S Pen in the box. It never needs charging to write, it has good pressure sensitivity, and Samsung Notes is one of the best handwriting apps anywhere. Add the price of a pen to any rival before comparing, and Samsung\'s tablets look better value than they first seem.</p>\n<h2>Tab A11: small and simple</h2>\n<p>At Rs 21,999 the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Tab A11</a> is an 8.7-inch tablet for children, reading and video. The screen is low in resolution. Samsung Kids, long support and easy service are the reasons to choose it over cheaper names. Take the 8GB version if it is within reach, as 4GB is tight.</p>\n<h2>Tab A11+: the family tablet</h2>\n<p>For Rs 41,999 the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11-plus\">A11+</a> has an 11-inch screen and a much faster Dimensity 7300. It suits homework, films and video calls. The Xiaomi Pad 7 at Rs 44,999 has a sharper, smoother screen and more speed. Samsung answers with updates, service and DeX support where available. No pen here.</p>\n<h2>Tab S10 Lite: the cheapest with a pen</h2>\n<p>At Rs 63,499 it brings the S Pen and an Exynos 1380. For lecture notes it is enough. The gap to the S10 FE is Rs 11,500, which buys a better screen, faster chip, water resistance and faster charging. We would stretch.</p>\n<h2>Tab S10 FE: our recommendation</h2>\n<p>At Rs 74,999 the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">S10 FE</a> is the student tablet to beat: S Pen, IP68, 90Hz, 45W charging and seven years of support. It can be used beside a sink, in the rain and through an entire degree. Add a keyboard cover later if needed.</p>\n<h2>Tab S11: flagship</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,29,999 the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11\">S11</a> has an AMOLED at 120Hz, a flagship chip and 12GB. For artists, film lovers and people who use DeX as a desktop it is excellent. It costs about as much as an iPad Air without pen. With the pen included, the Samsung is cheaper.</p>\n<h2>What DeX does</h2>\n<p>DeX gives you a desktop with windows, a taskbar and keyboard shortcuts. Word, Excel and browsers sit beside each other. With a keyboard cover, a mouse and an external monitor on models that support it, light office work is comfortable. Apps are still mobile versions. Anyone who needs full Excel, Tally or programming tools needs a laptop.</p>\n<h2>Handwriting in practice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Samsung Notes searches your handwriting, including some Devanagari depending on version.</li>\n<li>You can record a lecture while writing, and tap a word later to hear that moment.</li>\n<li>PDFs can be imported and marked.</li>\n<li>Notes can be exported to PDF, Word and Microsoft OneNote.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A matte protector makes the glass feel closer to paper, at a small loss of sharpness. Spare S Pens cost a few thousand rupees, and the pen attaches by magnet, so it is easily lost in a bag. Use a case with a holder.</p>\n<h2>For children</h2>\n<p>Samsung Kids locks a child in a safe area with time limits. Combine with Google Family Link for app approval. The Tab A11 in a foam case is the standard choice. Check that Widevine L1 is supported if films matter.</p>\n<h2>Screens explained</h2>\n<p>TFT and IPS LCD panels on the A and Lite models are fine indoors. The FE reaches 800 nits in high brightness. The S11\'s AMOLED is superb for films and dark rooms. If you read for hours, a good LCD with adjusted warmth is equally comfortable.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>From 5,100mAh on the A11 to 8,400 on the S11, with charging at 15 to 45W. The charger is not in the box for most. A 45W PD charger with PPS suits the FE and S11. With an old phone charger they take all night.</p>\n<h2>Wi-Fi or LTE/5G?</h2>\n<p>Cellular versions cost Rs 5,000 to 15,000 more. They are useful in the field or in a vehicle. They need MDMS registration. For home and campus, Wi-Fi is enough.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage</h2>\n<p>Avoid 4GB unless for a child. Most Galaxy Tabs have a microSD slot, a real advantage over iPads: films and PDFs can live on a Rs 1,500 card.</p>\n<h2>Service and warranty</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s service network covers tablets. Screens are expensive, up to half the price on cheap models. Official units have a Nepali warranty that you can see in Samsung Members. Imports exist, mostly Indian, with Indian warranty.</p>\n<h2>Used Galaxy Tabs</h2>\n<p>A Tab S7, S8 or S9 series in good condition with its pen is a fine purchase at Rs 45,000 to 80,000. AMOLED panels on the Plus and Ultra can show burn-in, so check with a grey image. Confirm that the pen writes in every corner and that the battery lasts. Make sure no Samsung or Google account remains.</p>\n<h2>Samsung, Apple or Xiaomi?</h2>\n<p><strong>Apple</strong> has better tablet apps, especially Procreate, and the longest life. <strong>Xiaomi</strong> has better screens and speed for the money, with pens sold separately. <strong>Samsung</strong> has the pen in the box, DeX, microSD and service. For study, Samsung. For drawing as a career, Apple. For films and games on a budget, Xiaomi.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Galaxy Tabs are discounted at Dashain and during back-to-school offers, often with a free cover. The S series falls when a new one is near. Education discounts exist through Samsung\'s channels from time to time.</p>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Child:</strong> Tab A11.</li>\n<li><strong>Family:</strong> Tab A11+ on offer.</li>\n<li><strong>Student:</strong> Tab S10 FE.</li>\n<li><strong>Creative and DeX:</strong> Tab S11.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>First-day settings</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update One UI.</li>\n<li>Turn on battery protection if it stays on a stand.</li>\n<li>Set up Samsung Kids or Family Link if shared.</li>\n<li>Insert a microSD card and set it for downloads.</li>\n<li>Pin your note app to the pen\'s Air command.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>In summary</h2>\n<p>If you will write by hand, buy the S10 FE and stop looking. If you will not, compare the A11+ with the Xiaomi Pad 7, and choose between service and screen. The complete field is in our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/samsung-galaxy-tab-s11.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(74,20,'Sony Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','sony-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Sony makes some of the best noise-cancelling earbuds you can buy, and its flagship WF-1000X series is often the benchmark other brands are measured against. In Nepal, prices run from Rs 16,400 for the <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-c510\">WF-C510</a> to Rs 64,999 for the <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">WF-1000XM6</a>.</p>\n<h2>Sony earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-c510\">Sony WF-C510</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,400</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wfc700n\">Sony WF‑C700N</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 28,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm5\">Sony WF-1000XM5</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 50,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">Sony WF-1000XM6</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 64,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Sony earbuds?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple and light:</strong> the WF-C510. Sony sound with no noise cancellation, good for gym and everyday use.</li>\n<li><strong>Value with ANC:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/sony-wfc700n\">WF-C700N</a> (Rs 28,500) adds noise cancellation and multipoint in a small, comfortable design.</li>\n<li><strong>The best:</strong> the WF-1000XM6 has class-leading noise cancellation, LDAC and excellent call quality. It\'s worth it for frequent flyers and long bus journeys. The previous <a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm5\">WF-1000XM5</a> is still excellent and costs about Rs 15,000 less.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sony\'s prices in Nepal are high next to Soundcore and CMF, so compare features first. See also our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Sony\'s earbuds</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-c510\">Sony WF-C510</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,400</td><td>Quick charge: 5 minutes = 1 hour playback</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wfc700n\">Sony WF‑C700N</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;28,500</td><td>Music: 7.5 hours (buds) / 10 hours (case); Qu...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm5\">Sony WF-1000XM5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;50,000</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 16 hours (case); Quic...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wf-1000xm6\">Sony WF-1000XM6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;64,999</td><td>Up to 24 hours (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3 with multipoint; AAC, SBC, LDAC...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Two leagues under one name</h2>\n<p>Sony sells four pairs in Nepal, and there is a canyon in the middle. The C510 and C700N are ordinary earbuds with good sound and high prices. The XM5 and XM6 are among the best in the world. If you are thinking about the first two, read carefully, because the same money goes further elsewhere. If you are thinking about the others, the question is which.</p>\n<h2>WF-C510: Rs 16,400</h2>\n<p>Small, light, comfortable, with clear sound, multipoint and no noise cancelling. Abroad it is a budget pair. In Nepal its price meets the Soundcore Liberty 5 and Redmi Buds 8 Pro, which have strong ANC and LDAC. Buy the C510 only for its tiny size, if your ears reject everything else.</p>\n<h2>WF-C700N: Rs 28,500</h2>\n<p>ANC, good battery, comfortable. It costs almost as much as Galaxy Buds 3 Pro on offer and more than the Liberty 5 that outperforms it. Look for it at half price or not at all.</p>\n<h2>WF-1000XM5: Rs 50,000</h2>\n<p>For two years the pair to beat. Superb sound with LDAC, strong noise cancelling, small comfortable body. <a href=\"/reviews/sony-wf-1000xm5-review-still-the-best-noise-cancelling-earbuds\">Our review</a> explains why it still makes sense at the right price. Around Rs 35,000 to 40,000 on sale, it is a joy.</p>\n<h2>WF-1000XM6: Rs 64,999</h2>\n<p>Better noise cancelling, microphones and transparency. <a href=\"/reviews/sony-wf-1000xm6-review-nepal\">Our review</a> finds it the quietest choice for Android users and asks whether Rs 15,000 more than the XM5 is worth it. For daily travellers, often yes. For most, the older one.</p>\n<h2>What Sony does best</h2>\n<p><strong>Sound.</strong> Balanced, detailed, with control over bass. <strong>LDAC.</strong> Sony invented it and implements it well. <strong>App.</strong> Deep settings: equaliser, adaptive sound by location, speak-to-chat, 360 Reality Audio. <strong>Noise cancelling.</strong> Broad and adaptive.</p>\n<h2>What it does less well</h2>\n<p>Controls can be fussy, cases are plain, some earlier models had battery ageing complaints, and the price in Nepal is high. There is no automatic switching across a Sony ecosystem in the way Apple and Samsung offer.</p>\n<h2>Foam tips</h2>\n<p>XM models use polyurethane foam tips that seal and block noise beautifully. They wear out in six to twelve months of daily use. Replacement sets cost Rs 2,000 to 3,000 where available. Third-party tips work, and may alter ANC. Include that in the cost of owning them.</p>\n<h2>Set-up that matters</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update firmware at once.</li>\n<li>Run the fit test for tip size.</li>\n<li>Turn off Speak-to-Chat unless you love it.</li>\n<li>Set equaliser through \"Find your equaliser\".</li>\n<li>Choose LDAC \"priority on sound quality\" at home and \"stable connection\" outside.</li>\n<li>Limit charging to 80 per cent if the option exists for your model.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>XM5 good, XM6 very good, C-series average. In wind, the XM6\'s processing is strong. If you take calls on a street for a living, test.</p>\n<h2>iPhone owners</h2>\n<p>No LDAC. They still sound excellent over AAC. AirPods Pro 3 at Rs 47,999 give you switching, find my and health features. Choose Sony for sound, Apple for life. <a href=\"/reviews/airpods-pro-3-review-nepal\">Our AirPods Pro 3 review</a> compares.</p>\n<h2>Galaxy owners</h2>\n<p>LDAC works on Galaxies. Buds 4 Pro at Rs 35,799 use Samsung\'s codec and integrate better. Sony cancels more and sounds more refined. The gap in price is large.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>About eight hours with ANC on the XM5, similar on the XM6, and less with LDAC. Cases charge wirelessly on XM models. Three minutes gives an hour.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>IPX4. Keep them dry. The glossy XM5 surface is slippery, so open the case over a table and not over a drain.</p>\n<h2>Official units and fakes</h2>\n<p>High-priced Sonys are copied, with convincing boxes. The Sony Sound Connect app recognises only real ones. Buy from a Sony dealer, check the serial, and ask for the warranty card. Grey units from Japan, Dubai and India are cheaper, with no local cover.</p>\n<h2>Lost buds</h2>\n<p>A single XM bud costs around a third of the pair and may take weeks to come. Turn on Find Your Equipment. Consider whether you lose things before spending Rs 65,000.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frequent flyer on Android:</strong> XM6.</li>\n<li><strong>Music lover with sense:</strong> XM5 on sale.</li>\n<li><strong>Tiny ears:</strong> C510, reluctantly.</li>\n<li><strong>Nobody at list price:</strong> C700N.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Alternatives for less</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 9,000:</strong> CMF Buds 2 Plus. <strong>Rs 15,000 to 17,500:</strong> Redmi Buds 8 Pro, Liberty 5. <strong>Rs 25,000 to 32,000:</strong> Galaxy Buds 3 Pro. They will give you 75 to 85 per cent of the XM5\'s experience.</p>\n<h2>Headphones instead?</h2>\n<p>At Rs 57,600 the WH-1000XM5 over-ear pair is more comfortable for desks and flights, with thirty hours of battery. If you do not need something pocketable, it is the better purchase. See <a href=\"/guides/sony-headphones-price-in-nepal\">Sony headphones</a>.</p>\n<h2>Used XM5 or XM4</h2>\n<p>Used pairs are tempting. The batteries in buds age quickly, hygiene matters, and fakes circulate. Check in the app, test hours per charge, replace the tips, and pay no more than half of the new price.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Sony discounts at festivals and when a successor lands. The XM5 has come down since the XM6 arrived, and there is room for more. Put a price in your head and wait for it.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Brush the mesh and charging pins monthly.</li>\n<li>Replace foam tips when they no longer spring back.</li>\n<li>Do not charge with wet buds.</li>\n<li>Avoid leaving them at zero.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Sony\'s best are worth their reputation. The XM5 at Rs 40,000 or less is the one we would buy. The XM6 is for people whose ears earn their living on the road. The C-series is for other countries, where it costs half as much.</p>\n<h2>One last comparison before you pay</h2>\n<p>Ask the shop for a Sony XM5 and a mid-range pair at a third of the price. Put one in each ear, the Sony in your left and the other in your right, and play the same song from two phones. It is an unfair way to listen and a very good way to learn how large the difference is for you. If your left ear is clearly happier, buy the Sony. If you need to concentrate to tell them apart, you have just saved Rs 30,000.</p>','gadgets/2026/sony-wf-1000xm6.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(75,20,'Sony Headphones Price in Nepal (November 2026)','sony-headphones-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Sony\'s over-ear headphones in Nepal cover three quite different groups of buyers: travellers who want the best noise cancellation, bass lovers, and gamers. Prices run from Rs 16,200 for the <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g300-inzone-h3\">INZONE H3</a> gaming headset to Rs 1,06,900 for the special-edition <a href=\"/products/sony-1000x-the-collexion\">1000X The Collexion</a>.</p>\n<h2>Sony headphones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g300-inzone-h3\">Sony WH-G300 INZONE H3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 16,200</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-ch720n\">Sony WH-CH720N</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 27,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g500-inzone-h5\">Sony WH-G500 INZONE H5</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 28,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-ult900n-ult-wear\">Sony WH-ULT900N ULT WEAR</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 35,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g900n-inzone-h9\">Sony WH-G900N INZONE H9</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 55,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm5\">Sony WH-1000XM5</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 57,600</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm6\">Sony WH-1000XM6</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 88,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-1000x-the-collexion\">Sony 1000X THE COLLEXION</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,06,900</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Sony headphones?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For travel and work:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm6\">WH-1000XM6</a> has some of the best noise cancellation and call quality available. The older <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm5\">WH-1000XM5</a> costs about Rs 31,000 less and is still excellent.</li>\n<li><strong>For bass:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-ult900n-ult-wear\">ULT Wear</a> (Rs 35,000) has a heavy, punchy sound, LDAC, multipoint and up to 50 hours of playback.</li>\n<li><strong>On a budget:</strong> the WH-CH720N is light and comfortable, with decent noise cancellation for its price.</li>\n<li><strong>For PC and PS5 gaming:</strong> the INZONE H3, H5 and H9 headsets have clear microphones and accurate positional sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Collexion is a luxury edition. It sounds great, but you\'re paying mostly for the materials. For more affordable noise cancellation, see <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Eight headphones, three audiences</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-ch720n\">Sony WH-CH720N</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;27,000</td><td>Music: 35 hours, With ANC on, 50 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-ult900n-ult-wear\">Sony WH-ULT900N ULT WEAR</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,000</td><td>Music: 30 hours, With ANC on, 50 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm5\">Sony WH-1000XM5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;57,600</td><td>Music: 30 hours, With ANC on; 3 hours, Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm6\">Sony WH-1000XM6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;88,999</td><td>Music: 30 hours, With ANC on, 40 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g300-inzone-h3\">Sony WH-G300 INZONE H3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,200</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g500-inzone-h5\">Sony WH-G500 INZONE H5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;28,999</td><td>Music: 28 hours; 3.5 hours, Quick charge: 10...</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g900n-inzone-h9\">Sony WH-G900N INZONE H9</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;55,499</td><td>Music: 32 hours, With ANC off; Quick charge:...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Find your row</h2>\n<p>Travellers and office workers want the 1000X line. Bass fans want ULT. Students on a budget look at the CH720N. Gamers look at INZONE. The four groups have little in common, so compare inside a row and not across the table.</p>\n<h2>WH-CH720N: Rs 27,000</h2>\n<p>At under 200 grams it is one of the lightest noise-cancelling headphones made, with 35 hours of battery and clear sound. It is comfortable for a whole day. The plastic feels cheap, and the price in Nepal is high for what is a budget model abroad. The Soundcore Space 2 at Rs 24,499 cancels more. The CMF Headphone Pro at Rs 13,999 does nearly as well for half. Choose the Sony if weight is your priority.</p>\n<h2>ULT Wear: Rs 35,000</h2>\n<p>A button adds bass, and another press adds more. Underneath is a good pair of noise-cancelling headphones with LDAC and fifty hours without ANC. If your playlist is hip-hop, EDM and dance, you will be very happy. With the button off it is balanced.</p>\n<h2>WH-1000XM5: Rs 57,600</h2>\n<p>Excellent cancelling, light, with a comfortable headband, superb app and clear calls. Its weaknesses are a design that does not fold and touch controls that misbehave in the cold. With the XM6 at Rs 88,999, the XM5 has become the sensible one, and on sale near Rs 48,000 it is outstanding.</p>\n<h2>WH-1000XM6: Rs 88,999</h2>\n<p>Stronger noise cancelling with more microphones, better calls, a folding hinge and refined sound. Is it Rs 31,000 better? If you fly monthly or work in a loud office, it might be. If you use headphones at home in the evening, no.</p>\n<h2>1000X The Collexion: Rs 1,06,900</h2>\n<p>A special edition with premium materials. A gift for someone who has everything.</p>\n<h2>INZONE H3, H5, H9</h2>\n<p>For PC and PlayStation. The <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g300-inzone-h3\">H3</a> at Rs 16,200 is wired, with a good boom microphone. The <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g500-inzone-h5\">H5</a> at Rs 28,999 is wireless with a 2.4GHz dongle, light and comfortable, and the best of the three for most players. The <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-g900n-inzone-h9\">H9</a> at Rs 55,499 adds noise cancelling and better materials. HyperX, Logitech and Razer offer rivals, and stock in Nepal decides.</p>\n<h2>How Sony\'s noise cancelling works for you</h2>\n<p>It analyses pressure and noise and adapts. The XM models reduce voices more than most, though never completely. For constant hum, such as aircraft and generators, they are brilliant. In the app, you can set modes by place, so home and office have their own behaviour.</p>\n<h2>LDAC and DSEE</h2>\n<p>With Android, LDAC gives more detail with good files. DSEE restores some sparkle to compressed music. Both cost battery. On iPhone, AAC sounds fine.</p>\n<h2>Comfort in our climate</h2>\n<p>Leatherette pads trap heat. In the Terai summer, an hour is the limit for closed pads. In winter they are earmuffs with music. The CH720N\'s small pads are the coolest. Wipe pads after use, and replace them every two years. Third-party pads cost Rs 1,500 to 3,000 and may reduce ANC.</p>\n<h2>Calls and meetings</h2>\n<p>XM6 best, XM5 very good, others fair. Multipoint connects a laptop and a phone. On Windows, use the laptop\'s microphone to keep music quality, or enable LE Audio if both sides support it.</p>\n<h2>Wired use</h2>\n<p>XM and ULT models accept a 3.5mm cable. They can be used on a plane\'s system or with the battery empty, at lower quality. CH720N too.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Thirty to fifty hours. Three minutes gives hours of play on XM5 and XM6. Batteries in headphones last many years. Avoid storing them empty.</p>\n<h2>Fakes and grey units</h2>\n<p>Counterfeit XM4s and XM5s are very convincing in photographs. Pair with Sony\'s app in the shop, check for firmware updates, test ANC against a fan and look at the serial number. Grey units from Dubai or Japan are genuine but carry no local warranty. Japanese ones may have region-locked language prompts.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Sony has authorised dealers and service. One year is usual. Headband cracks and hinge breaks are considered physical damage. Use the case.</p>\n<h2>Used XM4</h2>\n<p>A used XM4 at Rs 20,000 to 28,000 can be the bargain of the decade if genuine and healthy. It folds, sounds great, and cancels nearly as well as the XM5. Check the hinge, pads, microphones, ANC on both sides and battery hours. Factor in new pads.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lightness:</strong> CH720N on discount.</li>\n<li><strong>Bass:</strong> ULT Wear.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value for quiet:</strong> XM5 on sale, or used XM4.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for frequent travel:</strong> XM6.</li>\n<li><strong>PlayStation and PC:</strong> INZONE H5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Alternatives</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 14,000:</strong> CMF Headphone Pro. <strong>Rs 20,000 to 25,000:</strong> Soundcore Space Q45, Space 2. <strong>Rs 27,000:</strong> Nothing Headphone (a). <strong>Rs 40,000:</strong> JBL Tour One M2. Bose and Apple are alternatives near the XM6, mostly imported.</p>\n<h2>Setting them up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update firmware.</li>\n<li>Run the ANC optimiser, and again at altitude.</li>\n<li>Set the equaliser by ear with your own songs.</li>\n<li>Turn off Speak-to-Chat if you hum.</li>\n<li>Enable multipoint.</li>\n<li>Set the volume limiter.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>For remote work</h2>\n<p>If noise at home costs you an hour a day, an XM5 repays its price within a couple of months for most salaried people. A quiet room would be better and costs more.</p>\n<h2>When to buy</h2>\n<p>Festival offers on the XM5 and ULT are frequent. The XM6 rarely moves. INZONE drops around console launches and year end. Our product pages show the last recorded price.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The WH-1000XM5 at a good discount is the Sony for most people with a serious budget. The CH720N needs a lower price, the ULT is for those who know that they want it, and the XM6 is for travellers who will use every bit of it. Before paying any of these, listen to the CMF at Rs 13,999. It keeps everyone honest. See <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/sony-wh-1000xm5.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(76,20,'Tecno Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','tecno-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Tecno sells mostly budget phones in Nepal, and several of them offer screens or batteries you\'d normally pay more for. Prices run from Rs 11,499 for the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Spark Go 2</a> to Rs 65,999 for the <a href=\"/products/tecno-camon-slim\">Camon Slim</a>.</p>\n<h2>Tecno phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Tecno Spark Go 2</a></td><td>3GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 11,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 13,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-3\">Tecno Spark Go 3</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 21,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50-4g\">Tecno Spark 50 4G</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 32,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-30\">Tecno Spark 30</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Tecno Pova 6</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 44,990</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50-pro\">Tecno Spark 50 Pro</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 47,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-curve-2\">Tecno Pova Curve 2</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 52,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-8\">Tecno Pova 8</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-camon-slim\">Tecno Camon Slim</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 65,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Worth a look</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best screen for the money:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Spark 40 Pro</a> (Rs 33,999) has a 144Hz AMOLED and 45W charging. Few phones under Rs 35,000 match that display.</li>\n<li><strong>Battery and a curved screen:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-curve-2\">Pova Curve 2</a> (Rs 52,999) has an 8,000mAh battery and a curved AMOLED.</li>\n<li><strong>Slim and light:</strong> the Camon Slim is for people who want a thin phone that still looks premium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Spark Go models are very basic phones, so aim for at least 4GB/128GB. Tecno\'s software updates are shorter than Samsung\'s or Xiaomi\'s, so check that before buying a phone you\'ll keep for years. Compare with others in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">phones under Rs 40,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Models and specifications</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Tecno Spark Go 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;11,499</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>3GB / 4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50\">Tecno Spark 50</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>6.78-inch IPS LCD, HD+, 120Hz</td><td>MediaTek Helio G81 (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td><td>7000mAh; 18W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 4500 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Tecno Pova 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,990</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1300 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 70W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-50-pro\">Tecno Spark 50 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;47,999</td><td>6.78 inches IPS LCD, 120 Hz, Unspecifeid</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>6GB</td><td>6000mAh Li-Po, non-removable; 60W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-curve-2\">Tecno Pova Curve 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;52,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 1600 nits (HBM), 4...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7100 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-camon-slim\">Tecno Camon Slim</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;65,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144 Hz</td><td>MediaTek Helio G200 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>Si-Ca 5600mAh, non-removable; 60W Ultra Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What Tecno is good at</h2>\n<p>Tecno is Infinix\'s sibling, with a slightly different personality. Infinix goes after young gamers, and Tecno after people who want a phone that looks expensive, takes flattering selfies and does not run out. It sells some of the cheapest usable phones in Nepal as well as slim, stylish ones above Rs 60,000. As with any value brand, the trick is choosing the models where the money went to the right place.</p>\n<h2>Spark Go, Spark, Pova, Camon</h2>\n<p><strong>Spark Go</strong> is the entrance, from Rs 11,499. <strong>Spark</strong> is the main line. <strong>Pova</strong> has big batteries, bold design and a gaming flavour. <strong>Camon</strong> is for cameras and slimness. Pro in the name matters: a Spark 40 Pro has an AMOLED screen that a plain Spark does not.</p>\n<h2>Spark Go 2: the cheapest phone we would accept</h2>\n<p>In its 4GB/128GB version at about Rs 15,000, the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Spark Go 2</a> is a proper phone for light use with a 120Hz screen. The 3GB version is not. The newer Spark Go 3 is listed at Rs 21,999 with similar hardware, so the older one is the better deal while it lasts.</p>\n<h2>Spark 50 and 50 4G</h2>\n<p>Around Rs 30,000 for a 7,000mAh battery and a basic Helio G81. The endurance is excellent and the speed is not. <a href=\"/reviews/tecno-spark-50-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains that it suits people who use few apps and hate chargers. A Redmi 15 4G at Rs 27,999 is faster with the same battery.</p>\n<h2>Spark 40 Pro: the gem</h2>\n<p>At Rs 33,999 the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Spark 40 Pro</a> has a 144Hz AMOLED that gets extremely bright, 8GB, 256GB and 45W charging. The chip is a Helio G100, fine for daily life. If your phone is mostly a screen, this is the best Tecno and one of the best displays under Rs 40,000.</p>\n<h2>Spark 50 Pro: a step backwards?</h2>\n<p>It costs Rs 47,999 with an LCD and 6GB. Newer, dearer, and in the parts that you see, worse than the 40 Pro. Names do not always go forward.</p>\n<h2>Pova 6, Pova Curve 2, Pova 8</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-6\">Pova 6</a> at about Rs 45,000 has a 70W charger, 6,000mAh and an AMOLED with a design that people either love or cover. The <a href=\"/products/tecno-pova-curve-2\">Pova Curve 2</a> at Rs 52,999 has 8,000mAh and a curved 144Hz AMOLED with a more modern chip, an unusual combination. The Pova 8 at Rs 59,999 uses an old 4G chip and an LCD, and it is difficult to defend at that price.</p>\n<h2>Camon Slim</h2>\n<p>At Rs 65,999 the <a href=\"/products/tecno-camon-slim\">Camon Slim</a> is for people who want the thinnest phone in the room. It has a nice AMOLED, 60W charging and a 4G chip. POCO, Nothing, vivo and OnePlus all offer more phone at the price. Choose it if thinness is the whole point.</p>\n<h2>HiOS</h2>\n<p>HiOS is colourful and busy. It includes many apps and prompts. Spend ten minutes removing them, turn off Palm Store and lock-screen feeds, and limit notifications. Useful parts include the side panel, voice changer, IR remote on some models, and a good set of call tools. Updates are modest: one to three Android versions, depending on the line.</p>\n<h2>Cameras and selfies</h2>\n<p>Tecno has put years into processing for darker skin tones, which works well for many South Asian faces too. Selfies are flattering without turning everyone pale, a virtue some rivals lack. Main cameras are good in light, and weak in the dark below Camon.</p>\n<h2>Batteries and chargers</h2>\n<p>From 5,000 to 8,000mAh, with charging from 15W to 70W. The charger is in the box. As everywhere, a large battery with slow charging needs a night. Pova phones with bypass charging can be played while plugged in without heating.</p>\n<h2>Service and parts</h2>\n<p>Carlcare is the official service for Tecno, with centres in Kathmandu and other cities. Screens and batteries are inexpensive. Carry the bill and warranty card. In towns without a centre, popular Spark models can be repaired locally.</p>\n<h2>Where Tecno phones are found</h2>\n<p>Tecno is strong in district towns and smaller shops. Prices vary, and dealers add gifts. Ask for the price without them. Indian units differ in memory and sometimes in chip, so check the sticker and MDMS. Our <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">guide to official phones</a> shows how.</p>\n<h2>Compare before buying</h2>\n<p><strong>At Rs 15,000:</strong> Spark Go 2 against Infinix Smart 10 Plus. The Infinix has a bigger battery.</p>\n<p><strong>At Rs 22,000:</strong> nothing from Tecno beats the Infinix Note 50x.</p>\n<p><strong>At Rs 34,000:</strong> Spark 40 Pro for screen, Redmi 17 5G for battery, Infinix Note 50s for speed.</p>\n<p><strong>At Rs 45,000:</strong> Pova 6 for style, Redmi Note 15 5G for everything else.</p>\n<p><strong>At Rs 53,000:</strong> Pova Curve 2 for its curved screen and battery, Honor X9d for toughness.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy Tecno</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>First-time smartphone users.</li>\n<li>People who watch a lot of video.</li>\n<li>Those who like bold or slim design.</li>\n<li>Buyers near a Carlcare centre.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who want long updates.</li>\n<li>Heavy gamers, with the exception of some Povas.</li>\n<li>Those who resell often.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our Tecno shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest:</strong> Spark Go 2, 4GB/128GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> Spark 40 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Most interesting:</strong> Pova Curve 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Living with a budget phone</h2>\n<p>Keep 10GB of storage free, stop WhatsApp from saving every video, restart it once a week, use a case from the first day and do not install apps from links. With that, a Rs 30,000 Tecno can serve for three years without complaint.</p>\n<h2>In a sentence</h2>\n<p>Tecno has two very good phones and many ordinary ones. Ask for the Spark 40 Pro or the Pova Curve 2, and let the salesperson keep the rest.</p>','gadgets/2026/tecno-spark-go-3.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(77,20,'Xiaomi Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','xiaomi-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Xiaomi sells its earbuds under the Redmi name in Nepal, and they\'re some of the best value around, especially at the low end. Prices run from Rs 1,999 for the <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a> to Rs 14,999 for the <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-pro\">Redmi Buds 8 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 1,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-6-play\">Redmi Buds 6 Play</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-active\">Redmi Buds 8 Active</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-lite\">Redmi Buds 8 Lite</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8\">Redmi Buds 8</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 10,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-pro\">Redmi Buds 8 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 14,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest good pair:</strong> the Redmi Buds 5A. It\'s a great first pair of earbuds and costs less than a dinner out.</li>\n<li><strong>Best value with ANC:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-lite\">Redmi Buds 8 Lite</a> (Rs 4,999) and <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-active\">Buds 8 Active</a> add noise cancellation for under Rs 5,000.</li>\n<li><strong>The best Redmi earbuds:</strong> the Buds 8 Pro has strong noise cancellation, LDAC and multipoint. It competes with pairs costing twice as much.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you use a Xiaomi or Redmi phone, pairing and extra settings are a little easier, but these work fine with any phone. More options are in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Redmi Buds side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,999</td><td>Up to 30 hours without ANC (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4; SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-6-play\">Redmi Buds 6 Play</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,499</td><td>Music: 7.5 hours (buds) / 36 hours (case); Qu...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-active\">Redmi Buds 8 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>Music: 7 hours (buds) / 37 hours (case); Quic...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-lite\">Redmi Buds 8 Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 36 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8\">Redmi Buds 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;10,499</td><td>Music: 11 hours (buds) / 44 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-pro\">Redmi Buds 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 33 hours (case); Quic...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Six steps from Rs 1,999</h2>\n<p>Redmi Buds are the default cheap earbuds in much of Nepal, with good reason. They are in every Xiaomi shop, they are serviced by Xiaomi, and at each price they are decent. Not every step on the ladder is worth climbing, though. Two of the six stand out.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 5A: Rs 1,999</h2>\n<p>The best safe choice under Rs 2,000. Pairing is simple, the sound is acceptable and the battery lasts about a working week of commuting. Some versions offer basic noise reduction. As a first pair, a gift or a spare, buy it without thinking.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 6 Play: Rs 2,499</h2>\n<p>Longer battery, light, no ANC. The Realme T200x at Rs 2,999 has ANC and multipoint, and the Kick X Pro at Rs 2,599 as well. The Play is for people who want comfort and battery from a known brand.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 8 Active and 8 Lite: Rs 4,499 and 4,999</h2>\n<p>Here Redmi faces its hardest test, the OnePlus Nord Buds 4 at Rs 4,999. The OnePlus has stronger ANC and longer battery. The 8 Lite is a good pair for Xiaomi phone owners who enjoy the pop-up and system controls. The Active is a semi-open design for those who dislike a tight seal.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 8: Rs 10,499</h2>\n<p>Better drivers and ANC, eleven hours in the buds. The CMF Buds 2 Plus at Rs 8,999 makes it hard to choose unless you have a Xiaomi phone.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 8 Pro: Rs 14,999</h2>\n<p>The second standout. Strong ANC, LDAC, several drivers and good calls for Rs 14,999, close to pairs that cost twice as much. With a Xiaomi, Redmi or POCO phone it is the best earbud purchase under Rs 20,000. With other phones the Soundcore Liberty 5 is its equal at Rs 2,500 more. Both lead our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>With Xiaomi phones</h2>\n<p>You get instant pairing, battery in the notification shade, ANC control in the control centre, spatial audio and, on some models, higher-quality codecs. On HyperOS you can set gestures without another app. Phones connected to a Xiaomi tablet can hand over audio.</p>\n<h2>With other phones</h2>\n<p>Use the Xiaomi Earbuds app for equaliser, ANC and updates. Google Fast Pair works on many models. On iPhone, basic functions and AAC.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>Balanced to bassy. The \"enhance voice\" preset is useful for podcasts and news. The Pro has a custom equaliser and personal sound. The cheap ones have presets.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling expectations</h2>\n<p>8 Lite: useful for engines. Buds 8: good. 8 Pro: very good, with adaptive modes and natural transparency. 5A and 6 Play: do not expect any worth mentioning.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Fair on the cheap models indoors. The 8 Pro uses three microphones and handles wind better than anything under Rs 15,000 from Redmi.</p>\n<h2>Fit</h2>\n<p>Light and small. Three tip sizes. The 8 Active sits at the ear\'s entrance without tips. If a sealed pair hurts you, try it.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>From six or seven hours on the cheap ones to eleven on the Buds 8 without ANC. Cases add three or four charges by USB-C. Ten minutes gives two to four hours.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>IP54 on most. Enough for sweat and drizzle.</p>\n<h2>Game mode</h2>\n<p>Present on most. Good for casual play. Triple tap on some models, or in the app.</p>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi service in Nepal covers official units, usually for six months to a year on earbuds. Replacement is quick for common models. Indian units are common, with no local cover. Keep the bill and box.</p>\n<h2>Fakes</h2>\n<p>Redmi AirDots, the older name, are copied a great deal. Current models are too. A real pair shows its exact name in the Xiaomi Earbuds app and on Xiaomi phones. Prices under Rs 1,200 are a sign.</p>\n<h2>Which to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheapest good pair:</strong> Buds 5A.</li>\n<li><strong>Xiaomi phone, Rs 5,000:</strong> Buds 8 Lite.</li>\n<li><strong>Any phone, Rs 5,000:</strong> OnePlus Nord Buds 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Best Redmi:</strong> Buds 8 Pro.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Bundles</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi gives buds with phones at launch and during brand days. If you are buying a Redmi Note or POCO, ask for the earbud price with the phone. The 8 Pro at Rs 11,000 in a bundle is a lovely deal.</p>\n<h2>Fixing common troubles</h2>\n<p><strong>Only one side connects:</strong> return both to the case, hold the button for ten seconds, pair again. <strong>Lag in video:</strong> turn off LDAC. <strong>Low volume:</strong> brush the mesh, and check the safety limit in the phone. <strong>Will not update:</strong> keep both buds in the open case near the phone, above 30 per cent charge.</p>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<p>Dry them, brush them, charge the case weekly. Avoid the heat of a dashboard. Replace tips yearly. Two to three years is normal.</p>\n<h2>Compared with rivals</h2>\n<p><strong>Realme:</strong> better at Rs 3,000. <strong>OnePlus:</strong> better at Rs 5,000. <strong>CMF:</strong> better at Rs 9,000. <strong>Soundcore:</strong> better apps everywhere. <strong>Redmi:</strong> best at Rs 2,000 and at Rs 15,000, and strongest in service.</p>\n<h2>Children</h2>\n<p>For a teenager the 5A is perfect: cheap, replaceable. Set a volume limit. For younger children choose headphones with a fixed limit.</p>\n<h2>Is it worth waiting for a newer model?</h2>\n<p>Redmi Buds change every year. When a new generation arrives, the old one falls by a quarter. Cheap pairs are not worth waiting for. For the Pro, if a new number is in the news, wait a month.</p>\n<h2>Health</h2>\n<p>Use transparency on roads. Keep the volume under 60 per cent. Give your ears ten minutes off every hour.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Remember two names: Buds 5A at the bottom and Buds 8 Pro at the top. Between them, other brands usually do a little more for the money. For the cross-brand lists, see <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal\">under Rs 3,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">under Rs 5,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/redmi-buds-8-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(78,20,'Xiaomi Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','xiaomi-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Xiaomi, through its Redmi and POCO brands, sells more phone models in Nepal than almost anyone, from the Rs 20,499 <a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-4g\">Redmi A7</a> to the Rs 1,99,999 <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a>. The <strong>Redmi A and Redmi</strong> numbered phones are budget models, the <strong>Redmi Note</strong> series is the popular mid-range, <strong>POCO</strong> is performance for the money, and <strong>Xiaomi</strong>-branded phones are flagships.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-4g\">Redmi A7 4G</a></td><td>3GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 20,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro-4g\">Redmi A7 Pro 4G</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 21,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 23,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-15-4g\">Redmi 15 4G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 27,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-4g\">Redmi 17 4G</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 32,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 37,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 37,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 45,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-4g\">Redmi Note 15 4G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-pro-4g\">Redmi Note 15 Pro 4G</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 35,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-m8s\">POCO M8s</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 46,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-4g\">Redmi Note 17 4G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 42,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 47,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 43,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-5g\">Redmi Note 17 5G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 47,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 52,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 15 Pro 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 53,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 57,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-m8-pro\">POCO M8 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 58,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">Poco X7 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-pro-plus\">Redmi Note 15 Pro Plus 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 63,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-15t\">Xiaomi 15T</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 64,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro\">Poco X8 Pro</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 76,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17t\">Xiaomi 17T</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 92,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 99,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">Poco X8 Pro Max</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 94,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,04,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">Poco F8 Pro</a></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,04,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,99,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Budget 5G:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> (from Rs 33,999) has a huge battery and a smooth 120Hz screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Value:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/poco-m8s\">POCO M8s</a> (from Rs 39,999) pairs a 7,000mAh battery with a 120Hz AMOLED.</li>\n<li><strong>The Note series:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a> has a 10,000mAh battery and 100W charging. Read <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-pro-max-review-nepal\">our review</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance for less:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">POCO X8 Pro Max</a> (Rs 94,999) has an 8,500mAh battery and a Dimensity 9500s, flagship speed for under a lakh.</li>\n<li><strong>Camera flagship:</strong> the Xiaomi 17 Ultra, with Leica-tuned cameras.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Xiaomi often sells the same phone in 4G and 5G versions with similar names, such as the Redmi 17 4G and Redmi 17 5G. Check which one you\'re paying for.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>From Redmi A to Ultra</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-a7-pro\">Redmi A7 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,499</td><td>6.9-inch IPS LCD, HD+, 120Hz</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>6000mAh; 15W</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-15-4g\">Redmi 15 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;27,999</td><td>6.9 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 700 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">Poco X7 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>6.67 inches CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nit...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 90W HyperCharg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,50...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 17...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17t\">Xiaomi 17T</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;92,999</td><td>6.59-inch 1.5K AMOLED, 120Hz, 12-bit, Dolby V...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 8500 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>6,500mAh; 67W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">Xiaomi 17 Ultra</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>6.9-inch LTPO OLED, 2608×1200, 1–120Hz, 12-bi...</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>6,000mAh; 90W wired, 50W wireless, 22.5W reve...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Three names, one company</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi sells phones under three brands in Nepal, and the differences are smaller than the logos suggest. <strong>Redmi</strong> is for the budget and middle, sold everywhere. <strong>POCO</strong> is for speed, sold with less advertising at a lower price. <strong>Xiaomi</strong> is for cameras and design. All run HyperOS, all are serviced by the same centres, and some phones appear under two names with different backs. When a Redmi and a POCO share a chip and screen, buy the cheaper one.</p>\n<h2>The cheapest: Redmi A7 and A7 Pro</h2>\n<p>Around Rs 20,000 to 22,000, for calls, chat and video. They are fine as a first or second phone. The Pro has a bigger battery and 4GB. Expect slow charging and basic cameras. Read <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-a7-pro-review-nepal\">our A7 Pro review</a> before buying one as a main phone.</p>\n<h2>The popular middle: Redmi 15, 17 and the Notes</h2>\n<p>The numbers here run in parallel and confuse everyone. The plain Redmi (15, 17) is a large LCD phone with a large battery. The Redmi Note adds AMOLED, better cameras and more polish. Within the Notes there are 4G and 5G versions, Pro, Pro Plus and Pro Max, and a newer number is not always better than an older one with a higher rank.</p>\n<p>Our advice, after reading every sheet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Redmi 15 4G</strong> for battery on a small budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Redmi 17 5G</strong> for battery with 5G, in the 6GB version.</li>\n<li><strong>Redmi Note 15 5G</strong> for most people. It is the best all-round Redmi.</li>\n<li><strong>Redmi Note 17 5G</strong> only if you want its enormous battery and do not play.</li>\n<li><strong>Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</strong> for 10,000mAh and fast charging.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reviews of the <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-17-5g-review-nepal\">17 5G</a>, <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-5g-review-nepal\">Note 17 5G</a> and <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-pro-max-review-nepal\">Note 17 Pro Max</a> go into detail.</p>\n<h2>The fast ones: POCO</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-x7-pro\">X7 Pro</a> at around Rs 60,000, the <a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">F7</a> and <a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro\">X8 Pro</a> at Rs 70,000 and the <a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">F8 Pro</a> at Rs 1,05,000 are, rupee for rupee, the quickest phones in Nepal. Cameras are decent, batteries large, charging fast. If you play games, start and probably finish here.</p>\n<h2>The Leica ones: 15T, 17T, 17 Ultra</h2>\n<p>The T models are the thoughtful choice. They give Leica-tuned cameras with a telephoto, solid chips and good screens for less than the flagships. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-15t\">15T</a> at about Rs 65,000 is particularly good value as an older model. The <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-17-ultra\">17 Ultra</a> is for photographers, with a sensor larger than most compact cameras had. <a href=\"/reviews/xiaomi-17-ultra-review-nepal\">Our review</a> says who should carry it.</p>\n<h2>HyperOS: what to expect, what to switch off</h2>\n<p>It is fast and full of options. It also comes with extra apps, notifications that promote things and, on cheaper models, advertisements in system apps. Give it ten minutes on the first day:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Uninstall games and shopping apps.</li>\n<li>In Settings, search for \"personalised\" and turn off each recommendation.</li>\n<li>Open the Security, Themes and File Manager apps, and turn off recommendations in their settings.</li>\n<li>Revoke authorisation of the MSA system ads service.</li>\n<li>Allow WhatsApp and Messenger to autostart and run without battery limits, so messages are not late.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After that it is a pleasant system.</p>\n<h2>Updates</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi has improved. Flagships and the better Notes and POCOs now get around four Android versions and up to six years of patches. Entry models get less. Ask for the figure for your model. Updates arrive region by region, and an official global unit is the one that gets them in time.</p>\n<h2>China ROM and Indian units</h2>\n<p>Because Redmi is so popular, grey stock is everywhere. Phones with Chinese software may lack Nepali language support, delay notifications and be relocked or blocked by updates if the seller flashed a global version. Indian units work, and are covered by warranty in India. In Settings, About phone, the version should show Global, and the box should have a Nepali distributor\'s sticker. Check MDMS either way.</p>\n<h2>Service and spare parts</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s service network is among the widest after Samsung. Screens for popular Notes are available even in independent shops, at moderate prices. For the Ultra and T models, parts may need ordering.</p>\n<h2>Price behaviour</h2>\n<p>Redmi Notes hold their price for months and then drop Rs 2,000 to 5,000 when a successor is near. POCOs fall faster. Xiaomi runs brand days, festival offers and bundle deals with buds and bands. The first month after launch often has the best bundles, and the sixth month the best cash price.</p>\n<h2>Chargers in the box</h2>\n<p>One of Xiaomi\'s better habits in Nepal is that many phones still include the fast charger. Check the box. If it is missing from a POCO that supports 90W, the seller may have removed it.</p>\n<h2>Which Xiaomi phone for which person</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grandparent:</strong> Redmi A7 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Driver or field worker:</strong> Redmi 17 5G.</li>\n<li><strong>Student:</strong> Redmi Note 15 5G, or POCO X7 Pro for games.</li>\n<li><strong>Traveller:</strong> Redmi Note 17 Pro Max.</li>\n<li><strong>Creator on a budget:</strong> Xiaomi 15T.</li>\n<li><strong>Photographer:</strong> Xiaomi 17 Ultra.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When not to buy Xiaomi</h2>\n<p>If you dislike tinkering with settings, want seven years of updates, or live where only Samsung has a centre, pay a little more for a Galaxy. If resale matters a great deal, an iPhone or Samsung holds more. If you want the cleanest software, look at OnePlus, Nothing or Motorola.</p>\n<h2>What is coming</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/news/xiaomi-18-pro-snapdragon-8-elite-gen-6-launch\">Xiaomi 18 Pro</a> has been launched abroad, and a lightly changed <a href=\"/news/redmi-note-18-5g-bigger-battery-rumour\">Redmi Note 18 5G</a> is rumoured. Neither is a reason to delay a purchase you need. They are reasons to bargain on the present models.</p>\n<h2>Last word</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s range looks like a maze because it is one. Two numbers get you through it: the chip and the memory. Choose a Snapdragon 6, 7s or Dimensity 7000 series or better, and 8GB, and nearly any Redmi or POCO will serve you for three or four years.</p>','gadgets/2026/poco-x8-pro-max.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(79,20,'Xiaomi Smartwatch Price in Nepal (November 2026)','xiaomi-smartwatch-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Xiaomi\'s watches, mostly under the Redmi name, are a popular first smartwatch in Nepal: cheap, light and long-lasting. Prices run from Rs 4,499 for the <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a> to Rs 39,999 for the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-watch-s5\">Xiaomi Watch S5</a>.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi smartwatches price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 10,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-lite\">Redmi Watch 6 Lite</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 14,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6\">Redmi Watch 6</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 20,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-watch-s5\">Xiaomi Watch S5</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which one?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First smartwatch:</strong> the Redmi Watch 5 Active covers steps, heart rate, notifications and Bluetooth calling for very little money.</li>\n<li><strong>Our pick:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6\">Redmi Watch 6</a> (Rs 20,499) has an AMOLED screen and built-in GPS, and lasts up to about 12 days of normal use. That\'s a lot of watch for the price.</li>\n<li><strong>The more premium one:</strong> the Xiaomi Watch S5 has a round, classic design and better materials.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Watch 6 Active</a> and <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-lite\">Watch 6 Lite</a> sit in between. If you run outdoors, choose a model with built-in GPS so you don\'t need to carry your phone. Compare brands in our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">smartwatches under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Watches in the range</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>2.0-inch LCD (320×385), 500 nits</td><td>Up to 18 days normal use</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;10,999</td><td>1.85 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 18 days (light usage), Up to 12 days (n...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, BDS, Gal...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-lite\">Redmi Watch 6 Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>1.96 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 18 days (light usage), Up to 12 days (n...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, BDS, Gal...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6\">Redmi Watch 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;20,499</td><td>2.07 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 24 days (light usage), Up to 12 days (n...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, BDS, Gal...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-watch-s5\">Xiaomi Watch S5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>1.48-inch AMOLED (480×480), 2,500 nits</td><td>Up to 21 days (light use)</td><td>Dual-band GPS (L1 + L5)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A ladder with clear steps</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s five watches climb from Rs 4,499 to Rs 39,999, and each step adds something you can name. Step one is a screen on the wrist. Step two is AMOLED and GPS. Step three is size and style. Step four is refinement. The last is steel and long battery. That clarity is rare, and it makes it easy to stop at the right place.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 5 Active</h2>\n<p>For Rs 4,499, notifications, calls, steps and sleep with up to 18 days of battery. An LCD and no GPS. It is our pick <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-5000-nepal\">under Rs 5,000</a>, for a first watch or a teenager.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 6 Active</h2>\n<p>At Rs 10,999 you get AMOLED and built-in GPS, the two features that turn a toy into a tool. If you walk, run or cycle, this is the best value in the range and perhaps in the country.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 6 Lite</h2>\n<p>At Rs 14,999 it has a slightly larger screen and a nicer frame. Functions are the same. Buy it for looks.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 6</h2>\n<p>At Rs 20,499 the <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6\">Watch 6</a> has the largest and brightest AMOLED of the family, at 2.07 inches, up to 24 days of light use and better materials. For people who read messages on the wrist, the size is a pleasure. Compare it with the Huawei Watch Fit 4 at Rs 22,990, which has more refined sports tracking.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi Watch S5</h2>\n<p>At Rs 39,999 the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-watch-s5\">Watch S5</a> looks like a proper round watch, with interchangeable bezels and up to three weeks of battery. The Huawei Watch GT 6 at Rs 34,490 is its main rival and has the better reputation for sensors. Choose the Xiaomi if you use a Xiaomi phone and like the design.</p>\n<h2>HyperOS and Mi Fitness</h2>\n<p>These watches run a light system, not Wear OS. You cannot install WhatsApp or Google Maps on them. You read notifications, send quick replies on Android, control music, see weather and use a small set of apps. In exchange the battery lasts for weeks. The Mi Fitness app works on Android and iPhone, syncs with Strava, Apple Health and Google\'s Health Connect, and shows no drama.</p>\n<h2>With a Xiaomi phone</h2>\n<p>You get a few extras: unlock the phone nearby, remote camera, control of smart home devices and quicker pairing. They are pleasant and not essential. With other phones everything important works.</p>\n<h2>GPS and accuracy</h2>\n<p>On open roads the tracks are clean. In narrow streets they wander. Heart rate is dependable at rest and in steady walking, weaker in intervals. If you train by heart rate zones, use a chest strap that connects to your phone app. SpO2 and stress are indications only.</p>\n<h2>Sleep</h2>\n<p>Durations are close to reality, stages are estimates. The useful part is regularity: the app shows how your bedtime drifts through the week. Wear the watch a little looser at night.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Bluetooth calling is clear indoors. Keep the phone within ten metres. In a noisy street, use the phone.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>5ATM on most models allows swimming in a pool. Rinse after. Avoid hot showers, soap and saunas. The 5 Active has a lower rating, so check the box.</p>\n<h2>Straps and size</h2>\n<p>Redmi Watches use a proprietary quick-release on some models and standard pins on others. Spare straps are cheap online. The rectangular watches are big, so try them on a thin wrist. The S5 accepts standard 22mm straps.</p>\n<h2>Nepali and notifications</h2>\n<p>Devanagari shows correctly in notifications on current firmware. Long messages are cut. Emojis may be missing. Allow Mi Fitness to run in the background on your phone, disable battery limits for it, and lock it in recent apps. That fixes the usual complaint that notifications stop.</p>\n<h2>Privacy</h2>\n<p>Mi Fitness stores health data on Xiaomi\'s servers. You choose the region when you create an account, and you can export and delete data. Give location permission only while using, unless you need weather updates.</p>\n<h2>Service and warranty</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s service centres in Nepal handle watches, usually by replacement under warranty. One year is standard. Straps and glass scratches are not covered. Keep the bill.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First watch, small budget:</strong> Redmi Watch 5 Active.</li>\n<li><strong>Walkers, runners, most people:</strong> Redmi Watch 6 Active.</li>\n<li><strong>Large screen:</strong> Redmi Watch 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Dress watch with a Xiaomi phone:</strong> Watch S5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should look elsewhere</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who want to reply from the wrist: Galaxy Watch or Apple Watch.</li>\n<li>Serious runners: Amazfit Cheetah, Huawei GT Runner or Garmin.</li>\n<li>Trekkers with multi-day routes: Amazfit T-Rex or Garmin.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Xiaomi against rivals at each step</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 5,000:</strong> better than most unknown brands thanks to the app. <strong>Rs 11,000:</strong> the CMF Watch 3 Pro at Rs 12,999 has a nicer design. <strong>Rs 20,000:</strong> Huawei Watch Fit 4 has better sport modes. <strong>Rs 40,000:</strong> Huawei GT 6 and older Galaxy Watches.</p>\n<h2>Battery figures, adjusted</h2>\n<p>Take Xiaomi\'s light-use number and halve it for normal life with calls, continuous heart rate and one hour of GPS a week. With always-on display, divide by four. All still last more than four days.</p>\n<h2>Price and offers</h2>\n<p>Watches are the most common free gift in Xiaomi phone bundles. If you plan to buy both, wait for a brand day. Separate prices fall by 10 to 20 per cent in festival sales.</p>\n<h2>Copies</h2>\n<p>Fake Redmi watches exist, usually sold with other names. A real one appears in Mi Fitness at once and has a serial number. If the seller asks you to install an unfamiliar app, it is not a Xiaomi.</p>\n<h2>Setting up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create an account, choose region, pair.</li>\n<li>Update the firmware.</li>\n<li>Turn on continuous heart rate and sleep.</li>\n<li>Choose notifications.</li>\n<li>Connect Strava or Health Connect.</li>\n<li>Pick a simple watch face. Animated ones eat battery.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Redmi Watch 6 Active is all the watch that most people will ever need, at a price that leaves money for running shoes. Everything below it is for a first try, and everything above it is for pleasure. Compare it with other brands in <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal\">watches under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/redmi-watch-6.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(80,20,'Xiaomi Tablet Price in Nepal (November 2026)','xiaomi-tablet-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Xiaomi\'s tablets are some of the best value in Nepal, especially the Redmi Pads for videos and online classes. Prices run from Rs 25,999 for the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2-9-7\">Redmi Pad 2 9.7</a> to Rs 59,999 for the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-8\">Xiaomi Pad 8</a>.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi tablets price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2-9-7\">Redmi Pad 2 9.7</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 11-inch</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 34,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 44,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 49,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-8\">Xiaomi Pad 8</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 59,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 69,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>For most families:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 (11-inch)</a>, from Rs 29,999. Its 2.5K screen, quad speakers and big battery make it hard to beat for watching and studying. Choose the 8GB/256GB version if you can.</li>\n<li><strong>For students and creative work:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a> (from Rs 44,999) has a sharp 3.2K 144Hz screen and a Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3. It\'s the best performance under Rs 50,000.</li>\n<li><strong>The fastest:</strong> the Pad 8 steps up to a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 and a 9,200mAh battery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 9.7-inch Redmi Pad 2 is smaller and lighter, a good size for children. See also our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Four tablets, four budgets</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2-9-7\">Redmi Pad 2 9.7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>9.7 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 500 nits (typ), 60...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 7600 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 11-inch</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ), 600...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultra (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>11.2 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 8850 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-8\">Xiaomi Pad 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>11.2 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 800 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 9200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Short range, strong range</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi sells only four tablets in Nepal in our listing, and each of them is the one to beat at its price. That makes advice easy. The questions are how much speed you need, whether you will write with a pen, and whether you can tolerate tidying up the software on the first evening.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Pad 2 9.7</h2>\n<p>At Rs 25,999 the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2-9-7\">smaller Redmi Pad</a> has a 120Hz screen and is light enough to hold in one hand. It has 4GB of memory, so it does one thing at a time. For children, reading in bed and recipes it is ideal. For classes with a document beside the video, choose the larger one.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Pad 2 (11-inch)</h2>\n<p>At Rs 29,999 the <a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2</a> has a 2.5K screen, four speakers and a 9,000mAh battery. It is the most sensible family tablet in the country. Take 6GB or 8GB. The chip is adequate and the charging is slow at 18W. For video, lessons and web pages it is a pleasure.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi Pad 7</h2>\n<p>At Rs 44,999 the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Pad 7</a> changes class: 3.2K at 144Hz, 800 nits, a fast Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3 and a shape suited to documents. With Xiaomi\'s pen it is an excellent note tablet, and with the keyboard a light work machine. It leads our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-50000-nepal\">tablets under Rs 50,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi Pad 8</h2>\n<p>At Rs 59,999 the <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-8\">Pad 8</a> has a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4, close to flagship speed, with 9,200mAh. The screen is similar to the Pad 7\'s. If you play demanding games or edit video, it is worth it. If you read and write, the Pad 7 is the same experience for Rs 15,000 less, enough for the pen and keyboard.</p>\n<h2>Pen and keyboard: check stock first</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s pens and keyboards are sold separately and arrive in smaller quantities than the tablets. Each generation may use its own. Ask to see both before paying for the tablet, and ask the price. A tablet bought for handwriting without an available pen is a sad sight. Generic capacitive pens are no substitute, since they have no palm rejection.</p>\n<h2>HyperOS on a tablet</h2>\n<p>Multitasking is good: split screen, floating windows and a desktop-like mode on newer models. With a Xiaomi phone you can share clipboard, calls and files. On the first day, remove extra apps, turn off recommendations and stop promotional notifications. Updates cover a few Android versions and several years of patches on the Pad series, less on Redmi. Samsung and Apple last longer.</p>\n<h2>Screens and reading</h2>\n<p>All four use LCDs, which are comfortable for long reading with the paper mode turned on. The 3:2 shape of the Pad 7 and 8 shows more of a page than the wider Redmi. If you mainly watch films, the wider screen leaves thinner black bars.</p>\n<h2>Film quality</h2>\n<p>Widevine L1 is needed for HD on Netflix and similar services. Xiaomi\'s recent tablets have it, but check with the DRM Info app on the unit you are buying, since imported versions may differ.</p>\n<h2>Games</h2>\n<p>The Redmi Pads handle casual games and light shooters. The Pad 7 runs PUBG Mobile at high frame rates. The Pad 8 runs nearly everything. A large screen gives your thumbs room and makes a tablet heavy after an hour, so use a stand with a controller.</p>\n<h2>Cameras and calls</h2>\n<p>Front cameras on the long edge suit video lessons. Image quality is fine in light. Microphones are good enough for class. Nobody should choose a tablet for its rear camera. Use it for scanning documents.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Expect a full day of study from all, two from the Redmi Pad 2 in light use. The Pad 7 and 8 charge at 45W. Check whether the charger is in the box. Use a matching PD charger otherwise.</p>\n<h2>Connectivity</h2>\n<p>Most are Wi-Fi only here. USB-C on the Redmi models is slow and without video output. Pad models have faster ports and may support a display, so check the sheet if you plan to connect a monitor.</p>\n<h2>Service</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi\'s service centres handle tablets. Screen parts can take time, and they cost a large part of a Redmi Pad\'s price. A case that covers corners is essential. Indian and Chinese units are in the market. The Chinese ones have software without global services and are poor choices for most people.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Child:</strong> Redmi Pad 2 9.7 with a foam case.</li>\n<li><strong>Family and lessons:</strong> Redmi Pad 2, 6GB or more.</li>\n<li><strong>University student:</strong> Pad 7 with pen.</li>\n<li><strong>Gamer or editor:</strong> Pad 8.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should look elsewhere</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Daily handwriters who want the pen included: Galaxy Tab S10 FE.</li>\n<li>Artists: iPad Air.</li>\n<li>People who want seven years of updates: Samsung or Apple.</li>\n<li>Those who need LTE: OnePlus Pad Lite LTE or a Samsung cellular model.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Setting up for study</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Install your PDF and note app, for example OneNote, Xodo or Xiaomi\'s own.</li>\n<li>Set split-screen pairs for lecture and notes.</li>\n<li>Turn on paper mode after sunset.</li>\n<li>Limit social apps with focus mode.</li>\n<li>Back up notes to a cloud folder.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Setting up for a child</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a child Google account in Family Link.</li>\n<li>Approve apps one by one.</li>\n<li>Set hours and a bedtime.</li>\n<li>Use YouTube Kids.</li>\n<li>Remove the browser from the home screen.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Prices and timing</h2>\n<p>Redmi Pads have moved up with memory costs, and the version with 4GB that used to sell for less has become rarer. Discounts appear at brand days and Dashain. The Pad 7 may fall as the Pad 8 becomes common, and below Rs 42,000 it is a bargain.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi against the others at the same price</h2>\n<p><strong>Honor:</strong> larger batteries, weaker screens, with the Pad V9 a good rival to the Pad 8. <strong>OnePlus:</strong> cleaner software, the Pad Go 2 has a bigger screen. <strong>Samsung:</strong> longer support, pens included higher up. <strong>Blackview and Infinix:</strong> cheaper and less refined.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Choose the Redmi Pad 2 if you lean back and the Pad 7 if you lean forward. The other two are for a child and for a power user. That is the whole range, and none of them is a mistake, which is rare praise.</p>','gadgets/2026/xiaomi-pad-8.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(81,20,'ZTE Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','zte-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>ZTE, mainly through its <strong>nubia</strong> brand, sells affordable phones in Nepal, all under Rs 41,000. Prices run from Rs 15,999 for the <a href=\"/products/zte-a37e\">ZTE A37e</a> to Rs 40,999 for the <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-neo-3-5g\">Nubia Neo 3 5G</a>.</p>\n<h2>ZTE phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-a37e\">ZTE A37e</a></td><td>3GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 15,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a57\">ZTE Nubia A57</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 18,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 24,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a36\">ZTE Nubia A36</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 18,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a56\">ZTE Nubia A56</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a77\">ZTE Nubia A77</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 25,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-max\">ZTE Nubia V80 Max</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 31,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-design\">ZTE Nubia V80 Design</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 29,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 32,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-air\">ZTE nubia Air</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-neo-3-5g\">ZTE Nubia Neo 3 5G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 40,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Worth a look</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best overall:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-air\">nubia Air</a> (Rs 33,999) has a 120Hz AMOLED screen and IP68 water resistance in a slim body. That\'s unusual protection at this price.</li>\n<li><strong>For mobile gamers:</strong> the Nubia Neo 3 5G has a gaming-focused design and shoulder triggers.</li>\n<li><strong>The V80 series:</strong> the V80 Max and V80 Design are big-screen budget phones with 8GB of RAM.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The A-series phones at the bottom of the list are basic, and the 64GB versions fill up quickly. Compare with better-known brands in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">phones under Rs 25,000</a> guide, where Samsung and Realme offer longer software support.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Current models</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-a37e\">ZTE A37e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;15,999</td><td>6.52 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>3GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a57\">ZTE Nubia A57</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;18,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 740 nits (peak)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a77\">ZTE Nubia A77</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 740 nits (peak)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-max\">ZTE Nubia V80 Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,499</td><td>6.9 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 780 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-ion 6000 mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-design\">ZTE Nubia V80 Design</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1000 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7280</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Cha...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-air\">ZTE nubia Air</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;33,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1000 nits (HBM), 4...</td><td>Unisoc T8300 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-neo-3-5g\">ZTE Nubia Neo 3 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;40,999</td><td>6.8 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1000 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T8300 (6nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>33W Fast Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>ZTE and Nubia: who are they?</h2>\n<p>ZTE is a large Chinese telecom company whose equipment sits in many operators\' networks. Its phones appear under its own name and under Nubia, which began as its premium brand and is now used for nearly everything, from Rs 19,000 phones to gaming devices. REDMAGIC, the brand with fans and lights, is also part of the family. In Nepal the range is cheap to mid-priced, sold through a small network.</p>\n<h2>Why consider one?</h2>\n<p>Because the hardware for the price is sometimes generous. Two examples: 22.5W charging at Rs 18,999, where others offer 10 to 15W, and an AMOLED 5G phone with 8GB at Rs 33,999. Because it is a lesser-known name, dealers also discount more. And why not? Because there are few service points, few cases and protectors in shops, and few buyers when you resell.</p>\n<h2>The A series</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/zte-a37e\">A37e</a> at Rs 15,999 has 3GB and is too small for a main phone. The <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a57\">Nubia A57</a> at Rs 18,999 is the one worth a look: 120Hz, Android 16 and faster charging. Choose 128GB. The A36 and A56 at nearly the same price have slower screens and charging. The <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a77\">A77</a> at Rs 25,499 uses the same basic chip as the A57, and at that price an Infinix Note 50x, Galaxy A07 or Realme C71 is better.</p>\n<h2>V80 Max and V80 Design</h2>\n<p>Around Rs 30,000. The <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-max\">V80 Max</a> has a 6.9-inch screen, 6,000mAh and 8GB, but the same T7250 chip. The <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-v80-design\">V80 Design</a> is slimmer and better-looking. Both cost what a Redmi 15 4G or Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus does. Those are stronger. The V80s make sense only with a real discount, say Rs 22,000 to 24,000.</p>\n<h2>Nubia Air: the stylish one</h2>\n<p>At Rs 33,999 the <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-air\">Nubia Air</a> is the most attractive phone in the range: a slim body, AMOLED at 120Hz, 8GB and a Unisoc T8300 with 5G. It faces the Tecno Spark 40 Pro and Redmi 17 5G. The Tecno\'s screen is brighter, the Redmi\'s battery is larger. The Air is the lightest in the hand. If service is available near you, it is a fair choice.</p>\n<h2>Neo 3 5G</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-neo-3-5g\">Neo 3 5G</a> at Rs 40,999 is styled for gaming, with shoulder triggers on some versions and bold lines. Its chip is ordinary, and an Infinix Note 50s or GT 30 will run games better. Buy it for triggers if you have tried them and like them.</p>\n<h2>The chips explained</h2>\n<p>Unisoc supplies most of these phones. The T7200 and T7250 are 12nm parts for basic use. The T7280 is similar. The T8300 is a 6nm 5G chip for everyday speed. None is meant for demanding games. If you have used a phone with a Helio G85, expect about the same from the first group and somewhat more from the last.</p>\n<h2>Software: MyOS</h2>\n<p>MyOS is fairly clean, close to plain Android, with some tools added. Newer models run Android 15 or 16. Updates are the question: ZTE publishes little about its budget phones, and you should expect security patches for a while and perhaps no new Android version. If you use mobile banking, keep apps updated, avoid unknown APKs and plan to replace the phone in three years.</p>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>Daylight photos are acceptable. Night and motion are weak. The 50MP and 108MP labels on some models refer to single good sensors with decorative companions. Test in the shop\'s darkest corner.</p>\n<h2>Service and spare parts</h2>\n<p>Ask the dealer three things. Who is the distributor? Where is the service centre? What does a screen cost, and is it in stock? If the answers are vague, the warranty is vague. In bigger cities, independent technicians can fit batteries and ports, and screens may have to be ordered.</p>\n<h2>Accessories</h2>\n<p>Cases and tempered glass for Nubia phones are hard to find. Buy them with the phone. Universal flip covers are ugly, and better than a cracked screen.</p>\n<h2>MDMS and bills</h2>\n<p>Official units are registered. As always, dial *#06#, check the IMEI in the system, and get a VAT bill with the numbers. <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">How to check</a>.</p>\n<h2>Who might buy a ZTE</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Someone with Rs 19,000 who needs faster charging than usual.</li>\n<li>A person who wants a slim AMOLED 5G phone at Rs 34,000 and lives in Kathmandu.</li>\n<li>A business that needs several simple phones and negotiates a price with the distributor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Those outside the main cities.</li>\n<li>People who resell.</li>\n<li>Gamers and photographers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Alternatives by price</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 16,000:</strong> Infinix Smart 10 Plus.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 19,000:</strong> Nubia A57 is competitive, as is the Realme Note 70.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 25,000:</strong> Galaxy A07 or Infinix Note 50x.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 30,000:</strong> Redmi 15 4G.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 34,000:</strong> Spark 40 Pro, Redmi 17 5G.</p>\n<p><strong>Rs 41,000:</strong> Galaxy M36 5G or Infinix Note 50s with change.</p>\n<h2>What about REDMAGIC?</h2>\n<p>ZTE\'s group also makes some of the most extreme gaming phones in the world, and the <a href=\"/news/redmagic-12-pro-coming-to-nepal-december\">REDMAGIC 12 Pro</a> is expected in Nepal in December. It is a different world, with a price to match. Whether it will share service with Nubia phones here should become clear at launch.</p>\n<h2>How to negotiate</h2>\n<p>Lesser-known brands leave more room. Offer ten per cent below the board price and ask for the case, glass and a written one-year warranty. If the dealer insists on the full amount, say that a Redmi costs the same. It usually helps.</p>\n<h2>Keeping it alive</h2>\n<p>Use the included charger, keep 10GB free, remove apps you do not use, and protect the screen. A basic phone treated well lasts three years. A basic phone with a broken screen and no spare part lasts until the first drop.</p>\n<h2>Our verdict</h2>\n<p>The Nubia A57 in its 128GB version and the Nubia Air are the two ZTE phones we would consider. The rest are outclassed at their list prices. If a dealer near you offers either with a clear warranty and a discount, there is nothing wrong with saying yes.</p>','gadgets/2026/zte-a37e.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(82,20,'boAt Earbuds Price in Nepal (November 2026)','boat-earbuds-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>boAt is one of the biggest budget audio brands in South Asia, and in Nepal its earbuds cost between Rs 2,199 for the <a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-212\">Airdopes 212</a> and Rs 6,699 for the <a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc-pro\">Nirvana Ion ANC Pro</a>. The range is huge and the names are confusing, so here\'s how to make sense of it.</p>\n<h2>boAt earbuds price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-212\">boAt Airdopes 212</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,199</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-alpha-gen-2\">boAt Airdopes Alpha Gen 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-91-prime\">boAt Airdopes 91 Prime</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-131-gen-2\">boAt Airdopes 131 Gen 2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-111-v2\">boAt Airdopes 111 v2</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-immortal-131\">boAt Immortal 131</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,799</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-121-pro-plus\">boAt Airdopes 121 Pro Plus</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-120\">boAt Airdopes 120</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-alpha\">boAt Airdopes Alpha</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-161-anc-elite\">boAt Airdopes 161 ANC Elite</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,199</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-131-elite\">boAt Airdopes 131 Elite ANC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,199</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-supreme\">boAt Airdopes Supreme</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,449</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion\">boAt Nirvana Ion</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-800-hi-def\">boAt Airdopes 800 HI-DEF</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-loop\">boAt Airdopes Loop</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-iris\">boAt Nirvana Iris</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 4,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-space\">boAt Nirvana Space</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-crystl\">boAt Nirvana Crystl</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,199</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-411-anc\">boAt Airdopes 411 ANC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ivy\">boAt Nirvana Ivy</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,899</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-flex-454-anc\">boAt Airdopes Flex 454 ANC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-x-tws\">boAt Nirvana X TWS</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc\">boAt Nirvana Ion ANC</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 6,599</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc-pro\">boAt Nirvana Ion ANC Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 6,699</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>How to choose</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Airdopes (under Rs 3,500):</strong> everyday earbuds with a bass-forward sound and long battery life. Fine for calls and music. Models labelled \"ANC\" add basic noise cancellation.</li>\n<li><strong>Immortal:</strong> boAt\'s gaming line, with low-latency modes for mobile games.</li>\n<li><strong>Nirvana (Rs 4,000–6,700):</strong> the better-sounding range. The <a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc\">Nirvana Ion ANC</a> pairs noise cancellation with very long battery life, up to about 120 hours with the case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>boAt\'s strength is battery life and price, not sound quality or call clarity. At Rs 4,000–5,000, also compare the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">OnePlus Nord Buds 4</a> and <a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200\">Realme Buds T200</a> in our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>A selection of boAt earbuds</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-212\">boAt Airdopes 212</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,199</td><td>25 minutes (buds) / 1.75 hours (case), Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-120\">boAt Airdopes 120</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 40 hours (case), 50%...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-immortal-131\">boAt Immortal 131</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,799</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 40 hours (case), 60%...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion\">boAt Nirvana Ion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,999</td><td>Music: 24 hours (buds) / 120 hours (case), 60...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-loop\">boAt Airdopes Loop</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>Music: 60% volume; 40 minutes (buds) / 2 hour...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-411-anc\">boAt Airdopes 411 ANC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,299</td><td>Music: 60% volume; 1.5 hours (buds) / 2 hours...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc-pro\">boAt Nirvana Ion ANC Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,699</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 120 hours (case); 60...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Twenty-four names, four ideas</h2>\n<p>boAt floods the shelf. Underneath the names there are four kinds of product. <strong>Airdopes</strong> are everyday buds. <strong>Immortal</strong> are for games. <strong>Nirvana</strong> are the better ones, with bigger batteries or more drivers. <strong>Loop</strong> and similar are open-ear. Once you decide which idea suits you, the choice falls from 24 to three or four.</p>\n<h2>What boAt is</h2>\n<p>An Indian brand that designs and markets products made largely by the same factories as other value brands. It spends on style, colours, celebrities and bass. Quality varies by model. It is strong in Nepal because prices are low, shops are many and the border is near.</p>\n<h2>Under Rs 3,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-212\">Airdopes 212</a> at Rs 2,199 offers long battery and solid bass. The 91 Prime and <a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-120\">Airdopes 120</a> are similar. At Rs 2,599 the Kick X Pro has ANC and multipoint, and at Rs 1,999 the Redmi Buds 5A has better service. Among boAts here, the 212 is the value.</p>\n<h2>Immortal 131: gaming</h2>\n<p>At Rs 2,799 with a low-latency mode and lights. Fun for a teenager. The delay is acceptable for casual games. It is not a tournament tool.</p>\n<h2>Nirvana Ion: the battery king</h2>\n<p>For Rs 3,999 the <a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion\">Nirvana Ion</a> claims 24 hours in the buds and 120 with the case. Even at two-thirds of that, you charge twice a month. No ANC in this one. For watchmen, drivers on breaks and travellers it is a unique product.</p>\n<h2>Nirvana Ion ANC and ANC Pro</h2>\n<p>At Rs 6,599 to 6,699 they add noise cancelling to the same battery idea. The ANC is decent. Against the Soundcore R60i NC at Rs 5,499 and CMF Buds 2 at Rs 7,499, they win only on battery.</p>\n<h2>Airdopes 411 ANC, Nirvana Space, Nirvana X</h2>\n<p>At Rs 5,000 to 6,000 these meet the OnePlus Nord Buds 4 at Rs 4,999, which cancel noise better and have a better app. Unless discounted below Rs 4,000, choose the OnePlus.</p>\n<h2>Airdopes Loop: open ear</h2>\n<p>At Rs 4,499 the <a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-loop\">Loop</a> is one of the cheapest clip-style open earbuds. You hear your surroundings, the fit is comfortable, and bass is thin. If you are curious about open buds and not ready for Rs 15,000, try it.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>Bass, and then more bass. boAt\'s signature hides voices. If there is an equaliser in the app, lower it. Otherwise use your phone\'s. With that, even the cheap ones become pleasant.</p>\n<h2>Apps</h2>\n<p>The boAt Hearables app supports the newer and dearer ones, with equaliser, controls and updates. Cheap models have none. Check that yours is supported before paying extra for \"app support\".</p>\n<h2>Labels decoded</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ENx:</strong> helps your caller, not you.</li>\n<li><strong>BEAST mode:</strong> low latency.</li>\n<li><strong>ASAP Charge:</strong> quick charge.</li>\n<li><strong>IWP:</strong> wakes on opening the case.</li>\n<li><strong>Signature Sound:</strong> bass.</li>\n<li><strong>ANC 32dB:</strong> actual noise cancelling, modest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Four microphones are common in advertisements. Indoors they are fine. Outdoors they vary. Record a voice note in the shop near the door.</p>\n<h2>Battery claims</h2>\n<p>At 60 per cent volume, without ANC. Expect 70 per cent of the figure. Ten minutes of charge gives two hours or more.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>IPX4 to IPX7 depending on model. boAt does well here. Dry before charging regardless.</p>\n<h2>Fit</h2>\n<p>Stems and round types exist. The box has three tips. Nirvana models are larger. Try them.</p>\n<h2>Warranty</h2>\n<p>One year from the distributor on official units, usually with replacement. Indian units need a visit to India or a courier. Ask which it is. Register online where asked. Failures within the year are not rare with budget buds, so the warranty has real value.</p>\n<h2>Fakes</h2>\n<p>boAt is copied in the cheapest markets. Check the serial, the hologram sticker, the print and the name in Bluetooth. Very low prices online from unknown sellers are suspect.</p>\n<h2>Prices: ignore the MRP</h2>\n<p>The printed MRP can be three times the selling price. \"78% off\" means nothing. Compare the final price with the alternatives named above.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy boAt</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extreme battery:</strong> Nirvana Ion.</li>\n<li><strong>Cheap everyday:</strong> Airdopes 212.</li>\n<li><strong>Curious about open ear:</strong> Airdopes Loop.</li>\n<li><strong>Young gamer:</strong> Immortal 131.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>People who want balanced music.</li>\n<li>Commuters who want strong ANC.</li>\n<li>Professionals on calls.</li>\n<li>Those who dislike choosing from twenty-four names.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Better alternatives by price</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 2,000:</strong> Redmi Buds 5A. <strong>Rs 3,000:</strong> Realme T200x. <strong>Rs 5,000:</strong> OnePlus Nord Buds 4. <strong>Rs 5,500:</strong> Soundcore R60i NC. <strong>Rs 9,000:</strong> CMF Buds 2 Plus.</p>\n<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>\n<p><strong>One side dead:</strong> clean the pins, reset, re-pair. <strong>Pairs with someone else\'s phone:</strong> forget it there first. <strong>Echo in calls:</strong> lower the volume, and try one bud. <strong>Case not charging:</strong> try a 5V charger and cable, as some cases refuse fast chargers.</p>\n<h2>Neckbands</h2>\n<p>boAt\'s Rockerz neckbands at Rs 1,200 to 2,500 are excellent for delivery riders and shopkeepers: long battery, hard to lose, better microphones. If that is your day, they are a smarter purchase than buds.</p>\n<h2>Looking after hearing</h2>\n<p>Bass-heavy buds invite high volume. Keep below 60 per cent. Use transparency or one ear on roads.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>boAt\'s Nirvana Ion is special, and the Airdopes 212 and Loop are good at what they do. The rest are style for small money in a field with better sound and service. Choose by purpose, ignore the MRP, and test in the first week. For the wider field, read <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/boat-nirvana-ion-anc.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(83,20,'boAt Headphones Price in Nepal (November 2026)','boat-headphones-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>boAt\'s Rockerz headphones are among the cheapest wireless over-ear options in Nepal, from Rs 2,299 for the <a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-371\">Rockerz 371</a> to Rs 6,299 for the <a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-650-pro\">Rockerz 650 Pro</a>.</p>\n<h2>boAt headphones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-371\">boAt Rockerz 371</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-411\">boAt Rockerz 411</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,799</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-430\">boAt Rockerz 430</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-412\">boAt Rockerz 412</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-650-pro\">boAt Rockerz 650 Pro</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 6,299</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What to buy</h2>\n<p>If you can spend the extra, the <strong>Rockerz 650 Pro</strong> is the clear pick. It supports multipoint, so it stays connected to your phone and laptop together, and lasts up to about 80 hours on a charge. That\'s great for students who use one pair for classes and music.</p>\n<p>The Rockerz 371, 411, 412 and 430 are simple, bass-heavy headphones for home and travel. None of them has noise cancellation, and they feel lighter-built than pricier brands.</p>\n<p>If you want noise cancellation without spending much more, look at the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a> and other picks in our <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-5000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 5,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-10000-nepal\">under Rs 10,000</a> guides.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>boAt Rockerz headphones</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-371\">boAt Rockerz 371</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,299</td><td>Music: 50 hours, At 60% volume; 2 hours</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-411\">boAt Rockerz 411</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,799</td><td>Music: 40 hours, At 60% volume; 2 hours</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-430\">boAt Rockerz 430</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>Music: 60 hours, At 60% volume; 2 hours</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-412\">boAt Rockerz 412</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,099</td><td>Music: 50 hours, At 60% volume; 2 hours</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-650-pro\">boAt Rockerz 650 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,299</td><td>Music: 80 hours; 1 hour, Quick charge: 10 min...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Five pairs, one family</h2>\n<p>All of boAt\'s over-the-head pairs in our list are Rockerz, all are Bluetooth, and none has active noise cancelling. They cost between Rs 2,299 and Rs 6,299. They are for students and young listeners who want long battery, strong bass and bright colours for little money. The numbers do not rise with quality, so here is the order that matters.</p>\n<h2>Rockerz 371: Rs 2,299</h2>\n<p>The cheapest. On-ear, light, with a claimed fifty hours. It does the job for lessons and videos. Pads are thin, so an hour is the limit of comfort.</p>\n<h2>Rockerz 411 and 430: Rs 2,799 and 2,999</h2>\n<p>Small variations in battery and design. The 430 lists sixty hours. Choose by what feels better on your head. The differences in sound are slight.</p>\n<h2>Rockerz 412: Rs 3,099</h2>\n<p>Fifty hours and connection to two devices. Multipoint makes it the most useful of the cheap ones for anyone with a laptop. It is our pick under Rs 4,000.</p>\n<h2>Rockerz 650 Pro: Rs 6,299</h2>\n<p>Over-ear, softer pads, eighty hours, multipoint, gesture controls. It is a real step up in comfort. Spatial audio modes are gimmicks, and the battery is not. Against the Soundcore Q11i at Rs 4,999, which is better tuned, the 650 Pro offers longer battery and a livelier look. Against the Q20i at Rs 9,199, it lacks noise cancelling.</p>\n<h2>On-ear or over-ear?</h2>\n<p>The first four rest on your ears. They are cooler and smaller, and begin to press. The 650 Pro surrounds them, blocks more noise and is better for glasses. If you study for three hours at a time, the difference is worth Rs 3,000.</p>\n<h2>Sound</h2>\n<p>Bass-heavy, loud, with a bright top. Films and dance music are exciting. Voices sit behind. Use an equaliser on the phone: lower the first two bands, raise the fourth. On a laptop, free tools such as Equalizer APO do the same.</p>\n<h2>For online classes</h2>\n<p>They are comfortable enough and the microphones are passable in a quiet room. Choose the laptop\'s own microphone for your voice to keep sound quality high. Windows lists the headphones twice: pick \"Headphones (Stereo)\" and not \"Headset (Hands-Free)\".</p>\n<h2>Battery in real life</h2>\n<p>The figures are measured at 60 per cent volume. Expect two-thirds. That is still a week or two for most. Charging takes about two hours by USB-C. Ten minutes gives several hours.</p>\n<h2>Wired mode</h2>\n<p>Many Rockerz have a 3.5mm input, and some include a cable. Check. It is useful for computers without Bluetooth and for zero delay in games.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>Low-latency modes exist on some. For serious play use a wired connection or a gaming headset with a boom microphone for Rs 2,500 to 4,500.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>Plastic hinges and sliders are the weak points. Do not stretch them, do not sleep in them, and do not hang them by one cup. Pads flake after a year or two of sweat. Universal replacement pads cost Rs 300 to 600.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>No serious rating. Keep away from rain and heavy sweat.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>Official units come with up to a year. Keep the bill and box. Indian units, common at the border, do not have local cover. Test both sides, the microphone and charging in the first days.</p>\n<h2>Fakes and lookalikes</h2>\n<p>Very cheap headphones with names similar to boAt appear in markets. Check the serial number and the distributor\'s sticker. A genuine pair announces itself with the correct name in Bluetooth.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy which</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smallest budget:</strong> Rockerz 371.</li>\n<li><strong>Student with a laptop:</strong> Rockerz 412.</li>\n<li><strong>Long hours and comfort:</strong> Rockerz 650 Pro, or Soundcore Q11i.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone who needs noise cancelling.</li>\n<li>Musicians and editors.</li>\n<li>Professional callers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Compared with rivals</h2>\n<p><strong>Soundcore Q11i (Rs 4,999):</strong> better sound, app, comfort. The one to save for. <strong>JBL Tune 530BT (Rs 9,500):</strong> better built, costlier. <strong>Wired options (Rs 1,500 to 3,000):</strong> cleaner sound for desks. <strong>boAt earbuds:</strong> smaller, with shorter comfort.</p>\n<h2>Why headphones and not earbuds?</h2>\n<p>They last longer on a charge, are hard to lose, do not enter the ear canal and can be repaired with new pads. They are larger and hotter. For home study we would choose headphones at this budget, and for a bus, earbuds with ANC. Our article on <a href=\"/blog/earbuds-vs-headphones-which-should-you-buy\">earbuds or headphones</a> explores the choice.</p>\n<h2>Children</h2>\n<p>For children under twelve, buy limited-volume headphones. Rockerz go loud. If your child already has a pair, set the phone or tablet\'s volume limit and lock it with a PIN.</p>\n<h2>Setting up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Charge fully before first use.</li>\n<li>Pair with the phone, then the laptop if multipoint is supported.</li>\n<li>Set an equaliser.</li>\n<li>Adjust the band so pads sit flat.</li>\n<li>Learn the button presses: once to play, twice for next, hold for pairing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Fixing faults</h2>\n<p><strong>Sound from one side:</strong> test with the cable. If both sides work wired, reset Bluetooth. If not, it is a broken wire in the band: warranty. <strong>Crackling:</strong> move away from a crowded 2.4GHz area. <strong>Will not charge:</strong> use a plain 5V adapter. <strong>Cuts out:</strong> keep the phone on the same side as the main cup.</p>\n<h2>Price sense</h2>\n<p>Ignore the MRP. These models are sold at half of it all year. A fair price for the 412 is around Rs 2,800 to 3,100, and for the 650 Pro Rs 5,500 to 6,300. Below that, check for authenticity.</p>\n<h2>Hearing</h2>\n<p>Sixty per cent volume for an hour, then a break. If your ears ring, it was too loud. No discount will buy new ones.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Rockerz 412 is the useful cheap pair, and the 650 Pro the comfortable one. If you can wait and save to Rs 4,999, the Soundcore Q11i is better than both for listening. All are compared in <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-5000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 5,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-10000-nepal\">under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/boat-rockerz-650-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(84,20,'boAt Smartwatch Price in Nepal (November 2026)','boat-smartwatch-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>boAt\'s smartwatches are some of the cheapest in Nepal, with prices between Rs 2,999 for the <a href=\"/products/boat-wave-fury\">Wave Fury</a> and Rs 6,799 for the <a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-ember\">Ultima Ember</a>. They\'re popular as a first smartwatch, or as a gift.</p>\n<h2>boAt smartwatches price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-fury\">boAt Wave Fury</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 2,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-call-3\">boAt Wave Call 3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,199</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-sigma-3\">boAt Wave Sigma 3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,299</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-storm-call-3-plus\">boAt Storm Call 3 Plus</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,399</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-astra-3\">boAt Wave Astra 3</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 3,399</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-enigma-oreon\">boAt Enigma Orion</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 5,699</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-regal\">boAt Ultima Regal</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 6,099</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-enigma-radiant\">boAt Enigma Radiant</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 6,799</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-ember\">boAt Ultima Ember</a></td><td>Standard</td><td>Rs 6,799</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What you get</h2>\n<p>All of them show notifications, count steps and track heart rate, and most support Bluetooth calling, so you can answer calls from your wrist when the phone is in your bag. The <strong>Wave</strong> and <strong>Storm</strong> models are the cheapest, with square screens. The <strong>Enigma</strong> and <strong>Ultima</strong> models look more like traditional watches, with round or AMOLED screens.</p>\n<p>Our pick is the <a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-regal\">Ultima Regal</a> (Rs 6,099). Its AMOLED screen is easy to read outdoors, and it lasts up to about a week.</p>\n<p>Keep your expectations in check. These watches don\'t have built-in GPS, and the health readings are rough estimates. For accurate fitness tracking, the <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a> and Amazfit models in our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-10000-nepal\">smartwatches under Rs 10,000</a> guide are better.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The watches</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-fury\">boAt Wave Fury</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>1.83 inches, LCD</td><td>Up to 5 days (normal usage); 220 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.1</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-call-3\">boAt Wave Call 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,199</td><td>1.83 inches, LCD</td><td>Up to 10 days (normal usage); 300 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-sigma-3\">boAt Wave Sigma 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,299</td><td>2.01 inches, LCD</td><td>230 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-storm-call-3-plus\">boAt Storm Call 3 Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,399</td><td>1.96 inches, TFT LCD</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage); 230 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-enigma-oreon\">boAt Enigma Orion</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,699</td><td>1.39 inches</td><td>–</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-regal\">boAt Ultima Regal</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,099</td><td>2.01 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage); 300 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-enigma-radiant\">boAt Enigma Radiant</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,799</td><td>1.43 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 6 days (normal usage); 390 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-ember\">boAt Ultima Ember</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,799</td><td>1.96 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage); 300 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Who boAt is for</h2>\n<p>boAt is an Indian brand that sells style at a low price. Its watches look good in a photo, take calls, show messages and cost Rs 3,000 to 7,000. They are fashion accessories with sensors, and judged that way many are fine. Judged as fitness instruments they are weak. Knowing which you want saves disappointment.</p>\n<h2>How the names work</h2>\n<p><strong>Wave</strong> and <strong>Storm</strong> are the cheap rectangular lines. <strong>Enigma</strong> is round and dressy. <strong>Ultima</strong> has AMOLED and a better finish. \"Call\" means Bluetooth calling. Models change every few months, so shops hold a mixture of new and old.</p>\n<h2>Rs 3,000 to 3,500: Wave and Storm</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/boat-wave-call-3\">Wave Call 3</a> is the best balanced, with up to ten days of battery in normal use. The <a href=\"/products/boat-wave-sigma-3\">Wave Sigma 3</a> has a larger screen, the Storm Call 3 Plus and Astra 3 are variations, and the <a href=\"/products/boat-wave-fury\">Wave Fury</a> is the cheapest with about five days. Screens are LCD: good indoors, poor in the sun.</p>\n<p>Before choosing, consider adding Rs 1,000 for a <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a>, which has a better app, longer battery and steadier tracking.</p>\n<h2>Rs 5,700 to 6,800: Enigma and Ultima</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-regal\">Ultima Regal</a> has a 2-inch AMOLED and a metal look. The <a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-ember\">Ultima Ember</a> is similar. The <a href=\"/products/boat-enigma-radiant\">Enigma Radiant</a> is round with AMOLED, and the <a href=\"/products/boat-enigma-oreon\">Oreon</a> is the dressy one. They photograph beautifully. At this price the Samsung Galaxy Fit 3, Huawei Band 10 and Honor Watch X5i track better and last longer. So buy an Ultima or Enigma because you like how it looks on your wrist.</p>\n<h2>Accuracy</h2>\n<p>Steps are approximate. Heart rate at rest is acceptable, and during exercise it drifts. Sleep duration is fair. SpO2 and stress are for amusement. None has GPS of its own. No boAt watch measures blood pressure in a way you should act on.</p>\n<h2>Calling</h2>\n<p>Bluetooth calling works through your phone. The speaker is small, the microphone is good enough indoors. It is the most used feature by many owners: in the kitchen, on a motorbike stop, in a shop. Pair both the watch and its audio connection. If calls come to the watch when you do not want them, turn off the audio pairing in the phone\'s Bluetooth settings.</p>\n<h2>Apps</h2>\n<p>boAt Crest and other companion apps handle different models. They ask for many permissions and show promotions. Allow notifications, phone and Bluetooth. Deny contacts and location unless you use the related function. Keep the app allowed in the background, or messages stop arriving.</p>\n<h2>Notifications in Nepali</h2>\n<p>Some models show Devanagari properly, others show boxes. Send yourself a message in Nepali in the shop before paying. Firmware updates sometimes fix it.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Divide the claim by two if you use calling. A week on the better ones, two or three days on the smaller batteries with AMOLED always-on. They charge by a magnetic cable in under two hours. The cable is not standard, so look after it.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>IP67 or IP68 means sweat, rain and hand washing. Not swimming, and not hot water.</p>\n<h2>Straps</h2>\n<p>Mostly standard 22mm pins, which is good. A Rs 500 strap changes the character of the watch. Skin reactions come from trapped sweat: wash and dry the strap often.</p>\n<h2>Warranty in Nepal</h2>\n<p>boAt has a distributor here, and the warranty is commonly one year, handled by replacement or repair at the dealer. Indian units are common at the border with no local cover. Ask for a stamped card and test the watch fully within the first week.</p>\n<h2>Copies and lookalikes</h2>\n<p>Cheap watches with Apple-like designs and no brand are sold beside boAt for Rs 1,500. Their apps are from unknown developers, their readings are random, and they can drain your phone\'s battery. A known brand for Rs 3,000 is worth the difference.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy one</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Teenagers who want a stylish first watch.</li>\n<li>People who mainly want calls and messages on the wrist.</li>\n<li>Anyone buying a gift under Rs 4,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should not</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Runners and cyclists. Get GPS.</li>\n<li>People who care about sleep and heart data.</li>\n<li>iPhone owners who want a smooth experience.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Better alternatives by price</h2>\n<p><strong>Rs 4,500:</strong> Redmi Watch 5 Active. <strong>Rs 7,000:</strong> MWC Aether One Watch Pro with GPS, if you trust the warranty. <strong>Rs 8,000:</strong> Galaxy Fit 3 or Huawei Band 10. <strong>Rs 9,000:</strong> Honor Watch X5i. <strong>Rs 11,000:</strong> Redmi Watch 6 Active.</p>\n<h2>How to get the best from a boAt watch</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Update firmware first.</li>\n<li>Choose a simple watch face.</li>\n<li>Limit notifications to calls and two or three apps.</li>\n<li>Turn off continuous SpO2.</li>\n<li>Charge on a fixed day each week.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What breaks</h2>\n<p>The charging pins corrode from sweat, the strap pins loosen, and the screen scratches. Wipe the back after exercise, use a thin protector, and do not sleep on a charging watch. Two years is a fair life.</p>\n<h2>Price and bargains</h2>\n<p>List prices are high and discounts constant. A watch marked Rs 6,999 and sold at Rs 3,199 was never really Rs 6,999. Judge the final price against the alternatives above. Online sales of boAt products can be 20 per cent lower than shops.</p>\n<h2>Final advice</h2>\n<p>Buy a Wave Call 3 if your budget is around Rs 3,000 and style and calls are the point. Buy an Ultima if you love the look. If you catch yourself comparing sensors, you are in the wrong aisle. Walk over to our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-10000-nepal\">watches under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/boat-ultima-regal.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(85,20,'vivo Mobile Price in Nepal (November 2026)','vivo-phone-price-in-nepal','buying-guide','<p>vivo sells phones across every budget in Nepal, from the Rs 19,999 <a href=\"/products/vivo-y05e\">Y05e</a> to the Rs 1,99,999 <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">X300 Pro</a>. The <strong>Y series</strong> covers budget phones with big batteries, the <strong>V series</strong> is mid-range with slim designs and good selfie cameras, and the <strong>X series</strong> is camera-focused flagships.</p>\n<h2>vivo phones price list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Variant</th><th>Price in Nepal</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05e\">Vivo Y05e</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 19,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05\">Vivo Y05</a></td><td>4GB/64GB</td><td>Rs 24,974</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 28,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y31d\">Vivo Y31d</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 32,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 37,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 43,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 51,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y11d\">Vivo Y11d</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y32\">Vivo Y32</a></td><td>4GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 39,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 45,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>6GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 52,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y21-5g\">Vivo Y21 5G</a></td><td>6GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 40,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-lite-4g\">Vivo V60 Lite 4G</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 43,499</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-lite-5g\">Vivo V60 Lite 5G</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 51,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y500\">Vivo Y500</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 54,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 61,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v70-fe\">Vivo V70 FE</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 62,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 72,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v80-lite-5g\">Vivo V80 Lite</a></td><td>8GB/128GB</td><td>Rs 63,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 73,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">Vivo V60</a></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 74,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 79,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v70\">Vivo V70</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 84,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,02,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>12GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,22,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v80\">vivo V80</a></td><td>8GB/256GB</td><td>Rs 1,08,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>8GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,33,999</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a></td><td>16GB/512GB</td><td>Rs 1,99,999</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tough and long-lasting:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/vivo-y32\">vivo Y32</a> (from Rs 39,999) has an 8,000mAh battery and IP68 water resistance, a combination that\'s hard to find at this price.</li>\n<li><strong>Slim mid-ranger:</strong> the <a href=\"/products/vivo-v80-lite-5g\">V80 Lite</a> is a light phone with a good selfie camera, popular for social media.</li>\n<li><strong>Camera flagship:</strong> the X300 Pro is one of the best camera phones on sale in Nepal. It costs less than the Galaxy S26 Ultra, and its zoom is excellent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cheapest Y-series models start at 4GB/64GB, so pick a 128GB version. The X300 Pro went up in price in September 2026, as covered in <a href=\"/news/why-gadget-prices-rose-nepal-2026\">why gadget prices rose this year</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>How the range fits together</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05\">Vivo Y05</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,974</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1200 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7225 (12nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 6500 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y31d\">Vivo Y31d</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;32,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1250 nits (HBM)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7200 mAh, non-removable; 44W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y32\">Vivo Y32</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>6.87 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 1000 nits (HBM),...</td><td>Unisoc T7300 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 8000 mAh, non-removable; 44W Fast...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-lite-4g\">Vivo V60 Lite 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,499</td><td>6.77 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1800 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v70-fe\">Vivo V70 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;62,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1900 nits (HBM)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7360 Turbo (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">Vivo V60</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>6.77 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1500 nits (HBM), 5...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB / 16GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v70\">Vivo V70</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;84,999</td><td>6.59 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1500 nits (HBM), 5...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,99,999</td><td>6.78-inch BOE Q10+ AMOLED, 2800×1260, 120Hz,...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>6,510mAh; 90W wired, 40W wireless</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Three families</h2>\n<p><strong>Y</strong> is for daily use and long batteries. <strong>V</strong> is for portraits and looks. <strong>X</strong> is for photography without limits. Inside each, a higher number is usually newer, \"Lite\" and \"FE\" are cheaper branches, and a letter such as d or e marks a small variation. vivo\'s phones are sold through a very wide network of dealers, and that is why you see the name in towns where other brands are absent.</p>\n<h2>What you pay for with vivo</h2>\n<p>Read the sheets and a pattern appears. At each price, vivo\'s chip is a step behind a Redmi or Realme. Its battery is large, its charging is good, its body is slim and its camera processing is flattering. The company spends on design, on advertising and on people in shops. For many buyers that is a fair exchange: the phone is there to touch, the seller explains it, and the service centre is nearby. Buyers who read benchmarks should look elsewhere under Rs 60,000.</p>\n<h2>Y series: which ones are worth it</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y05\">Y05</a> is a decent basic phone, as we say in <a href=\"/reviews/vivo-y05-review-nepal\">our review</a>. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y31d\">Y31d</a> at Rs 32,999 is more interesting, with 7,200mAh, 44W charging and a better chip. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y32\">Y32</a> at Rs 39,999 has 8,000mAh. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y21-5g\">Y21 5G</a> adds 5G with an ordinary screen. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-y500\">Y500</a> reaches 8,100mAh and an AMOLED at Rs 54,999, where the Honor X9d is tougher for the same money.</p>\n<p>For a parent, we would choose the Y31d over the Y05 every time. The extra Rs 8,000 buys years.</p>\n<h2>V Lite and FE: the pretty middle</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-lite-4g\">V60 Lite 4G</a> has an AMOLED, 90W charging and a light body at Rs 43,499. It is a 4G phone with an older chip, and it is also lovely to hold. The 5G version at about Rs 52,000 is considerably faster. The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v70-fe\">V70 FE</a> at Rs 62,999 brings a 200MP stabilised camera and 7,000mAh, and it is the one that makes the best case for itself. The V80 Lite 5G at Rs 63,999 is newer with slower charging. Compare the two before paying.</p>\n<h2>V series: portraits</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/vivo-v60-5g\">V60 5G</a> and <a href=\"/products/vivo-v70\">V70</a> carry a proper telephoto camera tuned with Zeiss. That is why to buy them. Faces at two to three metres look natural and the background falls away gently. Wedding guests, parents of small children and people who sell clothes online benefit directly. The Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 is comfortable for everything except serious gaming. The V80 at about Rs 1,09,000 asks a lot: it meets the OnePlus 15R and POCO F8 Pro on price, and the OPPO Find X9 Pro is not much further. Wait for offers.</p>\n<h2>X300 Pro: one of the great cameras</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,99,999 it is expensive and special. The 200MP periscope makes stage, wildlife and portrait photos that no Samsung or iPhone can match at that distance. Battery and charging are excellent. <a href=\"/reviews/vivo-x300-pro-review-nepal\">Read our review</a>. If photography is your pleasure, try it beside a Xiaomi 17 Ultra.</p>\n<h2>Software: OriginOS</h2>\n<p>vivo has replaced Funtouch with OriginOS on newer phones. It is smooth and attractive, with useful tools for photos and a neat lock screen. It includes extra apps and a recommendation page that you can remove. Support is moderate: a few Android versions on V models, fewer on Y, more on X. Ask for the number.</p>\n<h2>Charging standards</h2>\n<p>vivo\'s FlashCharge needs vivo\'s charger for full speed. Phones rated 44W or 90W fall to ordinary speeds with other adapters. The charger is normally in the box in Nepal. Keep it, as a replacement costs Rs 2,000 to 4,000, and copies are unsafe.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: how to set them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>In portraits, lower the beauty setting from its default if skin looks like wax.</li>\n<li>Choose Natural colour for documents and products.</li>\n<li>Use the Zeiss styles on V and X phones for portraits, and stand further back with the telephoto.</li>\n<li>Turn on the grid, and clean the lens. Most haze in night photos is fingerprints.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where vivo phones are cheaper</h2>\n<p>Dealers have room. List prices are firm in advertisements and soft at the counter, especially on V models a few months old. Accessories, a free screen replacement within six months, or exchange bonuses are usual. Ask for the cash equivalent. Around Dashain the V series has seen the largest cuts.</p>\n<h2>Service and parts</h2>\n<p>vivo runs service centres in the main cities with fair prices and quick screen replacements for Y and V models. X series parts may need ordering. Curved screens on older V models cost more to replace, so prefer flat ones if you drop phones.</p>\n<h2>Official and imported</h2>\n<p>Indian vivo units are common near the border. They are cheaper by a few thousand rupees and covered only in India. Check the box for a Nepali distributor\'s sticker and the IMEI in MDMS, as described in <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">our guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>vivo against its neighbours</h2>\n<p><strong>Against OPPO:</strong> similar philosophy. vivo\'s portraits are a little better, OPPO\'s software a little smoother.</p>\n<p><strong>Against Samsung:</strong> vivo gives more battery and charging, and Samsung longer support and resale.</p>\n<p><strong>Against Xiaomi and Realme:</strong> they give more speed, and vivo more polish and a nearer shop.</p>\n<p><strong>Against iPhone:</strong> vivo\'s zoom and battery are ahead, video and longevity behind.</p>\n<h2>Our vivo shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parent or simple use:</strong> Y31d.</li>\n<li><strong>Slim and light under Rs 45,000:</strong> V60 Lite 4G.</li>\n<li><strong>All-round:</strong> V70 FE.</li>\n<li><strong>Portraits:</strong> V60 5G on offer, or V70.</li>\n<li><strong>Photography:</strong> X300 Pro.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Summary</h2>\n<p>vivo makes likeable phones for people who care about how photos and the phone itself look. Skip the cheapest Y models if you can add Rs 8,000, avoid paying list price on the V series, and take the X300 Pro seriously if pictures are your passion. For other brands at the same prices, see <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/vivo-x300-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(86,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 15,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-15000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 15,000 you\'re buying a phone for calls, WhatsApp, Facebook and YouTube, and that\'s fine, as long as you know what you\'re giving up. Every phone at this price uses an entry-level chip and a basic screen.</p>\n<h2>The few worth considering</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Tecno Spark Go 2</a>: from Rs 11,499. The cheapest phone here with a reasonable chip (Unisoc T7250). Pick the 4GB/128GB version at Rs 14,999 if you can.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10\">Infinix Smart 10</a>: Rs 14,999. The same chip as the Spark Go 2, in a slightly more modern design.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-10c\">Blackview Wave 10C</a>: from Rs 14,499. A simple, sturdy everyday phone.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/ai-plus-plus\">Ai+ Pulse</a>: Rs 14,999. Worth a look if you find it in a shop near you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Honest advice</h2>\n<p>Storage is the biggest trap at this price. A 32GB or 64GB phone fills up with WhatsApp photos within months. If you can add a few thousand rupees, the phones in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-20000-nepal\">under Rs 20,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">under Rs 25,000</a> guides are a noticeable step up and will last longer. As a backup phone or a first phone for a child, though, these do the job.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The shortlist in numbers</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Tecno Spark Go 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;11,499</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>3GB / 4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10\">Infinix Smart 10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>3GB / 4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-wave-10c\">Blackview Wave 10C</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,499</td><td>6.88 inches IPS LCD, 60Hz, 430 nits</td><td>Unisoc T310 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 10W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/ai-plus-plus\">Ai+ Pulse</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>6.745 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz</td><td>Unisoc T615 4G (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What Rs 15,000 really pays for</h2>\n<p>After customs, VAT, the distributor\'s margin and the shop\'s margin, very little of your Rs 15,000 is left for the phone itself. Makers therefore choose one of three things to do properly, and save on the rest. Some give you a smooth screen. Some give you storage. Some give you a battery that lasts. Nobody gives you all three. If you know which one you need, you will be happy. If you expect a small version of a Rs 40,000 phone, you will not.</p>\n<h2>Memory first, everything else second</h2>\n<p>Android 15 and 16 are heavier than the Android of five years ago. A 3GB phone spends its life closing apps to make room for others. You switch from Facebook to the camera and back, and Facebook starts again. With 4GB that still happens, only less. So the first rule is simple: do not buy a 3GB phone new in 2026 unless it will be used only for calls.</p>\n<p>The second rule is about storage. The system takes 15 to 20GB. WhatsApp takes another 10GB in a few months if you belong to family groups that share videos every morning. On a 64GB phone you will be deleting things by next Dashain. On 128GB you have a year or two. A memory card helps with photos and videos, though apps still live on the internal storage.</p>\n<h2>Looking at each phone again</h2>\n<p><strong>Tecno Spark Go 2.</strong> The 120Hz screen makes it feel quicker than it is, and the chip is as good as you will find here. It comes in several sizes. The 3GB/64GB version at the bottom is the one shown in advertisements. The 4GB/128GB version costs about Rs 3,500 more and is a different experience. Tecno\'s phones come with extra apps, so spend ten minutes removing them.</p>\n<p><strong>Infinix Smart 10.</strong> Very similar hardware from a sister brand, with a brighter screen in sunlight. It runs the Go edition of Android, built for small memory, which is sensible. The 8MP camera is for documents and daylight.</p>\n<p><strong>Blackview Wave 10C.</strong> It has the largest screen of the group at 6.88 inches with a plain 60Hz refresh rate, and the Unisoc T310 is the slowest chip here. Choose it only for someone who wants large text and simple use.</p>\n<p><strong>Ai+ Pulse.</strong> A newer name in the market. The T615 chip is a little stronger. Before buying, ask where it is serviced and whether parts exist in Nepal. A phone without a service centre is a disposable phone.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: what to expect</h2>\n<p>Outdoors, in good light, all of these take pictures that look fine on WhatsApp. Indoors, at night or with anything that moves, they struggle. The number of lenses on the back is decoration. Two or three of the circles on cheap phones are depth sensors or nothing at all. Judge by taking one photo of the inside of the shop and zooming in.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>Every phone here has about 5,000mAh, and slow chips with HD screens use little energy, so a day and a half is normal. Charging at 10 to 15W takes two and a half to three hours. That means charging at night. If your area has frequent cuts, a small power bank is more useful than a faster charger.</p>\n<h2>Who should buy at this price</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A parent or grandparent who calls, uses Messenger and watches bhajans or news on YouTube.</li>\n<li>A child\'s first phone, with parental controls on.</li>\n<li>A second phone for a shop, a delivery rider or a business SIM.</li>\n<li>A temporary phone while saving for a better one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should wait two months</h2>\n<p>If this will be your only phone, with banking, online classes, TikTok and games, the experience at Rs 15,000 will be frustrating within the year. Adding Rs 6,000 to 10,000 changes everything: more memory, faster charging, a better screen. See what that buys in our guides to phones <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-20000-nepal\">under Rs 20,000</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">under Rs 25,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>What about a used phone?</h2>\n<p>For Rs 15,000 the second-hand market offers two-year-old Redmi, Samsung and Realme phones that were Rs 25,000 to 30,000 new. A clean one is faster than anything on this page. The risks are a tired battery, a replaced screen, and an IMEI that is not registered. Check the last one first, with the method in <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">our guide to official phones</a>. Ask for the original bill, test every function, and keep Rs 2,000 aside for a new battery.</p>\n<h2>Five things to do on the first day</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Uninstall or disable the games and shopping apps you did not ask for.</li>\n<li>Turn off automatic media download in WhatsApp.</li>\n<li>Install Facebook Lite where it is available, or use Facebook in the browser.</li>\n<li>Switch animations to 0.5x in developer options. It makes slow phones feel quicker.</li>\n<li>Set up Find My Device and a screen lock.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Security on cheap phones</h2>\n<p>Budget phones receive few Android updates, sometimes none, and security patches for a limited time. If you use mobile banking or a wallet on one, install apps only from the Play Store, keep the apps updated, never install an APK sent on chat, and do not root the phone. Set a separate PIN for your wallet. These habits protect you more than any antivirus.</p>\n<h2>Our pick</h2>\n<p>For most people with exactly Rs 15,000, we would take the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-go-2\">Tecno Spark Go 2</a> in the 4GB/128GB version, if your shop has it at or under Rs 15,000. It has the smooth screen, enough memory to be usable and enough storage to last. If it is out of stock, the <a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10\">Infinix Smart 10</a> is nearly the same phone with a different badge. And if you can find Rs 1,000 more, the <a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">Infinix Smart 10 Plus</a> adds a larger battery with 128GB as standard, which is why it leads our next guide.</p>','gadgets/2026/infinix-smart-10.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(87,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 20,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-20000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 15,000–20,000 buys a decent everyday phone with enough storage to last. The chips are still entry-level, so these are phones for messaging, social media, video and calls rather than gaming.</p>\n<h2>Our shortlist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">Infinix Smart 10 Plus</a>: Rs 15,999. 4GB/128GB for the price of most 64GB phones. A sensible pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Realme Note 70</a>: from Rs 16,999. A dependable everyday phone from a brand with a big service network.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/honor-choice-t10\">Honor Choice T10</a>: from Rs 16,499. Choose the 4GB/64GB version or above.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a57\">ZTE Nubia A57</a>: from Rs 18,999. Pick the 128GB version.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05e\">vivo Y05e</a>: Rs 19,999. A big battery and vivo\'s wide after-sales network.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to check before paying</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>128GB storage</strong> if at all possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Charging speed:</strong> many phones here charge slowly at 10–15W. Plan on charging overnight.</li>\n<li><strong>That it\'s official.</strong> Here\'s <a href=\"/guides/how-to-check-phone-is-official-in-nepal\">how to check</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stretching to Rs 21,000–25,000 gets you faster charging and better screens. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">phones under Rs 25,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-smart-10-plus\">Infinix Smart 10 Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;15,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4</td><td>Li-Po 6000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-note-70\">Realme Note 70</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,999</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 563 nit (typ)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 6300 mAh, non-removable; 15W VOOC Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-choice-t10\">HONOR CHOICE T10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;16,499</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 450 nits (typ)</td><td>UNISOC T615 (12nm)</td><td>3GB / 4GB</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh; 10W charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/zte-nubia-a57\">ZTE Nubia A57</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;18,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 740 nits (peak)</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 22.5W Fast Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-y05e\">Vivo Y05e</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;19,999</td><td>6.74 inches IPS LCD, 90 Hz, 570 nits (HBM)</td><td>Unisoc T7225 (12nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 6500 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A crowded shelf of similar phones</h2>\n<p>Look at the chip column and you will see nearly the same name on every line. Unisoc\'s T7250 and its relatives are twelve-nanometre processors made for basic phones, and at Rs 20,000 everyone uses one. So speed will not help you choose. What separates these phones is storage, battery, charging speed, the screen and, above all, who repairs them.</p>\n<h2>How to rank them for yourself</h2>\n<p>Take a piece of paper and give each of these a mark from zero to three according to how much it matters to you.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Storage.</strong> If you keep videos and photos, 128GB is worth two marks by itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Battery.</strong> Above 6,000mAh, you can forget the charger for a day and a half.</li>\n<li><strong>Charging.</strong> 18 to 22W saves an hour compared with 10W.</li>\n<li><strong>Screen.</strong> 120Hz scrolls more smoothly. 90Hz is acceptable.</li>\n<li><strong>Service.</strong> Is there a centre within an hour of your home?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most people who do this end up with either the Infinix Smart 10 Plus or the Realme Note 70, for different reasons.</p>\n<h2>The picks, with their faults</h2>\n<p><strong>Infinix Smart 10 Plus.</strong> The strongest sheet here: 128GB as standard, a 6,000mAh battery, 18W charging and a 120Hz screen at Rs 15,999. The fault is the 8MP camera, which is weak indoors, and eMMC storage, which is slower than UFS. If you hardly take photos, this is the best value under Rs 20,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Realme Note 70.</strong> A 6,300mAh battery, a 50MP main camera that copes better in daylight and a wider service network. Charging is 15W. The 64GB version at the advertised price is too small. Look for 128GB, even if it nears Rs 19,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Honor Choice T10.</strong> It looks smart and costs little. With 10W charging, Android Go and as little as 32GB, it is for light use. We list it for people who find it on offer, and we would not pay full price.</p>\n<p><strong>ZTE Nubia A57.</strong> The fastest charging in the list at 22.5W, a 120Hz screen and Android 16. The brand\'s service footprint in Nepal is small, so ask before buying. The 13MP camera is average.</p>\n<p><strong>vivo Y05e.</strong> You pay a little more for vivo\'s reputation and service, and receive 64GB, an 8MP camera and a 6,500mAh battery. For an older relative in a small town with a vivo dealer, the trade makes sense.</p>\n<h2>What nobody tells you about 4GB phones</h2>\n<p>Advertisements speak of \"8GB RAM with extension\". The extension borrows storage and treats it as memory. It can keep a few more apps from closing, but storage is many times slower than memory, so it does not make the phone quick. Count only the physical number. At this price it is 4GB, which is enough for one thing at a time.</p>\n<h2>Games</h2>\n<p>Ludo, Carrom Pool, Subway Surfers and Candy Crush run well. Free Fire is playable at low settings. PUBG Mobile will open and be unpleasant. If someone in your family will definitely use the phone for games, save for an <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a> at around Rs 22,000. Its Dimensity chip is several times faster, and it is the single best Rs 2,000 to 3,000 you can add at this level.</p>\n<h2>4G and calls</h2>\n<p>All of these are 4G phones. On NTC and Ncell that is what you will be using anyway. More important is VoLTE, which carries calls over 4G with clearer sound and less dropping. Insert your SIM in the shop and check that the option exists in network settings and that it works for your operator. Some budget models support it on one operator and not the other.</p>\n<h2>Screens in daylight</h2>\n<p>Most screens here give between 450 and 740 nits. In Terai sunshine you will shade the phone with your hand. If you work outdoors, look at the display in the street before paying, and choose the brighter one over the smoother one.</p>\n<h2>After-sales: a realistic view</h2>\n<p>A cheap phone breaks in expensive ways. A screen can cost Rs 3,000 to 5,000, about a quarter of the phone\'s price. A tempered glass and a case for Rs 500 are the best accessories you can buy. Ask what the warranty covers: usually manufacturing faults for a year on the phone, six months on the charger, and nothing that looks like a drop or water. Keep the bill with the IMEI written on it.</p>\n<h2>Should you stretch to Rs 25,000?</h2>\n<p>If this is your main phone, yes, when you can. Five thousand rupees moves you to faster chips, 25 to 45W charging or a much better brand network. Our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-25000-nepal\">Rs 25,000 guide</a> and <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-30000-nepal\">Rs 30,000 guide</a> show what opens up. Paying the difference by EMI is rarely sensible at this level, since fees can eat the benefit.</p>\n<h2>Best choice by person</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Student with a small budget:</strong> Infinix Smart 10 Plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Parent in a district town:</strong> vivo Y05e or Realme Note 70, whichever has service nearby.</li>\n<li><strong>Delivery rider:</strong> Realme Note 70 for its battery. Add a mount and a power bank.</li>\n<li><strong>Shop counter phone:</strong> ZTE Nubia A57 for quick charging.</li>\n<li><strong>Child:</strong> the cheapest with 128GB, and parental controls from the first day.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Keep it feeling new</h2>\n<p>Restart the phone once a week. Keep 15GB free. Remove apps you have not opened in a month. Update apps over Wi-Fi at night. Charge between 20 and 80 per cent when it is convenient. A basic phone that is kept tidy lasts three years. One that is filled to the last gigabyte feels old in one.</p>\n<h2>A note for people sending a phone home</h2>\n<p>If you are abroad and arranging a phone for parents in Nepal, buy it in Nepal. Ask a relative to collect it from an authorised shop, with a bill in the parents\' name, a case and a glass fitted. Have the shop insert the SIM, turn on VoLTE and set the font to large. Set up video calling before they leave the counter. It costs the same as sending one, and the warranty, charger and registration will all be in order.</p>','gadgets/2026/infinix-smart-10-plus.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(88,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 30,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-30000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 20,000–30,000 is where budget phones start to feel genuinely good. You\'ll find bigger batteries, 120Hz screens and, at the top of the range, AMOLED displays.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a>: Rs 21,999. The fastest chip at this price (Dimensity 7300 Ultimate), a great choice for light gaming.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-50-pro-plus\">Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus</a>: Rs 25,999. A 120Hz AMOLED screen and 8GB/256GB. A lovely screen for the money.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-15-4g\">Redmi 15 4G</a>: from Rs 27,999. A 7,000mAh battery for people who hate charging.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a>: from Rs 24,999. Another 7,000mAh battery, though charging is slow.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a>: from Rs 25,499. The safe brand choice, with long software support.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a> (Rs 21,999) is a rugged alternative with IP68 and a 10,000mAh battery, if you need toughness more than looks.</p>\n<p>Remember that most phones in this range are 4G. That\'s fine on NTC and Ncell today, as we explain in <a href=\"/news/ntc-ncell-5g-rollout-nepal-2024\">our 5G explainer</a>. For a 5G phone, see <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">under Rs 40,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Compare the numbers</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50x\">Infinix Note 50x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>6.67 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 560 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-hot-50-pro-plus\">Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 550 nits (typ), 13...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 (6nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-15-4g\">Redmi 15 4G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;27,999</td><td>6.9 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 700 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 685 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-a6c\">OPPO A6c</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,999</td><td>6.75 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 9...</td><td>Unisoc T7250 (12 nm)</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a07\">Samsung Galaxy A07</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,499</td><td>6.7 inches PLS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/blackview-fort-1\">Blackview Fort 1</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>6.56 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 400 nits (typ) / 4...</td><td>Unisoc T615 (12nm)</td><td>4GB</td><td>Li-Po 10000 mAh, non-removable; 18W charging</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The budget where you can finally choose</h2>\n<p>Below Rs 20,000 you take what you can get. Between Rs 20,000 and 30,000 the market opens into different kinds of phone, and you can pick the one that matches your habits. There is a fast one, a pretty one, two long-lasting ones, a dependable one and a tough one. Here is how to tell which you are.</p>\n<h2>If you play games: Infinix Note 50x</h2>\n<p>The Dimensity 7300 Ultimate is built on a modern 4nm process. In practice that means PUBG Mobile at a steady frame rate, Free Fire at high settings, and a phone that stays cool. The storage is UFS 2.2, quick enough that apps open without a pause. Charging at 45W is strong. The screen is an LCD, the one place where money was saved, and the 8MP selfie camera is plain. At Rs 21,999 no rival is within reach on speed.</p>\n<h2>If you watch more than you play: Infinix Hot 50 Pro Plus</h2>\n<p>A 6.78-inch AMOLED at 120Hz changes films, reels and reading. Blacks are black, colours are lively and text is sharp. With 8GB and up to 256GB, it has room to last. The Helio G100 is fine for daily work and moderate for games. It launched with Android 14, so ask about the updates it will receive. At Rs 25,999 it has the best screen here by a distance.</p>\n<h2>If you dislike charging: Redmi 15 4G or OPPO A6c</h2>\n<p>Both have 7,000mAh. The Redmi costs more and gives you a better chip, a larger 144Hz screen, more memory and 33W charging. The OPPO is cheaper, with 15W charging and 64GB. If you can afford the Redmi, choose it. A 6.9-inch phone is big, so check that your thumb reaches across it.</p>\n<h2>If you want it to be someone else\'s problem: Galaxy A07</h2>\n<p>Samsung has the widest service network and a long update promise for its cheap phones. The hardware is modest. You buy it because it will still be receiving patches when the others have been forgotten, and because the service centre is in your city.</p>\n<h2>If it will be dropped: Blackview Fort 1</h2>\n<p>A thick, rubber-edged phone with IP68 and a 10,000mAh battery. It is for site engineers, guides, drivers and farmers. The chip is slow, the screen is dim and the camera is basic. Nothing else at this price survives a construction site. Ask where it is serviced.</p>\n<h2>What to weigh, in order</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Memory:</strong> 6GB is comfortable, 8GB is better, 4GB is a compromise at this price.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage type:</strong> UFS opens apps faster than eMMC. It matters more than another core in the chip.</li>\n<li><strong>Screen:</strong> AMOLED for contrast, Full HD+ for sharpness, brightness for outdoors.</li>\n<li><strong>Charging:</strong> 33W or more means a useful top-up during lunch.</li>\n<li><strong>Support:</strong> years of security patches, if you keep phones long.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Cameras under Rs 30,000</h2>\n<p>Here is an honest summary. In daylight, all of the 50MP phones produce photos that look good on a phone screen. The differences appear in three places: indoors with children, at night, and when zooming. Optical stabilisation, which helps with the first two, begins at about Rs 36,000 with phones such as the <a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a>. If photographs of your family matter to you, that gap is worth saving for.</p>\n<h2>Do not be fooled by a thin body</h2>\n<p>Shops place slim phones at the front. A slim phone with 5,000mAh lasts a day. A thicker one with 7,000mAh lasts two. Neither is wrong, but know which you are paying for. Also feel the back: glossy plastic slides off a table and a cheap case hides the design anyway.</p>\n<h2>Buying tips for this price band</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Dealers have room to move here. Ask for a tempered glass, a case or a 10,000mAh power bank.</li>\n<li>Infinix and Tecno phones are often sold with exchange bonuses. Compare the final figure.</li>\n<li>Check that the charger is in the box. Samsung\'s is not.</li>\n<li>Insert both SIMs if you use two. Some phones share the second slot with the memory card.</li>\n<li>Confirm the IMEI on the MDMS page.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n<p><strong>Is 4G still fine?</strong> Yes. Commercial 5G in Nepal is limited, and the phones above use 4G efficiently. Our <a href=\"/guides/top-reasons-buy-5g-phone-nepal\">5G article</a> says when it makes sense to pay for it.</p>\n<p><strong>Infinix or Redmi?</strong> Infinix offers more hardware. Redmi has more service points and a larger used market. If you resell phones often, Redmi holds value better.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I use these for online banking safely?</strong> Yes, if you keep the phone updated and install apps from the Play Store only.</p>\n<p><strong>Which is the best camera under Rs 30,000?</strong> In good light, the Note 50x and Galaxy A07. None is strong at night.</p>\n<h2>Where the next step leads</h2>\n<p>Add Rs 4,000 and you reach the <a href=\"/products/redmi-17-5g\">Redmi 17 5G</a> with a 7,500mAh battery and the <a href=\"/products/tecno-spark-40-pro\">Tecno Spark 40 Pro</a> with a brilliant AMOLED. Add Rs 6,000 and the Note 50s brings speed, stabilisation and a 144Hz screen together. If your budget is flexible, read <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-40000-nepal\">phones under Rs 40,000</a> before deciding.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For most people under thirty we would choose the Infinix Note 50x. For people who watch films on the bus, the Hot 50 Pro Plus. For parents, the Galaxy A07. For travellers, the Redmi 15. That four good phones with four different strengths exist under Rs 30,000 is the best thing about this part of the market.</p>','gadgets/2026/honor-x6d.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(89,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 50,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-50000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 35,000–50,000 is the busiest part of the Nepali phone market, and the competition shows. Phones here routinely have AMOLED screens, 120Hz refresh rates and OIS cameras, and some even have IP68 water resistance.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a>: Rs 35,999. The value pick, with an AMOLED screen, a fast Dimensity 7300 Ultimate and OIS for under Rs 36,000.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/poco-m8s\">POCO M8s</a>: from Rs 39,999. A 7,000mAh battery and a 120Hz AMOLED.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m36-5g\">Samsung Galaxy M36 5G</a>: from Rs 41,999. Samsung\'s software and update promise, with an OIS camera.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Motorola Moto G96 5G</a>: Rs 44,999. IP68 and a clean Android experience.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a>: Rs 49,999. IP68, OIS and a 120Hz AMOLED at the top of the budget.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Galaxy A17 5G</a> (from Rs 44,999) and <a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a> are also strong choices. In this range, pick on what matters to you: battery, screen, or how long you\'ll get software updates.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Eight phones, one table</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-note-50s\">Infinix Note 50s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;35,999</td><td>6.78 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 1300 nits (HDR)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultimate (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 5200 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-m8s\">POCO M8s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;39,999</td><td>6.9 inches AMOLED, 144Hz, 700 nits (typ), 850...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s Gen 3 (6 nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-C 7000 mAh, non-removable; 33W Charging; 1...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-m36-5g\">Samsung Galaxy M36 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;41,999</td><td>6.7 inches Super AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (HBM...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1380 (5nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Ion 5000 mAh, non-removable; 25W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-15-5g\">Redmi Note 15 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;43,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 3200 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5520 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/moto-g96\">Motorola Moto G96 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.67 inches P-OLED, 144Hz, 1600 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7s Gen 2 (4 nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-ion 5500 mAh, non-removable; 30W Fast Char...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A17 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.7-inch FHD+ Super AMOLED, 90Hz, 1,100 nits,...</td><td>Exynos 1330</td><td>6GB / 8GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 25W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p3\">Realme P3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 600 nits (Typ), 20...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-14-5g\">Realme 14 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,999</td><td>6.67 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Si-Ca 6000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Start with your own week</h2>\n<p>Before comparing anything, think about last week. How many hours did you spend on video? Did the battery reach the evening? How many photos did you take, and where? Did you play games? How old is your present phone? The right phone for a person who answered \"four hours of reels, dead by six, forty photos of my daughter\" is not the right one for \"two hours of PUBG and no photos at all\". With that in mind, here are the phones by the question they answer.</p>\n<h2>\"My battery never lasts\": POCO M8s, Realme P3, Realme 14 5G</h2>\n<p>The POCO has 7,000mAh and a 6.9-inch screen, and comfortably gets through a day and a half. The two Realmes have 6,000mAh silicon-carbon cells with better chips and 45W charging. If endurance is everything, the <a href=\"/products/honor-x8d\">Honor X8d</a> with 7,000mAh at about Rs 40,000 deserves a look, provided you can live with 4G.</p>\n<h2>\"My photos are blurry\": Redmi Note 15 5G, Moto G96, Galaxy M36 5G</h2>\n<p>All three have optical stabilisation. The Redmi\'s 108MP sensor is the most flexible in daylight. The Motorola\'s Sony sensor is larger and better indoors. Samsung\'s processing is the most consistent, with natural skin tones after a little tuning. Any of these will transform pictures of people who do not sit still.</p>\n<h2>\"My phone is slow\": Realme P3 and Realme 14 5G</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 is a modern 4nm chip with a clear lead over the Exynos 1330 and Snapdragon 6s Gen 3 phones here. The Infinix Note 50s with the Dimensity 7300 Ultimate is close and cheaper, with very fast LPDDR5X memory and UFS 3.1 storage. For smoothness per rupee, the Infinix wins.</p>\n<h2>\"I want it to last five years\": Samsung</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s promise of updates on the A17 and M36 goes well beyond the others. The M36 5G is the better hardware of the two: a stronger Exynos 1380, 120Hz and 8GB as standard, for less money. It is sold mostly online, so the A17 is the one you see in shops. If you can find an official M36 at Rs 41,999, it is the better buy. Read <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a17-5g-review-nepal\">our A17 review</a> for what Samsung does at this price.</p>\n<h2>\"I want it to feel expensive\": Moto G96</h2>\n<p>A curved 144Hz pOLED, a soft back and 178 grams. No other phone under Rs 50,000 is as pleasant to hold. The battery is average and service is limited. <a href=\"/reviews/moto-g96-review-nepal\">Our review</a> has the details.</p>\n<h2>\"It will get wet\": Realme P3 or Realme 14 5G</h2>\n<p>Both carry high IP ratings. They are the sensible choice for people who ride, trek or work in kitchens.</p>\n<h2>One phone for most people</h2>\n<p>If a friend asked us for one name without any explanation, we would say the <strong>Redmi Note 15 5G</strong>. Its screen reaches 3,200 nits, the main camera is stabilised, it has 5G, 256GB in the base version and a service centre in most cities. It does nothing best and nothing badly. The version with 8GB is worth the extra.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the differences in screens</h2>\n<p>Every phone in the list uses an OLED-type panel, so the fight is about brightness and smoothness. Peak numbers such as 3,200 or 2,000 nits apply to small areas in HDR video. The figure to look for is high brightness mode, the level you get in sunlight. Above 1,000 nits is good. POCO\'s 850 is the weakest here, and you will notice it outdoors. 144Hz on the Infinix, POCO and Moto is pleasant, but few people can separate it from 120Hz.</p>\n<h2>Memory and storage</h2>\n<p>At this price, accept nothing less than 8GB/128GB, and try for 256GB. The base versions of the A17, M8s and Note 15 start with 6GB. It is the small compromise that you will notice in the third year. Check whether the phone has a card slot if you shoot a lot of video. Most AMOLED phones have dropped it.</p>\n<h2>Price tactics</h2>\n<p>This is the most competitive shelf in the country, and dealers know you have eight choices. Use that. Ask two shops for their final price, including exchange, and tell each what the other offered. Watch for models about to be replaced. The Realme 14 5G and Infinix Note 50s are from 2025, and real discounts on them appear often. Look at their movement on our <a href=\"/price-tracker\">price tracker</a>.</p>\n<h2>What Rs 20,000 more would bring</h2>\n<p>Phones at Rs 60,000 to 70,000, covered in <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-70000-nepal\">the next guide</a>, add flagship-class speed, 90 to 100W charging, IP68 and better zoom. If you keep a phone for four years, that is Rs 400 a month. If you change every two years, it is Rs 800. Think about it in those terms, and not as one large sum.</p>\n<h2>Three mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Buying on megapixels. A 108MP camera without stabilisation loses to a stabilised 50MP one after sunset.</li>\n<li>Buying on 5G alone. See whether your street has it first.</li>\n<li>Buying the last piece on display. Display phones have been on full brightness for months.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Before you pay</h2>\n<p>Check the IMEI in MDMS, the bill, the warranty card and the variant. Test the fingerprint reader, as the ones under cheaper AMOLED screens vary. Make a call. Take a video while walking, to see the stabilisation at work. Then enjoy it, and stop reading about phones for at least a year.</p>','gadgets/2026/realme-14-5g.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(90,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 70,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-70000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>At Rs 55,000–70,000 you can buy a phone that\'s close to flagship speed. Several models here use chips from the top tier, and many add IP68 water resistance.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">POCO F7</a>: Rs 69,999. A Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 and IP68, the fastest phone at this price.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro\">POCO X8 Pro</a>: Rs 69,999. The Dimensity 8500 Ultra and IP68, an alternative to the F7.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a>: Rs 59,999. A 7,100mAh battery and OxygenOS, a smooth, long-lasting phone.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max</a>: Rs 69,999. A 10,000mAh battery and 100W charging.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/vivo-v70-fe\">vivo V70 FE</a>: from Rs 62,999. A slim design with a 7,000mAh battery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If cameras matter most, the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> (Rs 69,999) has a periscope zoom, rare at this price. For Samsung fans, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Galaxy A37 5G</a> costs a little more but gets long software support.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Compared at a glance</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f7\">Poco F7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 700 nits (typ), 17...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 90W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro\">Poco X8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.59 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 20...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8500 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-ce-5\">OnePlus Nord CE5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;59,999</td><td>6.77 inches Fluid AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (ty...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 8350 Apex (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 7100 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-note-17-pro-max-5g\">Redmi Note 17 Pro Max 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.83-inch 1.5K CrystalRes AMOLED, 120Hz, 3,50...</td><td>Snapdragon 6 Gen 5 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>10,000mAh silicon-carbon; 100W wired, 27W rev...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/vivo-v70-fe\">Vivo V70 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;62,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 1900 nits (HBM)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7360 Turbo (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Si-Ca 7000 mAh, non-removable; 90W FlashCharg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone (4a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;69,999</td><td>6.78-inch AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass 7i</td><td>Snapdragon 7s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>5,080mAh; 50W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g\">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>6.7-inch Super AMOLED, 120Hz, Gorilla Glass V...</td><td>Exynos 1480 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB</td><td>5,000mAh; 45W wired</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The most interesting money in phones</h2>\n<p>Spend Rs 70,000 and you can buy a phone that plays any game, or one that lasts three days, or one with a real zoom lens, or one that will be updated until 2032. You cannot buy all four. Two years ago that would have cost a lakh and a half, and it is why we think this band offers the greatest value for people who use their phones hard.</p>\n<h2>POCO F7: for speed</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 with 12GB and 512GB of UFS 4.1 storage is close to flagship speed at less than half the price. Games run at high settings and stay there. IP68, 90W charging and a 6,500mAh battery complete the sheet. The weak points are an ordinary ultra wide and no zoom, HyperOS with its extra apps, and a chunky feel. <a href=\"/reviews/poco-f7-review-nepal\">Our review</a> goes through them.</p>\n<h2>POCO X8 Pro: for speed in a smaller body</h2>\n<p>With a 6.59-inch screen it is easier to hold than the F7, and the Dimensity 8500 Ultra is just as quick in daily life. It charges at 100W. If you find most phones too big, look here first. Compare prices by memory version, because the F7\'s 12GB/512GB at the same price is the better deal if you want storage.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Note 17 Pro Max: for battery</h2>\n<p>Ten thousand milliampere-hours with 100W charging, in a phone that is still reasonable to hold. People who travel for work, guide treks or simply hate chargers will find it life-changing. The chip is a class below the POCOs, good for everything except the heaviest games. Read <a href=\"/reviews/redmi-note-17-pro-max-review-nepal\">the review</a>.</p>\n<h2>Nothing Phone 4a: for character and zoom</h2>\n<p>A 50MP periscope with 3.5x optical zoom is rare under Rs 1 lakh. It makes portraits look natural and brings stages and mountains closer. The software is clean and distinct, and the lights on the back are either charming or silly, depending on you. The battery is average at about 5,000mAh. See <a href=\"/reviews/nothing-phone-4a-review-nepal\">our review</a>.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus Nord CE5: for balance</h2>\n<p>At Rs 59,999 it is the cheapest of the group. It brings a Dimensity 8350 Apex, 7,100mAh, 80W charging and the smoothness of OxygenOS. Cameras are fine and nothing more. If you want to spend less than Rs 60,000 and avoid weaknesses, this is the phone.</p>\n<h2>vivo V70 FE: for portraits and style</h2>\n<p>A slim phone with a 200MP main camera and 7,000mAh, aimed at people who share photos. vivo\'s portrait processing flatters faces, and the service network is wide. The chip is adequate. Compare it with the Nothing if you care about zoom.</p>\n<h2>Galaxy A37 5G: for the long term</h2>\n<p>It has drifted above Rs 70,000 and is not the fastest. Six years of updates, IP68 and Samsung\'s service are the reasons to choose it. <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-a37-5g-review-nepal\">Our review</a> is frank about the price.</p>\n<h2>How to decide in three questions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Do you play demanding games more than three hours a week?</strong> POCO F7 or X8 Pro. Stop here.</li>\n<li><strong>Is running out of battery your main complaint?</strong> Redmi Note 17 Pro Max. Stop here.</li>\n<li><strong>Do you take more than ten photos a week of people?</strong> Nothing Phone 4a or vivo V70 FE. If not, Nord CE5 for value or Galaxy A37 for longevity.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What to know about charging watts</h2>\n<p>Ninety and one hundred watt charging work only with the right brick and cable. POCO and Redmi include them in most Nepali boxes. Check yours. With an ordinary USB-C charger they drop to about 18 to 27W. Fast charging does warm the battery, so use it when you need it and a slower one overnight. The phones manage themselves well, and modern cells keep most of their capacity for three or four years.</p>\n<h2>Water resistance at this level</h2>\n<p>IP68 is common now, on the POCOs, Redmi and Samsung. It means that a fall into a bucket is not the end. It does not mean swimming. Seals age, a repaired phone loses them, and warranty does not cover liquid. Treat it as insurance you hope never to claim.</p>\n<h2>Updates compared</h2>\n<p>Samsung leads with six Android versions on the A37. OnePlus and Nothing promise several versions and up to six years of patches on recent models, depending on the phone. POCO and Redmi have improved with around four versions on these series. vivo\'s FE models are shorter. Ask the seller for the number in writing, or check the maker\'s site. If you keep a phone for three years, all are enough.</p>\n<h2>New or last year\'s flagship?</h2>\n<p>Seventy thousand also buys a used Galaxy S24 or iPhone 14 in good condition. You get a better main camera, wireless charging and a premium body, along with a worn battery and no warranty. For photographers who can check a used phone it is a serious option. For everyone else, new phones at this price are so good that the risk is hard to justify. Our <a href=\"/reviews/samsung-galaxy-s24-review-snapdragon-8-gen-3-flagship-value\">Galaxy S24 review</a> explains how it has aged.</p>\n<h2>Final advice</h2>\n<p>Try the POCO F7 and Nothing Phone 4a side by side if you can. They are opposites: one is all engine, the other all character. Whichever you prefer after five minutes in the hand tells you which side of this list to buy from. And if neither attracts you, you may be a Nord CE5 person, which is a compliment: you want a phone that works and leaves Rs 10,000 in your pocket.</p>','gadgets/2026/poco-f7.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(91,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 1,00,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-100000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Just under a lakh is where \"flagship killers\" live: phones with top-tier chips and huge batteries that cost half what the big-name flagships do.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a>: from Rs 98,499. Flagship-level speed for under a lakh, and our pick for most people at this budget.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">POCO X8 Pro Max</a>: Rs 94,999. An 8,500mAh battery and the Dimensity 9500s.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">OnePlus Nord 6</a>: Rs 89,999. A 7,500mAh battery and a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a>: Rs 76,999. A 10,001mAh battery. It\'ll last two days of heavy use.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/honor-600\">Honor 600</a>: from Rs 84,999. A good-looking phone with a strong camera.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Spending this much, it\'s worth checking software support. Samsung, Google and OnePlus promise more years of updates than most. If you can stretch, the <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-fe\">Galaxy S26 FE</a> is covered in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-150000-nepal\">under Rs 1,50,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The field</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-15r\">OnePlus 15R</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;98,499</td><td>6.83-inch AMOLED, 165Hz, Dolby Vision, HDR10+</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,400mAh; 80W wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-x8-pro-max\">Poco X8 Pro Max</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;94,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ), 20...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500s (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 8500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-6\">OnePlus Nord 6</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>6.78-inch 1.5K AMOLED, 165Hz, 1,800 nits HBM,...</td><td>Snapdragon 8s Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>7,500mAh; 80W wired, 27W reverse wired</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600\">Honor 600</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;84,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 8000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Hono...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-p4-power\">Realme P4 Power</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,999</td><td>6.8-inch quad-curved 1.5K AMOLED, 144Hz, 6,50...</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 7400 Ultra (4nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>10,001mAh silicon-carbon; 80W wired, 27W reve...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">Poco F8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,04,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HBM), 3...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What changes just below a lakh</h2>\n<p>At Rs 70,000 you could have speed, battery or zoom. Here you can have two of them, sometimes with a third thrown in. Chips come from the top family, batteries pass 7,000mAh, screens reach 165Hz and memory starts at 12GB. What you still do not get, mostly, is a flagship camera system with a long zoom, wireless charging and the very longest support. Those begin above Rs 1.2 lakh.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus 15R: our choice for most buyers</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 8 Gen 5 is one step below the chip in two-lakh phones, and you would need a stopwatch to notice. The 165Hz AMOLED is superb, the 7,400mAh battery lasts well past a day, and OxygenOS is among the cleanest versions of Android. Cameras are good, with an 8MP ultra wide that lets the side down and no telephoto. If you want one phone under a lakh that will not irritate you for four years, this is it.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus Nord 6: nearly the same, for less</h2>\n<p>For about Rs 8,500 less you get a Snapdragon 8s Gen 4, a similar screen and a slightly larger battery. The differences in daily use are tiny. We would buy the Nord 6 unless you play the heaviest games, and use the difference for earbuds. The base version has 8GB. Choose 12GB if you can.</p>\n<h2>POCO X8 Pro Max: for people who finish batteries</h2>\n<p>An 8,500mAh cell with 100W charging and a Dimensity 9500s. It is a gaming phone in all but name. It is large and heavy, and HyperOS needs tidying on the first evening. If you stream, hotspot and play every day, nothing else here keeps up.</p>\n<h2>Honor 600: for a compact hand and a good camera</h2>\n<p>At 6.57 inches, with a 200MP stabilised main camera and a 12MP ultra wide, it is the most camera-minded choice here. Its 7,000mAh battery in that size is remarkable. The Snapdragon 7 Gen 4 is a class below the others, fine for anything except extreme gaming. Honor\'s service network is growing but still smaller, so ask first.</p>\n<h2>Realme P4 Power: two days, guaranteed</h2>\n<p>Ten thousand milliampere-hours. It costs about Rs 77,000, leaving money in your pocket. The chip is mid-range. It is for a driver, a guide or a traveller, as <a href=\"/reviews/realme-p4-power-review-nepal\">our review</a> explains.</p>\n<h2>And the tempting Rs 5,000 more</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">POCO F8 Pro</a> at Rs 1,04,999 brings a Snapdragon 8 Elite, 12GB/512GB and IP68. If your budget has any give, it offers the most performance for each rupee in the Nepali market today. It is in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-under-150000-nepal\">next guide</a> for that reason.</p>\n<h2>Performance in perspective</h2>\n<p>All of these phones run every app in the Play Store smoothly. The difference between them shows only in long gaming sessions and heavy video editing. So rank them by things you can feel daily instead: weight, size, screen, software, battery and camera.</p>\n<h2>Size and weight matter more than you expect</h2>\n<p>Hold each one for five minutes with a case on. A 6.83-inch, 220 gram phone is wonderful for films and tiring in bed. The Honor 600 is the only truly one-handed phone in the list. If you come from an iPhone or a small Samsung, try it before deciding that you need the biggest screen.</p>\n<h2>Cameras without zoom</h2>\n<p>With the exception of the Honor\'s high-resolution crop, these phones use a single serious camera. For food, people, streets and documents they are great. For portraits at a distance, school stages and mountains, a telephoto lens matters. If that is your life, two options sit on either side: the <a href=\"/products/nothing-phone-4a\">Nothing Phone 4a</a> with a 3.5x periscope at Rs 69,999, or a camera flagship above Rs 1.2 lakh.</p>\n<h2>Support and repairs</h2>\n<p>OnePlus promises several Android versions and up to six years of patches on the R series. Ask for the exact number on the model. POCO and Realme are a little shorter, and Honor has improved. For repairs, Realme and Xiaomi have more centres than OnePlus and Honor. A screen on any of these costs between Rs 15,000 and 30,000, so insurance or at least a decent protector is not a luxury.</p>\n<h2>iPhone or Samsung at this price?</h2>\n<p>A new iPhone under a lakh means an older generation from remaining stock, and a new Galaxy S means a discounted older model. An iPhone 15 or a Galaxy S24 can still be a good purchase if you value the ecosystem, the cameras and the resale price. You give up battery and charging speed. Check that older stock has not been activated and that the battery has been stored well.</p>\n<h2>Paying for it</h2>\n<p>At a lakh, the method of payment matters. EMI over twelve months at zero interest is only free if the cash price is the same. Ask for both. Bank cards have caps on cashback. An exchange bonus is worth it if it beats the private sale price of your old phone by more than your time is worth. The EMI estimate on each product page shows the monthly amount.</p>\n<h2>Who should choose what</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday user with high standards:</strong> OnePlus 15R.</li>\n<li><strong>Value hunter:</strong> OnePlus Nord 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Gamer and streamer:</strong> POCO X8 Pro Max, or stretch to the POCO F8 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Small hands, many photos:</strong> Honor 600.</li>\n<li><strong>Road warrior:</strong> Realme P4 Power.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our last word</h2>\n<p>Nobody needs to spend a lakh on a phone to have a good one. But if you use it five hours a day and keep it for four years, a lakh works out at under Rs 70 a day, or about Rs 14 for each hour of use. Choose the one that fits your hand and your evenings, and ignore the last ten per cent of benchmark scores.</p>','gadgets/2026/poco-x8-pro-max.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(92,20,'Best Phones Under Rs 1,50,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-phones-under-150000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Between one lakh and one and a half lakh, you\'re choosing between the best of the \"value flagships\" and the cheaper models from the big brands.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">POCO F8 Pro</a>: Rs 1,04,999. A Snapdragon 8 Elite and IP68 for just over a lakh. The speed-per-rupee pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-fe\">Samsung Galaxy S26 FE</a>: Rs 1,24,999. A Samsung flagship design, IP68 and long software support.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10\">Google Pixel 10</a>: Rs 1,28,000. Google\'s camera and clean software.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a>: Rs 1,25,999. A 7,500mAh battery and the Dimensity 9500, a serious flagship for the money.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a>: from Rs 1,29,999. A Snapdragon 8 Elite and a 7,000mAh battery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Compact phone fans should look at the <a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">OnePlus 13s</a> (Rs 1,19,999). The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Galaxy S26</a> (Rs 1,39,999) is the smallest current Samsung flagship. For the absolute best, see the flagships in our <a href=\"/guides/best-phones-in-nepal-by-budget\">phones by budget</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Seven phones worth the money</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/poco-f8-pro\">Poco F8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,04,999</td><td>6.83 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HBM), 3...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 6500 mAh, non-removable; 100W HyperChar...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-13s\">OnePlus 13s</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,19,999</td><td>6.32 inches LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>16GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Si-Ca 5850 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-fe\">Samsung Galaxy S26 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,24,999</td><td>6.7 inches Dynamic AMOLED 2X, 120Hz, 1, 900 n...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 2500 (3nm)</td><td>8GB</td><td>Li-Po 4900 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oppo-find-x9-pro\">OPPO Find X9 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,25,999</td><td>6.78 inches LTPO AMOLED, 120Hz, 800 nits (typ...</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 9500 (3nm)</td><td>12GB / 16GB</td><td>Si-Ca 7500 mAh, non-removable; 80W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/google-pixel-10\">Google Pixel 10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,28,000</td><td>6.3 inches OLED, 120Hz, 2000 nits (HDR), 3000...</td><td>Google Tensor G5 (3nm)</td><td>12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Ion 4970 mAh, non-removable; 30W USB-PD Ch...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-600-pro\">Honor 600 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>6.57 inches AMOLED, 120Hz, 8000 nits (HDR)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite (3nm)</td><td>12GB</td><td>Si/C Li-Ion 7000 mAh, non-removable; 80W Hono...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26\">Samsung Galaxy S26</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>6.3-inch FHD+ Dynamic AMOLED 2X, LTPO 1–120Hz...</td><td>Exynos 2600 (2nm) in most markets; Snapdragon...</td><td>12GB</td><td>4,300mAh; 25W wired, 15W wireless, 4.5W rever...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Two kinds of phone live here</h2>\n<p>Between a lakh and a lakh and a half there are phones from value brands that match flagships on the sheet, and entry flagships from big brands that match them in polish. A POCO F8 Pro is faster than a Galaxy S26 FE and costs Rs 20,000 less. The Samsung takes more dependable photos, has wireless charging and will be repaired in any city until 2033. Decide which of those sentences you care about, and half the list disappears.</p>\n<h2>OPPO Find X9 Pro: the surprise</h2>\n<p>At Rs 1,25,999 this is a complete flagship. The Dimensity 9500 is among the fastest chips made, the 7,500mAh battery is larger than any rival\'s, and the camera system with a 1/1.28-inch main sensor and a high-resolution telephoto belongs with phones that cost two lakhs. If photography and battery are both important, nothing else under Rs 1.5 lakh gives you so much. Check that the unit is official, as the price in different shops varies a lot.</p>\n<h2>POCO F8 Pro: the fastest for the price</h2>\n<p>Snapdragon 8 Elite, 12GB/512GB, IP68, 100W charging. It is the phone for players and for people who keep twenty apps open. The main camera is good, the rest are fine. The software carries extra apps that need removing.</p>\n<h2>Google Pixel 10: the cleverest camera</h2>\n<p>Pixels do more with software than anyone. Faces look natural, night photos are calm, and features such as Best Take and call screening feel useful. The 6.3-inch size is comfortable, and support runs for seven years. Two cautions for Nepal: there is no official Google service, so warranty depends on the importer, and the Tensor chip runs behind rivals in games. Confirm MDMS registration and VoLTE on your SIM before paying.</p>\n<h2>Galaxy S26 FE and Galaxy S26</h2>\n<p>The FE gives you Samsung\'s flagship look, a large screen, a telephoto lens and seven years of updates, with the Exynos 2500. At Rs 1,24,999 it is for people who want a big Samsung without paying two lakhs. The S26 at Rs 1,39,999 is smaller, faster and better made, with a weaker battery. If the larger screen is not essential, the S26 is worth Rs 15,000 more. If battery matters, neither is the best choice on this page.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus 13s: the small powerhouse</h2>\n<p>A 6.32-inch phone with a Snapdragon 8 Elite, a 2x telephoto and a 5,850mAh battery is rare. It charges at 80W and fits any pocket. The cameras are a step behind the Pixel and OPPO, and the ultra wide is missing. For people who miss small phones, it is the one.</p>\n<h2>Honor 600 Pro: the compact camera phone</h2>\n<p>A 200MP stabilised main camera with a 50MP telephoto in a 6.57-inch body, with a Snapdragon 8 Elite and 7,000mAh. On paper it is the best balanced phone in the list. Service and resale are the questions. If there is an Honor centre near you, take it seriously.</p>\n<h2>Cameras: how to judge at this level</h2>\n<p>Every phone here takes a good photo at noon. Judge them on three difficult shots: a person indoors in the evening, a moving child and a subject across the street. Ask the shop to let you try. A telephoto lens of 3x or more makes the third possible. Optical stabilisation and fast processing decide the first two. The Pixel and Samsung are the most reliable, the OPPO and Honor the most ambitious.</p>\n<h2>Battery: the surprising gap</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy S26 has 4,300mAh. The OPPO has 7,500. That is not a small difference. Samsung and Google have been slow to adopt silicon-carbon cells, and it shows by six in the evening. If you are out from seven to seven, give battery a high place in your list.</p>\n<h2>Should you buy last year\'s two-lakh phone?</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy S25 Ultra has been seen at well under its original price, and the iPhone 16 and 17 sit close to this range, at Rs 1,46,599 and Rs 1,64,999 in our listings. If you want an iPhone, the <a href=\"/products/iphone-17e\">iPhone 17e</a> at Rs 1,32,499 is the cheapest current one, with a single rear camera. Choose it for iOS and not for its hardware.</p>\n<h2>Resale value</h2>\n<p>If you change phones every two years, resale is part of the price. Samsung\'s S series and iPhones hold value best. Pixels hold it moderately, OnePlus less, POCO, Honor and OPPO least. A phone that costs Rs 1,40,000 and sells for Rs 70,000 after two years costs the same to own as one bought at Rs 1,05,000 and sold for Rs 35,000. Include that in your sums.</p>\n<h2>Paying and protecting</h2>\n<p>At this price, take a VAT bill, register the warranty online, and consider screen protection plans. Samsung Care+ and similar schemes cost a few thousand rupees and cover the most likely accident. Ask for the cash price, the EMI price and the exchange offer separately, and compare totals.</p>\n<h2>Quick recommendations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best all-round value:</strong> OPPO Find X9 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for games:</strong> POCO F8 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for service and resale:</strong> Galaxy S26.</li>\n<li><strong>Best photos without effort:</strong> Pixel 10, if you accept importer warranty.</li>\n<li><strong>Best small phone:</strong> OnePlus 13s.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for five years or more:</strong> Galaxy S26 FE or Pixel 10.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Is it worth going above Rs 1.5 lakh?</h2>\n<p>For most people, no. Above this line you pay for longer zoom, larger sensors, titanium frames, pens and status. They are real, and they matter to some. A professional who earns from photos or video can justify a <a href=\"/products/vivo-x300-pro\">vivo X300 Pro</a> or a <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-s26-ultra\">Galaxy S26 Ultra</a>. For the rest of us, the phones on this page are already better than we are.</p>','gadgets/2026/poco-x8-pro-max.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(93,20,'Best Laptops Under Rs 80,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-laptops-under-80000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 80,000 you can still buy a perfectly good laptop for school, college and office work, as long as you avoid the weakest chips.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">ASUS Vivobook 15 X1504VA</a>: Rs 79,999. A Core i5 and 16GB of RAM. The memory makes it the one most likely to stay usable for years.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-15-ag15-21pt-2024-ryzen-5-7520u\">Acer Aspire Go 15 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a>: Rs 76,000. A capable 4-core chip for study and office work.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-2025\">Dell 15 DC15250</a>: from Rs 78,999. Simple and well supported, with a 120Hz screen.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-14amn8-2023\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 14AMN8</a>: Rs 66,999. Light and cheap, for light work only.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to avoid</h2>\n<p>Laptops with Intel Celeron or N100 chips and 4GB of RAM, such as the Dell Wyse 5470 at the bottom of this range, are frustratingly slow on modern Windows. And remember that 8GB of RAM is fine for now but tight for the future. If you can reach a lakh, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">laptops under Rs 1,00,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The shortlist</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Asus VivoBook 15 X1504VA 2023 (Intel Core i5-1334U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>Intel Core i5-1334U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-go-15-ag15-21pt-2024-ryzen-5-7520u\">Acer Aspire Go 15 AG15-21PT 2024 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;76,000</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U (6nm), 4 cores / 8 threads</td><td>8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 53 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-15-dc15250-2025\">Dell 15 DC15250 2025</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;78,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, FHD (1920 x 1080 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core 3 100U (Intel 7), 6 cores / 8 thre...</td><td>8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 41 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-14amn8-2023\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 14AMN8 (Athlon Silver 7120U, 8GB/256GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;66,499</td><td>14&quot; FHD (1920x1080) TN, 250 nits, anti-glare</td><td>AMD Athlon™ Silver 7120U (2C / 2T, 2.4 / 3.5G...</td><td>8GB Soldered LPDDR5-5500</td><td>47Wh</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-14-al14-45p-ryzen-3-5400u\">Acer Aspire Lite 14 AL14-45P (Ryzen 3 5400U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;73,999</td><td>14&quot; WUXGA IPS Display (1920×1200, 300 nits, 1...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 3 5400U Processor (4 Cores / 8 Thr...</td><td>8GB DDR4 RAM</td><td>58Wh, 5100mAh, 11.4V, 3-Cell Li-ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-14iru8-core-i3-1315u\">Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 3 14IRU8 (Core i3-1315U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>14&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare</td><td>Intel Core™ i3-1315U, 6C (2P + 4E) / 8T, P-co...</td><td>8GB Soldered LPDDR5-4800</td><td>47Wh</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>One machine stands apart</h2>\n<p>Look down the memory column and one line is different. The Vivobook 15 X1504VA with a Core i5-1334U, 16GB and 512GB for about Rs 80,000 is a stronger laptop than several that cost a lakh. It is a 2023 model and stock is uneven, so call before travelling. If you find it new with an official warranty, the rest of this page is academic. Buy it.</p>\n<p>If you cannot find it, the choice is between machines with 8GB, and then it becomes a matter of processor, storage, screen and service.</p>\n<h2>Reading a budget processor</h2>\n<p>Names are confusing on purpose, so here is a ranking of what you will meet under Rs 80,000, from most to least capable for normal work:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Core i5-1334U:</strong> ten cores, quick in everything ordinary.</li>\n<li><strong>Core i3-1315U and Core 3 100U:</strong> six cores, close to each other, good for study and office.</li>\n<li><strong>Ryzen 3 5400U:</strong> four solid cores of an older generation.</li>\n<li><strong>Ryzen 5 7520U:</strong> four efficient cores, despite the \"5\" in its name.</li>\n<li><strong>Core 3 N355 or i3-N305:</strong> eight small cores, fine for light use.</li>\n<li><strong>Athlon Silver 7120U:</strong> two cores. Emails and little else.</li>\n<li><strong>N100, N150, Celeron:</strong> for a single simple task.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The IdeaPad Slim 3 14AMN8 in our list uses the Athlon. It is there as the cheapest acceptable machine from a good brand, and it should be treated as a typewriter with a browser. Its TN screen also loses colour when viewed from the side.</p>\n<h2>Why we keep saying 16GB</h2>\n<p>Windows 11 with a browser, Word and Teams uses about 8GB. On an 8GB laptop it borrows from the SSD all day. You feel it as short waits. In 2026 these are tolerable. In 2029, with heavier websites and programs, they will not be. If you buy an 8GB model, ask whether there is a free slot, because a second stick turns a four-year laptop into a six-year one. Some models, such as those with LPDDR5, have memory soldered and can never be upgraded.</p>\n<h2>Screens at this price</h2>\n<p>Expect Full HD, 250 nits and modest colour. The Dell 15 DC15250 offers 120Hz, which makes scrolling smoother, a pleasant extra. Make sure that the panel is IPS and not TN. To check, tilt the lid back and look from above. If the picture turns negative or pale, it is TN. For long reading, matte is better than glossy.</p>\n<h2>Storage</h2>\n<p>256GB is the minimum and 512GB is comfortable. An SSD can normally be replaced later for a few thousand rupees, so storage should not decide your purchase unless the machine has 128GB or uses slow eMMC, which is soldered.</p>\n<h2>Which one for which person</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bachelor-level student in any subject:</strong> Vivobook 15 X1504VA.</li>\n<li><strong>School student:</strong> Acer Aspire Lite 14 or IdeaPad Slim 3 14 with Core i3.</li>\n<li><strong>Office and billing:</strong> Dell 15 DC15250, for service and a smooth screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Home and parents:</strong> Acer Aspire Go 15, for its larger battery and number pad.</li>\n<li><strong>Programming student:</strong> the Vivobook, or wait until you can reach <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-100000-nepal\">Rs 1 lakh</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The used alternative</h2>\n<p>For Rs 50,000 to 75,000, used ThinkPads, Latitudes and EliteBooks from foreign offices come with 16GB, good keyboards and metal bodies. Many have an 11th or 12th generation Core i5, faster than most new laptops here. Check four things: battery health with the powercfg report, hinge tightness, screen marks, and whether a BIOS or company lock is present. Boot it, restart it, and make sure no \"managed by organisation\" message appears. A shop that gives three to six months of its own warranty is worth paying a little more.</p>\n<h2>What these laptops cannot do</h2>\n<p>They do not run modern games, 3D modelling or long video edits. They will run Valorant at low settings on the better chips and edit a short family video. If your course needs AutoCAD 3D, Revit, Blender or Premiere, saving for a machine with a graphics card is the honest advice. See <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptops</a>, as they double as engineering machines.</p>\n<h2>Costs after the purchase</h2>\n<p>Plan Rs 3,000 to 6,000 for a sleeve or bag, a mouse, and Office if you need the desktop version. Free alternatives work well: LibreOffice for documents, Google Docs for sharing, and Windows Security for protection. Do not pay a shop for pirated software. It is the most common way laptops in Nepal get infected.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and the bill</h2>\n<p>Ask for one year of official brand warranty, with the name of the service centre. \"Shop warranty\" on a new sealed laptop suggests an import. Check the serial number on the brand\'s website in front of the seller. Make sure that the VAT bill carries the serial number.</p>\n<h2>First evening checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Run Windows Update until nothing remains.</li>\n<li>Remove trial antivirus and shopping apps.</li>\n<li>Set the battery limit to 80 per cent if it lives on a desk.</li>\n<li>Turn on File History or cloud backup.</li>\n<li>Create a recovery USB stick.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our advice in short</h2>\n<p>Find the Vivobook with 16GB. If you cannot, buy the best 8GB machine with a free memory slot, and add a stick within the year. And if you can wait until a sale brings the Rs 95,000 machines within reach, the wait will be rewarded for years.</p>','gadgets/2026/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(94,20,'Best Laptops Under Rs 1,00,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-laptops-under-100000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>A lakh is the budget we\'d suggest for a laptop you want to keep for four or five years. It\'s enough for 16GB of RAM, a decent processor and a 512GB SSD.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">MSI Modern 14 F1MG</a>: Rs 93,000. In its Core 7 150U configuration with 16GB and 1TB, it\'s excellent value.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</a>: Rs 97,999. A 6-core Ryzen and 16GB.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15 (Ryzen 5 7520U)</a>: from Rs 90,999. Pick the 16GB version.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">ASUS Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</a>: Rs 89,999. An OLED screen and Core Ultra 5 125H at this price is rare, but it only has 8GB of RAM.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Also worth a look: the <a href=\"/products/dell-pro-15-pv15255-2025\">Dell Pro 15</a> with a 120Hz screen, and the <a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Vivobook 15 X1504VA</a> if you want to save Rs 15,000–20,000.</p>\n<p>None of these are for gaming or heavy video work. For that, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-gaming-laptops-nepal\">gaming laptop guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The candidates</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/msi-modern-14-2024-intel-core-7-150u\">MSI Modern 14 F1MG 2024</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;93,000</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pixe...</td><td>Intel Core 7 150U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12 th...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Po 46.8 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-lite-15-al15-41-2024-ryzen-5-7430u\">Acer Aspire Lite 15 AL15-41 2024 (Ryzen 5 7430U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;97,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7430U (7nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 58 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024\">HP 15 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB or 16GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>39.6 cm (15.6&quot;) diagonal, FHD (1920 x 1080),...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 7520U (up to 4.3 GHz max boost c...</td><td>8GB or 16GB LPDDR5-5500 (onboard)</td><td>3-cell, 41 Wh Li-ion polymer</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415\">Asus Zenbook 14 OLED Q415</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;89,999</td><td>14-inch FHD OLED, 60Hz</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 125H</td><td>8GB</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-pro-15-pv15255-2025\">Dell Pro 15 PV15255 (Ryzen 5 7520U, 8GB/512GB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;86,499</td><td>15.6&quot;, Non-Touch, FHD, 120Hz, WVA, IPS, Anti-...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7520U (4 cores, 8 threads, up to...</td><td>8 GB: LPDDR5, 5500 MT/s (onboard)</td><td>3 Cell Battery, 41 Wh, integrated</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-15-x1504va-2023-intel-core-i5-1334u-16gb-512gb-156-fhd\">Asus VivoBook 15 X1504VA 2023 (Intel Core i5-1334U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;79,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>Intel Core i5-1334U (Intel 7), 10 cores / 12...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What a lakh should buy</h2>\n<p>Here is a minimum standard you can take into any shop. A processor with at least six cores, or a recent Core i5 or i7 of the U series. Sixteen gigabytes of memory. A 512GB NVMe drive. A Full HD IPS screen. One year of official warranty. Three of the laptops above meet it, and plenty of laptops sold for a lakh do not. If a seller shows you 8GB and 256GB at this price, walk to the next counter.</p>\n<h2>MSI Modern 14: the strongest sheet</h2>\n<p>A Core 7 150U with 16GB and a terabyte for Rs 93,000 is remarkable. The processor is quick in short tasks, and the 14-inch body weighs little. The screen is plain, the battery at under 47Wh is average, and MSI\'s service network is smaller than HP\'s or Dell\'s. For a student who wants speed and storage in a light machine, it leads the list. Make sure you are offered that exact configuration, as the same model exists with less.</p>\n<h2>Acer Aspire Lite 15: the all-day choice</h2>\n<p>Six Ryzen cores, 16GB and a 58Wh battery at under a lakh. The larger screen and number pad suit accounting, spreadsheets and long documents. It does not look exciting. <a href=\"/reviews/acer-aspire-lite-15-ryzen-5-review-nepal\">Our review</a> explains why we like it anyway.</p>\n<h2>Zenbook 14 OLED: the beautiful one</h2>\n<p>An OLED screen and a Core Ultra 5 125H in a premium body, with only 8GB of soldered memory and usually an imported unit. If you mostly read, write and watch, it is a joy. If you run many programs, it is a trap. Read <a href=\"/reviews/asus-zenbook-14-oled-q415-review-nepal\">the review</a> before falling in love.</p>\n<h2>HP 15 and Dell Pro 15</h2>\n<p>Both use the Ryzen 5 7520U, a four-core chip that is slower than its name suggests. Their strength is service: HP and Dell have the most developed support for offices in Nepal. Choose the 16GB version of the HP. The Dell\'s 120Hz display is pleasant, but 8GB at Rs 86,500 is weak.</p>\n<h2>Vivobook 15 X1504VA: still the bargain</h2>\n<p>We list it in the <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-80000-nepal\">Rs 80,000 guide</a>, and it belongs here as well. If you find it, you keep Rs 15,000 to 20,000 for a monitor, a better bag or a year of internet.</p>\n<h2>By course and job</h2>\n<p><strong>BBA, BBS, MBA, law:</strong> Aspire Lite 15 or HP 15 (16GB). Number pads and large screens help.</p>\n<p><strong>BCA, CSIT, computing:</strong> MSI Modern 14. Compiling benefits from its speed. Add memory if a slot exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Engineering:</strong> any of the 16GB machines is enough for the first two years of 2D drawing and MATLAB. Third-year 3D work calls for a graphics card.</p>\n<p><strong>Medicine and nursing:</strong> portability and battery. MSI Modern 14.</p>\n<p><strong>Design and media:</strong> the Zenbook\'s screen is tempting, but 8GB is too little for Photoshop and Premiere. Look at the next budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Remote work:</strong> Aspire Lite 15 with an external monitor makes a good desk.</p>\n<h2>Power cuts and battery life</h2>\n<p>Battery size in watt-hours tells you most. The Acer\'s 58Wh leads. 41 to 47Wh machines manage half a day of real work. If your area still loses power regularly, choose the larger battery, or buy a 65W USB-C power bank for laptops that charge by USB-C. Check that the USB-C port supports charging. A port that only carries data looks the same.</p>\n<h2>Ports you will miss if they are absent</h2>\n<p>HDMI for projectors, at least two USB-A ports for pen drives and a mouse, and an Ethernet port if your college lab has unreliable Wi-Fi. Slim laptops drop some of these. A Rs 2,000 hub solves it, and it is one more thing to carry.</p>\n<h2>Upgrades that make sense</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Memory:</strong> an extra stick, if there is a slot, is the best value.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage:</strong> 1TB NVMe when 512GB fills.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitor:</strong> a 24-inch Full HD screen at home does more for your back and eyes than any processor. See <a href=\"/products?category=monitor\">monitors</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Keyboard and mouse:</strong> a basic wireless set for desk use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Festival offers and EMI</h2>\n<p>Laptop dealers discount around new academic sessions and Dashain. Free items are common: bags, mice, antivirus and headphones worth less than they claim. Ask for the cash price without gifts. EMI at zero interest is real when the cash price stays the same. The estimate on our product pages shows the monthly amount.</p>\n<h2>Mistakes we see</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing by brand and then accepting 8GB.</li>\n<li>Buying an older gaming laptop for college because it looks powerful, then carrying 2.5kg daily.</li>\n<li>Paying extra for a touchscreen that is used twice.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the keyboard. You will touch it more than anything else. Type a paragraph in the shop.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For most people with a lakh we suggest the Acer Aspire Lite 15. For those who carry their laptop and want it quick, the MSI Modern 14. For readers and writers with a love of screens, the Zenbook. For bargain hunters, the Vivobook. Any of these will see you through a degree if you treat it gently and keep it clean.</p>','gadgets/2026/hp-15-fc0043nq-2024.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(95,20,'Best Laptops Under Rs 1,50,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-laptops-under-150000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>At Rs 1–1.5 lakh, laptops split into two groups. Thin, efficient machines with long battery life suit students and professionals, while entry gaming laptops have dedicated graphics. Decide which you need first.</p>\n<h2>For work and study</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Acer Aspire 14 AI (Core Ultra 7 258V)</a>: Rs 1,39,999. 32GB of RAM, 1TB and long battery life. Hard to beat.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-445-g11-ryzen-5-7535u\">HP ProBook 445 G11</a>: Rs 1,14,999, and <a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">EliteBook 655 G10</a>: Rs 1,29,999. Business laptops with 32GB of RAM.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-oled-x-plus-16gb-1tb-15-6-3k-oled\">ASUS Vivobook S15 (Snapdragon X Plus)</a>: Rs 1,42,800. An OLED screen and outstanding battery life, but <a href=\"/news/qualcomm-oryon-snapdragon-x-elite-laptops-nepal-battery-benchmarks\">check app compatibility</a> first.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-ai-sfl14-54m-786p-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-155u-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga-oled-60hz\">Acer Swift Lite 14 AI</a>: Rs 1,44,999. A light laptop with an OLED screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>For gaming</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a> (from Rs 1,49,000) is the main option. Pick the RTX 4050 version. Our news piece on <a href=\"/news/best-gaming-laptops-nepal-under-150000-2024\">gaming laptops under Rs 1.5 lakh</a> covers the alternatives.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The main options</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">Acer Aspire 14 AI A14-52MT 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,39,999</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 258V (TSMC N3B), 8 cores /...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 65 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-probook-445-g11-ryzen-5-7535u\">HP ProBook 445 G11 (Ryzen 5 7535U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,14,999</td><td>14 inches, IPS LCD, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 7535U (6nm), 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 56 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-elitebook-655-g10-ryzen-5-pro-7530u\">HP EliteBook 655 G10 (Ryzen 5 Pro 7530U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,29,999</td><td>15.6 inches, IPS LCD, Full HD (1920 x 1080 pi...</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 Pro 7530U, 6 cores / 12 threads</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 42 Wh; 45W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-vivobook-s15-oled-x-plus-16gb-1tb-15-6-3k-oled\">Asus Vivobook S15 S5507QA-MA004W (Snapdragon X Plus)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,42,800</td><td>15.6 inches, OLED, 3K (2880 x 1620 pixels), 1...</td><td>Snapdragon X Plus X1P-42-100, 8 cores / 8 thr...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 70 Wh; 70W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-swift-lite-14-ai-sfl14-54m-786p-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-155u-16gb-512gb-14-wuxga-oled-60hz\">Acer Swift Lite 14 AI SFL14-54M-786P 2025 (Intel Core Ultra 7 155U)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,44,999</td><td>14 inches, OLED, WUXGA (1920 x 1200 pixels),...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155U (Intel 4), 12 cores /...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 50 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-victus-15\">HP Victus 15</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,49,000</td><td>15.6-inch FHD IPS, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 5 8645HS or Intel Core i5-13420H</td><td>16GB DDR5-5600</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>A budget with three roads</h2>\n<p>With Rs 1.5 lakh you can walk into a shop and come out with a light machine that lasts all day, a sturdy business laptop with lots of memory, or an entry gaming laptop. They are different tools. The commonest mistake at this level is to buy the third for the work of the first, and then spend four years carrying 2.3 kilos and looking for sockets.</p>\n<h2>Road one: light and long-lasting</h2>\n<p><strong>Acer Aspire 14 AI with Core Ultra 7 258V</strong> is our favourite laptop under Rs 1.5 lakh. Intel\'s Lunar Lake chip is thrifty in the way that phones are, so a working day on battery is realistic. It has 32GB and 1TB, which nobody else offers here with that chip. The screen is an ordinary 14-inch panel at 60Hz, fine for work. If you want one laptop for five years of study or office, stop here.</p>\n<p><strong>Acer Swift Lite 14 AI</strong> trades memory and chip efficiency for an OLED screen. <strong>Vivobook S15</strong> goes further, with a 3K 120Hz OLED and great battery life on a Snapdragon processor, and the compatibility homework that comes with it. <a href=\"/reviews/asus-vivobook-s15-snapdragon-review-nepal\">Our review</a> has a list of what runs and what may not.</p>\n<h2>Road two: business machines</h2>\n<p><strong>HP ProBook 445 G11</strong> at about Rs 1,15,000 comes with 32GB and Windows 11 Pro. <strong>EliteBook 655 G10</strong> adds a terabyte and a stronger build. Business lines have better keyboards, spill resistance, docking support and parts that are stocked for years. They are plain to look at and often outlive prettier machines. Offices, accountants and developers who keep many things open should look here first.</p>\n<h2>Road three: entry gaming</h2>\n<p><strong>HP Victus 15</strong> starts just under the limit. Choose the version with an RTX 4050 if it fits. With an RTX 3050 you get esports and older games. The same graphics cards accelerate Blender, Premiere and CUDA work for students. <a href=\"/reviews/hp-victus-15-review-nepal\">Read our review</a> for the versions and their differences.</p>\n<h2>What about a Mac?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> is priced from about Rs 1,33,499. It is light, silent and long-lasting, with 8GB of memory and 256GB of storage in the base model. For writing, study and browsing it is delightful. For developers and editors it is too small. <a href=\"/reviews/macbook-neo-review-nepal\">Our review</a> helps you decide which group you are in.</p>\n<h2>And raw power without a graphics card?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/lenovo-ideapad-slim-3-15ahp10-2025-ryzen-7-8840hs-16gb-512gb-153-wuxga\">IdeaPad Slim 3 with a Ryzen 7 8840HS</a> offers eight strong cores and the best integrated graphics at this price. Computing and data students who sit near a socket get the most from it.</p>\n<h2>Comparing processors quickly</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Core Ultra 7 258V:</strong> efficient, quick in short tasks, modern graphics.</li>\n<li><strong>Ryzen 7 8840HS:</strong> strongest in sustained multi-core work.</li>\n<li><strong>Core Ultra 7 155U:</strong> balanced, an earlier generation.</li>\n<li><strong>Ryzen 5 7535U and 7530U:</strong> older six-core parts, dependable and not fast.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapdragon X Plus:</strong> efficient, with Arm software limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In documents and browsing you will hardly notice a difference. In compiling, rendering and exports you will, in the order 8840HS, 258V, 155U, 7535U.</p>\n<h2>How much memory?</h2>\n<p>Sixteen gigabytes is comfortable for nearly everyone. Thirty-two matters for virtual machines, Android development, large datasets, many browser profiles or heavy photo work. On chips like the 258V, memory is part of the processor package, so choose the right amount on the first day.</p>\n<h2>Screen advice</h2>\n<p>If you edit pictures or watch films, OLED is worth it. If you work on spreadsheets with static toolbars for eight hours a day, a good IPS panel avoids the risk of uneven wear. Look for 16:10 screens, such as WUXGA, as they show more lines. Aim for at least 300 nits.</p>\n<h2>Warranty differences</h2>\n<p>Business laptops sold officially may carry up to three years of warranty, with on-site service in some contracts. Ask. Consumer laptops usually have one or two years. Many of the attractive configurations in Nepal, with 32GB and odd processors, come through importers. That is not wrong, but the promise then belongs to the shop. Get it in writing with the duration and what is covered.</p>\n<h2>Who should pick what</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One laptop for everything, five years:</strong> Acer Aspire 14 AI.</li>\n<li><strong>Company purchase:</strong> HP ProBook 445 G11.</li>\n<li><strong>Developer who plugs in:</strong> IdeaPad Slim 3 Ryzen 7.</li>\n<li><strong>Film and photo lover with simple software needs:</strong> Vivobook S15.</li>\n<li><strong>Student gamer:</strong> HP Victus 15 RTX 4050.</li>\n<li><strong>iPhone owner who writes:</strong> MacBook Neo.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Before you pay</h2>\n<p>Open Settings and read the exact processor and memory. Run a battery report. Connect to your phone\'s hotspot and play a video. Try the webcam in the light of the shop. Check that the charger is original and what wattage it has. For a laptop at this price, half an hour of checking is reasonable, and a good dealer will not mind.</p>\n<h2>Should you go up to two lakhs?</h2>\n<p>If you game seriously or need a better graphics card, yes. The <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-under-200000-nepal\">next guide</a> has laptops with an RTX 5050 at full power. If you do not, the Rs 1.4 lakh Acer already does what you need, and the extra fifty thousand is better spent on a monitor, a chair and a backup drive.</p>','gadgets/2026/dell-14-plus-2-in-1-db04255-2025-ryzen-ai-7-350-16gb-1tb-14-fhd.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(96,20,'Best Laptops Under Rs 2,00,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-laptops-under-200000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Up to Rs 2 lakh, you can choose between a capable gaming laptop and a premium thin-and-light.</p>\n<h2>For gaming</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025)</a>: Rs 1,94,999. An RTX 5050 at a full 100W and a 100% sRGB screen. Our top value pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-2026\">ASUS TUF A16</a>: Rs 1,83,999. An RTX 4050 and an 8-core Ryzen 7 170 in a sturdy 16-inch body.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx607-2024\">ASUS TUF F16 FX607</a>: from Rs 1,65,600. A cheaper TUF with a 16-inch screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>For work</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-14-ai-a14-52mt-94h5-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-288v-32gb-1tb-14-wuxga-touch\">Acer Aspire 14 AI (Ultra 9 288V)</a>: Rs 1,84,999. 32GB of RAM, a touchscreen and excellent battery life.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-plus-7440-intel-core-ultra-9-185h\">Dell Inspiron 14 Plus 7440</a>: Rs 1,74,999. A powerful 14-inch for developers.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">HP Spectre x360 14</a>: Rs 1,64,999. A premium 2-in-1 with a 3K OLED.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">MacBook Neo</a> (from Rs 1,34,999) is also in this range if you\'d rather have a Mac. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-laptops-nepal-students-gamers\">best laptops guide</a> for higher budgets.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-loq-15-2025\">Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025, Ryzen 5 220, RTX 5050)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,94,999</td><td>15.6&quot; FHD (1920x1080) IPS 300nits Anti-glare,...</td><td>AMD Ryzen™ 5 220 (6C / 12T, 3.2 / 4.9GHz, 6MB...</td><td>1x 16GB SO-DIMM DDR5-5600</td><td>60Wh</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-a16-fa607nuq-2026\">Asus TUF Gaming A16 FA607NUQ (2026)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,83,999</td><td>16-inch FHD+, 144Hz</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 170</td><td>16GB</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx607-2024\">Asus TUF Gaming F16 FX607 (2024)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,65,600</td><td>16-inch WUXGA, 144Hz</td><td>Intel Core 5 210H</td><td>8GB / 16GB</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-14-ai-a14-52mt-94h5-2025-intel-core-ultra-9-288v-32gb-1tb-14-wuxga-touch\">Acer Aspire 14 AI A14-52MT-94H5 (Core Ultra 9 288V, 32GB/1TB)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,86,499</td><td>14&quot; WUXGA (1920 x 1200) 60Hz, ComfyView (Matt...</td><td>Intel Core™ Ultra 9 288V, 3.30 GHz processor...</td><td>32 GB LPDDR5X</td><td>65Wh Lithium-Ion</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/dell-inspiron-14-plus-7440-intel-core-ultra-9-185h\">Dell Inspiron 14 Plus 7440</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,74,999</td><td>14.0 inches, IPS, 2.2K (2240 x 1400 pixels),...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 9 185H (Intel 4), 16 cores /...</td><td>32GB</td><td>Li-Ion 64 Wh; 100W AC Adapter, Supports Expre...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/hp-spectre-x360-14-eu0060ca-2024-intel-core-ultra-5-125h-16gb-16gb-14-3k-oled-touch\">HP Spectre x360 14-EU0060CA 2024 (Intel Core Ultra 5 125H)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>14 inches, OLED, 2.8K (2880 x 1800 pixels), 1...</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 7 155H (Intel 4), 14 cores /...</td><td>16GB</td><td>Li-Ion 68 Wh; 65W adapter</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition\">Lenovo Yoga Slim 7i Aura Edition</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,64,999</td><td>14-inch WUXGA OLED, 600 nits, 100% DCI-P3</td><td>Intel Core Ultra 5 226V (Intel Arc 130V graph...</td><td>16GB LPDDR5x-8533</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Decide what the laptop is for</h2>\n<p>Two lakhs is a serious amount, and the two halves of this page have almost nothing in common. On one side are machines with graphics cards, thick bodies and fans. On the other are thin ones with lovely screens, made for people who carry a computer every day. A good question to ask yourself is where the laptop will be at three in the afternoon on a normal Tuesday. If the answer is a desk with a socket, either side is open. If it is a classroom, a cafe or a client\'s office, choose the thin side.</p>\n<h2>Gaming and heavy work</h2>\n<p><strong>Lenovo LOQ 15 (2025).</strong> An RTX 5050 at its full 100W, a 100% sRGB screen and solid cooling for under two lakhs. The full power matters: other laptops with the same chip at 60 or 70W are noticeably slower. The colour-accurate screen makes it usable for design students as well. It ships with one 16GB stick and 512GB, so plan for a second stick and a second drive. <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-loq-15-2025-review-nepal\">Our review</a> covers it in full.</p>\n<p><strong>Asus TUF A16 (2026).</strong> A larger 16-inch display, a tough chassis and an eight-core Ryzen with an RTX 4050. It is the choice for people who are hard on their things. The graphics chip is a generation behind.</p>\n<p><strong>Asus TUF F16 FX607.</strong> Cheaper, with several versions that share one name. The graphics card and memory vary, so read the label twice. The base version with 8GB is not worth buying.</p>\n<h2>Thin, light and long-lasting</h2>\n<p><strong>Yoga Slim 7i Aura.</strong> At Rs 1,64,999, with an OLED screen, 16GB and the efficient Core Ultra 5 226V, it is the best value on this side. <a href=\"/reviews/lenovo-yoga-slim-7i-aura-edition-review-nepal\">Read more</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Acer Aspire 14 AI with Core Ultra 9 288V.</strong> The fastest version of Intel\'s efficient chip with 32GB and 1TB and a touchscreen. The body and screen are less premium than the Yoga\'s. For professionals who need memory and battery more than beauty, it is excellent. The cheaper <a href=\"/products/acer-aspire-ai-a14-52mt-2025-intel-core-ultra-7-258v\">258V version</a> gives you nine-tenths of it for Rs 46,000 less, so think before paying for the Ultra 9.</p>\n<p><strong>Dell Inspiron 14 Plus.</strong> A Core Ultra 9 185H with sixteen cores and 32GB in a 14-inch body. It is a developer\'s machine: fast at compiling, warm under load and average on battery.</p>\n<p><strong>HP Spectre x360 14.</strong> A convertible with a wonderful 2.8K OLED at 120Hz and a pen-friendly hinge. Listings disagree about its processor. The name says Core Ultra 5 125H and our sheet says Ultra 7 155H, so check the sticker. It is the most luxurious object on the page.</p>\n<h2>What an RTX laptop does for non-gamers</h2>\n<p>Students of architecture, civil engineering, animation and data science need a graphics card for Lumion, Twinmotion, Blender, Revit and CUDA libraries. An RTX 5050 with 8GB of video memory is a fair entry level. The VRAM amount matters more than the name, so look for 8GB. Video editors benefit from hardware encoding and effects acceleration too. If you are in one of those fields, the LOQ is a tool and not a toy.</p>\n<h2>Weight, heat and noise</h2>\n<p>Gaming laptops weigh 2.2 to 2.5kg and their chargers another half kilo or more. Their fans are loud under load. Their batteries last three to five hours in light use and one in games. Thin laptops weigh 1.2 to 1.5kg, are nearly silent and last eight hours or more. There is no machine that is both. Anyone who tells you otherwise is selling something.</p>\n<h2>Screens for creative work</h2>\n<p>Ask for the colour coverage. 100% sRGB is the minimum for design. DCI-P3 coverage of 95% or more, as on the OLED machines, is better for photo and video. Many gaming laptops still use 45% NTSC panels, which look washed out. The LOQ avoids that. Check the exact panel of the unit in the shop.</p>\n<h2>Desktop instead?</h2>\n<p>If the computer will not move, two lakhs builds a far stronger desktop with a monitor included. You can upgrade it for a decade. Try our <a href=\"/custom-pc-build\">PC builder</a> before deciding. Many students would be better served by a desktop at home and a Rs 45,000 used ThinkPad for class.</p>\n<h2>And a MacBook?</h2>\n<p>Within this budget it is the <a href=\"/products/macbook-neo\">Neo</a>, or a used M2 or M3 MacBook Air. A new <a href=\"/products/macbook-air-m5-13\">Air M5</a> is above Rs 2.3 lakh. For writers and students who like macOS, a clean used Air with 16GB is a very good buy.</p>\n<h2>Checklist for gaming laptops</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Graphics power (TGP) in watts, read from Nvidia Control Panel.</li>\n<li>Video memory: 8GB if possible.</li>\n<li>Memory: 16GB, and whether a slot is free.</li>\n<li>MUX switch or Advanced Optimus, for a few more frames.</li>\n<li>Panel: refresh rate, brightness, sRGB.</li>\n<li>Second M.2 slot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Checklist for thin laptops</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Battery in watt-hours and the chip family.</li>\n<li>Memory: soldered, so choose enough.</li>\n<li>Keyboard feel and backlight.</li>\n<li>USB-C charging.</li>\n<li>Webcam of 1080p or better.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<p>For games and technical courses, the Lenovo LOQ 15. For professional work, the Yoga Slim 7i Aura if 16GB is enough, or the Acer Aspire 14 AI with the 258V if it is not, saving enough for a good monitor. For developers who want muscle, the Inspiron 14 Plus. For people who love beautiful things and a pen, the Spectre.</p>','gadgets/2026/asus-tuf-gaming-f16-fx607-2024.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(97,20,'Best Earbuds Under Rs 3,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-earbuds-under-3000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>You can get surprisingly good earbuds for under Rs 3,000 in Nepal, some even with noise cancellation. Expect bass-heavy sound and basic microphones, but long battery life.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a>: Rs 2,599. Noise cancellation and multipoint, a rare combination under Rs 3,000. Our top pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a>: Rs 1,999. The cheapest good pair, from a brand with a big service network.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200x\">Realme Buds T200x</a>: Rs 2,999. ANC and multipoint, with up to about 48 hours of battery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-212\">boAt Airdopes 212</a>: Rs 2,199. Long battery life, up to around 75 hours with the case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-p20i\">Soundcore P20i</a> (Rs 2,999) is another solid choice with a good app. At this price, fit matters more than features. Try the different ear tips in the box for a better seal and fuller sound. For better noise cancellation, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Compare the five</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,599</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 1.66 hours playbac...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-5a\">Redmi Buds 5A</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;1,999</td><td>Up to 30 hours without ANC (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4; SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200x\">Realme Buds T200x</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>Music: 5 hours (buds) / 30 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-212\">boAt Airdopes 212</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,199</td><td>25 minutes (buds) / 1.75 hours (case), Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-p20i\">Soundcore P20i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 30 hours (case); Qui...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Cheap earbuds are no longer bad earbuds</h2>\n<p>Five years ago Rs 2,000 bought you a pair that disconnected when you turned your head. The chips inside budget earbuds have since become stable, batteries have doubled and Bluetooth 5.3 is normal. What has not changed is that makers still have to save somewhere. They save on microphones, on the tuning of the sound and on how long the parts last. Choose with those three in mind and you can be very happy for the price of a family lunch.</p>\n<h2>Sound: expect bass, look for balance</h2>\n<p>Almost every budget pair is tuned with heavy bass, because it impresses in a shop. After a week it hides voices and tires the ear. A companion app with an equaliser fixes this. Reduce the lowest two sliders a little and raise the middle, and speech and Nepali songs become much clearer. Soundcore, Realme and Redmi have usable apps. Pairs with no app leave you with whatever the maker liked.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling at Rs 2,600: what to believe</h2>\n<p>The Kick Phantom Buds X Pro and Realme Buds T200x advertise ANC. It is real and modest. Engine rumble on a bus drops by perhaps a third or a half, while horns and voices come through. A better seal from the right ear tips gives as much benefit again. If you have tried premium earbuds before, you will be underwhelmed. If you have not, you will notice the difference the first time you turn it off.</p>\n<h2>Calls</h2>\n<p>Indoors, all of these are fine for a short call. Outside, wind and traffic defeat them. If you work through phone calls, a wired earphone with a microphone on the cable still beats any wireless pair under Rs 5,000, and costs Rs 500.</p>\n<h2>Reading the battery claim</h2>\n<p>The big number on the box includes the case. The useful number is hours per charge for the buds themselves: the Soundcore P20i lists ten hours, and many others give five to seven with ANC on. Quick charging, ten minutes for an hour or two, is more useful than a total of 75 hours that you will never test.</p>\n<h2>Who should choose which</h2>\n<p><strong>Daily commuter:</strong> Kick Phantom Buds X Pro, for ANC and connection to two devices.</p>\n<p><strong>Student with one phone and a small budget:</strong> Redmi Buds 5A. Simple and reliable, and Xiaomi has service points.</p>\n<p><strong>Laptop and phone user:</strong> Realme Buds T200x, for multipoint and a balanced sound.</p>\n<p><strong>Someone who forgets to charge:</strong> Soundcore P20i or boAt Airdopes 212.</p>\n<p><strong>Gym:</strong> any with IPX4 or better and a snug fit. Try them while shaking your head.</p>\n<h2>Fit, the free upgrade</h2>\n<p>Open the packet of spare tips. Put in each size, play a song with bass and choose the one that sounds fullest without pressure. Many people need a different size in each ear. If none seals, buy a set of memory foam tips for a few hundred rupees. No other change improves cheap earbuds as much.</p>\n<h2>Fakes</h2>\n<p>At this price, counterfeits are common in markets and on social media pages. They copy AirPods, JBL and boAt. Signs include a price too good to be true, spelling mistakes on the box, no importer sticker, and a name in the Bluetooth list that does not match. Buy from a shop that gives a bill, and check the warranty card for a distributor\'s name.</p>\n<h2>How long will they last?</h2>\n<p>Two years is a fair expectation. Tiny batteries fade, the case hinge loosens, and one bud may go quiet when wax blocks the mesh. Clean the mesh with a dry, soft brush once a month. Do not leave the case in a hot car. Warranty is six months to a year. Some shops exchange a faulty bud, others repair only the case. Ask.</p>\n<h2>Earbuds or a neckband?</h2>\n<p>A neckband at the same price has a bigger battery, better call microphones and cannot be lost down a drain. It looks less tidy and catches on collars. For delivery riders, shopkeepers and anyone on calls all day, a neckband is the sensible choice.</p>\n<h2>Settings that help</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Turn on game mode only when gaming, as it can reduce quality.</li>\n<li>Update firmware in the app on the first day.</li>\n<li>Disable double-tap functions you trigger by accident.</li>\n<li>Use one bud at a time on long calls to double the battery.</li>\n<li>Keep volume under 60 per cent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n<p><strong>Will they work with an iPhone?</strong> Yes. Apps and codecs are limited, but AAC is supported on most.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I use them with a TV?</strong> With any Bluetooth TV, though there may be a delay in some apps.</p>\n<p><strong>Is a higher driver size better?</strong> Not by itself. Tuning and fit matter more.</p>\n<p><strong>Are they safe in rain?</strong> IPX4 and IPX5 handle sweat and drizzle. The case normally has no protection.</p>\n<h2>Should you spend Rs 2,000 more?</h2>\n<p>At Rs 4,500 to 5,000, noise cancelling becomes useful, calls improve and the apps are better. If you listen for more than an hour a day, see <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a>. If these are a spare pair or a gift for a teenager who loses things, stay here.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Kick Phantom Buds X Pro gives the most for the money, the Redmi Buds 5A is the most sensible cheap pair, and the P20i is for those who value battery and an app. Any of them is better than the unboxed \"AirPods\" on the footpath, which cost nearly as much.</p>\n<h2>Buying a pair as a gift</h2>\n<p>Cheap earbuds are the most common small gift in Nepal, and the easiest to get wrong. Ask quietly which phone the person uses and whether they already complain about things falling out of their ears. Choose a pair with several tip sizes and a brand with service in their town. Include the bill in the box so they can use the warranty. And pick a plain colour. The Redmi Buds 5A in black has pleased more people than any fashionable case we can think of.</p>','gadgets/2026/boat-airdopes-212.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(98,20,'Best Earbuds Under Rs 5,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 3,000–5,000 is the sweet spot for most people buying earbuds in Nepal. It\'s enough for real noise cancellation, multipoint and a decent app, and it\'s cheap enough that a lost earbud isn\'t a disaster.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">OnePlus Nord Buds 4</a>: Rs 4,999. Good noise cancellation, multipoint and a reliable app. Our top pick for most people.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200\">Realme Buds T200</a>: Rs 4,499. ANC, LDAC and multipoint, the most features for the money.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-p30i\">Soundcore P30i</a>: Rs 4,999. Solid ANC and a case that doubles as a phone stand.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a>: Rs 2,599. If you\'d rather spend less, it still has ANC and multipoint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Also worth a look: the <a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-lite\">Redmi Buds 8 Lite</a> (Rs 4,999), and the <a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-800-hi-def\">boAt Airdopes 800 HI-DEF</a> (Rs 4,299) for very long battery life.</p>\n<p>A tip: multipoint is worth having if you join calls on your laptop. The earbuds stay connected to the laptop and your phone at the same time. For stronger noise cancellation, step up to <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">under Rs 10,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The contenders</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-nord-buds-4\">OnePlus Nord Buds 4</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Up to 54 hours with ANC (earbuds + case)</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1 with multipoint; AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-t200\">Realme Buds T200</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 5 hours playback</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-p30i\">Soundcore P30i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 45 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-lite\">Redmi Buds 8 Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 36 hours (case), With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-airdopes-800-hi-def\">boAt Airdopes 800 HI-DEF</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,299</td><td>Music: 40 hours (buds) / 100 hours (case); 45...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/kick-phantom-buds-x-pro\">Kick Phantom Buds X Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,599</td><td>Quick charge: 10 minutes = 1.66 hours playbac...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why this is the sensible price</h2>\n<p>We have written about earbuds from Rs 2,000 to Rs 65,000. If a friend asked for a pair without further details, we would name one from this page. Below Rs 3,000 you accept compromises. Above Rs 10,000 each extra thousand buys less. Here you get noise cancelling that works on a bus, calls that people can understand, an app, multipoint and a battery that lasts all day. And if one bud falls into a drain, your week is spoiled but not your month.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus Nord Buds 4: the one to beat</h2>\n<p>They do everything properly. Noise cancelling is strong for the money, pairing with OnePlus, OPPO and Realme phones is instant, and the total battery of more than fifty hours with ANC is excellent. The sound is a little heavy in the bass and improves with the equaliser in the HeyMelody app. If you use an iPhone, they still work well, without the pop-up pairing.</p>\n<h2>Realme Buds T200: the codec choice</h2>\n<p>LDAC under Rs 5,000 is unusual. If your Android phone supports it and you listen to high-quality files in a quiet room, you may hear a cleaner treble. On the street you will not. They are light and comfortable.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore P30i: the practical one</h2>\n<p>Strong bass, decent ANC and a case that unfolds into a phone stand, which sounds like a gimmick until you watch a match on a train table. Soundcore\'s app is the best in this class, with a proper equaliser and controls you can change.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 8 Lite and boAt Airdopes 800</h2>\n<p>The Redmi pair is tidy and well supported. The boAt lists a remarkable 40 hours from the buds alone, with no ANC in use. If you go days between charges, that is the attraction.</p>\n<h2>What noise cancelling does in Kathmandu</h2>\n<p>It removes steady, low sounds: bus engines, fans, generators, aircraft. It reduces the hum of a crowd. It does little against horns, dogs, construction hammers and the person at the next table. On a microbus from Kalanki to Koteshwor, good ANC means that you can listen at half the volume, which is the real health benefit.</p>\n<p>Transparency mode is worth trying too. It lets outside sound in through the microphones, so you can hear an announcement or a shopkeeper. At this price it sounds a bit artificial.</p>\n<h2>Multipoint, explained with an example</h2>\n<p>You are in a Teams meeting on your laptop. Your phone rings. With multipoint, the earbuds switch to the phone and back without you touching anything. Without it, you disconnect one and pair the other, by which time the call has ended. If you work from a laptop, do not buy a pair without it.</p>\n<h2>Testing in the shop</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Put them in and shake your head.</li>\n<li>Play a familiar song. Listen for the voice, not the drum.</li>\n<li>Turn ANC on and off near the road.</li>\n<li>Call a friend and ask how you sound, first inside and then at the door.</li>\n<li>Press the touch controls ten times. Do they obey?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>For phones of each brand</h2>\n<p><strong>OnePlus, OPPO, Realme:</strong> Nord Buds 4 or Realme T200 for the matching features.</p>\n<p><strong>Xiaomi, Redmi, POCO:</strong> Redmi Buds 8 Lite for quick pairing, or Nord Buds 4 for better ANC.</p>\n<p><strong>Samsung:</strong> any of them. None has Samsung\'s own codec, so choose on fit and price.</p>\n<p><strong>iPhone:</strong> Nord Buds 4 or Soundcore P30i. LDAC is of no use to you.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>Game modes reduce delay to somewhere around 50 to 100 milliseconds. That is acceptable for casual play, and noticeable in shooting games. For competitive play on a phone, a wired earphone with a USB-C connector is still better, and costs Rs 800.</p>\n<h2>Water, dust and sweat</h2>\n<p>Look for IP54 or IP55. These ratings apply to the buds, rarely to the case. After exercise, wipe them before putting them back, as sweat corrodes the charging pins. That is the most common cause of a bud that stops charging after a year.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and spares</h2>\n<p>Official pairs come with six to twelve months. OnePlus, Realme, Xiaomi and Soundcore have distributors in Nepal. A lost single bud can sometimes be ordered, at about a third of the price. boAt and Kick vary by shop. Keep the bill and the box with the serial number.</p>\n<h2>How we would spend exactly Rs 5,000</h2>\n<p>Nord Buds 4 for most people. The T200 for a Realme owner who saves Rs 500. The P30i for someone who travels and watches. If you can reach Rs 5,500, the <a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r60i-nc\">Soundcore R60i NC</a> adds LDAC and ANC with fifty hours of battery, and at Rs 8,999 the <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a> is a clear step up. Those are in <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal\">the next guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Things to ignore on the box</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\"50dB noise cancelling\". Figures are measured at one frequency.</li>\n<li>\"HD audio\" without a codec named.</li>\n<li>Driver size compared across brands.</li>\n<li>\"100 hours\" unless you know the conditions.</li>\n<li>\"AI ENC\", which is ordinary call processing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Care</h2>\n<p>Charge the case once a week even if you do not use them. Brush the mesh monthly. Keep them away from hair oil and perfume. Store them in the case and not in a pocket with coins. With that, two to three years is realistic.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>If you have been putting off buying good earbuds because you assume they cost Rs 15,000, try the Nord Buds 4. They will not impress an audiophile. They will make your bus ride shorter, and for Rs 4,999 that is a fine deal.</p>','gadgets/2026/oneplus-nord-buds-4.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(99,20,'Best Earbuds Under Rs 10,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-earbuds-under-10000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 10,000, noise cancellation gets noticeably better and features like LDAC become common. This is good territory for daily commuters and anyone who takes a lot of calls.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a>: Rs 8,999. Strong ANC, LDAC and multipoint. The best all-rounder at this price.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-pro-2\">CMF Buds Pro 2</a>: Rs 9,599. The step-up CMF, with even better noise cancellation and a clever case dial.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-air-8\">Realme Buds Air 8</a>: Rs 8,499. Comfortable, with good ANC and long battery life.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r60i-nc\">Soundcore R60i NC</a>: Rs 5,499. ANC, LDAC and multipoint for barely more than budget pairs.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc\">boAt Nirvana Ion ANC</a>: Rs 6,599. For battery life above all, at up to about 120 hours with the case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you use an iPhone, LDAC won\'t help, because iPhones use AAC, so choose on noise cancellation and fit instead. Under Rs 5,000? See <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">our cheaper picks</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-2-plus\">CMF Buds 2 Plus</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,999</td><td>Music: 14 hours (buds) / 61.5 hours (case), W...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-pro-2\">CMF Buds Pro 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,599</td><td>Music: 11 hours (buds) / 43 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-buds-air-8\">Realme Buds Air 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,499</td><td>Music: 14 hours (buds) / 58 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-r60i-nc\">Soundcore R60i NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;5,499</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 50 hours (case); Qui...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-nirvana-ion-anc\">boAt Nirvana Ion ANC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,599</td><td>Up to 120 hours with case (ANC off), 100 hour...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, USB-C</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What the extra money does</h2>\n<p>Compared with a Rs 5,000 pair, you should expect four improvements here. Noise cancelling reaches higher into the range of human voices, so an office becomes calm. Microphones improve, with three on each side and wind reduction that does something. Sound becomes cleaner, with better separation between instruments. And the materials feel nicer, with cases that do not creak. What you still do not get is the near-silence of the best Sony and Apple pairs, or their clever switching between devices.</p>\n<h2>CMF Buds 2 Plus</h2>\n<p>CMF is the affordable side of Nothing, and it shows in the neat design and clean app. The Buds 2 Plus have LDAC, strong adaptive ANC, multipoint and up to 14 hours in the buds. They are the best all-round pair under Rs 10,000 in our view. The hearing test in the app adjusts the sound to your ears and is worth five minutes.</p>\n<h2>CMF Buds Pro 2</h2>\n<p>Two drivers in each bud, one for bass and one for treble, give it a livelier sound. The dial on the case is a delightful thing to fiddle with: turn it for volume, press it for play, hold it for ANC. Battery life is a bit shorter. If music matters more than battery, choose these.</p>\n<h2>Realme Buds Air 8</h2>\n<p>Comfortable, with 14 hours per charge and a solid app. Pairing with Realme and OPPO phones is seamless. The design is ordinary and the ANC good. It often appears in bundles with phones, when it becomes a bargain.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore R60i NC</h2>\n<p>At Rs 5,499 it is hardly above the previous budget. ANC, LDAC, multipoint and an excellent app. If you can live without the last ten per cent of noise cancelling and finish, it is the smart purchase on this page.</p>\n<h2>boAt Nirvana Ion ANC</h2>\n<p>The reason to buy it is 120 hours of total battery. Charge it twice a month. Sound is bass-heavy, with an app to correct it. Frequent travellers and forgetful people will like it.</p>\n<h2>Choosing by what annoys you</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Noise on the commute:</strong> CMF Buds Pro 2 or 2 Plus.</li>\n<li><strong>People cannot hear me:</strong> CMF Buds 2 Plus. Test a call outdoors before buying.</li>\n<li><strong>Always out of charge:</strong> boAt Nirvana Ion.</li>\n<li><strong>Music sounds flat:</strong> CMF Buds Pro 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Spent too much already:</strong> Soundcore R60i NC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>LDAC in everyday life</h2>\n<p>LDAC sends about three times as much data as the basic codec. To hear a difference you need an Android phone that supports it, a lossless or high-bitrate source, a quiet room and a good seal. It also shortens battery life and can stutter in crowded places. Switch it on at home and off on the bus. iPhones ignore it completely.</p>\n<h2>Comfort for small ears</h2>\n<p>Weights between 4.5 and 5.5 grams per bud are comfortable for most. The shape of the nozzle matters more. If ordinary earbuds hurt after an hour, look for oval nozzles and shorter stems, and try extra-small tips. Open designs such as semi-in-ear buds are gentler and cannot cancel much noise.</p>\n<h2>For work calls</h2>\n<p>If you spend four hours a day in meetings, think about a headset instead. A Rs 6,000 wired or dongle headset with a boom microphone will make your voice much clearer than earbuds at any price. For a couple of calls a day, the pairs here are good enough.</p>\n<h2>Which phone do you own?</h2>\n<p>With Nothing and CMF phones, the CMF pairs show up in the settings with no app. With Realme and OPPO, the Air 8 does the same. On Samsung, you lose the Samsung codec but everything else works. On iPhones, we would choose the Buds 2 Plus for ANC and comfort, or save for <a href=\"/products/airpods-4\">AirPods 4</a> if you want automatic switching.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and after-sales</h2>\n<p>Ask for an official unit. CMF and Nothing are sold through an authorised distributor, Realme and Soundcore as well. Check the serial number in the app where possible. Earbuds are the most counterfeited electronics in the country, and pairs just under Rs 10,000 are a favourite target.</p>\n<h2>Repair or replace?</h2>\n<p>When one bud fails after the warranty, it is usually the battery. A replacement bud, if sold, costs about a third of the set. Compare it with the price of a new pair, and recycle the old ones through a shop that accepts e-waste, as the batteries should not go into household rubbish.</p>\n<h2>Better value during sales</h2>\n<p>Audio gear is discounted more deeply than phones. Pairs on this page drop by 15 to 25 per cent in festival sales. If you are not in a hurry, note today\'s price and wait for Dashain, 11.11 or New Year. Our piece on <a href=\"/blog/dashain-tihar-tech-deals-how-to-spot-a-real-discount\">spotting a real discount</a> helps.</p>\n<h2>Should you go to Rs 15,000?</h2>\n<p>The next class, with the Redmi Buds 8 Pro and Soundcore Liberty series, improves noise cancelling again. Frequent flyers and people in loud offices will feel it. For everyone else, the Buds 2 Plus are close enough that we would keep the money. See <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 20,000</a> for the comparison.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>CMF has made this price range its own. The Buds 2 Plus for balance and the Buds Pro 2 for music are the two to try first. If both feel expensive, the Soundcore R60i NC gives up little and costs a third less.</p>\n<h2>If you can wait for a sale</h2>\n<p>Set yourself a number. We would buy the CMF Buds 2 Plus at any price below Rs 8,500, the Buds Pro 2 below Rs 8,800, the Realme Buds Air 8 below Rs 7,000 and the Soundcore R60i NC below Rs 5,000. Audio products reach such figures two or three times a year. Having the number written down protects you from the opposite danger, a \"sale\" that only returns a raised price to where it was in Bhadra.</p>','gadgets/2026/boat-nirvana-ion-anc.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(100,20,'Best Earbuds Under Rs 20,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-earbuds-under-20000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 10,000–20,000 gets you close to flagship earbuds: strong noise cancellation, good call quality and premium sound, without Sony or Apple prices.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5\">Soundcore Liberty 5</a>: Rs 17,499. Excellent ANC, LDAC and multipoint. Our top pick at this budget.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-pro\">Redmi Buds 8 Pro</a>: Rs 14,999. Nearly as good for less, and a great match for Xiaomi phone owners.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-liberty-4-nc\">Soundcore Liberty 4 NC</a>: Rs 12,999. Older, but its noise cancellation is still very good, and the price has come down.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8\">Redmi Buds 8</a>: Rs 10,499. The cheapest way into this tier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Don\'t overlook the <a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-pro-2\">CMF Buds Pro 2</a> (Rs 9,599) just below this budget. It\'s surprisingly close in performance. Samsung phone owners should also compare the <a href=\"/guides/samsung-earbuds-price-in-nepal\">Galaxy Buds</a> for the extra features they get with a Galaxy phone.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The shortlist</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-liberty-5\">Soundcore Liberty 5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;17,499</td><td>Music: 12 hours (buds) / 48 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8-pro\">Redmi Buds 8 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>Music: 8 hours (buds) / 33 hours (case); Quic...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/anker-soundcore-liberty-4-nc\">Soundcore Liberty 4 NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;12,999</td><td>Music: 10 hours (buds) / 50 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-buds-8\">Redmi Buds 8</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;10,499</td><td>Music: 11 hours (buds) / 44 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-buds-pro-2\">CMF Buds Pro 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,599</td><td>Music: 11 hours (buds) / 43 hours (case), Wit...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: AAC, SBC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Almost the best, for a third of the price</h2>\n<p>The finest earbuds in Nepal cost between Rs 45,000 and 65,000. The ones on this page deliver perhaps 80 per cent of that experience for Rs 10,000 to 18,000. The missing part is mostly polish: slightly less silence on a plane, slightly less natural transparency, slightly worse microphones in wind. If you do not fly every week, you may never miss it.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Liberty 5: top of the budget</h2>\n<p>Strong adaptive noise cancelling, LDAC, multipoint, wireless charging and one of the best apps. Twelve hours from the buds is generous. The stems are short and comfortable. The sound can be tuned with a detailed equaliser or a hearing test. Anker\'s pricing in Nepal varies by shop, so ask for the official distributor\'s price.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 8 Pro: the challenger</h2>\n<p>Nearly as good at Rs 14,999. Xiaomi has put serious drivers and ANC into its Pro line, and with a Xiaomi, Redmi or POCO phone you get instant pairing, spatial effects and settings without an app. With other phones you use the Earbuds app. If you own a Xiaomi, this is the pair.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Liberty 4 NC: last year\'s favourite</h2>\n<p>At Rs 12,999 it remains a fine noise canceller with ten hours per charge. Newer models do calls and adaptive modes better. Buy it when discounted to around Rs 10,000, at which point it is hard to resist.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Buds 8 and CMF Buds Pro 2</h2>\n<p>The Redmi Buds 8 open this class at about Rs 10,500. CMF\'s Pro 2 sit just below, at Rs 9,599, and honestly compete with both. Before paying Rs 17,000, try the CMF pair. If you cannot hear why the others cost more, you have saved Rs 8,000.</p>\n<h2>What to listen for</h2>\n<p>Take a song you know well, with a voice, a bass line and something delicate such as a sarangi or cymbals. In better earbuds the voice stands in the middle and stays clear when the bass arrives. The delicate sound does not turn to hiss. Then lower the volume by half. Good earbuds still sound full, poor ones collapse. Finally, turn ANC on: the character of the sound should not change.</p>\n<h2>Galaxy Buds, AirPods and Pixel Buds</h2>\n<p>Samsung\'s and Apple\'s own buds sit above or near the top of this budget. They are worth it when you use many features of the same brand: automatic switching, find my device, head-tracked audio, instant pairing. With a Galaxy phone, compare the <a href=\"/guides/samsung-earbuds-price-in-nepal\">Galaxy Buds range</a>. With an iPhone, the <a href=\"/products/airpods-4\">AirPods 4</a> at about Rs 25,000 are the nearest. With any other phone, these general-purpose pairs give more for less.</p>\n<h2>Noise cancelling and hearing safety</h2>\n<p>The quiet that ANC brings lets you listen at lower volumes, which protects your hearing. Yet people sometimes turn the music up simply because they can. Phones show your weekly listening level. Keep it below 80 decibels on average, and take a ten-minute break every hour.</p>\n<h2>Wireless charging and other extras</h2>\n<p>Wireless charging for a case is a small luxury, handy if you have a pad on your desk. In-ear detection, which pauses music when you remove a bud, is more useful every day. Spatial audio with head tracking is fun for films. Translation features sound impressive and need an internet connection. Nobody should choose on those alone.</p>\n<h2>Travel and flights</h2>\n<p>On a four-hour flight to Doha or a night bus to Dhangadhi, these pairs will last without recharging. Add foam tips for better isolation. Aircraft entertainment needs a small Bluetooth transmitter, which costs about Rs 2,000 to 3,000. If you travel every month, over-ear headphones are more comfortable for that use. See <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Gaming</h2>\n<p>Low-latency modes are decent on all, and good enough for mobile games. Nothing here replaces a wired headset for competitive play.</p>\n<h2>Buying tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Insist on an official unit with a one-year warranty.</li>\n<li>Ask whether a single lost bud or case can be ordered, and at what price.</li>\n<li>Register them in the app to activate find features.</li>\n<li>Check the manufacturing date on the box. Batteries age in storage.</li>\n<li>Watch for festival prices: these fall more than phones do.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How long should a Rs 15,000 pair last?</h2>\n<p>Three years with daily use is realistic. By then battery life will have dropped by a third or so. If you listen for six hours every day, expect to replace them sooner. That is about Rs 14 a day, a useful number when deciding whether to pay Rs 17,499 or Rs 9,599.</p>\n<h2>Which pair for which person?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Office worker in a noisy room:</strong> Liberty 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Xiaomi phone owner:</strong> Redmi Buds 8 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Value seeker:</strong> Liberty 4 NC on sale, or CMF Buds Pro 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Music first:</strong> Redmi Buds 8 Pro or Buds Pro 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Flyer:</strong> Liberty 5 with foam tips.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When to ignore this page</h2>\n<p>If you only listen to podcasts and calls, a Rs 5,000 pair is enough. If you sit at a desk for eight hours, over-ear headphones at Rs 14,000 to 20,000 are more comfortable and last longer. And if silence is worth a lot to you, save for the <a href=\"/reviews/sony-wf-1000xm6-review-nepal\">Sony WF-1000XM6</a> or <a href=\"/reviews/airpods-pro-3-review-nepal\">AirPods Pro 3</a> and enjoy them for years.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Liberty 5 is the best pair under Rs 20,000 for most phones, and the Redmi Buds 8 Pro is the cleverest purchase for Xiaomi owners. Just do not walk past the CMF Buds Pro 2 on your way to them.</p>\n<h2>When the cheaper pair is right</h2>\n<p>If most of your listening is podcasts, news and calls, the difference between Rs 9,000 and Rs 17,000 is small, because speech does not ask much of a driver. If most of it is music in a noisy place, the dearer pairs earn their price with quieter backgrounds and cleaner bass. Be honest about your week before choosing. A good test is your streaming history: if the top ten are programmes and not songs, keep the money for a dinner.</p>','gadgets/2026/soundcore-liberty-5.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(101,20,'Best Headphones Under Rs 5,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-headphones-under-5000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>There aren\'t many over-ear headphones under Rs 5,000 in Nepal, and none of them have proper noise cancellation. What you can get is comfortable wireless headphones with very long battery life, fine for music at home and online classes.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a>: Rs 4,999. The best-sounding pair here, with multipoint and up to about 60 hours of battery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-412\">boAt Rockerz 412</a>: Rs 3,099. Multipoint and long battery life for little money.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-371\">boAt Rockerz 371</a>: Rs 2,299. The cheapest decent pair.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Over-ear headphones are often more comfortable than earbuds for long study sessions, and they don\'t fall out. If you need noise cancellation, though, earbuds give you more for the money at this price. See our <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">earbuds under Rs 5,000</a>. Or stretch to the headphones in our <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-10000-nepal\">under Rs 10,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Three to consider</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 60 hours, With ANC off; 2 hours, Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-412\">boAt Rockerz 412</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,099</td><td>Music: 50 hours, At 60% volume; 2 hours</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-371\">boAt Rockerz 371</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;2,299</td><td>Music: 50 hours, At 60% volume; 2 hours</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why headphones at this price make sense</h2>\n<p>Earbuds under Rs 5,000 have more features. Headphones have two things that students and home users value more: comfort for hours and a battery that lasts a week. You can wear them through three online lectures without sore ears. You can forget the charger for days. You cannot drop one down a drain. For studying at a desk, they are the better tool.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Q11i</h2>\n<p>The best sound of the three, with a large driver, sixty hours of battery and connection to two devices at once. The app lets you change the sound and the controls. It folds for a bag. Padding is soft, and the clamping force is gentle enough for glasses. At Rs 4,999 it is our choice without hesitation.</p>\n<h2>boAt Rockerz 412</h2>\n<p>For Rs 3,099 you get fifty hours and multipoint. The sound has strong bass and a lively treble that suits pop and hip-hop. The plastic is light and a bit creaky. For a school student it is plenty.</p>\n<h2>boAt Rockerz 371</h2>\n<p>The cheapest pair we would recommend. It is simple: play, pause, calls. If it breaks in two years, you have had your money\'s worth.</p>\n<h2>On-ear, over-ear and why it matters</h2>\n<p>On-ear pads press on the ear itself. They are smaller and cooler, and begin to hurt after an hour or so. Over-ear pads surround the ear. They block more noise and feel better for long sessions, and get warm in summer. At this budget, try both in the shop with your glasses on, if you wear them.</p>\n<h2>Do you need noise cancelling?</h2>\n<p>Nothing under Rs 5,000 does it well in this shape. Closed pads already reduce sound by a useful amount. If traffic outside your window is the problem, move the desk before you change your budget. If you must have it, the <a href=\"/products/soundcore-q20i\">Soundcore Q20i</a> at about Rs 9,200 is the first honest one.</p>\n<h2>Microphones for classes</h2>\n<p>The microphones in these are fine for answering in class in a quiet room. They sound distant and thin in noise. For teaching, recording or interviews, a wired headset with a boom arm at Rs 2,500 to 4,000 sounds far better. Alternatively, use the laptop\'s microphone for your voice and the headphones for listening.</p>\n<h2>Wired is not dead</h2>\n<p>For Rs 2,000 to 4,000, wired studio-style headphones give cleaner sound than wireless ones at twice the price, with no battery and no delay. They suit music practice, video editing, piano lessons and gaming. You need a headphone socket, or a USB-C adapter. If you sit by a computer anyway, think about it.</p>\n<h2>Sound settings to try</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>In the app, choose a flat or balanced preset.</li>\n<li>Lower the bass by two steps if voices sound muffled.</li>\n<li>On a Windows laptop, select the stereo output and not \"Hands-Free\", which ruins music quality.</li>\n<li>Turn off loudness boosters and equalisers in other apps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That third point is the cause of most complaints about Bluetooth headphones sounding bad in meetings. The headset profile carries voice both ways at low quality. Using the laptop\'s microphone lets the headphones stay in high quality.</p>\n<h2>Comfort through a Nepali year</h2>\n<p>In winter, closed pads keep your ears warm. In the hot months, sweat breaks down the artificial leather, which then flakes. Wipe the pads after use, and cover them with thin cotton if you like. Replacement pads cost a few hundred rupees and make an old pair feel new.</p>\n<h2>Children</h2>\n<p>For children under twelve, choose headphones with a volume limit of 85 decibels, sold as kids\' headphones. The pairs on this page can play much louder. Use the parental volume limit on the phone or tablet if you buy a normal pair.</p>\n<h2>What breaks</h2>\n<p>The hinge and headband go first, followed by the charging port. Do not bend the headband outwards to show how flexible it is. Unplug by holding the connector. Store them in a drawer and not under books. Warranty is usually six months to a year, and physical damage is not covered.</p>\n<h2>Gaming on a budget</h2>\n<p>Bluetooth delay makes these poor for shooting games. For PC games, a wired gaming headset at Rs 2,500 to 4,500 is better. For a PlayStation, the controller\'s socket takes any wired pair with a 3.5mm plug.</p>\n<h2>Which is right for you?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>College student, long study hours:</strong> Soundcore Q11i.</li>\n<li><strong>School student:</strong> boAt Rockerz 412.</li>\n<li><strong>Spare pair or gift:</strong> boAt Rockerz 371.</li>\n<li><strong>Musician or editor:</strong> a wired pair.</li>\n<li><strong>Noisy home:</strong> save for the Q20i, or buy ANC earbuds from <a href=\"/guides/best-earbuds-under-5000-nepal\">our list</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where to buy</h2>\n<p>Choose a shop that lets you try them. Comfort cannot be read from a page. Check for a distributor\'s sticker and a bill. Online, look at the negative reviews first: complaints about the hinge or one side going silent tell you what will happen to yours.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n<p><strong>Can I use them with a TV?</strong> Yes, if it has Bluetooth. Some TVs show a slight lip-sync delay that can be adjusted in settings.</p>\n<p><strong>Do they work when the battery is empty?</strong> Models with a 3.5mm or USB-C audio input do. Check for the cable in the box.</p>\n<p><strong>How long do they take to charge?</strong> About two hours, for a week or more of use.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Spend the full Rs 4,999 on the Q11i if you can. It is comfortable, lasts for days and has the sound and controls of something more expensive. If the budget ends at Rs 3,000, the Rockerz 412 will do the job without complaint.</p>\n<h2>If you can borrow before you buy</h2>\n<p>Ask a friend to lend you their headphones for an evening and do exactly what you plan to do with them: two hours of lectures, an hour of music, one call home. Notice when you first want to take them off. If it is the heat, choose on-ear or earbuds. If it is pressure on the ears, choose over-ear. If it is the noise of the house, save for noise cancelling. An evening like that tells you more than any list, including this one.</p>','gadgets/2026/soundcore-q11i.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(102,20,'Best Headphones Under Rs 10,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-headphones-under-10000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 10,000 you can buy well-built wireless headphones from JBL and Soundcore with excellent battery life. Strong noise cancellation is still mostly found above this price, though.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530bt\">JBL Tune 530BT</a>: Rs 9,500. Up to about 76 hours of battery and multipoint, with JBL\'s punchy sound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q20i\">Soundcore Q20i</a>: Rs 9,199. Adds hybrid noise cancellation, the cheapest decent ANC headphones here.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-650-pro\">boAt Rockerz 650 Pro</a>: Rs 6,299. Multipoint and up to about 80 hours of battery for much less.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a>: Rs 4,999. If you want to save more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530c\">JBL Tune 530C</a> uses a USB-C cable rather than Bluetooth, which is good for laptops, so check that your device supports USB-C audio. For proper noise cancellation for travel, see <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Compare</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q20i\">Soundcore Q20i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,199</td><td>Music: 40 hours, With ANC on, 60 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530bt\">JBL Tune 530BT</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,500</td><td>Music: 76 hours; 2 hours, Quick charge: 5 min...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-rockerz-650-pro\">boAt Rockerz 650 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,299</td><td>Music: 80 hours; 1 hour, Quick charge: 10 min...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-q11i\">Soundcore Q11i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,999</td><td>Music: 60 hours, With ANC off; 2 hours, Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-530c\">JBL Tune 530C</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;9,499</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The first question: do you want silence?</h2>\n<p>Only one pair in this list has real noise cancelling, and that fact divides the choice in two. If you study beside a busy road, work in a shared office or travel by bus, take the Soundcore Q20i and read no further. If your room is quiet, the others put their money into sound, battery and lightness, and may suit you better.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Q20i</h2>\n<p>Hybrid noise cancelling with forty hours of battery while it is on, sixty with it off. It reduces fan, engine and air-conditioner noise by a clear amount, and the over-ear pads block more by themselves. The app has the same equaliser as Soundcore\'s expensive models. The build is plastic and a little bulky. For under Rs 10,000 it is the best tool against noise.</p>\n<h2>JBL Tune 530BT</h2>\n<p>On-ear, light, with 76 hours of battery and JBL\'s energetic sound. It folds small and charges quickly. The on-ear pads press after an hour, so it is for music on the move and not for all-day wear. JBL\'s name also brings many copies, so check the serial number on JBL\'s site.</p>\n<h2>boAt Rockerz 650 Pro</h2>\n<p>Eighty hours, multipoint and an over-ear fit for Rs 6,299. The sound is bassy and fun. Build quality is average. It is a lot of headphone for the money.</p>\n<h2>JBL Tune 530C</h2>\n<p>This one has a USB-C cable and no Bluetooth. That sounds old-fashioned, but it is a good choice for laptops and phones without a headphone socket: no battery, no delay, no pairing. Editors and online teachers should consider it. Check that your phone or laptop outputs audio over USB-C.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Q11i</h2>\n<p>At Rs 4,999 it gives up noise cancelling and keeps most of the sound. If you are hesitating at Rs 9,000, this is the one that makes hesitating easier.</p>\n<h2>Over-ear or on-ear?</h2>\n<p>For anything longer than an hour, over-ear. For walking, gym and hot weather, on-ear. For glasses, over-ear with soft pads. For sharing a bag with books, on-ear, as it folds smaller.</p>\n<h2>How noise cancelling feels at first</h2>\n<p>Some people feel a slight pressure in the ears, as in a lift. It passes after a few days for most, and never for a few. Try the pair for ten minutes before buying. If the feeling bothers you, choose a pair with adjustable levels or rely on thick pads.</p>\n<h2>For online classes and work</h2>\n<p>Multipoint is the feature to insist on, so that your phone and laptop stay connected together. Microphones on all of these are acceptable indoors. If you lead meetings, a small USB microphone for Rs 3,000 improves your voice more than headphones at any price here.</p>\n<p>On Windows, choose the headphones for output and another microphone for input. Otherwise Bluetooth switches into call mode and the sound becomes thin.</p>\n<h2>For music</h2>\n<p>All of these are tuned for fun, with strong bass. Use the equaliser to bring voices forward. If you listen to classical, ghazal or acoustic music, a wired pair from Audio-Technica, Sennheiser or Sony at the same price will sound more natural, at the cost of the cable.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>Headphones in this class fail at the hinge, the pads and the headband cover. Look for metal in the slider. Check whether pads can be replaced. Never sit on the bag that holds them. A hard case costs Rs 800 and doubles their life.</p>\n<h2>Battery facts</h2>\n<p>Numbers such as 76 and 80 hours are measured at moderate volume with nothing else on. At high volume expect a third less. Charging for five to ten minutes gives a few hours on all of these, which is the real safety net.</p>\n<h2>Is it better to save for Rs 14,000?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/cmf-headphone-pro\">CMF Headphone Pro</a> at about Rs 13,999 brings far better noise cancelling, LDAC, a nicer build and clever controls. If you will wear headphones every day, those Rs 5,000 are well spent. It is discussed in <a href=\"/guides/best-headphones-under-30000-nepal\">headphones under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Person by person</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Student near a main road:</strong> Soundcore Q20i.</li>\n<li><strong>Music on the way to college:</strong> JBL Tune 530BT.</li>\n<li><strong>Long battery on a budget:</strong> boAt Rockerz 650 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Editor with a USB-C laptop:</strong> JBL Tune 530C.</li>\n<li><strong>Home use in a quiet room:</strong> Soundcore Q11i.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Checking for copies</h2>\n<p>JBL is the brand most often copied. Scan the code on the box, compare the weight with the official figure, look at the printing inside the headband and open the JBL app: a copy will not be recognised. Prices far below those on our pages are a warning.</p>\n<h2>Health and volume</h2>\n<p>Because they block sound, over-ear headphones allow lower volumes. Use that. If someone standing a metre away can hear your music, it is too loud. Most phones offer a volume warning and a weekly report. Leave it on.</p>\n<h2>Questions readers send</h2>\n<p><strong>Can they be used wired?</strong> The Bluetooth models in this list vary. Look for a 3.5mm input if you need it.</p>\n<p><strong>Are they good for PUBG?</strong> Bluetooth adds delay. The USB-C model does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Do they work with a PS5?</strong> Not directly by Bluetooth. Use a wired pair or a USB dongle headset.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Buy the Q20i if noise is your enemy, the Rockerz 650 Pro if value is your aim, and the Tune 530BT if you want small and light. And if you find yourself wanting all three, wait a month for the CMF.</p>\n<h2>One pair for study, travel and calls?</h2>\n<p>Students often ask for a single pair that does everything. At this budget the Soundcore Q20i comes nearest. It quietens a hostel corridor, lasts through exam week and connects to a laptop and phone together. It is too bulky for a run and too warm for a Nepalgunj afternoon. If those are part of your life, divide the money: Rs 4,999 for the Q11i at your desk and Rs 4,500 to 5,000 for ANC earbuds outside. Two modest pairs cover more of the day than one compromise.</p>','gadgets/2026/boat-rockerz-650-pro.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(103,20,'Best Headphones Under Rs 30,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-headphones-under-30000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 15,000–30,000 is where noise-cancelling headphones get genuinely good. Buses, flights and noisy offices go quiet, and the sound quality improves too.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-q45\">Soundcore Space Q45</a>: Rs 19,999. Strong ANC, LDAC, multipoint and up to 65 hours of battery. Our value pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/nothing-headphone-a\">Nothing Headphone (a)</a>: Rs 26,999. A distinctive design with good ANC and LDAC.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-2\">Soundcore Space 2</a>: Rs 24,499. Light and comfortable, with up to 70 hours of battery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/cmf-headphone-pro\">CMF Headphone Pro</a>: Rs 13,999. Most of the features for much less.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-780nc\">JBL Tune 780NC</a>: Rs 19,500. JBL\'s bass-heavy sound with noise cancellation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Above this budget, the <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm5\">Sony WH-1000XM5</a> and XM6 offer the best noise cancellation around. See our <a href=\"/guides/sony-headphones-price-in-nepal\">Sony headphones guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The field</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-q45\">Soundcore Space Q45</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;19,999</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 65 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/nothing-headphone-a\">Nothing Headphone (a)</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;26,999</td><td>Music: 75 hours, With ANC on, 135 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/soundcore-space-2\">Soundcore Space 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;24,499</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 70 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.1, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-headphone-pro\">CMF Headphone Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;13,999</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 50% Volume, 100...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/jbl-tune-780nc\">JBL Tune 780NC</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;19,500</td><td>Music: 50 hours, With ANC on, 76 hours, With...</td><td>Bluetooth 6.0, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm5\">Sony WH-1000XM5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;57,600</td><td>Music: 30 hours, With ANC on; 3 hours, Quick...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, multipoint, Codecs: SBC, AAC,...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Where headphones become a serious purchase</h2>\n<p>Between Rs 14,000 and 30,000 headphones change from accessories into tools. They can make an open-plan office bearable, turn a night bus into a bedroom and let you work through the neighbour\'s construction. For remote workers in Nepal, where homes are rarely silent, this is one of the better investments available, ahead of a new chair and just behind a second screen.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Space Q45: the balanced choice</h2>\n<p>Strong adaptive noise cancelling, LDAC, multipoint and fifty hours with ANC on. Pads are soft and the headband does not press. The app is full of options. The design is plain, which some will see as a virtue. It has been on sale for a while, and discounts make it a bargain.</p>\n<h2>Nothing Headphone (a): the characterful one</h2>\n<p>Nothing\'s design is bold, with physical controls that work better than touch pads: a roller for volume and a paddle for tracks. Battery life is remarkable, at 75 hours with noise cancelling on according to the sheet. If you are tired of swiping at your ear and skipping songs by accident, this is the answer.</p>\n<h2>Soundcore Space 2: the light one</h2>\n<p>For all-day wear, weight is everything. The Space 2 is made for people who forget they are wearing headphones. Noise cancelling is strong in the low range, the part that engines occupy.</p>\n<h2>CMF Headphone Pro: the surprise at Rs 13,999</h2>\n<p>It has no right to be so good for the money. Adaptive ANC, LDAC, replaceable pads, a physical roller and a slider for bass and treble. The finish is plastic, and the passive isolation is a little lower than that of the dearer ones. If someone asks us for the best value in audio in Nepal, this is usually our answer.</p>\n<h2>JBL Tune 780NC</h2>\n<p>JBL\'s bass, a light body and 50 hours with ANC. It suits people who like energy in their music more than accuracy.</p>\n<h2>What about Sony and Bose?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/sony-wh-1000xm5\">WH-1000XM5</a> lists at about Rs 57,600. Its noise cancelling of voices, microphones and transparency are better than any pair on this page. Are they better by Rs 30,000? For a daily flyer, perhaps. For someone at home, no. Used XM4 units at Rs 22,000 to 28,000 are worth a look if the pads and battery are healthy. Our <a href=\"/guides/sony-headphones-price-in-nepal\">Sony headphones page</a> has the current list.</p>\n<h2>How to test noise cancelling in a shop</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Wear them without turning them on, and notice how much the pads block.</li>\n<li>Turn on ANC. Listen for the fan, the street and the voices separately.</li>\n<li>Move your jaw and tilt your head. Glasses can break the seal, so test with yours.</li>\n<li>Turn on transparency and speak. Your own voice should sound natural.</li>\n<li>Play music quietly. It should stay full.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Comfort is not a detail</h2>\n<p>You will wear these for hundreds of hours. Look at the weight, ideally under 270 grams. Feel where the headband presses. Check that your ears do not touch the driver inside. Warm ears are inevitable in summer, and fabric pads help. Replaceable pads matter, because they wear out in two years and new ones cost a fraction of the price.</p>\n<h2>Calls and meetings</h2>\n<p>Microphones in this class are good indoors and fair outdoors. All but the cheapest connect to two devices. For Teams and Zoom on Windows, use the latest Bluetooth driver. If your laptop supports LE Audio and the headphones do too, voice quality improves a lot. Otherwise use a separate microphone.</p>\n<h2>Wired use</h2>\n<p>Check for a 3.5mm or USB-C audio mode. It lets you listen on a plane, to a piano, or with an empty battery. Some models play over USB-C while charging, others do not.</p>\n<h2>Sound: bass, balance and equalisers</h2>\n<p>Consumer headphones lean to bass. In the app, start from flat, add a little bass to taste, and lift the upper middle for clearer vocals. Save it. Ten minutes spent here changes the headphones more than the next price step.</p>\n<h2>Remote workers: a small calculation</h2>\n<p>If noise costs you half an hour of focus a day, a Rs 20,000 pair repays itself within weeks at any professional salary. Add the benefit of lower volume for your ears. That is why we recommend spending more here than on a faster phone.</p>\n<h2>Care and lifespan</h2>\n<p>Expect four to six years with care. Store them folded in their case. Wipe the pads weekly with a barely damp cloth. Keep them out of direct sun. Charge between 20 and 80 per cent when convenient. Update firmware twice a year.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and authenticity</h2>\n<p>Soundcore, JBL, Nothing and Sony have distributors in Nepal. Ask for one year and a stamped card. High-priced headphones are copied, even with convincing boxes. The companion app is your friend: if it does not recognise the product, return it.</p>\n<h2>Which should you get?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best value:</strong> CMF Headphone Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Best balance:</strong> Soundcore Space Q45.</li>\n<li><strong>All-day comfort:</strong> Soundcore Space 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Controls and battery:</strong> Nothing Headphone (a).</li>\n<li><strong>Bass:</strong> JBL Tune 780NC.</li>\n<li><strong>The quietest possible:</strong> save for Sony.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Try the CMF first. If it is comfortable and quiet enough for your life, buy it and keep the change. If you sit in meetings all day, look at the Nothing for its controls or the Space Q45 for its quiet. Whichever you take, spend the first evening with the equaliser and the fit. That is where the money turns into music.</p>\n<h2>A small experiment for glasses wearers</h2>\n<p>Noise cancelling depends on a seal, and the arms of spectacles break it. In the shop, listen with your glasses on, then slide the arms up above the pads for a moment. If the noise drops sharply, those pads are too stiff for you. Softer, deeper pads such as those on the Space 2 or CMF bend around the frame. Thin metal frames also help. If you are choosing new glasses this year, that is a good reason to prefer slim arms.</p>','gadgets/2026/nothing-headphone-a.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(104,20,'Best Smartwatches Under Rs 5,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-smartwatches-under-5000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 5,000, a smartwatch is really a fitness band with a bigger screen. It counts steps, tracks heart rate and sleep, and shows notifications, and many can take calls over Bluetooth.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a>: Rs 4,499. The most reliable choice here, lasting up to about 18 days per charge.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-call-3\">boAt Wave Call 3</a>: Rs 3,199. Bluetooth calling and up to about 10 days of battery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/ultima-watch-active\">Ultima Watch Active</a>: Rs 4,299. A local favourite that does the basics well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Keep expectations realistic</h2>\n<p>None of these have built-in GPS, so runs and walks are estimated rather than mapped. Heart-rate and SpO2 readings are rough guides, not medical measurements. If accurate tracking matters, stretch to a watch with GPS in our <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal\">under Rs 20,000</a> guide.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The three</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-5-active\">Redmi Watch 5 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,499</td><td>2.0-inch LCD (320×385), 500 nits</td><td>Up to 18 days normal use</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-wave-call-3\">boAt Wave Call 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;3,199</td><td>1.83 inches, LCD</td><td>Up to 10 days (normal usage); 300 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/ultima-watch-active\">Ultima Watch Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;4,299</td><td>1.43 inches</td><td>–</td><td>Bluetooth v5.3</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What a Rs 4,000 watch is</h2>\n<p>It is a small screen that shows your notifications, counts your steps, estimates your heart rate and wakes you with a gentle buzz. Some let you answer calls. It is not a medical device, a GPS tracker or an app platform, whatever the box suggests. Used for what it is, it is a lovely thing. Many people sleep better and walk more simply because something on their wrist counts.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 5 Active</h2>\n<p>Our choice. A two-inch LCD at 500 nits, up to 18 days between charges, and Xiaomi\'s app, which is stable and keeps its data for years. Bluetooth calling works through the watch speaker. Tracking is reasonable for walking. It looks like a rectangular smartwatch and weighs little.</p>\n<h2>boAt Wave Call 3</h2>\n<p>Cheaper, with calling and many watch faces. The app is busier and the tracking less consistent. For someone who wants notifications and a bright face for Rs 3,199, it is fine.</p>\n<h2>Ultima Watch Active</h2>\n<p>A brand with a strong presence in Nepal, and that means local service and easy exchange. The round 1.43-inch face looks like a normal watch. If you value the shop around the corner, choose it.</p>\n<h2>Accuracy, honestly</h2>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong> good enough. All of them overcount on bumpy roads and undercount when you push a trolley.</p>\n<p><strong>Heart rate at rest:</strong> usually within a few beats.</p>\n<p><strong>Heart rate during exercise:</strong> often wrong, particularly with a loose strap, tattoos or cold skin.</p>\n<p><strong>SpO2 and blood pressure:</strong> treat SpO2 as a curiosity. Any watch at this price that claims blood pressure or blood sugar is guessing. Please do not change medicine because of a watch.</p>\n<p><strong>Sleep:</strong> start and end times are useful. Stages are estimates.</p>\n<p><strong>Calories:</strong> rough.</p>\n<h2>No GPS, and why it matters</h2>\n<p>Without GPS, distance is calculated from steps. Uphill and downhill confuse it. Some watches use the phone\'s GPS when you carry it, which works well. If you run or cycle without a phone, save for a watch with its own GPS. The <a href=\"/products/mwc-aether-one-watch-pro\">MWC Aether One Watch Pro</a> at about Rs 7,000 and the <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a> at Rs 10,999 are the first steps.</p>\n<h2>Screens: LCD at this level</h2>\n<p>LCDs are bright enough indoors and fair outside. They cannot show an always-on clock without draining the battery, so you raise your wrist to wake them. Test this in the shop. A watch that fails to wake when you lift it is annoying every single hour.</p>\n<h2>Calls on your wrist</h2>\n<p>Bluetooth calling uses your phone\'s connection. It is handy in the kitchen and rude in a microbus. The speaker is small. Make sure that it pairs as both a watch and a headset, and turn the second part off if you do not use it, as it drains both batteries.</p>\n<h2>Notifications and Devanagari</h2>\n<p>Check that messages in Nepali appear properly. Some cheap watches show boxes in place of Devanagari letters. Send yourself a message in the shop. Redmi handles it well, others vary.</p>\n<h2>Compatibility</h2>\n<p>All work with Android and iPhone, with fewer functions on an iPhone. On Android, allow the watch app to run in the background, or it will stop sending notifications after an hour. This is the most common complaint, and it takes one setting to fix.</p>\n<h2>Straps and skin</h2>\n<p>Silicone straps trap sweat. Rinse the strap and your wrist, and dry both, after exercise. If your skin becomes red, loosen the strap and switch wrists for a few days. Prefer a watch with a standard 20 or 22mm strap, so that you can change it for fabric or leather.</p>\n<h2>Water</h2>\n<p>IP68 or 5ATM is fine for washing hands and rain. Hot showers and soap are bad for seals. None of these is meant for swimming unless it says 5ATM and lists a swim mode.</p>\n<h2>Children and elders</h2>\n<p>For a child, remember that these watches have no SIM and no location. A child\'s watch with a SIM is a different product. For an elder, the useful parts are large text, the alarm and a vibration for calls. Fall detection is not available here.</p>\n<h2>Privacy</h2>\n<p>You give the app your sleep, heart rate, contacts and location. Use a known brand\'s app, deny permissions that make no sense, and set a password. Very cheap unbranded watches use apps from unknown developers. Avoid them.</p>\n<h2>How long will it last?</h2>\n<p>Two to three years. Batteries shrink and straps break. Repairs are rarely worth it at this price. Ask for a warranty of at least six months, and a year where possible.</p>\n<h2>Choose by purpose</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First smartwatch:</strong> Redmi Watch 5 Active.</li>\n<li><strong>Local service and classic look:</strong> Ultima Watch Active.</li>\n<li><strong>Lowest price with calling:</strong> boAt Wave Call 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Fitness with some accuracy:</strong> a band such as the <a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Huawei Band 10</a> at about Rs 8,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A cheaper idea</h2>\n<p>If you only want steps, your phone already counts them when it is in your pocket. Use it for a month. If you find yourself checking it, buy a watch. If you forget, you have saved Rs 4,500.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>For under Rs 5,000 the Redmi Watch 5 Active is the dependable answer. Treat its health numbers as hints, charge it every two weeks, and it will quietly improve your days. If you catch yourself wanting maps, accuracy or an AMOLED screen, go straight to <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-10000-nepal\">the next budget</a> and skip the regret.</p>\n<h2>A week with a cheap watch: what it feels like</h2>\n<p>On the first day you change faces twenty times. On the second you notice that you no longer miss calls in the kitchen. By the fourth, you have turned off notifications from three noisy groups. At the end of the week the sleep chart shows something you already knew: late nights on Friday, short sleep on Sunday. That is the real product. The numbers are rough, and the habit of noticing is worth more than their accuracy.</p>\n<p>If after two weeks the watch lies on a shelf, give it to a younger cousin and feel no guilt. At this price it was a fair experiment, and you have learned that you do not need a Rs 15,000 one.</p>','gadgets/2026/redmi-watch-5-active.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(105,20,'Best Smartwatches Under Rs 10,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-smartwatches-under-10000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Between Rs 5,000 and Rs 10,000, smartwatches get AMOLED screens that are easy to read in sunlight, and much better battery life.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/honor-watch-x5i\">Honor Watch X5i</a>: Rs 8,999. An AMOLED screen and up to about three weeks of typical use. Our top pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/mwc-aether-one-watch-pro\">MWC Aether One Watch Pro</a>: Rs 6,999. A rare find at this price: AMOLED <em>and</em> built-in GPS.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Samsung Galaxy Fit 3</a>: Rs 7,999. A slim band with a big AMOLED screen, and the obvious choice for Samsung phone owners.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Huawei Band 10</a>: Rs 7,990. Light, accurate and long-lasting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>boAt\'s <a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-regal\">Ultima Regal</a> (Rs 6,099) looks more like a classic watch, but its tracking is less accurate. For GPS and better health tracking, see <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal\">under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Five on the wrist</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-watch-x5i\">Honor Watch X5i</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;8,999</td><td>1.97 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 21 days (typical usage) / Up to 6 days...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.4</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/mwc-aether-one-watch-pro\">MWC Aether One Watch Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,999</td><td>1.43 inches, AMOLED</td><td>–</td><td>GPS: Yes, with GPS</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/galaxy-fit-3\">Samsung Galaxy Fit 3</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,999</td><td>1.6 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 13 days (normal usage); 208 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/huawei-band-10\">Huawei Band 10</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;7,990</td><td>1.47 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 8 days (normal usage), Up to 14 hours (...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.0</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/boat-ultima-regal\">boAt Ultima Regal</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;6,099</td><td>2.01 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 7 days (normal usage); 300 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>AMOLED changes everything</h2>\n<p>The step from Rs 4,000 to 8,000 is easiest to see in sunlight. AMOLED screens stay readable, show an always-on face and look like jewellery in the dark. They also tend to come with better sensors and more careful software. If you plan to wear a watch every day for two years, this is the first budget we would recommend without warnings.</p>\n<h2>Band or watch?</h2>\n<p>The Galaxy Fit 3 and Huawei Band 10 are slim bands. They weigh less, disappear under a sleeve and are comfortable in sleep. The Honor, MWC and boAt are full watches, with more room for text and better calling. If sleep tracking is important, choose a band. If you read messages on your wrist, choose a watch.</p>\n<h2>Honor Watch X5i</h2>\n<p>A large AMOLED rectangle with up to three weeks of typical use. It is comfortable, bright and simple. For notifications, calls and general tracking, it is the best balanced of the group, and our top pick. There is no GPS of its own.</p>\n<h2>MWC Aether One Watch Pro</h2>\n<p>Built-in GPS and AMOLED for under Rs 7,000 is extraordinary on paper. The brand is young, so ask about the app\'s reliability and the warranty. For runners on a tight budget it is worth trying, with a return option agreed.</p>\n<h2>Samsung Galaxy Fit 3</h2>\n<p>A refined band with a wide AMOLED, fall detection and emergency alerts, and it fits into Samsung Health. It works with Android and not with iPhone. For Samsung owners and for elders, it is the one to choose: press the button several times and it alerts chosen contacts.</p>\n<h2>Huawei Band 10</h2>\n<p>Huawei\'s sensors are among the best at this price, particularly for sleep and swimming. It is light and lasts more than a week. On Android, the Huawei Health app is installed from Huawei\'s site, because the Play Store version is old. That takes two minutes and puts some people off.</p>\n<h2>boAt Ultima Regal</h2>\n<p>It looks handsome with a metal frame. Tracking is weaker. Buy it as a watch that shows notifications.</p>\n<h2>Who needs GPS?</h2>\n<p>If you run, cycle or trek without your phone, you do. If you carry the phone, connected GPS does the job on most of these, at the cost of phone battery. If you walk in the neighbourhood, steps are enough.</p>\n<h2>Health features that are useful</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resting heart rate over weeks.</strong> A rising line can mean stress, illness or poor sleep.</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep duration and regularity.</strong> Most of us sleep less than we believe.</li>\n<li><strong>Inactivity reminders.</strong> Good for desk workers.</li>\n<li><strong>Cycle tracking.</strong> Present on Samsung, Huawei and Honor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>SpO2 is of some interest to trekkers, though a fingertip oximeter for Rs 1,500 is more accurate above 3,000 metres. None of these is a medical device. If numbers worry you, see a doctor.</p>\n<h2>Battery claims translated</h2>\n<p>Three weeks means light use without an always-on display. With always-on, continuous heart rate, sleep tracking and several calls, divide by two or three. All of these still last more than most people need: charging once a week on a Saturday morning is a habit that works.</p>\n<h2>Compatibility check</h2>\n<p>Galaxy Fit 3 is for Android only. Huawei, Honor and MWC work with both. On iPhones you cannot reply to messages from any of them. Make sure the app is available in your phone\'s store in Nepal, and that Devanagari shows correctly.</p>\n<h2>Swimming and water</h2>\n<p>5ATM watches such as the Galaxy Fit 3 and Huawei Band 10 can be used in a pool. Rinse them afterwards. IP68 alone is not for swimming. Hot water and steam are bad for all.</p>\n<h2>Straps</h2>\n<p>Bands often use their own connectors, so replacement straps must be ordered. Watches with 22mm straps can wear anything from Rs 300 upwards. Ask for a spare when you bargain.</p>\n<h2>Comparing with a used Apple or Galaxy Watch</h2>\n<p>Old Galaxy Watch 4 units appear near this price. They offer apps, replies and payments where supported, and need charging every day. Their batteries are tired. We would choose a new band over an old smartwatch, unless you want apps more than battery.</p>\n<h2>Set it up well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Enter your height, weight and age correctly.</li>\n<li>Wear it a finger\'s width above the wrist bone, snug in exercise and looser at night.</li>\n<li>Choose which apps may notify you. Five is plenty.</li>\n<li>Turn on do not disturb with your sleep schedule.</li>\n<li>Allow the app to run in the background on Android.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Which to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday watch:</strong> Honor Watch X5i.</li>\n<li><strong>Samsung owner or elder:</strong> Galaxy Fit 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep and swim tracking:</strong> Huawei Band 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Runner on a budget:</strong> MWC Aether One Watch Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Style:</strong> boAt Ultima Regal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Stretching to Rs 11,000</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a> has an AMOLED screen and its own GPS from a well-known brand, at Rs 10,999. If that fits, it replaces half of this list. See <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal\">watches under Rs 20,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Honor Watch X5i is the all-rounder, the Galaxy Fit 3 is the thoughtful gift and the Huawei Band 10 is the quiet athlete. Pick by whether you will look at it, give it or train with it.</p>\n<h2>Two common questions at this price</h2>\n<p><strong>Galaxy Fit 3 or Huawei Band 10?</strong> If you own a Samsung phone, the Fit 3, for its simple set-up, wider screen and emergency features. If you swim or care about sleep detail, the Band 10. With an iPhone, the Band 10 is the only one of the two that works.</p>\n<p><strong>Is it better to buy a used smartwatch for Rs 10,000?</strong> Usually not. Old smartwatches have tired batteries that barely last a day, and their updates have ended. A new band with two weeks of battery will be worn. The old watch will be charged, then forgotten. The one exception is a relative\'s Apple Watch with good battery health, given to you for little or nothing.</p>','gadgets/2026/honor-watch-x5i.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(106,20,'Best Smartwatches Under Rs 20,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-smartwatches-under-20000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Rs 10,000–20,000 is the best value range for smartwatches in Nepal. Most watches here have AMOLED screens, built-in GPS and battery life measured in weeks.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a>: Rs 10,999. AMOLED, GPS and up to about 12 days of normal use. The value pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-lite\">Redmi Watch 6 Lite</a>: Rs 14,999. A step up in design, with the same strong battery life.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/cmf-watch-3-pro\">CMF Watch 3 Pro</a>: Rs 12,999. A stylish round design with accurate GPS.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/realme-watch-s5\">Realme Watch S5</a>: Rs 15,999. A good screen and GPS, with up to 16 days of battery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">Amazfit GTR Mini</a>: Rs 17,499. A compact, classic-looking watch for smaller wrists.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of these work with Android and iPhone. Built-in GPS means you can leave your phone at home on a morning walk and still get an accurate route. For more premium watches, see <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Compare the picks</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Battery</th><th>Connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-active\">Redmi Watch 6 Active</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;10,999</td><td>1.85 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 18 days (light usage), Up to 12 days (n...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, BDS, Gal...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-watch-6-lite\">Redmi Watch 6 Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;14,999</td><td>1.96 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 18 days (light usage), Up to 12 days (n...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS, BDS, Gal...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/cmf-watch-3-pro\">CMF Watch 3 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;12,999</td><td>1.43 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 13 days (normal usage), Up to 60 days (...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS (L1+L5),...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/realme-watch-s5\">Realme Watch S5</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;15,999</td><td>1.43 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 16 days (normal usage), Up to 20 days (...</td><td>Bluetooth 5.3, GPS: Yes, with GPS, GLONASS, G...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/amazfit-gtr-mini\">Amazfit GTR Mini</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;17,499</td><td>1.28 inches, AMOLED</td><td>Up to 14 days (normal usage); 280 mAh</td><td>Bluetooth 5.2, GPS: Yes, with A-GPS</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The best-value shelf for watches</h2>\n<p>Spend Rs 11,000 to 17,000 and you get an AMOLED screen, built-in GPS, Bluetooth calls and a battery that lasts one to two weeks. Double the money and you gain materials, accuracy and apps. Half the money and you lose GPS and reliability. For a person who walks, runs a little and wants to leave the phone in the bag, this shelf is where to stop.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 6 Active</h2>\n<p>At Rs 10,999 it does nearly everything the others do. A bright AMOLED, GPS, calls and around twelve days of normal use. The frame is plastic. Xiaomi\'s app is easy and syncs with Strava and Apple Health. It is our value pick by a wide margin.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Watch 6 Lite</h2>\n<p>A larger display and a smarter body for Rs 4,000 more. Functions are alike. Buy it if you care about how it looks with formal clothes.</p>\n<h2>CMF Watch 3 Pro</h2>\n<p>Round, playful, with an interchangeable bezel and accurate GPS. The software is clean, as you would expect from Nothing\'s family, and it pairs well with Nothing and CMF phones. For style at this price it is our favourite.</p>\n<h2>Realme Watch S5</h2>\n<p>A round AMOLED with up to sixteen days of battery. It is the best fit for Realme phone owners. The app is simpler than Xiaomi\'s or Zepp.</p>\n<h2>Amazfit GTR Mini</h2>\n<p>A compact, classic watch with stainless steel and Zepp OS, for smaller wrists. It has been around for some time, and its GPS and sensors are respected. Zepp\'s app is strong on readiness and sleep. Price and stock vary, so ask.</p>\n<h2>GPS in the valley</h2>\n<p>Narrow streets and tall houses confuse any GPS. Dual-band watches cope better, and cost more. On open roads, ring road mornings and hill trails, the watches here record a clean track. Let the watch find satellites for a minute before you start, and keep the app updated with its satellite data.</p>\n<h2>Running and training</h2>\n<p>For a 5K plan, any of these is fine. For intervals and half marathons, wrist heart rate lags. A chest strap costs Rs 5,000 to 8,000 and connects to some watches. Amazfit supports external sensors better than Redmi or CMF. If you train seriously, look at Amazfit\'s Active or Cheetah lines, or a used Garmin.</p>\n<h2>Trekking</h2>\n<p>These can record a day on the trail. They are not made for a two-week trek: GPS eats the battery in ten to twenty hours, and none stores maps. Take a power bank, record in short sections, and use a phone app with offline maps for navigation. For trekking as a habit, a rugged watch with long GPS life makes more sense.</p>\n<h2>Living with notifications</h2>\n<p>Choose the apps that may disturb you. Messages from family, calls, calendar and the bank are enough. A watch that buzzes for every group message will be taken off within a month. All of these show Devanagari, though fonts differ. Replies are limited to short templates on Android and are not available on iPhone.</p>\n<h2>What about health claims?</h2>\n<p>Heart rate, sleep and activity trends are useful. Stress and body battery scores are playful. SpO2 is approximate. No watch at this price measures blood pressure or glucose. If you have a heart condition, ask your doctor which device is certified.</p>\n<h2>Battery expectations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Raise-to-wake, normal use: 10 to 14 days.</li>\n<li>Always-on display: 4 to 6 days.</li>\n<li>One hour of GPS a day: about 5 days.</li>\n<li>Frequent calls on the watch: 4 to 5 days.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Phone matching</h2>\n<p><strong>Xiaomi and POCO:</strong> Redmi Watch. <strong>Nothing and CMF:</strong> Watch 3 Pro. <strong>Realme and OPPO:</strong> Watch S5. <strong>Samsung:</strong> any, or consider a Galaxy Watch FE when discounted. <strong>iPhone:</strong> Amazfit GTR Mini or Redmi, both sync to Apple Health.</p>\n<h2>Durability and water</h2>\n<p>5ATM is the norm here, good for pool swimming. Glass scratches more easily than the sapphire in costlier watches, so a thin protector is worthwhile for round faces. Change the strap for fabric if your skin reacts to silicone.</p>\n<h2>Warranty and service</h2>\n<p>Xiaomi, Realme, CMF and Amazfit have distributors in Nepal. One year is standard. A watch that fails usually fails early, so wear it every day in the first weeks. Keep the box and the bill.</p>\n<h2>New or last year\'s premium?</h2>\n<p>Last year\'s Huawei Watch Fit or a discounted Amazfit can fall into this price during sales. A used Apple Watch SE can, too, for iPhone owners who accept daily charging and a worn battery. If you want replies, apps and tight integration, that might please you more than a new fitness watch.</p>\n<h2>Our recommendations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Value:</strong> Redmi Watch 6 Active.</li>\n<li><strong>Design:</strong> CMF Watch 3 Pro.</li>\n<li><strong>Small wrists:</strong> Amazfit GTR Mini.</li>\n<li><strong>Longest between charges:</strong> Realme Watch S5.</li>\n<li><strong>Office wear:</strong> Redmi Watch 6 Lite.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The next step up</h2>\n<p>From Rs 25,000 you meet the <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-fit-5\">Huawei Watch Fit 5</a> and, a little above, the <a href=\"/products/huawei-watch-gt-6\">Watch GT 6</a> with its excellent battery and sensors. They are covered in <a href=\"/guides/best-smartwatches-under-30000-nepal\">watches under Rs 30,000</a>.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Take the Redmi Watch 6 Active if you want the sensible answer and the CMF Watch 3 Pro if you want to smile when you look at your wrist. Both will count your steps. Only one will start conversations.</p>\n<h2>How to compare two watches in five minutes</h2>\n<p>Wear one on each wrist and walk to the corner and back. Compare the step counts with a count in your head for a hundred steps. Look at both screens in the sun. Raise each wrist ten times and note how often the screen wakes. Send a message in Nepali to both. Then take them off and look at the marks left by the straps. The better watch for you will usually be obvious, and it will not always be the more expensive one.</p>','gadgets/2026/redmi-watch-6-lite.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(107,20,'Best Tablets Under Rs 30,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-tablets-under-30000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Under Rs 30,000 is the right budget for a tablet that\'s mainly for YouTube, Netflix, reading and online classes. Big batteries are common at this price, but fast chips aren\'t.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 (11-inch)</a>: Rs 29,999. A sharp 2.5K screen, quad speakers and a 9,000mAh battery. Our top pick.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x8b\">Honor Pad X8b</a>: Rs 29,999. A huge 10,100mAh battery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2-9-7\">Redmi Pad 2 9.7</a>: Rs 25,999. Smaller and lighter, a good size for children.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab A11</a>: Rs 21,999. The cheapest Samsung, with long software support.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Blackview and Infinix sell many tablets at this price, such as the <a href=\"/products/infinix-xpad-20-pro\">Infinix XPAD 20 Pro</a>. They\'re fine for videos, but screens and software support are usually weaker than Xiaomi\'s or Samsung\'s. For note-taking with a pen, see our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>Five tablets compared</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 11-inch</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ), 600...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultra (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/honor-pad-x8b\">Honor Pad X8b</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 680 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB</td><td>Li-Ion 10100 mAh, non-removable</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2-9-7\">Redmi Pad 2 9.7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;25,999</td><td>9.7 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 500 nits (typ), 60...</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 6s 4G Gen 2</td><td>4GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 7600 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-a11\">Samsung Galaxy Tab A11</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;21,999</td><td>8.7 inches TFT LCD, 90Hz</td><td>Mediatek Helio G99 (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 5100 mAh, non-removable; 15W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/infinix-xpad-20-pro\">Infinix XPAD 20 Pro</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;26,999</td><td>12 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 450 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultimate (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>What a cheap tablet is good for</h2>\n<p>A tablet under Rs 30,000 is a window. It shows films, lessons, cartoons, books and video calls on a larger screen than your phone, with a battery that lasts an evening or three. It is not a computer. People who buy it as a window are happy for years. People who buy it to replace a laptop return to the shop within a month.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Pad 2 (11-inch)</h2>\n<p>The best screen of the group, at 2.5K, with four speakers and a 9,000mAh battery. The Helio G100 Ultra is adequate: YouTube, Zoom, reading and light games work smoothly. Charging at 18W is slow for such a battery, so leave it overnight. The listed Rs 29,999 is for the version most shops hold. Smaller ones cost less, so check memory. Take at least 6GB.</p>\n<h2>Honor Pad X8b</h2>\n<p>A 10,100mAh battery gives it the longest life. The Snapdragon 680 is an older chip and the screen is plainer than the Redmi\'s. It is for people who watch for hours away from a socket. The 4GB version is too small for comfort.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Pad 2 9.7</h2>\n<p>A compact tablet with a 120Hz screen, easy to hold in one hand for reading. It has 4GB of memory, so it suits one thing at a time. For a child or for reading in bed, the size is ideal.</p>\n<h2>Samsung Galaxy Tab A11</h2>\n<p>At 8.7 inches it is nearly a large phone. Samsung Kids, the long software support and the service network make it the natural choice for small children and grandparents. The screen resolution is low, so it is not for reading PDFs.</p>\n<h2>Infinix XPAD 20 Pro</h2>\n<p>A 12-inch screen with 45W charging at under Rs 27,000 is tempting, with a chip like the Redmi\'s. Infinix has less of a history in tablets and updates are uncertain. If you want the biggest picture for the money, see it in person.</p>\n<h2>Online classes: what matters</h2>\n<p>The front camera should sit on the long side, so that your face is centred in landscape. The microphone should be clear. The tablet should run Zoom or Meet with a document beside it, which needs 6GB or more. A stand case matters more than any of these, because a tablet leaning against books falls.</p>\n<h2>Reading PDFs and books</h2>\n<p>An A4 page on an 11-inch 2.5K screen is readable without zooming. On an 8.7-inch HD screen it is not. Students with scanned notes should choose the Redmi Pad 2. For long reading at night, use the reading mode and lower the brightness, as LCD backlights are tiring in the dark.</p>\n<h2>Pens on a budget</h2>\n<p>Most tablets here accept only simple capacitive pens, which are like writing with a finger. Some support the maker\'s own active pen, sold separately and not always in stock in Nepal. If handwriting is the reason you are buying, save for a <a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a> with its pen or a <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a>, which includes one.</p>\n<h2>Films and Widevine</h2>\n<p>Streaming apps limit quality on devices without Widevine L1 certification. Ask the seller to install DRM Info and show you the level. L3 means that Netflix will play at about 480p on your beautiful 2.5K screen. YouTube is not affected.</p>\n<h2>Games</h2>\n<p>The Helio G100 tablets run Free Fire, PUBG Mobile on smooth settings and all casual games. The Snapdragon 680 handles light games. A large screen makes the controls easier and the picture rougher. For serious play, look at the Xiaomi Pad 7.</p>\n<h2>Wi-Fi, SIM and hotspots</h2>\n<p>Cheaper versions have Wi-Fi only. If you need mobile data, sharing from your phone works well and costs nothing. LTE versions must be registered in MDMS like phones, so check the IMEI.</p>\n<h2>Refurbished and used</h2>\n<p>An old iPad in good condition is often offered at this price. A 9th generation iPad is still a fine reader and note-taker with a first-generation Pencil. Check the battery, the Apple ID lock and how many iPadOS versions remain. For a child, be careful: glass repairs are expensive.</p>\n<p>Very cheap new tablets with names you have never seen, at Rs 8,000 to 12,000, are best avoided. They have slow chips, dull screens, old Android and no updates, and they claim memory that they do not have.</p>\n<h2>Setting up for a child</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a child account and add it to Family Link.</li>\n<li>Set a daily limit and a bedtime.</li>\n<li>Approve every app and turn off purchases.</li>\n<li>Use YouTube Kids in place of YouTube.</li>\n<li>Put on a thick case with a handle.</li>\n<li>Keep the volume limit on if headphones are used.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery care</h2>\n<p>Tablets spend weeks on a shelf. A battery left empty for months can fail. Charge it to about half before storing it and top it up now and then. If it is always plugged in, for example as a kitchen screen, turn on battery protection where the option exists.</p>\n<h2>Questions people ask</h2>\n<p><strong>Is 4GB enough?</strong> For video and one app, yes. For classes and multitasking, choose 6GB or 8GB.</p>\n<p><strong>Can it connect to a TV?</strong> By casting, if the TV supports it. Most at this price have no video output through USB-C.</p>\n<p><strong>Can it run Microsoft Office?</strong> The mobile apps, yes. They are fine for reading and small edits.</p>\n<p><strong>How many years will it last?</strong> Three to five for video and reading, with a careful owner.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Redmi Pad 2 is the one to beat, and it is our recommendation for families and students who mostly read and watch. Choose the Tab A11 for small children and the Honor for battery. And if your needs include handwriting or speed, spending Rs 15,000 more takes you to a different class of tablet in our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-under-50000-nepal\">next guide</a>.</p>','gadgets/2026/redmi-pad-2.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00'),(108,20,'Best Tablets Under Rs 50,000 in Nepal (November 2026)','best-tablets-under-50000-nepal','buying-guide','<p>Between Rs 30,000 and Rs 50,000, tablets get faster chips and sharper, smoother screens. This is good territory for students who take notes and for light creative work.</p>\n<h2>Our picks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a>: Rs 44,999. A 3.2K 144Hz screen and a Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3. The best performance in this range.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oneplus-pad-go-2\">OnePlus Pad Go 2</a>: Rs 49,999. A 10,050mAh battery and a Dimensity 7300, with smooth software.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/oneplus-pad-lite\">OnePlus Pad Lite</a>: Rs 34,999. A cheaper OnePlus with a big battery, and there\'s an LTE version for using data away from Wi-Fi.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 (11-inch)</a>: from Rs 29,999. If you\'d rather save, it\'s still excellent for watching and reading.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you want to write by hand, check whether a pen is supported and if it\'s included. The <a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a> (Rs 74,999) is above this budget but comes with an S Pen. More options are in our <a href=\"/guides/best-tablets-nepal\">tablet guide</a>.</p>\n<!--depth-->\n<h2>The line-up</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Price in Nepal</th><th>Display</th><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"/products/xiaomi-pad-7\">Xiaomi Pad 7</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;44,999</td><td>11.2 inches IPS LCD, 144Hz, 800 nits (typ)</td><td>Qualcomm Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 8850 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-pad-go-2\">OnePlus Pad Go 2</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;49,999</td><td>12.1 inches IPS LCD, 120Hz, 900 nits (HBM)</td><td>Mediatek Dimensity 7300 Ultra (4 nm)</td><td>8GB LPDDR5X</td><td>Li-Po 10050 mAh; 33W Fast Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/oneplus-pad-lite\">OnePlus Pad Lite</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;34,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ)</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 (6nm)</td><td>6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9340 mAh, non-removable; 33W SUPERVOOC...</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/redmi-pad-2\">Redmi Pad 2 11-inch</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;29,999</td><td>11 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 500 nits (typ), 600...</td><td>Mediatek Helio G100 Ultra (6nm)</td><td>4GB / 6GB / 8GB LPDDR4X</td><td>Li-Po 9000 mAh, non-removable; 18W Charging</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"/products/samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe\">Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE</a></td><td>Rs&nbsp;74,999</td><td>10.8 - 10.9 inches IPS LCD, 90Hz, 600 nits (t...</td><td>Samsung Exynos 1580 (4nm)</td><td>8GB / 12GB LPDDR5</td><td>Li-Po 8000 mAh, non-removable; 45W Fast Charg...</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>The point where tablets become quick</h2>\n<p>Under Rs 30,000 a tablet waits for you to be patient. At Rs 45,000 that stops. Apps open at once, two of them share the screen without fuss, the pen keeps up with your hand and games look like games. If you plan to use a tablet for more than an hour every day, this is the level at which it feels like a proper tool.</p>\n<h2>Xiaomi Pad 7: our pick</h2>\n<p>The Snapdragon 7+ Gen 3 is by a wide margin the fastest chip under Rs 50,000. The 11.2-inch screen has a 3.2K resolution at 144Hz and 800 nits, with a 3:2 shape that suits documents better than the wide screens of its rivals. Reading a PDF, marking up slides or working with two apps side by side feels natural. HyperOS on tablets has good multitasking and some clutter to remove. Xiaomi sells a pen and a keyboard cover, and you should check that they are in stock and what they cost, because the total approaches Rs 65,000 with both.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus Pad Go 2: for long evenings</h2>\n<p>A 12.1-inch screen and a battery of more than 10,000mAh make it the best film tablet of the group. The Dimensity 7300 is a step below the Xiaomi\'s chip and perfectly smooth. OxygenOS is clean. If you watch more than you write, choose this one.</p>\n<h2>OnePlus Pad Lite: the one with a SIM option</h2>\n<p>With a Helio G100 it belongs to the slower class. Its attraction is a large battery, a simple interface and an LTE version for field workers, sales teams and families in places without fibre.</p>\n<h2>Redmi Pad 2: save Rs 15,000</h2>\n<p>It remains an excellent screen for reading and lessons. If your use is light, the faster chip in the Pad 7 will be wasted on you.</p>\n<h2>Stretching to the Galaxy Tab S10 FE</h2>\n<p>At Rs 74,999 it is outside the budget, and we mention it for one group of people. If you take handwritten notes daily, its included S Pen, water resistance and seven years of updates make it cheaper over a degree than buying a Rs 45,000 tablet plus a pen plus a replacement in three years.</p>\n<h2>Pen, keyboard, case: the true price</h2>\n<p>Work it out before you pay. A Xiaomi Pad 7 at Rs 45,000, a pen at around Rs 6,000 to 8,000, and a keyboard cover at about Rs 10,000 make Rs 60,000 or more. At that point an entry laptop with 16GB is not far away. If you need a keyboard every day, read <a href=\"/guides/laptop-vs-tablet-work-study-nepal\">laptop or tablet</a> first. If you need only the pen, the tablet is still the better choice.</p>\n<h2>Note-taking in practice</h2>\n<p>Try three things in the shop with the pen. Write small Devanagari at normal speed and see whether the strokes keep their shape. Rest your palm on the glass and see whether it leaves marks. Write for two minutes and feel whether the pen is too smooth. A matte protector helps a lot. Good note apps on Android include Samsung Notes on Samsung tablets, Xiaomi\'s own, and cross-platform choices such as OneNote, Squid and Xodo for PDFs.</p>\n<h2>As a second screen</h2>\n<p>Many people overlook this. With apps such as Spacedesk, or the maker\'s own sharing feature, an 11-inch tablet can act as an additional display for a Windows laptop. For students who write with a source open, that is a good reason to own one.</p>\n<h2>Games and creative work</h2>\n<p>The Pad 7 runs PUBG Mobile at high frame rates and handles most games in the store with ease. For drawing, look at Infinite Painter, Krita or ibisPaint. Video editing in CapCut is smooth. Professional illustrators will still prefer an iPad with Procreate, whose entry price is about Rs 83,500 plus the pencil.</p>\n<h2>Battery and charging</h2>\n<p>The Pad Go 2 lasts longest, followed by the Pad Lite and Redmi. The Xiaomi\'s screen and chip use more, but a full day of study is realistic. With 45W charging it refills fastest. Use a charger of matching wattage: an old phone charger will take all night.</p>\n<h2>Software support</h2>\n<p>Tablets are kept for a long time, so ask about updates. Xiaomi and OnePlus promise a few Android versions and several years of patches on these models. Samsung leads. If the tablet will hold office email or banking, give this more weight.</p>\n<h2>Durability</h2>\n<p>None of these except the Samsung has full water resistance. Large glass panels break easily when a tablet is dropped on a corner, and replacement costs are high. Use a case that covers the corners, and never carry a tablet in a bag beside a metal water bottle.</p>\n<h2>Questions before you buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Is it an official unit with a warranty in Nepal?</li>\n<li>Which memory version is it? Choose 8GB.</li>\n<li>Is the charger included, and how many watts?</li>\n<li>Is Widevine L1 supported?</li>\n<li>Are the pen and keyboard available now, and not \"soon\"?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Who should buy what</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>University student, heavy reading:</strong> Xiaomi Pad 7.</li>\n<li><strong>Family film tablet:</strong> OnePlus Pad Go 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Salesperson in the field:</strong> OnePlus Pad Lite LTE.</li>\n<li><strong>School student:</strong> Redmi Pad 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Daily handwriter:</strong> save for the Tab S10 FE.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>The Xiaomi Pad 7 is the rare product that is clearly the best in its price class. The OnePlus Pad Go 2 is the more relaxing one. Choose by asking whether you lean forward or lean back when you use a tablet. Forward means Xiaomi, back means OnePlus.</p>','gadgets/2026/xiaomi-pad-7.webp',1,'2026-11-22 18:15:00','2026-11-22 18:15:00');
